    <div type="book" osisID="1Cor">
      <title>1 Corinthians</title>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.1">
        <title>1 Corinthians 1 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.1">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">The document's genre and occasion:</hi> This is a letter (<hi type="italic">epistole</hi>) from Paul to a community he personally founded. Unlike Romans, where Paul wrote to introduce himself and his gospel to a community he had never visited, this letter addresses people Paul knows firsthand. According to the narrative of Acts 18:1-11, Paul came to Corinth from Athens, worked with Aquila and Priscilla as tentmakers, proclaimed the message in the synagogue every Sabbath, and then — after opposition from some in the synagogue — moved next door to the house of Titius Justus, a God-fearer. The Lord spoke to Paul in a night vision: "Do not be afraid, but go on speaking and do not be silent, for I am with you, and no one will attack you to harm you, for I have many people in this city" (Acts 18:9-10). Paul stayed in Corinth eighteen months, "teaching the word of God among them" (Acts 18:11). The Corinthian community was Paul's work. He laid the foundation (3:10).</p>
          <p>Corinth itself was a Roman colony, refounded by Julius Caesar in 44 BCE after lying in ruins for a century following its destruction by Rome in 146 BCE. By Paul's day it was the capital of the province of Achaia, a commercial crossroads connecting the Aegean and Adriatic seas, a city of significant wealth, cultural diversity, and social stratification. It was also a city dominated by Greek cultural values — rhetoric, philosophical debate, competition for honor and status. These characteristics of Corinthian culture will prove essential to understanding the problems Paul addresses.</p>
          <p>The occasion for writing is specific. Paul has received information from multiple sources. "Chloe's people" (1:11) have reported quarreling. The community has also apparently written Paul a letter with questions (7:1: "Now concerning the matters about which you wrote"). The letter therefore responds to both reports and inquiries, addressing a range of problems: factionalism (chapters 1-4), sexual immorality (chapter 5), lawsuits among believers (chapter 6), marriage and celibacy (chapter 7), food offered to idols (chapters 8-10), worship practice and head coverings (chapter 11), spiritual gifts and their use in assembly (chapters 12-14), and the reality of bodily resurrection (chapter 15). The letter is not a theological treatise; it is pastoral intervention in a community that Paul founded and that has gone badly wrong in multiple areas.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 2):</hi> Paul continues the argument about wisdom and the cross by turning to his own example. He reminds the Corinthians that when he first came to them, he did not come "proclaiming to you the testimony of God with lofty speech or wisdom" (2:1). Instead, "I decided to know nothing among you except Jesus Christ and him crucified" (2:2). He came "in weakness and in fear and much trembling" (2:3), and his message was not delivered "in plausible words of wisdom, but in demonstration of the Spirit and of power" (2:4). The purpose: "so that your faith might not rest in the wisdom of men but in the power of God" (2:5). Paul then introduces a distinction between the wisdom of this age and a hidden wisdom of God that is revealed through the Spirit (2:6-16). The Spirit searches everything, "even the depths of God" (2:10), and the "spiritual person" (<hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi>) judges all things but is judged by no one (2:15). The chapter closes: "But we have the mind of Christ" (2:16). Chapter 2 thus deepens the argument of chapter 1 by demonstrating that the rejection of worldly wisdom does not mean the rejection of all wisdom. There is a wisdom, but it is God's wisdom, revealed by the Spirit, and it is the wisdom of the cross — not the wisdom of rhetorical performance.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 1 connects to what follows:</hi> Chapter 1 performs three functions that the rest of the letter depends on. First, the opening and thanksgiving (1:1-9) establish the identity of the community: they are "the assembly of God" (<hi type="italic">ekklesia tou theou</hi>), sanctified, called, enriched with gifts, waiting for the Lord's revealing. This identity is defined by God's action, not by human achievement or affiliation. Second, the exposure of the faction problem (1:10-17) diagnoses the disease: the community has divided itself along the lines of human leaders, treating the gospel as a platform for competitive loyalty. This diagnosis governs not just chapters 1-4 but the entire letter, since nearly every problem Paul addresses — lawsuits, worship disorder, the abuse of spiritual gifts — is rooted in the same competitive, status-seeking mentality. Third, the argument about the cross and wisdom (1:18-31) provides the theological foundation for everything that follows. If God's power operates through apparent weakness and God's wisdom operates through apparent foolishness, then every form of human boasting and status competition is exposed as contrary to the gospel. The Corinthians' problems are not merely social failures; they are theological failures — a misunderstanding of what the cross reveals about how God works.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.1.1-1Cor.1.3">
          <title>Verses 1-3: The Opening — Sender, Recipients, and Greeting</title>
          <p>"Paul, called to be an apostle of Christ Jesus by the will of God, and our brother Sosthenes" (1:1).</p>
          <p>Paul identifies himself with the same language he used in Romans 1:1. He is "called" (<hi type="italic">kletos</hi>) — summoned by divine initiative, not self-appointed. The word <hi type="italic">kletos</hi> places Paul in the line of those whom God summoned for specific tasks: Abraham called from Ur (Genesis 12:1), Moses from the bush (Exodus 3:4), Isaiah in the temple (Isaiah 6:8-9), Jeremiah before his birth (Jeremiah 1:5). The calling is "by the will of God" (<hi type="italic">dia thelematos theou</hi>), removing any suggestion that Paul's authority rests on human decision or personal ambition. His apostleship originates in God's will, not his own.</p>
          <p>The co-sender is "our brother Sosthenes" (<hi type="italic">Sosthenes ho adelphos</hi>). In Acts 18:17, after Gallio dismissed the charges brought against Paul by the synagogue in Corinth, "they all seized Sosthenes, the ruler of the synagogue, and beat him in front of the tribunal." If this is the same Sosthenes — and the unusual name and Corinthian connection make this plausible — then a man who was once the leader of the Corinthian synagogue is now Paul's associate, addressed as "brother." The Jewish synagogue ruler has become a member of the community he may once have opposed. The Corinthians would recognize the name and the transformation it represents.</p>
          <p>"To the church of God that is in Corinth, to those sanctified in Christ Jesus, called to be saints, together with all those who in every place call upon the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, both their Lord and ours" (1:2).</p>
          <p>The recipients are described with extraordinary precision, and every element bears weight.</p>
          <p>"The church of God" (<hi type="italic">te ekklesia tou theou</hi>) — the word <hi type="italic">ekklesia</hi> in the LXX translates the Hebrew <hi type="italic">qahal</hi>, the formal assembly of Israel gathered before God. Deuteronomy 23:1-8 legislates who may enter "the assembly of the LORD" (<hi type="italic">ekklesia kyriou</hi>). 1 Chronicles 28:8 uses the phrase "the assembly of the LORD" (<hi type="italic">ekklesia kyriou</hi>) for all Israel gathered in the presence of God. Nehemiah 13:1 uses it for the assembly that hears the Torah read. The word carries the weight of Israel's identity as God's assembled people. Paul applies this term to a community of mostly Gentile believers in a Greek commercial city. But the genitive is critical: this is not "the church of Corinth" or "the church of Paul." It is "the church of God" (<hi type="italic">tou theou</hi>). The assembly belongs to God. This possessive will prove to be the first blow against the factionalism Paul is about to address. Those who say "I am of Paul" or "I am of Apollos" have already been told, in the greeting, whose they actually are.</p>
          <p>"That is in Corinth" (<hi type="italic">te ouse en Korintho</hi>) — the participle <hi type="italic">ouse</hi> ("being, existing") with the locative "in Corinth" identifies the assembly's location without defining its identity by that location. The assembly of God exists in Corinth, but it is not constituted by Corinth. It belongs to God and happens to be located in this city.</p>
          <p>"To those sanctified in Christ Jesus" (<hi type="italic">hegiasmenois en Christo Iesou</hi>) — a perfect passive participle. "Sanctified" (<hi type="italic">hegiasmenois</hi>, from <hi type="italic">hagiazein</hi>) means set apart, consecrated, made holy. The perfect tense indicates a completed action with ongoing results: they have been sanctified and remain in that sanctified state. The passive voice identifies the action as done to them, not by them. The agent of sanctification is God, and the sphere in which it occurred is "in Christ Jesus" (<hi type="italic">en Christo Iesou</hi>). In the LXX, <hi type="italic">hagiazein</hi> describes the consecration of priests (Exodus 28:41, 29:1), the tabernacle (Exodus 29:44), the Sabbath (Genesis 2:3), and Israel itself. Leviticus 20:8: "I am the LORD who sanctifies you" (<hi type="italic">ego kyrios ho hagiazein hymas</hi>). The Corinthians, for all their problems — and the problems are severe, as the letter will reveal — have been consecrated by God's action. This is not a statement about their moral achievement; it is a statement about God's claim on them. They are God's holy possession. The irony that Paul will exploit throughout the letter is the gap between their consecrated status and their actual behavior.</p>
          <p>"Called to be saints" (<hi type="italic">kletois hagiois</hi>) — identical to the phrase in Romans 1:7. The word <hi type="italic">hagioi</hi> ("holy ones, saints") in the LXX refers to those who belong to God's sacred sphere. They are "called" (<hi type="italic">kletois</hi>) — summoned — into this status. The repetition of the calling vocabulary (<hi type="italic">kletos</hi> in v. 1, <hi type="italic">kletois</hi> in v. 2) binds Paul and the Corinthians together: both owe their identity to God's summons, not to their own initiative.</p>
          <p>"Together with all those who in every place call upon the name of our Lord Jesus Christ" (<hi type="italic">syn pasin tois epikaloumenois to onoma tou kyriou hemon Iesou Christou en panti topo</hi>) — this phrase explodes the boundaries of the letter. The Corinthian community is not an isolated unit. It exists "together with" (<hi type="italic">syn</hi>) every community everywhere that calls on the name of Jesus. The phrase "call upon the name" (<hi type="italic">epikaleisthai to onoma</hi>) is loaded with scriptural resonance. Joel 2:32 (LXX 3:5): "And it shall come to pass that everyone who calls upon the name of the LORD shall be saved" (<hi type="italic">pas hos an epikalesetai to onoma kyriou sothesetai</hi>). Peter quoted this text at Pentecost (Acts 2:21). In Joel, the name called upon is YHWH. Here, without argument or explanation, the name called upon is "our Lord Jesus Christ." The Joel formula — which defined Israel's relationship to YHWH — is transferred to Jesus as a matter of established practice. The Corinthians are part of a worldwide community defined by this act of invocation.</p>
          <p>"Both their Lord and ours" (<hi type="italic">auton kai hemon</hi>) — the same Lord belongs to every community and to this one. The unity of lordship is the foundation of community unity. If the Corinthians share the same Lord with all believers everywhere, the division into competing factions around human leaders is not merely socially destructive; it contradicts the basic structure of their identity.</p>
          <p>"Grace to you and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ" (1:3).</p>
          <p>The standard Pauline greeting, combining the Greek epistolary convention (<hi type="italic">chairein</hi>, "greetings," transformed into <hi type="italic">charis</hi>, "grace") with the Hebrew greeting (<hi type="italic">shalom</hi>, rendered <hi type="italic">eirene</hi>, "peace"). The source of both is identified: "from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ." God and Jesus are placed side by side as co-source of grace and peace, a pairing that would be extraordinary to anyone trained in Israel's strict monotheism, yet which Paul states without defense, as though the point is already settled.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.1.4-1Cor.1.9">
          <title>Verses 4-9: Thanksgiving — God's Gifts and God's Faithfulness</title>
          <p>"I give thanks to my God always for you because of the grace of God that was given you in Christ Jesus" (1:4).</p>
          <p>Paul's thanksgivings in his letters typically signal the major themes that will follow, and this one is no exception. He thanks God — not the Corinthians themselves — for "the grace of God that was given you" (<hi type="italic">te chariti tou theou te dotheise hymin</hi>). Grace is a gift (<hi type="italic">dotheise</hi>, from <hi type="italic">didomi</hi>, "to give"), and it was given "in Christ Jesus" (<hi type="italic">en Christo Iesou</hi>). Everything the Corinthians possess spiritually is a gift from God received in the sphere of Christ. This is not incidental phrasing; it is the theological foundation Paul will use to dismantle their boasting. If everything is a gift, no one has grounds for self-congratulation. Paul will make this logic explicit in 4:7: "What do you have that you did not receive? And if you received it, why do you boast as if you did not receive it?"</p>
          <p>"That in every way you were enriched in him in all speech and all knowledge" (1:5).</p>
          <p>"Enriched" (<hi type="italic">eploutisthete</hi>) — aorist passive. They were made rich by God's action. The wealth is specified: "in all speech" (<hi type="italic">en panti logo</hi>) and "in all knowledge" (<hi type="italic">pase gnosei</hi>). Both <hi type="italic">logos</hi> and <hi type="italic">gnosis</hi> will become contested terms in this letter. The Corinthians clearly prized rhetorical ability and knowledge — the very things that fuel the factionalism Paul is about to address. Paul affirms these as genuine gifts of God's grace. He does not deny that the Corinthians have been enriched; he will challenge what they have done with their enrichment. The gifts are real; the problem is how they are being deployed.</p>
          <p>"Even as the testimony about Christ was confirmed among you" (1:6).</p>
          <p>"The testimony about Christ" (<hi type="italic">to martyrion tou Christou</hi>) — the proclamation concerning Christ. "Confirmed" (<hi type="italic">ebebaiothe</hi>) — the verb <hi type="italic">bebaioo</hi> means to make firm, validate, establish, guarantee. In Greek legal and commercial usage, <hi type="italic">bebaiosis</hi> was the guarantee that a seller provided for a transaction — the assurance that the sale was valid and would hold. The gospel was validated and established among the Corinthians — by the evidence of the Spirit's work, by the gifts that accompanied their reception of the message, by the transformation of their lives. The passive voice attributes the confirming to God.</p>
          <p>"So that you are not lacking in any gift, as you wait for the revealing of our Lord Jesus Christ" (1:7).</p>
          <p>"Not lacking in any gift" (<hi type="italic">me hysterousthai en medeni charismati</hi>) — the Corinthians possess the full range of spiritual gifts (<hi type="italic">charismata</hi>). This will be confirmed elaborately in chapters 12-14, where Paul addresses the diversity and proper use of gifts in the assembly. The community is overflowing with divine endowment. The irony is sharp: a community lacking nothing in gifts is tearing itself apart. The problem in Corinth is never the absence of divine provision; it is the misuse and misunderstanding of what has been abundantly given.</p>
          <p>"As you wait for" (<hi type="italic">apekdechomenous</hi>) — the present middle participle describes an ongoing posture of expectation. The community exists in a state of anticipation, oriented toward a future event. The object of that expectation is "the revealing of our Lord Jesus Christ" (<hi type="italic">ten apokalypsin tou kyriou hemon Iesou Christou</hi>). The word <hi type="italic">apokalypsis</hi> means "unveiling, uncovering, disclosure" — the moment when what is presently hidden becomes visible. The community lives between the gifts already received and the Lord not yet fully manifested. This "already/not yet" structure governs the entire letter: the Corinthians have been sanctified, enriched, and gifted, but the final revelation has not yet occurred. They live in the interval, and how they live in that interval matters.</p>
          <p>"Who will also sustain you to the end, guiltless in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ" (1:8).</p>
          <p>"Who" (<hi type="italic">hos</hi>) — the antecedent is most naturally "our Lord Jesus Christ" from the preceding clause, though the logic connects to God's action throughout the thanksgiving. "Sustain" (<hi type="italic">bebaiosei</hi>) — the same verb as "confirmed" (<hi type="italic">ebebaiothe</hi>) in verse 6. The one who confirmed the testimony among them will also confirm/sustain the believers themselves. The word carries the same legal force: a guarantee, a securing of what has been transacted. "To the end" (<hi type="italic">heos telous</hi>) — until the consummation, until the goal is reached, until the <hi type="italic">telos</hi> arrives.</p>
          <p>"Guiltless" (<hi type="italic">anenkletous</hi>) — literally "not called to account, without accusation, unimpeachable." The word is legal: it describes someone against whom no charge can be brought. The intended outcome of God's sustaining action is that the Corinthians will stand on the day of judgment without accusation against them. "The day of our Lord Jesus Christ" (<hi type="italic">en te hemera tou kyriou hemon Iesou Christou</hi>) transposes the prophetic concept of "the day of the LORD" (<hi type="italic">yom YHWH</hi>) — which in Amos 5:18-20, Isaiah 2:12, Joel 2:1, and Zephaniah 1:14-18 was a day of reckoning and judgment — onto Jesus Christ. Christ presides over the day of judgment.</p>
          <p>Two things must be noted about this verse. First, the sustaining to the end is attributed to God/Christ's action, not to the Corinthians' performance. The ground is God's initiative, not human effort. Second, this statement of divine commitment does not make the rest of the letter superfluous. Paul will go on to warn the Corinthians at length about conduct that endangers their standing: "Do you not know that the unrighteous will not inherit the kingdom of God?" (6:9); "let anyone who thinks that he stands take heed lest he fall" (10:12); "if I preach the gospel, that gives me no ground for boasting" (9:16); "I discipline my body and keep it under control, lest after preaching to others I myself should be disqualified" (9:27). The affirmation of God's faithfulness and the urgency of serious warnings coexist in the same letter. A first-century reader would not hear a contradiction but a description of how covenant relationship works: God is faithful to his commitments, and the community is responsible to live within the relationship God has established. God's faithfulness does not eliminate human responsibility; it grounds it. Israel's own story illustrates this pattern: God was faithful to his covenant with Israel while simultaneously holding Israel accountable for covenant violation (Deuteronomy 7:9-12, where God's faithfulness is described alongside the warning "know therefore that the LORD your God is God, the faithful God who keeps covenant... with those who love him and keep his commandments").</p>
          <p>"God is faithful, by whom you were called into the fellowship of his Son, Jesus Christ our Lord" (1:9).</p>
          <p>"God is faithful" (<hi type="italic">pistos ho theos</hi>) — this is the theological ground of everything in verses 4-8. The word <hi type="italic">pistos</hi> in the LXX describes God's essential character. Deuteronomy 7:9: "the faithful God" (<hi type="italic">ho theos ho pistos</hi>) who keeps covenant and steadfast love with those who love him and keep his commandments. Isaiah 49:7: "the LORD, who is faithful" (<hi type="italic">pistos estin kyrios</hi>). God can be relied upon. His faithfulness is not abstract; it is covenantal — he keeps his commitments, he does not abandon what he has begun. Paul will invoke this same principle again in 10:13: "God is faithful, and he will not let you be tested beyond your ability."</p>
          <p>"By whom you were called" (<hi type="italic">di hou eklethete</hi>) — passive voice with God as agent. The Corinthians did not choose God; God called them. The initiative is entirely on God's side. The verb <hi type="italic">kaleo</hi> ("to call") has appeared three times in two verses (<hi type="italic">kletos</hi> in v. 1, <hi type="italic">kletois</hi> in v. 2, <hi type="italic">eklethete</hi> here). The repetition is deliberate: everything about this community originates in God's summons.</p>
          <p>"Into the fellowship of his Son" (<hi type="italic">eis koinonian tou huiou autou</hi>) — the word <hi type="italic">koinonia</hi> is rich and specific. It means partnership, sharing, participation, joint involvement, communion. It is not mere association but active participation in a shared reality. The Corinthians were called into a shared life with Christ — a partnership in which they participate in his story, his death, his resurrection, his ongoing life. Paul will develop this concept in 10:16-17, where <hi type="italic">koinonia</hi> describes participation in the body and blood of Christ through the common meal, and where that participation constitutes the community as "one body." The factionalism that Paul is about to expose is a direct violation of this <hi type="italic">koinonia</hi>. God called them into shared life with his Son; they have divided that shared life into competing parties. The thanksgiving sets up the accusation.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.1.10-1Cor.1.17">
          <title>Verses 10-17: The Exposure of the Community's Division</title>
          <p>"I appeal to you, brothers, by the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, that all of you agree, and that there be no divisions among you, but that you be united in the same mind and the same judgment" (1:10).</p>
          <p>The transition from thanksgiving to problem is immediate. Paul uses <hi type="italic">parakalo</hi> ("I appeal, I urge, I exhort") — not the language of command (<hi type="italic">epitasso</hi>) but of urgent entreaty. The appeal is grounded "by the name of our Lord Jesus Christ" (<hi type="italic">dia tou onomatos tou kyriou hemon Iesou Christou</hi>). The name is the one "called upon" by all believers everywhere (v. 2). If one name unites all communities in every place, that same name should unite the factions within a single community.</p>
          <p>The appeal has three parts: "that all of you agree" (<hi type="italic">hina to auto legete pantes</hi> — literally "that you all say the same thing"); "that there be no divisions among you" (<hi type="italic">me e en hymin schismata</hi>); and "that you be united in the same mind and the same judgment" (<hi type="italic">ete de katertismenoi en to auto noi kai en te aute gnome</hi>).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">schismata</hi> ("divisions, tears, rents") is used elsewhere for the tearing of cloth (Matthew 9:16, Mark 2:21) and for a split in a crowd (John 7:43, 9:16, 10:19). The community has been torn apart, like a garment ripped along its seams. The verb <hi type="italic">katertismenoi</hi> ("made complete, restored, mended") is the word used for mending fishing nets (Matthew 4:21, Mark 1:19). The community that has been torn needs to be mended. The metaphor is domestic and practical: this is repair work, not abstract theology.</p>
          <p>"For it has been reported to me by Chloe's people that there is quarreling among you, my brothers" (1:11).</p>
          <p>Paul names his source: "Chloe's people" (<hi type="italic">ton Chloes</hi> — literally "those of Chloe"). Chloe is named without introduction, suggesting she is known to the Corinthians. Her "people" — household members, slaves, commercial associates, or emissaries — have brought Paul the report. Paul is transparent about where his information comes from. He is not trafficking in anonymous rumor. The problem is "quarreling" (<hi type="italic">erides</hi>) — strife, contention, competitive argument.</p>
          <p>"What I mean is that each one of you says, 'I follow Paul,' or 'I follow Apollos,' or 'I follow Cephas,' or 'I follow Christ'" (1:12).</p>
          <p>Four slogans. The formula "<hi type="italic">ego eimi Paulou</hi>" (I am of Paul) uses the genitive of belonging — "I belong to Paul." The factions are organized around human leaders:</p>
          <p>Paul — the community's founder, who spent eighteen months in Corinth, laid the foundation (3:10), and knows the community intimately.</p>
          <p>Apollos — described in Acts 18:24-28 as "an eloquent man" (<hi type="italic">aner logios</hi>), "competent in the Scriptures" (<hi type="italic">dynatos on en tais graphais</hi>), who came to Corinth after Paul's departure and "greatly helped those who through grace had believed" (Acts 18:27). In a city that prized rhetorical skill, Apollos's eloquence would have been magnetic. The "I am of Apollos" faction likely admired his oratorical and intellectual gifts — the very <hi type="italic">sophia logou</hi> ("wisdom of word") that Paul will identify as dangerous to the gospel's integrity.</p>
          <p>Cephas — the Aramaic name of Peter (<hi type="italic">Petros</hi>), the leading apostle of the Jerusalem community. Whether Peter visited Corinth personally or whether a faction simply claimed his authority as superior to Paul's, the "I am of Cephas" group represents an appeal to Jerusalem-based apostolic prestige.</p>
          <p>Christ — the fourth slogan is the most difficult to interpret. It could be Paul's own corrective interjection ("but I belong to Christ!"), or it could be a fourth faction that claims direct access to Christ without apostolic mediation, using the Lord's own name as a party label in the same competitive way others use Paul's or Apollos's. If the latter, the irony is devastating: the name that should unite all is being wielded as one more dividing marker.</p>
          <p>The deeper problem is not merely social discord but theological confusion. The Corinthians are treating the gospel as a platform for competing allegiances, evaluating teachers by the standards of rhetorical performance and personal prestige — the very standards that Paul will argue the cross overturns.</p>
          <p>"Is Christ divided? Was Paul crucified for you? Or were you baptized in the name of Paul?" (1:13).</p>
          <p>Three rhetorical questions, each demanding the answer "No."</p>
          <p>"Is Christ divided?" (<hi type="italic">memeristai ho Christos</hi>) — the perfect passive: has Christ been divided into portions, distributed among factions, so that the Paul-party gets one piece and the Apollos-party gets another? The question is absurd, and the absurdity is the argument. Christ is indivisible. A divided community implies a divided Christ, which is impossible. Therefore the division must be wrong.</p>
          <p>"Was Paul crucified for you?" (<hi type="italic">me Paulos estaurōthe hyper hymon</hi>) — the particle <hi type="italic">me</hi> expects a negative answer. Paul was not crucified for anyone. Only Christ was. The foundation of the community's identity is not which teacher they heard first but which person died for them. Belonging is determined by the cross, not by the classroom.</p>
          <p>"Were you baptized in the name of Paul?" (<hi type="italic">e eis to onoma Paulou ebaptisthete</hi>) — baptism is "into the name of" (<hi type="italic">eis to onoma</hi>) the one to whom one gives allegiance, the one under whose authority and ownership one is placed. The Corinthians were baptized into Christ's name, not Paul's. Their baptism constituted them as Christ's possession, not Paul's followers. The faction slogans contradict the logic of their own baptism.</p>
          <p>"I thank God that I baptized none of you except Crispus and Gaius, so that no one may say that you were baptized in my name" (1:14-15).</p>
          <p>Paul expresses relief at having baptized so few Corinthians. If he had baptized the entire community, the "I am of Paul" faction would have an additional claim: "we were baptized by Paul himself." The few he did baptize are named. Crispus is identified in Acts 18:8 as "the ruler of the synagogue" who "believed in the Lord, together with his entire household" — one of Paul's earliest and most significant converts in Corinth, a Jewish community leader who crossed over. Gaius is likely the same Gaius Paul mentions in Romans 16:23 as "host to me and to the whole church" — a man of sufficient means to host the entire community for assembly.</p>
          <p>"(I did baptize also the household of Stephanas. Beyond that, I do not know whether I baptized anyone else)" (1:16).</p>
          <p>The parenthetical correction is revealing. Paul's memory catches up with his pen: he forgot Stephanas's household. This is the voice of real correspondence, unpolished and authentic. Stephanas will reappear in 16:15 as part of "the firstfruits of Achaia," a household that "devoted themselves to the service of the saints." Paul's uncertainty ("I do not know whether I baptized anyone else") further demonstrates that he is not constructing a careful argument about baptism in the abstract; he is dealing with a concrete social situation and drawing practical conclusions.</p>
          <p>"For Christ did not send me to baptize but to preach the gospel, and not with words of eloquent wisdom, lest the cross of Christ be emptied of its power" (1:17).</p>
          <p>This verse is the pivot of the entire chapter. It connects the faction problem (vv. 10-16) to the theology of the cross (vv. 18-31) through a single hinge: the relationship between wisdom and the cross.</p>
          <p>"Christ did not send me to baptize but to preach the gospel" (<hi type="italic">ou gar apesteilen me Christos baptizein alla euangelizesthai</hi>) — Paul does not denigrate baptism. He ranks his commission. The verb <hi type="italic">apesteilen</hi> ("sent") connects to <hi type="italic">apostolos</hi> ("one sent") in verse 1. The apostle was sent to proclaim (<hi type="italic">euangelizesthai</hi>), and the baptizing of converts was a secondary activity that others could perform. The priority is the announcement.</p>
          <p>"Not with words of eloquent wisdom" (<hi type="italic">ouk en sophia logou</hi>) — literally "not in wisdom of word." This phrase diagnoses the Corinthian disease. The Corinthians live in a culture that prizes rhetorical sophistication. Corinth, like all Greek cities, valued the rhetorician — the person who could persuade, dazzle, and dominate through the power of speech. Traveling speakers (sophists) competed for followers by demonstrating their verbal skill. The Corinthians have imported this cultural value system into the community and are evaluating apostles the way they would evaluate competing rhetoricians: Who speaks best? Who is most impressive? Whose style do I prefer? Paul says he was sent to preach the gospel explicitly not in that mode.</p>
          <p>"Lest the cross of Christ be emptied of its power" (<hi type="italic">hina me kenothe ho stauros tou Christou</hi>) — the verb <hi type="italic">kenoō</hi> means "to empty, to make void, to drain of content." If the gospel is delivered in the packaging of rhetorical brilliance, the power shifts from the message to the messenger, from the event of the cross to the performance of the speaker, from God's act to human artistry. The cross's power — as Paul will now argue — resides precisely in its apparent weakness and foolishness. To dress it in the trappings of worldly wisdom is to neutralize what makes it effective. The cross is either foolish and powerful, or it is made eloquent and emptied.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.1.18-1Cor.1.25">
          <title>Verses 18-25: The Word of the Cross — God's Power Through Apparent Foolishness</title>
          <p>"For the word of the cross is folly to those who are perishing, but to us who are being saved it is the power of God" (1:18).</p>
          <p>"The word of the cross" (<hi type="italic">ho logos tou staurou</hi>) — the message whose content is the crucifixion. The <hi type="italic">stauros</hi> (cross, stake, upright pole for execution) was the instrument of the most degrading form of Roman capital punishment, reserved for slaves, insurrectionists, and the lowest classes of criminals. Roman citizens were exempt from it. Crucifixion was designed not merely to kill but to humiliate — the victim was displayed publicly, stripped, powerless, a spectacle of shame. Cicero called it "the most cruel and disgusting penalty" (<hi type="italic">crudelissimum taeterrimumque supplicium</hi>, <hi type="italic">In Verrem</hi> 2.5.165) and declared that "the very word 'cross' should be far removed not only from the person of a Roman citizen but from his thoughts, his eyes, and his ears" (<hi type="italic">Pro Rabirio</hi> 16). To first-century ears, the "word of the cross" was the announcement of a message centered on the most shameful event imaginable.</p>
          <p>"Folly" (<hi type="italic">moria</hi>) — not merely error but absurdity, manifest stupidity. The crucified man as God's agent of salvation was not just wrong by ancient standards; it was laughable. A graffito from the Palatine Hill in Rome, likely from the late first or early second century, depicts a man with a donkey's head on a cross, with the inscription "Alexamenos worships his god" — a mocking caricature of Christian devotion. The social contempt for the cross was real and pervasive.</p>
          <p>"To those who are perishing" (<hi type="italic">tois apollymenois</hi>) — present middle/passive participle. The present tense describes an ongoing process: those who are in the course of being destroyed, those currently on the path of destruction. This is not a fixed, past-tense assignment. Paul does not say "those who were destroyed" or "those who are the destroyed" (as if describing an unchangeable category). He describes a current trajectory.</p>
          <p>"To us who are being saved" (<hi type="italic">tois sozomenois hemin</hi>) — likewise a present passive participle. "Being saved" is an ongoing process, not a completed event. The Corinthians are in the course of being saved. Paul includes himself in this group (<hi type="italic">hemin</hi>, "to us"). The parallel between the two participles is precise: as the perishing are in the process of perishing, so the saved are in the process of being saved. Both descriptions indicate a current direction, not a finished destination. Paul will use similar language in 2 Corinthians 2:15, distinguishing between "those who are being saved and those who are perishing" (<hi type="italic">tois sozomenois kai en tois apollymenois</hi>) — again, present participles describing ongoing trajectories.</p>
          <p>"It is the power of God" (<hi type="italic">dynamis theou estin</hi>) — the cross, which appears to be the very embodiment of powerlessness, is in reality the operative power of God. The paradox is not rhetorical decoration; it is the argument. What the world sees as weakness is God's chosen instrument of power.</p>
          <p>"For it is written, 'I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the discernment of the discerning I will thwart'" (1:19).</p>
          <p>Paul quotes Isaiah 29:14 (LXX), drawing on a prophetic context that illuminates his point. In Isaiah 29, God pronounces judgment on Jerusalem ("Ariel, Ariel, the city where David encamped," Isaiah 29:1) because the people offer superficial worship: "this people draw near with their mouth and honor me with their lips, while their hearts are far from me, and their fear of me is a commandment taught by men" (Isaiah 29:13 LXX). God's response to this surface-level piety is a "wonderful and extraordinary" act (Isaiah 29:14 LXX) that will cause "the wisdom of the wise" to perish and "the understanding of the prudent" to be hidden. The pattern in Isaiah is this: when human religion becomes a performance of wisdom and tradition disconnected from genuine devotion, God acts in a way that shatters the entire framework.</p>
          <p>Paul sees the cross as the ultimate instance of this pattern. God has acted in a way — through a crucified Messiah — that destroys every system of human wisdom that claims to know how God should operate. The Isaiah quotation is not ornamental; it identifies the cross as the fulfillment of a pattern that runs through Israel's history: God repeatedly acts in ways that confound human expectations and overturn human calculations.</p>
          <p>"Where is the one who is wise? Where is the scribe? Where is the debater of this age? Has not God made foolish the wisdom of the world?" (1:20).</p>
          <p>Three rhetorical challenges, echoing the prophetic taunt form. Isaiah 33:18 (LXX): "Where are the scribes? Where are the counselors? Where is the one who counts those being nourished?" — directed at the now-vanished threat of Assyria, whose intimidating power proved empty before God's deliverance. Isaiah 19:12: "Where then are your wise men?" — addressed to Egypt, whose sages could not fathom God's purposes. The form itself — "where is...?" — is the language of prophetic triumph: the thing that seemed so formidable has been swept away by God's action.</p>
          <p>"The one who is wise" (<hi type="italic">sophos</hi>) represents philosophical and intellectual achievement. "The scribe" (<hi type="italic">grammateus</hi>) represents learned scholarship — the expert in texts and traditions. "The debater of this age" (<hi type="italic">syzetetees tou aionos toutou</hi>) represents the skilled disputant, the one who wins arguments. The phrase "of this age" (<hi type="italic">tou aionos toutou</hi>) is crucial. It identifies the wisdom, learning, and debating skill as belonging to a particular era — "this age" as opposed to the age to come. The framework is eschatological: there is a present age, governed by certain standards, and there is God's coming age, which operates on entirely different principles. The wisdom of "this age" has been rendered obsolete by God's action in the cross.</p>
          <p>"Has not God made foolish the wisdom of the world?" (<hi type="italic">ouchi emoranen ho theos ten sophian tou kosmou</hi>) — the verb <hi type="italic">morainein</hi> means "to make foolish, to show to be foolish, to expose as absurd." God has not merely disagreed with worldly wisdom; he has actively unmasked it as foolishness through his own surprising action. The cross is the demonstration that proves worldly wisdom bankrupt.</p>
          <p>"For since, in the wisdom of God, the world did not know God through wisdom, it pleased God through the folly of what we preach to save those who believe" (1:21).</p>
          <p>This verse contains a remarkable logical structure. "In the wisdom of God" (<hi type="italic">en te sophia tou theou</hi>) — God's own wisdom determined the outcome. It was part of God's design that the world would fail to reach God through human wisdom. The failure of philosophy and rhetoric to arrive at the knowledge of God was not an accident; it was built into God's plan. "The world did not know God through wisdom" (<hi type="italic">ouk egno ho kosmos dia tes sophias ton theon</hi>) — despite all its intellectual achievements, the world (<hi type="italic">ho kosmos</hi>, the ordered system of human civilization and thought) did not arrive at knowledge of God. The wisdom project failed.</p>
          <p>"It pleased God" (<hi type="italic">eudokesen ho theos</hi>) — the verb <hi type="italic">eudokeo</hi> means "to be well-pleased, to determine with delight, to resolve upon." God's choice of an alternative path was not reluctant but deliberate and pleased. "Through the folly of what we preach" (<hi type="italic">dia tes morias tou kerygmatos</hi>) — the foolishness of the proclamation, the absurdity of the announced message. The word <hi type="italic">kerygma</hi> is not the act of preaching (as if the style of delivery were foolish) but the content of the proclamation — what is preached, namely the crucified Messiah. "To save those who believe" (<hi type="italic">sosai tous pisteuontas</hi>) — the present participle <hi type="italic">pisteuontas</hi> ("those who believe, those who are believing") identifies the recipients of salvation by their characteristic activity: ongoing trust, active faith.</p>
          <p>"For Jews demand signs and Greeks seek wisdom, but we preach Christ crucified, a stumbling block to Jews and folly to Gentiles" (1:22-23).</p>
          <p>Two cultural groups, two demands, one message that offends both.</p>
          <p>"Jews demand signs" (<hi type="italic">Ioudaioi semeia aitousin</hi>) — the demand for miraculous authentication was embedded in Jewish messianic expectation. The Messiah was expected to perform signs that would validate his identity and demonstrate God's power. The exodus from Egypt was accompanied by signs; the prophets performed signs; the Messiah should surpass them all. A Messiah who ended on a Roman cross, without miraculous deliverance, without overthrowing the occupying power, met none of these expectations. The demand for signs is the demand for visible, undeniable demonstrations of power.</p>
          <p>"Greeks seek wisdom" (<hi type="italic">Hellenes sophian zetousin</hi>) — the Greek intellectual tradition valued rational coherence, philosophical sophistication, and persuasive argument. The sophists and philosophers who shaped Greek education sought truth through <hi type="italic">logos</hi> — reason, argument, discourse. A message centered on the execution of a Jewish artisan from Galilee offered nothing that this tradition would recognize as wisdom.</p>
          <p>"But we preach Christ crucified" (<hi type="italic">hemeis keryssomen Christon estauromenon</hi>) — Paul places the proclamation in direct opposition to both demands. The perfect passive participle <hi type="italic">estauromenon</hi> ("having been crucified, in the state of crucifixion") emphasizes the enduring condition. This is not "Christ who was once crucified and has now moved past it" but "Christ as the crucified one" — the crucifixion defines his identity permanently. The Messiah bears the marks of the cross as his permanent characteristic.</p>
          <p>"A stumbling block to Jews" (<hi type="italic">Ioudaiois skandalon</hi>) — the word <hi type="italic">skandalon</hi> means the trigger of a trap, the stick that springs the snare. It does not merely disappoint; it actively trips and ensnares. A crucified Messiah was not simply unexpected for Jewish hope; it was offensive, scandalous, a direct contradiction. Deuteronomy 21:22-23 declared: "if a man has committed a crime punishable by death and he is put to death, and you hang him on a tree, his body shall not remain all night on the tree, but you shall bury him the same day, for a hanged man is cursed by God." The Messiah, God's anointed, cannot be under God's curse. A crucified claimant was, by the logic of Deuteronomy, proven to be a false messiah. The cross was not neutral evidence; it was disqualifying evidence.</p>
          <p>"Folly to Gentiles" (<hi type="italic">ethnesin morian</hi>) — to the Greco-Roman world, the claim was simply absurd. To worship a crucified provincial was cultural insanity. The cross carried no grandeur, no philosophical sophistication, no rhetorical power. It was the death of slaves and rebels.</p>
          <p>"But to those who are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God" (1:24).</p>
          <p>"Those who are called" (<hi type="italic">tois kletois</hi>) — the same adjective from verse 1 (Paul is "called apostle") and verse 2 ("called saints"). The "called" are those who have responded to God's summons. Among the called, the division between Jew and Greek is transcended: "both Jews and Greeks" (<hi type="italic">Ioudaiois te kai Hellesin</hi>). The cultural categories that define how the cross is received — <hi type="italic">skandalon</hi> for Jews, <hi type="italic">moria</hi> for Greeks — are overcome within the community of the called.</p>
          <p>To these called ones, Christ is simultaneously "the power of God" (<hi type="italic">theou dynamin</hi>) and "the wisdom of God" (<hi type="italic">theou sophian</hi>). The two things the cross appears to lack — power and wisdom — are the two things it actually embodies. The cross is God's power because it accomplishes what human power cannot: the reconciliation of humanity to God, the defeat of the forces that enslave. The cross is God's wisdom because it achieves what human wisdom cannot even conceive: salvation through apparent defeat, victory through apparent shame.</p>
          <p>"For the foolishness of God is wiser than men, and the weakness of God is stronger than men" (1:25).</p>
          <p>Paul does not concede that God is actually foolish or weak. The construction is comparative and rhetorical: even if you categorize God's action (the cross) as foolishness, that divine "foolishness" exceeds all human wisdom. Even if you call it weakness, that divine "weakness" exceeds all human strength. The comparison is not between entities on the same scale but between entirely different orders of reality. What appears foolish from below is wisdom from above. What appears weak from below is power from above. The categories of human evaluation are inverted.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.1.26-1Cor.1.31">
          <title>Verses 26-31: The Evidence in the Community Itself — God Chooses What the World Discards</title>
          <p>"For consider your calling, brothers: not many of you were wise according to worldly standards, not many were powerful, not many were of noble birth" (1:26).</p>
          <p>"Consider your calling" (<hi type="italic">blepete ten klesin hymon</hi>) — the verb <hi type="italic">blepete</hi> ("look at, observe, examine") directs them to examine the empirical evidence of their own community. The "calling" (<hi type="italic">klesis</hi>) functions simultaneously as the divine act of summoning and as the community that resulted from that summons. The evidence is visible: look at who God actually called.</p>
          <p>"Not many wise according to the flesh" (<hi type="italic">ou polloi sophoi kata sarka</hi>) — not according to the world's standards of wisdom. "Not many powerful" (<hi type="italic">ou polloi dynatoi</hi>) — not many with social, political, or economic influence. "Not many of noble birth" (<hi type="italic">ou polloi eugeneis</hi>) — literally "well-born," from <hi type="italic">eu</hi> ("well") and <hi type="italic">genos</hi> ("birth, family"). Not many from the aristocratic families that dominated civic life.</p>
          <p>Paul says "not many" (<hi type="italic">ou polloi</hi>), not "none." There were people of standing in the community: Crispus the synagogue ruler (Acts 18:8), Gaius who hosted the entire assembly (Romans 16:23), Erastus the city treasurer (Romans 16:23), Stephanas whose household was among the firstfruits of Achaia (16:15). But the community was primarily composed of people without social capital — workers, freedmen, slaves, the ordinary and undistinguished. This demographic reality is not a failure of the mission; it is evidence of how God works. The composition of the community embodies the logic of the cross.</p>
          <p>"But God chose what is foolish in the world to shame the wise; God chose what is weak in the world to shame the strong; God chose what is low and despised in the world, even things that are not, to bring to nothing things that are" (1:27-28).</p>
          <p>Three parallel statements of God's election, each escalating in intensity:</p>
          <p>God chose (<hi type="italic">exelexato</hi>) the foolish things (<hi type="italic">ta mora tou kosmou</hi>) to shame (<hi type="italic">hina kataischyne</hi>) the wise.</p>
          <p>God chose the weak things (<hi type="italic">ta asthene tou kosmou</hi>) to shame the strong.</p>
          <p>God chose the low-born (<hi type="italic">ta agene</hi> — literally "without family, of no lineage") and the despised (<hi type="italic">ta exouthenemena</hi> — "treated as nothing, counted worthless") and the things that are not (<hi type="italic">ta me onta</hi>) to bring to nothing (<hi type="italic">hina ta onta katargese</hi>) the things that are (<hi type="italic">ta onta</hi>).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">exelexato</hi> ("chose") is the LXX's standard term for God's election. Its most foundational use is in Deuteronomy 7:6-7: "The LORD your God has chosen you (<hi type="italic">exelexato</hi>) to be a people for his treasured possession, out of all the peoples who are on the face of the earth. It was not because you were more in number than any other people that the LORD set his love on you and chose you, for you were the fewest of all peoples." God's election of Israel was explicitly not based on Israel's greatness, numerical strength, or merit. It was based on God's love and God's oath to the patriarchs (Deuteronomy 7:8). The logic Paul employs is identical: God chose the foolish, weak, and lowborn not despite their lack of worldly standing but precisely through that lack. God's method of operation consistently bypasses what the world considers valuable.</p>
          <p>The final phrase is the most radical: "the things that are not, to bring to nothing the things that are" (<hi type="italic">ta me onta, hina ta onta katargese</hi>). God chose what has no standing, no recognized existence in the world's hierarchy — what literally "is not" by the world's measurement — in order to nullify what does have standing, what "is" by those standards. In Greek philosophical categories, "being" (<hi type="italic">to on</hi>) is the highest term; "non-being" (<hi type="italic">to me on</hi>) is the lowest. God inverts even this most fundamental distinction. The things that count as real in the world's accounting are nullified by God's choice of the things that count as nothing. The verb <hi type="italic">katargeo</hi> ("to render inoperative, to make of no effect, to nullify") is strong: the existing power structures are not merely challenged but abolished by God's selection of the powerless.</p>
          <p>"So that no human being might boast in the presence of God" (1:29).</p>
          <p>"So that" (<hi type="italic">hopos</hi>) — purpose clause. Everything described in verses 18-28 — God's choice of the cross, God's choice of the foolish and weak, God's inversion of human hierarchies — serves a single unified purpose: the elimination of human boasting before God. The word <hi type="italic">kauchaomai</hi> ("to boast, to glory, to take pride") will become a major theme throughout 1 and 2 Corinthians. "All flesh" (<hi type="italic">pasa sarx</hi> — "every human being" in their creaturely limitation) is deprived of grounds for self-congratulation in God's presence.</p>
          <p>This is not arbitrary divine humiliation. The point is not that God delights in shaming the powerful for its own sake. The point is that the nature of the gospel — the cross, grace, divine initiative — is incompatible with human boasting. If God saves through weakness, no one can claim strength as the basis of their standing. If God chooses through foolishness, no one can claim wisdom as their qualification. The elimination of boasting is the natural consequence of a salvation that operates entirely through divine grace and initiative.</p>
          <p>"And because of him you are in Christ Jesus, who became to us wisdom from God, righteousness and sanctification and redemption" (1:30).</p>
          <p>"Because of him" (<hi type="italic">ex autou</hi>) — literally "out of him, from him." The Corinthians' existence "in Christ Jesus" originates from God. Their location in Christ is God's doing, not their achievement. The phrase "in Christ Jesus" (<hi type="italic">en Christo Iesou</hi>) — Paul's characteristic expression for the sphere of existence that defines the community — is grounded in divine initiative.</p>
          <p>"Who became to us wisdom from God" (<hi type="italic">hos egenethe sophia hemin apo theou</hi>) — Christ is the wisdom that the Greeks sought (v. 22). But this wisdom is "from God" (<hi type="italic">apo theou</hi>), not from human discovery. The <hi type="italic">sophia</hi> that the world sought through philosophy and rhetoric is found, paradoxically and exclusively, in the crucified Messiah.</p>
          <p>Three further characterizations follow, joined to "wisdom" as either coordinate terms or as specifications of what that wisdom includes:</p>
          <p>"Righteousness" (<hi type="italic">dikaiosyne</hi>) — right standing before God, the condition of being in right relationship with God. The word in the LXX (<hi type="italic">tsedaqah</hi>) encompasses both God's saving action and the status of those who are rightly aligned with God's purposes.</p>
          <p>"Sanctification" (<hi type="italic">hagiasmos</hi>) — the process of being made holy, set apart for God. This connects directly back to verse 2, where the Corinthians are described as "sanctified in Christ Jesus" (<hi type="italic">hegiasmenois en Christo Iesou</hi>). What was stated as their identity in the greeting is now grounded in Christ: he is the agent and sphere of their sanctification.</p>
          <p>"Redemption" (<hi type="italic">apolytrosis</hi>) — release through the payment of a ransom, liberation from bondage. The word carries deep resonance with Israel's foundational narrative: God "redeemed" Israel from Egypt (Exodus 6:6, Deuteronomy 7:8, 9:26, 13:5, 15:15). The verb <hi type="italic">lytroo</hi> and the noun <hi type="italic">lytrosis/apolytrosis</hi> describe God's liberating action, the act of buying back what belongs to him. In the LXX, God is repeatedly called Israel's "redeemer" (<hi type="italic">lytrotes, goēl</hi>). The Corinthians' redemption is located in Christ, who became for them what God became for Israel in the exodus.</p>
          <p>All four — wisdom, righteousness, sanctification, redemption — are located in Christ, not in human achievement. Christ did not merely teach these things or point the way toward them. He "became" (<hi type="italic">egenethe</hi>) them. He is the embodiment of what God provides.</p>
          <p>"So that, as it is written, 'Let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord'" (1:31).</p>
          <p>The quotation is from Jeremiah 9:23-24 (LXX 9:22-23): "Thus says the LORD: 'Let not the wise man boast in his wisdom, let not the mighty man boast in his might, let not the rich man boast in his riches, but let him who boasts boast in this, that he understands and knows me, that I am the LORD who practices steadfast love, justice, and righteousness in the earth. For in these things I delight, declares the LORD.'"</p>
          <p>The Jeremiah context is extraordinarily precise in its application to Paul's argument. Jeremiah explicitly identifies three objects of illegitimate boasting: wisdom (<hi type="italic">sophia</hi>), might (<hi type="italic">ischys</hi>), and riches (<hi type="italic">ploutos</hi>). These correspond directly to the three things Paul has said the Corinthians lack: "not many wise, not many powerful, not many of noble birth" (v. 26). The Jeremiah passage is not a detached proof-text selected at random; it is the scriptural foundation of the entire argument of 1:18-31. Paul has been arguing, in effect, an extended commentary on Jeremiah 9:23-24.</p>
          <p>Jeremiah redirects boasting from human achievements to knowledge of God's character: "that he understands and knows me, that I am the LORD who practices steadfast love (<hi type="italic">chesed</hi>), justice (<hi type="italic">mishpat</hi>), and righteousness (<hi type="italic">tsedaqah</hi>) in the earth." The God Jeremiah describes is known by his covenant loyalty, his justice, and his righteousness — and he delights in these things. Paul's abbreviated quotation — "Let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord" — captures the redirection: all boasting belongs to the Lord, not to human agents.</p>
          <p>This citation closes the chapter's argument with the same logic that opened it. The community belongs to God (v. 2: "the church of God"). The community was called by God (vv. 1, 2, 9, 24, 26). The community was sanctified by God (v. 2, 30). The community was enriched by God (v. 5). Everything is gift. Everything originates in divine initiative. Therefore, all boasting in human leaders, human wisdom, or human status is excluded. The only legitimate object of boasting is the Lord himself — the God who works through the cross, who chooses the weak, who gives what cannot be earned.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.1">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 1 builds a single sustained argument through three movements. The opening (vv. 1-9) establishes the community's identity as God's creation: called, sanctified, enriched, sustained, and summoned into fellowship with God's Son. Every predicate is passive — things done to them by God, not achieved by them. The exposure of factionalism (vv. 10-17) then reveals that the community has contradicted its own identity by dividing along the lines of human leaders and human eloquence. The theology of the cross (vv. 18-31) provides the reason why factionalism is not merely a social problem but a theological error: the gospel operates through apparent foolishness and weakness, God chose the lowly to nullify the powerful, and therefore all human boasting — including boasting in one's preferred teacher — is excluded.</p>
          <p>The three movements are inseparable. Without the opening's emphasis on divine initiative, the faction problem would seem like a mere social inconvenience. Without the faction problem, the theology of the cross would seem like abstract speculation. Without the theology of the cross, the appeal for unity would lack any grounding deeper than pragmatism. Paul weaves the three together: you belong to God (opening), therefore stop claiming to belong to human leaders (faction problem), because the entire logic of the gospel — the cross, the choice of the weak, the exclusion of boasting — makes such claims absurd (theology of the cross).</p>
          <p>The word "called" (<hi type="italic">kletos/klesis/kaleo</hi>) is the thread that stitches the chapter together. Paul is "called" as an apostle (v. 1). The Corinthians are "called" saints (v. 2). They were "called" into fellowship with God's Son (v. 9). Christ is power and wisdom "to those who are called" (v. 24). Their "calling" is visible in the community's composition (v. 26). The repetition drives a single point: everything in this community originates in God's summons. No human initiative, no human wisdom, no human leader is the source of their identity. God called. They responded. That is the entire basis of their existence. Factionalism around human figures is, therefore, not just unfortunate; it is a repudiation of the calling itself.</p>
          <p>The LXX citations anchor the argument in Israel's own scriptures. Isaiah 29:14 (v. 19) establishes that God has always confounded human wisdom when it became self-referential and detached from genuine devotion. Isaiah 33:18 and 19:12 (echoed in v. 20) provide the prophetic taunt form that declares human expertise irrelevant before God's action. Jeremiah 9:23-24 (v. 31) provides the climactic scriptural command: boast only in the Lord. Paul does not innovate; he applies Israel's own prophetic tradition to the community's situation. The Corinthians' problem — competitive attachment to human wisdom and eloquence — is the same problem the prophets diagnosed in Israel: a confusion of human performance with genuine knowledge of God.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.2">
        <title>1 Corinthians 2 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.2">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 1):</hi> Paul opened this letter by identifying himself as "called as an apostle of Christ Jesus by the will of God" (1:1) and addressing "the assembly of God that is in Corinth, those sanctified in Christ Jesus, called as saints" (1:2). After thanksgiving (1:4-9), he launched immediately into the presenting crisis: factionalism. "It has been reported to me by Chloe's people that there are quarrels among you" (1:11). The quarrels take a specific form: "each one of you says, 'I am of Paul,' or 'I am of Apollos,' or 'I am of Cephas,' or 'I am of Christ'" (1:12). The community has fractured along the lines of allegiance to different teachers. Paul's response is sharp: "Has Christ been divided? Was Paul crucified for you? Were you baptized in the name of Paul?" (1:13). The rhetorical questions expose the absurdity of the factions by pointing to the one event that constitutes their community — the crucifixion of Christ, not the ministry of any teacher.</p>
          <p>Paul then made a pivotal turn. He was sent "not to baptize but to proclaim the gospel, and not with wisdom of speech, lest the cross of Christ be emptied" (1:17). The phrase <hi type="italic">sophia logou</hi> ("wisdom of speech" or "eloquent wisdom") introduces the concept that dominates the rest of the chapter and all of chapter 2: <hi type="italic">sophia</hi> — wisdom. Paul's claim is that a certain kind of wisdom empties the cross. The word <hi type="italic">kenoo</hi> ("to empty, to render void, to make of no effect") is strong: the cross itself loses its power when the message is delivered through impressive human rhetoric.</p>
          <p>From 1:18 onward, Paul developed the antithesis between God's wisdom and the world's. "The word of the cross is foolishness to those who are perishing, but to us who are being saved it is the power of God" (1:18). He quoted Isaiah 29:14 (LXX): "I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the discernment of the discerning I will set aside." He asked three rhetorical questions: "Where is the wise one? Where is the scribe? Where is the debater of this age?" (1:20) — three categories of recognized wisdom-bearers (Greek philosopher, Jewish Torah scholar, public disputant), all challenged. God made the world's wisdom foolish (1:20). Since the world through its wisdom did not know God, it pleased God through the foolishness of the proclaimed message to save those who believe (1:21). Jews demand signs, Greeks seek wisdom, "but we proclaim Christ crucified — to Jews a stumbling block and to Gentiles foolishness, but to those who are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God" (1:23-24).</p>
          <p>The chapter closed with the Corinthians' own example. Paul reminded them of their origins: "not many of you were wise according to the flesh, not many were powerful, not many were of noble birth" (1:26). God chose the foolish things to shame the wise, the weak things to shame the strong, the low and despised things and the things that are not, to bring to nothing the things that are — "so that no flesh may boast before God" (1:28-29). Christ himself became for them "wisdom from God — righteousness and sanctification and redemption" (1:30). The chapter concluded with a quotation from Jeremiah 9:24 (LXX 9:23): "Let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord" (1:31). The entire argument was designed to undercut the basis of the Corinthians' factionalism: they were evaluating teachers by worldly wisdom and rhetorical skill, the very criteria God has overturned through the cross.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 3):</hi> Paul opens chapter 3 by revealing the point of everything he has argued in chapter 2. He addresses the Corinthians directly: "And I, brothers, could not address you as spiritual people (<hi type="italic">pneumatikois</hi>) but as fleshly people (<hi type="italic">sarkinois</hi>), as infants in Christ (<hi type="italic">nepiois en Christo</hi>)" (3:1). The vocabulary is precise and pointed. The word <hi type="italic">pneumatikois</hi> ("spiritual") is the same word that dominated 2:13-15 — the <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi> person who discerns the things of the Spirit. The word <hi type="italic">sarkinois</hi> ("made of flesh, fleshly") describes someone whose conduct is governed by the flesh rather than the Spirit. The word <hi type="italic">nepiois</hi> ("infants, babes") indicates immaturity rather than wickedness. Paul is saying: despite everything I taught you about the Spirit's wisdom, you are behaving as though you had never received the Spirit at all.</p>
          <p>The evidence of their immaturity is stated: "I gave you milk to drink, not solid food, for you were not yet ready for it. And even now you are not yet ready, for you are still fleshly" (3:2-3a). The milk/solid food distinction maps onto the wisdom distinction of chapter 2: the "wisdom spoken among the mature" (2:6) is the solid food; the basic proclamation of Christ crucified (2:2) is the milk. The Corinthians received the milk but have not matured to handle solid food — not because Paul withheld it, but because their behavior demonstrates they have not grown.</p>
          <p>The proof of their fleshliness is precisely the factionalism from 1:12: "For when one says, 'I am of Paul,' and another, 'I am of Apollos,' are you not merely human?" (3:4). The word <hi type="italic">anthropoi</hi> ("human beings, merely human") is Paul's judgment on their behavior. They are acting <hi type="italic">kata anthropon</hi> ("according to a human standard") rather than <hi type="italic">kata pneuma</hi> ("according to the Spirit"). The very act of evaluating teachers by worldly criteria and attaching themselves to human leaders demonstrates that they are operating from natural, fleshly reasoning rather than spiritual discernment.</p>
          <p>Paul then corrected their understanding of his role and Apollos's. "What then is Apollos? What is Paul? Servants through whom you believed, as the Lord assigned to each" (3:5). The word <hi type="italic">diakonoi</hi> ("servants, attendants, those who serve") strips the teachers of the status the Corinthians have been attributing to them. Paul planted, Apollos watered, "but God gave the growth" (3:6). Neither the planter nor the waterer is anything; only God who gives the growth matters (3:7). The agricultural metaphor dissolves the factions: the field is God's, the workers are God's agents, and the Corinthians are "God's field, God's building" (3:9). Paul then shifted to a construction metaphor: he laid a foundation — "Jesus Christ" — and others build on it (3:10-11). The quality of each builder's work will be tested by fire on "the day" (3:13). The warning applies to the teachers: some may build with gold, silver, and precious stones; others with wood, hay, and straw. The fire will reveal the difference. A builder whose work survives will receive a reward; a builder whose work burns up will "suffer loss, though he himself will be saved, but only as through fire" (3:15). The chapter then issues a solemn warning: "If anyone destroys God's temple, God will destroy him. For God's temple is holy, and you are that temple" (3:17).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 2 connects these:</hi> Chapter 1 identified the problem (factionalism rooted in worldly wisdom-evaluation) and stated the principle (God overturns human wisdom through the cross). Chapter 3 will apply the principle directly to the Corinthians' situation, naming their behavior as fleshly and infantile. Chapter 2 is the bridge: it explains how God's wisdom actually works, how it is communicated, and why the Corinthians' criteria for evaluating teachers are fundamentally wrong.</p>
          <p>The chapter accomplishes this in two movements. In the first (2:1-5), Paul uses his own arrival in Corinth as a case study: he deliberately did not come with impressive rhetoric, so that their faith would rest on God's power, not on human wisdom. This is not merely autobiographical humility; it is an argument against the very criteria the Corinthians are using to rank their teachers. If Paul intentionally refused to deploy rhetorical skill, then evaluating teachers by rhetorical skill misses the entire point.</p>
          <p>In the second movement (2:6-16), Paul introduces a category the Corinthians may not have expected: there is a genuine wisdom, spoken among the mature. But this wisdom is not the world's wisdom — it is God's hidden wisdom, revealed by the Spirit, inaccessible to natural human reasoning. The person who operates from natural faculties alone (the <hi type="italic">psychikos anthropos</hi>) cannot discern spiritual realities. Only the person who has received the Spirit (the <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi>) can evaluate and discern them. The chapter climaxes with the declaration that "we have the mind of Christ" (2:16) — the community that has received the Spirit possesses the capacity to evaluate all things from Christ's perspective. This sets up chapter 3's devastating application: if you have the mind of Christ, why are you evaluating things by the world's criteria? Your factionalism proves that you are not thinking with the mind you have been given.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.2.1-1Cor.2.5">
          <title>Verses 1-5: Paul's Manner of Coming to Corinth</title>
          <p>"And I, when I came to you, brothers, did not come proclaiming the testimony of God to you with lofty speech or wisdom" (2:1).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">kago elthon pros hymas</hi> ("and I, when I came to you") is personal and emphatic. The <hi type="italic">kago</hi> ("and I" or "I also") connects Paul's own practice to the principle established in chapter 1. Paul was not merely articulating a theory about the irrelevance of worldly wisdom; he enacted it in his own ministry among the Corinthians. The words <hi type="italic">kath' hyperochen logou e sophias</hi> ("with superiority of speech or wisdom") describe precisely what Paul avoided. The noun <hi type="italic">hyperoche</hi> ("superiority, preeminence, excess, surpassing quality") indicates speech that rises above, that towers over the audience through its rhetorical brilliance. The combination of <hi type="italic">logos</hi> ("speech, eloquence, rhetorical expression") and <hi type="italic">sophia</hi> ("wisdom") captures both the form (impressive delivery) and content (philosophical depth) of what the Greek world valued in its orators and teachers.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">to martyrion tou theou</hi> ("the testimony of God") identifies what Paul did proclaim. Some manuscripts read <hi type="italic">mysterion</hi> ("mystery") instead of <hi type="italic">martyrion</hi> ("testimony"), and this variant is significant because mysterionis the word Paul will use in verse 7. Either reading works within the argument: Paul came to deliver God's witness (<hi type="italic">martyrion</hi>) or God's mystery (<hi type="italic">mysterion</hi>), not to showcase his own rhetorical abilities. The genitive <hi type="italic">tou theou</hi> ("of God") is either subjective (the testimony God gives) or objective (the testimony about God). In either case, the message belongs to God, not to Paul.</p>
          <p>The Corinthian context makes Paul's statement pointed. Corinth was a city saturated with competitive rhetoric. The Greco-Roman world valued rhetorical prowess as one of the highest social goods. Traveling orators (sophists) drew large crowds, charged fees, competed for students, and built reputations through their speaking skill. The Corinthians, shaped by this culture, were naturally inclined to evaluate teachers the way they evaluated orators: by the impressiveness of their speech. Paul's statement that he deliberately refused this approach strikes at the root of their value system.</p>
          <p>"For I decided to know nothing among you except Jesus Christ and him crucified" (2:2).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">ekrina</hi> ("I decided, I judged, I resolved") indicates a deliberate decision, not an inability. Paul was not incapable of rhetorical sophistication — his letters themselves demonstrate considerable argumentative skill, familiarity with Greek literary conventions, and complex reasoning. His avoidance of "lofty speech" was a choice, made for theological reasons. The phrase <hi type="italic">ou gar ekrina ti eidenai en hymin</hi> ("for I decided not to know anything among you") uses the infinitive <hi type="italic">eidenai</hi> ("to know") with the emphatic negation: Paul resolved to act as though he knew nothing — no philosophical system, no rhetorical technique, no competing wisdom tradition — except one thing.</p>
          <p>The exception: <hi type="italic">ei me Iesoun Christon kai touton estauromenon</hi> — "except Jesus Christ and him crucified." The word order places <hi type="italic">Iesoun Christon</hi> ("Jesus Christ") first and then intensifies: <hi type="italic">kai touton estauromenon</hi> — "and this one crucified." The perfect passive participle <hi type="italic">estauromenon</hi> ("having been crucified") describes a past event with continuing results. Jesus was crucified, and he remains the crucified one. The cross is not a stage Christ passed through on the way to something more palatable; the crucifixion is the permanent identity of the one Paul proclaims. This is the <hi type="italic">moria</hi> ("foolishness") of 1:23 — Christ crucified, a scandal to Jews and absurdity to Greeks. Paul's deliberate limitation of his message to the crucified Messiah is itself an enactment of the principle of 1:17-25: God's power works through what the world considers foolish and weak.</p>
          <p>"And I was with you in weakness and in fear and in much trembling" (2:3).</p>
          <p>Paul describes his own condition among the Corinthians with three terms. The word <hi type="italic">astheneia</hi> ("weakness, frailty, lack of strength") is the opposite of the <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> ("power") the Corinthians valued. The word <hi type="italic">phobos</hi> ("fear") and <hi type="italic">tromos</hi> ("trembling") together form a pair that appears in the LXX for the human response to overwhelming circumstances: in Exodus 15:16, "terror and dread fall upon them" (<hi type="italic">phobos kai tromos</hi>); in Psalm 55:5 (LXX 54:6), "fear and trembling come upon me"; in Isaiah 19:16, "Judah shall become a terror to Egypt." The combination <hi type="italic">phobos kai tromos</hi> in Paul's own usage appears in Philippians 2:12 ("work out your salvation with fear and trembling") and 2 Corinthians 7:15 (Titus was received "with fear and trembling"). It describes the posture of a person who is acutely aware of the gravity of the task and their own inadequacy for it.</p>
          <p>Paul's arrival in Corinth, as recorded in Acts 18:1-4, confirms this picture. He arrived alone, after the relative failure in Athens, and supported himself by manual labor as a tentmaker. He was rejoined by Silas and Timothy only later (Acts 18:5), and it was only after a divine vision promising "I have many people in this city" (Acts 18:10) that he stayed for an extended period. The weakness, fear, and trembling were not rhetorical exaggeration; they described the actual condition of a man who came to a cosmopolitan commercial city without companions, without financial backing, and without the social status that Corinthian culture respected.</p>
          <p>"And my speech and my message were not in plausible words of wisdom, but in demonstration of the Spirit and of power" (2:4).</p>
          <p>Paul distinguishes two modes of persuasion. The first — <hi type="italic">en peithois sophias logois</hi> ("in persuasive words of wisdom") — describes the orator's stock in trade. The adjective <hi type="italic">peithos</hi> ("persuasive, plausible, enticing") comes from <hi type="italic">peitho</hi> ("to persuade") and describes speech that wins assent through rhetorical technique. This is how the sophists operated: they constructed arguments designed to be irresistible through their verbal artistry, regardless of whether the content was true. Paul says his <hi type="italic">logos</hi> ("speech") and his <hi type="italic">kerygma</hi> ("proclamation, message") were not in this mode.</p>
          <p>The second mode: <hi type="italic">en apodeixei pneumatos kai dynameoos</hi> — "in demonstration of the Spirit and of power." The word <hi type="italic">apodeixis</hi> ("demonstration, proof, showing forth") is itself a rhetorical term — it refers to proof or evidence that establishes a conclusion. But the proof Paul offers is not verbal brilliance; it is <hi type="italic">pneumatos</hi> ("of the Spirit") and <hi type="italic">dynameoos</hi> ("of power"). The genitive construction could be objective (proof consisting of the Spirit and power — the Spirit's activity and God's power were the evidence) or subjective (proof that the Spirit and power produce). In either case, the demonstration was not Paul's doing; it was the Spirit's activity manifesting God's power. Paul does not specify what form this demonstration took — it may refer to the Corinthians' own experience of the Spirit (which chapters 12-14 will describe in detail: tongues, prophecy, healings, and other manifestations), to transformed lives, to the very existence of the community as evidence that God was at work despite the absence of impressive rhetoric.</p>
          <p>"So that your faith might not rest on the wisdom of men but on the power of God" (2:5).</p>
          <p>The purpose clause (<hi type="italic">hina</hi>) reveals the <hi type="italic">telos</hi> of Paul's deliberate strategy. The word <hi type="italic">pistis</hi> ("faith, trust, reliance") is what Paul is concerned to protect. Faith has a ground — something it stands on, something it rests in. Paul's concern is that the Corinthians' faith should rest (<hi type="italic">e</hi>, "might be") on the right foundation. If Paul had come with impressive wisdom, their faith might have been grounded in <hi type="italic">sophia anthropon</hi> ("wisdom of human beings") — they would have believed because they were impressed by Paul's speaking, and their faith would collapse whenever a more impressive speaker came along. This is, in fact, exactly what has happened: Apollos arrived, and he was "eloquent" (<hi type="italic">logios</hi>) and "mighty in the Scriptures" (<hi type="italic">dynatos en tais graphais</hi>, Acts 18:24). Some Corinthians now prefer Apollos to Paul, precisely because Apollos is the more impressive speaker. Their faith has been resting, at least partially, on human wisdom rather than on divine power.</p>
          <p>Paul's goal was that their faith rest on <hi type="italic">dynamei theou</hi> ("the power of God"). Power, not wisdom, is the foundation. This echoes 1:18: the word of the cross "is the power of God" (<hi type="italic">dynamis theou estin</hi>). The power of God and the wisdom of God converge in the crucified Christ (1:24), but Paul insists on using the word <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> because the Corinthians associate <hi type="italic">sophia</hi> with rhetorical skill. The power of God is what changes people, what forms communities, what overcomes sin and death. Rhetorical skill does none of these things.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.2.6-1Cor.2.9">
          <title>Verses 6-9: God's Wisdom in a Mystery</title>
          <p>"Yet among the mature we do speak wisdom, though it is not a wisdom of this age or of the rulers of this age, who are being brought to nothing" (2:6).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">de</hi> ("yet, however, but") introduces an important qualification. Paul has not said there is no wisdom at all; he has said he did not come with the world's wisdom. Now he clarifies: <hi type="italic">sophian de laloumen en tois teleiois</hi> — "we do speak wisdom among the mature." The verb <hi type="italic">laleo</hi> ("to speak, to talk, to utter") is straightforward; the claim is direct. Paul does speak wisdom. The location of this wisdom-speaking is <hi type="italic">en tois teleiois</hi> — "among the mature."</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">teleiois</hi> ("mature, complete, fully developed, perfect") comes from <hi type="italic">telos</hi> ("end, goal, completion"). In the LXX, <hi type="italic">teleios</hi> describes what is complete or whole — an animal without blemish fit for sacrifice (Exodus 12:5, LXX: <hi type="italic">probaton teleion</hi>), a heart wholly devoted to God (1 Kings 8:61, LXX: <hi type="italic">kardia teleia</hi>). In Paul's own usage, the word describes the person who has progressed beyond initial stages of growth. In Philippians 3:15, Paul writes: "Let those of us who are mature (<hi type="italic">teleioi</hi>) think this way" — where the "mature" are those who press forward toward the goal rather than resting on past attainments. In 1 Corinthians 14:20, Paul will urge: "in your thinking be mature (<hi type="italic">teleioi</hi>)" — do not be children (<hi type="italic">paidia</hi>) in understanding. The <hi type="italic">teleioi</hi> are not an elite spiritual class within the community; they are believers who have grown past infancy into the capacity for deeper understanding. The contrast with <hi type="italic">nepioi</hi> ("infants") that Paul will use in 3:1 is essential: the Corinthians should be <hi type="italic">teleioi</hi> but are behaving as <hi type="italic">nepioi</hi>. Paul speaks wisdom among those who are ready for it, but the Corinthians are not ready.</p>
          <p>The wisdom Paul speaks is qualified by two negations. First: <hi type="italic">ou tou aionos toutou</hi> — "not of this age." The phrase <hi type="italic">ho aion houtos</hi> ("this age") in Jewish apocalyptic thought designates the present era of human history — the age under the power of sin, death, and rebellion against God — as opposed to <hi type="italic">ho aion ho mellon</hi> ("the age to come"), the age of God's full reign. Paul used the same phrase in 1:20: "Where is the debater of this age?" The wisdom of "this age" is the wisdom that operates within the horizons of the present world order, ignorant of God's purposes, unable to perceive what God is doing.</p>
          <p>Second: <hi type="italic">oude ton archonton tou aionos toutou ton katargoumenon</hi> — "nor of the rulers of this age, who are being brought to nothing." The word <hi type="italic">archontes</hi> ("rulers, authorities, those who exercise power") requires careful attention. In the LXX, <hi type="italic">archontes</hi> translates the Hebrew <hi type="italic">sarim</hi> and describes human rulers: the princes of Egypt (Genesis 12:15), the rulers of Israel (Exodus 16:22), the officials of the nations (Psalm 2:2). In Acts, the word describes human authorities who acted against Jesus: in Acts 3:17, Peter says to the people of Jerusalem, "I know that you acted in ignorance, as did also your rulers (<hi type="italic">archontes</hi>)"; in Acts 4:26, the apostles quote Psalm 2: "the rulers (<hi type="italic">archontes</hi>) gathered together against the Lord and against his Anointed"; and in Acts 13:27, Paul tells the synagogue in Pisidian Antioch that "the inhabitants of Jerusalem and their rulers (<hi type="italic">archontes</hi>), because they did not recognize him nor understand the utterances of the prophets, fulfilled them by condemning him." In all these passages, the <hi type="italic">archontes</hi> who acted against Jesus are the human political and religious authorities — the Sanhedrin, Pilate, Herod.</p>
          <p>Paul will say in verse 8 that these <hi type="italic">archontes</hi> are the ones who "crucified the Lord of glory." Since crucifixion was carried out by identifiable human authorities acting within the political structures of the Roman province of Judea, the most immediate referent for "the rulers of this age" is the human powers who condemned and executed Jesus — the chief priests, the Sanhedrin, and the Roman governor. The qualifier "of this age" identifies them with the present world order and its wisdom, and the participial phrase <hi type="italic">ton katargoumenon</hi> ("who are being brought to nothing") uses the present passive participle of <hi type="italic">katargeo</hi> ("to render inoperative, to abolish, to bring to nothing"). The same verb appears in 1:28, where God chose the things that are not "to bring to nothing the things that are" (<hi type="italic">katargese</hi>). The rulers of this age, for all their present power, are in the process of being abolished. Their authority is temporary; their wisdom is passing away. Paul uses the same verb in 15:24-26 for the final defeat of "every rule (<hi type="italic">arche</hi>) and every authority (<hi type="italic">exousia</hi>) and power (<hi type="italic">dynamis</hi>)" when Christ hands over the kingdom to the Father. The present tense of the participle indicates that the abolishing is already underway — it began with the cross and will be completed at the consummation.</p>
          <p>"But we speak God's wisdom in a mystery, the hidden wisdom that God decreed before the ages for our glory" (2:7).</p>
          <p>The conjunction <hi type="italic">alla</hi> ("but, rather") introduces the positive counterpart. What Paul does speak is <hi type="italic">theou sophian en mysterio</hi> — "God's wisdom in a mystery." The word <hi type="italic">mysterion</hi> ("mystery, secret, hidden thing") does not mean "mysterious" in the sense of puzzling or incomprehensible. In Paul's usage, a <hi type="italic">mysterion</hi> is something that was hidden in God's plan but has now been revealed. In Romans 16:25-26, Paul speaks of "the mystery that was kept secret for long ages but has now been disclosed and through the prophetic writings has been made known to all nations." In 1 Corinthians 4:1, Paul and the other apostles are "stewards of the mysteries of God" — their role is to dispense what was hidden but is now entrusted to them. The mystery is not esoteric knowledge for an elite; it is God's plan that was concealed within the age's wisdom structures and has now been uncovered through the cross and the Spirit.</p>
          <p>The mystery is further described as <hi type="italic">ten apokekrymmenen</hi> ("the hidden one") — a perfect passive participle of <hi type="italic">apokrypto</hi> ("to hide, to conceal, to keep secret"). The hiddenness was not accidental; God concealed this wisdom. The verb <hi type="italic">proorisen</hi> ("decreed beforehand, predestined") is the same verb Paul uses in Romans 8:29-30 for God's predetermining purpose. The phrase <hi type="italic">pro ton aionon</hi> ("before the ages") places God's decree before the succession of world-ages, before history itself. This wisdom is not a response to circumstances; it is the eternal plan.</p>
          <p>The goal of the decree is <hi type="italic">eis doxan hemon</hi> — "for our glory." The word <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> ("glory, splendor, honor, radiance") is the consummation that Paul described in Romans 8:17-18 — the glory to be revealed, the glory that creation itself awaits. God's hidden wisdom, decreed before the ages, has as its goal the glorification of those who receive it. The pronoun <hi type="italic">hemon</hi> ("our") includes Paul and the Corinthians — this glory belongs to the community that has received the mystery.</p>
          <p>"None of the rulers of this age understood this, for if they had, they would not have crucified the Lord of glory" (2:8).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">egnoken</hi> (perfect active of <hi type="italic">ginosko</hi>, "to know, to understand, to recognize") with the negation <hi type="italic">oudeis</hi> ("none, not one") is absolute: not a single one of the rulers of this age grasped God's hidden wisdom. Their ignorance was comprehensive. The conditional clause <hi type="italic">ei gar egnosan</hi> ("for if they had known") states a contrary-to-fact condition: they did not know, and the evidence of their ignorance is stated in the result clause — <hi type="italic">ouk an ton kyrion tes doxes estaurosan</hi> ("they would not have crucified the Lord of glory").</p>
          <p>The title <hi type="italic">ton kyrion tes doxes</hi> ("the Lord of glory") is extraordinary. In the LXX, the phrase appears in the Psalms. Psalm 24:7-10 (LXX 23:7-10) asks: "Who is this King of glory (<hi type="italic">basileus tes doxes</hi>)?" and answers: "The LORD of hosts, he is the King of glory." The divine title "King of glory" is transferred to the crucified Jesus, but Paul uses <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> ("Lord") rather than <hi type="italic">basileus</hi> ("King"). The identification is staggering: the one the rulers crucified was the Lord whose glory fills the temple, the Lord whom the heavens proclaim. Their ignorance was not merely a failure to recognize a teacher or prophet; they failed to recognize the Lord of glory himself. The crucifixion, from the perspective of this age's wisdom, was a political execution of a troublesome provincial subject; from the perspective of God's hidden wisdom, it was the murder of the Lord of glory — an act so catastrophically misguided that it could only have occurred through ignorance of who the victim was.</p>
          <p>The irony Paul constructs is devastating. The very act by which the rulers demonstrated their power — executing a condemned man by crucifixion, the most shameful death Rome could inflict — was simultaneously the act by which God accomplished his hidden plan. The rulers' supreme exercise of worldly power and worldly wisdom (they judged Jesus to be a threat and eliminated him) was the instrument of their own defeat. The cross, which this age's wisdom interprets as evidence of Jesus' weakness and failure, is in God's wisdom the mechanism of redemption and the revelation of divine glory. The rulers' ignorance did not frustrate God's plan; it unwittingly served it.</p>
          <p>"But, as it is written, 'What no eye has seen, nor ear heard, nor the heart of man imagined, what God has prepared for those who love him'" (2:9).</p>
          <p>The introductory formula <hi type="italic">kathos gegraptai</hi> ("as it is written") signals a scriptural citation, but the quotation that follows does not correspond exactly to any single passage in the LXX. The closest parallel is Isaiah 64:4 (LXX 64:3): "From of old we have not heard, nor have our eyes seen a god besides you, and your works which you will do for those who wait for you" (<hi type="italic">apo tou aionos ouk ekousamen oude hoi ophthalmoi hemon eidon theon plen sou kai ta erga sou ha poieseis tois hypomenousin eleon</hi>). Paul's version differs significantly: he restructures the quotation around three organs of perception — eye (<hi type="italic">ophthalmos</hi>), ear (<hi type="italic">ous</hi>), and heart (<hi type="italic">kardia</hi>) — and replaces "a god besides you and your works" with "what God has prepared."</p>
          <p>The triple negation — eye has not seen, ear has not heard, heart has not conceived — represents the totality of human cognitive capacity. Sight, hearing, and the heart's ability to conceive or imagine exhaust the ways a human being can acquire knowledge. What God has prepared exceeds all three. The word <hi type="italic">hetoimasen</hi> ("has prepared, has made ready") is the aorist of <hi type="italic">hetoimazo</hi>, used in the LXX for God's preparation of good things: Psalm 31:19 (LXX 30:20), "How abundant is your goodness, which you have stored up for those who fear you, which you have prepared for those who take refuge in you." The beneficiaries are <hi type="italic">tois agaposin auton</hi> ("those who love him") — the same phrase that appeared in Romans 8:28 to describe those for whom "all things work together for good."</p>
          <p>Paul's adaptation of the Isaiah text serves his argument precisely. The wisdom of this age relies on what can be seen, heard, and conceived by human intelligence. God's wisdom consists of what cannot be accessed by any of these means. The rulers of this age did not understand God's plan because it was beyond the reach of their faculties — not because they lacked intelligence, but because the plan was of a kind that no human faculty could reach. The quotation creates a problem, however: if this wisdom is beyond all human perception, how can anyone know it? Paul answers this in the next verse.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.2.10-1Cor.2.13">
          <title>Verses 10-13: The Spirit as the Revealer of God's Depths</title>
          <p>"These things God has revealed to us through the Spirit. For the Spirit searches everything, even the depths of God" (2:10).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">hemin</hi> ("to us") is emphatic by position and pivotal to the argument. The things that no eye saw, no ear heard, and no heart conceived — these inaccessible realities — God <hi type="italic">apekalypsen</hi> ("revealed, uncovered, disclosed") to us. The verb <hi type="italic">apokalypto</hi> ("to reveal, to uncover") is the opposite of <hi type="italic">apokrypto</hi> ("to hide, to conceal"), the verb used in verse 7 for the hidden wisdom. What was hidden has been uncovered. The agent of revelation is <hi type="italic">dia tou pneumatos</hi> — "through the Spirit." The Spirit is the means by which the gap between divine hiddenness and human perception is bridged. What human faculties cannot reach, the Spirit delivers.</p>
          <p>The reason the Spirit can do this is stated: <hi type="italic">to gar pneuma panta erauna, kai ta bathe tou theou</hi> — "for the Spirit searches all things, even the depths of God." The verb <hi type="italic">erauna</hi> (or <hi type="italic">ereunao</hi>, "to search, to examine, to investigate thoroughly") is the same verb used in the LXX for God's searching of human hearts (Psalm 139:1, LXX 138:1: <hi type="italic">ereunas me</hi>; Jeremiah 17:10: <hi type="italic">etazon kardias kai dokimazon nephrous</hi>). In Romans 8:27, it was God who searched hearts and knew the mind of the Spirit; here the Spirit searches the depths of God. The relationship is reciprocal: God knows the Spirit's mind (Romans 8:27), and the Spirit knows God's depths (1 Corinthians 2:10). The word <hi type="italic">bathe</hi> ("depths, deep things") indicates the most interior, most hidden aspects of God's being and purpose. The Spirit has access to what is deepest and most concealed in God.</p>
          <p>The implications for the Corinthians' factionalism are significant, though Paul will not draw them explicitly until chapter 3. If the only access to God's wisdom is through the Spirit, then no human teacher — however eloquent — can serve as the source of that wisdom. Paul, Apollos, and Cephas are all channels at best; the Spirit is the source. Evaluating teachers by their rhetorical skill is therefore evaluating the wrong thing.</p>
          <p>"For who knows a person's thoughts except the spirit of that person, which is in him? So also no one comprehends the thoughts of God except the Spirit of God" (2:11).</p>
          <p>Paul offers an analogy from common experience. The word <hi type="italic">ta tou anthropou</hi> ("the things of the person, the person's thoughts and inner life") describes the interior world of a human being — thoughts, intentions, feelings, deliberations. The question <hi type="italic">tis gar oiden anthropon ta tou anthropou</hi> ("for who among human beings knows the things of a human being?") is rhetorical. The answer is <hi type="italic">ei me to pneuma tou anthropou to en auto</hi> — "except the spirit of the person that is in him." Only the spirit (<hi type="italic">pneuma</hi>) that indwells a person has access to that person's inner world. Another person may observe behavior and hear words, but the thoughts themselves remain private, accessible only to the indwelling spirit.</p>
          <p>The analogy is then applied: <hi type="italic">houtos kai ta tou theou oudeis egnoken ei me to pneuma tou theou</hi> — "so also, no one has known the things of God except the Spirit of God." The parallel is precise. As the human spirit alone has access to the person's interior, so the divine Spirit alone has access to God's interior. No creature, no angel, no ruler of this age, no human philosopher can know <hi type="italic">ta tou theou</hi> ("the things of God") — God's purposes, God's plan, God's hidden wisdom — unless the Spirit of God grants that knowledge.</p>
          <p>The analogy also establishes an important point about the Spirit's relationship to God. The human spirit is not something external to the person; it is the person's own interior reality. By analogy, the Spirit of God is not an external agent reporting about God from the outside; the Spirit is God's own interior reality — the aspect of God that knows God from within. This is why the Spirit can search "the depths of God" (v. 10): the Spirit is within those depths.</p>
          <p>"Now we have received not the spirit of the world, but the Spirit who is from God, that we might understand the things freely given us by God" (2:12).</p>
          <p>The pronoun <hi type="italic">hemeis</hi> ("we") is emphatic: we — the apostles and the believing community — have received (<hi type="italic">elabomen</hi>, aorist of <hi type="italic">lambano</hi>) not <hi type="italic">to pneuma tou kosmou</hi> ("the spirit of the world") but <hi type="italic">to pneuma to ek tou theou</hi> ("the Spirit that is from God"). The phrase "the spirit of the world" is not further defined, but its meaning is clear from context: it is the governing principle of the <hi type="italic">kosmos</hi> in its present configuration — the mindset that evaluates by human wisdom, that judges by appearances, that operates within the horizons of "this age." The Corinthians, in their factionalism, have been operating by the spirit of the world even though they have received the Spirit from God.</p>
          <p>The purpose of receiving the Spirit is stated: <hi type="italic">hina eidomen ta hypo tou theou charisthenta hemin</hi> — "so that we might know the things graciously given to us by God." The verb <hi type="italic">eidomen</hi> (aorist subjunctive of <hi type="italic">oida</hi>, "to know, to perceive, to understand") describes knowing in the sense of comprehension and perception. The verb <hi type="italic">charisthenta</hi> (aorist passive participle of <hi type="italic">charizomai</hi>, "to give freely, to grant as a gift, to bestow graciously") is built on <hi type="italic">charis</hi> ("grace"). The things God has given are grace-gifts — freely bestowed, not earned or purchased. The Spirit's role is to make the recipients aware of what they have already been given. The problem is not that God has withheld his gifts but that the recipients may not perceive what they have received. The Spirit opens their eyes to the realities that are already theirs in Christ — the righteousness, sanctification, and redemption of 1:30, the glory of 2:7, the inheritance that belongs to God's people.</p>
          <p>"And we speak of these things in words not taught by human wisdom but taught by the Spirit, interpreting spiritual things to spiritual people" (2:13).</p>
          <p>The clause <hi type="italic">ha kai laloumen ouk en didaktois anthropines sophias logois</hi> ("which things we also speak, not in words taught by human wisdom") returns to the contrast between human wisdom and divine revelation, now applied specifically to the mode of communication. The adjective <hi type="italic">didaktois</hi> ("taught, instructed, learned") is passive: the words Paul uses are not words he learned from a human wisdom tradition. The phrase <hi type="italic">all' en didaktois pneumatos</hi> ("but in words taught by the Spirit") identifies the Spirit as the teacher. The Spirit not only reveals the content (v. 10) but provides the vocabulary — the words and categories adequate to express what has been revealed.</p>
          <p>The final phrase <hi type="italic">pneumatikois pneumatika synkrinontes</hi> is difficult and has been read multiple ways. The verb <hi type="italic">synkrino</hi> can mean "to compare, to combine, to interpret, to explain." The word <hi type="italic">pneumatikois</hi> can be either neuter ("spiritual things") or masculine ("spiritual people"), and <hi type="italic">pneumatika</hi> is neuter plural ("spiritual things"). The possible readings include: (a) "combining spiritual things with spiritual words" — expressing spiritual realities in Spirit-taught language; (b) "interpreting spiritual things to spiritual people" — communicating spiritual realities to those who are spiritually equipped to receive them; (c) "comparing spiritual things with spiritual things" — interpreting one spiritual reality by reference to another. All three readings are consistent with the argument. Paul is saying that the entire communication chain — the content, the words, and the audience — must be spiritual. The message comes from the Spirit, is expressed in Spirit-taught language, and is received by Spirit-equipped people.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.2.14-1Cor.2.16">
          <title>Verses 14-16: The Natural Person and the Spiritual Person</title>
          <p>"The natural person does not accept the things of the Spirit of God, for they are folly to him, and he is not able to understand them because they are spiritually discerned" (2:14).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> ("natural, soulish, pertaining to the soul/psyche") is the critical term. It derives from <hi type="italic">psyche</hi> ("soul, life, natural life"), which designates the animating principle of natural human existence. A <hi type="italic">psychikos anthropos</hi> is a person operating from the soul — from natural human faculties of reason, perception, and judgment — without the illumination of God's Spirit. The term is not the same as <hi type="italic">sarkikos</hi> or <hi type="italic">sarkinos</hi> ("fleshly"), which Paul will use in 3:1-3 for the Corinthians. The <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> person is not necessarily immoral; they may be highly intelligent, well-educated, culturally refined, and ethically admirable. But they are operating without the Spirit and therefore lack the capacity to perceive what the Spirit reveals.</p>
          <p>In the LXX, <hi type="italic">psyche</hi> translates the Hebrew <hi type="italic">nephesh</hi> — the life-breath, the animate self. The <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> person is the <hi type="italic">nephesh</hi>-person, the person whose resources for knowledge are limited to what the natural self can achieve. This is the person described in verse 9 for whom the things God has prepared are beyond what eye has seen, ear has heard, or heart has imagined. The <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> person's faculties are intact but insufficient for the task of perceiving God's hidden wisdom.</p>
          <p>The <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> person <hi type="italic">ou dechetai ta tou pneumatos tou theou</hi> ("does not accept the things of the Spirit of God"). The verb <hi type="italic">dechomai</hi> ("to receive, to welcome, to accept, to take in") indicates more than intellectual comprehension; it involves a willing reception, an embrace of what is offered. The <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> person does not merely fail to understand; he or she does not welcome, does not receive, does not take in what the Spirit offers. The reason is stated: <hi type="italic">moria gar auto estin</hi> — "for they are foolishness (<hi type="italic">moria</hi>) to him." The word <hi type="italic">moria</hi> is the same word from 1:18 (the word of the cross is <hi type="italic">moria</hi> to those who are perishing) and 1:23 (the crucified Christ is <hi type="italic">moria</hi> to Gentiles). From the perspective of natural human wisdom, the Spirit's things look foolish — they do not conform to the categories and standards by which the natural mind evaluates truth.</p>
          <p>The further explanation: <hi type="italic">kai ou dynatai gnonai</hi> ("and he is not able to know/understand"). The verb <hi type="italic">dynatai</hi> ("is able") with the negation <hi type="italic">ou</hi> indicates incapacity, not mere unwillingness. The <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> person cannot understand, because the things of the Spirit <hi type="italic">hoti pneumatikos anakrinetai</hi> ("are discerned spiritually" or "because they are examined by spiritual means"). The verb <hi type="italic">anakrino</hi> ("to examine, to investigate, to judge, to discern, to evaluate") is a legal and forensic term — it describes the process of examination that leads to a judgment. The adverb <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi> ("spiritually, by means of the Spirit") specifies the instrument required. Spiritual realities require spiritual examination. They cannot be evaluated by the tools available to the natural person. This is not anti-intellectualism; it is a claim about epistemological domains. As one cannot taste colors or hear textures — the faculty does not match the object — so one cannot discern spiritual realities with natural faculties alone. A different capacity is required, and that capacity comes only from the Spirit.</p>
          <p>"The spiritual person judges all things, but is himself judged by no one" (2:15).</p>
          <p>The <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi> ("spiritual person") is the counterpart of the <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi>. The <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi> is the person in whom the Spirit of God dwells (cf. Romans 8:9-11), who has received "the Spirit who is from God" (v. 12), and who therefore possesses the faculty necessary for spiritual discernment. This person <hi type="italic">anakrineipanta</hi> — "examines/judges all things." The verb <hi type="italic">anakrino</hi> is the same as in verse 14, but now its subject is the spiritual person rather than the spiritual method. The spiritual person has the capacity to evaluate everything — both the spiritual realities that the <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> cannot access and the natural realities that are available to all. The <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi> sees more, not less, than the <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi>. The spiritual person can evaluate the natural world and the spiritual world; the natural person can evaluate only the natural world.</p>
          <p>The second clause: <hi type="italic">autos de hyp' oudenos anakrinetai</hi> — "but he himself is examined/judged by no one." The spiritual person is not subject to evaluation by the <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi>. This does not mean the spiritual person is above criticism or accountability (Paul will spend considerable portions of this letter correcting the Corinthians, who are supposed to be <hi type="italic">pneumatikoi</hi>). It means that the <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> person lacks the capacity to evaluate the spiritual person's judgments. The natural person, lacking the Spirit, cannot assess what the spiritual person perceives. The judgment of the spiritual person on spiritual matters stands because the tools required to overturn that judgment are not available to the one who lacks the Spirit.</p>
          <p>This verse carries an implicit warning to the Corinthians, though Paul will not make it explicit until chapter 3. The Corinthians have been evaluating Paul and Apollos and Cephas by natural criteria — rhetorical skill, social prestige, wisdom-tradition credentials. They have been acting as <hi type="italic">psychikoi</hi>, using <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> categories to judge <hi type="italic">pneumatikoi</hi>. Paul's argument here says: that evaluation is invalid. You are using the wrong instrument. You cannot assess what is happening in the community of the Spirit by the standards of the world's wisdom. The factionalism itself is evidence that the Corinthians are not exercising the spiritual discernment they should possess.</p>
          <p>"'For who has known the mind of the Lord so as to instruct him?' But we have the mind of Christ" (2:16).</p>
          <p>The quotation is from Isaiah 40:13 (LXX): <hi type="italic">tis egno noun kyriou, kai tis autou symboulos egeneto, hos symbibai auton</hi> — "Who has known the mind of the Lord? And who has been his counselor, who will instruct him?" In the original context of Isaiah 40, the passage is part of the great comfort oracle that opens Second Isaiah. The prophet declares God's incomparable greatness: no one has measured the waters in the hollow of his hand or marked off the heavens (40:12); no one directed the Spirit of the LORD or served as his counselor (40:13); the nations are like a drop from a bucket (40:15). The rhetorical question expects the answer "no one" — no human being has ever known the mind of the LORD well enough to instruct him.</p>
          <p>Paul quotes this text and then makes a startling claim: <hi type="italic">hemeis de noun Christou echomen</hi> — "but we have the mind of Christ." The word <hi type="italic">nous</hi> ("mind, understanding, intellect, way of thinking") is what Isaiah said no one could know when it belonged to <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> (the LORD). Paul identifies <hi type="italic">Christos</hi> with the <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> of Isaiah 40:13 — the Lord whose mind is incomprehensible to unaided human intelligence. And then he says: we have that mind. The pronoun <hi type="italic">hemeis</hi> ("we") is emphatic by position, standing in contrast to the implied "no one" of the Isaiah quotation. No one has known the mind of the Lord — but we have it.</p>
          <p>How? The entire preceding argument (vv. 10-15) provides the answer. The Spirit, who searches the depths of God (v. 10), who alone knows the things of God (v. 11), has been given to the believing community (v. 12) precisely so that they might know what God has freely given (v. 12). The "mind of Christ" is not a separate endowment; it is the consequence of having received the Spirit who knows God's interior life and communicates it to those who receive him. To have the Spirit is to have access to the mind of Christ. The <hi type="italic">nous Christou</hi> is the faculty of spiritual discernment — the capacity to evaluate all things (v. 15) from the perspective of God's hidden wisdom rather than from the perspective of this age.</p>
          <p>The substitution of "Christ" for "Lord" in the Isaiah quotation is theologically loaded. The <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> of Isaiah 40 is YHWH, Israel's God. Paul applies this title to Christ without comment or apology. Christ is the <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> whose mind no one can know — except by the Spirit. This identification, made in passing, reveals how thoroughly Paul has identified Jesus with the God of Israel's scriptures.</p>
          <p>The verse also completes the argument against the Corinthians' factionalism, though the application will come in chapter 3. If the community has the mind of Christ, they possess the capacity to evaluate everything — teachers, messages, practices, disputes — from Christ's perspective. The problem is not that they lack the capacity; the problem is that they are not using it. They are evaluating by the world's criteria instead of by the mind of Christ. They have been given <hi type="italic">nous Christou</hi> but are exercising <hi type="italic">nous kosmou</hi>. Paul's entire argument in chapters 1-2 has been building to this point: you have been given the most extraordinary epistemological gift imaginable — the mind of the Lord himself — and you are using it to pick teams based on who is the most impressive speaker.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.2">
          <title>Summary of the Argument's Movement</title>
          <p>Paul's argument in chapter 2 moves through four stages, each building on the previous:</p>
          <p>First (vv. 1-5): Paul's own practice in Corinth embodied the principle of chapter 1. He deliberately avoided impressive rhetoric so that the Corinthians' faith would rest on God's power rather than on human wisdom. This is not merely autobiography; it is evidence that evaluating teachers by rhetorical skill contradicts the very foundation of the Corinthian community's existence.</p>
          <p>Second (vv. 6-9): There is a genuine wisdom, but it is God's wisdom, hidden from the rulers of this age and inaccessible to natural human perception. The rulers' ignorance of this wisdom led them to crucify the Lord of glory — the supreme demonstration that worldly wisdom, however powerful, cannot perceive what God is doing. What God has prepared exceeds the capacity of eye, ear, and heart to discover.</p>
          <p>Third (vv. 10-13): The Spirit bridges the epistemological gap. What cannot be discovered by human faculties has been revealed by the Spirit, who alone knows the depths of God. The believing community has received this Spirit precisely so that they might understand what God has given them. The entire communication of God's wisdom — content, language, and reception — is the Spirit's work.</p>
          <p>Fourth (vv. 14-16): Two categories of persons are defined by their capacity for spiritual discernment. The <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> person, operating from natural faculties alone, cannot receive or understand the things of the Spirit; they appear foolish to the natural mind. The <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi> person, indwelt by the Spirit, can evaluate all things and possesses what no one else can claim: the mind of Christ.</p>
          <p>The argument is designed to dismantle the Corinthians' basis for factionalism from the inside. They have been evaluating teachers by the criteria of this age — rhetorical skill, philosophical sophistication, social prestige. Paul has argued that these criteria belong to a wisdom that is being abolished, that failed to recognize the Lord of glory, and that is fundamentally incapable of perceiving what God is doing. The only adequate evaluative instrument is the Spirit, and the community that has received the Spirit should be using the mind of Christ, not the mind of the world, to assess everything — including their teachers. Chapter 3 will draw the devastating conclusion: their factionalism proves they are not doing this.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.3">
        <title>1 Corinthians 3 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.3">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 2):</hi> Paul used his own arrival in Corinth as evidence of the principle he established in chapter 1. He came deliberately without rhetorical brilliance, "in weakness and in fear and in much trembling" (2:3), so that the Corinthians' faith would rest on "the power of God" rather than "the wisdom of men" (2:5). He then introduced a critical distinction. There is a genuine wisdom, but it belongs to God, was hidden from the rulers of this age (whose ignorance led them to crucify "the Lord of glory," 2:8), and is inaccessible to any human faculty — eye, ear, or heart (2:9). This wisdom is revealed exclusively through the Spirit, who alone "searches everything, even the depths of God" (2:10). Paul drew a sharp line between two kinds of persons: the <hi type="italic">psychikos anthropos</hi> ("natural person"), who operates from unaided human faculties and cannot receive the things of the Spirit because they appear foolish to him (2:14); and the <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi> ("spiritual person"), who possesses the Spirit and therefore can "judge all things" (2:15). The chapter climaxed with a breathtaking claim drawn from Isaiah 40:13: "Who has known the mind of the Lord so as to instruct him? But we have the mind of Christ" (2:16). The community that has received the Spirit possesses the very mind of the Lord — the capacity to evaluate all things from Christ's perspective.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 4):</hi> Paul extends the argument about how the Corinthians should regard their teachers. "This is how one should regard us, as servants of Christ and stewards of the mysteries of God" (4:1). The key qualification for stewards is faithfulness, not impressiveness: "it is required of stewards that they be found faithful" (4:2). Paul then says something remarkable about judgment: "I do not even judge myself... It is the Lord who judges me" (4:3-4). The judgment that matters belongs to the Lord, who "will bring to light the things now hidden in darkness and will disclose the purposes of the heart" (4:5) — language that echoes both the "hidden wisdom" of 2:7 and the fire that tests builders' work in 3:13. Paul then reveals that his references to himself and Apollos have been representative: "I have applied all these things to myself and Apollos for your benefit, brothers, that you may learn by us not to go beyond what is written, that none of you may be puffed up in favor of one against another" (4:6). The phrase "puffed up" (<hi type="italic">physioumenoi</hi>) introduces the word that will become Paul's signature diagnosis of the Corinthian disease — it appears seven times in this letter, more than in all his other letters combined. Paul then drives the point home with the question that distills chapters 1-3 into a single sentence: "What do you have that you did not receive? If then you received it, why do you boast as if you did not receive it?" (4:7). Everything is gift. Boasting is absurd.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 3 connects these:</hi> Chapter 2 built the theoretical framework: there are two kinds of persons (<hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> and <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi>), two kinds of wisdom (this age's and God's), and two kinds of discernment (natural and Spirit-given). Chapter 3 now turns that framework directly on the Corinthians and tells them which category they fall into. The answer is devastating: despite possessing the Spirit, they are behaving as <hi type="italic">sarkinoi</hi> ("fleshly") and <hi type="italic">nepioi</hi> ("infants"), not as <hi type="italic">pneumatikoi</hi>. The proof is their factionalism — the very thing reported in 1:11-12. Chapter 3 then reframes the relationship between the community and its teachers through two metaphors — agriculture and construction — that strip the teachers of the status the Corinthians have been attributing to them and place God as the only one who matters. The chapter climaxes with a warning about the community as God's temple and a final inversion: rather than the community belonging to its teachers, all things — including the teachers — belong to the community, and the community belongs to Christ, and Christ belongs to God. Chapter 4 will then complete the argument by defining the proper way to regard apostles (as servants and stewards) and exposing the Corinthians' inflated self-assessment. The movement from chapter 2 to chapter 4 through chapter 3 is seamless: you have been given the mind of Christ (2:16) — but you are not using it, as your factionalism proves (3:1-4) — so here is how teachers actually function (3:5-17) — and here is the proper posture toward them (3:18-23, continued in 4:1-7).</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.3.1-1Cor.3.4">
          <title>Verses 1-4: The Devastating Application — You Are Not Spiritual</title>
          <p>"And I, brothers, could not address you as spiritual people but as people of the flesh, as infants in Christ" (3:1).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">kago</hi> ("and I") connects directly back to Paul's personal testimony that opened chapter 2 ("and I, when I came to you," 2:1). The particle <hi type="italic">kai</hi> links what follows to everything Paul has just argued about the <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> and the <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi>. Having described these two categories in 2:14-16, Paul now reveals where the Corinthians stand. The opening is emphatic: <hi type="italic">ouk edyneethen lalesai hymin hos pneumatikois</hi> — "I was not able to speak to you as to spiritual people." The verb <hi type="italic">edyneethen</hi> ("I was not able") echoes <hi type="italic">ou dynatai</hi> in 2:14 (the <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> person "is not able" to understand the things of the Spirit). But the application is reversed: it was not the Corinthians who lacked the ability to receive; it was Paul who lacked the ability to deliver his message in its fullness because the audience was not ready.</p>
          <p>Three terms define the Corinthians' condition:</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Pneumatikois</hi> ("spiritual") — the same word from 2:13 and 2:15. This is what they should be. They have received the Spirit (2:12). They possess the mind of Christ (2:16). They lack nothing in any gift (1:7). By every theological measure, they should qualify as <hi type="italic">pneumatikoi</hi>. But Paul could not address them as such.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Sarkinois</hi> ("fleshly, made of flesh") — derived from <hi type="italic">sarx</hi> ("flesh"), with the suffix <hi type="italic">-inos</hi> indicating material composition. The word describes what something is made of: a <hi type="italic">sarkinos</hi> person is a person whose substance is flesh. In 2 Corinthians 3:3, Paul uses the same word for "tablets of human hearts" — "not on tablets of stone but on tablets of fleshly hearts" (<hi type="italic">en plaxin kardiais sarkinais</hi>). The word does not necessarily carry a moral judgment; it describes a condition of being constituted by flesh rather than being governed by the Spirit. Paul's use of <hi type="italic">sarkinois</hi> here describes the Corinthians as they were when he first arrived — immature, not yet developed in the Spirit, operating from their natural human constitution.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Nepiois en Christo</hi> ("infants in Christ") — the word <hi type="italic">nepios</hi> means an infant, a small child who cannot yet speak (the etymology is <hi type="italic">ne-epos</hi>, "not yet speaking"). In the LXX, it describes those who are simple, naive, immature — Psalm 19:7 (LXX 18:8): "the testimony of the LORD is sure, making wise the simple (<hi type="italic">ta nepia</hi>)"; Psalm 119:130 (LXX 118:130): "the unfolding of your words gives light; it gives understanding to the simple (<hi type="italic">nepiois</hi>)." The word does not describe wickedness but immaturity — the state of one who has not yet grown into the understanding available to them. The phrase <hi type="italic">en Christo</hi> ("in Christ") is crucial: the Corinthians are infants, but they are infants in Christ. They are genuinely within the sphere of Christ's lordship. Their problem is not that they are outside the faith but that they have not grown within it.</p>
          <p>"I fed you with milk, not solid food, for you were not yet ready for it. And even now you are not yet ready" (3:2).</p>
          <p>The milk/solid food distinction maps directly onto the wisdom categories of chapter 2. The "milk" (<hi type="italic">gala</hi>) corresponds to the basic proclamation: "Jesus Christ and him crucified" (2:2) — the foundational message Paul delivered when he first came to Corinth. The "solid food" (<hi type="italic">broma</hi>) corresponds to the "wisdom among the mature" (<hi type="italic">sophia en tois teleiois</hi>) that Paul described in 2:6 — the deeper understanding of God's hidden plan, the mystery decreed before the ages for the community's glory. The word <hi type="italic">teleioi</hi> ("mature") in 2:6 is the antonym of <hi type="italic">nepioi</hi> ("infants") here. The Corinthians should have progressed from <hi type="italic">nepioi</hi> to <hi type="italic">teleioi</hi>, from milk to solid food. They have not.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ou gar edynasthe</hi> ("for you were not yet able") uses the same verb of inability (<hi type="italic">dynamai</hi>) that appeared in 2:14 for the <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> person. But the sting is in the next clause: <hi type="italic">all' oud' eti nyn dynasthe</hi> — "and even now you are not yet able." The word <hi type="italic">eti</hi> ("still") and <hi type="italic">nyn</hi> ("now") intensify the indictment. This is not merely a description of their past state; it is their present condition. Time has passed since Paul's initial visit. Apollos has come and gone (Acts 18:27-19:1). The community has been taught by multiple teachers. Yet they have not matured. They are stuck.</p>
          <p>"For you are still of the flesh. For while there is jealousy and strife among you, are you not of the flesh and behaving only in a human way?" (3:3).</p>
          <p>Paul shifts from <hi type="italic">sarkinois</hi> (v. 1, "made of flesh," describing material composition) to <hi type="italic">sarkikoi</hi> (v. 3, "pertaining to the flesh, characterized by flesh," describing operative behavior). The shift is subtle but significant. In verse 1, Paul described their condition when he first arrived — constitutionally immature, naturally fleshy. In verse 3, he describes their current behavior — actively operating according to the flesh, their conduct governed by fleshly patterns rather than the Spirit. The move from <hi type="italic">-inos</hi> to <hi type="italic">-ikos</hi> marks a transition from "what you were made of" to "how you are behaving." They should have outgrown their initial fleshliness; instead, their behavior continues to be characterized by it.</p>
          <p>The evidence Paul cites is precise: <hi type="italic">zelos kai eris</hi> — "jealousy and strife." These are not random vices. They are the specific symptoms of competitive factionalism. The word <hi type="italic">zelos</hi> ("jealousy, envy, rivalry, zeal") in its negative sense describes the desire to have what another possesses or to surpass another's standing. The word <hi type="italic">eris</hi> ("strife, quarreling, contention") is the same word from 1:11, where Paul reported that "there are quarrels (<hi type="italic">erides</hi>) among you." Paul is circling back to the opening diagnosis. The evidence for fleshliness is not sexual immorality (that will come in chapter 5), not idolatry (chapters 8-10), not worship disorder (chapters 11-14). The evidence is the faction problem itself. Their competitive attachment to human teachers is the proof that they are operating <hi type="italic">kata anthropon</hi> ("according to a human standard") — literally "according to man."</p>
          <p>The rhetorical question <hi type="italic">ouk anthropoi este</hi> ("are you not [merely] human?") is withering. The word <hi type="italic">anthropoi</hi> ("human beings") is used here almost as a term of rebuke. After everything Paul has argued in chapters 1-2 — that the community has received the Spirit who searches the depths of God (2:10), that they possess the mind of Christ (2:16), that they have been enriched in every way (1:5) — they are behaving as though none of this were true. They are acting like people who have not received the Spirit at all, evaluating everything by the criteria of the present age, competing for status through teacher-allegiance as though they were attending a sophist's lecture hall rather than participating in the assembly of God.</p>
          <p>"For when one says, 'I follow Paul,' and another, 'I follow Apollos,' are you not being merely human?" (3:4).</p>
          <p>Paul repeats the faction slogans from 1:12, but this time he names only two: "I am of Paul" and "I am of Apollos." The reduction from four slogans to two is strategic. Paul is about to address his own relationship with Apollos directly (vv. 5-9), using their collaboration as a model for how the Corinthians should understand the relationship between teachers. The Cephas and Christ factions drop away because the argument that follows will focus on the specific case Paul can illustrate from his own experience: he and Apollos, working in the same field, serving the same God.</p>
          <p>The question <hi type="italic">ouk anthropoi este</hi> ("are you not [merely] human?") repeats the verdict of verse 3. The repetition drives the point home. The factionalism is the definitive evidence of spiritual immaturity. It is not one problem among many; it is the diagnostic symptom that reveals the underlying condition. A community that divides along the lines of human leaders is a community operating from human — not spiritual — criteria.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.3.5-1Cor.3.9">
          <title>Verses 5-9: The Agricultural Metaphor — Servants in God's Field</title>
          <p>"What then is Apollos? What is Paul? Servants through whom you believed, as the Lord assigned to each" (3:5).</p>
          <p>The questions <hi type="italic">ti oun estin Apollos? ti de estin Paulos?</hi> are deliberately framed with the neuter interrogative <hi type="italic">ti</hi> ("what"), not the masculine <hi type="italic">tis</hi> ("who"). Paul does not ask "who is Apollos?" — which would invite a personal evaluation, a comparison of credentials and qualities. He asks "what is Apollos?" — which asks about function, role, category. The question strips the teachers of personal significance and reduces them to their functional role.</p>
          <p>The answer: <hi type="italic">diakonoi di' hon episteusate</hi> — "servants through whom you believed." The word <hi type="italic">diakonoi</hi> means attendants, servers, those who carry out tasks assigned by another. In Greek usage, a <hi type="italic">diakonos</hi> was a table server, a messenger, one who performed practical service at another's direction. The word carries no connotation of authority or prestige; it denotes subordinate service. The preposition <hi type="italic">di'</hi> ("through") is critical. The teachers are the instruments through whom the Corinthians came to faith, not the source of that faith. They are channels, not fountains. The Corinthians' faith passed through Paul and Apollos on its way from God to them. Attaching oneself to the channel rather than to the source is a category error.</p>
          <p>The qualifier <hi type="italic">hos ho kyrios edoken hekasto</hi> ("as the Lord assigned to each") introduces a further limitation. Each teacher's role was not self-chosen but assigned (<hi type="italic">edoken</hi>, "gave," from <hi type="italic">didomi</hi>) by the Lord. The word <hi type="italic">hekasto</hi> ("to each") individualizes the assignment: Paul received one task, Apollos another, and neither chose his own. The Lord distributed the roles. The Corinthians' preference for one teacher over another is therefore an implicit critique of the Lord's assignment.</p>
          <p>"I planted, Apollos watered, but God was causing the growth" (3:6).</p>
          <p>Paul introduces the agricultural metaphor with three verbs arranged in a deliberate sequence. <hi type="italic">Ego ephyteusa</hi> ("I planted") — the aorist tense marks the action as a completed past event. Paul's planting was his founding visit to Corinth, the eighteen months during which he established the community (Acts 18:1-11). <hi type="italic">Apollos epotisen</hi> ("Apollos watered") — also aorist, also a completed past event. Apollos's watering was his subsequent ministry in Corinth, during which he "greatly helped those who through grace had believed" (Acts 18:27). The two aorists mark two discrete, completed contributions.</p>
          <p>But the third verb breaks the pattern: <hi type="italic">ho de theos euxanen</hi> ("but God was giving the growth"). The imperfect tense <hi type="italic">euxanen</hi> contrasts sharply with the two aorists. The aorists describe punctual actions completed and finished. The imperfect describes continuous, ongoing action. While Paul planted (once) and Apollos watered (once), God was continuously, persistently, without interruption, causing the growth. The growth is God's ongoing work, not a single event. Paul and Apollos performed time-bound tasks; God's activity sustains the field from beginning to end. The imperfect tense is not incidental; it is the argument in miniature. The human agents come and go; God's work is ceaseless.</p>
          <p>The agricultural metaphor carries deep resonance in Israel's scriptures. Israel itself was God's planting. Isaiah 5:1-7: "My beloved had a vineyard on a very fertile hill. He dug it and cleared it of stones, and planted it with choice vines... the vineyard of the LORD of hosts is the house of Israel, and the men of Judah are his pleasant planting." Psalm 80:8-11 (LXX 79:9-12): "You brought a vine out of Egypt; you drove out the nations and planted it." Jeremiah 2:21: "I planted you a choice vine, wholly of pure seed." Isaiah 61:3: "they will be called oaks of righteousness, the planting of the LORD, that he may be glorified." In every case, the planter is God. The vine, the field, the planting belongs to him. Human workers may tend the field, but the field is God's. A first-century hearer steeped in Israel's scriptures would hear Paul claiming for the Corinthian community the same identity Israel bore: God's planting.</p>
          <p>"So neither he who plants is anything, nor he who waters, but only God who gives the growth" (3:7).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">oudeis</hi> ("nothing") is absolute. The planter is nothing. The waterer is nothing. The word <hi type="italic">ti</hi> after <hi type="italic">oute</hi> ("not anything") strips the teachers of independent significance. This is not false modesty; it is a theological claim grounded in the agricultural metaphor. A field does not owe its existence to the farmhand who scattered seed or poured water. The field owes its existence to the one who owns it and who alone can cause life to emerge from dead seed buried in soil. No amount of planting or watering produces growth if God does not act. The teachers are necessary instruments — Paul does not deny this; he planted, and Apollos watered, and these were real contributions — but they are instruments of a process that belongs entirely to God.</p>
          <p>The Corinthians' factionalism treats the instruments as though they were the source. It is as though a field quarreled over which farmhand it preferred, while ignoring the owner who gave it life.</p>
          <p>"He who plants and he who waters are one, and each will receive his own wages according to his own labor" (3:8).</p>
          <p>Two statements, each important. First: <hi type="italic">ho phyteuon kai ho potizon hen eisin</hi> — "the one planting and the one watering are one." The word <hi type="italic">hen</hi> ("one thing" — neuter, not masculine <hi type="italic">heis</hi>) indicates unity of purpose and function, not identity of person. Paul and Apollos are not the same person, but they are engaged in a single enterprise. The factions treat them as competitors; Paul declares them collaborators. The neuter <hi type="italic">hen</hi> is the same word Jesus uses in John 10:30 ("I and the Father are one" — <hi type="italic">hen esmen</hi>) and John 17:11 ("that they may be one" — <hi type="italic">hen osin</hi>). It describes an organic unity of purpose and work, not numerical identity.</p>
          <p>Second: <hi type="italic">hekastos de ton idion misthon lepsetai kata ton idion kopon</hi> — "each will receive his own reward according to his own labor." The word <hi type="italic">misthon</hi> ("wages, reward, payment") introduces a concept Paul will develop in verses 10-15. Each worker will be evaluated and compensated individually, but the evaluation is based on <hi type="italic">kopon</hi> ("labor, toil, effort"), not on the number of followers each attracts. The word <hi type="italic">kopos</hi> in Paul's usage describes hard, often thankless work — in 1 Thessalonians 2:9, Paul recalls his "labor and toil" (<hi type="italic">kopon kai mochthon</hi>) among the Thessalonians, working night and day so as not to burden anyone. In 1 Corinthians 15:58, he will urge the Corinthians that their "labor in the Lord is not in vain" (<hi type="italic">ho kopos hymon ouk estin kenos en kyrio</hi>). The evaluation criteria is faithfulness in labor, not celebrity among disciples.</p>
          <p>The juxtaposition of the two clauses is precise. The planter and waterer are <hi type="italic">hen</hi> ("one") in their corporate function — they serve the same field, the same God, the same purpose. Yet each will receive his <hi type="italic">idion</hi> ("own, individual") reward according to his <hi type="italic">idion</hi> ("own, individual") labor. Unity of mission does not erase individuality of accountability. Paul and Apollos are one in their service; they will be evaluated separately for the quality of that service.</p>
          <p>"For we are God's fellow workers; you are God's field, God's building" (3:9).</p>
          <p>This verse is the hinge between the agricultural metaphor (vv. 5-8) and the construction metaphor (vv. 10-17). Three possessive genitives, each beginning with <hi type="italic">theou</hi> ("of God"), hammer the same point:</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Theou gar esmen synergoi</hi> — "for we are God's fellow workers." The word <hi type="italic">synergoi</hi> ("co-workers, collaborators") can be parsed two ways: (a) "workers together with God" — Paul and Apollos are God's co-laborers, working alongside God in his field; or (b) "fellow workers belonging to God" — Paul and Apollos are co-workers who belong to God, working together in his service. Either reading serves Paul's argument. The genitive <hi type="italic">theou</hi> establishes ownership: whether "co-workers with God" or "co-workers belonging to God," the workers belong to God, not to the Corinthians.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Theou georgion</hi> — "God's field." The word <hi type="italic">georgion</hi> (from <hi type="italic">ge</hi>, "earth/land," and <hi type="italic">ergon</hi>, "work") means a cultivated plot, a farm, a tilled field. The genitive <hi type="italic">theou</hi> makes God the owner. The Corinthians are God's cultivated land. The field does not belong to the farmer who plants it or the laborer who waters it; it belongs to the landowner. The entire factionalism is thereby exposed as a confusion of ownership. The Corinthians have been claiming to belong to their teachers ("I am of Paul," "I am of Apollos"); Paul says they belong to God.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Theou oikodome</hi> — "God's building." The word <hi type="italic">oikodome</hi> (from <hi type="italic">oikos</hi>, "house," and <hi type="italic">domo</hi>, "to build") means a construction, an edifice, a building in progress. The genitive <hi type="italic">theou</hi> again establishes ownership. The transition from <hi type="italic">georgion</hi> to <hi type="italic">oikodome</hi> shifts the metaphor from agriculture to construction, preparing for the extended building imagery of verses 10-17. The community is not only a field God tends but a structure God is constructing. Both metaphors serve the same purpose: the community belongs to God, and human workers are subordinate agents in God's project.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.3.10-1Cor.3.15">
          <title>Verses 10-15: The Construction Metaphor — Foundation, Builders, and Fire</title>
          <p>"According to the grace of God given to me, like a skilled master builder I laid a foundation, and someone else is building upon it. Let each one take care how he builds upon it" (3:10).</p>
          <p>Paul now develops the <hi type="italic">oikodome</hi> metaphor. He describes his own role with precision: <hi type="italic">kata ten charin tou theou ten dotheisan moi</hi> — "according to the grace of God given to me." The grace (<hi type="italic">charis</hi>) was given (<hi type="italic">dotheisan</hi>, passive participle of <hi type="italic">didomi</hi> — a gift received, not a qualification earned). Paul's role as foundation-layer rests entirely on grace, not on personal merit or rhetorical skill. The phrase echoes 1:4, where Paul thanked God for "the grace of God that was given you" (<hi type="italic">te chariti tou theou te dotheise hymin</hi>). What Paul possesses, like what the Corinthians possess, is a gift.</p>
          <p>The title <hi type="italic">sophos architekton</hi> ("skilled master builder") uses the word <hi type="italic">sophos</hi> ("wise, skilled") in its practical, artisanal sense — not the philosophical <hi type="italic">sophia</hi> he has been critiquing, but the craftsman's wisdom, the knowledge of how to build something properly. The <hi type="italic">architekton</hi> is the chief builder, the one who designs and oversees the construction, who ensures the foundation is correctly laid. In the LXX, the word appears in Isaiah 3:3, where among the skilled people God will remove from Jerusalem is the <hi type="italic">sophos architekton</hi> — the "skillful craftsman" or "expert builder." Paul claims this role for himself: he laid the foundation of the Corinthian community.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">themelion</hi> ("foundation") is crucial. In the LXX, the word is used for the foundation of the temple: 1 Kings 5:17 (LXX 3 Kingdoms 5:17) describes Solomon laying the foundation (<hi type="italic">themelios</hi>) of the house of the LORD; Ezra 3:10-11 describes the laying of the temple foundation with priestly trumpets and Levitical cymbals, and the people shouting with a great shout when "the foundation of the house of the LORD was laid." Isaiah 28:16: "Behold, I am laying in Zion a foundation stone (<hi type="italic">lithon... eis ta themelia</hi>), a tested stone, a precious cornerstone, of a sure foundation: whoever believes will not be put to shame." Paul's claim to have laid a <hi type="italic">themelion</hi> connects directly to the temple imagery he will invoke explicitly in verses 16-17. The Corinthian community is not just any building; it is the building that corresponds to the temple of God, and Paul laid its foundation.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">allos de epoikodomei</hi> ("and another builds upon it") uses the present tense, indicating ongoing construction. Others are building now, on the foundation Paul laid. The word <hi type="italic">allos</hi> ("another") is indefinite and includes Apollos but is not limited to him. Multiple builders may contribute.</p>
          <p>The warning <hi type="italic">blepeto... pos epoikodomei</hi> ("let each one take care how he builds") shifts the focus from the foundation (which is settled) to the superstructure (which is under construction). The verb <hi type="italic">blepeto</hi> ("let him watch, let him take care, let him be careful") is a command. The concern is not whether to build — building will happen — but how to build. The quality of the construction matters, and it is the builder's responsibility.</p>
          <p>"For no one can lay a foundation other than the one that is laid, which is Jesus Christ" (3:11).</p>
          <p>The foundation is identified with absolute clarity: <hi type="italic">themelion gar allon oudeis dynatai theinai para ton keimenon, hos estin Iesous Christos</hi> — "for no one is able to lay another foundation besides the one that is laid, which is Jesus Christ." The verb <hi type="italic">dynatai</hi> ("is able") with the negation <hi type="italic">oudeis</hi> ("no one") expresses impossibility. This is not a prohibition (you must not lay another foundation) but a statement of impossibility (no one can). The foundation has been laid, and it cannot be changed or replaced. The relative clause <hi type="italic">hos estin Iesous Christos</hi> ("which is Jesus Christ") names the foundation without ambiguity.</p>
          <p>The present participle <hi type="italic">ton keimenon</hi> ("the one lying [there]") uses the present tense to describe the foundation as currently in place, already established, a fait accompli. This is the same Jesus Christ Paul preached: "Jesus Christ and him crucified" (2:2). The foundation is the proclamation of the crucified Messiah — not a philosophical system, not a rhetorical tradition, not a human teacher's wisdom. Any construction on the community must begin from this ground. Any builder who attempts to substitute a different foundation — a different basis for the community's identity — is attempting the impossible.</p>
          <p>"Now if anyone builds on the foundation with gold, silver, precious stones, wood, hay, straw" (3:12).</p>
          <p>Six materials, arranged in two groups of three. The first three — <hi type="italic">chrysion</hi> ("gold"), <hi type="italic">argyrion</hi> ("silver"), <hi type="italic">lithous timious</hi> ("precious stones") — are durable, fireproof, valuable. The second three — <hi type="italic">xyla</hi> ("wood"), <hi type="italic">chorton</hi> ("hay, grass"), <hi type="italic">kalamen</hi> ("straw, stubble") — are flammable, cheap, perishable. The contrast is not between good and evil but between enduring and perishable, between quality that survives testing and work that does not. The materials represent the quality of what is built on the foundation — the teaching, the leadership, the community formation that various builders contribute. Gold, silver, and precious stones are the materials of the temple itself: 1 Chronicles 29:2 lists David's provision for the temple as "gold... silver... bronze... iron... wood... onyx stones, stones for setting, antimony, stones of various colors, and all sorts of precious stones (<hi type="italic">lithous timious</hi>) and marble." 1 Kings 7:9-10 (LXX 3 Kingdoms 7:9-10) describes the temple's stones as "costly stones" (<hi type="italic">lithoi timioi</hi>). The durable materials evoke temple construction — appropriate, since Paul will identify the community as God's temple in verse 16.</p>
          <p>Wood, hay, and straw, by contrast, are the materials of temporary structures and animal shelter. The image is vivid: some builders construct with temple-quality materials; others slap together something from field cuttings. Both build on the same foundation. The difference is in what they add to it.</p>
          <p>The pronoun <hi type="italic">tis</hi> ("anyone") is indefinite and inclusive. This is not addressed exclusively to Paul and Apollos but to every teacher, leader, and contributor who builds on the community's foundation. The scope expands: anyone who exercises influence over the community's formation is a builder and will be held accountable for the quality of their work.</p>
          <p>"Each one's work will become manifest, for the Day will disclose it, because it will be revealed by fire, and the fire itself will test what sort of work each one has done" (3:13).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">hekastou</hi> ("of each one") individualizes the judgment. The assessment is personal and unavoidable. The phrase <hi type="italic">to ergon phaneron genesetai</hi> ("the work will become manifest, visible, apparent") uses the future tense: the quality of each builder's work, which may be unclear now, will become undeniably visible. The present obscurity of the quality is temporary.</p>
          <p>The agent of disclosure is <hi type="italic">he hemera</hi> ("the Day"). The article <hi type="italic">he</hi> makes this a specific, recognized day — not just any day, but the Day. In Paul's usage, this connects directly to 1:8: "the day of our Lord Jesus Christ" (<hi type="italic">ten hemeran tou kyriou hemon Iesou Christou</hi>). Behind this lies the prophetic concept of the <hi type="italic">yom YHWH</hi> — the day of the LORD — which in Amos 5:18-20, Joel 2:1-11, Zephaniah 1:14-18, Malachi 3:2, and Isaiah 2:12 is the day when God acts decisively in judgment and salvation. The Day will <hi type="italic">deloun</hi> ("disclose, make clear, reveal") the character of each builder's work.</p>
          <p>The instrument of testing is <hi type="italic">pyr</hi> ("fire"). The phrase <hi type="italic">en pyri apokalyptetai</hi> ("is revealed by/in fire") uses the same root (<hi type="italic">apokalypto</hi>) as "the revealing of our Lord Jesus Christ" in 1:7. Fire in the prophetic tradition serves as God's instrument of purification and judgment. Malachi 3:2-3: "For he is like a refiner's fire and like fullers' soap. He will sit as a refiner and purifier of silver, and he will purify the sons of Levi and refine them like gold and silver." Isaiah 48:10: "Behold, I have refined you, but not as silver; I have tried you in the furnace of affliction." Zechariah 13:9: "I will put this third into the fire, and refine them as one refines silver, and test them as gold is tested." Fire in these texts does not annihilate indiscriminately; it separates what is durable from what is not. It consumes dross while purifying gold. The fire Paul describes functions identically: it tests (<hi type="italic">dokimasei</hi>, "will assay, will prove, will examine") the quality (<hi type="italic">hopoion</hi>, "what sort, what kind") of each builder's work.</p>
          <p>The object of the fire's testing is the work, not the worker. The phrase <hi type="italic">to ergon hekastou hopoion estin</hi> ("what sort of work each one has done") focuses the judgment on the construction, not on the builder's person. This distinction is essential for understanding verses 14-15.</p>
          <p>"If the work of anyone survives, he will receive a reward" (3:14).</p>
          <p>The conditional <hi type="italic">ei tinos to ergon menei</hi> ("if anyone's work remains, survives, endures") uses the verb <hi type="italic">meno</hi> ("to remain, to abide, to survive"). The fire tests, and work built from gold, silver, and precious stones endures the test. The builder whose work survives <hi type="italic">misthon lepsetai</hi> ("will receive a reward"). The word <hi type="italic">misthon</hi> is the same as in verse 8 — "wages, payment, recompense for labor." The reward is not salvation itself (which is not described as a <hi type="italic">misthon</hi> anywhere in Paul's letters) but compensation for faithful and quality labor. The builder who constructed well will receive recognition and reward from the Lord who assigned the task (cf. 4:5: "then each one will receive his commendation from God").</p>
          <p>"If anyone's work is burned up, he will suffer loss, though he himself will be saved, but only as through fire" (3:15).</p>
          <p>The conditional <hi type="italic">ei tinos to ergon katakaesetai</hi> ("if anyone's work is burned up") describes the opposite outcome. The builder constructed with wood, hay, and straw; the fire consumes the entire superstructure. The builder <hi type="italic">zemioothesetai</hi> ("will suffer loss, will be penalized, will forfeit"). The verb <hi type="italic">zemioo</hi> means to incur damage, to be fined, to lose what one might have had. The loss is the <hi type="italic">misthon</hi> — the reward for quality work. The work is gone. The investment of labor produced nothing that endures.</p>
          <p>But then the remarkable qualifier: <hi type="italic">autos de soothesetai</hi> ("he himself will be saved"). The builder whose work is destroyed is not destroyed with it. The pronoun <hi type="italic">autos</hi> ("he himself") is emphatic, distinguishing the builder from his work. The work perishes; the person survives. The verb <hi type="italic">soothesetai</hi> ("will be saved") is the future passive of <hi type="italic">sozo</hi> — the same word used throughout chapters 1-2 for salvation (1:18: "to us who are being saved"; 1:21: "to save those who believe"). The builder who built poorly is still saved.</p>
          <p>The qualification <hi type="italic">houtos de hos dia pyros</hi> ("but in this manner — as through fire") describes the nature of the salvation. The image is vivid: a person escaping from a burning building, losing everything they built but getting out alive. The phrase <hi type="italic">hos</hi> ("as, like") makes this a comparison, not a literal description of the mechanism of salvation. The builder's salvation is real but bare — stripped of all the work they invested, saved with nothing to show for their labor, like someone who survives a fire but loses their house and everything in it.</p>
          <p>This passage is addressed to builders — those who teach, lead, and form the community. The subject throughout has been the quality of ministry exercised by teachers within the community, not the spiritual condition of ordinary community members. The "work" that is tested is the work of building on the foundation — the teaching, formation, and leadership that contributes to the community's growth. Paul is warning teachers (including himself) that their ministry will be tested, and some will discover that their contribution, however extensive, was worthless — wood, hay, and straw rather than gold, silver, and precious stones. The warning applies directly to the faction problem: teaching that divides the community, that promotes personal followings, that operates by the criteria of worldly wisdom rather than the mind of Christ, may well be building with straw.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.3.16-1Cor.3.17">
          <title>Verses 16-17: The Temple Warning — Destruction Brings Destruction</title>
          <p>"Do you not know that you are God's temple and that God's Spirit dwells in you?" (3:16).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ouk oidate</hi> ("do you not know?") is one of Paul's characteristic rhetorical devices in this letter. It appears ten times in 1 Corinthians (3:16, 5:6, 6:2, 6:3, 6:9, 6:15, 6:16, 6:19, 9:13, 9:24), always introducing something the Corinthians should already know but are failing to act upon. The formula conveys: this is not new information; this is a truth you already possess that should be governing your behavior but is not.</p>
          <p>The claim: <hi type="italic">naos theou este</hi> — "you are God's temple." The word <hi type="italic">naos</hi> (not <hi type="italic">hieron</hi>) is specific. In Greek temple architecture, the <hi type="italic">hieron</hi> designated the entire temple complex — courts, porticos, surrounding structures. The <hi type="italic">naos</hi> designated the inner sanctuary, the dwelling place of the god, the holy of holies. In the LXX, <hi type="italic">naos</hi> is used for the innermost part of the temple where God's presence dwelled: 1 Kings 6:5 (LXX 3 Kingdoms 6:5) uses <hi type="italic">naos</hi> for the inner sanctuary of Solomon's temple; Psalm 18:6 (LXX 17:7): "from his temple (<hi type="italic">naou</hi>) he heard my voice"; Habakkuk 2:20: "the LORD is in his holy temple (<hi type="italic">en nao hagio autou</hi>); let all the earth keep silence before him." The <hi type="italic">naos</hi> is where God is present.</p>
          <p>The pronoun <hi type="italic">hymeis</hi> ("you" — second person plural) is emphatic and corporate. Paul is not describing individual believers as individual temples; he is describing the community as a whole as God's temple. The "you" is the assembled community in Corinth. Together, collectively, as a body, they constitute the <hi type="italic">naos theou</hi>. This is confirmed by the next clause: <hi type="italic">to pneuma tou theou oikei en hymin</hi> — "the Spirit of God dwells in you [plural]." The verb <hi type="italic">oikei</hi> ("dwells, resides, inhabits") is the characteristic term for taking up residence. The Spirit has taken up residence in the community as God's presence once dwelled in the tabernacle and then the temple. The community is the location of God's presence on earth. The preposition <hi type="italic">en</hi> with the plural <hi type="italic">hymin</hi> means "among you" or "in you [as a group]" — the Spirit inhabits the community, not just individual members in isolation.</p>
          <p>The temple identification carries extraordinary weight. The Jerusalem temple was the center of Israel's life — the place where heaven and earth intersected, where God's name dwelled (Deuteronomy 12:5: "the place that the LORD your God will choose... to put his name and make his habitation there"), where sacrifices were offered, where forgiveness was obtained, where pilgrims gathered. For Paul to say that a community of mostly Gentile believers in a pagan commercial city constitutes God's <hi type="italic">naos</hi> is a claim of staggering proportions. It also raises the stakes of the factionalism to the highest possible level: the quarreling is not merely disrupting a social club; it is tearing apart the dwelling place of God.</p>
          <p>"If anyone destroys God's temple, God will destroy him. For God's temple is holy, and you are that temple" (3:17).</p>
          <p>The conditional <hi type="italic">ei tis ton naon tou theou phtheirei, phtherei touton ho theos</hi> — "if anyone destroys/corrupts God's temple, God will destroy/corrupt that person." The verb <hi type="italic">phtheiro</hi> has a range of meaning: "to corrupt, to ruin, to destroy, to spoil, to defile." Paul uses the same verb in both the protasis and apodosis: the action and the consequence mirror each other. Whoever corrupts God's temple will be corrupted by God. The <hi type="italic">lex talionis</hi> pattern — the punishment matching the crime — is deliberate. This is not an arbitrary punishment but an exact correspondence: what you do to God's dwelling, God does to you.</p>
          <p>This warning must be distinguished from the scenario in verses 14-15. There, a builder whose work is poor (wood, hay, straw) suffers the loss of his work but is personally saved. Here, the person who destroys the temple is destroyed by God. The difference is between building poorly and destroying. One builder adds inadequate materials to the structure; the structure may be weakened, but the building still stands, and the builder, though losing his reward, survives. Another person actively corrupts or ruins the temple itself — not merely building poorly on it but tearing it apart, defiling it, destroying its integrity. That person faces not loss of reward but God's destruction.</p>
          <p>In the immediate context, what would constitute destroying God's temple? Paul has been arguing since 1:10 that the community is being torn apart by factionalism. The <hi type="italic">schismata</hi> of 1:10 (the word for tearing cloth) are ripping the community — God's temple — into pieces. Anyone whose behavior or teaching divides the community, corrupts its unity, defiles the dwelling place of God's Spirit, is destroying God's temple. The warning is not abstract or eschatological only; it addresses the present situation directly. The faction-promoters, those who divide the community along the lines of teacher-allegiance, are engaging in temple-destruction. The consequence Paul announces is severe: <hi type="italic">phtherei touton ho theos</hi> — God will destroy that person.</p>
          <p>The grounding for this severity: <hi type="italic">ho gar naos tou theou hagios estin</hi> — "for the temple of God is holy." The word <hi type="italic">hagios</hi> ("holy, sacred, set apart, consecrated") connects to the letter's opening: the Corinthians are "sanctified" (<hi type="italic">hegiasmenois</hi>) in Christ Jesus, "called saints" (<hi type="italic">kletois hagiois</hi>) (1:2). The holiness of the temple is the holiness of the community — both are set apart for God, consecrated by God's own action, belonging to God's sacred sphere. To violate what is holy is to invite the consequences that Israel's scriptures consistently attach to desecration. When Nadab and Abihu offered unauthorized fire before the LORD, fire came out from the LORD and consumed them (Leviticus 10:1-2). When Uzzah touched the ark, the LORD struck him down (2 Samuel 6:6-7). When Belshazzar used the vessels from God's temple for his banquet, the writing appeared on the wall and his kingdom was taken (Daniel 5:1-30). The principle is consistent: what is holy belongs to God, and violating it brings God's direct action.</p>
          <p>The final clause <hi type="italic">hoitines este hymeis</hi> ("which [temple] you are") reiterates the corporate identification. The pronoun <hi type="italic">hymeis</hi> ("you" plural) is emphatic by position: you — the Corinthian community, the squabbling, faction-ridden, infant-like community — you are that holy temple. The weight falls on the incongruity: the temple is holy, and the people who are that temple are tearing it apart. The indictment could not be sharper.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.3.18-1Cor.3.23">
          <title>Verses 18-23: The Final Inversion — All Things Are Yours</title>
          <p>"Let no one deceive himself. If anyone among you thinks that he is wise in this age, let him become a fool that he may become wise" (3:18).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">medeis heauton exapatato</hi> ("let no one deceive himself") uses the present imperative of <hi type="italic">exapatao</hi> ("to thoroughly deceive, to beguile"). The self-deception Paul targets is specific: <hi type="italic">ei tis dokei sophos einai en hymin en to aioni touto</hi> ("if anyone among you thinks he is wise in this age"). The verb <hi type="italic">dokei</hi> ("thinks, supposes, imagines") does not describe actual wisdom but the illusion of it. The qualifiers <hi type="italic">en hymin</hi> ("among you") and <hi type="italic">en to aioni touto</hi> ("in this age") locate the supposed wisdom within the community and within the present age's categories. Someone in the community fancies himself wise by the standards of this age — the same standards Paul has been demolishing since 1:18.</p>
          <p>The prescription: <hi type="italic">moros genestho, hina genetai sophos</hi> — "let him become a fool, that he may become wise." The paradox is deliberate and matches the paradox of the cross throughout the letter. The path to genuine wisdom passes through what this age calls foolishness. The person who clings to this age's wisdom must release it — must accept the label "fool" from the world's perspective — in order to receive the wisdom that is from God. This is not anti-intellectualism; it is a reorientation of the epistemological framework. The mind of Christ (2:16) is not reached by escalating worldly wisdom to higher levels but by abandoning worldly wisdom's claims to sovereignty and receiving what God reveals through the Spirit.</p>
          <p>"For the wisdom of this world is foolishness with God. For it is written, 'He catches the wise in their craftiness'" (3:19).</p>
          <p>Paul grounds the inversion in two scriptural quotations, both introduced as authoritative ("it is written").</p>
          <p>The first is from Job 5:13 (LXX): <hi type="italic">ho drassomenos tous sophous en te panourgia auton</hi> — "he who seizes the wise in their craftiness." In the context of Job, these are the words of Eliphaz in his first speech. Eliphaz is describing God's power to overturn human plans: God "does great things and unsearchable, marvelous things without number" (Job 5:9); "he sets on high those who are lowly, and those who mourn are lifted to safety" (Job 5:11); "he catches the wise in their own craftiness, and the schemes of the wily are brought to a quick end" (Job 5:12-13). The word <hi type="italic">panourgia</hi> ("craftiness, cunning, shrewdness, clever scheming") describes intelligence deployed for self-serving ends — wisdom used as a tool for personal advantage. God does not merely outthink the cunning; he turns their very craftiness into the trap that catches them. Their own scheming becomes the instrument of their undoing.</p>
          <p>This is precisely what happened at the cross. The rulers of this age, exercising their worldly wisdom and political power, crucified Jesus — and in doing so, unwittingly accomplished God's hidden plan (2:7-8). Their craftiness became the mechanism of their defeat. The Job quotation is not ornamental; it describes the actual pattern of the cross.</p>
          <p>"And again, 'The Lord knows the thoughts of the wise, that they are futile'" (3:20).</p>
          <p>The second quotation is from Psalm 94:11 (LXX 93:11), but Paul modifies it. The LXX reads: <hi type="italic">kyrios ginoskei tous dialogismous ton anthropon, hoti eisin mataioi</hi> — "the Lord knows the thoughts of human beings (<hi type="italic">anthropon</hi>), that they are futile." Paul substitutes <hi type="italic">ton sophon</hi> ("of the wise") for <hi type="italic">ton anthropon</hi> ("of human beings"). The modification is deliberate: Paul applies to the wise specifically what the psalm says of humanity generally. The Lord's knowledge is comprehensive and penetrating — he knows the <hi type="italic">dialogismous</hi> ("reasonings, deliberations, calculations") of the wise, and his verdict is that they are <hi type="italic">mataioi</hi> ("vain, empty, futile, purposeless"). The word <hi type="italic">mataios</hi> in the LXX describes what is insubstantial and accomplishes nothing. Ecclesiastes repeatedly uses the cognate <hi type="italic">mataiotes</hi> ("vanity, futility") for human endeavors that fail to achieve lasting significance. The Lord's evaluation of human wisdom — even at its most sophisticated — is that it produces nothing of enduring value. It is <hi type="italic">mataios</hi>.</p>
          <p>Together, the two quotations complete the argument that began in 1:19 (Isaiah 29:14: "I will destroy the wisdom of the wise"). God catches the wise in their craftiness (Job 5:13). God knows the wise's thoughts are futile (Psalm 94:11). The wisdom of this age is not merely inferior to God's wisdom; it is actively self-defeating and ultimately empty. The Corinthians' attachment to human wisdom as the criterion for evaluating teachers is attachment to something God has declared futile.</p>
          <p>"So let no one boast in men" (3:21a).</p>
          <p>The conjunction <hi type="italic">hoste</hi> ("so, therefore, consequently") marks the conclusion drawn from everything in chapters 1-3. The prohibition <hi type="italic">medeis kauchastho en anthropois</hi> ("let no one boast in human beings") returns to the theme of 1:29 ("so that no flesh may boast before God") and 1:31 ("let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord"). The specific application is now clear: boasting "in men" (<hi type="italic">en anthropois</hi>) means boasting in human teachers — "I am of Paul," "I am of Apollos," "I am of Cephas." The faction slogans are a form of boasting in human beings. Paul declares it over.</p>
          <p>"For all things are yours, whether Paul or Apollos or Cephas or the world or life or death or the present or the future — all are yours" (3:21b-22).</p>
          <p>The reason no one should boast in particular teachers is not that the teachers are worthless but that all the teachers already belong to the community: <hi type="italic">panta gar hymon estin</hi> — "for all things are yours." The reversal is stunning. The Corinthians have been saying "I belong to Paul" or "I belong to Apollos" — treating themselves as possessions of their preferred teacher, defining their identity by attachment to a human leader. Paul inverts the ownership: Paul belongs to you. Apollos belongs to you. Cephas belongs to you. You do not belong to them; they belong to you. The faction slogans were exactly backward.</p>
          <p>Paul lists the teachers first — <hi type="italic">eite Paulos eite Apollos eite Kephas</hi> — and then expands the scope of what belongs to the community to breathtaking proportions: <hi type="italic">eite kosmos eite zoe eite thanatos eite enestota eite mellonta</hi> — "whether the world or life or death or the present or the future." The world (<hi type="italic">kosmos</hi>) — the entire created order — belongs to the community. Life and death, the two ultimate realities of human existence, belong to the community. The present and the future — the totality of time — belong to the community. The scope is total. Nothing is excluded from the community's inheritance. This echoes Romans 8:38-39, where Paul declares that "neither death nor life... nor things present nor things to come" can separate the community from God's love.</p>
          <p>The <hi type="italic">panta hymon estin</hi> ("all things are yours") rests on the community's identity as God's people, heirs of God's promises. In Genesis 1:28-30, God gave humanity dominion over all living things. In Psalm 8:6 (LXX 8:7): "You have given him dominion over the works of your hands; you have put all things under his feet." In Daniel 7:27: "the kingdom and the dominion and the greatness of the kingdoms under the whole heaven shall be given to the people of the saints of the Most High." The community of the Messiah inherits the dominion that belongs to God's people. Attaching oneself to one teacher as a faction-marker is not merely unnecessary; it is absurd — like an heir to the entire estate fighting over which servant he prefers, when all the servants already work for him.</p>
          <p>"And you are Christ's, and Christ is God's" (3:23).</p>
          <p>The chain of belonging reaches its climax. The Corinthians have been saying "I am of Paul" (<hi type="italic">ego Paulou</hi>). Paul has said "all things are yours" (<hi type="italic">panta hymon</hi>). Now: <hi type="italic">hymeis de Christou</hi> — "and you are Christ's." The Corinthians belong to Christ. Not to Paul, not to Apollos, not to Cephas. To Christ. The genitive <hi type="italic">Christou</hi> ("of Christ") is the genitive of belonging: you are Christ's possession, Christ's people, under Christ's lordship. This was already established in 1:2 (they are "sanctified in Christ Jesus") and 1:9 (they were "called into the fellowship of his Son, Jesus Christ our Lord"). The faction slogans misplaced the community's belonging; Paul restores it.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Christos de theou</hi> — "and Christ is God's." The chain terminates in God. All things belong to the community. The community belongs to Christ. Christ belongs to God. The entire hierarchy of belonging resolves into God as the ultimate possessor of all things. This is not a statement about Christ's subordination in nature or essence; it is a statement about the structure of the economy of salvation. In this letter, Paul will develop this theme further: in 11:3, "the head of Christ is God"; in 15:24-28, Christ will hand over the kingdom to God the Father, and "the Son himself will be subjected to the one who put all things in subjection under him, so that God may be all in all." The pattern is consistent: Christ's relationship to God is one of derivation and submission within the saving plan, just as the community's relationship to Christ is one of belonging and dependence. Everything points back to God, and everything proceeds from God. The faction slogans, which pointed to human teachers, are replaced by a chain that points to God through Christ.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.3">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 3 completes the argument begun in 1:10 and developed through chapters 1-2. The chapter performs five functions, each building on the last:</p>
          <p>First (vv. 1-4), Paul applies the categories of chapter 2 to the Corinthians themselves. The <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi>/<hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> distinction was not abstract theory; it was a diagnostic tool. The diagnosis: the Corinthians are <hi type="italic">sarkinoi</hi>, <hi type="italic">nepioi</hi>, <hi type="italic">anthropoi</hi> — fleshly, infantile, merely human. The evidence is their factionalism. Every claim Paul made in chapter 2 about possessing the mind of Christ and discerning all things spiritually is now turned against the Corinthians: you should be exercising spiritual discernment, but your behavior proves you are not.</p>
          <p>Second (vv. 5-9), Paul dismantles the basis of factionalism by redefining the role of teachers. They are not competing leaders to be ranked by followers; they are <hi type="italic">diakonoi</hi> (servants) performing tasks assigned by the Lord. The agricultural metaphor strips them of independent significance: the planter and the waterer are nothing; only God who gives the growth matters. The field belongs to God, not to the farmhands.</p>
          <p>Third (vv. 10-15), Paul shifts to a construction metaphor and introduces accountability for builders. He laid the foundation (Jesus Christ), and others build on it. The quality of each builder's work will be tested by fire on the Day. Some work will survive; some will be consumed. The builder whose work burns loses his reward but is personally saved. This section addresses teachers directly: their ministry will be evaluated, and inferior work — including the kind that promotes factions and operates by worldly wisdom — will be exposed as worthless.</p>
          <p>Fourth (vv. 16-17), Paul raises the stakes to their maximum height by identifying the community as God's temple — the <hi type="italic">naos</hi>, the inner sanctuary where God's Spirit dwells. Anyone who destroys this temple will be destroyed by God. The factionalism is not poor building technique; it is temple-destruction. It defiles and tears apart the dwelling place of God. The warning is the most severe in the letter so far.</p>
          <p>Fifth (vv. 18-23), Paul issues the final prohibition against boasting in human beings and replaces the faction slogans with a chain of belonging that resolves in God. All things belong to the community — including the teachers they were fighting over. The community belongs to Christ. Christ belongs to God. The competitive attachment to human leaders is not only spiritually immature; it inverts the actual order of things. The teachers serve the community, not the reverse.</p>
          <p>The three chapters together (1-3) form a single sustained argument: the Corinthian community was created by God's calling and grace (1:1-9); it has fractured along the lines of human teacher-allegiance (1:10-17); the theology of the cross exposes all human wisdom-boasting as incompatible with the gospel (1:18-31); Paul's own practice embodied this principle (2:1-5); God's wisdom is hidden from the world and revealed only through the Spirit (2:6-16); the Corinthians, despite possessing the Spirit, are behaving as though they do not (3:1-4); teachers are servants in God's field and builders on God's foundation (3:5-15); the community is God's holy temple, and destroying it brings God's judgment (3:16-17); therefore, stop boasting in human teachers, because all things already belong to you, you belong to Christ, and Christ belongs to God (3:18-23).</p>
          <p>The movement from 1:10 to 3:23 is a single arc: from the diagnosis of faction (1:10-12) through the theology that exposes its absurdity (1:13-2:16) to the direct application to the Corinthians (3:1-4) and the correct understanding of teachers (3:5-17) and finally to the proper ordering of all relationships under God (3:18-23). The argument is complete. Chapter 4 will add a personal appeal and a warning about Paul's forthcoming visit, but the theological case against factionalism is finished by 3:23.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.4">
        <title>1 Corinthians 4 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.4">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 3):</hi> Paul opened chapter 3 by applying the argument of chapters 1-2 directly to the Corinthians. Despite everything he taught them about the Spirit's wisdom, they are behaving as "fleshly people" (<hi type="italic">sarkinois</hi>) and "infants in Christ" (<hi type="italic">nepiois en Christo</hi>) rather than as "spiritual people" (<hi type="italic">pneumatikois</hi>) (3:1). The evidence of their immaturity is the factionalism itself: "For when one says, 'I am of Paul,' and another, 'I am of Apollos,' are you not merely human?" (3:4). Paul then corrected their understanding of apostles: "What then is Apollos? What is Paul? Servants through whom you believed, as the Lord assigned to each" (3:5). The word <hi type="italic">diakonoi</hi> ("servants, attendants") strips the teachers of the exalted status the Corinthians have been conferring on them. Paul planted, Apollos watered, but God gave the growth; neither planter nor waterer is anything — only God matters (3:6-7).</p>
          <p>Paul then shifted to a construction metaphor. He laid a foundation — "Jesus Christ" (3:11) — and others build on it. The quality of each builder's work will be tested by fire on "the day" (3:13). A builder whose work survives receives a reward; a builder whose work burns up will "suffer loss, though he himself will be saved, but only as through fire" (3:15). Then came a solemn warning: "If anyone destroys God's temple, God will destroy him. For God's temple is holy, and you are that temple" (3:17). The pronoun "you" (<hi type="italic">hymeis</hi>) is plural — the temple is the community, not the individual, and the one who destroys it faces God's destruction. The warning applies to anyone whose factional behavior tears apart the community that is God's sacred dwelling.</p>
          <p>The chapter closed with a return to the wisdom/foolishness theme. "If anyone among you thinks that he is wise in this age, he must become foolish, so that he may become wise" (3:18). Paul quoted Job 5:13 — "He catches the wise in their craftiness" — and Psalm 94:11 (LXX 93:11) — "The Lord knows the thoughts of the wise, that they are futile." Both citations reinforce the argument from 1:19-20: God overturns human wisdom. The climax of chapter 3 inverted the Corinthians' entire framework: "So then, no one is to be boasting in people. For all things belong to you, whether Paul or Apollos or Cephas, or the world or life or death, or things present or things to come; all things belong to you, and you belong to Christ, and Christ belongs to God" (3:21-23). The Corinthians have been saying "I belong to Paul" or "I belong to Apollos." Paul says the opposite: Paul belongs to you. Apollos belongs to you. The teachers are your servants, not your patrons. The hierarchy runs not from apostle down to community but from God through Christ to the community, with apostles included among the community's possessions.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 5):</hi> Paul transitions from the factionalism crisis to a specific case of sexual immorality: "It is actually reported that there is sexual immorality among you, and sexual immorality of such a kind as does not exist even among the Gentiles, namely, that someone has his father's wife" (5:1). The case is extreme — a man cohabiting with his stepmother, a violation of Leviticus 18:8 ("You shall not uncover the nakedness of your father's wife") and Deuteronomy 22:30 (LXX 23:1) ("A man shall not take his father's wife"). Even the surrounding Gentile culture, which lacked the Torah's prohibitions, recognized this as transgressive. Roman law prohibited marriage between a stepson and stepmother.</p>
          <p>The critical link between chapters 4 and 5 is the word <hi type="italic">phusioo</hi> ("to puff up, to inflate, to make arrogant"). Paul used it in 4:6 ("that no one of you will become arrogant [<hi type="italic">physiothe</hi>] in behalf of one against the other"), 4:18 ("Now some have become arrogant [<hi type="italic">ephysiothesan</hi>]"), and 4:19 ("the words of those who are arrogant [<hi type="italic">pephysiomenon</hi>]"). In 5:2, the same word reappears: "You have become arrogant [<hi type="italic">pephysiomenoi</hi>] and have not mourned instead." The arrogance that Paul diagnosed as the root of factionalism in chapters 1-4 is the same arrogance that now prevents the community from dealing with open sin. They are "puffed up" — inflated with self-importance about their spiritual status, their wisdom, their gifts — and this inflation has made them incapable both of properly evaluating their teachers (the faction problem) and of exercising moral discernment within the community (the immorality problem). The disease is the same; chapter 5 is a new symptom.</p>
          <p>Paul's response in chapter 5 also demonstrates exactly what he claimed in chapter 4. In 4:19-21, he warned that he was coming and would test not words but power, and asked whether the Corinthians wanted him to come "with a rod." In 5:3-5, he exercises that authority: "For I, on my part, though absent in body but present in spirit, have already judged him who has so committed this." The apostolic authority Paul asserted in chapter 4 is immediately deployed in chapter 5. The father who warned his children (4:14-15) now disciplines his household.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 4 connects these:</hi> Chapter 4 is the conclusion of the entire argument that began in 1:10. Chapters 1-3 established the theological foundation: the cross overturns human wisdom (ch. 1), the Spirit provides a different kind of wisdom inaccessible to natural faculties (ch. 2), and the Corinthians' factionalism proves they are operating by the world's criteria rather than the Spirit's (ch. 3). Chapter 4 now draws the practical conclusions. It does four things: (1) it defines how the Corinthians should regard apostles — as servants and stewards, not as competing patrons to be ranked (vv. 1-5); (2) it reveals that Paul has been using himself and Apollos as illustrations throughout, and commands the Corinthians not to go "beyond what is written" in their evaluations (vv. 6-7); (3) it deploys devastating irony to expose the Corinthians' pretensions by contrasting their self-satisfied condition with the apostles' actual suffering (vv. 8-13); and (4) it shifts from irony to paternal appeal, asserting Paul's unique relationship to the community as their father and warning the arrogant that he is coming (vv. 14-21). The chapter thus bridges the theological argument (chs. 1-3) and the specific disciplinary action (ch. 5) by establishing Paul's authority and the Corinthians' accountability.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.4.1-1Cor.4.5">
          <title>Verses 1-5: How to Regard Apostles — Servants, Stewards, and the Lord's Judgment</title>
          <p>"This is how one should regard us: as servants of Christ and stewards of the mysteries of God" (4:1).</p>
          <p>The opening word <hi type="italic">houtos</hi> ("thus, in this way, so") points backward to everything Paul has argued in chapters 1-3. The Corinthians have been regarding Paul and Apollos as competing wisdom-figures, ranking them by rhetorical skill and attaching themselves to one against another. Paul now prescribes how they should regard the apostles instead. The verb <hi type="italic">logizomai</hi> ("to reckon, to consider, to regard, to calculate") is a term of accounting and assessment — it describes the mental act of placing something in the correct category. The Corinthians have miscategorized the apostles. Paul tells them to recalculate.</p>
          <p>The first category is <hi type="italic">hyperetas Christou</hi> ("servants of Christ"). The word <hi type="italic">hyperetes</hi> is distinct from <hi type="italic">diakonos</hi> (the word Paul used in 3:5). A <hi type="italic">diakonos</hi> is a general servant or attendant. A <hi type="italic">hyperetes</hi> was originally an "under-rower" — one who rowed in the lower tier of a galley ship, taking direction from above. In first-century usage, it described an assistant, an attendant who carries out the orders of a superior: in the synagogue, the <hi type="italic">hyperetes</hi> was the attendant who handled the scrolls (Luke 4:20); in the courts, the <hi type="italic">hyperetes</hi> was the officer who carried out the judge's sentences (Matthew 5:25); in Acts 13:5, John Mark served as <hi type="italic">hyperetes</hi> to Paul and Barnabas. The word emphasizes subordination and instrumentality. The apostles are not independent authorities competing for followers; they are under-rowers taking direction from Christ. They have no independent standing that would justify competitive allegiance.</p>
          <p>The second category is <hi type="italic">oikonomous mysterion theou</hi> ("stewards of the mysteries of God"). The word <hi type="italic">oikonomos</hi> ("steward, household manager") describes the person entrusted with managing another's household or estate. In the LXX and in first-century Greco-Roman society, the <hi type="italic">oikonomos</hi> was typically a slave or freedman who managed the master's property, distributed provisions to the household, and was accountable to the owner for the management of resources that were never his own. The word appears in Jesus' parables about stewards who must give account (Luke 12:42-48; 16:1-8). The critical feature of the <hi type="italic">oikonomos</hi> is that he manages what belongs to someone else. The mysteries are God's, not Paul's. Paul dispenses them; he does not originate them.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">mysteria</hi> ("mysteries") connects directly to 2:7, where Paul spoke of "God's wisdom in a mystery" (<hi type="italic">theou sophian en mysterio</hi>) — the hidden plan of God, concealed from the rulers of this age, now revealed through the Spirit. The plural "mysteries" may indicate the various aspects of God's revealed plan — the gospel of the crucified Messiah, the inclusion of the Gentiles, the coming resurrection, the nature of the present age and the age to come. Paul and the other apostles are entrusted with dispensing these revealed secrets to the community. They are not the source of wisdom; they are its distributors. Evaluating them by their personal rhetorical skill is like evaluating a steward by his wardrobe rather than by whether he faithfully distributes his master's provisions.</p>
          <p>"In this case, moreover, it is required of stewards that one be found trustworthy" (4:2).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">pistos</hi> ("faithful, trustworthy, reliable") identifies the single criterion by which a steward is properly evaluated. Not eloquence. Not philosophical sophistication. Not rhetorical impressiveness. Trustworthiness. The verb <hi type="italic">heurisko</hi> ("to find, to discover") in the passive (<hi type="italic">heurethe</hi>, "be found") implies that the steward's faithfulness will be discovered upon examination — when the master returns and inspects the accounts. The only question that matters about a steward is: did he faithfully manage what was entrusted to him? This reframes the entire Corinthian evaluation. The Corinthians have been asking, "Which teacher is most impressive?" Paul says the only relevant question is, "Which steward is faithful to the master's trust?"</p>
          <p>"But to me it is an insignificant matter that I would be examined by you, or by any human court; in fact, I do not even examine myself" (4:3).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">anakrino</hi> ("to examine, to investigate, to judge, to evaluate") is the same word that dominated 2:14-15. There, Paul established two categories: the <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> person "does not accept the things of the Spirit" and cannot examine (<hi type="italic">anakrino</hi>) them; the <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi> person "examines all things but is himself examined by no one" (2:15). Now Paul applies that principle to his own case. The phrase <hi type="italic">eis elachiston estin</hi> ("it is a very small thing, it is insignificant") is emphatic — Paul treats the Corinthians' evaluation of him as a matter of negligible importance.</p>
          <p>Three courts of judgment are dismissed. First, "by you" (<hi type="italic">hyph' hymon</hi>) — the Corinthians' own assessment. They have been evaluating Paul and finding him less impressive than Apollos. Paul declares this evaluation irrelevant. Second, "by any human court" (<hi type="italic">hypo anthropines hemeras</hi>) — literally "by a human day," where <hi type="italic">hemera</hi> ("day") functions as a metonym for a day of judicial examination, a court session, a day of reckoning. Any human tribunal, any merely human standard of evaluation, is incapable of rendering a valid verdict on an apostle's faithfulness. Third, and most strikingly, "I do not even examine myself" (<hi type="italic">all' oude emauton anakrino</hi>). Paul declines to be his own judge. This is not a claim of perfection; it is a claim about jurisdiction. The question of whether Paul has been a faithful steward is not one that any human judgment — including his own — is competent to settle.</p>
          <p>"For I am not aware of anything against myself; however I am not vindicated by this, but the one who examines me is the Lord" (4:4).</p>
          <p>Paul's clean conscience (<hi type="italic">ouden gar emauto synoida</hi>, "I know nothing against myself" — the verb <hi type="italic">synoida</hi> means "to be conscious of, to share knowledge with oneself") does not constitute acquittal. The phrase <hi type="italic">all' ouk en touto dedikaiomei</hi> ("but I am not justified/vindicated by this") uses the perfect passive of <hi type="italic">dikaioo</hi> ("to justify, to declare righteous, to acquit"). Paul's self-examination, even when it finds nothing amiss, does not produce a verdict of acquittal. The reason: <hi type="italic">ho de anakrinon me kyrios estin</hi> — "the one who examines me is the Lord." The Lord is the only competent judge of an apostle's stewardship. Human courts lack the jurisdiction. Personal conscience lacks the reliability. Only the Lord possesses both the authority and the knowledge required for a valid assessment.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> ("Lord") here carries its full weight. In the argument of chapters 1-3, the <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> is the one whose mind the community possesses (2:16), the one who assigns roles to servants (3:5), the one whose building the community is (3:9). The Lord who commissioned the steward is the Lord who will evaluate the steward. No other evaluator is competent.</p>
          <p>"Therefore do not go on passing judgment before the time, but wait until the Lord comes, who will both bring to light the things hidden in the darkness and disclose the motives of human hearts; and then praise will come to each person from God" (4:5).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">hoste</hi> ("therefore, so then, consequently") draws a practical conclusion from the preceding argument. The imperative <hi type="italic">me krinete</hi> ("do not judge") with <hi type="italic">pro kairou</hi> ("before the time, before the appointed occasion") does not prohibit all judgment — Paul will exercise fierce judgment in chapter 5. It prohibits premature judgment, specifically the ranking and evaluating of apostles that the factionalism represents. The "time" (<hi type="italic">kairos</hi>) is specified: <hi type="italic">heos an elthe ho kyrios</hi> — "until the Lord comes." The verb <hi type="italic">erchomai</hi> ("to come") describes the Lord's future arrival, his <hi type="italic">parousia</hi> — the event the community is awaiting (1:7, where they "wait for the revealing of our Lord Jesus Christ").</p>
          <p>What the Lord will do at his coming is described with two verbs. First, <hi type="italic">photise ta krypta tou skotous</hi> — "he will illuminate the hidden things of darkness." The verb <hi type="italic">photizo</hi> ("to illuminate, to bring to light, to make visible") means to shine light on what is concealed in <hi type="italic">skotos</hi> ("darkness"). The hidden things are not necessarily evil; they are the concealed realities of each person's service — the true quality and motivation of their work, which cannot be assessed from external appearances. Second, <hi type="italic">phanerosei tas boulas ton kardion</hi> — "he will make manifest the purposes/deliberations of the hearts." The verb <hi type="italic">phaneroo</hi> ("to make manifest, to reveal, to make visible") and the noun <hi type="italic">boule</hi> ("purpose, intention, plan, counsel") together describe the exposure of interior motivation. The <hi type="italic">kardiai</hi> ("hearts") in Hebrew and LXX usage are the seat of will, intention, and deliberation — not merely emotion. The Lord will reveal what each person was actually trying to accomplish, what motivated their service, what they were building and why.</p>
          <p>The result: <hi type="italic">kai tote ho epainos genesetai hekasto apo tou theou</hi> — "and then the praise will come to each from God." The word <hi type="italic">epainos</hi> ("praise, commendation, approval") is striking. Paul does not say "then judgment" or "then punishment" but "then praise." The emphasis is on commendation from God as the only praise that matters. The Corinthians have been distributing praise according to their own preferences — praising Apollos for his eloquence, or Paul for his founding role, or Cephas for his apostolic seniority. Paul says: the only praise that counts will come from God, and it will come at the appointed time, not before.</p>
          <p>The implication for the factionalism is direct. The Corinthians' entire enterprise of evaluating and ranking teachers is doubly illegitimate: they lack the competence (only the Lord can judge) and they lack the timing (the judgment belongs to the future coming of the Lord). Their factionalism is premature, unauthorized judgment.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.4.6-1Cor.4.7">
          <title>Verses 6-7: The Application Revealed — Not Beyond What Is Written</title>
          <p>"Now these things, brothers and sisters, I have figuratively applied to myself and Apollos on your account, so that in us you may learn not to exceed what is written, so that no one of you will become arrogant in behalf of one against the other" (4:6).</p>
          <p>This verse is a key disclosure. The verb <hi type="italic">metaschematizo</hi> ("to transfer the form of, to apply figuratively, to transform the outward appearance of") means that Paul has taken a principle and repackaged it in terms of himself and Apollos as concrete examples. The word <hi type="italic">schema</hi> ("form, outward appearance, figure") is what has been transferred. Paul has used himself and Apollos as case studies — not because the problem is primarily about Paul and Apollos personally, but because by using himself and Apollos as examples, he could address the Corinthians' broader pattern of competitive allegiance to human leaders without directly naming those who are causing the most damage. The <hi type="italic">di' hymas</hi> ("on your account, for your benefit") confirms the pedagogical purpose.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">to me hyper ha gegraptai</hi> ("not beyond what is written") has generated extensive discussion, but within the context of Paul's argument its force is clear. Throughout chapters 1-3, Paul has repeatedly anchored his argument in Scripture: Isaiah 29:14 (quoted in 1:19), Jeremiah 9:24 (quoted in 1:31), Isaiah 64:4 (adapted in 2:9), Job 5:13 (quoted in 3:19), and Psalm 94:11 (quoted in 3:20). Every one of these scriptures taught the same lesson: God overturns human wisdom, catches the wise in their craftiness, knows that the thoughts of the wise are futile, and is the only legitimate object of boasting. "What is written" refers to these scriptural principles that Paul has been deploying throughout the argument. The command "not to go beyond" (<hi type="italic">me hyper</hi>) means: do not exceed the boundaries that Scripture itself has established for evaluating God's servants and distributing praise. Scripture says "let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord" (1:31, citing Jeremiah 9:24); the Corinthians are boasting in human leaders, going beyond what is written.</p>
          <p>The purpose clause <hi type="italic">hina me heis hyper tou henos physiosthe kata tou heterou</hi> ("so that no one of you will be puffed up in favor of one against the other") introduces the verb <hi type="italic">phusioo</hi> ("to inflate, to puff up, to make arrogant") for the first time in the letter. This word will become one of Paul's signature terms for the Corinthian disease. It appears in 4:6, 4:18, 4:19, 5:2, 8:1 ("knowledge puffs up, but love builds up"), and 13:4 ("love is not puffed up"). The image is of inflation — a balloon swollen with air rather than filled with substance. The Corinthians are inflated with self-importance about their preferred teacher, swollen with the honor they derive from their factional affiliation. The preposition <hi type="italic">hyper</hi> ("on behalf of, in favor of") with <hi type="italic">kata</hi> ("against") describes the partisan dynamic: puffed up for one teacher against another.</p>
          <p>"For who considers you as superior? What do you have that you did not receive? And if you did receive it, why do you boast as if you had not received it?" (4:7).</p>
          <p>Three rapid questions, each escalating in force.</p>
          <p>The first question — <hi type="italic">tis gar se diakrinei</hi> ("for who distinguishes you?" or "who considers you as superior?") — challenges the basis of the Corinthians' self-differentiation. The verb <hi type="italic">diakrino</hi> can mean "to distinguish, to differentiate, to set apart, to make a difference." Paul asks: who gave you the right to consider yourselves superior on the basis of your factional allegiance? If everything comes from God, what basis exists for one group to elevate itself over another?</p>
          <p>The second question — <hi type="italic">ti de echeis ho ouk elabes</hi> ("what do you have that you did not receive?") — is the devastating center of Paul's argument against boasting, and it connects directly to the thanksgiving of 1:4-7. There Paul affirmed that the Corinthians were "enriched in every way" and "not lacking in any gift." Everything they possess spiritually — their speech, their knowledge, their spiritual gifts, their very existence as a community — is a gift (<hi type="italic">charisma</hi>, from <hi type="italic">charizomai</hi>, "to give freely"). The verb <hi type="italic">lambano</hi> ("to receive") appeared in 2:12: "we have received not the spirit of the world, but the Spirit who is from God." The logic is airtight: if everything is received, nothing is earned, and therefore nothing provides grounds for boasting against another.</p>
          <p>The third question — <hi type="italic">ei de kai elabes, ti kauchase hos me labon</hi> ("and if you did receive it, why do you boast as if you had not received it?") — exposes the contradiction at the heart of the Corinthian behavior. To boast about a gift as though it were an achievement is to deny its character as gift. The Corinthians are treating their spiritual endowments — including whatever benefits they gained from association with Paul or Apollos — as personal accomplishments that elevate them over other believers. Paul says this is incoherent. You cannot simultaneously acknowledge that something is a gift and boast about it as if you earned it. The act of boasting converts grace into merit and thereby falsifies the entire transaction.</p>
          <p>This verse also closes a circle begun in 1:31. There Paul quoted Jeremiah 9:24: "Let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord." Now he identifies why the Corinthians cannot legitimately boast in anything except the Lord: everything they have was received. The exclusion of boasting is not an arbitrary command; it follows logically from the nature of grace.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.4.8-1Cor.4.13">
          <title>Verses 8-13: The Ironic Contrast — The Corinthians' Self-Satisfaction and the Apostles' Suffering</title>
          <p>"You are already filled, you have already become rich, you have become kings without us; and indeed, I wish that you had become kings so that we also might reign with you!" (4:8).</p>
          <p>The transition is abrupt and the tone shifts to biting irony. Paul employs three words that describe the Corinthians' self-assessment, each in the aorist tense, indicating their presumed completed achievement.</p>
          <p>First: <hi type="italic">ede kekoresmenoi este</hi> ("you are already filled, already satiated"). The verb <hi type="italic">korennymi</hi> ("to satiate, to fill to satisfaction, to gorge") describes the condition of someone who has eaten to the point of complete satisfaction. In the LXX, the word appears in contexts of fullness and abundance: Deuteronomy 31:20, "when they have eaten and are satisfied and grown fat"; Psalm 17:14 (LXX 16:14), where the wicked are "satisfied with children." The Corinthians consider themselves spiritually satiated — they need nothing more.</p>
          <p>Second: <hi type="italic">ede eploutesate</hi> ("you have already become rich"). The verb <hi type="italic">plouteo</hi> ("to become rich, to be wealthy") echoes the language of 1:5, where Paul said they were "enriched" (<hi type="italic">eploutisthete</hi>) in every way. But there Paul attributed the enrichment to God's grace; here the Corinthians have claimed the wealth as their own status marker.</p>
          <p>Third: <hi type="italic">choris hemon ebasileusate</hi> ("without us you have become kings" or "you have reigned"). The verb <hi type="italic">basileuo</hi> ("to reign, to rule as king") is the most pointed term. The Corinthians consider themselves to have already entered into the fullness of the kingdom — they are already reigning. The phrase <hi type="italic">choris hemon</hi> ("without us, apart from us") carries multiple edges. It may mean "without waiting for us," suggesting they have raced ahead of the apostles into a state of spiritual completion; or "without needing us," suggesting they have outgrown their teachers; or both. The irony is devastating: the community considers itself to have arrived at the consummation while the apostles who brought them the gospel are still suffering.</p>
          <p>The exclamation <hi type="italic">kai ophelon ge ebasileusate, hina kai hemeis hymin symbasileusomen</hi> ("and I wish that you had indeed become kings, so that we also might reign with you!") contains a wish contrary to fact. The particle <hi type="italic">ophelon</hi> expresses an unfulfilled wish. Paul wishes the Corinthians really had entered the kingdom's fullness — not because their self-assessment is correct, but because if it were true, the apostles would share in that reign. The implication is clear: the apostles are not reigning, and therefore neither are the Corinthians. The kingdom's fullness has not yet arrived. The Corinthians have confused their present spiritual gifts with the consummation. They are living as though the "not yet" has already become "already" — as though the resurrection glory is a present possession rather than a future hope. Paul will address this confusion at length in chapter 15, where he will argue for the bodily resurrection that has not yet occurred. The Corinthians' realized eschatology — their sense that they have already arrived at spiritual completion — is the theological root of many of their problems.</p>
          <p>"For I think, God has exhibited us, the apostles, last of all as men condemned to death, because we have become a spectacle to the world, both to angels and to mankind" (4:9).</p>
          <p>The conjunction <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") introduces the evidence that contradicts the Corinthians' self-assessment. If the kingdom had fully arrived, the apostles would not be suffering. Paul uses the verb <hi type="italic">apodeiknymi</hi> ("to display, to exhibit, to put on show") — the same root as <hi type="italic">apodeixis</hi> ("demonstration") in 2:4, where Paul's preaching was "in demonstration of the Spirit and of power." Now God's demonstration takes a different form: he displays the apostles as <hi type="italic">epithanatious</hi> ("condemned to death, sentenced to die, doomed to execution"). The word describes someone who has received a death sentence and is being paraded to execution.</p>
          <p>The image is drawn from the Roman triumphal procession (<hi type="italic">pompa triumphalis</hi>), in which conquered enemies and condemned prisoners were displayed publicly as they were led to their deaths in the arena. The general rode in glory at the front; the captives stumbled in chains at the rear. Paul says God has placed the apostles "last of all" (<hi type="italic">eschatous</hi>) — at the very end of the procession, in the position of maximum humiliation. The apostles are not the triumphant generals; they are the condemned captives.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">theatron</hi> ("spectacle, theater") confirms the public-display imagery. The apostles have been made a theatrical exhibition — put on stage for the cosmos to watch. The audience is comprehensive: <hi type="italic">to kosmo kai angelois kai anthropois</hi> ("to the world, both to angels and to people"). The scope is universal. The apostles' suffering is visible to every order of being — heavenly and earthly, angelic and human. The world watches the spectacle of men who are condemned, suffering, and dying — and this, Paul says, is how God displays his apostles. The contrast with the Corinthians' self-image as reigning kings could not be sharper.</p>
          <p>"We are fools on account of Christ, but you are prudent in Christ! We are weak, but you are strong! You are distinguished, but we are without honor!" (4:10).</p>
          <p>Paul constructs three antitheses, each placing the apostles and the Corinthians on opposite sides of the categories that have dominated the argument since chapter 1.</p>
          <p>First: <hi type="italic">hemeis moroi dia Christon, hymeis de phronimoi en Christo</hi> — "we are fools for Christ's sake, but you are prudent/wise in Christ." The word <hi type="italic">moroi</hi> ("fools") is the adjective form of <hi type="italic">moria</hi> ("foolishness"), the word Paul used in 1:18 ("the word of the cross is foolishness"), 1:21 ("the foolishness of the proclamation"), 1:23 ("foolishness to Gentiles"), and 1:25 ("the foolishness of God"). The apostles embody the very foolishness that Paul has been arguing is God's chosen instrument. The word <hi type="italic">phronimoi</hi> ("prudent, wise, sensible") is used with a sting — the Corinthians consider themselves sophisticated and discerning, "wise in Christ," while their teachers are treated as fools.</p>
          <p>Second: <hi type="italic">hemeis astheneis, hymeis de ischyroi</hi> — "we are weak, but you are strong." The word <hi type="italic">astheneis</hi> ("weak") echoes 1:25 ("the weakness of God is stronger than men"), 1:27 ("God chose what is weak to shame the strong"), and 2:3 ("I was with you in weakness"). The apostles embody the weakness that Paul argued is God's instrument of power. The Corinthians consider themselves <hi type="italic">ischyroi</hi> ("strong, powerful") — robust, secure, lacking nothing.</p>
          <p>Third: <hi type="italic">hymeis endoxoi, hemeis de atimoi</hi> — "you are distinguished/honored, but we are without honor/dishonored." The word <hi type="italic">endoxos</hi> ("glorious, distinguished, held in esteem") and <hi type="italic">atimos</hi> ("without honor, despised, dishonored") describe social status. The Corinthians have achieved social respectability in their own eyes; the apostles have none.</p>
          <p>The irony cuts in multiple directions. On one level, Paul is simply exposing the absurdity of the Corinthians' position: they claim to be wise, strong, and honored while the apostles who brought them the gospel are fools, weak, and dishonored. On another level, Paul is deploying the categories of chapters 1-2 to show which group more closely resembles the pattern of the cross. The cross works through foolishness, weakness, and dishonor (1:23-25, 27-28). The apostles — fools, weak, dishonored — conform to the cross's pattern. The Corinthians — wise, strong, honored — conform to the world's pattern. The question is left implicit but devastating: whose condition reflects the gospel, and whose contradicts it?</p>
          <p>"Up to this present hour we are both hungry and thirsty, and are poorly clothed and roughly treated and homeless; and we labor, working with our own hands" (4:11-12a).</p>
          <p>Paul now moves from irony to plain description. The present tense (<hi type="italic">achri tes arti horas</hi>, "up to the present hour") indicates that this is not past history but current reality. The apostles' condition is described in concrete, physical terms: <hi type="italic">peinomen</hi> ("we hunger"), <hi type="italic">dipsomen</hi> ("we thirst"), <hi type="italic">gymniteumen</hi> ("we are poorly clothed, we are exposed"), <hi type="italic">kolaphizometha</hi> ("we are struck, beaten, roughly treated" — the verb <hi type="italic">kolaphizo</hi> means literally "to strike with the fist"), <hi type="italic">astatoumen</hi> ("we are homeless, without a settled dwelling").</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ergazomenoi tais idiais chersin</hi> ("working with our own hands") refers to Paul's practice of manual labor — his tentmaking, described in Acts 18:3. In Greco-Roman culture, manual labor was generally despised by the educated classes. Philosophers and rhetoricians were supported by patrons; working with one's hands was the mark of low social status. For a teacher to support himself by manual labor was, in the eyes of Corinthian society, evidence of inferiority. The Corinthians may have been embarrassed by Paul's self-support, preferring the model of Apollos or other teachers who may have accepted patronage. Paul's insistence on manual labor is part of the pattern of apostolic weakness that the Corinthians evaluate by the wrong criteria.</p>
          <p>"When we are verbally abused, we bless; when we are persecuted, we endure it; when we are slandered, we reply as friends. We have become as the scum of the world, the dregs of all things, even until now" (4:12b-13).</p>
          <p>Three responses to mistreatment, each following the pattern of returning good for evil: <hi type="italic">loidoroumenoi eulogoumen</hi> ("being reviled, we bless"), <hi type="italic">diokomenoi anechometha</hi> ("being persecuted, we endure"), <hi type="italic">dysphemoumenoi parakaloumen</hi> ("being slandered, we appeal/encourage/entreat"). This triad echoes the teaching attributed to Jesus: "Bless those who curse you, pray for those who mistreat you" (Luke 6:28); "Love your enemies, do good to those who hate you" (Luke 6:27). Paul's response to suffering is not retaliation but blessing — the apostles embody the ethic of the cross in their daily lives.</p>
          <p>The concluding description is the most extreme: <hi type="italic">hos perikatharma tou kosmou egenetheemen, panton peripsema heos arti</hi> — "we have become as the scum of the world, the dregs of all things, until now." The word <hi type="italic">perikatharma</hi> ("that which is removed by cleansing, scum, filth, offscouring") has a specific background. In Greek usage, <hi type="italic">perikalharma</hi> could refer to a human scapegoat — a person of low social standing who was ritually expelled from a city during times of plague or disaster, theoretically absorbing the community's pollution. The related term <hi type="italic">peripsema</hi> ("what is scraped off, scrapings, refuse, offscouring") carries a similar sense of what is discarded after cleansing. In Tobit 5:19 (some manuscripts), the word appears in the sense of a ransom or substitute. Proverbs 21:18 (LXX) uses <hi type="italic">perikatharma</hi> for the wicked person who serves as a "ransom" for the righteous.</p>
          <p>Paul applies both terms to the apostles: they are the refuse of the world, the scrapings of all things. The language of scapegoat and ritual pollution is not accidental. The apostles occupy the position of those cast out for the sake of others — they absorb contempt, suffer deprivation, and are treated as disposable. This is the condition of the people the Corinthians have been evaluating and ranking as though they were competing rhetoricians.</p>
          <p>The contrast between 4:8 and 4:11-13 is the heart of Paul's argument in this section. The Corinthians are satiated, rich, and reigning. The apostles are hungry, homeless, and treated as garbage. If the gospel operates through weakness and apparent foolishness (as Paul argued in chapters 1-2), which group's condition reflects the gospel's pattern? The question answers itself. The Corinthians' comfortable self-assessment is not evidence of spiritual maturity; it is evidence that they have adopted the world's values, the very wisdom "of this age" that Paul has been dismantling since 1:18.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.4.14-1Cor.4.17">
          <title>Verses 14-17: The Father's Appeal and the Sending of Timothy</title>
          <p>"I do not write these things to shame you, but to admonish you as my beloved children" (4:14).</p>
          <p>The shift in tone is abrupt. Paul moves from devastating irony to tender affection. The verb <hi type="italic">entrepo</hi> ("to shame, to put to shame, to cause to turn inward in embarrassment") describes what Paul says he is not doing. The verb <hi type="italic">noutheteo</hi> ("to admonish, to warn, to instruct, to put into one's mind") describes what he is doing. The distinction matters: shaming aims to humiliate and diminish; admonishment aims to correct and redirect. Paul's purpose is not to crush the Corinthians but to redirect them. The phrase <hi type="italic">hos tekna mou agapeta</hi> ("as my beloved children") introduces a new relational category. Throughout chapters 1-3, Paul has been arguing about apostles and the community's relationship to them. Now he specifies the nature of his own relationship: he is their father.</p>
          <p>"For if you were to have countless tutors in Christ, yet you would not have many fathers, for in Christ Jesus I became your father through the gospel" (4:15).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">paidagogous</hi> ("tutors, guardians, child-attendants") is specific. A <hi type="italic">paidagogos</hi> in the first-century Greco-Roman world was not a teacher but a household slave who supervised a child's daily life — walking the child to school, overseeing behavior, providing discipline and protection. The <hi type="italic">paidagogos</hi> was a temporary figure in a child's development; the father was permanent. Paul uses the same word in Galatians 3:24-25, where the Torah served as a <hi type="italic">paidagogos</hi> "until Christ came." The <hi type="italic">paidagogos</hi> is useful but temporary, secondary to the parent who generated the child.</p>
          <p>Paul's claim is that whatever number of subsequent instructors the Corinthians may have (<hi type="italic">myrioi</hi>, "ten thousand, countless" — the word is hyperbolic), they have only one father. The phrase <hi type="italic">ego hymas egennesa</hi> ("I fathered you, I begot you") uses the verb <hi type="italic">gennao</hi> ("to beget, to give birth to, to generate"). The means of this generation is specified: <hi type="italic">en Christo Iesou dia tou euangeliou</hi> ("in Christ Jesus through the gospel"). Paul did not beget them in his own name or through his own wisdom; he begot them in Christ, through the instrumentality of the gospel message. The paternity is real but derivative — Paul is the human agent; the gospel is the instrument; Christ is the sphere.</p>
          <p>This claim carries weight for the factionalism argument. The Corinthians have been treating their teachers as interchangeable competitors — Paul, Apollos, and Cephas as rival brands. Paul says the relationship is not interchangeable. Other teachers are <hi type="italic">paidagogoi</hi> — useful, perhaps numerous, but not constitutive. Paul is the father — the one through whom their communal existence originated. This does not make Paul superior to Apollos in general; it makes his relationship to this particular community unique and non-transferable. The argument is not about hierarchical ranking of apostles but about the specific, unrepeatable relationship of originator to community.</p>
          <p>"Therefore I urge you, be imitators of me" (4:16).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">mimetai</hi> ("imitators") from <hi type="italic">mimeomai</hi> ("to imitate, to follow the pattern of") calls the Corinthians to conform their lives to Paul's pattern. Given the preceding context (4:9-13), what Paul is asking them to imitate is clear: the apostolic pattern of weakness, suffering, self-denial, and blessing in the face of abuse. He is not asking them to imitate his personal style or rhetorical approach (he has just spent three chapters arguing that rhetorical style is irrelevant). He is asking them to imitate the pattern of the cross as it is lived out in apostolic existence — the willingness to be fools, weak, dishonored, hungry, and homeless for the sake of Christ. This is the antidote to the Corinthians' self-satisfaction: not more knowledge, not better teacher-selection, but the adoption of a cruciform way of life.</p>
          <p>Paul will make this even more explicit in 11:1: "Be imitators of me, as I am of Christ." The chain of imitation runs from Christ through Paul to the community. Paul imitates Christ's pattern of self-giving; the Corinthians are to imitate Paul's enactment of that same pattern.</p>
          <p>"For this reason I have sent to you Timothy, who is my beloved and faithful child in the Lord, and he will remind you of my ways which are in Christ, just as I teach everywhere in every church" (4:17).</p>
          <p>Paul has sent Timothy as his representative. Timothy is described as <hi type="italic">mou teknon agapeton kai piston en kyrio</hi> ("my beloved and faithful child in the Lord") — the same parental language Paul just used for the Corinthians (<hi type="italic">tekna mou agapeta</hi> in v. 14). Timothy is Paul's child in the same sense that the Corinthians are — through the gospel. But Timothy is also <hi type="italic">pistos</hi> ("faithful") — the very quality that Paul identified as the requirement for stewards in verse 2. Timothy is a faithful child; the question is whether the Corinthians are.</p>
          <p>Timothy's assignment is to <hi type="italic">hypomnese</hi> ("remind" — from <hi type="italic">hypomimnesko</hi>, "to remind, to call to mind, to bring to remembrance") the Corinthians of <hi type="italic">tas hodous mou tas en Christo</hi> ("my ways which are in Christ"). The word <hi type="italic">hodoi</hi> ("ways, paths, manner of life") describes Paul's pattern of conduct — his practical way of living the gospel. The same word appears in Acts 9:2, where the early movement is called "the Way" (<hi type="italic">he hodos</hi>). The phrase <hi type="italic">kathos pantachou en pase ekklesia didasko</hi> ("just as I teach everywhere in every church") indicates that Paul's ways are not peculiar to his relationship with Corinth; they are universal. What he teaches the Corinthians is what he teaches everywhere. The Corinthians are not a special case requiring unique treatment; they are a community that has deviated from the universal standard.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.4.18-1Cor.4.21">
          <title>Verses 18-21: Warning to the Arrogant — Words versus Power</title>
          <p>"Now some have become arrogant, as though I were not coming to you" (4:18).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">phusioo</hi> ("to puff up, to inflate") reappears — <hi type="italic">ephysiothesan tines</hi> ("some have been puffed up"). The "some" (<hi type="italic">tines</hi>) are unnamed individuals within the community who have become inflated with self-importance, presumably the leaders or instigators of the factions. The clause <hi type="italic">hos me erchomenou mou pros hymas</hi> ("as though I were not coming to you") reveals their assumption: they believe Paul will not come in person and that they can therefore behave with impunity. The sending of Timothy (v. 17) may have emboldened this assumption — they may have concluded that Timothy was Paul's substitute, not his advance guard. Paul's words are a warning: Timothy's arrival does not mean Paul's absence is permanent.</p>
          <p>"But I will come to you soon, if the Lord wills, and I shall find out, not the words of those who are arrogant, but their power" (4:19).</p>
          <p>Paul asserts his intention to come (<hi type="italic">eleusomai de tacheoos pros hymas</hi>, "I will come to you soon"), qualified by <hi type="italic">ean ho kyrios thelese</hi> ("if the Lord wills") — the same Lord who is the only competent judge (v. 4) and who will come at the appointed time (v. 5). Paul does not claim autonomous control over his movements; the Lord determines the timing.</p>
          <p>When Paul arrives, he will <hi type="italic">gnosomai</hi> ("know, find out, learn") not <hi type="italic">ton logon ton pephysiomenon</hi> ("the word/talk of those who are puffed up") but <hi type="italic">ten dynamin</hi> ("the power"). The contrast between <hi type="italic">logos</hi> ("word, speech, talk") and <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> ("power") reaches back to the foundations of the entire argument. In 1:17, the cross was emptied by <hi type="italic">sophia logou</hi> ("wisdom of speech"); in 1:18, the word of the cross is <hi type="italic">dynamis theou</hi> ("the power of God"); in 2:4, Paul's proclamation was "not in persuasive words of wisdom but in demonstration of the Spirit and of power (<hi type="italic">dynamis</hi>)"; in 2:5, faith should rest on <hi type="italic">dynamis theou</hi>, not <hi type="italic">sophia anthropon</hi>. Throughout, Paul has distinguished between impressive speech (which the Corinthians value) and actual power (which God provides through the cross and Spirit).</p>
          <p>The arrogant Corinthians may have impressive <hi type="italic">logoi</hi> — eloquent speech, sophisticated arguments, displays of knowledge. Paul says he will ignore all of that and test their <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> — the actual power of God at work in their lives. If the kingdom operates through power, not words, then the test of any person's standing is not what they say but what God is doing through them.</p>
          <p>"For the kingdom of God is not in words, but in power" (4:20).</p>
          <p>This compressed statement — <hi type="italic">ou gar en logo he basileia tou theou all' en dynamei</hi> — is the principle that underlies Paul's entire evaluation. The phrase <hi type="italic">basileia tou theou</hi> ("kingdom of God") appears here for the first time in 1 Corinthians (it will appear again in 6:9-10, 15:24, and 15:50). The kingdom of God — God's reign, God's sovereign activity, the sphere where God's will is operative — does not consist in <hi type="italic">logos</hi> ("speech, words, rhetorical performance") but in <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> ("power, effective force, operative energy"). The Corinthians have been evaluating everything — their teachers, their spiritual status, their factional allegiances — by words. Paul says the kingdom is elsewhere. It is in the power that transforms lives, that sustains apostles through suffering, that forms communities out of nobodies, that works through the crucified Messiah. Words without power are empty performance. The arrogant Corinthians may have the words; Paul will come to test whether they have the power.</p>
          <p>"What do you desire? That I come to you with a rod, or with love and a spirit of gentleness?" (4:21).</p>
          <p>The closing question offers two alternatives. The word <hi type="italic">rhabdos</hi> ("rod, staff") is an instrument of discipline. In the LXX, the rod appears as a tool of correction: Proverbs 13:24, "He who spares his rod hates his son, but he who loves him is diligent to discipline him"; Proverbs 22:15, "Foolishness is bound up in the heart of a child; the rod of discipline will remove it far from him"; Proverbs 23:13-14, "Do not withhold discipline from a child; if you strike him with the rod, he will not die." The rod is a father's instrument. Paul has just identified himself as the Corinthians' father (v. 15). The question is therefore parental, not tyrannical: a father may come with correction or with tenderness, depending on the child's behavior.</p>
          <p>The alternative is <hi type="italic">en agape pneumati te praytetos</hi> ("with love and a spirit of gentleness"). The word <hi type="italic">praiotes</hi> ("gentleness, meekness, mildness") describes a disposition of restraint and kindness. Paul's preference is clearly the second option. But the choice belongs to the Corinthians: their behavior between now and his arrival will determine which mode of fatherly presence they experience.</p>
          <p>The question also transitions to chapter 5. Paul will, in fact, exercise the rod — not against the Corinthians as a whole, but against the specific case of the man living with his father's wife. The father who offered gentleness will be compelled by the severity of the case to act with authority. Chapter 5 thus answers the question of 4:21: the Corinthians' failure to deal with the immoral man forces Paul to come, in effect, with the rod.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.4">
          <title>Summary of the Argument's Movement</title>
          <p>Chapter 4 concludes the four-chapter unit (1 Corinthians 1-4) that addresses the Corinthian factionalism, and it does so by moving through four stages:</p>
          <p>First (vv. 1-5): Paul establishes how apostles should be evaluated — not by rhetorical skill or social prestige but solely by faithfulness to their role as servants and stewards. He then removes the evaluation from human hands entirely. No human court, not even the apostle's own conscience, is competent to render a verdict. Only the Lord, at his coming, will judge with the necessary knowledge of hidden motives. The Corinthians' premature ranking of teachers is therefore doubly illegitimate: wrong criteria and wrong timing.</p>
          <p>Second (vv. 6-7): Paul reveals that he has been using himself and Apollos as pedagogical illustrations, and commands the Corinthians not to go beyond the scriptural principles he has cited throughout the argument. The devastating question of verse 7 — "What do you have that you did not receive?" — eliminates all grounds for competitive boasting. Everything is gift. Boasting about gifts as though they were achievements contradicts the nature of grace itself.</p>
          <p>Third (vv. 8-13): Paul deploys sustained irony to expose the gap between the Corinthians' self-image and the apostles' actual condition. The Corinthians are satiated, rich, and reigning; the apostles are hungry, homeless, and treated as refuse. The irony is not mere mockery; it forces the question of which condition reflects the pattern of the cross. If God's power operates through weakness, foolishness, and dishonor (as Paul argued in chapters 1-2), then the apostles' suffering confirms the gospel's pattern while the Corinthians' comfort-seeking confirms the world's pattern. The Corinthians have adopted a realized eschatology — acting as though the kingdom has already fully arrived — while the apostles live in the reality of the "not yet," bearing in their bodies the marks of the present age's hostility to the gospel.</p>
          <p>Fourth (vv. 14-21): Paul shifts from irony to paternal appeal, claiming a unique relationship to the community as their father through the gospel. He sends Timothy to remind them of his ways and warns the arrogant that he is coming personally. The final contrast — "the kingdom of God is not in words but in power" — recapitulates the entire argument of chapters 1-4 in a single sentence. The Corinthians have been operating in the realm of words — evaluating by eloquence, boasting in human leaders, inflated with self-assessed wisdom. The kingdom operates in the realm of power — the power of the cross, the power of the Spirit, the power that sustains apostles through suffering and forms communities out of the world's refuse. Paul's closing question — rod or gentleness? — places the responsibility on the Corinthians and transitions to the disciplinary action of chapter 5.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">phusioo</hi> ("puffed up") serves as the chapter's diagnostic thread. It appears three times (vv. 6, 18, 19) and will reappear in 5:2. This inflation — self-importance derived from factional allegiance, spiritual gifts, and worldly wisdom — is the disease that produces both the factionalism of chapters 1-4 and the moral failure of chapter 5. The cure Paul prescribes is the opposite of inflation: the pattern of the cross, embodied in apostolic suffering, which deflates all human pretension and grounds the community's life in the power of God rather than the words of human beings.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.5">
        <title>1 Corinthians 5 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.5">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 3-4):</hi> After establishing in chapters 1-2 that God's wisdom operates through the cross rather than through human rhetorical skill, and that the Spirit provides the only adequate faculty for spiritual discernment, Paul drew the devastating application in chapter 3: the Corinthians are not behaving as <hi type="italic">pneumatikoi</hi> (spiritual people) but as <hi type="italic">sarkinoi</hi> (fleshly people), as <hi type="italic">nepioi</hi> (infants) in Christ (3:1). The proof is their factionalism: "when one says, 'I am of Paul,' and another, 'I am of Apollos,' are you not merely human?" (3:4). Paul reframed the relationship between teachers and community: Paul planted, Apollos watered, but God gave the growth (3:6). Neither planter nor waterer is anything; God is the one who matters (3:7). The Corinthians are "God's field, God's building" (3:9) — they belong to God, not to any teacher.</p>
          <p>Paul then introduced the building metaphor. He laid a foundation — Jesus Christ — and others build on it, but each builder's work will be tested by fire on "the day" (3:13). The quality of the materials matters: gold, silver, and precious stones survive; wood, hay, and straw burn. A builder whose work survives receives a reward; a builder whose work burns "will suffer loss, though he himself will be saved, but only as through fire" (3:15). Then came a solemn warning of a different order: "Do you not know that you are God's temple and that God's Spirit dwells in you? If anyone destroys God's temple, God will destroy him. For God's temple is holy, and you are that temple" (3:16-17). The word <hi type="italic">naos</hi> ("temple, shrine, inner sanctuary") identifies the community as the dwelling place of God's Spirit. The warning is symmetrical and severe: destroying God's temple results in God's destroying the destroyer. The holiness of the temple is not negotiable.</p>
          <p>Chapter 3 concluded by returning to the boasting theme: "So let no one boast in human beings. For all things are yours, whether Paul or Apollos or Cephas or the world or life or death or the present or the future — all are yours, and you are Christ's, and Christ is God's" (3:21-23). Everything belongs to the community because they belong to Christ, who belongs to God. The hierarchy is clear and the direction of belonging is upward, not lateral.</p>
          <p>Chapter 4 developed the role of apostles as "servants of Christ and stewards of God's mysteries" (4:1). The key obligation of stewards is faithfulness (4:2). Paul declared his indifference to the Corinthians' judgment: "It is the Lord who judges me" (4:4). He warned them not to judge "before the time, before the Lord comes, who will bring to light the things now hidden in darkness and will disclose the purposes of the heart" (4:5). He then drew a sharp contrast between the Corinthians' self-satisfaction and the apostles' suffering. The Corinthians are "already satisfied," "already rich," "reigning as kings" (4:8) — dripping with irony. Meanwhile the apostles are "last of all, like men sentenced to death" (4:9), "fools for Christ's sake" while the Corinthians are "wise in Christ," "weak" while they are "strong," "held in disrepute" while they are "held in honor" (4:10). The apostles are hungry, thirsty, poorly dressed, beaten, homeless, reviled, persecuted, slandered (4:11-13). Paul does not say this to shame them but to "admonish" them as "my beloved children" (4:14). He is their father in Christ Jesus through the gospel (4:15) and urges them to imitate him (4:16).</p>
          <p>The chapter closes with a direct warning. Paul is sending Timothy, but he himself will come soon, "if the Lord wills" (4:19). "And I will find out not the talk of these arrogant people but their power" (4:19). The word <hi type="italic">phusioo</hi> ("to puff up, to inflate, to make arrogant") appears here and has appeared throughout chapter 4 (4:6, 4:18, 4:19). Paul's final question: "What do you wish? Shall I come to you with a rod, or with love and a spirit of gentleness?" (4:21). The "rod" (<hi type="italic">rhabdos</hi>) is the instrument of discipline. Paul leaves the choice to them.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 6):</hi> Chapter 6 begins with another case of community failure: "When one of you has a grievance against another, does he dare go to law before the unrighteous instead of the saints?" (6:1). The community members are taking disputes before pagan courts instead of resolving them internally. Paul asks: "Do you not know that the saints will judge the world?" (6:2). If they are destined to judge the world and even angels (6:3), they are certainly competent to judge "trivial cases" (6:2). He tells them to "appoint as judges even those of no standing in the assembly" (6:4) — even the least among them would be better than pagan judges for internal matters. "I say this to your shame. Can it be that there is no one among you wise enough to settle a dispute between the brothers?" (6:5). The connection to chapter 5 is direct: in chapter 5 the community failed to exercise internal discipline when it should have; in 6:1-8 the community goes to external courts when it should handle matters internally. Both failures reveal the same disease — the community does not understand the boundary between inside and outside, between the assembly and the world.</p>
          <p>Chapter 6 then shifts to sexual immorality (6:12-20), connecting directly back to the <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> of chapter 5. Paul addresses the slogan "all things are lawful for me" (6:12) — apparently a phrase the Corinthians used to justify their behavior — and counters: "but not all things are beneficial" and "I will not be dominated by anything" (6:12). He argues that the body is not for <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> but "for the Lord, and the Lord for the body" (6:13). The body will be raised (6:14). The believers' bodies are "members of Christ" (6:15) — to unite Christ's members with a prostitute is to make Christ one flesh with her (6:15-16). "The one who joins himself to the Lord becomes one spirit with him" (6:17). "Flee from sexual immorality" (6:18). "Your body is a temple of the Holy Spirit within you, whom you have from God" (6:19). "You are not your own, for you were bought with a price. So glorify God in your body" (6:20). The individual body as temple (6:19) extends the communal body as temple (3:16-17). The <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> case of chapter 5 is the specific instance; chapter 6:12-20 provides the theological reasoning for why <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> is incompatible with community identity.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 5 connects to what precedes and follows:</hi> Chapter 5 is not a change of subject. It is the first concrete case demonstrating the disease Paul has been diagnosing since chapter 1. The factionalism, the arrogance, the worldly wisdom, the self-congratulation — all of these produced a community so inflated with its own spiritual status that it tolerated within itself a sexual arrangement that even pagans found unacceptable. The word <hi type="italic">phusioo</hi> ("puffed up"), which appeared four times in chapter 4 (4:6, 4:18, 4:19) to describe the Corinthians' arrogant self-regard, reappears in 5:2 as the precise diagnosis of their failure in this case: "And you are puffed up!" Their arrogance is not an abstract theological error; it has a concrete consequence — they have failed to act when action was required.</p>
          <p>The chapter also transitions the letter from the factionalism problem (chapters 1-4) to a series of specific behavioral failures (chapters 5-6). But the transition is organic, not abrupt. The specific cases are symptoms of the same root disease. A community that evaluates by worldly wisdom rather than by the Spirit's discernment, that boasts in human leaders rather than in the Lord, that considers itself spiritually rich and reigning as kings (4:8) — such a community will also fail to recognize when its own holiness has been violated. The arrogance that produces factions also produces tolerance of sin.</p>
          <p>Chapter 5 also sets up chapter 6 by establishing the principle of internal community discipline. The assembly is responsible for judging those inside (5:12). This principle, stated at the end of chapter 5, immediately generates the problem of chapter 6: the community that should judge its own members (chapter 5) is instead outsourcing its disputes to pagan courts (6:1-8). Both chapters address the community's failure to exercise the judgment that its identity requires.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.5.1-1Cor.5.2">
          <title>Verses 1-2: The Report and the Community's Failure</title>
          <p>"It is actually reported that there is sexual immorality among you, and of a kind that is not tolerated even among pagans, for a man has his father's wife" (5:1).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">holos</hi> ("actually, altogether, in general") intensifies the report. This is not rumor or speculation; the matter is widely known. The word <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> ("sexual immorality, fornication") is the broad term for illicit sexual conduct. In the LXX, <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> and its cognates translate the Hebrew <hi type="italic">zanah</hi> and its derivatives, covering a wide range of sexual transgressions that violate God's ordering of human relationships. The word will appear again in 6:13, 6:18, and 7:2, each time describing conduct incompatible with belonging to the Lord.</p>
          <p>Paul's description is precise: "a man has his father's wife" (<hi type="italic">gynaika tina tou patros echein</hi>). The present infinitive <hi type="italic">echein</hi> ("to have") indicates an ongoing arrangement, not a single act. The phrase "his father's wife" (<hi type="italic">tou patros gynaika</hi>) rather than "his mother" (<hi type="italic">meter</hi>) strongly implies a stepmother — the father's wife by a marriage other than the one that produced this man. The father may be dead or divorced; the text does not specify. What matters is the relationship: a man is maintaining a sexual relationship with a woman who is or was his father's wife.</p>
          <p>The Old Testament background is unmistakable and would have been immediately recognizable to any Jew or God-fearer in the Corinthian assembly. Leviticus 18:8 (LXX): "You shall not uncover the nakedness of your father's wife; it is your father's nakedness" (<hi type="italic">aschemosynen gynaikos patros sou ouk apokalypseis; aschemosynen patros sou estin</hi>). The phrase "uncover the nakedness" (<hi type="italic">apokalypsai aschemosynen</hi>) is the Levitical idiom for sexual intercourse with a forbidden partner. Leviticus 18:8 places this prohibition within the comprehensive list of forbidden sexual relationships in Leviticus 18, introduced by the warning: "You shall not do as they do in the land of Egypt, where you lived, and you shall not do as they do in the land of Canaan, to which I am bringing you" (18:3). The punishment prescribed is severe: "every person who does any of these abominations — the persons who do them shall be cut off from among their people" (Leviticus 18:29, LXX: <hi type="italic">exolethreuthesontai hai psychai hai poiousai</hi> — "the souls that do them shall be utterly destroyed/cut off").</p>
          <p>Deuteronomy 22:30 (23:1 in Hebrew) states it with stark brevity: "A man shall not take his father's wife, and he shall not uncover his father's covering" (<hi type="italic">ouk lepsetai anthropos gynaika tou patros autou kai ouk apokalypsei synkalymma tou patros autou</hi>). The metaphor of the "covering" (<hi type="italic">synkalymma</hi>, translating the Hebrew <hi type="italic">kanaph</hi>, "wing, skirt") represents the father's sexual claim and the relationship it constitutes. To take the father's wife is to violate the father's own boundary.</p>
          <p>Deuteronomy 27:20 includes this sin in the covenant curses pronounced at Mount Ebal: "Cursed be anyone who lies with his father's wife, because he has uncovered his father's nakedness" (<hi type="italic">epikataratos ho koimomenos meta gynaikos tou patros autou, hoti apokalypsai synkalymma tou patros autou</hi>). The assembly responds "Amen" — the entire community ratifies the curse.</p>
          <p>The Old Testament also records a narrative precedent. In Genesis 35:22, Reuben "went and lay with Bilhah his father's concubine." Jacob's response, recorded in Genesis 49:3-4 at his deathbed, was to strip Reuben of the firstborn's preeminence: "Unstable as water, you shall not have preeminence, because you went up to your father's bed; then you defiled it" (LXX: <hi type="italic">exebrasas hosper hydor, me ekzeseis; anebas gar epi ten koiten tou patros sou; tote emianas ten stronen hou anebas</hi>). Reuben lost his position as firstborn because of this act. The precedent demonstrates that taking a father's wife was considered a fundamental violation of familial order, with lasting consequences.</p>
          <p>Paul adds a comparison that sharpens the indictment: "of a kind that is not tolerated even among pagans" (<hi type="italic">hetis oude en tois ethnesin</hi>). The word <hi type="italic">ethnesin</hi> ("nations, Gentiles, pagans") refers to those outside the covenant community. Roman law prohibited marriage between a man and his stepmother. Cicero, in <hi type="italic">Pro Cluentio</hi> 5.14, prosecuted a case involving a woman (Sassia) who married her former son-in-law, calling it "an incredible act, unheard of in all experience save for this single instance" (<hi type="italic">incredibile et praeter hanc unam in omni vita inauditum</hi>). Gaius, in the <hi type="italic">Institutes</hi> (1.63), classified marriage with a stepmother among unions forbidden by Roman law. The point is devastating: the Corinthian community, which has been enriched with every spiritual gift (1:7), which possesses the mind of Christ (2:16), which is God's temple indwelt by the Spirit (3:16), is tolerating conduct that even the pagan world surrounding them finds unacceptable. The community that should exceed the moral standards of the world has fallen below them.</p>
          <p>"And you are arrogant! Ought you not rather to mourn? Let him who has done this be removed from among you" (5:2).</p>
          <p>"And you are arrogant" (<hi type="italic">kai hymeis pephysiomenoi este</hi>) — the verb <hi type="italic">phusioo</hi> ("to puff up, to inflate, to make arrogant") appears here in the perfect passive participle: "you have been puffed up and remain puffed up." The perfect tense indicates an established condition, not a momentary lapse. This is the same word that dominated chapter 4: "some are arrogant" (<hi type="italic">ephusothesan tines</hi>, 4:18), "the talk of these arrogant people" (<hi type="italic">ton pephusiomenon</hi>, 4:19). The disease Paul diagnosed abstractly in chapter 4 now has a concrete manifestation. The community's self-inflation has produced moral blindness. They are so convinced of their spiritual superiority that they cannot recognize — or cannot bring themselves to act upon — a case of flagrant sexual transgression in their midst.</p>
          <p>What should the community's response have been? Paul states it: "Ought you not rather to mourn?" (<hi type="italic">ouchi mallon epenthesate</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">pentheo</hi> ("to mourn, to grieve, to lament") describes the response appropriate to a death or a catastrophe. In the LXX, the word describes mourning for the dead (Genesis 50:3, 2 Samuel 11:27), lamentation over disaster (Ezra 10:6, Nehemiah 1:4), and grief over sin. In Ezra 10:6, Ezra "did not eat bread or drink water, for he was mourning over the faithlessness of the exiles" (<hi type="italic">epenthei epi te asyntheteia tes apoikesias</hi>). The appropriate response to sin within the community is grief, not indifference, and certainly not arrogance.</p>
          <p>The purpose of mourning is connected to action: "Let him who has done this be removed from among you" (<hi type="italic">hina arthe ek mesou hymon ho to ergon touto praxas</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">airo</hi> ("to remove, to take away, to lift up") with <hi type="italic">ek mesou hymon</hi> ("from your midst") echoes Deuteronomy's formula for purging evil from the community. The aorist subjunctive <hi type="italic">arthe</hi> indicates decisive action: the one who has done this thing (<hi type="italic">ho to ergon touto praxas</hi> — "the one having practiced this deed") is to be expelled. Paul uses the word <hi type="italic">ergon</hi> ("work, deed") rather than <hi type="italic">hamartia</hi> ("sin") or <hi type="italic">porneia</hi>, which may emphasize the deliberate, ongoing nature of the arrangement — this is something being practiced, not something that happened once.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.5.3-1Cor.5.5">
          <title>Verses 3-5: Paul's Judgment and the Assembly's Action</title>
          <p>"For though absent in body, I am present in spirit; and as if present, I have already pronounced judgment on the one who did such a thing" (5:3).</p>
          <p>Paul's language is precise about his relationship to the situation. He is <hi type="italic">agon to somati</hi> ("absent in body") — physically in another location. But he is <hi type="italic">paron de to pneumati</hi> ("present in spirit"). The word <hi type="italic">pneuma</hi> here most likely refers to Paul's own spirit — his personal presence in intentionality and authority — rather than to the Holy Spirit, though the distinction is not absolute since Paul understands his own spiritual activity as connected to the Spirit of God. The phrase communicates that physical distance does not diminish Paul's pastoral authority or his capacity to act.</p>
          <p>"I have already pronounced judgment" (<hi type="italic">ede kekrika</hi>) — the perfect tense of <hi type="italic">krino</hi> ("to judge, to decide, to determine, to render a verdict") indicates a completed verdict with present consequences. Paul has not waited for further information or deliberation. He has already judged. The man's case is decided. The word <hi type="italic">kekrika</hi> will be echoed in the chapter's closing verses (5:12-13), where Paul distinguishes between judging those inside and those outside the community.</p>
          <p>"In the name of our Lord Jesus, when you are assembled, and my spirit is with you, with the power of our Lord Jesus, you are to deliver this man to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, so that his spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord" (5:4-5).</p>
          <p>These verses describe an action to be taken by the assembled community. The syntax is complex, but the elements are identifiable:</p>
          <p>The setting: "when you are assembled" (<hi type="italic">synachthenton hymon</hi>) — the action takes place when the community gathers for assembly. This is not a private act by individuals but a corporate decision of the <hi type="italic">ekklesia</hi> gathered together. The word <hi type="italic">synago</hi> ("to gather together, to assemble") is the verb from which <hi type="italic">synagoge</hi> derives; it describes the formal gathering of the community.</p>
          <p>The authorization: "in the name of our Lord Jesus" (<hi type="italic">en to onomati tou kyriou hemon Iesou</hi>) — the action is performed under the authority and in the sphere of the Lord Jesus. The "name" carries the full weight of the person's authority, as it did in 1:2, 1:10, and 1:13. This is not mob action or personal vendetta; it is authorized by the Lord whose community this is.</p>
          <p>The attendant presence: "and my spirit is with you" (<hi type="italic">kai tou emou pneumatos</hi>) — Paul's apostolic presence and authority are part of the proceeding even though he is physically absent.</p>
          <p>The power: "with the power of our Lord Jesus" (<hi type="italic">syn te dynamei tou kyriou hemon Iesou</hi>) — the action is accomplished by the Lord's own power. The word <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> ("power") has been a key term since 1:18 (the word of the cross is "the power of God") and 1:24 (Christ is "the power of God and the wisdom of God") and 2:4-5 (Paul came "in demonstration of the Spirit and of power" so that their faith would rest on "the power of God"). The same power that constitutes the community's existence also authorizes its discipline.</p>
          <p>The action: "you are to deliver this man to Satan" (<hi type="italic">paradounai ton toiouton to Satana</hi>) — the verb <hi type="italic">paradidomi</hi> ("to hand over, to deliver, to entrust, to turn over") is the word used for Judas's betrayal of Jesus (Matthew 26:15, Mark 14:10, Luke 22:4) and for the legal handing over of a prisoner to authorities (Matthew 27:2, Acts 8:3). The person is being transferred from one sphere of authority to another. "Satan" (<hi type="italic">ho Satanas</hi>) is the adversary — the accuser (the Hebrew <hi type="italic">satan</hi> means "adversary, opponent") who in Job 1-2 operates with God's permission as the agent who tests and afflicts. In the LXX of Job, Satan presents himself before God and receives permission to afflict Job's possessions and then his body, but with limits: "only do not stretch out your hand against him" (Job 1:12), and "he is in your hand; only spare his life" (Job 2:6). The paradigm is significant: Satan operates under divine permission and within divine limits. The "handing over to Satan" is not abandonment to a power outside God's control; it is a transfer to the realm outside the community's protective boundaries, where the adversary operates.</p>
          <p>The purpose of the handing over has two parts, and they are stated as a single sequence:</p>
          <p>"For the destruction of the flesh" (<hi type="italic">eis olethron tes sarkos</hi>) — the word <hi type="italic">olethros</hi> ("destruction, ruin, loss") appears in the LXX in contexts of divine judgment: 1 Samuel 5:9, 11 (the destruction brought by the ark upon the Philistines); Jeremiah 48:3 (LXX 31:3, "a voice of crying and great destruction"). The word <hi type="italic">sarx</hi> ("flesh") has been a significant term in this letter. In 1:26, "not many wise according to the flesh" (<hi type="italic">kata sarka</hi>) described evaluation by worldly, human standards. In 3:1, Paul could not address them "as spiritual people but as fleshly people" (<hi type="italic">sarkinois</hi>). The "flesh" in Paul's usage in this letter represents the sphere of merely human existence oriented away from the Spirit — the natural, worldly mode of living that the Corinthians have been operating from despite having received the Spirit. The "destruction of the flesh" is not necessarily physical death (though it may include physical suffering); it is the breaking of the person's fleshly, self-oriented mode of existence. Removed from the community's protection and subjected to Satan's domain, the person would experience the consequences of life outside God's assembly — consequences that would, ideally, break the pattern of sinful behavior.</p>
          <p>"So that his spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord" (<hi type="italic">hina to pneuma sothe en te hemera tou kyriou</hi>) — the conjunction <hi type="italic">hina</hi> ("so that, in order that") marks this as a purpose clause. The ultimate goal of the entire action — the assembly, the judgment, the handing over, the destruction of the flesh — is salvation, not punishment for its own sake. The "spirit" (<hi type="italic">pneuma</hi>) of the man is to be "saved" (<hi type="italic">sothe</hi>, aorist passive subjunctive of <hi type="italic">sozo</hi>) "in the day of the Lord" (<hi type="italic">en te hemera tou kyriou</hi>). The "day of the Lord" was introduced in 1:8 as "the day of our Lord Jesus Christ," transposing the prophetic <hi type="italic">yom YHWH</hi> onto Christ. The same day that Paul's thanksgiving in 1:8 described as the occasion when believers would stand "guiltless" (<hi type="italic">anenkletous</hi>) is now the standard against which the discipline of this man is measured. The discipline is designed to bring the man to the point where he will be saved on that day.</p>
          <p>The logic is restorative, not eliminative. The community does not expel the man because he is worthless or beyond hope. The community expels the man precisely because he is worth saving, and the current arrangement — tolerated within the community — is leading him toward destruction on the day of the Lord. The expulsion is a drastic intervention intended to break the pattern and redirect the trajectory. The precedent in Israel's history is instructive: when God sent Israel into exile, it was not the end of the relationship but a severe measure within the covenant designed to produce repentance and return (Deuteronomy 30:1-6; Jeremiah 29:10-14; Hosea 2:14-23). The pattern of severe action with restorative intent is deeply embedded in the scriptural tradition.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.5.6-1Cor.5.8">
          <title>Verses 6-8: The Leaven and the Passover</title>
          <p>"Your boasting is not good. Do you not know that a little leaven leavens the whole lump?" (5:6).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">kauchema</hi> ("boasting, ground of boasting, that in which one boasts") connects directly to the boasting theme that has run through the letter since 1:29 ("so that no flesh may boast before God"), 1:31 ("let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord"), 3:21 ("so let no one boast in human beings"), and 4:7 ("why do you boast as if you did not receive it?"). The Corinthians' boasting has been consistently identified as misplaced — directed at human teachers, human wisdom, and human status rather than at the Lord. Here the boasting is not good (<hi type="italic">ou kalon</hi>) because it coexists with unchecked sin. A community cannot simultaneously boast in its spiritual status and tolerate a violation that pagans would not tolerate. The boasting and the tolerance are related: the arrogance that inflates the community's self-regard also blinds it to its own corruption.</p>
          <p>"Do you not know" (<hi type="italic">ouk oidate</hi>) — this phrase appears ten times in 1 Corinthians (3:16, 5:6, 6:2, 6:3, 6:9, 6:15, 6:16, 6:19, 9:13, 9:24). Each occurrence introduces something the Corinthians should already know but are acting as though they do not. The repeated formula suggests a community that has received extensive teaching but is failing to apply it.</p>
          <p>"A little leaven leavens the whole lump" (<hi type="italic">mikra zyme holon to phyrama zymoi</hi>) — this is a proverbial saying about the pervasive effect of a small agent on a large mass. Leaven (<hi type="italic">zyme</hi>) is a piece of fermented dough that, when mixed into a new batch, causes the entire mass (<hi type="italic">phyrama</hi>, "lump of dough") to rise. The action is invisible, gradual, and total: once introduced, the leaven cannot be contained. It works through the entire lump.</p>
          <p>In the LXX and in Jewish practice, leaven carried a dual significance. In everyday life, leaven was essential for bread-making and morally neutral. But in the context of Passover, leaven represented what must be eliminated. Exodus 12:15 (LXX): "Seven days you shall eat unleavened bread. On the first day you shall remove leaven from your houses" (<hi type="italic">exareite zymen ek ton oikion hymon</hi>). Exodus 12:19: "For seven days no leaven shall be found in your houses. If anyone eats what is leavened, that person shall be cut off from the assembly of Israel" (<hi type="italic">exolethreuthsetai he psyche ekeine ek synagoges Israel</hi>). Exodus 13:7: "No leavened bread shall be seen with you, and no leaven shall be seen with you in all your territory." The Passover purging of leaven was a comprehensive, house-by-house removal of every trace. Nothing leavened could remain. The connection to the man in chapter 5 is direct: as Israel purged every trace of leaven before Passover, so the community must purge the sinful element from its midst.</p>
          <p>Paul is not making a general observation about impurity. He is applying a specific Exodus requirement to a specific community situation. The "little leaven" is the tolerated sin; the "whole lump" is the community. If the sin remains, it will work through the entire body, corrupting what should be holy.</p>
          <p>"Cleanse out the old leaven that you may be a new lump, as you really are unleavened" (5:7a).</p>
          <p>"Cleanse out" (<hi type="italic">ekkatharate</hi>) — the aorist imperative of <hi type="italic">ekkathairo</hi> ("to cleanse thoroughly, to purge completely"). The prefix <hi type="italic">ek-</hi> intensifies the cleansing: this is thorough removal, not partial reduction. The Exodus instructions required Israel to remove all leaven, not most of it. The word "old" (<hi type="italic">palaian</hi>) identifies the leaven with the former state — the old existence that should have been left behind. Paul commands them to become in practice what they already are in identity: "as you really are unleavened" (<hi type="italic">kathos este azymoi</hi>). The word <hi type="italic">azymoi</hi> ("unleavened") describes their true identity. They are unleavened — their identity in Christ is pure, set apart, consecrated (1:2, <hi type="italic">hegiasmenois</hi>). The imperative to cleanse out the leaven is therefore not a command to achieve a new status but to align their practice with a status they already possess. The indicative grounds the imperative: because you are unleavened, become unleavened. Because you are God's holy people, act like God's holy people. This is the same logic that operates throughout the letter: the Corinthians have been sanctified (1:2) but must live in accordance with their sanctification.</p>
          <p>"For Christ, our Passover lamb, has been sacrificed" (5:7b).</p>
          <p>"For" (<hi type="italic">kai gar</hi>) introduces the ground of the command. The statement <hi type="italic">to pascha hemon etythe Christos</hi> is compact and rich. The word <hi type="italic">pascha</hi> ("Passover") in the LXX translates the Hebrew <hi type="italic">pesach</hi>, the lamb slaughtered on the fourteenth of Nisan as described in Exodus 12:3-13. The Passover lamb was to be "without blemish" (<hi type="italic">probaton teleion</hi>, Exodus 12:5 LXX), slaughtered "between the evenings" (12:6), its blood applied to the doorposts and lintel so that the destroyer would pass over the house (12:7, 12:13, 12:23). The Passover was not merely a memorial; it was the foundational act of Israel's deliverance from Egypt. The annual observance reenacted and participated in the original event.</p>
          <p>Paul identifies Christ as "our Passover" (<hi type="italic">to pascha hemon</hi>) — the community's Passover lamb. The verb <hi type="italic">etythe</hi> (aorist passive of <hi type="italic">thyo</hi>, "to sacrifice, to slaughter, to offer") places the action in the past: Christ has already been sacrificed. The Passover sacrifice has occurred. This is stated as a fact from which consequences follow. If the Passover lamb has been slaughtered, then the festival has begun. And if the festival has begun, the leaven must already have been removed.</p>
          <p>The logic is temporal and sequential, drawn from the Passover calendar. In Jewish practice, the search for and removal of leaven (<hi type="italic">bedikat chametz</hi>) occurred before the lamb was slaughtered. By the time the Passover meal was eaten, the house was to be entirely free of leaven. Paul is telling the Corinthians: the lamb has already been slaughtered. You should already have removed the leaven. The fact that leaven remains in your midst is a violation of the Passover order itself. You are celebrating the festival with leaven still in the house.</p>
          <p>The identification of Christ with the Passover lamb connects the community's life to the Exodus narrative. Israel's entire national existence began with the Passover: the blood of the lamb, the meal eaten in haste, the departure from slavery, the passage through the sea. The community in Corinth exists because of an analogous event: the sacrifice of Christ, the new Passover. Their communal life is a perpetual festival (as the next verse will make explicit), and the conditions of that festival include the removal of leaven. The Exodus is not merely a typological backdrop; it defines the community's identity and the expectations that follow from that identity.</p>
          <p>"Let us therefore celebrate the festival, not with the old leaven, the leaven of malice and evil, but with the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth" (5:8).</p>
          <p>"Let us celebrate the festival" (<hi type="italic">hoste heortazomen</hi>) — the word <hi type="italic">heortazo</hi> ("to keep festival, to celebrate a feast") appears only here in the New Testament. In the LXX, the verb describes the observance of Israel's festivals: Exodus 5:1, "let my people go, that they may celebrate a festival to me in the wilderness" (<hi type="italic">heortasatosin moi en te eremo</hi>). The present subjunctive (<hi type="italic">heortazomen</hi>) indicates ongoing action: the community's entire life is the festival. This is not a reference to a specific annual observance but to the permanent condition of the community whose Passover lamb has been sacrificed. Every day is Passover. Every gathering is the festival. And therefore every day requires the absence of leaven.</p>
          <p>Paul now interprets the leaven metaphor, translating it from the agricultural to the moral sphere. The "old leaven" (<hi type="italic">palaia zyme</hi>) is identified as "the leaven of malice and evil" (<hi type="italic">zyme kakias kai ponerias</hi>). The word <hi type="italic">kakia</hi> ("malice, wickedness, ill-will, depravity") in the LXX describes the general condition of moral corruption. The word <hi type="italic">poneria</hi> ("evil, wickedness, baseness") is a near-synonym that emphasizes active malice. Together they characterize the old existence — the life before and apart from Christ, the unreformed condition that should have been left behind.</p>
          <p>The alternative is "the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth" (<hi type="italic">azymois eilikrinias kai aletheias</hi>). The word <hi type="italic">eilikrinia</hi> ("sincerity, purity, genuineness") comes from a root that may mean "tested by sunlight" (<hi type="italic">eilikrineia</hi>, from <hi type="italic">heile</hi>, "sunlight," and <hi type="italic">krino</hi>, "to judge") — that which proves genuine when held up to the light. In 2 Corinthians 1:12 and 2:17, Paul uses the word for the sincerity of his own ministry. The word <hi type="italic">aletheia</hi> ("truth") in Paul's usage denotes reality as opposed to pretense, the genuine state of affairs as opposed to appearances. The unleavened bread of the community's festival life consists of genuine integrity and truthfulness — the opposite of the pretense involved in boasting about spiritual status while harboring unaddressed sin.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.5.9-1Cor.5.13">
          <title>Verses 9-13: The Boundary Between Inside and Outside</title>
          <p>"I wrote to you in my letter not to associate with sexually immoral people" (5:9).</p>
          <p>Paul refers to a previous letter — not 1 Corinthians itself but an earlier communication that has not survived. The word <hi type="italic">epistole</hi> ("letter") and the aorist <hi type="italic">egrapsa</hi> ("I wrote") point to a prior correspondence. This confirms that 1 Corinthians is not Paul's first communication with the community; at least one earlier letter existed. Its content included an instruction about association with sexually immoral people (<hi type="italic">pornoi</hi>). The Corinthians apparently misunderstood — or chose to misunderstand — this instruction, prompting Paul's clarification.</p>
          <p>"Not at all meaning the sexually immoral of this world, or the greedy and swindlers, or idolaters, since then you would need to go out of the world" (5:10).</p>
          <p>"Not at all" (<hi type="italic">ou pantos</hi>) — Paul emphatically clarifies. His earlier instruction was not about avoiding all contact with sinful people in the surrounding society. Such avoidance would be impossible: <hi type="italic">opheilete ara ek tou kosmou exelthein</hi> — "you would then need to depart from the world." The community lives in Corinth, a commercial city. Its members work, trade, and interact with pagans daily. Paul lists the types of people they cannot avoid: <hi type="italic">pornoi</hi> ("sexually immoral"), <hi type="italic">pleonektai</hi> ("greedy, covetous"), <hi type="italic">harpages</hi> ("swindlers, robbers, extortioners"), and <hi type="italic">eidololatrai</hi> ("idolaters"). These four categories represent the moral landscape of pagan urban life. The community cannot withdraw from the world; it must live in it.</p>
          <p>This clarification is important for understanding Paul's view of the community's relationship to the surrounding culture. The community is not a sect that retreats from society. It is "the assembly of God that is in Corinth" (1:2) — located in the city, engaged with the city, but not defined by the city's moral standards. The boundary is not geographical but relational and covenantal.</p>
          <p>"But now I am writing to you not to associate with anyone who bears the name of brother if he is guilty of sexual immorality or greed, or is an idolater, reviler, drunkard, or swindler — not even to eat with such a one" (5:11).</p>
          <p>"Now" (<hi type="italic">nyn de</hi>) marks the new, clarified instruction. The key phrase is <hi type="italic">ean tis adelphos onomazomenos</hi> — "if anyone named a brother" or "anyone who bears the name of brother." The word <hi type="italic">onomazomenos</hi> (present passive participle of <hi type="italic">onomazo</hi>, "to name, to call, to designate") describes someone who carries the title <hi type="italic">adelphos</hi> ("brother") — someone who is identified as a member of the community, who claims the name, who is recognized by others as belonging. The instruction is not about pagans but about insiders. The community's responsibility is not to reform the world but to maintain its own integrity.</p>
          <p>The list of offenses expands beyond <hi type="italic">porneia</hi>. Six categories are named: <hi type="italic">pornos</hi> ("sexually immoral person"), <hi type="italic">pleonektes</hi> ("greedy person"), <hi type="italic">eidololatres</hi> ("idolater"), <hi type="italic">loidoros</hi> ("reviler, abuser, one who insults"), <hi type="italic">methysos</hi> ("drunkard"), and <hi type="italic">harpax</hi> ("swindler, robber"). The list combines sexual, economic, religious, verbal, and social offenses. <hi type="italic">Porneia</hi> is not uniquely disqualifying; greed, idolatry, abusive speech, drunkenness, and extortion are equally grounds for the community's refusal of fellowship. The issue is not one particular sin but a pattern of life incompatible with the community's identity as God's holy assembly.</p>
          <p>"Not even to eat with such a one" (<hi type="italic">to toiouto mede synesthiein</hi>) — the prohibition of shared meals is the most severe form of social exclusion available to the community. In the ancient world, shared meals constituted and expressed social bonds. To eat with someone was to declare solidarity, partnership, and mutual acceptance. In the community's life, the common meal — which Paul will address at length in 11:17-34 — was the central act of fellowship (<hi type="italic">koinonia</hi>, 1:9; 10:16-17). To exclude someone from the table was to declare that the bond of fellowship was severed. The person retains the name "brother" in the text's description (<hi type="italic">onomazomenos adelphos</hi>), but the community's action declares that the name does not match the reality. The exclusion from the meal is the practical expression of the expulsion commanded in verse 2.</p>
          <p>"For what have I to do with judging outsiders? Is it not those inside whom you judge? God judges those outside. 'Purge the evil person from among you'" (5:12-13).</p>
          <p>Paul draws a sharp boundary between two spheres of responsibility: inside (<hi type="italic">esothen</hi>/<hi type="italic">eso</hi>) and outside (<hi type="italic">exothen</hi>/<hi type="italic">exo</hi>). The question <hi type="italic">ti gar moi tous exo krinein</hi> ("for what business is it of mine to judge those outside?") is rhetorical. Paul disclaims jurisdiction over those outside the community. His responsibility, and the community's responsibility, extends only to <hi type="italic">tous eso</hi> ("those inside"). The question <hi type="italic">ouchi tous eso hymeis krinete</hi> ("is it not those inside whom you judge?") expects the answer "yes." The community already exercises judgment over its own members — the question implies that internal judgment is a recognized function of the assembly. The problem is not that they lack the authority to judge but that they have failed to exercise it in this case.</p>
          <p>"God judges those outside" (<hi type="italic">tous de exo ho theos krinei</hi>) — the outsiders are not beyond all judgment; they are simply beyond the community's judgment. God himself judges them. The community's jurisdiction is limited; God's is not. This division of judicial responsibility liberates the community from the impossible task of reforming pagan society while making them fully responsible for their own internal life.</p>
          <p>The chapter closes with a direct quotation from Deuteronomy: "Purge the evil person from among you" (<hi type="italic">exarate ton poneron ex hymon auton</hi>). The LXX formula appears repeatedly throughout Deuteronomy with minor variations:</p>
          <list type="x-bullet">
            <item>Deuteronomy 17:7 (LXX): <hi type="italic">kai exareis ton poneron ex hymon auton</hi> — "and you shall purge the evil from among yourselves." Context: the execution of anyone who serves other gods, after investigation and testimony of two or three witnesses.</item>
            <item>Deuteronomy 17:12: "and you shall purge the evil from Israel." Context: the person who presumes to disobey the priest or judge.</item>
            <item>Deuteronomy 19:19: "and you shall purge the evil from your midst." Context: the false witness.</item>
            <item>Deuteronomy 21:21: "and you shall purge the evil from your midst." Context: the rebellious son.</item>
            <item>Deuteronomy 22:21: "and you shall purge the evil from your midst." Context: the woman found not to be a virgin.</item>
            <item>Deuteronomy 22:24: "and you shall purge the evil from your midst." Context: the case of adultery.</item>
            <item>Deuteronomy 24:7: "and you shall purge the evil from your midst." Context: the kidnapper.</item>
          </list>
          <p>The formula is a refrain of communal purification in Deuteronomy's legal code. Every time Israel identifies a fundamental violation within the community, the response is the same: purge the evil. The formula's repetition in Deuteronomy establishes a principle: the community's holiness requires the removal of that which corrupts it. Israel could not maintain its identity as God's covenant people while harboring within itself conduct that violated the covenant. Paul applies the identical logic to the Corinthian assembly. The community is God's temple (3:16-17), God's field and building (3:9), the sanctified people of God (1:2). Its holiness requires the same vigilance that Israel's holiness required. The Deuteronomic formula is not arbitrarily chosen; it identifies the Corinthian assembly with Israel and applies Israel's own communal discipline to the new situation.</p>
          <p>The final word of the chapter is therefore a scriptural command, not Paul's personal opinion. By closing with the Deuteronomic formula, Paul places his instruction within the continuous tradition of God's people maintaining their communal purity. The community is not innovating; it is doing what God's people have always been required to do.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.5">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 5 is built on a single controlling logic: the community's identity determines its responsibilities, and its failure to act on those responsibilities constitutes a betrayal of that identity.</p>
          <p>The identity has been established in chapters 1-4: the community is "the assembly of God" (1:2), sanctified (1:2), called (1:2, 9, 24, 26), enriched with gifts (1:5-7), sustained by God's faithfulness (1:8-9), and constituted as God's temple where the Spirit dwells (3:16). This identity carries obligations. A community that is God's holy temple cannot harbor conduct that defiles it. A community that has been sanctified must maintain its sanctification. A community whose Passover lamb has been sacrificed must remove the leaven.</p>
          <p>The Corinthians' failure is double. First, they have tolerated the sin itself — a man maintaining a sexual arrangement with his father's wife, a violation of explicit Levitical and Deuteronomic law, a practice that even pagans would not countenance. Second, and in Paul's presentation even more striking, they are "puffed up" about it — their arrogance has rendered them incapable of recognizing the danger.</p>
          <p>Paul's remedy operates on two levels. At the practical level, the man must be expelled from the community's fellowship — removed from the table, handed over to the sphere outside the community's protective boundary. At the theological level, the community must understand why this expulsion is necessary. The leaven metaphor (vv. 6-8) explains: tolerated sin is not contained sin. A little leaven works through the entire lump. The Passover metaphor provides the ground: Christ, the Passover lamb, has been sacrificed, and therefore the community's entire existence is the Passover festival, which requires the thorough removal of all leaven.</p>
          <p>The chapter also establishes a principle that governs the rest of the letter's argument about community behavior. The distinction between inside and outside (vv. 9-13) defines the community's sphere of responsibility. The assembly judges those within; God judges those without. This principle will be applied immediately in chapter 6, where Paul rebukes the community for taking internal disputes to external courts (6:1-8) — the mirror image of the problem in chapter 5. In chapter 5, the community fails to judge when it should. In chapter 6:1-8, the community seeks outside judgment when it should judge for itself. Both failures stem from the same confusion about the community's boundaries and responsibilities.</p>
          <p>The argument's most remarkable feature is the restorative purpose that governs the severity of the discipline. The harshest action Paul commands — handing the man over to Satan for the destruction of the flesh — is explicitly directed toward salvation: "so that his spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord" (5:5). The severity serves the man's ultimate good. The community expels not to destroy but to reclaim. This pattern reflects the scriptural precedent of divine discipline as a means of restoration: God's judgments on Israel throughout the prophetic tradition were aimed not at termination but at return. The exile was not the end of the covenant but a severe measure within it. The "rod" that Paul mentioned in 4:21 is the same kind of instrument: painful, but wielded by a father toward his children (4:14-15), not by an executioner toward the condemned.</p>
          <p>The closing Deuteronomic quotation — "Purge the evil person from among you" — identifies the Corinthian community with Israel and holds it to Israel's standard. The community that inherited Israel's Passover, Israel's temple language, and Israel's identity as God's holy people also inherits Israel's obligation to maintain communal holiness through communal discipline. The chapter does not introduce a new principle; it applies an old one to a new context. The people of God have always been responsible for the integrity of their own assembly. The Corinthians, for all their gifts and knowledge and spiritual endowment, have forgotten this.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.6">
        <title>1 Corinthians 6 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.6">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 5):</hi> Paul confronted the community with a specific case of sexual immorality — a man maintaining a sexual arrangement with his father's wife, a violation of Leviticus 18:8 and Deuteronomy 22:30, conduct so extreme that even pagans would not tolerate it (5:1). The community's response was not grief but arrogance: "you are puffed up" (<hi type="italic">pephysiomenoi este</hi>, 5:2), using the same word (<hi type="italic">phusioo</hi>) that diagnosed the Corinthian disease throughout chapter 4. Paul commanded the community to assemble and deliver the man to Satan "for the destruction of the flesh, so that his spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord" (5:5). The discipline was restorative in purpose — severe intervention aimed at salvation, not elimination. Paul then developed the Passover metaphor: Christ, the Passover lamb, has been sacrificed (5:7), and therefore the community must purge the old leaven from its midst, celebrating the ongoing festival with "the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth" (5:8). The chapter concluded by establishing a sharp boundary between inside and outside. Paul disclaimed jurisdiction over outsiders: "what have I to do with judging those outside?" (5:12). But the community is responsible for judging those inside: "is it not those inside whom you judge?" (5:12). The closing command was the Deuteronomic formula: "purge the evil person from among you" (5:13, quoting Deuteronomy 17:7; 19:19; 21:21; 22:21; 24:7). The community failed to exercise internal judgment when it was required; it tolerated what should have been expelled.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 7):</hi> Chapter 7 opens with a new formula: "Now concerning the matters about which you wrote" (<hi type="italic">peri de hon egrapsate</hi>, 7:1). This signals a shift in the letter's structure. From this point forward, Paul is responding to specific questions the Corinthians raised in a letter to him. The first question concerns marriage and sexual relations. Paul's response addresses the unmarried and married, widows and virgins, divorce and separation. The governing principle is stated in 7:17: "let each person lead the life that the Lord has assigned to him, and to which God has called him" — a principle Paul applies to circumcision (7:18-19), slavery (7:21-24), and marriage (7:25-40). The chapter's tone is pastoral and advisory: "I say this by way of concession, not of command" (7:6); "I have no command of the Lord, but I give my judgment" (7:25). The urgency underlying the advice is eschatological: "the appointed time has grown very short" (7:29); "the present form of this world is passing away" (7:31).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 6 connects to what precedes and follows:</hi> Chapter 6 is not a new topic but the continuation and completion of the argument Paul began in chapter 5. The two chapters address the same underlying failure from opposite directions. In chapter 5, the community failed to exercise internal judgment when it should have — it tolerated flagrant sin within its midst. In 6:1-8, the community goes to external courts when it should judge for itself — it outsources internal disputes to pagan judges. Both failures expose the same confusion about the community's boundaries and identity. A community that does not understand the difference between inside and outside will simultaneously fail to judge insiders (chapter 5) and appeal to outsiders for judgment of insiders (6:1-8). The principle stated at the end of chapter 5 — "is it not those inside whom you judge?" (5:12) — generates both problems.</p>
          <p>The second half of chapter 6 (vv. 12-20) circles back to the <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> of chapter 5, providing the theological foundation for why sexual immorality is incompatible with membership in the community. The man in chapter 5 was a specific case; 6:12-20 supplies the general principle. The body belongs to the Lord (6:13), the body's members are members of Christ (6:15), the body is a temple of the Holy Spirit (6:19), and the community was bought with a price (6:20). These claims explain why the case in chapter 5 required such drastic action.</p>
          <p>Chapter 6 also transitions the letter from Paul's initiative (chapters 1-6, where Paul addresses problems he has learned about from reports) to the Corinthians' initiative (chapters 7-16, where Paul responds to their written questions). The transition is thematic as well: the <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> discussion in 6:12-20 leads directly into the marriage and sexuality questions of chapter 7. The slogan "all things are lawful for me" (6:12), which Paul quotes and qualifies, represents the Corinthian attitude that generates both the sexual permissiveness of chapters 5-6 and the questions about marriage in chapter 7.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.6.1-1Cor.6.6">
          <title>Verses 1-6: Internal Disputes Before External Courts</title>
          <p>"When one of you has a grievance against another, does he dare go to law before the unrighteous instead of the saints?" (6:1).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">tolma</hi> ("to dare, to have the audacity, to presume") carries a note of astonishment. Paul is not merely reporting a problem; he is expressing disbelief that it occurs. The phrase <hi type="italic">pragma echon pros ton heteron</hi> ("having a matter/case against another") uses <hi type="italic">pragma</hi> ("thing, matter, business, legal case") in its juridical sense — a dispute that requires adjudication. The dispute is between community members: one believer against another.</p>
          <p>The contrast is between two forums. The first: <hi type="italic">epi ton adikon</hi> ("before the unrighteous"). The word <hi type="italic">adikos</hi> ("unrighteous, unjust") describes the pagan judges to whom the Corinthians are bringing their disputes. The word is loaded. Paul does not call them "judges" or "magistrates" or even "outsiders" (though he used <hi type="italic">exo</hi>, "outside," in 5:12-13). He calls them "the unrighteous" — people who, by definition, stand outside the community of those who have been made righteous. The word will reappear in verse 9 as the first category of those who "will not inherit the kingdom of God." The second forum: <hi type="italic">epi ton hagion</hi> ("before the saints"). The word <hi type="italic">hagioi</hi> ("saints, holy ones, those set apart") is Paul's standard designation for community members (1:2, "called saints"). The community consists of <hi type="italic">hagioi</hi> — people consecrated to God, set apart from the world. The fact that they are bringing their disputes before <hi type="italic">adikoi</hi> rather than <hi type="italic">hagioi</hi> is an inversion of the proper order.</p>
          <p>The connection to chapter 5 is immediate. In 5:12, Paul established the principle: the community judges those inside; God judges those outside. In 6:1, the community is violating this principle in reverse: instead of judging its own members, it is asking outsiders to judge them. The community that failed to exercise internal discipline in the case of the incestuous man (chapter 5) now runs to external courts for its internal disputes. Both failures reveal the same inability to understand and maintain the boundary between the community and the world.</p>
          <p>"Do you not know that the saints will judge the world? And if the world is to be judged by you, are you incompetent to try trivial cases?" (6:2).</p>
          <p>The formula <hi type="italic">ouk oidate</hi> ("do you not know?") appears here for the third time in the letter (after 3:16 and 5:6) and will appear four more times in this chapter alone (6:3, 6:9, 6:15, 6:16, 6:19). Its repeated use signals that the Corinthians possess knowledge they are not applying. They know these things; they are not acting on what they know.</p>
          <p>The claim <hi type="italic">hoti hoi hagioi ton kosmon krinousin</hi> ("that the saints will judge the world") draws on a tradition rooted in Israel's scriptures and developed in Second Temple Jewish literature. Daniel 7:22 (LXX): "judgment was given to the saints of the Most High" (<hi type="italic">krima edoke hagiois hypsistou</hi>). Daniel 7:27: "the kingdom and the dominion and the greatness of the kingdoms under the whole heaven shall be given to the people of the saints of the Most High." In Daniel's vision, the "one like a son of man" receives dominion (7:13-14), and this dominion is shared with "the saints of the Most High" (7:18, 22, 27). The Wisdom of Solomon 3:8 describes the righteous: "they will judge nations and rule over peoples, and the Lord will reign over them forever." In the Enochic tradition (<hi type="italic">1 Enoch</hi> 1:9; 95:3; 96:1; 98:12), the righteous participate in judgment upon the wicked. Jesus himself promised his disciples: "you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel" (Matthew 19:28; cf. Luke 22:30).</p>
          <p>Paul's argument is from the greater to the lesser (a <hi type="italic">qal wahomer</hi>, "light and heavy" argument, a standard rabbinic form). If the saints will judge the entire cosmos — the world in its totality — then certainly they are competent to adjudicate <hi type="italic">kriteria elachista</hi> ("the most trivial cases," literally "the smallest courts/judgments"). The word <hi type="italic">elachistos</hi> is the superlative of <hi type="italic">mikros</hi> ("small") — these are the very smallest matters, petty disputes. If you are destined for cosmic judgment, how can you be unable to settle a property disagreement?</p>
          <p>"Do you not know that we will judge angels? How much more, then, matters pertaining to this life!" (6:3).</p>
          <p>The escalation continues. Not only the world but <hi type="italic">angelous krinoumon</hi> ("we will judge angels"). The background is complex. In the Enochic literature, the fallen Watchers — the angels of Genesis 6:1-4 who left their proper station — are subject to judgment (<hi type="italic">1 Enoch</hi> 10:4-6, 12-13; 16:1; Jude 6: "the angels who did not stay within their own position of authority... he has kept in eternal chains under gloomy darkness until the judgment of the great day"). The participation of the righteous in this angelic judgment is part of the eschatological expectation. If the community will participate in judging celestial beings, then <hi type="italic">biotika</hi> ("matters pertaining to this life," from <hi type="italic">bios</hi>, "life, livelihood, daily existence") — mundane disputes about property, contracts, and money — should be well within their competence. The word <hi type="italic">biotika</hi> is deliberately dismissive. These earthly, material disputes are beneath the dignity of a people destined for cosmic judicial authority.</p>
          <p>"So if you have ordinary cases, do you appoint as judges those who have no standing in the assembly?" (6:4).</p>
          <p>The verse is syntactically difficult, and its tone may be either ironic or directive. The word <hi type="italic">exouthemenous</hi> (perfect passive participle of <hi type="italic">exoutheneo</hi>, "to despise, to treat as nothing, to regard as of no account") describes people who are "despised" or "of no standing." If the sentence is ironic: Paul is asking, "When you have everyday disputes, do you really seat as judges people who count for nothing in the assembly?" — meaning the pagan judges, who have no standing within the community. The irony would be biting: you seat as judges over your disputes the very people who are nothing in the community's estimation. If the sentence is directive: Paul is saying, "Appoint even those of lowest standing in the assembly" — even the least qualified community member would be better as a judge of internal disputes than a pagan outsider. Either reading serves the argument: the community possesses sufficient resources to handle its own disputes without recourse to external courts.</p>
          <p>"I say this to your shame. Is it so, that there is not among you one wise person who will be able to decide between his brothers?" (6:5).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">pros entropen hymin lego</hi> ("I say this to your shame") uses the word <hi type="italic">entrope</hi> ("shame, embarrassment, turning inward") — the very thing Paul said he was not doing in 4:14 (<hi type="italic">ouk entrepon hymas grapho</hi>, "I do not write to shame you"). There he withheld shame because he was admonishing his children. Here, addressing this particular failure, he deliberately invokes shame. The situation is shameful.</p>
          <p>The irony cuts deep: <hi type="italic">outos ouk eni en hymin oudeis sophos</hi> ("is there not among you even one wise person?"). The word <hi type="italic">sophos</hi> ("wise") drips with irony given the Corinthians' self-assessment. This is the community that prided itself on wisdom (1:17-2:16), that boasted in its teachers' rhetorical brilliance, that considered itself rich and reigning (4:8). Yet among all these supposedly wise people, not one can be found competent to adjudicate a dispute between brothers? The word <hi type="italic">diakrinai</hi> ("to judge, to decide, to distinguish") echoes the vocabulary of judgment that runs through the letter. The phrase <hi type="italic">ana meson tou adelphou autou</hi> ("between his brother") uses the singular <hi type="italic">adelphos</hi>, emphasizing the familial relationship that makes the recourse to outside courts so incongruous. These are brothers going to court against brothers.</p>
          <p>"But brother goes to law against brother, and that before unbelievers!" (6:6).</p>
          <p>Paul restates the offense with maximum bluntness. <hi type="italic">Adelphos meta adelphou krinetai</hi> ("brother goes to law against brother") — the repetition of <hi type="italic">adelphos</hi> hammers the relational betrayal. The word <hi type="italic">apistos</hi> ("unbeliever, unfaithful, one without trust/faith") replaces <hi type="italic">adikos</hi> ("unrighteous") from verse 1. The two terms are not synonymous but complementary: the pagan judges are both <hi type="italic">adikoi</hi> (outside the sphere of God's righteousness) and <hi type="italic">apistoi</hi> (outside the sphere of faith/trust in God). The community is airing its internal conflicts before people who share neither its righteousness nor its faith.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.6.7-1Cor.6.8">
          <title>Verses 7-8: The More Fundamental Failure</title>
          <p>"To have lawsuits at all with one another is already a defeat for you. Why not rather suffer wrong? Why not rather be defrauded?" (6:7).</p>
          <p>Paul now deepens the argument beyond the question of venue. The problem is not merely where the disputes are adjudicated but that they exist at all. The word <hi type="italic">hettema</hi> ("defeat, loss, shortcoming, diminishment") describes a failure, a falling short. The phrase <hi type="italic">holos hettema hymin estin</hi> ("it is already altogether a defeat for you") uses <hi type="italic">holos</hi> ("altogether, at all, completely") — the same intensifier from 5:1 ("it is actually reported"). Even before the question of pagan courts arises, the very existence of lawsuits among community members represents a defeat — a failure of the community to embody the ethic it professes.</p>
          <p>Paul poses two rhetorical questions that invoke a radically different standard of behavior. <hi type="italic">Dia ti ouchi mallon adikeisthe</hi> ("why not rather be wronged?") — the verb <hi type="italic">adikeo</hi> ("to wrong, to treat unjustly, to injure") in the passive means "to suffer injustice." <hi type="italic">Dia ti ouchi mallon apostereisthe</hi> ("why not rather be defrauded?") — the verb <hi type="italic">apostereo</hi> ("to deprive, to defraud, to rob, to withhold what is owed") in the passive means "to suffer deprivation, to be cheated."</p>
          <p>The ethic Paul invokes has clear roots in Jesus' teaching. Matthew 5:39-40: "If anyone slaps you on the right cheek, turn to him the other also. And if anyone would sue you and take your tunic, let him have your cloak as well." Luke 6:29-30: "From one who takes away your cloak, do not withhold your tunic either. Give to everyone who begs from you, and from one who takes away your goods do not demand them back." The willingness to absorb injustice rather than retaliate or litigate is not passivity but a deliberate choice grounded in the cross. Paul has already argued that the apostolic pattern involves suffering wrong without retaliation: "when reviled, we bless; when persecuted, we endure; when slandered, we respond graciously" (4:12-13). The community should embody the same pattern. A community formed by the cross — the paradigmatic case of the righteous one suffering unjustly — should be willing to absorb wrong rather than fight in pagan courts over property and money.</p>
          <p>"But you yourselves wrong and defraud — even your own brothers!" (6:8).</p>
          <p>The reality is the opposite of the ideal. <hi type="italic">Alla hymeis adikeite kai apostereite</hi> ("but you yourselves wrong and defraud") — the same two verbs from verse 7 now appear in the active voice. Not only are the Corinthians unwilling to suffer wrong; they are the ones inflicting it. They are not merely failing to absorb injustice; they are perpetrating it. The phrase <hi type="italic">kai touto adelphous</hi> ("and this against brothers!") intensifies the indictment. The <hi type="italic">kai touto</hi> ("and this!") expresses outrage. They wrong and defraud, and their victims are their own brothers — members of the same community, children of the same Father, members of the same body.</p>
          <p>The progression of the argument is devastating: (1) You should not take disputes to pagan courts (vv. 1-6). (2) You should not have disputes at all; you should absorb the wrong (v. 7). (3) In fact, you are the ones doing the wronging (v. 8). The Corinthians are not merely victims seeking a forum for redress; they are perpetrators using pagan courts as instruments of injustice against fellow believers. Each layer of the argument peels away another layer of pretense.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.6.9-1Cor.6.11">
          <title>Verses 9-11: Those Who Will Not Inherit the Kingdom, and What the Community Was</title>
          <p>"Or do you not know that the unrighteous will not inherit the kingdom of God? Do not be deceived: neither the sexually immoral, nor idolaters, nor adulterers, nor men who practice homosexuality, nor thieves, nor the greedy, nor drunkards, nor revilers, nor swindlers will inherit the kingdom of God" (6:9-10).</p>
          <p>The formula <hi type="italic">e ouk oidate</hi> ("or do you not know?") introduces what Paul considers established teaching. The conjunction <hi type="italic">e</hi> ("or") links this to the preceding argument: "or" have you forgotten this fundamental truth that should be governing your behavior? The verb <hi type="italic">planasthe</hi> ("do not be deceived, do not wander, do not be led astray") is the present passive imperative of <hi type="italic">planao</hi> ("to lead astray, to deceive, to cause to wander"). In the LXX, the word describes Israel's straying from God's path: Isaiah 53:6 (LXX), "all we like sheep have gone astray" (<hi type="italic">pantes hos probata eplanenthemen</hi>). The warning <hi type="italic">me planasthe</hi> ("do not be deceived") signals that the Corinthians may be in danger of self-deception on this point — perhaps because the slogan "all things are lawful for me" (which Paul will address in v. 12) has led some to believe that behavioral categories no longer apply to those who have been spiritually endowed.</p>
          <p>The claim: <hi type="italic">adikoi theou basileian ou kleronomesousin</hi> ("the unrighteous will not inherit the kingdom of God"). The word <hi type="italic">adikoi</hi> ("unrighteous") is the same word Paul used in verse 1 for the pagan judges. Its reappearance here creates a deliberate resonance: the community is taking its disputes before the <hi type="italic">adikoi</hi> (v. 1), while those who are <hi type="italic">adikoi</hi> will not inherit the kingdom (v. 9). The implication is uncomfortable: community members who practice injustice against their brothers (v. 8) are behaving like the <hi type="italic">adikoi</hi> who have no share in the kingdom.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">kleronomeo</hi> ("to inherit") is deeply embedded in Israel's scriptural vocabulary. In the LXX, the verb and its cognates describe Israel's inheritance of the promised land — the central promise of the covenant. Genesis 15:7 (LXX): "I am the LORD who brought you out from Ur of the Chaldeans to give you this land to inherit it" (<hi type="italic">kleronomesai auten</hi>). Deuteronomy 1:8: "Go in and take possession of the land that the LORD swore to your fathers... to give to them and to their offspring after them." Psalm 37:9 (LXX 36:9): "those who wait for the LORD shall inherit the land" (<hi type="italic">kleronomesousin gen</hi>); 37:11 (LXX 36:11): "the meek shall inherit the land." The inheritance is not something earned but something received as the fulfillment of God's promise. However, possession of the inheritance was always conditional on faithfulness within the covenant. Israel inherited the land but could be — and was — expelled from it for covenantal unfaithfulness. The inheritance was a gift, but remaining in the inheritance required maintaining the covenant relationship.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">basileian theou</hi> ("kingdom of God") appeared in 4:20: "the kingdom of God does not consist in talk but in power." It will appear again in 15:50: "flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God." The kingdom of God is the sphere of God's sovereign reign — the reality that the community already participates in partially and will fully enter at the consummation. To "not inherit" the kingdom is to be excluded from this final, complete participation in God's reign.</p>
          <p>Paul then provides a catalogue of ten types of persons who will not inherit the kingdom:</p>
          <list type="x-ordered">
            <item><hi type="italic">Pornoi</hi> ("sexually immoral") — the same word from 5:9, 10, 11. Those who engage in <hi type="italic">porneia</hi>, the broad term for sexual conduct that violates God's ordering of human relationships.</item>
            <item><hi type="italic">Eidololatrai</hi> ("idolaters") — those who worship idols. Listed in 5:10, 11 as well. Paul will address idolatry at length in chapters 8-10.</item>
            <item><hi type="italic">Moichoi</hi> ("adulterers") — those who violate the marriage covenant through sexual union with another's spouse. The word is distinct from <hi type="italic">pornos</hi>: <hi type="italic">moicheia</hi> is specifically the violation of an existing marriage bond.</item>
          </list>
          <p>4-5. <hi type="italic">Malakoi</hi> ("soft ones") and <hi type="italic">arsenokoitai</hi> ("men who bed males"). The word <hi type="italic">malakos</hi> literally means "soft" and in moral discourse described a person of weak character, one who yielded to pleasures or who played the passive role in male sexual relations. The word <hi type="italic">arsenokoites</hi> is a compound of <hi type="italic">arsen</hi> ("male") and <hi type="italic">koite</hi> ("bed," with sexual connotation, from which the English "coitus" derives). The compound appears to be drawn directly from Leviticus 18:22 (LXX): <hi type="italic">meta arsenos ou koimethese koiten gynaikos</hi> ("with a male you shall not lie the bed of a woman") and Leviticus 20:13 (LXX): <hi type="italic">hos an koimethe meta arsenos koiten gynaikos</hi> ("whoever lies with a male the bed of a woman"). The term identifies a specific category of sexual conduct prohibited in the Levitical code.</p>
          <list type="x-ordered">
            <item><hi type="italic">Kleptai</hi> ("thieves") — those who take what belongs to another by stealth.</item>
            <item><hi type="italic">Pleonektai</hi> ("greedy, covetous") — those who grasp for more than their share, who are driven by the desire to possess at others' expense. The word appeared in 5:10, 11.</item>
            <item><hi type="italic">Methysoi</hi> ("drunkards") — those characterized by habitual drunkenness. Listed in 5:11.</item>
            <item><hi type="italic">Loidoroi</hi> ("revilers, verbal abusers") — those who use speech to wound and demean. Listed in 5:11.</item>
            <item><hi type="italic">Harpages</hi> ("swindlers, robbers, extortioners") — those who seize what belongs to others by violence or fraud. The word appeared in 5:10, 11.</item>
          </list>
          <p>The list overlaps substantially with the list in 5:11 (<hi type="italic">pornos, pleonektes, eidololatres, loidoros, methysos, harpax</hi>) but expands it with four additional categories (<hi type="italic">moichoi, malakoi, arsenokoitai, kleptai</hi>). The overlap is not accidental. In 5:11, Paul listed those with whom the community should not even eat — those who bear the name of brother but whose behavior contradicts the name. Here in 6:9-10, Paul states the eschatological consequence: such people will not inherit the kingdom of God. The communal discipline of chapter 5 (expulsion from the fellowship) reflects the eschatological reality of chapter 6 (exclusion from the kingdom). The community's present judgment anticipates God's future judgment.</p>
          <p>The structure of the list merits attention. It moves from sexual offenses (<hi type="italic">pornoi, eidololatrai, moichoi, malakoi, arsenokoitai</hi>) through property offenses (<hi type="italic">kleptai, pleonektai</hi>) to social offenses (<hi type="italic">methysoi, loidoroi, harpages</hi>). The range is comprehensive. Paul is not singling out one category of sin as uniquely disqualifying. Sexual immorality, greed, theft, drunkenness, verbal abuse, and extortion all stand on the same ground: all are incompatible with inheriting the kingdom.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">kleronomesousin</hi> is future tense: "will not inherit." The exclusion is eschatological — it pertains to the final inheritance, the full entry into God's kingdom. But the present-tense warning <hi type="italic">me planasthe</hi> ("do not be deceived") indicates that present behavior determines future inheritance. The kingdom is not inherited regardless of conduct. The one who practices these things — who is characterized by this pattern of life — will not receive the inheritance. This is consistent with the pattern throughout Israel's scriptures: the inheritance was given by grace, but maintaining it required faithfulness. Israel received the land as a gift; Israel lost the land through disobedience.</p>
          <p>"And such were some of you. But you were washed, but you were sanctified, but you were justified in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ and by the Spirit of our God" (6:11).</p>
          <p>This verse is the hinge of the entire section. The first clause — <hi type="italic">kai tauta tines ete</hi> ("and such things some of you were") — uses the imperfect <hi type="italic">ete</hi> ("you were"), pointing to a past state that has ended. The word <hi type="italic">tauta</hi> ("such things, these things") is neuter plural, referring to the entire list of behaviors and identities just catalogued. Some of the Corinthians were <hi type="italic">pornoi, eidololatrai, moichoi, malakoi, arsenokoitai, kleptai, pleonektai, methysoi, loidoroi, harpages</hi>. The community was not drawn from the morally respectable; it was drawn from the very categories Paul has just identified as excluded from the kingdom. The word <hi type="italic">tines</hi> ("some") indicates that not all were characterized by every vice, but the community collectively included people from every category on the list.</p>
          <p>Then three verbs, each introduced by the strong adversative <hi type="italic">alla</hi> ("but"), each in the aorist middle or passive, each marking a decisive break between the past state and the present one:</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Apeloususthe</hi> ("you were washed") — the aorist middle of <hi type="italic">apolouo</hi> ("to wash off, to wash away, to cleanse"). The word describes a thorough cleansing. In Acts 22:16, Ananias tells Paul: "Rise and be baptized and wash away your sins" (<hi type="italic">apolousai tas hamartias sou</hi>). The connection to baptism is natural: the physical act of immersion corresponds to the spiritual reality of cleansing from the past. The old identity — the <hi type="italic">pornos</hi>, the thief, the drunkard — was washed away. The middle voice (<hi type="italic">apeloususthe</hi> rather than the passive <hi type="italic">apelutheete</hi>) may indicate that the Corinthians participated in the washing: they submitted to it, accepted it, presented themselves for it. The washing was not imposed on them against their will but entered into voluntarily.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Hegiasthete</hi> ("you were sanctified") — the aorist passive of <hi type="italic">hagiazo</hi> ("to sanctify, to make holy, to set apart, to consecrate"). The passive voice indicates that God is the agent: the Corinthians were sanctified by God's action. This connects directly to 1:2, where Paul addressed them as <hi type="italic">hegiasmenois en Christo Iesou</hi> ("those who have been sanctified in Christ Jesus"). The sanctification is the same: they have been set apart from the world, consecrated to God, transferred from the realm of the <hi type="italic">adikoi</hi> to the realm of the <hi type="italic">hagioi</hi>. The word carries its full LXX weight: in Exodus 19:10, God commands Moses to "sanctify" (<hi type="italic">hagiason</hi>) the people before the giving of the Torah at Sinai; in Leviticus 20:7-8, "sanctify yourselves and be holy, for I am the LORD your God... I am the LORD who sanctifies you" (<hi type="italic">ego kyrios ho hagizon hymas</hi>). Sanctification separates a people from common use and dedicates them to God's purposes.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Edikaioothete</hi> ("you were justified") — the aorist passive of <hi type="italic">dikaioo</hi> ("to justify, to declare righteous, to vindicate, to acquit"). The Corinthians were declared righteous — their status before God was transformed from <hi type="italic">adikoi</hi> to <hi type="italic">dikaioi</hi>. The word connects to 4:4, where Paul said "I am not justified by this" (<hi type="italic">ouk en touto dedikaiomei</hi>), reserving the verdict of justification for the Lord's judgment. Here the justification has occurred: God has already declared the Corinthians righteous. The past tense is significant. Justification is presented as a completed event, not an ongoing process.</p>
          <p>The instrumentality is specified: <hi type="italic">en to onomati tou kyriou Iesou Christou kai en to pneumati tou theou hemon</hi> ("in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ and in/by the Spirit of our God"). The "name" (<hi type="italic">onoma</hi>) of the Lord Jesus Christ carries the full weight of his person and authority, as it did in 1:2, 1:10, 5:4. The Spirit of God is the agent through whom the transformation occurred — the same Spirit who indwells the community (3:16), who reveals God's wisdom (2:10-12), and who constitutes the community as God's temple.</p>
          <p>The three verbs together describe a comprehensive transformation. The washing cleansed the old life away. The sanctification set them apart for God. The justification declared them righteous in God's sight. The order is notable: washed, then sanctified, then justified. This sequence does not match any systematic theological order but follows an experiential logic: first the cleansing from the old (washing), then the dedication to the new (sanctification), then the declaration of standing (justification). All three are aorist — completed actions, decisive breaks with the past.</p>
          <p>The force of this verse within the argument is twofold. First, it establishes the magnitude of the transformation the Corinthians have undergone. They were the very people on the list of the excluded. They are no longer those people. The change is real and radical. Second, it intensifies the warning. If they have been washed, sanctified, and justified — removed from the categories that exclude from the kingdom — then to return to those behaviors is to un-become what they have become. Paul does not state this consequence explicitly here, but the logic is inescapable: if the <hi type="italic">adikoi</hi> will not inherit the kingdom, and you were <hi type="italic">adikoi</hi> but have been transformed, then to revert to <hi type="italic">adikia</hi> is to place yourself back among those who will not inherit. The transformation was a rescue from a defined condition. Returning to that condition is returning to its consequences.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.6.12-1Cor.6.14">
          <title>Verses 12-14: The "All Things Are Lawful" Slogan and the Body's Purpose</title>
          <p>"All things are lawful for me, but not all things are beneficial. All things are lawful for me, but I will not be dominated by anything" (6:12).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">panta moi exestin</hi> ("all things are lawful for me" or "all things are permissible for me") is almost certainly a Corinthian slogan that Paul is quoting back to them. The word <hi type="italic">exestin</hi> ("it is permitted, it is lawful, it is within one's rights") expresses permission or liberty. The slogan likely arose from a misapplication of Paul's own teaching about freedom from the law. If believers are no longer under Torah as a legal code, the Corinthians reasoned, then all behavioral restrictions have been removed. Everything is permissible. Paul taught them freedom; they heard license.</p>
          <p>Paul responds to the slogan not by denying it outright but by qualifying it with two counters.</p>
          <p>First: <hi type="italic">all' ou panta sympherei</hi> ("but not all things are beneficial/advantageous"). The verb <hi type="italic">symphero</hi> ("to bring together, to be profitable, to be advantageous, to be beneficial") introduces a criterion beyond mere permissibility. Even if an action is permitted, the question remains: does it benefit? Does it build up? Does it serve the good of the community and the individual? The word <hi type="italic">symphero</hi> will appear again in 10:23 and 12:7 ("the manifestation of the Spirit is given for the common benefit"). Freedom is real, but freedom without regard for consequences is not wisdom — it is the kind of folly Paul has been diagnosing since chapter 1.</p>
          <p>Second: <hi type="italic">all' ouk ego exousiasthesomai hypo tinos</hi> ("but I will not be mastered/dominated by anything"). The verb <hi type="italic">exousiazo</hi> ("to have authority over, to exercise power over, to dominate") in the passive means "to be brought under the power of, to be mastered by, to be enslaved to." The wordplay is deliberate: <hi type="italic">exestin</hi> (it is "permissible," from <hi type="italic">exousia</hi>, "authority/right") becomes <hi type="italic">exousiasthesomai</hi> ("I will be placed under the authority of"). The Corinthians claim authority over all things (<hi type="italic">panta moi exestin</hi> — "I have authority/permission over all things"); Paul counters that the exercise of unrestrained license actually produces the opposite of authority — it produces slavery. The person who does whatever is permissible without regard for consequences does not demonstrate freedom but demonstrates bondage to appetite. True freedom is the power to say no, not merely the right to say yes.</p>
          <p>"Food is meant for the stomach and the stomach for food — and God will destroy both one and the other" (6:13a).</p>
          <p>This appears to be another Corinthian slogan, or at least the beginning of one. The phrase <hi type="italic">ta bromata te koilia kai he koilia tois bromasin</hi> ("foods for the stomach and the stomach for foods") expresses a natural correspondence: food and stomach are made for each other. The apparent logic: just as the stomach is designed for food and food for the stomach, so the body has natural appetites that should be satisfied. If hunger justifies eating, then sexual desire justifies sexual fulfillment. The body wants what it wants; give it what it was made for.</p>
          <p>Paul's insertion — <hi type="italic">ho de theos kai tauten kai tauta katargesei</hi> ("but God will destroy both the one and the others") — disrupts the analogy. The verb <hi type="italic">katargeo</hi> ("to render inoperative, to abolish, to destroy, to nullify") indicates that the stomach-food correspondence is temporary. God will abolish both. The digestive system and its objects belong to the present age, which is passing away (7:31). The body's appetites for food are not a permanent feature of existence; they belong to the mortal, perishable order that God will bring to an end.</p>
          <p>"But the body is not meant for sexual immorality, but for the Lord, and the Lord for the body" (6:13b).</p>
          <p>Paul breaks the Corinthian analogy. The Corinthians wanted to extend the stomach-food logic to the body-sex relationship: as the stomach is for food, so the body is for sex. Paul denies the parallel. The body (<hi type="italic">soma</hi>) is not for <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> ("sexual immorality") but for the Lord (<hi type="italic">to kyrio</hi>). The correspondence is not body-sex but body-Lord. The body belongs to the Lord, and the Lord belongs to the body. The phrase <hi type="italic">ho kyrios to somati</hi> ("the Lord for the body") is remarkable: it asserts a reciprocal relationship. The body is given to the Lord for his service; the Lord is given to the body for its redemption and glorification. The Lord does not discard the body; he claims it.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">soma</hi> ("body") in Paul's usage is not merely the physical frame. It is the whole person as a concrete, embodied being — the person in their physicality, their capacity for relationship, their public, tangible existence. What Paul is asserting is that the entire embodied person belongs to the Lord. The body is not a disposable container for an immaterial soul; it is the person as God made them, and it has a Lord who claims it.</p>
          <p>"And God raised the Lord and will also raise us up by his power" (6:14).</p>
          <p>The conjunction <hi type="italic">de</hi> ("and, but") connects the body's belonging to the Lord with the evidence for that belonging: the resurrection. <hi type="italic">Ho theos kai ton kyrion egeiren</hi> ("God raised the Lord") — the aorist tense marks the resurrection as a completed historical event. <hi type="italic">Kai hemas exegerei dia tes dynameos autou</hi> ("and he will raise us through his power") — the future tense marks the believers' resurrection as a future event, accomplished by God's <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> ("power"). The same word <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> that described the gospel's power (1:18, 24) and the Spirit's power (2:4-5) and the kingdom's power (4:20) now describes the power by which God will raise the dead.</p>
          <p>The argument against the Corinthian slogan is now complete. The stomach-food relationship is temporary; God will destroy both. But the body-Lord relationship is permanent. God raised the Lord's body from the dead, and God will raise the believers' bodies from the dead. The body is not disposable; it is destined for resurrection. Therefore the body cannot be treated as morally neutral territory where any appetite may be indulged. The body that God will raise is the body that must be used for the Lord's purposes now. What you do with your body matters precisely because your body has an eternal future.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.6.15-1Cor.6.17">
          <title>Verses 15-17: Members of Christ and Union with a Prostitute</title>
          <p>"Do you not know that your bodies are members of Christ? Shall I then take the members of Christ and make them members of a prostitute? Never!" (6:15).</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Ouk oidate</hi> ("do you not know?") appears again. The claim: <hi type="italic">ta somata hymon mele Christou estin</hi> ("your bodies are members of Christ"). The word <hi type="italic">mele</hi> ("members, limbs, body parts") describes the organic connection between the individual believer's body and Christ. The image is not metaphorical in the sense of being merely illustrative; Paul means that the believers' bodies are genuinely incorporated into Christ. They are limbs of Christ's body. The word <hi type="italic">mele</hi> will be developed extensively in chapter 12, where Paul will elaborate the body of Christ metaphor: "you are the body of Christ and individually members of it" (12:27). But the foundation is laid here: the physical bodies of believers are Christ's members.</p>
          <p>The question that follows is constructed to provoke horror: <hi type="italic">aras oun ta mele tou Christou poieso pornes mele</hi> ("shall I then take the members of Christ and make them members of a prostitute?"). The verb <hi type="italic">airo</hi> ("to take, to take up, to lift, to remove") followed by <hi type="italic">poieo</hi> ("to make, to create, to produce") describes the act of detaching Christ's members and re-attaching them to a prostitute (<hi type="italic">porne</hi>). The word <hi type="italic">porne</hi> is the feminine form of <hi type="italic">pornos</hi> and designates a woman who sells sexual access to her body. In Corinth, a major commercial city with a significant transient population, prostitution was widely available and socially tolerated among pagans.</p>
          <p>The response: <hi type="italic">me genoito</hi> ("may it never be!" or "let it not happen!" — literally "may it not come to be"). This is Paul's strongest formula of rejection, used throughout his letters to deny conclusions that would follow from a faulty premise (Romans 3:4, 6, 31; 6:2, 15; 7:7, 13; 9:14; 11:1, 11; Galatians 2:17; 3:21). The phrase expresses not merely disagreement but revulsion at the very idea.</p>
          <p>"Or do you not know that he who is joined to a prostitute becomes one body with her? For, as it is written, 'The two will become one flesh'" (6:16).</p>
          <p>Paul grounds the horror in Scripture. The verb <hi type="italic">kollao</hi> ("to join, to glue, to cement, to unite, to cling to") in the passive (<hi type="italic">kollomenos</hi>) means "the one who is joined/united/glued to." In the LXX, the word describes close attachment: Deuteronomy 10:20, "you shall fear the LORD your God; you shall serve him and hold fast to him" (<hi type="italic">auto kollethese</hi>); Ruth 1:14, Ruth "clung to" (<hi type="italic">ekolleethe</hi>) Naomi. The verb carries a sense of permanent adhesion, not casual contact. The one who is joined to a prostitute <hi type="italic">hen soma estin</hi> ("is one body") — the sexual act creates a union, a one-body reality.</p>
          <p>The scriptural authority is Genesis 2:24 (LXX): <hi type="italic">esontai hoi dyo eis sarka mian</hi> ("the two shall become one flesh"). Paul introduces it with <hi type="italic">gephsin gar</hi> ("for it says" or "for [Scripture] says"). Genesis 2:24 in its original context describes the marriage bond: "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother and hold fast to his wife, and they shall become one flesh." The "one flesh" (<hi type="italic">sarx mia</hi>) language describes the organic union that sexual intercourse creates. Paul's application is direct: if the marriage union makes two people one flesh, then sexual union with a prostitute likewise makes two people one flesh. The Genesis principle operates regardless of the relational context. The one-flesh union is not an optional metaphysical upgrade available only within marriage; it is an inherent consequence of sexual intercourse itself. To unite with a prostitute is therefore to create a one-flesh bond between Christ's member and that prostitute — to drag Christ's own body into a union with <hi type="italic">porneia</hi>.</p>
          <p>"But he who is joined to the Lord becomes one spirit with him" (6:17).</p>
          <p>The parallel structure is deliberate: <hi type="italic">ho kollomenos to kyrio hen pneuma estin</hi> ("the one who is joined/united to the Lord is one spirit"). The contrast between verse 16 and verse 17 is between <hi type="italic">hen soma</hi> ("one body") and <hi type="italic">hen pneuma</hi> ("one spirit"). Union with a prostitute creates a body-union; union with the Lord creates a spirit-union. The two unions are incompatible. One cannot simultaneously be one spirit with the Lord and one body with a prostitute. The claim is not that the body is irrelevant but precisely the opposite: because the body is a member of Christ (v. 15), what the body does matters for the spirit-union with the Lord. The body and the spirit are not separable compartments; they are aspects of a single person. To defile the body is to assault the spirit-union.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">kollao</hi> is the same in both verses. The one who is "joined" to the Lord is joined with the same kind of adhesion as the one who is "joined" to a prostitute. Both unions are real. Both are consequential. They cannot coexist.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.6.18-1Cor.6.20">
          <title>Verses 18-20: Flight from <hi type="italic">Porneia</hi> and the Body as Temple</title>
          <p>"Flee from sexual immorality. Every other sin a person commits is outside the body, but the sexually immoral person sins against his own body" (6:18).</p>
          <p>The imperative <hi type="italic">pheugete ten porneian</hi> ("flee from sexual immorality") is urgent and unqualified. The verb <hi type="italic">pheugo</hi> ("to flee, to run away from, to escape") describes not resistance but flight — not standing to fight but running in the opposite direction. The same verb describes Joseph's response to Potiphar's wife in Genesis 39:12 (LXX): <hi type="italic">ephygen kai exelthen exo</hi> ("he fled and went outside"). The command recognizes that <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> is not a temptation to be negotiated with but a danger to be escaped. The present imperative (<hi type="italic">pheugete</hi>) indicates that this is to be an ongoing pattern: keep fleeing, make it your habit.</p>
          <p>The next statement — <hi type="italic">pan hamartema ho ean poiese anthropos ektos tou somatos estin, ho de porneoun eis to idion soma hamartanei</hi> ("every sin that a person commits is outside the body, but the one who commits sexual immorality sins against his own body") — is debated. Some scholars read the first half as another Corinthian slogan: "every sin a person commits is outside the body" would be the Corinthians' argument that bodily actions do not affect one's spiritual standing. Paul would then counter: "but the one who commits sexual immorality sins against his own body." Whether or not the first half is a slogan, Paul's own point is clear: <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> is a sin with a unique relationship to the body. Other sins may use the body as an instrument, but <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> turns the body itself — the member of Christ, the temple of the Spirit — into the site of the violation. The body is not merely the tool by which the sin is committed; the body is the victim of the sin. The sexually immoral person sins <hi type="italic">eis to idion soma</hi> ("against his own body") — the preposition <hi type="italic">eis</hi> ("into, against, toward") indicates that the sin's damage is directed inward, against the very body that belongs to Christ and will be raised by God's power.</p>
          <p>"Or do you not know that your body is a temple of the Holy Spirit within you, whom you have from God?" (6:19a).</p>
          <p>This is the sixth <hi type="italic">ouk oidate</hi> in the chapter. The claim extends the temple metaphor from 3:16-17. There, the community as a whole was identified as <hi type="italic">naos theou</hi> ("God's temple") in which God's Spirit dwells. Here, the individual believer's body (<hi type="italic">to soma hymon</hi>, "your body" — though the plural <hi type="italic">hymon</hi> includes the whole community) is <hi type="italic">naos tou hagiou pneumatos</hi> ("a temple of the Holy Spirit"). The word <hi type="italic">naos</hi> is the same: the inner sanctuary, the dwelling place of the divine presence. The Holy Spirit (<hi type="italic">to hagion pneuma</hi>) is specified as <hi type="italic">tou en hymin</hi> ("who is in you") — the Spirit resides within the believer's body, as God's presence once resided in the temple's inner sanctuary.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">hou echete apo theou</hi> ("whom you have from God") identifies the Spirit as a gift received from God. The verb <hi type="italic">echete</hi> ("you have") is present tense — the Spirit is currently possessed, an ongoing reality. The preposition <hi type="italic">apo</hi> ("from") identifies the source: the Spirit comes from God. The Spirit is not the community's own possession by right; it is a gift from God that establishes the body as God's dwelling place.</p>
          <p>The implications of the temple identification are far-reaching. In 3:16-17, Paul warned that anyone who destroys God's temple will be destroyed by God. Now the individual body is also identified as God's temple. The logic of 3:17 applies: to defile or destroy what is holy — what God has made his dwelling place — is to invite God's corresponding action. The holiness of the temple is not negotiable, whether the temple is the community (3:16) or the individual body (6:19).</p>
          <p>"You are not your own, for you were bought with a price. So glorify God in your body" (6:19b-20).</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Ouk este heautoon</hi> ("you are not your own") — the negative claim of self-ownership. The Corinthian slogan "all things are lawful for me" (v. 12) implied autonomous self-determination: I have the right to do as I please with my own body. Paul denies the premise. You do not own yourself. The language of purchase — <hi type="italic">egorasthete gar times</hi> ("for you were bought with a price") — draws from the marketplace. The verb <hi type="italic">agorazo</hi> ("to buy, to purchase, to acquire in the marketplace") and the noun <hi type="italic">time</hi> ("price, value, cost") describe a commercial transaction. Something of value was paid, and as a result, ownership transferred. The Corinthians were acquired; they now belong to the one who purchased them.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">agorazo</hi> appears in the LXX in various commercial contexts, but its most theologically significant usage connects to redemption. In Leviticus 25:47-55, the laws of redemption provide for a kinsman to "buy back" (a form of redemption language) a family member who has sold himself into slavery. The redeemed person's freedom was purchased; the redeemer paid the price. The concept of God as Israel's redeemer (<hi type="italic">go'el</hi>) — the one who buys back what has been lost or enslaved — runs throughout the prophets: Isaiah 43:1, "I have redeemed you; I have called you by name, you are mine"; Isaiah 44:22-23, "I have redeemed you... for the LORD has redeemed Jacob"; Isaiah 52:3, "you were sold for nothing, and you shall be redeemed without money." Paul does not specify what the "price" (<hi type="italic">time</hi>) was in this passage, but the context of the letter — "Christ, our Passover lamb, has been sacrificed" (5:7) — points to Christ's death as the payment. The community was purchased by the death of Christ. Their bodies belong to the one who bought them.</p>
          <p>The consequence: <hi type="italic">doxasate de ton theon en to somati hymon</hi> ("therefore glorify God in your body"). The verb <hi type="italic">doxazo</hi> ("to glorify, to honor, to magnify") is the aorist imperative — a command for decisive, concrete action. The preposition <hi type="italic">en</hi> ("in, with, by means of") followed by <hi type="italic">to somati hymon</hi> ("your body") indicates that the body is the instrument and location of glorification. Glorifying God is not a purely internal, spiritual activity; it takes place in and through the physical body. The body that is Christ's member, the temple of the Holy Spirit, the possession purchased with a price, must be used for its owner's glory. The command closes the argument by uniting every thread: the body belongs to the Lord (v. 13), the body is a member of Christ (v. 15), the body is united to the Lord in spirit (v. 17), the body is the Spirit's temple (v. 19), the body was purchased (v. 20). Therefore, use the body for the purpose for which it was purchased: the glory of God.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.6">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 6 carries forward and completes the argument about community identity and boundaries that Paul began in chapter 5. The chapter has two major sections, but they are not unrelated subjects. They are two manifestations of the same disease: the community does not understand what it is, what it was purchased for, and how its members must conduct themselves in light of that identity.</p>
          <p>The first section (vv. 1-11) addresses the judicial failure. The community that should judge its own members (5:12) is instead submitting its disputes to pagan courts (6:1-6). Paul's argument escalates through three levels: (1) You should handle these disputes internally, because you are destined to judge the world and angels (vv. 1-6). (2) You should not have lawsuits at all, because you should be willing to absorb wrong rather than litigate against brothers (v. 7). (3) In fact, you are the ones committing the wrong, and wronging your own brothers at that (v. 8). The escalation exposes not merely a procedural error (wrong venue) but a moral failure (the community is perpetrating injustice). The vice catalogue of verses 9-10 widens the lens: the behaviors that exclude from God's kingdom include the very things some Corinthians are doing (wronging and defrauding brothers, v. 8). The reminder in verse 11 — "such were some of you, but you were washed, sanctified, justified" — establishes the stakes. The transformation was real. To revert is to undo what God did.</p>
          <p>The second section (vv. 12-20) addresses the sexual failure. The Corinthian slogan "all things are lawful" (v. 12) expresses a theology of freedom without accountability — a distortion of Paul's own teaching about freedom from the law. Paul counters the slogan with three qualifications: not all things are beneficial (v. 12a), freedom exercised without restraint becomes slavery (v. 12b), and the body is destined for resurrection, not for disposal (vv. 13-14). The body is a member of Christ (v. 15); sexual union with a prostitute creates a one-flesh bond that drags Christ's member into <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> (vv. 15-16). The body is the Spirit's temple (v. 19); defiling it is defiling God's dwelling place. The body was purchased at a price (v. 20); using it for <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> is misusing what belongs to another.</p>
          <p>The two sections converge on a single point: the Corinthians' bodies and communal life are not their own to dispose of as they see fit. They belong to God, who purchased them through Christ and indwells them through the Spirit. Their behavior — judicial, sexual, social — must reflect this ownership. The chapter thus extends the holiness argument of 3:16-17 (the community is God's temple and must not be destroyed) and 5:6-8 (the Passover community must purge the leaven) into two specific areas of Corinthian failure: their use of pagan courts and their tolerance of sexual immorality.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ouk oidate</hi> ("do you not know?") appears six times in this chapter (vv. 2, 3, 9, 15, 16, 19), more than in any other chapter of the letter. The repetition is significant. Paul is not introducing new doctrine. He is calling the Corinthians back to what they already know — truths they have received, teachings they have been given, realities about their own identity that they are systematically failing to live out. The Corinthian problem is not ignorance but inconsistency. They know what they are; they do not act on what they know. They know they are saints destined to judge the world (v. 2), yet they cannot settle petty disputes among themselves. They know they are washed, sanctified, and justified (v. 11), yet they practice the behaviors of the unwashed. They know their bodies are Christ's members and the Spirit's temple (vv. 15, 19), yet they join those bodies to prostitutes.</p>
          <p>The transition to chapter 7 is prepared by the final verses of chapter 6. The body's belonging to God, the significance of sexual conduct, the proper use of the body — these themes generate the questions about marriage and celibacy that the Corinthians raised in their letter and that Paul will address beginning in 7:1. The <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> that Paul has been addressing in chapters 5-6 becomes the background against which Paul's counsel about marriage — "because of the temptation to sexual immorality, each man should have his own wife and each woman her own husband" (7:2) — should be read. Marriage is not merely a social arrangement; it is part of the framework by which the community maintains the holiness of the body that belongs to God.</p>
          <p>The argument across chapters 5-6 can be summarized as a single sustained claim: the community's identity as God's holy people, purchased by Christ, indwelt by the Spirit, and destined for resurrection and cosmic judgment, requires the community both to exercise internal discipline (chapter 5, handling the incestuous man; 6:1-8, handling disputes internally) and to maintain the holiness of its members' bodies (5:1-5, expelling the man whose sexual conduct defiles the community; 6:12-20, refusing to join Christ's members to prostitutes). The community cannot be God's temple and simultaneously harbor what defiles God's temple. The community cannot be the body of Christ and simultaneously unite Christ's members to <hi type="italic">porneia</hi>. The community cannot be the assembly of the saints and simultaneously behave as the <hi type="italic">adikoi</hi> who will not inherit the kingdom. What the community is determines what the community must do — and what it must not tolerate.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.7">
        <title>1 Corinthians 7 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 5-6):</hi> After concluding his four-chapter treatment of the factionalism crisis (chapters 1-4), Paul turned to a series of concrete failures within the Corinthian community. Chapter 5 addressed a specific case of sexual immorality — a man maintaining a sexual arrangement with his father's wife, a violation of Leviticus 18:8 and Deuteronomy 22:30 (LXX 23:1) — which the community had not only tolerated but was "puffed up" about (5:2). Paul commanded the community to expel the man, "handing him over to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, so that his spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord" (5:5). The chapter established the distinction between inside and outside: the community judges those within; God judges those without (5:12-13). It closed with the Deuteronomic formula: "Purge the evil person from among you" (5:13, citing Deuteronomy 17:7 et al.).</p>
          <p>Chapter 6 addressed two further failures. First (6:1-11), members were taking disputes to pagan courts instead of resolving them internally — the mirror image of chapter 5's failure. The community that should have judged the immoral man (chapter 5) was outsourcing its other disputes to outsiders (6:1-8). Paul asked: "Do you not know that the saints will judge the world?" (6:2). He then listed categories of people who "will not inherit the kingdom of God" — <hi type="italic">pornoi, eidololatrai, moichoi, malakoi, arsenokoitai, kleptai, pleonektai, methysoi, loidoroi, harpages</hi> (6:9-10) — and declared: "And such were some of you. But you were washed, you were sanctified, you were justified in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ and by the Spirit of our God" (6:11). The past tense is emphatic: those identities were left behind.</p>
          <p>The second half of chapter 6 (6:12-20) addressed sexual immorality in general, specifically union with prostitutes. This section is the immediate precursor to chapter 7 and its arguments flow directly into what follows. Paul quoted and corrected a Corinthian slogan: "All things are lawful for me" (<hi type="italic">panta moi exestin</hi>, 6:12) — apparently a phrase some Corinthians used to justify their conduct. Paul's response: "but not all things are beneficial" (<hi type="italic">all' ou panta sympherei</hi>) and "I will not be dominated by anything" (<hi type="italic">all' ouk ego exousiasthesomai hypo tinos</hi>, 6:12). He then argued that the body is not for <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> but "for the Lord, and the Lord for the body" (6:13). God raised the Lord and will raise the believers by his power (6:14). The believers' bodies are "members of Christ" (<hi type="italic">mele Christou</hi>, 6:15). To unite Christ's members with a prostitute is to become one body with her, citing Genesis 2:24: "the two shall become one flesh" (6:16). "But the one who joins himself to the Lord is one spirit with him" (6:17). Paul commanded: "Flee <hi type="italic">porneia</hi>" (6:18). Every sin a person commits is "outside the body" (<hi type="italic">ektos tou somatos</hi>), but the sexually immoral person "sins against his own body" (<hi type="italic">eis to idion soma hamartanei</hi>, 6:18). The body is "a temple of the Holy Spirit within you" (<hi type="italic">naos tou en hymin hagiou pneumatos</hi>, 6:19). The final declaration: "You are not your own, for you were bought with a price. So glorify God in your body" (6:19-20).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 8):</hi> Paul introduces a new topic with the same formula he uses to open chapter 7: "Now concerning" (<hi type="italic">peri de</hi>). "Now concerning food offered to idols" (<hi type="italic">peri de ton eidolothyton</hi>, 8:1). This is the second major question the Corinthians raised in their letter. Paul addresses the tension between "knowledge" (<hi type="italic">gnosis</hi>) — which "puffs up" (<hi type="italic">physioi</hi>, using the same verb that has diagnosed the Corinthian disease since 4:6) — and "love" (<hi type="italic">agape</hi>), which "builds up" (<hi type="italic">oikodomei</hi>, 8:1). The issue is whether believers may eat meat that has been sacrificed in pagan temples. Paul acknowledges the knowledge that "an idol is nothing" (8:4) and that "there is no God but one" (8:4). But he warns that this knowledge, deployed without regard for the "weak" (<hi type="italic">astheneis</hi>) conscience of other believers, can destroy the brother "for whom Christ died" (8:11). Chapter 8 thus continues the pattern established in chapters 5-7: Paul addresses specific practical questions about how the community's identity in Christ governs its daily conduct — sexual behavior (chapters 5-7) giving way to eating practices (chapters 8-10). The thread connecting all these sections is the relationship between the believer's body, the community's holiness, and the surrounding pagan culture.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 7 connects to what precedes and follows:</hi> Chapter 7 does not change subjects abruptly. It is the direct continuation of the body theology that Paul developed in 6:12-20. Having argued that the body belongs to the Lord, that the body is a temple of the Holy Spirit, that "you were bought with a price," and having commanded the community to "flee <hi type="italic">porneia</hi>," Paul now addresses the positive side of the same question: what is the proper place of sexual union, of marriage, of celibacy? The Corinthians' letter apparently included questions about these matters, and Paul signals this with the formula "<hi type="italic">peri de</hi>" ("now concerning") in 7:1 — the first use of a phrase that will structure much of the rest of the letter (7:1, 7:25, 8:1, 12:1, 16:1, 16:12).</p>
          <p>The connection operates at multiple levels. At the level of vocabulary, the word <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> appears in 7:2 as the danger that proper marriage guards against, connecting directly to the <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> Paul condemned in 5:1, 6:13, and 6:18. The phrase "your own body" (<hi type="italic">to idion soma</hi>) from 6:18 reappears conceptually in 7:4, where Paul says neither spouse has authority over their own body. The language of "calling" (<hi type="italic">kaleo, klesis</hi>) that opened the letter (1:1-2, 9, 24, 26) reappears as a major structuring concept in 7:15, 17, 18, 20, 21, 22, 24, where Paul establishes the principle that each person should remain in the condition in which God called them. The eschatological framework — the community living between the "already" of Christ's work and the "not yet" of his return — which has been present since 1:7-8 ("as you wait for the revealing of our Lord Jesus Christ") becomes explicit and urgent in 7:26-31, where Paul describes "the present distress" and the "shortened time" that shapes all his practical counsel.</p>
          <p>At the level of argument, chapter 7 completes a logical sequence. Chapter 5 said: this sexual arrangement violates your identity; expel the offender. Chapter 6 said: your body belongs to the Lord; flee <hi type="italic">porneia</hi>. Chapter 7 now says: here is how sexual life properly functions within the community's identity, and here is the broader principle that governs all questions about changing one's status — remain in the condition in which God called you, because the form of this world is passing away. Chapter 7 also transitions toward chapter 8 by establishing the pattern of responding to specific Corinthian questions with pastoral counsel that applies theological principles to concrete situations.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7.1">
          <title>Verse 1: The Corinthian Slogan and Paul's Response</title>
          <p>"Now concerning the matters about which you wrote: 'It is good for a man not to touch a woman'" (7:1).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">peri de hon egrapsate</hi> ("now concerning the things about which you wrote") signals a new section of the letter. Paul shifts from addressing problems he has heard about through reports (Chloe's people, 1:11; the general report, 5:1) to responding to questions the Corinthians themselves raised in a letter to him. The verb <hi type="italic">egrapsate</hi> ("you wrote," aorist of <hi type="italic">grapho</hi>) confirms what was implied in 5:9 — there is ongoing correspondence between Paul and this community. Their letter contained questions, and Paul is now working through them.</p>
          <p>The statement <hi type="italic">kalon anthropo gynaikos me haptesthai</hi> ("it is good for a man not to touch a woman") is almost certainly a quotation from the Corinthians' own letter or a slogan circulating in the community. Several features support this reading. First, Paul uses the same technique elsewhere in this letter, quoting Corinthian slogans and then qualifying or correcting them: "all things are lawful for me" (6:12), "food is for the stomach and the stomach for food" (6:13), "all of us possess knowledge" (8:1). Second, the immediately following verse (7:2) begins with a "but" (<hi type="italic">de</hi>) that introduces a qualification, suggesting that verse 1b states a position Paul is about to modify. Third, the content of the slogan — an absolute prohibition on sexual contact — is more extreme than anything Paul himself argues in this chapter; Paul's own position, as the chapter will make clear, is considerably more nuanced.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">haptesthai</hi> ("to touch, to take hold of, to cling to") is a euphemism for sexual contact. In the LXX, the verb <hi type="italic">hapto</hi> in its middle voice carries this sense: Genesis 20:6, where God tells Abimelech, "I did not let you touch her" (<hi type="italic">ouk apheka se hapsasthai autes</hi>), referring to Sarah; Proverbs 6:29, "so is he who goes in to his neighbor's wife; no one who touches her will go unpunished" (<hi type="italic">pas ho haptomenos autes ou kathariethesetai</hi>). The slogan is not about casual physical contact but about sexual union.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">kalon</hi> ("good, beautiful, fitting, appropriate") asserts that sexual abstinence is a good thing. Some in the Corinthian community have apparently taken Paul's body theology — the body is the Lord's temple, flee <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> — and extended it into a general ascetic principle: all sexual contact, even within marriage, should be avoided. This may reflect the influence of certain Greco-Roman philosophical traditions. Stoic thinkers such as Musonius Rufus (a near-contemporary of Paul) debated whether sexual pleasure was permissible even within marriage, arguing that intercourse should be limited to procreation. Certain streams of Cynic philosophy idealized the renunciation of bodily desires altogether. Within Jewish tradition, temporary sexual abstinence for purposes of prayer and purity had precedent (Exodus 19:15, where Moses tells the people to "not go near a woman" before the theophany at Sinai; 1 Samuel 21:4-5, where David's men affirm their purity from women before eating the bread of the Presence). But the Corinthian slogan goes further than temporary abstinence; it asserts abstinence as an absolute good.</p>
          <p>Paul's response throughout the chapter will neither fully endorse nor fully reject this slogan. He will affirm that celibacy has genuine value (7:7-8, 26, 32-35, 38, 40) while insisting that marriage is proper, that married couples have mutual obligations, and that the ascetic impulse must not be imposed on those who do not possess the capacity for it.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7.2-1Cor.7.5">
          <title>Verses 2-5: Marriage, Mutual Obligation, and the Body</title>
          <p>"But because of cases of sexual immorality, each man should have his own wife and each woman her own husband" (7:2).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">de</hi> ("but") introduces Paul's qualification of the Corinthian slogan. The phrase <hi type="italic">dia de tas porneias</hi> ("but because of the cases of sexual immorality") identifies the practical danger that unqualified abstinence creates. The plural <hi type="italic">porneias</hi> ("sexual immoralities, instances of fornication") does not refer to one specific case (as in chapter 5) but to the general threat of sexual transgression — the same <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> Paul commanded them to flee in 6:18. If sexual abstinence is imposed as an absolute rule, including on those who are unable to sustain it, the result will not be holiness but <hi type="italic">porneia</hi>. The ascetic program, taken to its extreme, will produce the very thing it seeks to eliminate.</p>
          <p>Paul's counsel is concrete: <hi type="italic">hekastos ten heautou gynaika echeto kai hekaste ton idion andra echeto</hi> — "each man should have his own wife, and each woman should have her own husband." The verb <hi type="italic">echo</hi> ("to have") in the present imperative indicates an ongoing arrangement: each person should maintain the marital relationship. The reciprocity is notable. Paul does not say "each man should have a wife" (which would leave the woman passive). He says each man should have his own wife and each woman should have her own husband. The two clauses are parallel and symmetrical. Marriage is presented not as a male prerogative but as a mutual arrangement in which both partners have legitimate claims.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">idion</hi> ("one's own") and <hi type="italic">heautou</hi> ("his own") emphasize the exclusivity and particularity of the marital bond. This is not a general permission for sexual activity but a direction toward the specific, committed relationship of marriage. The language echoes the singular "his own body" (<hi type="italic">to idion soma</hi>) from 6:18 and anticipates the body-authority language that follows immediately.</p>
          <p>"The husband should give to his wife her conjugal rights, and likewise the wife to her husband" (7:3).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ten opheilomenen eunoian</hi> ("the owed/obligated goodwill" or, in many manuscripts, <hi type="italic">ten opheilen</hi>, "the debt, the obligation") identifies the sexual dimension of marriage as a matter of obligation, not merely permission. The verb <hi type="italic">opheilo</hi> ("to owe, to be obligated, to be indebted") treats the sexual relationship as something owed — a debt that each partner carries toward the other. The concept is not primarily about desire or pleasure but about mutual obligation within the covenant of marriage.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">eunoia</hi> ("goodwill, favor, affection") in some manuscripts, or the simpler <hi type="italic">opheile</hi> ("debt, what is owed") in others, describes the conjugal dimension of the marriage relationship. Paul is not innovating here. Jewish tradition, particularly as reflected in Exodus 21:10, recognized the husband's obligation to provide his wife with food, clothing, and conjugal rights (<hi type="italic">onah</hi>). The <hi type="italic">Mishnah</hi> (Ketubot 5:6, though codified later, reflecting earlier practice) specifies the frequency of conjugal obligation based on the husband's occupation. Paul extends the obligation in both directions: not only does the husband owe the wife, but the wife also owes the husband. The reciprocity is remarkable in the ancient context, where sexual obligation was typically framed as a one-directional male right.</p>
          <p>"For the wife does not have authority over her own body, but the husband does. Likewise the husband does not have authority over his own body, but the wife does" (7:4).</p>
          <p>This verse is one of the most striking statements about marriage in any first-century text. The verb <hi type="italic">exousiazo</hi> ("to have authority over, to exercise power over") comes from <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> ("authority, right, power"). In 6:12, Paul used the related form <hi type="italic">exousiasthesomai</hi> ("I will not be brought under the authority of anything") to describe his refusal to be dominated. Now the same root describes the authority that each spouse holds over the other's body.</p>
          <p>The structure is perfectly symmetrical:</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">He gyne tou idiou somatos ouk exousiazei alla ho aner</hi> — "the wife does not have authority over her own body, but the husband does."</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Homoios de kai ho aner tou idiou somatos ouk exousiazei alla he gyne</hi> — "likewise also the husband does not have authority over his own body, but the wife does."</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">homoios</hi> ("likewise, similarly, in the same way") forces the second clause to mirror the first exactly. What is true of the wife's body with respect to the husband is equally true of the husband's body with respect to the wife. Neither spouse is autonomous. Each has yielded bodily authority to the other. The concept of mutual authority over the other's body is without parallel in surviving first-century literature at this level of explicit reciprocity. Roman marriage law gave the <hi type="italic">paterfamilias</hi> authority over the household, including his wife; Jewish marriage law placed certain obligations on the husband but did not describe the wife as having authority over the husband's body. Paul's formulation is distinctively equalizing.</p>
          <p>The theological ground for this mutual surrender of bodily autonomy was laid in 6:19-20: "You are not your own, for you were bought with a price." If the individual believer does not have autonomous authority over his or her body because that body belongs to the Lord, then within marriage, the body also belongs to the spouse. The hierarchy of belonging runs: the body belongs to the Lord (6:19-20); within the marriage relationship, it also belongs to the spouse (7:4). Neither claim cancels the other. The Lord's ownership of the body includes the marriage relationship as a legitimate expression of bodily life; the spouse's claim on the body operates within the Lord's prior claim.</p>
          <p>"Do not deprive one another, except perhaps by agreement for a limited time, that you may devote yourselves to prayer; but then come together again, so that Satan may not tempt you because of your lack of self-control" (7:5).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">apostereo</hi> ("to deprive, to defraud, to rob, to withhold what is owed") is a strong word. In 6:7-8, Paul used the same verb: "Why not rather be defrauded?" (<hi type="italic">ti ouchi mallon apostereisthe</hi>, 6:7). In that context, it described the financial injustice that believers were suffering from other believers. Here it describes sexual withholding within marriage. The use of the same verb connects the two situations: withholding conjugal rights from a spouse is a form of defrauding them — taking what belongs to them. The present imperative with <hi type="italic">me</hi> (<hi type="italic">me apostereite</hi>) is a prohibition of ongoing action: stop depriving one another, or do not make a practice of depriving one another.</p>
          <p>Paul allows one exception: <hi type="italic">ei meti an ek symphonou pros kairon</hi> — "unless perhaps by agreement for a time." Three conditions limit the exception. First, <hi type="italic">ek symphonou</hi> ("by mutual agreement, by consent") — the abstinence must be jointly decided, not unilaterally imposed by one partner on the other. The word <hi type="italic">symphonos</hi> (from <hi type="italic">syn</hi>, "together," and <hi type="italic">phone</hi>, "voice") means to be in agreement, to sound together. Neither spouse may impose celibacy on the other. Second, <hi type="italic">pros kairon</hi> ("for a time, for a season") — the abstinence is temporary, not permanent. The word <hi type="italic">kairos</hi> ("time, season, appointed time") identifies a bounded period. Third, the purpose is specified: <hi type="italic">hina scholasete te proseuche</hi> — "so that you may devote yourselves to prayer." The verb <hi type="italic">scholazo</hi> ("to be free, to have leisure, to devote oneself") means to make oneself available, to clear space for something. The purpose of temporary abstinence is not ascetic self-denial for its own sake but a positive dedication to prayer. This echoes the Old Testament precedent of temporary sexual abstinence for sacred purposes (Exodus 19:15).</p>
          <p>The instruction to "come together again" (<hi type="italic">kai palin epi to auto ete</hi>, or in variant readings, <hi type="italic">synerchesthe</hi>) after the period of prayer requires the resumption of the sexual relationship. The reason: <hi type="italic">hina me peiraze hymas ho Satanas dia ten akrasian hymon</hi> — "so that Satan may not tempt you because of your lack of self-control." The word <hi type="italic">Satanas</hi> ("Satan, the adversary") appeared in 5:5 in connection with the expelled man; here the adversary operates differently — not as the agent of external punishment but as the exploiter of internal weakness. The word <hi type="italic">akrasia</hi> ("lack of self-control, incontinence") describes the inability to restrain one's desires. Paul is realistic about human capacity. Not everyone possesses the same ability to sustain celibacy. Prolonged abstinence within marriage, when one or both partners lack the capacity for it, creates vulnerability to temptation. The ascetic program that the Corinthian slogan advocates may sound spiritually impressive, but if it exceeds the actual capacity of the people involved, it opens the door to exactly the <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> it claims to prevent.</p>
          <p>The logic is deeply practical. Paul is not theorizing about ideal spiritual states; he is assessing real human beings with varying capacities and directing them toward arrangements that will actually sustain their faithfulness, not arrangements that look impressive but collapse under the pressure of reality.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7.6-1Cor.7.7">
          <title>Verses 6-7: Concession and Gift</title>
          <p>"Now I say this by way of concession, not of command" (7:6).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">syngnome</hi> ("concession, pardon, allowance, indulgence") means that what Paul has just said in verses 2-5 is a concession — an allowance for human weakness — not a command (<hi type="italic">epitage</hi>). Paul is not commanding marriage. He is not commanding sexual activity within marriage. He is granting permission, recognizing that for most people, marriage and its sexual dimension are the appropriate and necessary arrangement. The distinction between command and concession reveals Paul's own preference, which the next verse states explicitly.</p>
          <p>"I wish that all were as I myself am. But each has his own gift from God, one of one kind and one of another" (7:7).</p>
          <p>Paul's personal preference is clear: <hi type="italic">thelo de pantas anthropous einai hos kai emauton</hi> — "I wish all people were as I myself am." The verb <hi type="italic">thelo</hi> ("to wish, to desire, to want") expresses a genuine desire, not a command. Paul's own condition appears to be celibacy. Whether Paul was never married, widowed, or divorced is debated and the text does not specify. What is clear is that at the time of writing, Paul lives without a wife and considers this condition advantageous.</p>
          <p>But Paul immediately qualifies his preference with a theological principle: <hi type="italic">alla hekastos idion echei charisma ek theou, ho men houtos, ho de houtos</hi> — "but each has his own gift from God, one in this way, one in that way." The word <hi type="italic">charisma</hi> ("gift, grace-gift") is the same word used in 1:7 for the spiritual gifts the Corinthians possess in abundance. Paul places both celibacy and marriage under the category of <hi type="italic">charisma</hi> — divine gifts. Neither is earned, neither is a moral achievement, and neither is universally given. Some receive the <hi type="italic">charisma</hi> of celibacy; others receive the <hi type="italic">charisma</hi> of marriage. The phrase <hi type="italic">ek theou</hi> ("from God") identifies God as the source of both gifts. A person who lacks the gift of celibacy is not spiritually inferior; they have received a different gift. A person who possesses the gift of celibacy is not morally superior; they have received one gift rather than another.</p>
          <p>This theological framing is crucial for the chapter's argument. The Corinthian slogan ("it is good for a man not to touch a woman") implies that celibacy is the higher spiritual state. Paul responds that both are <hi type="italic">charismata</hi> from God. The hierarchy that the slogan assumes is flattened. The question is not which state is higher but which gift each person has received.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7.8-1Cor.7.9">
          <title>Verses 8-9: To the Unmarried and Widows</title>
          <p>"To the unmarried and the widows I say that it is good for them to remain single, as I am" (7:8).</p>
          <p>Paul now addresses two specific categories: <hi type="italic">tois agamois</hi> ("the unmarried") and <hi type="italic">tais cherais</hi> ("the widows"). The word <hi type="italic">agamos</hi> (from the alpha privative <hi type="italic">a-</hi> and <hi type="italic">gamos</hi>, "marriage") simply means "unmarried." It could include those who have never married, the divorced, or the widowed. The separate mention of widows (<hi type="italic">cherai</hi>) alongside the unmarried suggests that <hi type="italic">agamois</hi> here refers primarily to the never-married or the divorced, with widows named as an additional group.</p>
          <p>Paul reaffirms his personal judgment: <hi type="italic">kalon autois ean meinosin hos kago</hi> — "it is good for them if they remain as I am." The word <hi type="italic">kalon</hi> ("good") is the same word from the Corinthian slogan in verse 1. Paul agrees that celibacy is <hi type="italic">kalon</hi> — a genuine good. But his agreement is qualified: it is good for those who can sustain it. The verb <hi type="italic">meno</hi> ("to remain, to stay, to abide") introduces a concept that will become the chapter's governing principle. Paul will develop the idea of "remaining" extensively in verses 17-24, where it becomes the foundational instruction: remain in the condition in which God called you.</p>
          <p>"But if they cannot exercise self-control, they should marry. For it is better to marry than to burn with passion" (7:9).</p>
          <p>The conditional <hi type="italic">ei de ouk enkrateuontai</hi> ("but if they are not exercising self-control") uses the present indicative, describing an actual ongoing condition rather than a hypothetical possibility. The verb <hi type="italic">enkrateuomai</hi> ("to exercise self-control, to have power over oneself, to be continent") describes the capacity to restrain one's desires. Paul treats this capacity as something that some people possess and others do not — consistent with the <hi type="italic">charisma</hi> framework of verse 7. If a person does not have the gift of self-control sufficient for celibacy, marriage is the proper course.</p>
          <p>The imperative <hi type="italic">gamesatosan</hi> ("let them marry") is an aorist imperative — decisive action. The rationale: <hi type="italic">kreisson gar estin gamesai e pyrousthai</hi> — "for it is better to marry than to burn." The verb <hi type="italic">pyrousthai</hi> ("to burn, to be on fire") is a present passive infinitive indicating an ongoing state of burning. In context, this burning refers to the consuming fire of unfulfilled sexual desire. The metaphor of fire for desire appears in Jewish wisdom literature: Sirach 9:8, "Turn away your eyes from a shapely woman... many have been misled by a woman's beauty, and by it passion is kindled like a fire" (<hi type="italic">hos pyr ekkainetai</hi>). Paul's judgment is practical: an ongoing state of burning desire is destructive and dangerous, creating exactly the vulnerability to <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> that verse 2 identified. Marriage resolves this burning by providing a legitimate context for sexual expression.</p>
          <p>The comparison "better to marry than to burn" (<hi type="italic">kreisson gamesai e pyrousthai</hi>) is a comparative, not an absolute. Paul is not saying marriage is bad and celibacy good; he is saying that for those who burn, marriage is the better path. The assumption is that both options are open and both are legitimate, but the person's actual capacity determines which is appropriate.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7.10-1Cor.7.11">
          <title>Verses 10-11: The Lord's Command on Divorce</title>
          <p>"To the married I give this charge (not I, but the Lord): the wife should not separate from her husband" (7:10).</p>
          <p>Paul now addresses <hi type="italic">tous gegamekotas</hi> ("those who have married") — believers married to believers, as the context and the contrast with verses 12-16 (mixed marriages) make clear. The parenthetical <hi type="italic">ouk ego alla ho kyrios</hi> ("not I but the Lord") is striking. Paul distinguishes between his own pastoral counsel and a direct command from the Lord. Throughout this chapter, Paul has been careful to identify the source and authority of his instructions: verse 6 was a concession, not a command; verses 8-9 reflected his own judgment; now he cites a command that originates not from himself but from the Lord.</p>
          <p>The "Lord's" command is a reference to the teaching of Jesus on divorce, which is preserved in the synoptic tradition: Mark 10:2-12 (Jesus declares that what God has joined, no human being should separate, and that anyone who divorces and remarries commits adultery); Matthew 5:31-32 (the exception clause for <hi type="italic">porneia</hi>); Matthew 19:3-9 (the fuller version of the same teaching); Luke 16:18 (the compressed version). Paul has access to this tradition — whether through oral transmission from those who knew Jesus' teaching, or through early collections of Jesus' sayings — and he treats it as carrying the Lord's direct authority, distinct from his own apostolic counsel.</p>
          <p>The instruction: <hi type="italic">gynaika apo andros me choristhenai</hi> — "a wife should not be separated from her husband." The verb <hi type="italic">chorizo</hi> ("to separate, to divide, to put apart") in the passive (<hi type="italic">choristhenai</hi>) may be either passive ("be separated") or middle ("separate herself"). In the context of divorce proceedings, it functions as a technical term for the dissolution of a marriage. Paul addresses the wife first, which may reflect the specific situation in Corinth — some women, perhaps influenced by the ascetic slogan of verse 1, may have been leaving their husbands as a spiritual act.</p>
          <p>"(But if she does separate, let her remain unmarried or else be reconciled to her husband), and the husband should not divorce his wife" (7:11).</p>
          <p>The parenthetical <hi type="italic">ean de kai choristhe</hi> ("but if she does separate") acknowledges the reality that some separations may occur despite the Lord's command. Paul does not say the separation is legitimate; he addresses the situation that results from it. Two options are available to the separated wife: <hi type="italic">meneto agamos</hi> ("let her remain unmarried") or <hi type="italic">to andri katallageto</hi> ("let her be reconciled to her husband"). The verb <hi type="italic">katallasso</hi> ("to reconcile, to restore to right relationship, to exchange hostility for friendship") describes the repair of a broken relationship. Paul uses this word in 2 Corinthians 5:18-20 for God's reconciliation of the world to himself through Christ. The same word applied to the separated wife and husband indicates that restoration is the desired outcome. Remarriage to a different person is not among the options.</p>
          <p>The second instruction — <hi type="italic">kai andra gynaika me aphienai</hi> ("and a husband should not divorce his wife") — uses the verb <hi type="italic">aphiemi</hi> ("to send away, to let go, to release, to divorce"). In Greek legal usage, <hi type="italic">aphienai</hi> described the husband's act of formally dismissing his wife. The instruction mirrors the one given to the wife: the husband should not divorce. The Lord's command applies symmetrically to both partners.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7.12-1Cor.7.16">
          <title>Verses 12-16: Mixed Marriages — Believers Married to Unbelievers</title>
          <p>"To the rest I say (I, not the Lord): if any brother has a wife who is an unbeliever, and she consents to live with him, he should not divorce her" (7:12).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">tois de loipois</hi> ("to the rest") addresses a category not covered by the Lord's command in verses 10-11: marriages in which one partner is a believer and the other is not. The parenthetical <hi type="italic">lego ego, ouch ho kyrios</hi> ("I say, not the Lord") does not mean Paul's counsel lacks authority; it means that the historical Jesus did not address this specific situation in his teaching on divorce (which dealt with Jewish marriages under the Torah). Paul is making clear that for this case, the apostolic pastoral counsel is his own, exercised under the authority of his apostleship but distinct from a dominical command that he can cite directly.</p>
          <p>The term <hi type="italic">apistos</hi> ("unbeliever, faithless one, one who does not believe") identifies the non-believing spouse. In Paul's usage in this letter, the word describes someone outside the community of faith (cf. 6:6, where <hi type="italic">adelphos meta adelphou krinetai kai touto epi apiston</hi> refers to believers going before unbelievers in court; 14:22-24, where <hi type="italic">apistoi</hi> enter the assembly and hear prophecy). The situation Paul addresses is one that arose naturally in the early communities: a person came to faith after marriage, but the spouse did not. The conversion of one partner within an existing marriage created the question of whether the marriage could or should continue.</p>
          <p>Paul's instruction: <hi type="italic">me aphieto auton</hi> ("he should not divorce her," or more precisely, "let him not send her away"). If the unbelieving wife <hi type="italic">syneudokei oikein met' autou</hi> ("consents to live with him"), the believing husband should maintain the marriage. The verb <hi type="italic">syneudokeo</hi> ("to consent, to agree, to approve together") indicates that the unbeliever's willingness is a necessary condition. Paul does not instruct the believer to force the continuation of the marriage; the arrangement depends on the unbeliever's consent.</p>
          <p>"If any woman has a husband who is an unbeliever, and he consents to live with her, she should not divorce him" (7:13).</p>
          <p>The instruction is perfectly reciprocal. What applies to the believing husband with an unbelieving wife applies identically to the believing wife with an unbelieving husband. The verb <hi type="italic">aphieto</hi> ("let her not send away, let her not divorce") is the same. The principle is the same. Paul's counsel does not differentiate between the genders in mixed-marriage situations.</p>
          <p>"For the unbelieving husband is made holy because of his wife, and the unbelieving wife is made holy because of her husband. Otherwise your children would be unclean, but as it is, they are holy" (7:14).</p>
          <p>This is one of the most remarkable statements in the chapter. The verb <hi type="italic">hegiastai</hi> ("has been sanctified, has been made holy") is the perfect passive of <hi type="italic">hagiazo</hi> — the same verb used in 1:2 for the community's own sanctification ("to those sanctified in Christ Jesus," <hi type="italic">hegiasmenois en Christo Iesou</hi>). The unbelieving spouse is "sanctified" through the believing spouse. The preposition <hi type="italic">en</hi> ("in, by, through") identifies the believing partner as the means or sphere of this sanctification.</p>
          <p>What does this sanctification mean? It cannot mean that the unbelieving spouse has become a believer, since Paul continues to call them <hi type="italic">apistos</hi> and addresses the possibility of their departure in verse 15. The sanctification is relational, not personal conversion. The believing spouse's connection to the holy community extends a sphere of holiness that encompasses the marriage and the household. This concept has deep roots in Israel's understanding of holiness. In the Old Testament, holiness is not merely an abstract quality but a contagious reality that extends through relationships and spaces. The tabernacle consecrated the items within it; the priest's holiness affected the offerings he handled; the holy land set apart the people who dwelled in it. Haggai 2:12 illustrates the principle, though there the prophet argues that holiness does not transfer by second-order contact in the same way impurity does. Paul's application inverts the expected direction: instead of the unbeliever's impurity contaminating the believer (which might have been the Corinthians' fear, given their concern with purity in chapters 5-6), the believer's holiness sanctifies the unbeliever.</p>
          <p>The argument from children confirms this: <hi type="italic">epei ara ta tekna hymon akatharta estin, nyn de hagia estin</hi> — "since otherwise your children would be unclean, but as it is, they are holy." The word <hi type="italic">akatharta</hi> ("unclean, impure") is the opposite of <hi type="italic">hagia</hi> ("holy, consecrated, set apart"). Paul appeals to something the Corinthians apparently already accept: their children, born of mixed marriages, are regarded as "holy" within the community. If the children are holy despite having one unbelieving parent, then the same principle of holiness-through-relationship applies to the unbelieving spouse. Paul argues from the accepted case (the children) to the disputed case (the spouse). The logic is: you already treat your children as holy; the same principle that makes your children holy also makes your unbelieving spouse holy; therefore, do not divorce.</p>
          <p>"But if the unbelieving partner separates, let it be so. In such cases the brother or sister is not enslaved. God has called you to peace" (7:15).</p>
          <p>The conditional <hi type="italic">ei de ho apistos chorizetai, chorizestho</hi> — "but if the unbeliever separates, let them separate." The present indicative <hi type="italic">chorizetai</hi> ("is separating, separates") describes an action already underway or a real condition. The imperative <hi type="italic">chorizestho</hi> ("let them separate") grants permission: the believer is not required to prevent the departure by force or compromise. If the unbelieving spouse decides to leave, the believing partner is released.</p>
          <p>The declaration <hi type="italic">ou dedoulotai ho adelphos e he adelphe en tois toioutois</hi> ("the brother or sister is not enslaved in such cases") uses the perfect passive of <hi type="italic">douloo</hi> ("to enslave, to make a slave of, to bring into bondage"). The perfect tense indicates an ongoing state: the believing spouse has not been and is not now enslaved. The word <hi type="italic">douloo</hi> is strong — it describes the condition of a slave (<hi type="italic">doulos</hi>), bound to serve without freedom to leave. Paul says the believing partner is not in bondage. The implication is that the marriage bond, when dissolved by the unbeliever's departure, does not continue to bind the believer. The believer is free.</p>
          <p>The theological ground: <hi type="italic">en de eirene kekleken hymas ho theos</hi> — "but God has called you in peace" (or "to peace"). The verb <hi type="italic">kaleo</hi> ("to call") reintroduces the calling language that has been foundational since the letter's opening (1:1-2, 9, 24, 26). God's calling of the believer was a calling into <hi type="italic">eirene</hi> ("peace, wholeness, well-being"). The Hebrew concept of <hi type="italic">shalom</hi>, which lies behind <hi type="italic">eirene</hi>, denotes not merely the absence of conflict but the presence of right order and wholeness. A marriage in which the unbeliever's departure has created an unbridgeable rupture is not a context of peace. Forcing the believer to remain bound to a dissolved relationship would violate the character of God's calling.</p>
          <p>"For how do you know, wife, whether you will save your husband? Or how do you know, husband, whether you will save your wife?" (7:16).</p>
          <p>The question <hi type="italic">ti gar oidas, gynai, ei ton andra soseis</hi> — "for how do you know, wife, whether you will save your husband?" — is a genuine question about an uncertain future, not a statement of hope. The verb <hi type="italic">oida</hi> ("to know, to perceive") asks about the spouse's knowledge of an outcome that is inherently unknown. The verb <hi type="italic">sozo</hi> ("to save, to rescue, to deliver") carries its full weight: the question is whether continuing in the marriage will result in the unbelieving spouse's salvation — their incorporation into the community of faith and the deliverance that belongs to it.</p>
          <p>The question functions as a reason for accepting the unbeliever's departure rather than as a reason for holding on. Paul's logic runs: if you do not know whether staying will save your spouse, then you should not destroy your own peace on the uncertain possibility. The calling "to peace" (v. 15b) is certain; the salvation of the departing spouse is uncertain. Paul does not forbid the hope of the spouse's conversion, but he does not allow that hope to become a chain that binds the believing partner to an untenable situation.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7.17-1Cor.7.24">
          <title>Verses 17-24: The Principle of Remaining — Called, Circumcised, Enslaved</title>
          <p>"Only let each person lead the life that the Lord has assigned to him, and to which God has called him. This is my rule in all the churches" (7:17).</p>
          <p>This verse introduces the organizing principle that governs the entire chapter and that Paul draws from the specific cases already discussed. The phrase <hi type="italic">hekasto hos emerisen ho kyrios, hekaston hos kekleken ho theos, houtos peripateto</hi> — "each one as the Lord has distributed, each one as God has called, thus let them walk" — establishes a twofold ground for the instruction to remain: the Lord's distribution (<hi type="italic">emerisen</hi>, from <hi type="italic">merizo</hi>, "to divide, to distribute, to assign a portion") and God's calling (<hi type="italic">kekleken</hi>, perfect of <hi type="italic">kaleo</hi>, "to call"). Both are divine actions. The condition in which a person finds themselves when they are called by God is itself part of God's assignment. The person's task is to "walk" (<hi type="italic">peripateo</hi>, "to walk, to live, to conduct one's life") within that assignment.</p>
          <p>The scope of the instruction is universal: <hi type="italic">houto en tais ekklesiais pasais diatassomai</hi> — "this is what I direct in all the assemblies." The verb <hi type="italic">diatasso</hi> ("to arrange, to order, to direct, to prescribe") is a word of authoritative instruction. Paul's counsel here is not peculiar to Corinth; it is his standard teaching for every community. The Corinthians are not a special case receiving customized advice. They are being brought into conformity with the practice Paul establishes everywhere.</p>
          <p>"Was anyone at the time of his call already circumcised? Let him not seek to remove the marks of circumcision. Was anyone at the time of his call uncircumcised? Let him not seek circumcision" (7:18).</p>
          <p>The first illustration of the "remain" principle is circumcision — the covenant sign of Israel. The word <hi type="italic">peritetmenos</hi> ("circumcised") identifies a Jew; <hi type="italic">akrobystia</hi> ("uncircumcision, foreskin") identifies a Gentile. Paul addresses both: the circumcised man should not seek <hi type="italic">epispasmo</hi> ("to draw over, to pull forward" — the verb <hi type="italic">epispaomai</hi> refers to the surgical procedure, practiced by some Hellenized Jews, of restoring the appearance of a foreskin to reverse circumcision). This practice is attested in 1 Maccabees 1:15, where Jews under Antiochus Epiphanes "made themselves uncircumcised, and abandoned the holy covenant." Conversely, the uncircumcised man should not seek circumcision. Neither physical status — circumcised or uncircumcised — needs to be altered after the call.</p>
          <p>"Circumcision is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing. Keeping the commandments of God is what matters" (7:19).</p>
          <p>The statement <hi type="italic">he peritome ouden estin kai he akrobystia ouden estin</hi> — "circumcision is nothing and uncircumcision is nothing" — is radical in a Jewish context. Circumcision was the sign of the Abrahamic covenant (Genesis 17:10-14), the mark that identified a male as belonging to the covenant community. For Paul to declare it "nothing" is not to deny its historical significance but to assert that in the new situation created by Christ, the physical marker no longer determines one's standing before God. This is consistent with Paul's argument in Galatians 5:6: "in Christ Jesus neither circumcision nor uncircumcision means anything, but faith working through love" (<hi type="italic">en gar Christo Iesou oute peritome ti ischyei oute akrobystia alla pistis di' agapes energoumene</hi>); and in Galatians 6:15: "neither circumcision counts for anything, nor uncircumcision, but a new creation" (<hi type="italic">oute gar peritome ti estin oute akrobystia alla kaine ktisis</hi>).</p>
          <p>What does matter is <hi type="italic">teresis entolon theou</hi> — "keeping the commandments of God." The word <hi type="italic">entole</hi> ("commandment, instruction, injunction") in the LXX refers to God's commands throughout the Torah and the prophets (Deuteronomy 4:2, 6:1, 8:1). Paul does not specify which commandments; the phrase functions as a summary of the obedient life that God requires. The logic is striking: circumcision, which was itself one of God's commandments to Abraham, is now declared "nothing" — while "keeping the commandments of God" is everything. Paul is operating within the prophetic tradition that distinguished between the external sign and the internal reality the sign was meant to represent. Jeremiah 4:4: "Circumcise yourselves to the LORD; remove the foreskin of your hearts, O men of Judah and inhabitants of Jerusalem" (<hi type="italic">peritemethetete to theo hymon kai peritelesthe ten akrobystian tes kardias hymon</hi>). Deuteronomy 10:16: "Circumcise therefore the foreskin of your heart, and be no longer stubborn" (<hi type="italic">peritemesthe ten sklerokardian hymon</hi>). The prophets already distinguished between the physical act and the heart-obedience it was supposed to signify. Paul brings that distinction to its culmination.</p>
          <p>"Each one should remain in the condition in which he was called" (7:20).</p>
          <p>The verse <hi type="italic">hekastos en te klesei he eklethee, en taute meneto</hi> — "each person in the calling in which they were called, in this let them remain" — is the chapter's thesis statement in compressed form. The word <hi type="italic">klesis</hi> ("calling, vocation, summons") is the noun form of <hi type="italic">kaleo</hi> ("to call"). It refers to the circumstances in which a person was situated when God's call reached them. The verb <hi type="italic">meno</hi> ("to remain, to stay, to abide, to continue") is the chapter's controlling verb. It appeared in verse 8 (the unmarried should "remain" as Paul is), verse 11 (the separated wife should "remain" unmarried), and will appear again in verses 24, 26, and 40.</p>
          <p>The principle is not passivity or fatalism. Paul is not saying that social circumstances are irrelevant or that no change is ever appropriate (as verse 21 will immediately demonstrate). He is saying that the call of God does not require a change in external status. The Corinthians apparently assumed that their new identity in Christ demanded a corresponding change in their social arrangements — that the circumcised should become uncircumcised, that the married should become celibate, that slaves should seek freedom (or the reverse). Paul says the call transforms the person within their existing circumstances; it does not mandate the alteration of those circumstances.</p>
          <p>"Were you a slave when called? Do not be concerned about it. But if you can gain your freedom, avail yourself of the opportunity" (7:21).</p>
          <p>The second illustration is slavery. The word <hi type="italic">doulos</hi> ("slave, servant, bondservant") describes a person in legal servitude. Slavery in the first-century Roman world was pervasive and diverse, ranging from the brutal conditions of agricultural and mining slaves to the relatively privileged positions of educated household slaves who managed estates, practiced medicine, or taught children. Corinth, as a major commercial city and Roman colony, would have had a significant enslaved population.</p>
          <p>Paul's first instruction — <hi type="italic">me soi meleto</hi> ("do not be concerned about it, do not let it trouble you") — addresses the slave's anxiety about their status. The call of God has reached them in their enslaved condition; that condition does not impede the reality of their calling.</p>
          <p>The second clause, <hi type="italic">all' ei kai dynasai eleutheros genesthai, mallon chresai</hi>, is among the most debated in the chapter. The verb <hi type="italic">chraomai</hi> ("to use, to make use of, to avail oneself of") requires an object, and the question is whether the implied object is "freedom" (if you can gain your freedom, use the opportunity) or "slavery" (even if you can gain your freedom, rather make use of your slavery). The grammar and the context favor the first reading: <hi type="italic">mallon chresai</hi> most naturally means "rather make use of it" — i.e., take the opportunity for freedom. This reading is consistent with Paul's argument: remain in your condition as a general rule, but when circumstances change and freedom becomes available, take it. The principle of "remaining" is not absolute; it is a default posture that does not preclude taking advantage of legitimate opportunities when they arise.</p>
          <p>"For he who was called in the Lord as a slave is a freedman of the Lord. Likewise he who was free when called is a slave of Christ" (7:22).</p>
          <p>The wordplay is deliberate and subversive. The <hi type="italic">doulos</hi> ("slave") who is called "in the Lord" (<hi type="italic">en kyrio</hi>) becomes <hi type="italic">apeleutheros kyriou</hi> ("a freedman of the Lord"). The word <hi type="italic">apeleutheros</hi> is a technical term in Roman society: a former slave who has been formally manumitted, freed by their master and now bearing the patron-client relationship to the former owner. In Roman law, the freedman owed continuing obligations and loyalty to the patron who freed them. Paul applies this to the believing slave: though still a <hi type="italic">doulos</hi> in social terms, they are the Lord's freedman — liberated by Christ, owing loyalty and service to Christ as patron. Their true master is the Lord, regardless of their legal status.</p>
          <p>Conversely, the <hi type="italic">eleutheros</hi> ("free person") who is called becomes <hi type="italic">doulos Christou</hi> ("a slave of Christ"). The free person's social liberty does not exempt them from total obligation to Christ. The slave is raised; the free person is bound. Both are redefined by their relationship to Christ. The social categories of slave and free are not abolished (Paul does not tell slaves to rebel or free persons to sell themselves into slavery), but they are relativized. Neither status determines one's fundamental identity. Both are encompassed within the more fundamental relationship of belonging to Christ.</p>
          <p>"You were bought with a price; do not become slaves of human beings" (7:23).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">times egoresthete</hi> ("you were bought with a price") repeats exactly the statement from 6:20. The verb <hi type="italic">agorazo</hi> ("to buy, to purchase, to acquire in the marketplace") comes from <hi type="italic">agora</hi> ("marketplace"). The metaphor is commercial: the Corinthians were purchased. The "price" (<hi type="italic">time</hi>) is not specified in this passage but the context of the letter identifies it as Christ's death — the sacrifice of "our Passover" (5:7). In 6:20, the purchasing language grounded the command to "glorify God in your body." Here it grounds a different command: <hi type="italic">me ginesthe douloi anthropon</hi> — "do not become slaves of human beings."</p>
          <p>This instruction operates at multiple levels. For the literally enslaved person, it means that their ultimate loyalty is to Christ, not to any human master, and that they should not voluntarily enter into bondage. For all believers, it means that their identity as Christ's purchased possession precludes ultimate allegiance to any human authority, faction, or teacher. The connection to the factionalism of chapters 1-4 is unmistakable: the Corinthians who declared "I am of Paul" or "I am of Apollos" were making themselves <hi type="italic">douloi anthropon</hi> — slaves of human leaders. The connection to the ascetic program of verse 1 is also present: those who impose ascetic requirements on others are attempting to bring them under human authority rather than leaving them in the freedom that belongs to Christ's purchased people.</p>
          <p>"So, brothers, in whatever condition each was called, there let him remain with God" (7:24).</p>
          <p>The verse summarizes and closes the "remain" section. The phrase <hi type="italic">para theo</hi> ("with God" or "before God") adds a crucial dimension. The remaining is not mere social inertia; it is remaining "with God" — in God's presence, under God's authority, within the relationship that God's calling established. The condition — married or unmarried, circumcised or uncircumcised, slave or free — is the context in which one lives <hi type="italic">coram deo</hi> ("before God"). The call of God sanctifies the circumstances of one's life without necessarily changing them.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7.25-1Cor.7.28">
          <title>Verses 25-28: Concerning the Unmarried and the Present Distress</title>
          <p>"Now concerning the betrothed, I have no command from the Lord, but I give my judgment as one who by the Lord's mercy is trustworthy" (7:25).</p>
          <p>A new <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> ("now concerning") section opens, indicating another question from the Corinthians' letter. The word <hi type="italic">parthenoi</hi> (literally "virgins") has been variously understood. In its basic sense, it refers to unmarried young women (or, less commonly, young men) who have not had sexual relations. The context will suggest that Paul is addressing a specific situation: fathers (or guardians) who must decide whether to give their virgin daughters in marriage, and/or betrothed couples who must decide whether to proceed with marriage.</p>
          <p>Paul again distinguishes his authority: <hi type="italic">epitagen kyriou ouk echo</hi> ("I have no command from the Lord") — Jesus did not address this specific question in his teaching. Paul offers his own <hi type="italic">gnome</hi> ("judgment, opinion, counsel") as one who has received mercy from the Lord and is therefore <hi type="italic">pistos</hi> ("trustworthy, faithful"). The word <hi type="italic">pistos</hi> echoes 4:2, where faithfulness was the essential quality of a steward, and 7:17, where Paul's instructions carried the weight of what he directs "in all the assemblies." Paul is not diminishing his authority; he is being precise about its source. He is a trustworthy counselor, even when he cannot cite a direct word from the Lord.</p>
          <p>"I think that in view of the present distress it is good for a person to remain as he is" (7:26).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">dia ten enestoson anagken</hi> ("because of the present distress/necessity") introduces the eschatological factor that shapes all of Paul's practical counsel in this section. The word <hi type="italic">enestosan</hi> (perfect active participle of <hi type="italic">enistemi</hi>, "to be present, to be at hand, to impend") describes something that is currently present or imminent. The word <hi type="italic">anagke</hi> ("necessity, distress, compulsion, constraint") in the LXX frequently describes the pressure of calamity, the constraint of crisis, the hardship of difficult times. In Luke 21:23, Jesus used <hi type="italic">anagke</hi> for the distress that would come upon the land: "great distress shall be upon the earth" (<hi type="italic">anagke megale epi tes ges</hi>). In 2 Corinthians 6:4, Paul listed <hi type="italic">anagkai</hi> ("hardships, necessities") among the afflictions he endured as a servant of God.</p>
          <p>What is the "present distress"? Paul does not specify. It may refer to persecution that the early communities were already experiencing, to the general tribulations expected before the Lord's return, to famine or social upheaval in Corinth, or to the combined pressures of living as a counter-cultural community in a hostile environment. Whatever its specific referent, the "present distress" creates a context in which Paul's practical counsel tilts toward minimizing new obligations and entanglements. Marriage is good, but it brings additional responsibilities and vulnerabilities; in a time of distress, those without such obligations are freer to navigate the crisis.</p>
          <p>Paul's judgment: <hi type="italic">kalon anthropo to houtos einai</hi> — "it is good for a person to remain as they are." Again, <hi type="italic">kalon</hi> ("good") and the verb "to be" (<hi type="italic">einai</hi>) as they are (<hi type="italic">houtos</hi>). The principle of remaining is applied to the current crisis: given the distress that is present, changing one's situation is inadvisable.</p>
          <p>"Are you bound to a wife? Do not seek to be free. Are you free from a wife? Do not seek a wife" (7:27).</p>
          <p>Two parallel instructions: <hi type="italic">dedesai gynaiki? me zetei lysin</hi> — "are you bound to a wife? Do not seek release." The verb <hi type="italic">deo</hi> ("to bind, to tie") in the perfect passive (<hi type="italic">dedesai</hi>, "you have been bound and remain bound") describes the marriage bond. The word <hi type="italic">lysis</hi> ("loosing, release, dissolution") is the noun from <hi type="italic">lyo</hi> ("to loose, to unbind, to divorce"). The married man should not seek dissolution of his marriage.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Lelusai apo gynaikos? me zetei gynaika</hi> — "have you been loosed from a wife? Do not seek a wife." The perfect passive <hi type="italic">lelusai</hi> ("you have been loosed") describes someone whose marriage has already been dissolved — by death, divorce, or separation. This person should not seek to enter a new marriage. Both instructions follow the "remain as you are" principle.</p>
          <p>"But if you do marry, you have not sinned, and if a betrothed woman marries, she has not sinned. Yet those who marry will have worldly troubles, and I would spare you that" (7:28).</p>
          <p>Paul immediately qualifies the preceding instructions: <hi type="italic">ean de kai games, ouch hemartes</hi> — "but if you do marry, you have not sinned." The verb <hi type="italic">hamartano</hi> ("to sin, to miss the mark, to transgress") is denied: marriage is not a sin. If the virgin (<hi type="italic">he parthenos</hi>) marries, she has not sinned either. Paul's counsel against marriage in the present circumstances is pragmatic, not moral. There is no sin in marrying. The <hi type="italic">charisma</hi> framework of verse 7 remains operative: marriage is a gift from God.</p>
          <p>The practical concern: <hi type="italic">thlipsin de te sarki hexousin hoi toioutoi</hi> — "but such people will have tribulation in the flesh." The word <hi type="italic">thlipsis</hi> ("tribulation, affliction, pressure, distress") describes the external pressures and difficulties of life. Those who marry will face additional <hi type="italic">thlipsis</hi> — the practical difficulties that marriage brings, compounded by the "present distress" of verse 26. In a time of persecution or social upheaval, family obligations multiply the burdens a person must bear. Paul's desire: <hi type="italic">ego de hymon pheidomai</hi> — "I am sparing you." The verb <hi type="italic">pheidomai</hi> ("to spare, to refrain from harming, to treat leniently") expresses pastoral concern. Paul's counsel toward celibacy in the present crisis is motivated by care for the Corinthians' well-being, not by an abstract preference for sexual renunciation.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7.29-1Cor.7.31">
          <title>Verses 29-31: The Shortened Time and the Passing Form of the World</title>
          <p>"This is what I mean, brothers: the appointed time has grown short. From now on, let those who have wives live as though they had none" (7:29).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">touto de phemi, adelphoi, ho kairos synestalmenos estin</hi> — "this I say, brothers: the time has been shortened" — states the eschatological reality that grounds all of Paul's practical counsel. The word <hi type="italic">kairos</hi> ("time, season, appointed time, critical moment") is not <hi type="italic">chronos</hi> ("time in general, duration") but the specific, decisive time that determines the character of the present moment. The verb <hi type="italic">systello</hi> ("to shorten, to compress, to draw together, to wrap up") in the perfect passive participle (<hi type="italic">synestalmenos</hi>) describes a completed compression: the time has already been shortened and remains in its shortened state. The image is of a sail being furled, a rope being gathered in, a space being constricted. There is less time than there used to be. The interval between Christ's death and resurrection and his return is finite and is drawing to its close.</p>
          <p>Paul draws a radical practical consequence: <hi type="italic">to loipon, hina kai hoi echontes gynaikas hos me echontes osin</hi> — "from now on, let even those who have wives be as though they had none." This is not a command to abandon marriages or cease conjugal relations (which would contradict verses 3-5). It is a command to hold all earthly arrangements with a loose grip — to live within them without being defined by them, to maintain them without being absorbed by them. The marriage relationship continues, but it no longer constitutes the believer's ultimate horizon.</p>
          <p>"And those who mourn as though they were not mourning, and those who rejoice as though they were not rejoicing, and those who buy as though they had no possessions" (7:30).</p>
          <p>Paul extends the "as though not" (<hi type="italic">hos me</hi>) formula across four domains of life:</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Hoi klaiontes hos me klaiontes</hi> — "those who weep as though not weeping." The verb <hi type="italic">klaio</hi> ("to weep, to cry, to lament") describes grief. The sorrow of present circumstances is real but not ultimate.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Hoi chairontes hos me chairontes</hi> — "those who rejoice as though not rejoicing." The verb <hi type="italic">chairo</hi> ("to rejoice, to be glad") describes happiness. Present joy, like present sorrow, is relativized by the shortened time.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Hoi agorazontes hos me katechontes</hi> — "those who buy as though not possessing." The verb <hi type="italic">agorazo</hi> ("to buy, to purchase") and <hi type="italic">katecho</hi> ("to hold fast, to possess, to retain") describe economic transactions and the ownership they produce. Even property ownership is to be held lightly.</p>
          <p>The <hi type="italic">hos me</hi> ("as though not") formula does not negate the activities themselves. Paul does not command believers to stop marrying, mourning, rejoicing, or buying. He commands them to engage in these activities without ultimate attachment — to participate in the present order while recognizing that it is not the final order. The formula produces a posture of engaged detachment: fully present in the world but not anchored to it.</p>
          <p>"For the present form of this world is passing away" (7:31).</p>
          <p>The ground of the <hi type="italic">hos me</hi> posture: <hi type="italic">paragei gar to schema tou kosmou toutou</hi> — "for the form of this world is passing away." The word <hi type="italic">schema</hi> ("form, outward appearance, figure, shape") describes the external configuration of something — its visible arrangement, its present structure. The word was used in 4:6, where Paul said he had "applied these things figuratively" (<hi type="italic">meteschematisa</hi>) to himself and Apollos. The <hi type="italic">schema</hi> is the outward form, not the underlying substance. The world's present form — its social arrangements, its power structures, its economies, its institutions, including marriage — is <hi type="italic">paragei</hi> ("passing away, going by, coming to an end"). The verb <hi type="italic">parago</hi> ("to pass by, to go past, to pass away") describes a procession moving along and leaving the observer behind, or a scene changing. The present arrangement of things is in transit. It is not permanent. It is giving way to something else.</p>
          <p>This is the theological foundation that supports the entire chapter's practical counsel. The reason to hold marriage loosely, to avoid unnecessary entanglements, to remain in one's present condition, to treat circumcision and slavery as matters of indifference — all of this flows from the recognition that the <hi type="italic">schema</hi> of this world is temporary. The question of whether to marry or not, whether to change one's social status or not, is set against a larger backdrop: the present order is passing. Decisions should be made in light of what is coming, not in light of what is leaving.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7.32-1Cor.7.35">
          <title>Verses 32-35: Undivided Devotion</title>
          <p>"I want you to be free from anxieties. The unmarried man is anxious about the things of the Lord, how to please the Lord" (7:32).</p>
          <p>Paul now explains why he prefers celibacy for those who have the gift for it. The word <hi type="italic">amerimnous</hi> ("free from anxiety, without care, without worry") comes from the alpha privative <hi type="italic">a-</hi> and <hi type="italic">merimna</hi> ("care, anxiety, concern"). The verb <hi type="italic">merimnao</hi> ("to be anxious, to be concerned, to worry") will appear four times in verses 32-34. Paul's concern is about the division of attention and energy that marriage inevitably creates.</p>
          <p>The unmarried man <hi type="italic">merimna ta tou kyriou, pos arese to kyrio</hi> — "is anxious about the things of the Lord, how to please the Lord." The verb <hi type="italic">aresko</hi> ("to please, to satisfy, to be pleasing to") describes the desire to satisfy someone whose approval matters. The unmarried person can direct this desire entirely toward the Lord.</p>
          <p>"But the married man is anxious about worldly things, how to please his wife, and his interests are divided" (7:33-34a).</p>
          <p>The married man <hi type="italic">merimna ta tou kosmou, pos arese te gynaiki</hi> — "is anxious about the things of the world, how to please his wife." The phrase <hi type="italic">ta tou kosmou</hi> ("the things of the world") does not describe sinful things but the legitimate concerns of maintaining a household — provision, protection, the practical demands of family life. The married man's attention is <hi type="italic">kai memeristai</hi> ("and divided" or "and he is divided"). The verb <hi type="italic">merizo</hi> ("to divide, to distribute, to separate into parts") is the same verb from verse 17, where God "distributed" to each person their circumstances. Here the division is internal: the married man's devotion is split between the Lord and his wife. This is not presented as sin but as the structural reality of marriage.</p>
          <p>"And the unmarried or betrothed woman is anxious about the things of the Lord, how to be holy in body and spirit. But the married woman is anxious about worldly things, how to please her husband" (7:34b).</p>
          <p>The same analysis applies to women, with perfect symmetry. The unmarried woman and the <hi type="italic">parthenos</hi> ("virgin, betrothed woman") can devote themselves to being <hi type="italic">hagia kai to somati kai to pneumati</hi> — "holy both in body and in spirit." The phrase "holy in body and spirit" connects to the body theology of 6:19-20: the body is a temple of the Holy Spirit. The unmarried woman can direct the totality of her being — body and spirit — toward holiness before the Lord. The married woman, by contrast, must attend to <hi type="italic">ta tou kosmou, pos arese to andri</hi> — "the things of the world, how to please her husband."</p>
          <p>Paul's description is observational, not judgmental. He is not condemning marriage or declaring the married person's attention to their spouse sinful. He is describing the structural reality that marriage divides attention. For those living in "the present distress" (v. 26), with "the time shortened" (v. 29), this division has practical consequences. The person whose devotion is undivided is better positioned to navigate the crisis.</p>
          <p>"I say this for your own benefit, not to lay any restraint upon you, but to promote good order and to secure your undivided devotion to the Lord" (7:35).</p>
          <p>Paul clarifies his motive: <hi type="italic">touto de pros to hymon auton sympheron lego</hi> — "this I say for your own advantage." The word <hi type="italic">sympheron</hi> ("advantageous, beneficial, profitable") echoes 6:12, where Paul countered the Corinthian slogan with "but not all things are beneficial" (<hi type="italic">all' ou panta sympherei</hi>). Paul is seeking what is genuinely beneficial for the Corinthians, not imposing arbitrary restrictions.</p>
          <p>He is not throwing a <hi type="italic">brochos</hi> ("noose, restraint, halter") on them — <hi type="italic">ouch hina brochon hymin epibalo</hi>. The word <hi type="italic">brochos</hi> means a rope or snare, something that restricts freedom. Paul is not trying to trap them into celibacy. His purpose is <hi type="italic">euschemon kai euparedron to kyrio aperispastos</hi> — "for seemly devotion and faithful attendance upon the Lord without distraction." The word <hi type="italic">euschemon</hi> ("seemly, proper, becoming") describes what is fitting and honorable. The word <hi type="italic">euparedron</hi> ("sitting well beside, constantly attending") describes the posture of someone who sits attentively beside another, devoted and present. The word <hi type="italic">aperispastos</hi> ("without distraction, undiverted") is the goal: undivided attention to the Lord.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7.36-1Cor.7.38">
          <title>Verses 36-38: The Case of the Betrothed</title>
          <p>"If anyone thinks that he is not behaving properly toward his betrothed, if his passions are strong, and it has to be, let him do as he wishes: let them marry — it is no sin" (7:36).</p>
          <p>This passage addresses a specific situation. The phrase <hi type="italic">ei de tis aschemonein epi ten parthenon autou nomizei</hi> — "if anyone thinks he is behaving improperly toward his virgin" — describes a man who is betrothed to a virgin and who faces a practical crisis. The verb <hi type="italic">aschemoneo</hi> ("to behave improperly, to act unseemly, to disgrace") from the root <hi type="italic">aschemon</hi> ("unseemly, shapeless, dishonoring") describes behavior that violates what is fitting. In 12:23, Paul will use the same root for the "unpresentable" (<hi type="italic">aschemon</hi>) body parts. The man considers it unseemly to leave his betrothed in a state of permanent engagement without consummating the marriage.</p>
          <p>The condition <hi type="italic">ean e hyperakmos</hi> ("if she/he is past the bloom of youth" or "if his passions are strong") has been debated. The word <hi type="italic">hyperakmos</hi> could describe the virgin who has passed the ideal age for marriage (making delay increasingly inappropriate) or the man whose sexual desire has become too strong to sustain the engagement without consummation. In either reading, the situation has reached a point where continued delay is creating problems rather than solving them.</p>
          <p>The resolution: <hi type="italic">kai houtos opheilei ginesthai, ho thelei poieito, ouch hamartanei, gameitosan</hi> — "and if it must happen, let him do what he wishes, he does not sin; let them marry." The verb <hi type="italic">opheilei</hi> ("it is necessary, it must be") indicates that circumstances have made marriage the necessary course. Once again, Paul affirms: <hi type="italic">ouch hamartanei</hi> — "he does not sin." Marriage is not sin. The repeated assurance (also in v. 28) suggests that some in Corinth were teaching or implying that marriage was sinful — a position Paul explicitly rejects every time the question arises.</p>
          <p>"But whoever is firmly established in his heart, being under no necessity but having his desire under control, and has determined this in his heart, to keep her as his betrothed, he will do well" (7:37).</p>
          <p>The alternative case: the man who is <hi type="italic">hedraios en te kardia</hi> ("firmly established in his heart"), who has no <hi type="italic">anagke</hi> ("necessity, compulsion") — his passions do not overwhelm him — and who has <hi type="italic">exousian peri tou idiou thelematos</hi> ("authority over his own will"), meaning he has the capacity to sustain the engagement without consummation. If he has <hi type="italic">kekriken en te idia kardia</hi> ("decided in his own heart") to keep his virgin (<hi type="italic">terein ten heautou parthenon</hi>), he <hi type="italic">kalos poiesei</hi> ("will do well"). The word <hi type="italic">kalos</hi> ("well, finely, nobly") affirms his decision positively.</p>
          <p>"So then he who marries his betrothed does well, and he who refrains from marriage will do even better" (7:38).</p>
          <p>The comparative judgment: <hi type="italic">hoste kai ho gamizoin ten heautou parthenon kalos poiei, kai ho me gamizoin kreisson poiesei</hi> — "so then both the one who marries his virgin does well, and the one who does not marry does better." The word <hi type="italic">kreisson</hi> ("better") establishes a relative preference, not an absolute moral hierarchy. Both courses are <hi type="italic">kalos</hi> ("well, good"); one is <hi type="italic">kreisson</hi> ("better"). Paul's preference for celibacy, stated repeatedly throughout the chapter (vv. 7-8, 26, 32-35), is here formulated as a comparative: better, not exclusively right. The person who marries does well; the person who refrains, given the conditions Paul has described (present distress, shortened time, undivided devotion), does even better. But "better" is not "righteous versus sinful"; it is "good versus more advantageous in the present circumstances."</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7.39-1Cor.7.40">
          <title>Verses 39-40: Widows</title>
          <p>"A wife is bound to her husband as long as he lives. But if her husband dies, she is free to be married to whom she wishes, only in the Lord" (7:39).</p>
          <p>Paul returns to the category of widows (introduced in v. 8) and states the duration of the marriage bond: <hi type="italic">gyne dedetai eph' hoson chronon ze ho aner autes</hi> — "a wife is bound for as long a time as her husband lives." The verb <hi type="italic">deo</hi> ("to bind") in the perfect passive (<hi type="italic">dedetai</hi>, "has been and remains bound") describes the marriage bond as a binding that persists throughout the husband's life. The word <hi type="italic">chronos</hi> ("time, duration") here is used in its straightforward sense: the binding lasts as long as the husband's life lasts.</p>
          <p>If the husband dies (<hi type="italic">ean de koimethe ho aner</hi>, "but if the husband falls asleep" — using the euphemism <hi type="italic">koimaomai</hi>, "to sleep," for death, which Paul also uses in 11:30 and 15:6, 18, 20, 51), the wife is <hi type="italic">eleuthera estin ho thelei gametheenai</hi> — "free to be married to whom she wishes." The word <hi type="italic">eleuthera</hi> ("free") contrasts with the <hi type="italic">dedetai</hi> ("bound") of the first clause. Death dissolves the bond. The widow's freedom to remarry is genuine: <hi type="italic">ho thelei</hi> ("to whom she wishes") leaves the choice entirely to her.</p>
          <p>The qualification: <hi type="italic">monon en kyrio</hi> — "only in the Lord." The phrase <hi type="italic">en kyrio</hi> ("in the Lord") has been the letter's defining sphere since 1:2, where the community was described as "sanctified in Christ Jesus." The widow may marry whom she wishes, but the marriage must be "in the Lord" — within the sphere of the community's identity, to a believer. This restriction is consistent with Paul's treatment of mixed marriages in verses 12-16: existing mixed marriages should be maintained if the unbeliever consents, but new marriages should be contracted within the community.</p>
          <p>"Yet in my judgment she is happier if she remains as she is. And I think that I too have the Spirit of God" (7:40).</p>
          <p>Paul states his own preference one final time: <hi type="italic">makarioterra de estin ean houtos meine</hi> — "but she is more blessed if she remains as she is." The word <hi type="italic">makarioterra</hi> (comparative of <hi type="italic">makarios</hi>, "blessed, happy, fortunate") uses the language of beatitude — the blessed condition. The widow who remains unmarried is, in Paul's judgment, in a more blessed condition. This is consistent with the entire chapter's trajectory: celibacy, for those who can sustain it, is preferred in light of the present distress and the shortened time.</p>
          <p>The closing phrase — <hi type="italic">doko de kago pneuma theou echein</hi> — "and I think that I too have the Spirit of God" — is delivered with understated irony. The verb <hi type="italic">dokeo</hi> ("to think, to suppose, to seem") combined with <hi type="italic">kago</hi> ("I too, I also") suggests that Paul is aware of competing claims of spiritual authority in Corinth. Some may be claiming the Spirit's authority for positions different from Paul's. Paul's response is quietly assertive: he too has the Spirit of God. The counsel he gives throughout this chapter is not merely human opinion; it comes from one who possesses the Spirit (2:10-16), who has been commissioned as an apostle (1:1), and who is a trustworthy steward of God's mysteries (4:1-2). The ironic understatement — "I think I too have the Spirit" — echoes the same tone Paul used in 4:8-13, where his ironic self-deprecation masked a serious claim to authority.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.7">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 7 addresses a cluster of practical questions about marriage, celibacy, divorce, and social status, but it is organized by a single theological principle and shaped by a single eschatological conviction.</p>
          <p>The theological principle is "remain in the condition in which God called you" (vv. 17, 20, 24). This principle applies to marriage (vv. 2-16, 27), to circumcision (vv. 18-19), to slavery (vv. 21-23), and to betrothal and widowhood (vv. 25-40). The principle does not absolutize the status quo — Paul allows for the gaining of freedom (v. 21), for marriage when burning with desire (v. 9), for remarriage of widows (v. 39). But the default posture is to remain. The call of God sanctifies the circumstances in which it finds the person. The believer does not need to change their external situation to serve God fully. The circumcised Jew does not need to become uncircumcised; the uncircumcised Gentile does not need to be circumcised; the married person does not need to become celibate; the celibate person does not need to marry; the slave does not need to become free (though they may if the opportunity arises). God's call reaches people where they are and transforms their relationship to their circumstances without necessarily transforming the circumstances themselves.</p>
          <p>The eschatological conviction is that "the appointed time has grown short" and "the present form of this world is passing away" (vv. 29, 31). This conviction shapes all of Paul's practical counsel. He prefers celibacy not because marriage is sinful (he explicitly denies this at least three times: vv. 9, 28, 36) but because the present crisis and the approaching end make undivided devotion to the Lord the wisest course. The <hi type="italic">hos me</hi> ("as though not") formula of verses 29-31 defines the community's posture: engaged in the world's activities but not absorbed by them, holding all arrangements loosely because the form in which those arrangements exist is temporary.</p>
          <p>The chapter also demonstrates Paul's pastoral method. He carefully distinguishes between the Lord's direct command (v. 10), his own apostolic counsel (vv. 12, 25), his concession (v. 6), and his personal preference (v. 7). He does not flatten all instruction into a single level of authority. He repeatedly affirms that marriage is not sin (vv. 9, 28, 36), countering what appears to be an ascetic faction in Corinth that was treating celibacy as morally superior. He addresses men and women with remarkable symmetry, granting each gender the same obligations (vv. 3-4), the same rights (v. 4), the same freedom (v. 15), the same spiritual capacity (v. 34), and the same options (vv. 27-28, 36-38, 39-40).</p>
          <p>The chapter's connection to the broader argument of the letter is threefold. First, it continues the treatment of the body that Paul developed in 6:12-20. The body that belongs to the Lord and is a temple of the Holy Spirit is also the body that participates in marriage, sexual union, and the practical circumstances of daily life. Paul's body theology is not dualistic: the body is not rejected in favor of the spirit but integrated into the community's life with the Lord. Second, the "remain" principle addresses the same confusion about identity that produced the factionalism of chapters 1-4. The Corinthians have been changing allegiances, seeking new statuses, competing for position. Paul says: stop seeking change. Your identity is settled by God's calling. Remain in it. The frenetic pursuit of new spiritual positions — new teachers, new statuses, new ascetic achievements — is unnecessary and harmful. Third, the eschatological urgency of verses 29-31 provides the framework for the entire second half of the letter. The questions about food offered to idols (chapters 8-10), about worship practices (chapter 11), about spiritual gifts (chapters 12-14), and about the resurrection (chapter 15) all take place within the same temporal horizon: the time is short, the form of this world is passing, and the community must live accordingly.</p>
          <p>The word that holds the chapter together is <hi type="italic">meno</hi> — "remain." The believer remains in their marriage or their singleness, remains in their circumcision or their uncircumcision, remains in their slavery or their freedom, remains with God (v. 24) in whatever condition the call found them. The remaining is not passive resignation but active faithfulness within the circumstances of one's calling, lived in the knowledge that all such circumstances are temporary and that the ultimate reality — the Lord's coming, the day of judgment, the fulfillment of God's purposes — is drawing near.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.8">
        <title>1 Corinthians 8 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.8">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 7):</hi> Paul turned from the disciplinary cases of chapters 5-6 to respond to questions the Corinthians had written to him, introduced by the formula <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> ("now concerning") in 7:1. The topic was marriage and sexual relationships within the community. Paul opened by engaging a Corinthian slogan — "It is good for a man not to touch a woman" (7:1) — and then addressed a series of practical situations: married couples and their sexual obligations to each other (7:2-7), the unmarried and widows (7:8-9), the prohibition of divorce among believers (7:10-11), the case of believers married to unbelievers (7:12-16), and virgins or the betrothed (7:25-38). Throughout these varied situations, Paul articulated a governing principle: "Each person should remain in the condition in which he was called" (7:17, repeated in 7:20 and 7:24). The word <hi type="italic">klesis</hi> ("calling") does not refer to a vocation but to the moment of divine summons into the community. Circumcised or uncircumcised, slave or free — the external condition at the time of calling does not need to change (7:18-23). Paul grounded this counsel in the eschatological situation: "the appointed time has grown very short" (7:29) and "the present form of this world is passing away" (7:31). The community lives in the overlap between the present age and the coming one, and this eschatological awareness should shape how believers relate to worldly arrangements — marriage, possessions, commerce, and mourning alike (7:29-31). Paul concluded with practical counsel about widows (7:39-40), framed by the same concern: the widow is free to remarry, but only "in the Lord" (7:39), and in Paul's judgment she would be happier remaining as she is (7:40).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 9):</hi> Paul shifts from the abstract principle of chapter 8 — that those with knowledge should limit their freedom for the sake of the weak — to a concrete, personal demonstration of that principle in his own apostolic practice. He opens with a defense of his apostolic status: "Am I not free? Am I not an apostle? Have I not seen Jesus our Lord? Are not you my workmanship in the Lord?" (9:1). He then catalogues the rights (<hi type="italic">exousiai</hi>) he possesses as an apostle: the right to eat and drink at the community's expense (9:4), the right to be accompanied by a wife as the other apostles are (9:5), the right to refrain from manual labor (9:6). He argues for these rights from multiple authorities: common sense analogies (soldiers, vinedressers, shepherds — 9:7), the Torah (the ox that treads grain must not be muzzled, citing Deuteronomy 25:4 — 9:9), the principle of the temple (those who serve at the altar share in the altar's offerings — 9:13), and the Lord's own command (those who proclaim the gospel should get their living by the gospel — 9:14). Paul has established beyond doubt that he possesses these rights. Then comes the decisive turn: "But I have made no use of any of these rights" (9:15). He preaches the gospel free of charge (9:18). He has made himself a slave to all so that he might win more of them (9:19). He became as a Jew to Jews, as one under the law to those under the law, as one outside the law to those outside the law, as weak to the weak (9:20-22). "I do it all for the sake of the gospel, that I may share with them in its blessings" (9:23). The chapter concludes with the athletic metaphor: the runner who competes for a perishable wreath exercises self-control in everything; Paul disciplines his body and keeps it under control, "so that after preaching to others I myself should not be disqualified" (<hi type="italic">adokimos</hi>, 9:27). The word <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> ("disqualified, rejected, failing the test") is striking — Paul himself could be disqualified if he does not exercise self-discipline. The chapter demonstrates that the principle Paul taught in chapter 8 is not merely advice for others; it is the governing pattern of his own apostolic life. He who has the most rights surrenders the most rights.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 8 connects these:</hi> Chapter 7 ended Paul's treatment of questions about marriage and calling. Chapter 8 opens a new section with the same <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> formula ("now concerning") that introduced chapter 7, signaling that Paul is turning to another question from the Corinthians' letter. The new topic is <hi type="italic">eidolothyta</hi> — food offered to idols. But the transition is not merely topical. Chapter 7 addressed how believers should navigate social arrangements (marriage, slavery, circumcision) in light of their calling. Chapter 8 addresses how believers should navigate a different social arrangement — participation in meals where idol-food is served — in light of their communal responsibility. Both chapters deal with the tension between individual freedom and the constraints imposed by belonging to the community of Christ.</p>
          <p>More deeply, chapter 8 introduces a problem — the knowledgeable believer whose exercise of freedom damages the weak — that chapter 9 then illustrates from Paul's own life. The argument is not complete at the end of chapter 8; it continues through chapter 9 and into chapter 10, where Paul will return to the idol-food question with the additional weight of Israel's wilderness failures (10:1-13) and a severe prohibition against actual participation in idol worship (10:14-22), before giving practical guidelines for marketplace purchases and private dinners (10:23-11:1). Chapters 8-10 form a single argumentative unit, with chapter 8 laying the principle, chapter 9 illustrating the principle from Paul's own practice, and chapter 10 completing the argument with both warning and practical instruction. Chapter 8 cannot be read as a self-contained statement; it is the opening move of a three-chapter argument.</p>
          <p>The key vocabulary of chapter 8 also connects it to themes that have run through the entire letter. The verb <hi type="italic">phusioo</hi> ("to puff up, to inflate") reappears in 8:1, linking the Corinthians' knowledge-based arrogance to the same disease diagnosed in 4:6, 4:18, 4:19, and 5:2. The verb <hi type="italic">oikodomeo</hi> ("to build up") appears in 8:1 and 8:10, connecting to the building metaphor of 3:9-17. The word <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> ("right, authority") in 8:9 introduces a concept that will dominate chapter 9. And the verb <hi type="italic">apollymi</hi> ("to destroy, to perish") in 8:11 uses the same word that described "those who are perishing" in 1:18, placing the stakes of the argument at the highest possible level.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.8.1">
          <title>Verse 1: The Corinthian Slogan and Paul's Correction</title>
          <p>"Now concerning food offered to idols: we know that 'all of us possess knowledge.' This 'knowledge' puffs up, but love builds up" (8:1).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">peri de ton eidolothyton</hi> ("now concerning food offered to idols") marks the transition to a new topic from the Corinthians' letter, using the same formula as 7:1 (<hi type="italic">peri de hon egrapsate</hi>, "now concerning the things about which you wrote"), 7:25 (<hi type="italic">peri de ton parthenon</hi>, "now concerning virgins"), and later 12:1 (<hi type="italic">peri de ton pneumatikon</hi>, "now concerning spiritual gifts"), 16:1 (<hi type="italic">peri de tes logeias</hi>, "now concerning the collection"), and 16:12 (<hi type="italic">peri de Apollou</hi>, "now concerning Apollos"). Each <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> section responds to a specific question or topic raised in the Corinthians' letter to Paul. The Corinthians have asked about idol-food, and Paul is responding.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">eidolothyton</hi> ("food offered to idols") is itself significant. It is a compound word: <hi type="italic">eidolon</hi> ("idol, image, likeness") and <hi type="italic">thyo</hi> ("to sacrifice, to slaughter, to offer"). The term is characteristically Jewish — it frames the sacrifice from the perspective of one who regards the idol as a non-entity. The Gentile equivalent would be <hi type="italic">hierothyton</hi> ("that which has been offered in sacred rite"), which Paul will use later in 10:28 when describing what a pagan dinner guest might say. The very word <hi type="italic">eidolothyton</hi> embeds a theological judgment: the sacrifice was made to an idol, which is to say, to nothing. The question the Corinthians are asking is whether believers may eat such food.</p>
          <p>"We know that all of us possess knowledge" (<hi type="italic">oidamen hoti pantes gnosin echomen</hi>) — Paul appears to be quoting a Corinthian claim. The phrase has the character of a slogan: confident, comprehensive ("all of us"), assertive ("we know"). The Corinthians are claiming that the whole community possesses <hi type="italic">gnosis</hi> — "knowledge" — presumably knowledge about the nature of idols and the truth of monotheism. This knowledge, they apparently argue, settles the question of idol-food: since idols are nothing, the food offered to them is just food, and therefore believers may eat it freely.</p>
          <p>Paul's response is immediate and sharp: <hi type="italic">he gnosis physioi, he de agape oikodomei</hi> — "knowledge puffs up, but love builds up."</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">physioi</hi> ("puffs up") is the present active indicative of <hi type="italic">phusioo</hi>, the word that has served as the diagnostic term for the Corinthian disease throughout the letter. In 4:6, Paul warned "that none of you will be puffed up in favor of one against the other." In 4:18, "some are puffed up." In 4:19, "the talk of those who are puffed up." In 5:2, "you are puffed up" in the face of tolerated sin. Now knowledge itself is identified as a cause of puffing up. The same inflation that produced factionalism (chapters 1-4) and moral blindness (chapter 5) is now producing a different symptom: the careless exercise of theological knowledge without regard for its effect on vulnerable community members. The disease is the same; the manifestation is new.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">oikodomei</hi> ("builds up") is the present active indicative of <hi type="italic">oikodomeo</hi> ("to build, to construct, to edify"). This word reaches back to 3:9-14, where Paul described the community as God's building (<hi type="italic">oikodome</hi>) and discussed how various builders construct on the foundation of Christ. Building is constructive, communal, structural — it creates something that serves the whole. Puffing up is individual, unstable, and empty — a balloon has no structural integrity. Paul's contrast is architectural: knowledge without love inflates the individual; love builds up the community. The word <hi type="italic">oikodomeo</hi> will appear again in 8:10 with devastating ironic force, and it will become a central term in chapter 14, where Paul argues that spiritual gifts should be exercised for the building up of the assembly (14:3, 4, 5, 12, 17, 26).</p>
          <p>The contrast between <hi type="italic">gnosis</hi> and <hi type="italic">agape</hi> is not a rejection of knowledge. Paul himself will affirm the content of the Corinthians' knowledge in verses 4-6. The problem is not that they know too much but that they know without loving. Knowledge that operates in isolation from love produces the same arrogance that has been the Corinthians' characteristic failing from the beginning. Knowledge placed in the service of love produces building — the strengthening and upholding of the community.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.8.2-1Cor.8.3">
          <title>Verses 2-3: The Insufficiency of Knowledge and the Priority of Being Known</title>
          <p>"If anyone thinks he knows something, he does not yet know as he ought to know. But if anyone loves God, he is known by him" (8:2-3).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">dokeo</hi> ("to think, to suppose, to imagine, to consider") introduces a note of caution about the knower's self-assessment. The person who thinks (<hi type="italic">dokei</hi>) they have arrived at knowledge (<hi type="italic">egnokenai</hi> — perfect active infinitive of <hi type="italic">ginosko</hi>, indicating a settled, completed state of knowing: "to have come to know and to continue in that knowledge") has not yet come to know (<hi type="italic">oupo egno</hi> — aorist, "has not yet known") as one ought to know (<hi type="italic">kathos dei gnonai</hi>). The triple repetition of <hi type="italic">ginosko</hi> in a single sentence drives the point home: to think you know is not to know; true knowledge requires something the self-confident knower lacks. The word <hi type="italic">oupo</hi> ("not yet") is temporal — the person has not arrived at the finish line they believe they have crossed. The word <hi type="italic">dei</hi> ("it is necessary, one ought") indicates that there is a standard of knowing that this person has not met.</p>
          <p>What is the deficiency? Paul does not specify the missing content of knowledge. Instead he shifts the entire framework: "But if anyone loves God, he is known by him" (<hi type="italic">ei de tis agapa ton theon, houtos egnostai hyp' autou</hi>).</p>
          <p>Several moves happen simultaneously in this sentence.</p>
          <p>First, the criterion shifts from knowing to loving. Paul does not say "if anyone knows God" but "if anyone loves God" (<hi type="italic">agapa ton theon</hi>). Love, not knowledge, is the relational posture that matters. The Corinthians have been emphasizing what they know; Paul redirects to how they relate. The verb <hi type="italic">agapao</hi> ("to love") describes not an emotion but a disposition of active commitment and care.</p>
          <p>Second, the direction of knowing reverses. Instead of the person knowing God, God knows the person: <hi type="italic">egnostai hyp' autou</hi> — "he has been known by him." The perfect passive indicative (<hi type="italic">egnostai</hi>) indicates a completed state with ongoing consequences: the person has been known by God and remains known. The agency belongs to God. Being known by God is prior to and more fundamental than knowing about God.</p>
          <p>The LXX background for God "knowing" a person is covenantal. In Amos 3:2, God says to Israel: "Only you have I known [<hi type="italic">egno</hi>] of all the families of the earth; therefore I will punish you for all your iniquities." God's "knowing" is not cognitive awareness (he is obviously aware of all nations) but covenantal recognition — the choosing of a people for relationship and accountability. In Exodus 33:12, Moses says to God, "You have said, 'I know you by name [<hi type="italic">egno ka se para pantas</hi>], and you have also found favor in my sight.'" To be known by God by name is to be in a relationship of particular divine regard. In Nahum 1:7 (LXX), "The Lord is good, a stronghold in the day of trouble; he knows [<hi type="italic">ginoskon</hi>] those who take refuge in him." God's knowing is protective, relational, and discriminating.</p>
          <p>Paul's point is that the Corinthians have the priority backwards. They are boasting in what they know about God and idols. But the more fundamental reality is whether God knows them — whether they are in covenantal relationship with him. And the evidence of that relationship is not the possession of correct theological information but the practice of love. Knowledge without love is deficient not because it lacks additional facts but because it lacks the relational reality that validates all knowing. A person who knows everything about God but does not love God does not truly know God at all. A person who loves God is known by God — and that is what matters.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.8.4-1Cor.8.6">
          <title>Verses 4-6: The Content of the Knowledge — Monotheistic Confession</title>
          <p>"Therefore, as to the eating of food offered to idols, we know that 'an idol has no real existence in the world' and that 'there is no God but one.' For although there may be so-called gods, whether in heaven or on earth — as indeed there are many 'gods' and many 'lords' — yet for us there is one God, the Father, from whom are all things and for whom we exist, and one Lord, Jesus Christ, through whom are all things and through whom we exist" (8:4-6).</p>
          <p>Having established that knowledge without love is insufficient (vv. 1-3), Paul now turns to the content of the knowledge itself. He does not deny that the Corinthians possess correct theology. He affirms it — and then reformulates it.</p>
          <p>"An idol has no real existence in the world" (<hi type="italic">ouden eidolon en kosmo</hi>) — literally, "nothing is an idol in the world" or "an idol is nothing in the world." This is the standard Jewish theological position, rooted deeply in the prophetic and wisdom traditions of the LXX. The idol-polemic runs throughout Israel's scriptures:</p>
          <p>Isaiah 44:9-20 contains the most sustained mockery of idol-making in the Old Testament. The craftsman takes a piece of wood; half of it he burns for fuel, warms himself, bakes bread; and from the other half he makes a god and bows down to it: "He feeds on ashes; a deluded heart has led him astray, and he cannot deliver himself or say, 'Is there not a lie in my right hand?'" (Isaiah 44:20). The idol is the product of human labor, made from the same material as firewood. It has no independent existence as a deity.</p>
          <p>Psalm 115:4-8 (LXX 113:12-16): "Their idols are silver and gold, the work of human hands. They have mouths, but do not speak; eyes, but do not see. They have ears, but do not hear; noses, but do not smell." The gods of the nations are artifacts, not agents. They possess the form of living beings but none of the capacities. The psalm concludes with a symmetrical judgment: "Those who make them become like them; so do all who trust in them" (115:8).</p>
          <p>Psalm 96:5 (LXX 95:5): "For all the gods of the peoples are idols [<hi type="italic">eidola</hi>], but the Lord made the heavens." The LXX rendering here uses <hi type="italic">eidola</hi> for the Hebrew <hi type="italic">elilim</hi> ("worthless things, nothings"). The gods of the nations are nothings; the Lord is the maker of all that exists.</p>
          <p>Jeremiah 10:3-5: "The customs of the peoples are vanity. A tree from the forest is cut down and worked with an axe by the hands of a craftsman. They decorate it with silver and gold; they fasten it with hammer and nails so that it cannot move. Their idols are like scarecrows in a cucumber field, and they cannot speak; they have to be carried, for they cannot walk."</p>
          <p>The Letter of Jeremiah (Baruch 6 in the LXX), a text widely circulated in the first century, offers an extended catalogue of reasons why idols should not be feared: they cannot save themselves from rust, moths, thieves, fire, or war; they are less useful than a door, a pillar, or a household vessel.</p>
          <p>The Corinthians' claim that "an idol is nothing in the world" is therefore solidly grounded in the Jewish scriptural tradition. Paul does not dispute it.</p>
          <p>"There is no God but one" (<hi type="italic">oudeis theos ei me heis</hi>) — this is the monotheistic confession, the Shema of Deuteronomy 6:4 in distilled form. The LXX of Deuteronomy 6:4 reads: <hi type="italic">akoue Israel, kyrios ho theos hemon kyrios heis estin</hi> — "Hear, O Israel: the Lord our God, the Lord is one." This confession defined Jewish identity. It was recited daily. It was the fundamental theological claim from which all other claims followed. The Corinthians — including the Gentile converts who had learned this from Paul's teaching — possess this knowledge. Idols are nothing; God is one.</p>
          <p>Paul then acknowledges the experiential reality of the Greco-Roman religious landscape: "For although there may be so-called gods, whether in heaven or on earth — as indeed there are many 'gods' and many 'lords'" (8:5). The word <hi type="italic">legomenoi</hi> ("so-called," present passive participle of <hi type="italic">lego</hi>, "to call, to name") carefully qualifies these entities: they are called gods, but the designation is a human attribution, not a divine reality. The phrase <hi type="italic">eite en ourano eite epi ges</hi> ("whether in heaven or on earth") encompasses the entire ancient cosmology — celestial deities (Zeus, Aphrodite, Apollo), terrestrial powers (local gods, river gods, deified rulers), and everything in between. The concession <hi type="italic">hosper eisin theoi polloi kai kyrioi polloi</hi> ("as indeed there are many gods and many lords") acknowledges that, from the perspective of the surrounding culture, the world is populated with divine beings. Paul does not deny that these beings are experienced as real powers; he denies them the title <hi type="italic">theos</hi> in its proper theological sense.</p>
          <p>Then comes the confession that reformulates the Shema christologically: "Yet for us there is one God, the Father, from whom are all things and for whom we exist, and one Lord, Jesus Christ, through whom are all things and through whom we exist" (8:6).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">hemin de</hi> ("but for us") marks the boundary between the worldview of the surrounding culture (many gods, many lords) and the confession of the community (one God, one Lord). The structure is precise and deliberate:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">One God, the Father (<hi type="italic">heis theos ho pater</hi>):</hi></p>
          <list type="x-bullet">
            <item>"from whom are all things" (<hi type="italic">ex hou ta panta</hi>) — God as the ultimate source (<hi type="italic">ex</hi>, "from, out of") of all existence. Everything that exists derives from the Father.</item>
            <item>"and for whom we exist" (<hi type="italic">kai hemeis eis auton</hi>) — the community exists for God, directed toward him (<hi type="italic">eis</hi>, "toward, for") as their purpose and goal.</item>
          </list>
          <p><hi type="bold">One Lord, Jesus Christ (<hi type="italic">heis kyrios Iesous Christos</hi>):</hi></p>
          <list type="x-bullet">
            <item>"through whom are all things" (<hi type="italic">di' hou ta panta</hi>) — Christ as the agent (<hi type="italic">dia</hi>, "through") of creation. All things came into existence through him.</item>
            <item>"and through whom we exist" (<hi type="italic">kai hemeis di' autou</hi>) — the community exists through Christ as the mediating agent of their calling and formation.</item>
          </list>
          <p>The prepositions distinguish the roles without separating the persons: God is the source (<hi type="italic">ex</hi>) and goal (<hi type="italic">eis</hi>); Christ is the agent (<hi type="italic">dia</hi>) through whom the source acts. This is not a second deity alongside the first but a differentiation within the one divine identity. The title <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> ("Lord") is the word the LXX uses to render the divine name YHWH throughout the scriptures. To call Jesus <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> is to place him within the identity of the God confessed in the Shema. The one God of Deuteronomy 6:4 is now confessed as Father and Lord — the Father as source and goal, the Lord Jesus Christ as agent and mediator.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">di' hou ta panta</hi> ("through whom all things") attributes to Christ a role in creation itself. This places the confession alongside the Wisdom tradition in which God creates through his Wisdom: Proverbs 8:22-31 (LXX), where Wisdom says, "The Lord created me at the beginning of his ways, for his works... when he established the heavens, I was there"; Wisdom of Solomon 7:22, where Wisdom is "the fashioner of all things" (<hi type="italic">he ton panton technitis</hi>); Wisdom of Solomon 9:1-2, "O God of my ancestors and Lord of mercy, who have made all things by your word, and by your wisdom have formed humankind." Paul places Christ in the role that Jewish wisdom speculation assigned to God's creative Wisdom. Christ is the agent through whom the one God made and sustains all things.</p>
          <p>For the first-century audience in Corinth, this confession functions as a counter-claim. The city was filled with temples to many gods and many lords. The imperial cult honored the emperor as <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi>. Guild meetings, civic festivals, and family celebrations all involved acknowledging various deities. The confession "for us there is one God... and one Lord" is a declaration of exclusive allegiance. The community recognizes no other god as source and no other lord as agent. This has direct practical implications for the idol-food question: if idols are nothing and there is only one God and one Lord, then food offered to non-entities is just food. The theological reasoning is sound. The question Paul will press is whether sound theology alone is sufficient to determine behavior.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.8.7-1Cor.8.8">
          <title>Verses 7-8: The Weak Conscience and the Limits of Knowledge</title>
          <p>"However, not all possess this knowledge. But some, through being accustomed to idols until now, eat food as really offered to an idol, and their conscience, being weak, is defiled" (8:7).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">alla</hi> ("however, but") introduces the qualification that transforms the entire argument. The <hi type="italic">gnosis</hi> that "all of us possess" (v. 1) turns out not to be universal after all. Some (<hi type="italic">tines</hi>) within the community do not share this knowledge. The Corinthians' confident slogan — "all of us possess knowledge" — was an overstatement. Paul corrects it: not all.</p>
          <p>The reason is given: <hi type="italic">te synetheia heos arti tou eidolou</hi> ("through being accustomed to the idol until now"). The word <hi type="italic">synetheia</hi> ("custom, habit, long familiarity") indicates formation over time. These community members have spent their lives in the world of idol worship. They grew up attending temple festivals, offering libations, participating in meals where the food was understood to be the god's provision. Their entire previous existence was shaped by the assumption that the gods were real and that food offered to them carried the deity's presence or power. This formation cannot be undone by a simple announcement that "idols are nothing." Intellectual conviction requires time to reshape the habits of perception and the reflexes of conscience.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">heos arti</hi> ("until now") is significant. It indicates that these people are recent converts or that their old associations have persisted even after entering the community. The old way of seeing has not yet been replaced. When they encounter food that has been offered to an idol, they experience it <hi type="italic">hos eidolothyton</hi> ("as genuinely offered to an idol") — that is, they perceive the food as actually connected to the deity, as carrying the weight of a real offering to a real divine being. They cannot detach the meat from its ritual context. For them, eating this food is not a neutral act; it is participation in idol worship.</p>
          <p>The result: <hi type="italic">he syneidesis auton asthenes ousa molynetai</hi> — "their conscience, being weak, is defiled." The word <hi type="italic">syneidesis</hi> ("conscience") describes the internal faculty by which a person evaluates their own actions as right or wrong. It is the capacity for moral self-judgment. The word appears in this chapter at verses 7, 10, and 12, and then prominently in 10:25, 27, 28, and 29. In each case it refers to the individual's internal moral compass — the awareness that accompanies action and either approves or condemns it.</p>
          <p>The conscience is described as <hi type="italic">asthenes</hi> ("weak"). The word <hi type="italic">asthenes</hi> has appeared throughout the letter: in 1:25, "the weakness of God is stronger than men"; in 1:27, "God chose what is weak in the world to shame the strong"; in 4:10, "we are weak, but you are strong." The "weak" in chapter 8 are not morally inferior or spiritually deficient in some absolute sense. Their weakness consists in the fact that their conscience has not been informed by the knowledge that idols are nothing. They still operate with the old perceptual framework. A "weak" conscience is not a malfunctioning conscience; it is one that has not yet been liberated from its former associations. It still responds to idol-food as though the idol were real, and therefore condemns the eater for participating in what it perceives as genuine idol worship.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">molyno</hi> ("to defile, to stain, to pollute") describes the effect on the conscience. The word appears in the LXX in contexts of ritual and moral contamination. In Jeremiah 23:11 (LXX), "both prophet and priest are defiled" (<hi type="italic">emolynthesan</hi>). The person whose weak conscience encounters idol-food and perceives it as a real offering to a real deity is defiled — not because the food is inherently contaminating (Paul has just affirmed that idols are nothing) but because the person's own conscience condemns the act. The defilement is real for the person experiencing it, even though the theological basis for it is mistaken. Paul takes the weak person's subjective experience seriously as a moral reality. The conscience that condemns is violated by the act, regardless of whether the act is objectively permissible.</p>
          <p>"Food will not commend us to God. We are no worse off if we do not eat, and no better off if we do" (8:8).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">paristemi</hi> ("to present, to commend, to bring near, to place before") can describe presenting oneself before a king or judge. Food (<hi type="italic">broma</hi>) does not affect one's standing before God. This statement appears to be another Corinthian claim that Paul endorses at the level of principle. The logic is impeccable: if idols are nothing and God is one, then food — regardless of its ritual history — has no effect on the person's relationship with God. Neither eating nor abstaining changes one's status <hi type="italic">coram Deo</hi>.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">oute ean me phagomen hysteroimetha, oute ean phagomen perisseuomen</hi> ("neither if we do not eat are we worse off, nor if we eat are we better off") establishes the adiaphorous character of food. The verb <hi type="italic">hystereo</hi> ("to be lacking, to fall short, to be worse off") and <hi type="italic">perisseuo</hi> ("to abound, to be better off, to have more than enough") describe relative advantage and disadvantage. Food gives no spiritual advantage and its absence creates no spiritual disadvantage. This principle is correct. But correctness alone does not resolve the question. The problem is not the theology of food but the effect of food-related behavior on other people.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.8.9-1Cor.8.12">
          <title>Verses 9-12: The Stumbling Block, the Destruction of the Brother, and Sin Against Christ</title>
          <p>"But take care that this right of yours does not somehow become a stumbling block to the weak" (8:9).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">blepete</hi> ("take care, watch out, beware") is an imperative of warning. Paul shifts from theological affirmation to practical caution. The word <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> ("right, authority, liberty, power, freedom") appears here for the first time in the idol-food argument. The Corinthians' correct theology gives them <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> — the legitimate right to eat idol-food without guilt. Paul does not deny this right. He warns about its exercise.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> will dominate chapter 9, where Paul will catalogue his own apostolic rights (the right to food and drink, to a wife, to financial support — 9:4-6) and then demonstrate that he has declined to exercise any of them. The introduction of <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> here in 8:9 prepares for the extended illustration of chapter 9. The pattern is: Paul establishes the principle of self-limited <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> in chapter 8, then demonstrates it from his own life in chapter 9.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">proskomma</hi> ("stumbling block, obstacle, cause of falling") describes something that causes another person to trip and fall. In the LXX, the related noun <hi type="italic">skandalon</hi> ("snare, trap") appears in contexts of moral and spiritual danger: Psalm 140:5 (LXX 139:6), "the arrogant have set a trap for me, and cords; they have spread a net by the wayside; they have set snares [<hi type="italic">skandala</hi>] for me"; Leviticus 19:14, "You shall not put a stumbling block before the blind." The stumbling block is not a minor inconvenience; it is something that causes falling — spiritual falling, the kind of fall from which one may not recover.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">tois asthenesin</hi> ("to the weak") identifies who is at risk. The weak — those whose consciences have not been formed by the knowledge that idols are nothing — are the ones who will stumble. The knowledgeable person's exercise of <hi type="italic">exousia</hi>, harmless to themselves, becomes dangerous to the person who lacks their theological formation.</p>
          <p>"For if anyone sees you who possess knowledge eating in an idol's temple, will not the conscience of the one who is weak be built up to eat food offered to idols?" (8:10).</p>
          <p>Paul constructs a concrete scenario. The phrase <hi type="italic">en eidoleio katakeimenon</hi> ("reclining in an idol's temple") places the knowledgeable person at a meal in a temple dining room. The word <hi type="italic">eidoleion</hi> ("idol's temple") denotes the precinct of an idol — the temple building or its associated dining facilities. Archaeological evidence from Corinth confirms the existence of dining rooms attached to temples. The Asklepieion (temple of Asclepius) near the center of the city had dining rooms where meals were served; the sanctuaries of Demeter and Kore on Acrocorinth likewise provided spaces for communal dining. In Greco-Roman culture, temple dining rooms served as the venue for a wide range of social occasions: guild meetings, trade association gatherings, birthday celebrations, wedding banquets, and business dinners. An invitation to dine "in the temple of Serapis" (as documented in papyrus invitations from the period) was a routine social event. For a Corinthian of any social standing, refusing all temple-associated meals would mean withdrawing from virtually the entire fabric of civic and commercial social life.</p>
          <p>The knowledgeable person, secure in the knowledge that the idol is nothing, sees no problem reclining at such a meal. The food is just food. The god is no god. The setting is socially convenient. But the weak person — someone who sees the knowledgeable person eating there — is affected differently.</p>
          <p>Paul's description of the effect on the weak person is devastatingly ironic. He uses the verb <hi type="italic">oikodomeo</hi>: <hi type="italic">ouchi he syneidesis autou ... oikodomethesetai eis to ta eidolothyta esthiein</hi> — "will not his conscience be built up to eat food offered to idols?" In verse 1, Paul contrasted puffing up (knowledge) with building up (love). Building up was the positive alternative — the thing love does. Now Paul takes the same word and applies it to the destruction of a person. The weak person's conscience is "built up" — not toward maturity, not toward the edification of the community, but toward eating food that their own conscience condemns. The word <hi type="italic">oikodomeo</hi> is used with savage irony: the knowledgeable person's example "builds up" the weak person toward self-destruction. The verb that should describe the constructive work of love is turned into a description of how one believer's freedom demolishes another.</p>
          <p>The mechanism is imitation. The weak person sees a community member they respect eating in the temple and concludes: if they can do it, so can I. But for the weak person, eating idol-food is not a theologically neutral act. Their conscience still registers it as participation in idol worship. They eat — but they eat against their own conscience. They do what they believe to be wrong. And this violation of conscience is not a minor educational step toward greater freedom; it is a defilement (8:7) and, as the next verse will say, a destruction.</p>
          <p>"And so by your knowledge this weak person is destroyed, the brother for whom Christ died" (8:11).</p>
          <p>This is the theological center of the chapter. The verb <hi type="italic">apollytai</hi> (present middle/passive of <hi type="italic">apollymi</hi>, "to destroy, to ruin, to perish, to be lost") carries the full weight of its usage throughout the letter and in the broader scriptural tradition.</p>
          <p>In 1:18, Paul used the same word to describe two categories of people: "For the word of the cross is folly to those who are perishing [<hi type="italic">tois apollymenois</hi>], but to us who are being saved [<hi type="italic">tois sozomenois</hi>] it is the power of God." The <hi type="italic">apollymenoi</hi> are those on the path to destruction — those for whom the cross remains foolishness. The <hi type="italic">sozomenoi</hi> are those on the path to salvation. These are the two trajectories. Now, in 8:11, Paul says that the weak brother is <hi type="italic">apollytai</hi> — is being destroyed, is perishing. The same verb, the same trajectory. The knowledgeable person's exercise of freedom is pushing a brother onto the path of destruction — the path that leads away from salvation.</p>
          <p>The verb is in the present tense (<hi type="italic">apollytai</hi>), indicating a process in motion: the person is being destroyed, is in the act of perishing. This is not a hypothetical or a mild exaggeration. Paul is describing what is happening to the weak person when the knowledgeable person's example leads them to violate their conscience.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ho adelphos di' hon Christos apethanen</hi> ("the brother for whom Christ died") raises the stakes to the highest possible level. Three elements demand attention.</p>
          <p>First, the person is called <hi type="italic">adelphos</hi> ("brother"). This is a member of the community, not an outsider. The community is a family (4:14-15 — Paul is their father; they are his children and his brothers). The one being destroyed is a sibling.</p>
          <p>Second, Christ died for this person. The preposition <hi type="italic">di' hon</hi> ("for whom, on account of whom") indicates that Christ's death had this specific person in view. The death of Christ was not an abstraction; it was for particular people, including this weak brother. The verb <hi type="italic">apethanen</hi> ("died") is the aorist of <hi type="italic">apothnesko</hi> — Christ's death is a completed historical event with enduring consequences. The weak brother is someone for whom the Messiah gave his life.</p>
          <p>Third, the destruction of this brother undoes what Christ's death accomplished. Christ died to save; the knowledgeable person's freedom is destroying. The two actions work at cross purposes. The cross was the means of rescue; the exercise of <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> is the means of ruin. Paul does not soften the collision. The person whom Christ died to save is being destroyed by a fellow believer's theological confidence.</p>
          <p>The combination of <hi type="italic">apollymi</hi> with the reminder of Christ's death creates a jarring juxtaposition. In 1:18, the perishing (<hi type="italic">apollymenoi</hi>) were those outside the community — those for whom the cross was foolishness. Now a person inside the community — a brother, one for whom Christ died — is being placed on that same trajectory. The knowledgeable person's freedom is accomplishing what the world's rejection of the cross accomplishes: the destruction of someone who should be on the path of salvation.</p>
          <p>"Thus, sinning against your brothers and wounding their weak conscience, you sin against Christ" (8:12).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">hamartano</hi> ("to sin") appears twice, framing the verse. The first occurrence is aimed at the brothers: <hi type="italic">hamartanontes eis tous adelphous</hi> — "sinning against the brothers." The second occurrence is aimed at Christ: <hi type="italic">eis Christon hamartanete</hi> — "you sin against Christ." The two sins are not separate offenses but the same offense viewed from two perspectives. To sin against the brother is to sin against Christ. The logic is christological: the brother belongs to Christ (3:23, "you are Christ's"); Christ died for the brother (8:11). To damage someone who belongs to Christ and for whom Christ died is to commit an offense against Christ himself.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">typtein</hi> ("to strike, to beat, to wound") describes what the knowledgeable person does to the weak conscience. The word is physical — it describes a blow. The weak person's conscience is beaten, struck, battered by the knowledgeable person's example. The language is violent. Paul does not describe a gentle misunderstanding or a minor social friction. He describes an assault on the interior moral faculty of a vulnerable person.</p>
          <p>The progression of the argument is relentless. In verse 9, the exercise of <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> might become a stumbling block — the possibility is raised. In verse 10, the weak person's conscience is "built up" toward eating idol-food — the mechanism is described. In verse 11, the weak person is being destroyed — the consequence is named. In verse 12, the action is identified as sin against Christ — the theological verdict is rendered. Each step escalates. What began as a question about dietary freedom has become a matter of sin against the Lord who died for the community.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.8.13">
          <title>Verse 13: Paul's Own Resolution — Total Self-Limitation</title>
          <p>"Therefore, if food makes my brother stumble, I will never eat meat again, that I might not make my brother stumble" (8:13).</p>
          <p>Paul draws the practical conclusion with extreme language. The word <hi type="italic">dioper</hi> ("for this very reason, therefore") introduces the application. Paul does not merely instruct the Corinthians; he states what he himself would do.</p>
          <p>The double negative <hi type="italic">ou me</hi> with the aorist subjunctive (<hi type="italic">phago</hi>) is the strongest form of negation in Greek: "I will absolutely not eat" — there is no possibility, no circumstance, no exception. The scope is also extreme: <hi type="italic">krea</hi> ("meat, flesh") — not just idol-food but all meat. The temporal scope is likewise unlimited: <hi type="italic">eis ton aiona</hi> ("into the age, forever, for all time"). Paul would give up all meat permanently and without exception if his eating would cause a brother to stumble.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">skandalizo</hi> ("to cause to stumble, to cause to fall, to be a snare") appears twice, first in the conditional (<hi type="italic">ei broma skandalizei ton adelphon mou</hi>, "if food causes my brother to stumble") and then in the purpose clause (<hi type="italic">hina me ton adelphon mou skandaliso</hi>, "so that I might not cause my brother to stumble"). The word <hi type="italic">skandalon</hi> in the LXX translates the Hebrew <hi type="italic">moqesh</hi> ("snare, trap") — an instrument that catches and holds, that prevents escape, that leads to capture and death. In the teaching tradition associated with Jesus, the <hi type="italic">skandalon</hi> carries the most severe warnings: "Whoever causes one of these little ones who believe in me to stumble, it would be better for him to have a great millstone fastened around his neck and to be drowned in the depth of the sea" (Matthew 18:6, reflecting language that circulated in the early communities). To cause a brother to stumble is to place a lethal trap in the path of someone whom Christ died to save.</p>
          <p>Paul's resolution is not a command but an example. He does not say "you must never eat meat again." He says "I will never eat meat again" — if that is what the situation requires. The principle is self-limitation for the sake of the vulnerable. The knowledgeable person has the right to eat. But love constrains the exercise of that right. The correct theology that idols are nothing and food is indifferent does not override the obligation to protect a brother from destruction.</p>
          <p>This verse also prepares for chapter 9, where Paul will provide the extended demonstration of this principle in his own life. His willingness to give up meat forever is not an isolated statement; it is the headline for an entire chapter in which Paul shows how he has voluntarily surrendered right after right — financial support, marital companionship, freedom from labor — for the sake of the gospel and the people it serves. The man who would give up meat forever has already given up far more.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.8">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 8 is built on a single argumentative structure: correct theology, rightly understood, does not grant permission to act without regard for the effect of one's actions on other people. The Corinthians possess genuine knowledge: idols are nothing, God is one, food is indifferent. Paul affirms every element of this theology. But he subordinates the entire structure to a prior obligation: love for the brother.</p>
          <p>The chapter proceeds through four stages.</p>
          <p>First (vv. 1-3): Paul engages the Corinthians' confident claim — "we all possess knowledge" — and immediately qualifies it. Knowledge puffs up; love builds up. The person who thinks they know has not yet reached the knowing that matters. The person who loves God is known by God. The relational reality (being known by God, loving God) is more fundamental than the intellectual reality (knowing about idols). This opening salvo reframes the entire question. The issue is not "what do we know about idols?" but "how does our knowledge affect the people around us?"</p>
          <p>Second (vv. 4-8): Paul affirms the content of the Corinthians' knowledge: idols are nothing, there is one God and one Lord. He reformulates the monotheistic confession in explicitly christological terms — one God the Father as source and goal, one Lord Jesus Christ as agent. He grants that food is indifferent before God. But he immediately introduces the qualification: not everyone possesses this knowledge. Some, shaped by years of idol worship, cannot eat idol-food without their conscience registering it as participation in genuine idol worship. Their conscience, being weak, is defiled. The theological truth that idols are nothing does not change the experiential reality that some believers still perceive idols as real. Knowledge has not yet penetrated their formed habits of perception.</p>
          <p>Third (vv. 9-12): Paul warns that the knowledgeable person's exercise of <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> can become a stumbling block to the weak. The specific scenario is eating in a temple dining room, where the weak person sees the behavior, is emboldened to imitate it, and is destroyed. The word <hi type="italic">apollymi</hi> — "to destroy, to perish" — uses the same vocabulary as 1:18, placing the stakes at the level of eschatological ruin. The brother being destroyed is one "for whom Christ died." The sin is twofold: against the brother and against Christ himself. The exercise of freedom that damages a brother is not the innocent application of correct theology; it is sin.</p>
          <p>Fourth (v. 13): Paul states his own resolution: he would give up all meat forever rather than cause a brother to stumble. The strongest possible negation (<hi type="italic">ou me ... eis ton aiona</hi>) matches the strongest possible concern (the destruction of a brother for whom Christ died). This verse is both conclusion and transition — it concludes the chapter's argument and prepares for the personal demonstration of chapter 9.</p>
          <p>Several features of the chapter's argument deserve attention as the letter's larger context is kept in view.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">phusioo</hi> ("to puff up") in 8:1 identifies the Corinthians' knowledge-based freedom as another manifestation of the same disease that produced their factionalism (4:6, 18, 19) and their tolerance of sin (5:2). The Corinthians' consistent problem is inflation — self-importance generated by what they possess (gifts, knowledge, rhetorical skill, teacher-allegiance) without regard for how that possession affects the community. Knowledge that puffs up operates just like wisdom that puffs up and spiritual gifts that puff up: it makes the individual feel superior while the community suffers.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">apollymi</hi> in 8:11 places this chapter's stakes at the same level as 1:18. In 1:18, the word described those who are perishing — those for whom the cross is foolishness, those on the trajectory toward destruction. In 8:11, the same word describes what is happening to a brother within the community because of another brother's careless exercise of freedom. A person whom Christ died to save is being destroyed by a fellow believer. The vocabulary does not allow this to be read as mere inconvenience or emotional discomfort. Paul is describing genuine ruin — the undoing of what Christ accomplished through his death. The knowledgeable person's freedom, exercised without love, works against the purpose of the cross.</p>
          <p>The relationship between <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> and <hi type="italic">agape</hi> is the chapter's governing framework, and it will continue to govern through chapters 9 and 10. The right to eat is real. The obligation to love is higher. When the two conflict, love determines behavior, not right. This principle does not destroy freedom; it directs freedom. The free person voluntarily limits their freedom for the sake of someone else's well-being. This is not weakness but strength — the strength to forgo what one is entitled to, for the sake of someone who would be harmed by it. Paul will demonstrate in chapter 9 that this is exactly how he conducts his own apostolic life: he has rights; he declines to use them; he does this for the gospel and for the people the gospel has reached.</p>
          <p>The chapter also carries forward the communal logic that has shaped the letter from the beginning. The Corinthians are not a collection of individual knowers exercising individual rights. They are an assembly — God's temple (3:16), God's field and building (3:9), the body of Christ (as Paul will argue in chapter 12). The actions of one member affect all members. The knowledgeable person who eats in the temple is not an isolated individual making a private dietary choice. They are a member of a body, and their action causes another member to be destroyed. The communal nature of the assembly means that freedom is never merely individual. Every exercise of <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> takes place within the network of relationships that constitute the body. Love — the disposition that builds up rather than puffs up — is the faculty that recognizes this interconnection and acts accordingly.</p>
          <p>Paul will return to the idol-food question in chapter 10 with additional arguments. He will draw on Israel's wilderness experience as a warning against overconfidence (10:1-13), issue a severe prohibition against actual participation in idol worship on the grounds that it constitutes fellowship with demons (10:14-22), and then provide practical guidelines for specific situations (10:23-11:1). Chapter 8 is the opening statement — it establishes the principle that knowledge without love destroys, and that the destruction of a brother for whom Christ died is sin against Christ himself. The remaining chapters will fill in the details, but the principle stands from the beginning: the measure of correct behavior is not "what do I know?" but "what does love require?"</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.9">
        <title>1 Corinthians 9 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.9">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 8):</hi> Paul opened a new section of the letter in response to the Corinthians' question about food offered to idols (<hi type="italic">eidolothyta</hi>), introduced by the formula <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> ("now concerning") at 8:1. He engaged a Corinthian slogan — "all of us possess knowledge" — and immediately qualified it: knowledge puffs up (<hi type="italic">physioi</hi>), but love builds up (<hi type="italic">oikodomei</hi>). He then affirmed the content of the Corinthians' theology: idols are nothing, God is one, food is indifferent before God (8:4-8). But he introduced the decisive qualification: not everyone possesses this knowledge. Some members, shaped by years of idol worship, still perceive idol-food as genuinely offered to a real deity, and their conscience — being weak (<hi type="italic">asthenes</hi>) — is defiled when they eat it (8:7). The knowledgeable person's exercise of their right (<hi type="italic">exousia</hi>, 8:9) to eat in a temple dining room can become a stumbling block (<hi type="italic">proskomma</hi>) to the weak, leading the weak person to eat against their own conscience. The result is devastating: the weak brother "is destroyed" (<hi type="italic">apollytai</hi>) — the same verb used in 1:18 for those who are perishing — "the brother for whom Christ died" (8:11). The sin is simultaneously against the brother and against Christ (8:12). Paul concluded with an extreme personal declaration: he would give up all meat forever (<hi type="italic">ou me phago krea eis ton aiona</hi>) if eating would cause a brother to stumble (8:13). This declaration was not merely a conclusion to chapter 8 but a bridge to chapter 9, where Paul would demonstrate at length that voluntary self-limitation of rights is the pattern of his entire apostolic life.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 10):</hi> Paul returns from the personal illustration of chapter 9 to the idol-food question, but now with the weight of Israel's history behind him. He opens with "For I do not want you to be unaware, brothers" (10:1), signaling that what follows is essential to the argument. He recounts Israel's wilderness experience: all (<hi type="italic">pantes</hi>, repeated five times in 10:1-4) were under the cloud, all passed through the sea, all were baptized into Moses, all ate the same spiritual food, all drank from the same spiritual rock. Yet "with most of them God was not pleased, and they were struck down in the wilderness" (10:5). The universal participation in God's provision did not prevent destruction. The wilderness failures are then catalogued: idolatry (10:7, citing Exodus 32:6 — the golden calf episode), sexual immorality (10:8, the incident at Baal-Peor), testing God (10:9, the serpent plague), grumbling (10:10, the destroying angel). The conclusion: "these things happened to them as examples, and they were written down for our instruction, upon whom the ends of the ages have come" (10:11). The word <hi type="italic">typikos</hi> ("as examples, typologically") signals that Israel's history is not merely informational but instructive — it establishes a pattern that applies to the Corinthian community. The warning follows: "Therefore let anyone who thinks that he stands take heed lest he fall" (10:12). Paul then issues a severe prohibition against actual participation in idol worship on the grounds that the cup and table of demons cannot coexist with the cup and table of the Lord (10:14-22), before concluding with practical guidelines for specific situations (10:23-11:1).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 9 connects these:</hi> The connection is not merely associative but structural. Chapters 8-10 form a single argumentative unit on the idol-food question. Chapter 8 established the principle: correct knowledge about idols does not grant permission to exercise one's rights without regard for the effect on vulnerable community members. Love constrains the exercise of freedom. Chapter 9 is the personal demonstration of that principle. Paul does not merely tell the Corinthians to limit their freedom; he shows them how he has already done so, at far greater cost than anything he is asking of them. Chapter 10 then completes the argument by showing that overconfidence in one's spiritual standing — the assumption that knowledge or participation in sacred rites guarantees safety — has a catastrophic precedent in Israel's own history.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> ("right, authority") is the hinge. It appeared in 8:9, where Paul warned that "this right of yours" (<hi type="italic">he exousia hymon haute</hi>) might become a stumbling block. In chapter 9, Paul catalogues his own extensive <hi type="italic">exousiai</hi> — his rights as an apostle that he has voluntarily declined to exercise. The word appears six times in this chapter (9:4, 5, 6, 12 [twice], 18), dominating the argument. What Paul asked the Corinthians to consider doing with one right (their right to eat idol-food) he has already done with every right he possesses. The man who told them to limit their <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> has already surrendered all of his own.</p>
          <p>The chapter also serves as the link between the general principle of chapter 8 and the historical warning of chapter 10. Paul's statement in 9:27 — "lest after preaching to others I myself should be disqualified (<hi type="italic">adokimos</hi>)" — anticipates the wilderness generation of chapter 10, who participated in all of God's saving acts yet were destroyed. The self-discipline Paul describes in 9:24-27 is exactly the vigilance that Israel failed to exercise in 10:1-13. The three chapters form a progression: principle (ch. 8), personal example (ch. 9), historical warning (ch. 10).</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.9.1-1Cor.9.2">
          <title>Verses 1-2: Paul's Assertion of Freedom and Apostolic Status</title>
          <p>"Am I not free? Am I not an apostle? Have I not seen Jesus our Lord? Are not you my workmanship in the Lord?" (9:1).</p>
          <p>Four rapid rhetorical questions, each expecting the answer "yes," establish the platform from which the rest of the chapter will argue. The force of the questions depends on their answers being beyond dispute.</p>
          <p>The first question — <hi type="italic">ouk eimi eleutheros</hi> ("am I not free?") — places freedom (<hi type="italic">eleutheria</hi>) at the head of the list. The word <hi type="italic">eleutheros</hi> ("free, not enslaved, at liberty") describes the status of one who is not under compulsion or servitude. Paul is free. His freedom is not in question. This opening is essential because the chapter will describe Paul's voluntary self-enslavement (<hi type="italic">edoulosa</hi>, 9:19), and the reader must understand from the outset that Paul's service is a choice made from a position of freedom, not a necessity imposed by someone else's authority.</p>
          <p>The second question — <hi type="italic">ouk eimi apostolos</hi> ("am I not an apostle?") — asserts Paul's specific role. The word <hi type="italic">apostolos</hi> ("one sent, an emissary, an envoy") in Paul's usage designates those commissioned directly by the risen Christ. The question implies that Paul's apostolic status was being questioned in Corinth — or at least that some might use his financial self-support as evidence that he lacked the standing of the "real" apostles (the pillars in Jerusalem, Peter and the others). Paul asserts what some evidently doubted.</p>
          <p>The third question — <hi type="italic">ouchi ton kyrion hemon Iesoun heoraka</hi> ("have I not seen Jesus our Lord?") — provides the credential for apostleship. The verb <hi type="italic">heoraka</hi> (perfect active of <hi type="italic">horao</hi>, "to see") describes a completed act of seeing whose result persists: "I have seen and continue to be one who has seen." This is Paul's claim to having encountered the risen Jesus — the same claim he will elaborate in 15:8: "last of all, as to one untimely born, he appeared also to me." Seeing the risen Lord was the defining qualification of an apostle (cf. Acts 1:21-22, where the replacement for Judah must be "one of the men who have accompanied us during all the time that the Lord Jesus went in and out among us... a witness to his resurrection"). Paul's seeing of the Lord is his authorization.</p>
          <p>The fourth question — <hi type="italic">ou to ergon mou hymeis este en kyrio</hi> ("are not you my workmanship in the Lord?") — turns from credential to evidence. The word <hi type="italic">ergon</hi> ("work, deed, product, outcome") is concrete: the Corinthians themselves are the tangible result of Paul's apostolic labor. Their very existence as a community of faith is proof that Paul is a genuine apostle. The phrase <hi type="italic">en kyrio</hi> ("in the Lord") locates this work within the sphere of Christ's authority. Paul did not create the Corinthian assembly by his own power; he did it in the Lord. But the fact remains: they exist because Paul labored among them.</p>
          <p>"If to others I am not an apostle, at least I am to you, for you are the seal of my apostleship in the Lord" (9:2).</p>
          <p>The concession — <hi type="italic">ei allois ouk eimi apostolos</hi> ("if to others I am not an apostle") — uses a first-class conditional (<hi type="italic">ei</hi> + indicative), which assumes the condition for the sake of argument without confirming its truth. Even granting the worst-case reading — that some people do not recognize Paul's apostleship — the Corinthians cannot deny it, because they are his <hi type="italic">sphragis</hi> ("seal"). The word <hi type="italic">sphragis</hi> describes a stamped impression that authenticates a document or marks ownership. A seal verified that a letter was genuine, that a container's contents were untampered, that property belonged to its owner. In the LXX, the seal carries connotations of authenticity and divine commissioning. The Corinthians are the authenticating stamp of Paul's apostleship: their existence as a believing community validates the genuineness of Paul's commission. You cannot doubt my apostleship, Paul argues, without doubting the reality of your own conversion, because my apostleship produced your community.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.9.3-1Cor.9.6">
          <title>Verses 3-6: The Catalogue of Rights</title>
          <p>"This is my defense to those who would examine me" (9:3).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">apologia</hi> ("defense, legal defense, formal reply") is a judicial term. It describes the response of a defendant to charges or examination. The word <hi type="italic">anakrino</hi> ("to examine, to investigate, to interrogate") was used in 2:14-15 and 4:3-4 in the context of judgment and evaluation. Paul treats the questioning of his apostolic rights as a formal proceeding and offers his formal response. The verse is transitional — it introduces the catalogue that follows as a reasoned defense, not a spontaneous outburst.</p>
          <p>"Do we not have the right to eat and drink?" (9:4).</p>
          <p>The first <hi type="italic">exousia</hi>: the right to eat and drink at the community's expense. The word <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> (here translated "right") means "authority, liberty, right of disposal, power." The verb <hi type="italic">echein exousian</hi> ("to have the right") asserts legitimate entitlement. The eating and drinking (<hi type="italic">phagein kai piein</hi>) refers to being sustained by the community — receiving food and drink as provisions from those to whom one ministers.</p>
          <p>"Do we not have the right to be accompanied by a believing wife, as do the other apostles and the brothers of the Lord and Cephas?" (9:5).</p>
          <p>The second <hi type="italic">exousia</hi>: the right to be accompanied by <hi type="italic">adelphen gynaika</hi> ("a sister-wife," i.e., a wife who is a fellow believer). The verb <hi type="italic">periagein</hi> ("to lead around, to take along, to bring with") indicates that the apostle's wife would travel with him, supported by the communities they serve. Paul names three categories of people who exercise this right: <hi type="italic">hoi loipoi apostoloi</hi> ("the other apostles"), <hi type="italic">hoi adelphoi tou kyriou</hi> ("the brothers of the Lord"), and <hi type="italic">Kephas</hi> ("Cephas," the Aramaic name for Peter). The mention of these figures serves two purposes: it demonstrates that the right is widely recognized and exercised, and it establishes that Paul's choice not to exercise it is voluntary, not a reflection of lower status. The "brothers of the Lord" — James, Joses, Simon, and Judas (cf. Mark 6:3) — evidently held recognized positions in the early communities and exercised the right to bring their wives along on their missions.</p>
          <p>"Or is it only Barnabas and I who have no right to refrain from working for a living?" (9:6).</p>
          <p>The third <hi type="italic">exousia</hi>: the right to refrain from manual labor (<hi type="italic">me ergazesthai</hi>, "not to work"). The question is framed negatively: "do we alone lack this right?" The answer is obviously no — Paul and Barnabas possess the same right as the other apostles. The mention of Barnabas connects Paul to his earlier mission partner (Acts 13-15) and confirms that both of them pursued the same practice of self-support. Paul and Barnabas chose to work with their hands rather than accept community support. Paul worked as a tentmaker (<hi type="italic">skenopoios</hi>, Acts 18:3) during his time in Corinth, laboring alongside Aquila and Priscilla. The right to stop working and be supported by the community is acknowledged; the choice to continue working is deliberate.</p>
          <p>The three rights — food and drink, marital companionship on mission, freedom from manual labor — constitute the standard package of apostolic entitlements. Paul will now spend verses 7-14 proving that these rights are legitimate, establishing beyond any doubt that he possesses them, before revealing in verse 15 that he has declined to use any of them.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.9.7-1Cor.9.14">
          <title>Verses 7-14: Four Arguments for the Legitimacy of Apostolic Support</title>
          <p>Paul does not ask the Corinthians simply to take his word that these rights exist. He assembles four independent lines of argument — from common experience, from Torah, from the principle of reciprocity and temple service, and from the Lord's own command — each one sufficient on its own, and together overwhelming.</p>
          <title level="2">Common Sense Analogies (v. 7)</title>
          <p>"Who serves as a soldier at his own expense? Who plants a vineyard and does not eat its fruit? Or who tends a flock and does not drink the milk of the flock?" (9:7).</p>
          <p>Three analogies drawn from everyday life, each making the same point: the worker benefits from his own labor. The soldier (<hi type="italic">stratiotes</hi>, from <hi type="italic">strateuomai</hi>, "to serve as a soldier, to wage war") is provisioned by whoever commissioned him — no army expects its soldiers to fund their own campaigns. The one who plants (<hi type="italic">phyteuon</hi>) a vineyard (<hi type="italic">ampelona</hi>) eats its fruit (<hi type="italic">karpon</hi>). The one who shepherds (<hi type="italic">poimainon</hi>) a flock (<hi type="italic">poimnen</hi>) drinks its milk (<hi type="italic">gala</hi>). The imagery draws from Israel's scriptures and the wider ancient world simultaneously. Shepherding and vine-tending are both deeply embedded in Israel's prophetic and wisdom traditions as images of leadership and care (Ezekiel 34; Psalm 23; Isaiah 5:1-7). The argument is <hi type="italic">a fortiori</hi>: if even secular workers benefit from their labor, how much more should those who labor in the service of God?</p>
          <title level="2">The Torah Argument: The Ox and the Threshing Floor (vv. 8-10)</title>
          <p>"Do I say these things on human authority? Does not the Law say the same?" (9:8).</p>
          <p>Paul escalates from common sense (<hi type="italic">kata anthropon</hi>, "according to human reasoning") to divine authority. The question anticipates the objection that his analogies are merely prudential, not authoritative. The Law (<hi type="italic">ho nomos</hi>) says the same thing — this is not Paul's opinion but Torah's command.</p>
          <p>"For it is written in the Law of Moses, 'You shall not muzzle an ox when it treads out the grain.' Is it for oxen that God is concerned?" (9:9).</p>
          <p>The citation is Deuteronomy 25:4 (LXX): <hi type="italic">ou phimoseis boun aloonta</hi> ("you shall not muzzle an ox while it is threshing"). The verb <hi type="italic">phimoo</hi> ("to muzzle, to silence, to stop the mouth") describes the act of preventing the animal from eating while it works. The ox (<hi type="italic">bous</hi>) treads the grain (<hi type="italic">aloon</hi>, from <hi type="italic">aloao</hi>, "to thresh") on the threshing floor, separating the kernels from the chaff by walking over the harvested sheaves. The command forbids preventing the working animal from eating some of the grain it is processing. Even the animal deserves to benefit from its own labor.</p>
          <p>Paul's question — <hi type="italic">me ton boon melei to theo</hi> ("is it for the oxen that God is concerned?") — is not a denial that God cares about animals. The Deuteronomic context itself indicates divine concern for living creatures. Paul's point is interpretive: the Torah's principle extends beyond the literal case. If God commands that even an ox should benefit from the labor it performs, how much more does the principle apply to human laborers — specifically, to those who labor in God's service?</p>
          <p>"Or does he speak entirely for our sake? It was written for our sake, because the plowman should plow in hope and the thresher thresh in hope of sharing in the crop" (9:10).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">di' hemas pantos legei</hi> ("does he speak entirely for our sake?") employs a principle of interpretation attested in first-century Jewish exegesis: Torah's provisions for specific cases establish principles that apply to broader situations. The word <hi type="italic">pantos</hi> ("entirely, certainly, by all means") intensifies the claim. The principle "was written for our sake" (<hi type="italic">di' hemas egrathe</hi>) — Paul reads the ox command as establishing a universal principle: the laborer should benefit from the labor. The plowman (<hi type="italic">ho arotrioon</hi>) plows <hi type="italic">ep' elpidi</hi> ("in hope") — the expectation of eventual harvest motivates the work. The thresher (<hi type="italic">ho alooon</hi>) threshes in hope of receiving a share (<hi type="italic">metechein</hi>, "to share, to partake, to participate"). The connection between labor and benefit is not merely practical but moral: it is right that the one who works should receive a share of the product.</p>
          <title level="2">The Principle of Reciprocity and Temple Service (vv. 11-13)</title>
          <p>"If we sowed spiritual things among you, is it too much if we reap material things from you?" (9:11).</p>
          <p>The agricultural metaphor continues. Paul sowed (<hi type="italic">espeiramen</hi>, aorist of <hi type="italic">speiro</hi>, "to sow") <hi type="italic">pneumatika</hi> ("spiritual things") — the gospel, the teaching, the formation of the community. The question: is it "too much" (<hi type="italic">mega</hi>, "a great thing, an excessive thing") if he reaps <hi type="italic">sarkika</hi> ("fleshly things, material things") — provisions for bodily sustenance? The disproportion actually favors Paul. Spiritual things are incomparably more valuable than material things. If he gave the greater, receiving the lesser in return is hardly excessive.</p>
          <p>"If others share this rightful claim over you, do not we even more?" (9:12a).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> appears again. Others (<hi type="italic">alloi</hi>) — apparently other teachers or leaders who have visited Corinth — have exercised this right and received material support. If they can claim it, Paul's claim is stronger: he is the one who founded the community. The phrase <hi type="italic">mallon hemeis</hi> ("even more we") asserts priority. The one who planted the vineyard has a greater claim to its fruit than those who arrived afterward.</p>
          <p>"Nevertheless, we have not made use of this right, but we endure anything rather than put an obstacle in the way of the gospel of Christ" (9:12b).</p>
          <p>The decisive disclosure, stated here and then restated emphatically in verse 15. The word <hi type="italic">estegomen</hi> is the present active indicative of <hi type="italic">stego</hi>, which means "to cover, to endure, to bear patiently, to contain, to withstand." The verb describes the act of enduring hardship without complaint — the bearing of weight. Paul endures "all things" (<hi type="italic">panta</hi>) rather than create an <hi type="italic">enkopen</hi> ("obstacle, hindrance, impediment") to the gospel of Christ. The word <hi type="italic">enkope</hi> comes from <hi type="italic">enkopto</hi> ("to cut into, to hinder, to impede") and describes something that blocks a road or cuts off progress. Paul refuses to let his exercise of apostolic rights create any barrier — however small — to the gospel's advance. The logic parallels chapter 8 exactly: there, the knowledgeable person's exercise of their right to eat idol-food creates a <hi type="italic">proskomma</hi> ("stumbling block," 8:9) for the weak; here, the apostle's exercise of his right to financial support could create an <hi type="italic">enkope</hi> ("obstacle") for the gospel. In both cases, the right is real; in both cases, the right is voluntarily forfeited for the sake of others.</p>
          <p>"Do you not know that those who are employed in the temple service get their food from the temple, and those who serve at the altar share in what is sacrificed on the altar?" (9:13).</p>
          <p>The formula <hi type="italic">ouk oidate</hi> ("do you not know?") appears again, as it did repeatedly in chapter 6. The temple argument draws on Israel's established practice. The Levitical priesthood was supported by the temple's provisions. Numbers 18:8-24 establishes that the Levites and priests receive portions of the offerings brought to the tabernacle/temple: "To the Levites I have given every tithe in Israel for an inheritance, in return for their service that they do, their service in the tent of meeting" (Numbers 18:21). Deuteronomy 18:1-5: "The Levitical priests, all the tribe of Levi, shall have no portion or inheritance with Israel. They shall eat the LORD's food offerings as their inheritance." The priests eat from the altar — they receive portions of the sacrificial animals, the grain offerings, the firstfruits. This was not charity but a divinely established right: those who perform sacred service are sustained by the sacred offerings.</p>
          <p>The analogy to apostolic ministry is direct. As temple workers serve at the altar and receive their sustenance from the altar's offerings, so those who serve in the gospel should receive their sustenance from those to whom the gospel is proclaimed. The sacred worker lives from the sacred work.</p>
          <title level="2">The Lord's Own Command (v. 14)</title>
          <p>"In the same way, the Lord commanded that those who proclaim the gospel should get their living by the gospel" (9:14).</p>
          <p>The final and highest authority: <hi type="italic">ho kyrios dietaxen</hi> ("the Lord commanded"). The verb <hi type="italic">diatasso</hi> ("to order, to arrange, to command, to prescribe") describes a formal directive. This is not advice but a command, and it comes from the Lord himself. The content of the command — "those who proclaim the gospel should get their living by the gospel" (<hi type="italic">tous to euangelion katangellontas ek tou euangeliou zen</hi>) — echoes a saying of Jesus preserved in the tradition: "the laborer deserves his wages" (Luke 10:7; cf. Matthew 10:10, "the laborer deserves his food"). When Jesus sent out the Twelve and later the Seventy, he instructed them to eat and drink what was provided by their hosts, because the worker is worthy of payment. Paul cites this as a dominical command — a directive from Jesus himself that established the principle of gospel workers being supported by those they serve.</p>
          <p>The progression of the four arguments is deliberate. Paul moves from common human experience (v. 7) to Torah (vv. 8-10) to the principle of reciprocity and temple practice (vv. 11-13) to the Lord's own command (v. 14). Each argument is stronger than the last. By verse 14, the case is beyond dispute: Paul possesses the right to material support from the Corinthians. Common sense, Torah, temple precedent, and the Lord's command all converge on the same conclusion. This overbuilding of the case is essential to the rhetorical strategy. Paul must prove beyond all doubt that he possesses the right before he can demonstrate the significance of his choice not to exercise it.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.9.15-1Cor.9.18">
          <title>Verses 15-18: The Voluntary Renunciation and Its Motive</title>
          <p>"But I have made no use of any of these rights, nor am I writing these things to secure any such provision. For I would rather die than have anyone deprive me of my ground for boasting" (9:15).</p>
          <p>The adversative <hi type="italic">ego de</hi> ("but I") marks the decisive turn of the chapter. Everything from verses 1-14 established the right; verse 15 declares the refusal. <hi type="italic">Ou kechremai oudeni touton</hi> ("I have not used any of these things") — the perfect tense (<hi type="italic">kechremai</hi>, from <hi type="italic">chraomai</hi>, "to use, to make use of, to employ") indicates a settled, ongoing state: Paul has not used these rights, and he continues not to use them. The word <hi type="italic">oudeni</hi> ("none, not one") is comprehensive. Not one of these rights — food, drink, marital companionship on mission, freedom from labor — has Paul exercised. The catalogue of verses 3-6 now reads as a list of sacrifices.</p>
          <p>Paul immediately clarifies his motive for writing: <hi type="italic">ouk egrapsa de tauta hina houtos genetai en emoi</hi> ("I am not writing these things so that it should be done thus for me"). He is not angling for support. He is not dropping hints. The purpose of the argument is not to extract payment but to demonstrate the principle of voluntary self-limitation.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">mallon gar apothanein e — to kauchema mou oudeis kenosei</hi> ("for I would rather die than — no one shall make void my ground for boasting") — is striking in its emotional intensity. The sentence breaks off mid-construction (an anacoluthon), as if Paul's feelings outrun his grammar. He begins "I would rather die than" — and then the expected completion ("than accept support") is replaced by an emphatic declaration: "no one shall make void my boasting." The verb <hi type="italic">kenoo</hi> ("to empty, to make void, to hollow out, to nullify") is the same word Paul used in 1:17: "lest the cross of Christ be emptied of its power" (<hi type="italic">hina me kenothe ho stauros tou Christou</hi>). Paul's <hi type="italic">kauchema</hi> ("boasting, ground for boasting, the thing boasted in") is his preaching of the gospel free of charge. To accept payment would empty this boasting of its content, just as human wisdom would empty the cross of its power. The parallel is not accidental. Paul's free preaching is an expression of the cross's logic: power through self-emptying, gain through surrender.</p>
          <p>"For if I preach the gospel, that gives me no ground for boasting. For necessity is laid upon me. Woe to me if I do not preach the gospel!" (9:16).</p>
          <p>Paul distinguishes between preaching and preaching freely. The act of preaching itself is not his <hi type="italic">kauchema</hi>, because preaching is not voluntary for him — it is compulsory. The word <hi type="italic">anagke</hi> ("necessity, compulsion, constraint, pressure") describes an obligation that cannot be refused. Paul did not choose to preach; he was commissioned. The phrase <hi type="italic">anagke gar moi epikeitai</hi> ("for necessity is laid upon me") uses <hi type="italic">epikeimai</hi> ("to press upon, to be placed upon, to lie upon"), which describes a weight resting on someone. The compulsion sits on Paul like a burden he cannot shake off. The prophetic precedent is clear: Jeremiah 20:9, "If I say, 'I will not mention him, or speak any more in his name,' there is in my heart as it were a burning fire shut up in my bones, and I am weary with holding it in, and I cannot." Amos 3:8, "The lion has roared; who will not fear? The Lord GOD has spoken; who can not prophesy?" The prophet does not choose to speak; he is compelled to speak by the one who sent him. Paul stands in this tradition.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ouai gar moi estin ean me euangelizmai</hi> ("woe to me if I do not preach the gospel") uses the prophetic formula of woe (<hi type="italic">ouai</hi>), which in the LXX introduces divine judgment and lament (Isaiah 5:8, 11, 18, 20, 21, 22; Amos 5:18; 6:1; Habakkuk 2:6, 9, 12, 15, 19). Paul is not speaking hyperbolically. If he refuses his commission, he is subject to the same kind of judgment that fell on Jonah, who fled from the word of the Lord and was swallowed by the sea creature (Jonah 1:1-17). The preaching is not Paul's choice; it is his assignment.</p>
          <p>"For if I do this of my own will, I have a reward, but if not of my own will, I am still entrusted with a stewardship" (9:17).</p>
          <p>The verse presents two scenarios. If Paul preaches voluntarily (<hi type="italic">hekon</hi>, "willingly, of one's own accord"), he has a <hi type="italic">misthos</hi> ("reward, wages, recompense"). If he preaches involuntarily (<hi type="italic">akon</hi>, "unwillingly, against one's will"), he has been entrusted with an <hi type="italic">oikonomia</hi> ("stewardship, administration, management of a household"). The word <hi type="italic">oikonomia</hi> comes from <hi type="italic">oikos</hi> ("house") and <hi type="italic">nomos</hi> ("law, management") — it is the management of a household's affairs by a steward who acts on the owner's behalf. The steward in 4:1-2 was required to be <hi type="italic">pistos</hi> ("faithful, trustworthy"): "it is required of stewards that they be found faithful." Paul has been entrusted with the management of the gospel — not as owner but as steward. Whether he preaches willingly or under compulsion, the stewardship remains his obligation. He cannot refuse the commission any more than a household steward can refuse to manage the estate.</p>
          <p>"What then is my reward? That in my preaching I may present the gospel free of charge, so as not to make full use of my right in the gospel" (9:18).</p>
          <p>Paul's <hi type="italic">misthos</hi> — his reward — is the act of giving the gospel away for nothing. The phrase <hi type="italic">adapanon theso to euangelion</hi> ("I may present the gospel free of charge") uses <hi type="italic">adapanos</hi> ("without cost, without expense"), an adjective meaning "costing nothing." Paul's reward is not receiving a reward. The paradox is deliberate: the compensation for preaching is the renunciation of compensation. The phrase <hi type="italic">eis to me katachresasthai te exousia mou en to euangelio</hi> ("so as not to make full use of my right in the gospel") uses <hi type="italic">katachraomai</hi> ("to use fully, to use up, to make complete use of"), an intensified form of <hi type="italic">chraomai</hi> ("to use"). Paul possesses the <hi type="italic">exousia</hi>; he refuses to exhaust it. The word <hi type="italic">katachraomai</hi> implies that using the right fully would somehow consume or spoil it. Restraint preserves what full use would destroy.</p>
          <p>The logic of verses 15-18 is crucial to the chapter's function within the larger argument. Paul's "boasting" is that he preaches free of charge. This is not mere financial strategy. It is the concrete expression of the principle he taught in chapter 8: love constrains the exercise of rights. Paul's free preaching removes any possible barrier — any <hi type="italic">enkope</hi> (9:12) — between the gospel and its hearers. No one can accuse him of preaching for profit. No one can suspect that the message is for sale. No one's reception of the gospel is complicated by the financial transaction that would accompany it. The gospel arrives as a gift, unentangled from commerce, because Paul absorbs the cost himself.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.9.19-1Cor.9.23">
          <title>Verses 19-23: Voluntary Self-Adaptation</title>
          <p>"For though I am free from all, I have made myself a servant to all, that I might win more of them" (9:19).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">eleutheros</hi> ("free") from verse 1 returns. Paul restates his freedom — he is free <hi type="italic">ek panton</hi> ("from all people") — and then describes what he has done with that freedom: <hi type="italic">emauton edoulosa</hi> ("I enslaved myself"). The verb <hi type="italic">douloo</hi> ("to make a slave, to enslave, to bring into bondage") is in the aorist active, indicating a decisive act. Paul did not drift into service; he chose it. The reflexive <hi type="italic">emauton</hi> ("myself") underscores the voluntary nature: no one enslaved Paul; Paul enslaved Paul. The purpose: <hi type="italic">hina tous pleionas kerdeso</hi> ("that I might win the greater number"). The verb <hi type="italic">kerdaino</hi> ("to gain, to profit, to win") describes the acquisition of something valuable. In this context, it means bringing people into the community of faith. The word will be repeated four more times in verses 20-22 (<hi type="italic">kerdeso, kerdeso, kerdeso, soso</hi>), creating a relentless drumbeat of purpose: every act of self-adaptation serves the goal of gaining people for the gospel.</p>
          <p>The paradox is sharp. Freedom expresses itself as slavery. The free person voluntarily enters servitude. This echoes the pattern Paul has identified in the cross itself: God's power is displayed through weakness (1:25), God's wisdom is displayed through folly (1:21), the apostles are the "refuse of the world" (4:13). The pattern of self-emptying that characterizes God's action in Christ characterizes Paul's apostolic practice. Freedom that clings to its rights is freedom that fails to accomplish its purpose. Freedom that surrenders its rights for the sake of others is freedom in its fullest expression.</p>
          <p>"To the Jews I became as a Jew, in order to win Jews. To those under the law I became as one under the law — though not being myself under the law — that I might win those under the law" (9:20).</p>
          <p>Paul describes his practice of cultural and situational adaptation. <hi type="italic">Tois Ioudaiois egenomen hos Ioudaios</hi> ("to the Jews I became as a Jew"). The word <hi type="italic">hos</hi> ("as, like") is important: Paul does not say he became a Jew (he already was one) but that he became as a Jew — adopting Jewish customs and practices when among Jewish audiences in order to remove unnecessary barriers to the gospel. This would include observing food laws, participating in synagogue worship, and following ritual practices that were not, in his understanding, binding on those in Christ but that would prevent offense and open doors for communication.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">tois hypo nomon hos hypo nomon</hi> ("to those under law as one under law") describes the same category from a different angle — those whose identity is defined by Torah observance. The parenthetical <hi type="italic">me on autos hypo nomon</hi> ("though not being myself under the law") clarifies Paul's own theological position: he does not consider himself bound by Torah as a legal system. But he voluntarily submits to Torah observance when among those who are, in order to gain access to them.</p>
          <p>"To those outside the law I became as one outside the law — not being outside the law of God but under the law of Christ — that I might win those outside the law" (9:21).</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Tois anomois hos anomos</hi> ("to those without law as one without law"). The word <hi type="italic">anomos</hi> ("lawless, without Torah, outside the legal framework") describes Gentiles who do not live under the Mosaic covenant. Among Gentiles, Paul did not impose Jewish customs or require Torah observance. He ate with them, socialized with them, met them in their own cultural space. The parenthetical <hi type="italic">me on anomos theou all' ennomos Christou</hi> ("not being without God's law but under Christ's law, in-lawed to Christ") provides the theological guardrail: Paul's freedom from Torah does not mean freedom from all moral authority. He is <hi type="italic">ennomos Christou</hi> — "in the law of Christ," "under the jurisdiction of Christ," "subject to Christ's own rule." The word <hi type="italic">ennomos</hi> (<hi type="italic">en</hi> + <hi type="italic">nomos</hi>, "within law") is the precise opposite of <hi type="italic">anomos</hi> (<hi type="italic">a</hi> + <hi type="italic">nomos</hi>, "without law"). Paul is never lawless in the absolute sense; he has simply changed jurisdictions. His governing authority is not Moses but Christ. The "law of Christ" is not a new legal code but the pattern of life defined by Christ's own self-giving, the pattern Paul has been describing throughout this chapter: voluntary self-limitation for the sake of others.</p>
          <p>"To the weak I became weak, that I might win the weak" (9:22a).</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Tois asthenesin egenomen asthenes</hi> ("to the weak I became weak"). The word <hi type="italic">asthenes</hi> ("weak") is the same word used in 8:7, 9, 10, 11, 12 for the community members whose consciences are vulnerable regarding idol-food. Paul's willingness to become "weak" — to adopt the scruples and restrictions of the weak rather than flaunting his theological freedom — is the direct application of the principle of chapter 8. He does not eat idol-food when among those for whom it is a stumbling block. He does not exercise his freedom in their presence. He becomes what they are so that he can serve them where they are.</p>
          <p>"I have become all things to all people, that by all means I might save some" (9:22b).</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Tois pasin gegona panta</hi> ("to all I have become all things"). The verb <hi type="italic">gegona</hi> (perfect of <hi type="italic">ginomai</hi>, "to become") indicates a settled pattern, not a one-time act: Paul has become and remains one who adapts. The purpose clause — <hi type="italic">hina pantos tinas soso</hi> ("that by all means I might save some") — introduces the verb <hi type="italic">sozo</hi> ("to save, to rescue, to deliver, to preserve"). The shift from <hi type="italic">kerdaino</hi> ("to gain," vv. 19-22a) to <hi type="italic">sozo</hi> ("to save," v. 22b) raises the stakes. Gaining people is saving people. The adaptation is not marketing strategy; it is rescue work. The word <hi type="italic">tinas</hi> ("some") is realistic — not all will be saved, but Paul will leave no barrier unremoved that might prevent those who could be reached.</p>
          <p>"I do it all for the sake of the gospel, that I may share with them in its blessings" (9:23).</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Panta de poio dia to euangelion</hi> ("I do all things on account of the gospel"). The gospel is not merely the message Paul delivers; it is the reality he participates in. The phrase <hi type="italic">hina synkoinonos autou genomai</hi> ("that I might become a fellow-sharer in it") uses <hi type="italic">synkoinonos</hi> ("co-sharer, fellow-participant, co-partner"), compounded from <hi type="italic">syn</hi> ("together with") and <hi type="italic">koinonos</hi> ("sharer, partner"). Paul does not merely announce the gospel; he shares in its blessings along with those who receive it. His adaptation, his self-limitation, his enslavement — all of this is not altruistic self-destruction but participation in the very reality he proclaims. The gospel that saves others also saves Paul, but only if Paul remains a participant in it, not merely a herald of it. This anticipates the urgency of verses 24-27: Paul's own participation in the gospel's blessings is not guaranteed by his role as its proclaimer. He must discipline himself to remain a sharer, not just a speaker.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.9.24-1Cor.9.27">
          <title>Verses 24-27: The Athletic Metaphor — Discipline and the Possibility of Disqualification</title>
          <p>"Do you not know that in a race all the runners run, but only one receives the prize? Run in such a way that you may win it" (9:24).</p>
          <p>Paul turns to the athletic contest (<hi type="italic">agon</hi>) for his closing illustration. The city of Corinth hosted the Isthmian Games, one of the four great Panhellenic athletic festivals, held every two years at the nearby sanctuary of Poseidon at Isthmia. The Corinthian audience would have been intimately familiar with competitive athletics: the races, the wrestling, the boxing, the training regimen, and the award ceremonies. The imagery lands with particular force in this city.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">hoi en stadio trechontes</hi> ("those who run in the stadium") places the image in the specific venue — the <hi type="italic">stadion</hi>, the running track where footraces were held. <hi type="italic">Pantes men trechousin, heis de lambanei to brabeion</hi> ("all indeed run, but one receives the prize"). The word <hi type="italic">brabeion</hi> ("prize, award") occurs only here and in Philippians 3:14 in the Pauline letters. In the athletic games, the <hi type="italic">brabeion</hi> was the wreath awarded to the victor. At the Isthmian Games, the wreath was made of pine or celery — a perishable crown, as Paul will note in verse 25. The exhortation <hi type="italic">houtos trechete hina katalabete</hi> ("so run that you may lay hold of it") uses the verb <hi type="italic">katalambano</hi> ("to seize, to lay hold of, to overtake, to grasp"), which carries the sense of aggressive pursuit — not passive hope but active straining toward the goal.</p>
          <p>The analogy is not an exact parallel in every detail. Paul is not saying that only one believer will be saved (the way only one runner wins). The point of comparison is the attitude: run with the intensity and commitment of someone who intends to win, not with the casualness of someone who is merely participating. The competition metaphor illuminates the required seriousness, not a limitation on the number who can succeed.</p>
          <p>"Every athlete exercises self-control in all things. They do it to receive a perishable wreath, but we an imperishable" (9:25).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">pas ho agonizomenos</hi> ("everyone who competes," from <hi type="italic">agonizomai</hi>, "to contend, to compete, to fight, to struggle") describes not just the race itself but the entire discipline of athletic training. The phrase <hi type="italic">panta engkrateuetai</hi> ("exercises self-control in all things") uses <hi type="italic">engkrateuomai</hi> ("to exercise self-control, to master oneself, to restrain"), from <hi type="italic">engkrateia</hi> ("self-control, mastery over desire"). The word appeared in 7:9 in the context of sexual self-control within marriage discussions. Here it applies to the total discipline of the athlete. The phrase <hi type="italic">panta</hi> ("in all things") is comprehensive: the athlete's entire life is organized around the competition. Diet, sleep, exercise, sexual abstinence (attested in ancient training regimens — Epictetus, <hi type="italic">Discourses</hi> 3.15.2-5, describes the athlete's regimen of strict diet, enforced training, and regulated conduct), daily routine — everything is subordinated to the goal of winning.</p>
          <p>The contrast: <hi type="italic">ekeinoi men oun phtharton stephanon</hi> ("they do it for a perishable wreath"). The word <hi type="italic">phthartos</hi> ("perishable, corruptible, subject to decay") describes something that rots. The pine wreath from the Isthmian Games would wither within days. The athlete endures years of rigorous self-denial for a crown that decays almost immediately. <hi type="italic">Hemeis de aphtharton</hi> ("but we an imperishable one"). The word <hi type="italic">aphthartos</hi> ("imperishable, incorruptible, undecaying") describes what does not rot, what endures. The same word will dominate chapter 15: "the dead will be raised imperishable" (15:52); "this perishable body must put on the imperishable" (15:53-54). The believers' prize does not decay.</p>
          <p>Paul's argument is again <hi type="italic">a fortiori</hi>. If athletes discipline everything for a wreath that rots in a week, how much more should those who pursue an imperishable prize discipline their lives? The disproportion between the discipline (total) and the reward (perishable) in the athlete's case makes the disproportion between the believer's discipline and the believer's reward (imperishable) even more striking. If they do that for a dead leaf, what should we be willing to do for an eternal prize?</p>
          <p>"So I do not run aimlessly; I do not box as one beating the air" (9:26).</p>
          <p>Two negatives describe Paul's own practice. <hi type="italic">Ouk adelos trecho</hi> ("I do not run without clear direction"). The word <hi type="italic">adelos</hi> ("without being seen, unclear, uncertain, aimless") is the adverbial form of <hi type="italic">adelos</hi> ("unseen, uncertain"). Paul's running has a clear destination — he is not jogging in circles. He knows where the finish line is and runs toward it. <hi type="italic">Ouk aera deron</hi> ("I do not box as one punching air," literally "not beating the air"). The verb <hi type="italic">dero</hi> ("to flay, to beat, to strike") is vivid. A boxer who swings at empty air wastes energy and accomplishes nothing. Paul's combat is directed, purposeful, effective. Every blow lands.</p>
          <p>The boxing image is particularly apt for the Isthmian Games, where boxing (<hi type="italic">pygme</hi>) was a featured event. The Corinthian audience would visualize the boxer in training, shadow-boxing with precision and intent, not flailing wildly. Paul's self-discipline is targeted and effective, not performative or unfocused.</p>
          <p>"But I discipline my body and keep it under control, lest after preaching to others I myself should be disqualified" (9:27).</p>
          <p>This is the climactic verse of the chapter and one of the most consequential statements in the entire letter.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">hypopiazo</hi> (literally "to strike under the eye, to give a black eye, to beat the face") is a boxing term that describes landing a blow to the opponent's face. The word comes from <hi type="italic">hypo</hi> ("under") and <hi type="italic">ops</hi> ("eye, face") — a punch below the eye, a bruising blow. Paul applies it to his own <hi type="italic">soma</hi> ("body"): he treats his body as the opponent in the ring, delivering controlled, bruising discipline. The word does not describe self-punishment for its own sake but the rigorous subjection of bodily appetites and impulses to the control of a directed will. The athlete beats his body into submission so that it serves his purpose rather than mastering him.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">doulagogeo</hi> ("to lead into slavery, to subjugate, to bring under control") intensifies the image. The word is compounded from <hi type="italic">doulos</hi> ("slave") and <hi type="italic">ago</hi> ("to lead, to bring, to conduct"). Paul leads his body into slavery — he makes it his slave rather than allowing it to become his master. This connects back to 6:12, where Paul declared "I will not be mastered (<hi type="italic">exousiasthesomai</hi>) by anything." The same principle governs both passages: the body is a servant, not a master. Freedom is not the absence of restraint but the ability to direct oneself toward one's proper end.</p>
          <p>The purpose clause is devastating: <hi type="italic">hina me pos allois keryxas autos adokimos genomai</hi> ("lest after preaching to others I myself should be disqualified"). Several elements require close attention.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">keryxas</hi> ("having preached, having served as a herald") comes from <hi type="italic">kerysso</hi> ("to herald, to proclaim, to announce publicly"). The <hi type="italic">keryx</hi> ("herald") in the Greek athletic contests was the official who announced the rules, called the competitors, and proclaimed the victor's name. Paul uses the same word for his gospel preaching. He is the herald who announces the contest and calls others to compete. But the herald is also a competitor. Paul is not exempt from the race he announces.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">allois</hi> ("to others") distinguishes Paul's audience from Paul himself. He has preached to others — called them to the race, announced the rules, urged them to compete. His preaching has been effective; others have responded. But his success as a herald does not secure his own place.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> is the chapter's final and most consequential term. The word is compounded from <hi type="italic">a</hi> (alpha privative, "not") and <hi type="italic">dokimos</hi> ("tested and approved, genuine, passing inspection"). <hi type="italic">Dokimos</hi> describes something that has been tested and found genuine — a coin that has been assayed and proved to contain the proper metal, a person who has been examined and approved. <hi type="italic">Adokimos</hi> is the opposite: tested and found wanting, rejected after examination, failing the test, disqualified. In the context of the athletic games, the <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> is the competitor who is disqualified — either because he broke the rules, because he failed to complete the proper training, or because he did not meet the standards of competition. The victor's wreath goes to the one who is <hi type="italic">dokimos</hi>; the <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> receives nothing.</p>
          <p>Paul is saying that he himself — the apostle who founded the Corinthian community, who has seen the risen Lord, who preaches the gospel under divine compulsion — could be <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi>. He could be disqualified. He could preach the gospel to others and then fail the test himself. The word does not describe a theoretical impossibility that Paul raises merely for rhetorical effect. The entire athletic metaphor depends on the reality of the danger: the runner who does not run with discipline risks losing the race; the boxer who does not land real blows risks losing the fight; the herald who does not compete risks being found <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi>.</p>
          <p>The connection to chapter 10 is immediate. In 10:1-5, Paul will describe Israel in the wilderness: a people who all participated in God's saving acts (all under the cloud, all through the sea, all eating the same spiritual food, all drinking from the same spiritual rock) and yet "with most of them God was not pleased, and they were struck down in the wilderness" (10:5). Israel was, collectively, <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> — tested and found wanting, disqualified despite their participation in the divine provision. The warning of 10:12 follows directly: "let anyone who thinks that he stands take heed lest he fall." Paul's concern about his own potential disqualification in 9:27 is not hypothetical but is grounded in the historical reality of a people who received everything and lost everything. If Israel could be disqualified after the Exodus, the Sea, the manna, and the water from the rock, then Paul can be disqualified after his apostolic commission, his encounter with the risen Lord, and his successful preaching.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> will reappear later in Paul's correspondence with Corinth. In 2 Corinthians 13:5-7, Paul writes: "Examine yourselves, to see whether you are in the faith. Test yourselves. Or do you not realize this about yourselves, that Jesus Christ is in you? — unless indeed you fail to meet the test (<hi type="italic">adokimoi</hi>)! I hope you will find out that we have not failed to meet the test (<hi type="italic">adokimoi</hi>)... But we pray to God that you may not do wrong... even though we may seem to have failed to meet the test (<hi type="italic">adokimoi</hi>)." The vocabulary of testing, examination, and the possibility of being found <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> runs as a thread through Paul's dealings with this community. The test is real. The possibility of failure is genuine. Paul does not exempt himself from it.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.9">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 9 is not a digression from the idol-food argument of chapters 8-10. It is the argumentative center of that unit — the personal demonstration that transforms an abstract principle into a lived reality. Paul does not merely tell the Corinthians what to do with their right to eat idol-food; he shows them what he has already done with his own far greater rights.</p>
          <p>The chapter's architecture follows a precise logic:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">First (vv. 1-2):</hi> Paul establishes that he possesses freedom and apostolic authority. His credentials are beyond dispute.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Second (vv. 3-14):</hi> Paul proves, by four independent arguments, that he possesses specific, legitimate rights: to eat and drink at the community's expense, to bring a wife on his travels, and to refrain from manual labor. Common sense, Torah, temple precedent, and the Lord's own command all confirm these rights. The proof is deliberately overwhelming — Paul piles argument upon argument so that no one can claim his self-support reflects a lack of rights rather than a choice to forgo them.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Third (vv. 15-18):</hi> Paul reveals that he has used none of these rights. His refusal is not reluctant but passionate — he would rather die than accept support, because free preaching is his boasting, the one reward he has chosen for himself. His compulsion to preach is divine; his freedom in how he preaches is his own.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Fourth (vv. 19-23):</hi> Paul generalizes the principle. His self-limitation extends beyond finances to his entire mode of life. He becomes whatever each audience needs him to be — Jew to Jews, lawless to lawless, weak to weak — all for the sake of winning people for the gospel. Every adaptation is a surrender of personal preference for the sake of others.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Fifth (vv. 24-27):</hi> Paul grounds the urgency of self-discipline in the reality that the prize can be lost. The athlete who does not train is disqualified. Paul himself could be disqualified. The herald who announces the race can fail to finish it. This closing section transforms the chapter from a personal illustration into a warning: if Paul, with all his credentials and all his labor, must discipline himself to avoid disqualification, how much more must the Corinthians exercise care in how they use their freedom?</p>
          <p>Several threads connect this chapter to the letter's wider argument:</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> ties chapter 9 to chapter 8. In 8:9, Paul warned that the Corinthians' <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> might become a stumbling block. In chapter 9, he demonstrates what proper handling of <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> looks like: acknowledge the right, prove the right, and then voluntarily decline to exercise it when doing so would hinder the gospel or harm others. The Corinthians are asked to limit one right (eating idol-food) in one circumstance (when the weak are present). Paul has limited all his rights in every circumstance.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> ties chapter 9 to chapter 10. Paul's concern about being disqualified despite being a preacher (9:27) anticipates Israel's disqualification despite participating in God's saving acts (10:1-5). Both cases establish the same principle: participation in divine provision does not guarantee final approval. The test must be passed, not merely entered.</p>
          <p>The pattern of self-enslavement (<hi type="italic">edoulosa</hi>, 9:19) connects to the cross-shaped pattern of the letter from its beginning. In 1:18-2:5, God's power was revealed through the weakness of the cross. In 4:9-13, the apostles were exhibited as last of all, like men sentenced to death. In 9:19-27, the free apostle voluntarily enslaves himself and beats his body into submission. The downward movement — from freedom to slavery, from privilege to self-denial, from authority to self-discipline — is the same movement the gospel narrates about Christ and that Paul embodies in his apostolic practice.</p>
          <p>Paul's concern about his own possible disqualification (9:27) also connects to 3:13-15, where Paul described the builder whose work would be tested by fire: "If the work that anyone has built on the foundation survives, he will receive a reward. If anyone's work is burned up, he will suffer loss, though he himself will be saved, but only as through fire." The vocabulary of testing, reward, and potential loss runs through the letter. Paul's own work — his apostolic labor among the Corinthians — will be tested. His own standing will be examined. The test is the same for everyone, apostle and community member alike: not what you know, not what you profess, not what spiritual powers you display, but whether you have disciplined yourself to run the race with integrity.</p>
          <p>The chapter thus functions at three levels simultaneously. At the rhetorical level, it provides a personal example that makes the abstract principle of chapter 8 concrete and compelling. At the argumentative level, it bridges the general principle of rights-limitation (ch. 8) to the historical warning against overconfidence (ch. 10). At the theological level, it establishes that no one — not even an apostle who has seen the risen Lord and founded churches — is exempt from the discipline required to finish the race. The gospel is not merely a message Paul delivers to others; it is a reality in which he must continue to participate, at the cost of relentless self-discipline, or risk being found <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> when the test comes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.10">
        <title>1 Corinthians 10 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.10">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 9):</hi> Chapter 9 is Paul's personal demonstration of the principle he laid down in chapter 8 — that the knowledgeable person must voluntarily limit the exercise of freedom for the sake of the vulnerable. Paul opened by asserting his apostolic credentials and the rights (<hi type="italic">exousiai</hi>) those credentials entail: the right to eat and drink at the community's expense (9:4), to be accompanied by a believing wife (9:5), to refrain from manual labor (9:6). He argued for these rights from common sense (soldiers, vinedressers, shepherds — 9:7), the Torah (the unmuzzled ox of Deuteronomy 25:4 — 9:9), the temple (those who serve at the altar share its offerings — 9:13), and the Lord's own command (those who proclaim the gospel should get their living by the gospel — 9:14). Having established his rights beyond dispute, Paul announced that he has exercised none of them (9:15). He made himself a slave to all (9:19). He became as a Jew to Jews, as one under the law to those under the law, as weak to the weak (9:20-22). "I do it all for the sake of the gospel, that I may share with them in its blessings" (9:23). The chapter then moved to the athletic metaphor: the runner competing for a perishable wreath, the boxer who does not beat the air (9:24-26). Paul disciplines his body and keeps it under control, "so that after preaching to others I myself should not be disqualified" (<hi type="italic">adokimos</hi>, 9:27). The word <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> ("disqualified, rejected, failing the test") is the chapter's final word, and it introduces the possibility that even Paul — the apostle, the one who has seen the Lord, the one who founded the Corinthian community — could be rejected if he does not exercise self-discipline. The chapter demonstrated that the principle of self-limited freedom is not merely advice Paul gives to others; it is the governing pattern of his own life, pursued under the real threat of disqualification.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 11:1-2 and following):</hi> The first verse of chapter 11 — "Be imitators of me, as I am of Christ" — belongs structurally to the argument of chapters 8-10, not to what follows. It is the capstone of the entire three-chapter unit: Paul has stated the principle (chapter 8), demonstrated it from his own life (chapter 9), grounded it in Israel's history and the theology of participation (chapter 10), and now calls the Corinthians to imitate his pattern of self-limitation as he imitates Christ's pattern of self-giving. The chain is: Christ gave himself for others (the brother "for whom Christ died," 8:11); Paul gives up his rights for others (chapter 9, and 10:33 — "not seeking my own advantage, but that of many, that they may be saved"); the Corinthians are to give up their freedoms for others ("let no one seek his own good, but the good of his neighbor," 10:24).</p>
          <p>Beginning at 11:2, Paul turns to a new set of problems related to the community's gathered worship. He commends the Corinthians for maintaining the traditions he delivered (11:2), then addresses head coverings during prayer and prophecy (11:3-16), the disorders at the communal meal (11:17-34), and the misuse of spiritual gifts (chapters 12-14). The shift at 11:2 is marked by the word <hi type="italic">epainō</hi> ("I commend") and by the introduction of entirely new subject matter: how the community conducts itself when it assembles. The idol-food argument that began at 8:1 has reached its conclusion at 11:1.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 10 connects these:</hi> Chapter 10 is not a new topic but the culmination of the argument that began in 8:1 with the question of food offered to idols. The chapter opens with the conjunction <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for"), which signals that what follows is the ground or reason for what precedes. The <hi type="italic">gar</hi> of 10:1 connects directly to 9:27: Paul has just said that even he could be "disqualified" (<hi type="italic">adokimos</hi>) if he fails to exercise self-discipline. Why is this a real danger? "For" (<hi type="italic">gar</hi>) — because Israel's example proves that divine privilege does not guarantee perseverance, and that those who enjoy every spiritual advantage can still be overthrown. The Israelites had everything: the cloud, the sea, the spiritual food, the spiritual drink, the Rock that was Christ. "Nevertheless, with most of them God was not pleased, for they were overthrown in the wilderness" (10:5). The <hi type="italic">gar</hi> of 10:1 makes Israel's catastrophe the warrant for Paul's own anxiety about disqualification and the basis for his warning to the Corinthians.</p>
          <p>The chapter then proceeds in three movements. First (10:1-13), Paul draws the Israel typology: the wilderness generation had all the privileges corresponding to the Corinthians' sacramental life, yet most were destroyed, and these events were written as warnings for the community on whom the end of the ages has come. Second (10:14-22), Paul issues a severe theological prohibition against participation in idol worship, grounded not in the weak brother's conscience (the argument of chapter 8) but in the nature of participation itself — eating at the Lord's table and eating at the table of demons are mutually exclusive acts of communion. Third (10:23-33 plus 11:1), Paul gives practical guidelines for specific daily situations — the meat market, the unbeliever's dinner, the informed conscience — governed by the comprehensive principle of doing everything for God's glory and the neighbor's salvation.</p>
          <p>The three-chapter unit (8:1-11:1) thus moves from principle (8: knowledge without love destroys) to personal example (9: Paul's own self-limitation) to historical warning and theological ground (10: Israel's failure, the reality of demonic participation, practical guidelines), concluding with the call to imitation (11:1). Chapter 10 supplies what chapters 8 and 9 could not: the scriptural precedent that proves privileged people can fall, and the theological reason that idol-meals are not merely unloving but are incompatible with belonging to Christ.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.10.1-1Cor.10.5">
          <title>Verses 1-5: The Privileges and the Overthrow</title>
          <p>"For I do not want you to be unaware, brothers, that our fathers were all under the cloud, and all passed through the sea, and all were baptized into Moses in the cloud and in the sea, and all ate the same spiritual food, and all drank the same spiritual drink. For they drank from the spiritual Rock that followed them, and the Rock was Christ. Nevertheless, with most of them God was not pleased, for they were overthrown in the wilderness" (10:1-5).</p>
          <p>The opening word <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") is the structural hinge that connects this passage to everything that precedes it. Paul has just said that he disciplines himself lest he be disqualified (9:27). The <hi type="italic">gar</hi> introduces the reason this is not paranoia but sober realism: Israel's history proves it can happen. The phrase <hi type="italic">ou thelo hymas agnoein</hi> ("I do not want you to be unaware") is a disclosure formula Paul uses to introduce material of particular importance (cf. Romans 1:13, 11:25; 2 Corinthians 1:8; 1 Thessalonians 4:13). He is not presenting information the Corinthians may or may not find interesting; he is telling them something they must grasp. The address <hi type="italic">adelphoi</hi> ("brothers") marks the communal character of the audience — this warning is for the whole assembly.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">hoi pateres hēmōn</hi> ("our fathers") is striking. Paul is writing to a community that is substantially Gentile. Yet he calls the Israelites of the Exodus "our fathers." This is not ethnic genealogy but covenantal identification: the Corinthian community, composed of both Jews and Gentiles, is the continuation of Israel. The story of the Exodus is their story. What happened to the wilderness generation is directly relevant to them because they belong to the same ongoing history of God's dealings with his people. The pronoun <hi type="italic">hēmōn</hi> ("our") draws the Corinthians into Israel's narrative as participants, not spectators.</p>
          <p>Paul then enumerates five privileges of the wilderness generation, and the word <hi type="italic">pantes</hi> ("all") appears five times in four verses. The repetition is emphatic and deliberate:</p>
          <list type="x-ordered">
            <item>"All were under the cloud" (<hi type="italic">pantes hypo tēn nephelēn ēsan</hi>) — The cloud is the pillar of cloud and fire that guided Israel from Egypt through the wilderness (Exodus 13:21-22). In the LXX, the cloud is consistently associated with the glory and presence of God: "And the Lord went before them by day in a pillar of cloud to lead them along the way, and by night in a pillar of fire to give them light" (Exodus 13:21). The cloud covered the tabernacle (Exodus 40:34-38) and represented the divine presence dwelling among the people. To be "under the cloud" is to be under God's visible, protective, guiding presence. Every Israelite, without exception, was under this cloud.</item>
            <item>"All passed through the sea" (<hi type="italic">pantes dia tēs thalassēs diēlthon</hi>) — The crossing of the Red Sea (Exodus 14:21-29) was Israel's definitive act of deliverance. The waters parted; the people walked through on dry ground; the pursuing army was drowned. This was the event that constituted Israel as a people. In the Song of the Sea (Exodus 15), it became the paradigmatic act of divine salvation. Every Israelite, without exception, passed through.</item>
            <item>"All were baptized into Moses in the cloud and in the sea" (<hi type="italic">pantes eis ton Mōusēn ebaptisthēsan en tē nephelē kai en tē thalassē</hi>) — Paul interprets the cloud and the sea as a baptism. The verb <hi type="italic">ebaptisthēsan</hi> ("were baptized") is the same verb used for the community's baptism into Christ (cf. 12:13, "For in one Spirit we were all baptized into one body"; Romans 6:3, "Do you not know that all of us who have been baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into his death?"). The parallel is deliberate: as the Corinthians were baptized into Christ, so Israel was baptized into Moses. The phrase <hi type="italic">eis ton Mōusēn</hi> ("into Moses") mirrors the baptismal formula <hi type="italic">eis Christon</hi> ("into Christ"). Israel's passage through the sea under the cloud constituted them as a people bound to Moses as mediator, just as the Corinthians' baptism constituted them as a people bound to Christ. Every Israelite, without exception, was baptized.</item>
            <item>"All ate the same spiritual food" (<hi type="italic">pantes to auto pneumatikon brōma ephagon</hi>) — The spiritual food is the manna, described in Exodus 16:4-35. The LXX of Psalm 78:24-25 (LXX 77:24-25) calls it "the bread of heaven" and "the bread of angels": "He rained down on them manna to eat and gave them the grain of heaven. Man ate of the bread of the angels; he sent them food in abundance." The adjective <hi type="italic">pneumatikon</hi> ("spiritual") does not mean the food was immaterial — the manna was real bread that sustained physical life. Rather, <hi type="italic">pneumatikon</hi> indicates its origin: it was provided by the Spirit, by divine action, supernaturally. It came from God, not from human agriculture. The phrase <hi type="italic">to auto</hi> ("the same") emphasizes that every Israelite received the same provision. There was no hierarchy of nourishment. The manna fell equally on all the camp. Every Israelite, without exception, ate.</item>
            <item>"All drank the same spiritual drink" (<hi type="italic">pantes to auto pneumatikon epion poma</hi>) — The spiritual drink is the water from the rock. In Exodus 17:1-7, at Rephidim, Moses struck the rock at Horeb and water came out. In Numbers 20:2-13, at Kadesh, God commanded Moses to speak to the rock (though Moses struck it), and water flowed abundantly. These two events, separated by nearly forty years, bracket the wilderness period. The adjective <hi type="italic">pneumatikon</hi> again indicates divine origin. As with the food, the phrase <hi type="italic">to auto</hi> ("the same") stresses universal participation. Every Israelite, without exception, drank.</item>
          </list>
          <p>Paul then adds an interpretive comment: "For they drank from the spiritual Rock that followed them, and the Rock was Christ" (10:4b). The phrase <hi type="italic">petra ... akolouthousēs</hi> ("the Rock that followed") draws on an interpretive tradition reflected in Jewish sources where the rock that gave water was understood to have accompanied Israel through the wilderness. The water appeared at Horeb (Exodus 17) and again at Kadesh (Numbers 20), and the tradition developed that it was the same rock, traveling with the people. This tradition appears in embryonic form in Psalm 78:15-16 (LXX 77:15-16): "He split rocks in the wilderness and gave them drink abundantly as from the deep. He made streams come out of the rock and caused waters to flow down like rivers." The rock gives water not once but repeatedly; the language of abundance and flowing rivers suggests an ongoing provision.</p>
          <p>Paul's identification — <hi type="italic">hē petra de ēn ho Christos</hi> ("and the Rock was Christ") — is the most remarkable statement in the passage. Paul does not say the rock symbolized Christ or pointed to Christ; he says the Rock was Christ. Christ was present with Israel in the wilderness. Christ was the source of their spiritual drink. This identification is consistent with the way Paul has already described Christ: as the one "through whom are all things and through whom we exist" (8:6). The pre-existent Christ who mediated creation also accompanied Israel and sustained them. This identification will be reinforced in 10:9, where Paul will say that the Israelites "put Christ to the test" — another statement that places Christ as the divine presence in the wilderness events.</p>
          <p>The LXX background for "Rock" as a divine title is well established. In Deuteronomy 32:4, Moses' Song opens: "The Rock, his work is perfect" (<hi type="italic">ho theos</hi> — but the Hebrew has <hi type="italic">tsur</hi>, "Rock"). In Deuteronomy 32:15, Israel "scoffed at the Rock of his salvation" (<hi type="italic">ton theon ton poiēsanta auton</hi> / Hebrew <hi type="italic">tsur yeshu'ato</hi>). In Deuteronomy 32:18, "You were unmindful of the Rock that bore you" (<hi type="italic">petran</hi> — in some LXX traditions — / Hebrew <hi type="italic">tsur</hi>). In Deuteronomy 32:31, "their rock is not as our Rock." In 2 Samuel 22:2-3 (the Song of David), "The Lord is my rock and my fortress and my deliverer." In Psalm 18:2 (LXX 17:3), "The Lord is my rock." The title "Rock" for God runs throughout the scriptures, and Paul takes this title and applies it to Christ, identifying Christ as the divine Rock who sustained Israel in the wilderness.</p>
          <p>The five-fold <hi type="italic">pantes</hi> establishes beyond all doubt that the Israelites' participation in divine provision was total and universal. No one was excluded from the cloud, the sea, the baptism, the food, or the drink. The parallels to the Corinthians' own experience are unmistakable: the Corinthians have been baptized (12:13), they eat spiritual food and drink spiritual drink at the Lord's table (10:16-17, 11:23-26), they live under the presence of Christ. The Corinthians enjoy every privilege that corresponds to Israel's privileges.</p>
          <p>Then comes the devastating turn: <hi type="italic">all' ouk en tois pleiosin autōn eudokēsen ho theos</hi> — "Nevertheless, with most of them God was not pleased" (10:5).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">alla</hi> ("nevertheless, but, however") introduces the sharpest possible contrast. Five statements of universal privilege are followed by one statement of near-universal rejection. The word <hi type="italic">pleiosin</hi> (dative plural of <hi type="italic">pleiōn</hi>, "most, the majority, the greater number") is carefully chosen. Paul does not say "some of them" but "most of them." In the scriptural record, the actual numbers are worse than "most": of the entire generation that left Egypt, only two adults — Joshua and Caleb — entered the Promised Land (Numbers 14:29-30, 26:65). The rest of that generation died in the wilderness over forty years. Paul's "most" is, if anything, an understatement.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">eudokēsen</hi> (aorist of <hi type="italic">eudokeō</hi>, "to be well pleased, to take pleasure in, to be satisfied with") is negated: God was not pleased. The word <hi type="italic">eudokeō</hi> in the LXX describes God's favorable regard toward his people and their worship. In Psalm 44:3 (LXX 43:4), "For not by their own sword did they win the land, nor did their own arm save them... for you took pleasure [<hi type="italic">eudokēsas</hi>] in them." In Psalm 149:4, "For the Lord takes pleasure [<hi type="italic">eudokei</hi>] in his people." The negative use here — <hi type="italic">ouk eudokēsen</hi> — means that God's favorable regard was withdrawn. The people who had every privilege lost God's pleasure.</p>
          <p>The result: <hi type="italic">katestrōthēsan en tē erēmō</hi> — "they were overthrown in the wilderness." The verb <hi type="italic">katastrōnnymi</hi> (<hi type="italic">katestrōthēsan</hi> is the aorist passive) means "to spread out, to strew, to lay low, to scatter." The image is of bodies strewn across the desert floor. The wilderness became a graveyard. The word appears in the LXX of Numbers 14:16, where Moses argues with God: "Because the Lord was not able to bring this people into the land which he swore to give to them, he has slain them [<hi type="italic">katestōsen</hi> — related form] in the wilderness." The privileged people — baptized, fed, watered, guided, protected — were scattered as corpses in the desert.</p>
          <p>The force of the passage for the Corinthians is unmistakable. They possess the equivalents of everything Israel possessed: baptism into Christ (corresponding to baptism into Moses), spiritual food and drink at the Lord's table (corresponding to manna and water from the rock), the presence of Christ (corresponding to the cloud and the Rock). But Israel's privileges did not protect them from judgment when they sinned. The five-fold "all" followed by "most of them" demonstrates that universal participation in divine provision does not guarantee perseverance. Those who had everything were overthrown. The warning to the Corinthians is implicit but inescapable: your baptism, your participation in the Lord's table, your possession of spiritual gifts — none of these make you immune to the same judgment that fell on Israel.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.10.6-1Cor.10.10">
          <title>Verses 6-10: The Catalogue of Wilderness Sins</title>
          <p>"Now these things took place as examples for us, that we might not desire evil as they did" (10:6).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">tauta</hi> ("these things") refers to the events just narrated — the cloud, the sea, the food, the drink, the overthrow. All of it. Paul says these things <hi type="italic">egenēthēsan typoi hēmōn</hi> — "became types/examples of us." The word <hi type="italic">typos</hi> (plural <hi type="italic">typoi</hi>) means a pattern, a mold, a stamp, a mark left by a blow. A <hi type="italic">typos</hi> is not merely an illustration or a moral lesson; it is a pattern that the later reality fills. The events of the Exodus are <hi type="italic">typoi</hi> of the Corinthians' own situation — they are the pattern that the Corinthians' experience is filling. Israel's privileges are the type of the Corinthians' privileges; Israel's sins are the type of the sins the Corinthians are now committing; Israel's judgment is the type of the judgment the Corinthians face. The phrase <hi type="italic">typoi hēmōn</hi> ("types of us / examples for us") draws the Corinthians into the pattern: these things happened as our types, in order that we might learn.</p>
          <p>The purpose clause <hi type="italic">eis to mē einai hēmas epithymētas kakōn</hi> ("that we might not be desirers of evil things") states the goal of the typology: the community should learn from Israel's example not to repeat Israel's sins. The word <hi type="italic">epithymētēs</hi> ("one who desires, craver, lusting person") comes from <hi type="italic">epithymeō</hi> ("to desire, to long for, to set the heart upon"). In the LXX, the noun and verb cluster carry dangerous connotations. Numbers 11:4 (LXX): "the mixed multitude among them had a strong craving" (<hi type="italic">epethymēsan epithymian</hi>) — this is the incident at Kibroth-hattaavah, where the people craved meat, and God sent quail but also struck them with a plague (Numbers 11:33-34). The place was named Kibroth-hattaavah, "Graves of Craving" (<hi type="italic">mnēmata tēs epithymias</hi> in the LXX), because there they buried the people who craved. Paul's instruction not to be "desirers of evil" thus reaches back to a specific wilderness incident where desire itself was the sin, and the consequence was death.</p>
          <p>Paul then catalogues four specific sins from the wilderness period, each matched to a specific scriptural incident. The four sins are: idolatry, sexual immorality, testing Christ, and grumbling. Each one has direct relevance to the Corinthians' situation.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">First Sin — Idolatry (10:7):</hi></p>
          <p>"Do not be idolaters as some of them were; as it is written, 'The people sat down to eat and drink and rose up to play'" (10:7).</p>
          <p>The command <hi type="italic">mēde eidōlolatrai ginesthe</hi> ("do not become idolaters") uses the present imperative with the prohibitive particle <hi type="italic">mēde</hi>, indicating an ongoing condition to be avoided. The quotation is from Exodus 32:6 (LXX), the golden calf incident. The people had asked Aaron to make them gods (Exodus 32:1). Aaron fashioned a golden calf and declared, "Tomorrow shall be a feast to the Lord" (Exodus 32:5). The next day, "the people sat down to eat and drink and rose up to play" (<hi type="italic">ekathisen ho laos phagein kai pein kai anestēsan paizein</hi>).</p>
          <p>The connection to the Corinthians' situation is precise. The golden calf incident involved eating and drinking in an idolatrous context. Aaron had announced the event as a feast "to the Lord" (<hi type="italic">tō kyriō</hi>) — the people may have believed they were honoring YHWH through the calf image. But the act was idolatry regardless of their intention. The Corinthians who eat in idol temples (8:10) may similarly regard their behavior as theologically innocent — after all, they know the idol is nothing. But the scriptural precedent shows that eating and drinking in the context of idol worship constitutes idolatry, regardless of the eater's theological sophistication. The Israelites at Sinai did not think they were abandoning YHWH; Aaron explicitly invoked YHWH's name. Yet God's judgment was devastating: three thousand fell by the sword that day (Exodus 32:28), and a plague struck the people afterward (Exodus 32:35).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">paizein</hi> ("to play, to sport, to amuse oneself") in the Exodus context carries connotations of the revelry that accompanied pagan worship — feasting, dancing, and possibly sexual license. The LXX uses the same verb in Genesis 26:8, where Abimelech sees Isaac "playing" (<hi type="italic">paizonta</hi>) with Rebekah in a way that reveals she is his wife, not his sister — the word can carry sexual overtones. The golden calf celebration was not merely a meal but a ritual event that blended eating, drinking, and festive excess in the pattern of Canaanite worship.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Second Sin — Sexual Immorality (10:8):</hi></p>
          <p>"We must not indulge in sexual immorality as some of them did, and twenty-three thousand fell in a single day" (10:8).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">porneuōmen</hi> ("let us not commit sexual immorality") is the first person plural hortatory subjunctive — Paul includes himself in the prohibition. The reference is to the incident at Baal Peor (Numbers 25:1-9). Israel was camped at Shittim, and the people "began to whore with the daughters of Moab. These invited the people to the sacrifices of their gods, and the people ate and bowed down to their gods. So Israel yoked himself to Baal of Peor" (Numbers 25:1-3). The connection between sexual immorality and idol worship in this incident is not incidental — it is organic. The Moabite women invited the Israelites to their sacrificial meals (their <hi type="italic">eidōlothyta</hi>, to use the language of 1 Corinthians 8), and participation in the meals led to bowing to the gods and to sexual relations with the worshipers. Idolatry and <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> were two sides of the same act.</p>
          <p>This connection is directly relevant to the Corinthians. Paul has already dealt with <hi type="italic">porneia</hi> at length in chapters 5-6, and the idol-food question of chapters 8-10 is now shown to be related. The Corinthian who reclines in a temple dining room (8:10) is in the same position as the Israelite who accepted the Moabite women's invitation to their sacrificial meal. The path from casual participation in the social life of the temple to full participation in its worship and its sexual practices is short — Israel's history proves it.</p>
          <p>Paul's number — twenty-three thousand (<hi type="italic">eikosi treis chiliades</hi>) — differs from the number in Numbers 25:9, which records twenty-four thousand. Various explanations have been proposed: Paul may be combining sources, using a slightly different textual tradition, or distinguishing those who fell "in a single day" (<hi type="italic">en mia hēmera</hi>) from the total who died over the course of the entire incident. The phrase "in a single day" emphasizes the swiftness and severity of the judgment. Whatever the precise reckoning, the scale is staggering: thousands of Israelites — members of the covenant community, recipients of every divine privilege — died because they participated in the worship and sexual practices of Baal Peor.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Third Sin — Testing Christ (10:9):</hi></p>
          <p>"We must not put Christ to the test, as some of them did and were destroyed by serpents" (10:9).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">ekpeirazōmen</hi> ("let us not test, let us not tempt") is strengthened by the prefix <hi type="italic">ek-</hi>, intensifying the basic verb <hi type="italic">peirazō</hi>. Again Paul uses the first person plural, including himself. The reference is to Numbers 21:4-9, where the people became impatient and "spoke against God and against Moses, 'Why have you brought us up out of Egypt to die in the wilderness? For there is no food and no water, and we loathe this worthless food'" (Numbers 21:5). The Lord sent fiery serpents (<hi type="italic">opheis tous pyrinous</hi> in the LXX) among the people, and many died. The sin was testing God — questioning whether God was truly providing, demanding proof, expressing contempt for God's provision. At Massah and Meribah, the paradigmatic testing incident, the question was: "Is the Lord among us or not?" (Exodus 17:7). The test was a challenge to God's presence and faithfulness.</p>
          <p>Paul says they put <hi type="italic">Christon</hi> to the test — "Christ." This identification is consistent with verse 4, where Paul said "the Rock was Christ." Christ was the divine presence accompanying Israel. When Israel challenged God in the wilderness, they were challenging Christ. The continuity between Israel's experience and the Corinthians' situation is maintained: the same Christ who sustained Israel with water from the rock and who judged Israel when they tested him is the Christ whom the Corinthians know at the Lord's table. To test Christ is not a historical curiosity but a present danger.</p>
          <p>The Corinthians' behavior constitutes a form of testing Christ: their confident assumption that they can eat at idol tables without consequence is a challenge to the Lord's patience, a presumption upon his grace, a testing of the boundaries of what he will tolerate. Paul will make this explicit in 10:22: "Shall we provoke the Lord to jealousy? Are we stronger than he?"</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Fourth Sin — Grumbling (10:10):</hi></p>
          <p>"Nor grumble, as some of them did and were destroyed by the Destroyer" (10:10).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">gongyzete</hi> ("do not grumble") may be the second person plural imperative (a direct command to the Corinthians) or a first person plural hortatory subjunctive (following the pattern of the preceding prohibitions). In either case, the Corinthians are warned against grumbling. The word <hi type="italic">gongyzo</hi> ("to murmur, to grumble, to mutter complaints") appears repeatedly in the LXX wilderness narratives. In Numbers 14:2, "all the people of Israel grumbled (<hi type="italic">diegongysan</hi>) against Moses and Aaron." In Numbers 16:41, "all the congregation of the people of Israel grumbled (<hi type="italic">egongysan</hi>) against Moses and against Aaron." In Numbers 14, the grumbling followed the spies' report and resulted in the sentence that the entire generation (except Joshua and Caleb) would die in the wilderness (Numbers 14:29-30). In Numbers 16:41-50, the grumbling followed the destruction of Korah and his followers and resulted in a plague that killed 14,700 people.</p>
          <p>The "Destroyer" (<hi type="italic">olothreutes</hi>) is a figure from the LXX tradition. The word appears in the Wisdom of Solomon 18:20-25, where, in the context of the Egyptian plague of the firstborn, "the experience of death touched also the righteous, and a plague came upon the multitude in the wilderness, but the wrath did not long continue, for a blameless man was quick to act as their champion... and he withstood the anger and put an end to the disaster" (Wisdom 18:20-21, 23). The root <hi type="italic">olethros/olothreuo</hi> appears in Exodus 12:23, where the Lord passes through Egypt to strike the firstborn and "will not allow the Destroyer (<hi type="italic">ton olethruonta</hi>) to enter your houses to strike you." The Destroyer is the agent of divine judgment — a figure associated with plague, death, and the execution of God's wrath upon those who have violated the covenant.</p>
          <p>Paul says the grumblers were destroyed (<hi type="italic">apōlonto</hi>) by the Destroyer (<hi type="italic">hypo tou olothreutou</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">apollymi</hi> appears again — the same word that described the destruction of the weak brother in 8:11 ("this weak person is destroyed") and the perishing of those who reject the cross in 1:18. The vocabulary is consistent: those who were destroyed in the wilderness experienced the same kind of ruin that threatens the Corinthians. The grumblers were members of the covenant people; they had been delivered from Egypt; they had received every divine provision. Their grumbling — their dissatisfaction with God's arrangement, their complaint against God's appointed leaders — brought the Destroyer upon them.</p>
          <p>The relevance to the Corinthians is not difficult to see. The letter has already documented the Corinthians' complaints and dissatisfactions: their factionalism (chapters 1-4), their disputes with one another (chapter 6), their challenges to Paul's authority and apostolic practice (chapter 9). Grumbling against God's provision and God's appointed leadership is not merely a personality flaw; it is a sin that brought destruction upon Israel and can bring destruction upon the community.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.10.11-1Cor.10.13">
          <title>Verses 11-13: The Warning Applied and the Faithfulness of God</title>
          <p>"Now these things happened to them as an example, but they were written down for our instruction, on whom the end of the ages has come" (10:11).</p>
          <p>Paul restates and intensifies the typological principle of verse 6. The word <hi type="italic">typikōs</hi> ("as examples, in a typological manner") repeats the typological claim: these events were not accidental; they happened as types, as patterns that point beyond themselves. But now Paul adds a second dimension: <hi type="italic">egraphē de pros nouthesian hēmōn</hi> — "they were written for our instruction." The events happened as types; the scriptural record of those events was written for the community's instruction. History and scripture work together: the events constitute the type; the text preserves the warning. The word <hi type="italic">nouthesia</hi> ("instruction, admonition, warning") comes from <hi type="italic">nous</hi> ("mind") and <hi type="italic">tithēmi</hi> ("to place") — it means to place something in the mind, to instruct with urgency, to warn. This is not casual reading but urgent instruction from scripture directed at the present community.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">eis hous ta telē tōn aiōnōn katēntēken</hi> ("on whom the ends of the ages have come") is the eschatological grounding for the entire typological argument. The word <hi type="italic">telē</hi> (plural of <hi type="italic">telos</hi>, "end, goal, completion") and <hi type="italic">aiōnōn</hi> (plural of <hi type="italic">aiōn</hi>, "age, era, epoch") together describe a convergence: the ends of the ages have arrived, have met, have reached their destination at the point where the Corinthian community now stands. The verb <hi type="italic">katantaō</hi> ("to arrive at, to come to, to reach") describes reaching a destination. The ages of history — the age of the patriarchs, the age of Moses, the age of the prophets — have been moving toward a destination, and that destination is now. The Corinthian community lives at the juncture where the old age ends and the new age begins. They are the people upon whom the climax of history has arrived.</p>
          <p>This eschatological location intensifies the warning. If Israel, living at the beginning of the story, faced such severe consequences for idolatry, sexual immorality, testing God, and grumbling, how much more should those living at the climax of the story take heed? The greater privilege of living at the end of the ages brings greater responsibility, not greater immunity. The Corinthians are not more protected than Israel; they are more accountable.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Verse 12: The Direct Application:</hi></p>
          <p>"Therefore let anyone who thinks that he stands take heed lest he fall" (10:12).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">hōste</hi> ("therefore, so then") draws the conclusion from the entire Israel typology. This verse is the point toward which verses 1-11 have been driving.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ho dokōn hestanai</hi> ("the one who thinks he stands") uses the same verb <hi type="italic">dokei</hi> ("thinks, supposes, imagines") that appeared in 8:2: "If anyone thinks he knows something, he does not yet know as he ought to know." In chapter 8, the person who "thinks" they know is overconfident in their knowledge. Here, the person who "thinks" they stand is overconfident in their standing. The verb <hi type="italic">dokei</hi> introduces a note of uncertainty: the person's self-assessment may be wrong. They think they stand — but the Israel precedent has just demonstrated that people who had every reason to think they stood were overthrown.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">hestanai</hi> ("to stand," perfect active infinitive of <hi type="italic">histēmi</hi>) describes a settled, established position — the person believes they are firmly planted, secure, immovable. The contrast is <hi type="italic">pesē</hi> ("lest he fall," aorist active subjunctive of <hi type="italic">piptō</hi>), which describes a sudden collapse. The "standing" is subjective — the person thinks they stand. The "falling" is objective — it describes what actually happens when overconfidence meets testing.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">blepetō</hi> ("let him take heed, let him watch, let him beware") is the imperative of <hi type="italic">blepō</hi>, the same warning verb that appeared in 8:9: "Take care (<hi type="italic">blepete</hi>) that this right of yours does not somehow become a stumbling block to the weak." In both passages, <hi type="italic">blepō</hi> introduces a warning against the danger that the Corinthians' self-confidence blinds them to. They are so sure of their knowledge (8:9) and so sure of their standing (10:12) that they fail to see the danger they are in.</p>
          <p>For the first-century hearer, this verse lands with devastating force against the backdrop of verses 1-5. Israel "stood" — they had the cloud, the sea, the baptism, the food, the drink, the Rock that was Christ. If ever a people had reason to believe they were secure, it was the Exodus generation. They fell. The Corinthians "stand" — they have baptism, the Lord's table, spiritual gifts, Christ's presence. They are in danger of the same fall. The verse does not say the Corinthians will fall; it says they can fall, and that the self-assurance that says "I stand" is itself a warning sign. The overconfidence that chapter 8 diagnosed as puffing up (<hi type="italic">phusioo</hi>) is the same overconfidence that chapter 10 diagnoses as a false sense of security.</p>
          <p>The entire passage from 10:1-12 constitutes one of the most direct warnings in the letter. It says, in effect: you are not safe simply because you have been baptized and share in the Lord's table. Israel had the equivalent of all your privileges and was overthrown. Do not think your standing is guaranteed. Watch yourselves.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Verse 13: The Faithfulness of God:</hi></p>
          <p>"No temptation has overtaken you that is not common to man. God is faithful, and he will not let you be tempted beyond your ability, but with the temptation he will also provide the way of escape, that you may be able to endure it" (10:13).</p>
          <p>After the severity of verses 1-12, Paul offers assurance — but the assurance is carefully framed. It does not say "you will not fall." It says God provides the resources to endure.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">peirasmos</hi> ("temptation, trial, testing") describes the pressure that leads to sin. The phrase <hi type="italic">ei mē anthrōpinos</hi> ("except what is human / that is not common to man") assures the Corinthians that their testing is not unique, not unprecedented, not beyond the scope of what human beings face and can survive. The temptations they encounter — the pressure to participate in temple meals, the social cost of refusing, the theological arguments for doing as they please — are human-scale challenges, not superhuman ones.</p>
          <p>Then comes the theological ground: <hi type="italic">pistos de ho theos</hi> ("but God is faithful"). The adjective <hi type="italic">pistos</hi> ("faithful, trustworthy, reliable") describes God's character. God can be relied upon. He keeps his commitments. In the LXX, God's faithfulness (<hi type="italic">pistis</hi>, <hi type="italic">alētheia</hi>) is a recurring theme: Deuteronomy 7:9, "Know therefore that the Lord your God is God, the faithful God (<hi type="italic">ho theos ho pistos</hi>) who keeps covenant and steadfast love with those who love him and keep his commandments." God's faithfulness is the basis of the assurance.</p>
          <p>But the assurance has a specific shape. God <hi type="italic">hos ouk easei hymas peirasthēnai hyper ho dynasthe</hi> — "who will not allow you to be tempted beyond what you are able." The verb <hi type="italic">eaō</hi> ("to allow, to permit, to let") indicates that God controls the scope of temptation. He sets limits. The temptation will not exceed the community's capacity (<hi type="italic">dynasthe</hi>, "you are able, you have the power"). But the verse does not say God prevents the temptation altogether or that he automatically preserves the community from falling. He limits the temptation and provides a way through it.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">alla poiēsei syn tō peirasmō kai tēn ekbasin tou dynasthai hypenegkein</hi> — "but with the temptation he will also provide the way of escape, that you may be able to endure it." The word <hi type="italic">ekbasis</hi> ("way out, escape, exit") comes from <hi type="italic">ekbainō</hi> ("to go out, to step out"). God provides an exit — a path through the testing that allows the community to bear it (<hi type="italic">hypenegkein</hi>, "to endure, to bear up under, to carry through"). The word <hi type="italic">dynasthai</hi> ("to be able") reappears: God's provision makes endurance possible. It does not make endurance automatic. The way of escape must be taken; the endurance must be exercised. God provides the ability; the community must exercise it.</p>
          <p>The verse is therefore not a promise of unconditional preservation but a promise of adequate provision. God will not allow temptation to exceed capacity, and he will provide a way through. But the entire context — verses 1-12 — has demonstrated that people with every divine provision can still fall if they do not heed the warning. Israel had every provision and was overthrown. The Corinthians have every provision plus the assurance of 10:13. But the assurance works in tandem with the warning, not as a replacement for it. The Corinthians must take heed (10:12) and use the way of escape God provides (10:13). The two verses together form a complete pastoral unit: take heed, because falling is possible (v. 12); take heart, because God is faithful and endurance is possible (v. 13).</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.10.14-1Cor.10.22">
          <title>Verses 14-22: The Incompatibility of Two Tables</title>
          <p>"Therefore, my beloved, flee from idolatry" (10:14).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">dioper</hi> ("for this very reason, therefore") draws the practical conclusion from the entire preceding argument — from Israel's overthrow, from the catalogue of sins, from the warning to those who think they stand. The address <hi type="italic">agapētoi mou</hi> ("my beloved") is pastorally warm; Paul is not attacking his audience but warning people he loves. The verb <hi type="italic">pheugete</hi> ("flee") is a present imperative, commanding habitual, ongoing action: keep fleeing, make it your constant practice to flee. The word <hi type="italic">pheugo</hi> ("to flee, to run away from") describes physical flight from danger. Paul does not say "be cautious about idolatry" or "think carefully about idolatry." He says flee — run, get away, put distance between yourself and it. The same verb appeared in 6:18 regarding sexual immorality: "Flee from sexual immorality." In both cases, the danger is too great for careful negotiation; the only safe response is flight.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">eidōlolatria</hi> ("idolatry") names the sin that the Israel typology warns against. This is the word that governs the entire section from 10:14 to 10:22. Paul is about to argue that eating at the table of an idol constitutes real participation in idolatry, not merely a harmless social activity. The flight he commands is flight from the temple dining rooms, from the cultic meals, from any form of eating that constitutes communion with the powers behind the idols.</p>
          <p>"I speak as to sensible people; judge for yourselves what I say" (10:15).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">phronimoi</hi> ("sensible, prudent, wise") engages the Corinthians' self-image. They consider themselves knowledgeable (8:1-2, <hi type="italic">gnōsin echomen</hi>); Paul now appeals to their capacity for judgment (<hi type="italic">krinate</hi>, "judge, evaluate, assess"). The appeal to their discernment is genuine: Paul is about to lay out an argument, and he invites them to evaluate it on its merits. He is confident the argument will persuade anyone willing to think it through.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Verses 16-17: The Lord's Table as Participation in Christ:</hi></p>
          <p>"The cup of blessing that we bless, is it not a participation in the blood of Christ? The bread that we break, is it not a participation in the body of Christ? Because there is one bread, we who are many are one body, for we all partake of the one bread" (10:16-17).</p>
          <p>Paul begins his argument with what the Corinthians already know and practice: the communal meal, which he will describe more fully in 11:23-26. The phrase <hi type="italic">to potērion tēs eulogias</hi> ("the cup of blessing") is a technical term from the Jewish meal tradition. In the Passover meal and other festive Jewish meals, the cup of blessing (<hi type="italic">kos shel berakah</hi> in Hebrew) was the cup over which the blessing (<hi type="italic">berakah/eulogia</hi>) was pronounced — the prayer of thanksgiving to God. Paul says <hi type="italic">ho eulogoumen</hi> ("which we bless") — the community together blesses the cup by pronouncing the thanksgiving.</p>
          <p>The question <hi type="italic">ouchi koinōnia estin tou haimatos tou Christou</hi> ("is it not a participation in the blood of Christ?") expects the answer "yes." The word <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> is the theological center of the entire argument. It means "sharing, participation, fellowship, communion, partnership." It describes not an external association but an internal bond — a mutual indwelling, a shared reality. When the community drinks the cup of blessing, they participate in (share in, have communion with, are joined to) the blood of Christ. The blood of Christ is the blood of his death (cf. 11:25, "This cup is the new covenant in my blood"). To participate in his blood is to be joined to his death and to everything his death accomplished.</p>
          <p>Similarly, the bread (<hi type="italic">ton arton hon klōmen</hi>, "the bread which we break") is <hi type="italic">koinōnia tou sōmatos tou Christou</hi> ("a participation in the body of Christ"). The breaking of the bread is participation in Christ's body. The community is joined to Christ's body through the act of eating the broken bread.</p>
          <p>Paul then draws a consequence in verse 17: <hi type="italic">hoti heis artos, hen sōma hoi polloi esmen</hi> — "because there is one bread, we who are many are one body." The logic runs: one bread, shared by all, makes all the sharers into one body. The bread creates the body. The communal act of eating from the one bread constitutes the many as a unity — the body of Christ. The phrase <hi type="italic">hoi gar pantes ek tou henos artou metechomen</hi> ("for we all partake of the one bread") uses the verb <hi type="italic">metechō</hi> ("to share in, to partake of, to have a portion of"), which will reappear in 10:21 and 10:30. Participation in the one bread is what makes the many one.</p>
          <p>This is not metaphor or symbol for Paul. The <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> is real participation. The drinking and eating create a real bond with Christ and a real bond among the participants. This reality is the foundation for the argument that follows: if eating at the Lord's table creates real communion with Christ, then eating at an idol's table creates real communion with whatever stands behind the idol.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Verse 18: Israel's Altar as a Second Example:</hi></p>
          <p>"Consider the people of Israel: are not those who eat the sacrifices participants in the altar?" (10:18).</p>
          <p>Paul now introduces a second example of the same principle. The phrase <hi type="italic">blepete ton Israēl kata sarka</hi> ("look at Israel according to the flesh") directs attention to Israel's own sacrificial system. The phrase <hi type="italic">kata sarka</hi> ("according to the flesh") distinguishes the historical, ethnic Israel from the spiritual reality Paul has been discussing. The question is straightforward: <hi type="italic">ouchi hoi esthiontes tas thysias koinōnoi tou thysiastēriou eisin</hi> — "are not those who eat the sacrifices participants in the altar?" Those who eat the portions of the sacrificial offerings are <hi type="italic">koinōnoi</hi> ("sharers, participants, partners") of the altar. The altar is not merely the physical stone structure; it represents the entire sacrificial system and the God to whom the sacrifices are offered. To eat from the altar's offerings is to be in partnership with the altar — that is, to be in relationship with the deity the altar serves.</p>
          <p>The background is Leviticus 7:6, 14-15, 31-34, where the priests and the offerers share portions of the peace offerings and other sacrifices. In the Levitical system, eating the sacrificial meal was not a mere social event; it was participation in the sacrifice itself, a communion between the worshiper, the priest, and God. The meal was the completion of the sacrifice. To eat was to share in the altar.</p>
          <p>Paul has now established the principle from two sources: the Lord's table (vv. 16-17) and Israel's altar (v. 18). In both cases, eating the sacred meal constitutes real participation in the deity or reality that the meal serves. The conclusion for idol-meals follows inevitably.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Verses 19-20: The Theological Turn — Demons Behind the Idols:</hi></p>
          <p>"What do I imply then? That food offered to idols is anything, or that an idol is anything? No, I imply that what pagans sacrifice they offer to demons and not to God. I do not want you to be participants with demons" (10:19-20).</p>
          <p>Paul anticipates an objection. The Corinthians might say: "But you agreed in chapter 8 that idols are nothing! Are you now saying idol-food is something after all? Are you saying idols are real?" Paul's answer to both questions is emphatically negative: <hi type="italic">ouch hoti eidōlothyton ti estin ē hoti eidōlon ti estin</hi> — "not that idol-food is anything or that an idol is anything." Paul has not reversed his position. Idols remain nothing. The food remains just food.</p>
          <p>But — and this is the decisive theological qualification — <hi type="italic">alla hoti ha thyousin ta ethnē, daimoniois kai ou theō thyousin</hi> — "what the nations sacrifice, they sacrifice to demons and not to God." The idol may be nothing, but behind the idol stands something that is not nothing. The word <hi type="italic">daimoniois</hi> ("to demons") comes directly from the LXX of Deuteronomy 32:17, Moses' Song: <hi type="italic">ethysan daimoniois kai ou theō, theois hois ouk ēdeisan</hi> — "they sacrificed to demons and not to God, to gods they had never known." Paul is quoting Israel's own scripture to describe what the Gentile world is doing. The nations think they are sacrificing to their gods; in reality, they are sacrificing to demons. The idol is an empty shell, but the demonic reality behind the idol receives the worship that the idol cannot.</p>
          <p>This is not a contradiction of chapter 8 but a deepening of the argument. In chapter 8, Paul addressed the situation of individual believers eating food that had been associated with idols — food purchased in the marketplace, food served at a dinner. His concern there was the effect on the weak brother's conscience. In chapter 10, Paul addresses the situation of believers participating in the cultic meal itself — eating at the idol's table, in the temple, as part of the ritual act of worship. His concern here is not conscience but participation: the act of eating at the idol's table creates <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> with demons, just as eating at the Lord's table creates <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> with Christ.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ou thelō de hymas koinōnous tōn daimoniōn ginesthai</hi> ("I do not want you to become participants/partners with demons") uses the same <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> vocabulary that described participation in Christ's blood and body (v. 16) and participation in Israel's altar (v. 18). The structure is parallel:</p>
          <list type="x-bullet">
            <item>Cup of blessing = <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> of Christ's blood (v. 16)</item>
            <item>Broken bread = <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> of Christ's body (v. 16)</item>
            <item>Eating Israel's sacrifices = <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> of the altar (v. 18)</item>
            <item>Eating idol sacrifices = <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> of demons (v. 20)</item>
          </list>
          <p>In each case, the meal creates the bond. The bond is real. The first three are not metaphorical, so neither is the fourth. Those who eat at idol tables become partners with demons.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Verses 21-22: The Declaration of Incompatibility:</hi></p>
          <p>"You cannot drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of demons. You cannot partake of the table of the Lord and the table of demons. Shall we provoke the Lord to jealousy? Are we stronger than he?" (10:21-22).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">dynasthe</hi> ("you are able, you can") is negated twice: <hi type="italic">ou dynasthe</hi> — "you are not able." This is not a prohibition ("you must not") but a declaration of impossibility or incompatibility. The two cups and the two tables are mutually exclusive. One cannot be in <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> with Christ and in <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> with demons simultaneously. The relationship with Christ is exclusive, as the confession of 8:6 already implied: "for us there is one God... and one Lord." The one Lord does not share his table with demons.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">potērion kyriou ... potērion daimoniōn</hi> ("cup of the Lord... cup of demons") and <hi type="italic">trapezēs kyriou ... trapezēs daimoniōn</hi> ("table of the Lord... table of demons") set up a stark either/or. The Lord has a cup and a table — the communal meal, the eucharistic celebration. The demons have a cup and a table — the sacrificial meal at the idol's temple. The Corinthian who participates in both is attempting to maintain two incompatible relationships. It cannot be done.</p>
          <p>The question <hi type="italic">ē parazēloumen ton kyrion</hi> ("shall we provoke the Lord to jealousy?") reaches directly back to the Song of Moses in Deuteronomy 32. The verb <hi type="italic">parazēloō</hi> ("to provoke to jealousy, to make jealous") is the same word used in Deuteronomy 32:21 (LXX): <hi type="italic">parezēlōsan me ep' ou theō, parōrgisan me en tois eidōlois autōn</hi> — "they made me jealous with what is no god; they provoked me to anger with their idols." Israel's sacrifices to demons (Deuteronomy 32:17) provoked God to jealousy (Deuteronomy 32:21). Paul asks: are the Corinthians about to do the same thing? By eating at the table of demons while also eating at the table of the Lord, they are provoking the Lord to the same jealousy that Israel provoked when they offered sacrifices to the golden calf, to Baal of Peor, to the gods they met in the wilderness. The entire Israel typology of 10:1-11 lies behind this question. Israel provoked God and was overthrown. Will the Corinthians repeat the pattern?</p>
          <p>The final question — <hi type="italic">mē ischyroteroi autou esmen</hi> ("are we stronger than he?") — is bitterly rhetorical. The comparative <hi type="italic">ischyroteroi</hi> ("stronger") recalls 1:25, where Paul said "the weakness of God is stronger (<hi type="italic">ischyroteron</hi>) than men." If God's weakness is stronger than human strength, then the idea that the Corinthians could provoke God's jealousy and survive the consequences is absurd. Israel was not stronger than God; neither are the Corinthians. The question implies its own answer: no, we are not stronger. If we provoke him, we will face the same kind of judgment that Israel faced.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.10.23-1Cor.10.30">
          <title>Verses 23-30: Practical Guidelines for Daily Situations</title>
          <p>"'All things are lawful,' but not all things are helpful. 'All things are lawful,' but not all things build up" (10:23).</p>
          <p>Paul now shifts from the absolute prohibition of cultic idol-meals (10:14-22) to the practical daily situations where the idol-food question is less clear-cut. He begins by engaging a Corinthian slogan: <hi type="italic">panta exestin</hi> ("all things are lawful, everything is permitted"). This same slogan appeared in 6:12, where Paul also qualified it. The Corinthians apparently used this phrase to justify their exercise of freedom. Paul does not deny the principle — he qualifies it with two criteria.</p>
          <p>First: <hi type="italic">all' ou panta sympherei</hi> — "but not all things are helpful/profitable/beneficial." The verb <hi type="italic">sympherō</hi> ("to bring together, to be advantageous, to benefit, to be profitable") measures actions by their effect. The question is not "am I allowed?" but "does this help?" Does this action benefit anyone? Does it serve the community?</p>
          <p>Second: <hi type="italic">all' ou panta oikodomei</hi> — "but not all things build up." The verb <hi type="italic">oikodomeō</hi> ("to build, to construct, to edify") has been a key term throughout the letter. In 3:9-14, Paul described the community as God's building and warned about how builders construct on the foundation. In 8:1, love builds up while knowledge puffs up. In 8:10, the verb was used with devastating irony — the weak person's conscience being "built up" toward self-destruction. Now Paul returns to the verb's proper meaning: the measure of an action is whether it builds up the community. Actions that are lawful but do not build up are not good enough. The Corinthians' question is always "am I free to do this?" Paul's counter-question is always "does this build up the body?"</p>
          <p>"Let no one seek his own good, but the good of his neighbor" (10:24).</p>
          <p>The principle is stated in its most comprehensive form: <hi type="italic">mēdeis to heautou zēteitō alla to tou heterou</hi> — "let no one seek the things of himself but the things of the other." This is the governing ethic of the entire three-chapter unit. It is the same principle that produced Paul's self-limitation in chapter 9 ("I have made myself a servant to all," 9:19) and that produced his resolution in 8:13 ("if food makes my brother stumble, I will never eat meat again"). The word <hi type="italic">heteros</hi> ("the other") replaces the focus on the self. Freedom is real; but freedom exercised without regard for the other is not the freedom that love produces.</p>
          <p>Paul then addresses three specific situations.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Situation One — The Meat Market (10:25-26):</hi></p>
          <p>"Eat whatever is sold in the meat market without raising any question on the ground of conscience. For 'the earth is the Lord's, and the fullness thereof'" (10:25-26).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">makellon</hi> ("meat market, butcher's market") is a Latin loanword (<hi type="italic">macellum</hi>) that describes the commercial market where meat was sold in a Greco-Roman city. Some of the meat sold in the <hi type="italic">makellon</hi> would have come from temple sacrifices — after the ritual portion was offered to the god and the priests took their share, the remainder was often sold in the public market. The Corinthians might wonder: should we interrogate the butcher about the meat's ritual history? Should we ask whether this cut of lamb was offered to Apollo before it reached the market stall?</p>
          <p>Paul's answer is comprehensive and liberating: <hi type="italic">pan to en makellō pōloumenon esthiete mēden anakrinontes dia tēn suneidēsin</hi> — "eat everything sold in the meat market, investigating nothing on account of conscience." The verb <hi type="italic">anakrinō</hi> ("to examine, to investigate, to inquire into, to interrogate") describes a thorough inquiry. Paul says: do not conduct such an inquiry. Do not ask questions. Buy the meat and eat it. The reason is theological: <hi type="italic">tou kyriou gar hē gē kai to plērōma autēs</hi> — "for the earth is the Lord's, and the fullness thereof."</p>
          <p>This quotation is from Psalm 24:1 (LXX 23:1): <hi type="italic">tou kyriou hē gē kai to plērōma autēs, hē oikoumenē kai pantes hoi katoikountes en autē</hi> — "The earth is the Lord's and the fullness thereof, the world and all who dwell therein." The principle is one of divine ownership. Everything in the created world belongs to God. The meat in the market belongs to God regardless of what ritual it may have passed through. No pagan ritual can change the fundamental ownership of God's creation. The food is God's; the Corinthian can receive it with thanksgiving as God's provision.</p>
          <p>This is the practical outworking of the theological principle established in 8:4-6: there is one God, and idols are nothing. The food that passed through a temple on its way to the market was never really offered to a god, because the idol is not a god. The food belongs to God and can be eaten freely. The conscience (<hi type="italic">syneidēsis</hi>) need not be troubled by food purchased in the ordinary course of commercial life.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Situation Two — Dinner at an Unbeliever's House (10:27):</hi></p>
          <p>"If one of the unbelievers invites you to dinner and you are disposed to go, eat whatever is set before you without raising any question on the ground of conscience" (10:27).</p>
          <p>The scenario is a private dinner invitation from a pagan (<hi type="italic">tis tōn apistōn</hi>, "someone of the unbelievers"). Paul does not prohibit accepting such invitations — he says <hi type="italic">ei theleta poreuesthai</hi> ("if you want to go"), leaving the decision to the individual. If the Corinthian chooses to attend, the same rule applies as in the market: <hi type="italic">pan to paratithemenon hymin esthiete mēden anakrinontes</hi> — "eat everything set before you, investigating nothing." Do not ask what was offered where. Do not conduct a ritual interrogation of the host. Eat freely.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Situation Three — When Someone Identifies the Food as Sacred (10:28-30):</hi></p>
          <p>"But if someone says to you, 'This has been offered in sacrifice,' then do not eat it, for the sake of the one who informed you, and for the sake of conscience — I do not mean your conscience, but his. For why should my liberty be determined by someone else's conscience? If I partake with thankfulness, why am I denounced because of that for which I give thanks?" (10:28-30).</p>
          <p>The situation changes when someone explicitly identifies the food. The word <hi type="italic">hierothyton</hi> ("offered in sacred rite") is significant. Paul does not use <hi type="italic">eidōlothyton</hi> ("idol-sacrifice"), the Jewish term from 8:1 and 10:19 that frames the offering from the perspective of one who regards the idol as nothing. Instead, he uses <hi type="italic">hierothyton</hi>, the term a pagan would use — "sacred offering," a word that frames the sacrifice from the perspective of one who regards it as genuinely sacred. The person who speaks — <hi type="italic">ean de tis hymin eipē</hi>, "if someone says to you" — appears to be either a pagan dinner guest or the host, someone who regards the food as genuinely consecrated to a deity and who draws attention to that fact.</p>
          <p>Paul says: <hi type="italic">mē esthiete</hi> — "do not eat." The reason is <hi type="italic">di' ekeinon ton mēnysanta kai tēn suneidēsin</hi> — "for the sake of the one who pointed it out and for the sake of conscience." When the food has been explicitly identified as a sacred offering, eating it communicates something to the person who identified it. It says: "I do not take your gods seriously, but I will eat their food anyway." Or worse, it says: "I share in your gods." Either way, the act of eating becomes a public statement that damages the informant's understanding of the community's commitments. Abstaining, by contrast, communicates: "I will not participate in what you regard as sacred worship of your gods."</p>
          <p>The qualifying phrase — "I do not mean your conscience, but his" (<hi type="italic">ou tēn heautou legō alla tēn tou heterou</hi>) — clarifies that the Corinthian's own conscience is not the issue. The knowledgeable person knows the idol is nothing and can eat with a clear conscience. The concern is the other person's conscience — their perception of what the eating means.</p>
          <p>Verses 29b-30 — "For why should my liberty be determined by someone else's conscience? If I partake with thankfulness, why am I denounced because of that for which I give thanks?" — may represent a Corinthian objection that Paul is voicing. The protest is familiar: why should my freedom (<hi type="italic">eleutheria</hi>) be judged (<hi type="italic">krinetai</hi>) by another person's conscience? If I give thanks to God for the food, what is wrong? These questions express the same resistance that has been evident throughout the discussion: the Corinthian assertion that correct knowledge and personal gratitude are sufficient to justify any behavior. Paul has already answered this objection in multiple ways: knowledge without love destroys (chapter 8), rights voluntarily surrendered serve the gospel (chapter 9), Israel's overconfidence led to catastrophe (10:1-12), and participation in idol worship creates real communion with demons (10:14-22). The objection is understandable but has been answered. The Corinthians' freedom is genuine, but their freedom is not the only consideration. The other person exists, and the other person's well-being is more important than the exercise of liberty.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.10.31-1Cor.10.33">
          <title>Verses 31-33 and 11:1: The Comprehensive Principle and the Call to Imitation</title>
          <p>"So, whether you eat or drink, or whatever you do, do all to the glory of God" (10:31).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">oun</hi> ("so, therefore") signals the summary. Paul now states the principle that governs all the specific cases — the meat market, the private dinner, the informed conscience, and every other situation: <hi type="italic">eite oun esthiete eite pinete eite ti poieite, panta eis doxan theou poieite</hi> — "whether you eat or drink or whatever you do, do everything for the glory of God." The phrase <hi type="italic">eis doxan theou</hi> ("for the glory of God, to the glory of God") is the ultimate criterion. Every action, including the most mundane — eating, drinking — is to be directed toward God's glory. The word <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> ("glory, honor, splendor, weight, reputation") describes God's manifest worth. To act "for the glory of God" is to act in a way that displays and honors who God is. This means that eating which damages a brother (chapter 8), eating which participates in demonic worship (10:14-22), and eating which scandalizes a pagan observer (10:28) all fail the test. They do not glorify God. Eating which is done freely, with thanksgiving, in the awareness of God's ownership of all creation, and with care for the effect on others — this glorifies God.</p>
          <p>"Give no offense to Jews or to Greeks or to the church of God" (10:32).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">aproskopoi</hi> ("without offense, without causing stumbling") echoes the <hi type="italic">proskomma</hi> ("stumbling block") of 8:9. Paul has argued throughout that the knowledgeable person's exercise of freedom can become a stumbling block to the weak. Now he expands the scope: give no offense to anyone — to Jews (who would be scandalized by participation in idol worship), to Greeks (who would misunderstand the community's commitments if believers ate idol-food casually), or to the church of God (the community itself, where weak members could be led to destruction).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">tē ekklēsia tou theou</hi> ("the church of God, the assembly of God") has appeared throughout the letter. In 1:2, the letter was addressed to "the church of God that is in Corinth." In 3:16-17, Paul warned that God's temple — the community — must not be destroyed: "If anyone destroys God's temple, God will destroy him." In 6:19-20, the body is a temple of the Holy Spirit. The church of God is the community that belongs to God, and causing offense within it is an offense against God.</p>
          <p>"Just as I try to please everyone in everything I do, not seeking my own advantage, but that of many, that they may be saved" (10:33).</p>
          <p>Paul returns one final time to his own example, the pattern he has been demonstrating since chapter 9. The phrase <hi type="italic">kathōs kagō panta pasin areskō</hi> ("just as I also please everyone in everything") does not describe flattery or people-pleasing in the pejorative sense. It describes the apostolic disposition of accommodating oneself to others for their benefit — the same disposition described in 9:19-23, where Paul became all things to all people. He seeks not <hi type="italic">to idion sympheron</hi> ("my own advantage") but <hi type="italic">to tōn pollōn</hi> ("the advantage of the many"). The purpose clause is soteriological: <hi type="italic">hina sōthōsin</hi> — "that they may be saved." The verb <hi type="italic">sōzō</hi> ("to save, to rescue, to deliver") brings the entire discussion to its ultimate horizon. Self-limitation is not merely polite; it is salvific. Paul's surrender of rights, his accommodation to every kind of person, his refusal to seek his own advantage — all of this serves the salvation of others. The opposite behavior — the insistence on exercising freedom regardless of its effect — endangers the salvation of others (cf. 8:11, where the weak brother is "destroyed").</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">11:1: The Call to Imitation:</hi></p>
          <p>"Be imitators of me, as I am of Christ" (11:1).</p>
          <p>This verse is the capstone of the entire three-chapter argument (8:1-11:1). The verb <hi type="italic">ginesthe</hi> ("become, be") is the present imperative: keep being, continually be, make it your ongoing practice to be imitators (<hi type="italic">mimētai</hi>) of Paul. The word <hi type="italic">mimētēs</hi> ("imitator, one who models after another") establishes a chain of imitation:</p>
          <p>Christ is the original pattern. Christ gave himself for others — "the brother for whom Christ died" (8:11). Christ's self-giving is the ultimate act of surrendered rights for the sake of the vulnerable. Paul imitates Christ by surrendering his own apostolic rights for the sake of the gospel and the people it reaches (chapter 9). The Corinthians are to imitate Paul by surrendering their own freedom to eat idol-food for the sake of the weak, the unbeliever, and the community.</p>
          <p>The chain runs: Christ —&gt; Paul —&gt; Corinthians. At each link, the pattern is the same: the one who has rights voluntarily limits the exercise of those rights for the benefit of others. This is not weakness but strength exercised through restraint. It is not ignorance but knowledge governed by love. It is not bondage but freedom directed toward its highest purpose — the glory of God and the salvation of the many.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.10">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 10 is the climactic chapter of the three-chapter unit (8:1-11:1) that addresses the question of food offered to idols. Where chapter 8 established the principle (knowledge without love destroys) and chapter 9 illustrated the principle from Paul's own life (apostolic rights voluntarily surrendered), chapter 10 provides the historical precedent, the theological ground, and the practical application. It is the argument's conclusion, and it carries the greatest weight.</p>
          <p>The chapter's force depends on the recognition that it forms a continuous argument with what precedes. The <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") of 10:1 anchors the entire Israel typology to Paul's statement in 9:27 about his own potential disqualification. Paul disciplines himself because the danger of disqualification is real. It is real because — "for" — Israel's history proves that universal participation in divine privilege does not prevent judgment. The five-fold "all" of 10:1-4 matches the Corinthians' own situation: they have all been baptized, they all share in the Lord's table, they all belong to Christ. But Israel's "all" was followed by catastrophic loss: "with most of them God was not pleased, for they were overthrown in the wilderness" (10:5). The word <hi type="italic">katestrōthēsan</hi> ("they were overthrown, they were strewn down") describes bodies scattered across the desert — the corpses of people who had every privilege.</p>
          <p>The catalogue of sins in 10:6-10 is not random. Each sin corresponds to a temptation the Corinthians face:</p>
          <list type="x-bullet">
            <item>Desiring evil (10:6) — the general category of disordered appetite that has manifested throughout the letter in sexual immorality (chapters 5-6), litigation (chapter 6), and the insistence on exercising freedom without restraint.</item>
            <item>Idolatry (10:7) — the specific danger of eating at idol tables, which is the presenting issue of chapters 8-10. The golden calf quotation from Exodus 32:6 links idolatry to eating and drinking, making the connection to the Corinthians' temple dining inescapable.</item>
            <item>Sexual immorality (10:8) — the sin Paul already addressed in chapters 5-6, now shown to be organically connected to idol worship through the Baal Peor incident, where sacrificial meals led directly to sexual sin.</item>
            <item>Testing Christ (10:9) — the overconfident assumption that one can push the boundaries of acceptable behavior without consequences, which is precisely the Corinthians' posture. They are testing how far they can go in participating in the cultural life of the temples without crossing a line.</item>
            <item>Grumbling (10:10) — dissatisfaction with God's arrangement and God's appointed leaders, a disposition evident in the Corinthians' factionalism and challenges to Paul's authority.</item>
          </list>
          <p>The warning of 10:12 — "let anyone who thinks that he stands take heed lest he fall" — is the verse that crystallizes the chapter's message. The verb <hi type="italic">dokei</hi> ("thinks") signals the danger of self-deception. The Corinthians think they are secure because they have knowledge, baptism, and the Lord's table. Israel thought they were secure because they had the cloud, the sea, the manna, and the Rock. Both were wrong — or rather, both had the privileges but mistook the privileges for a guarantee. The privileges are real; the guarantee is not. Standing requires ongoing vigilance, not passive presumption.</p>
          <p>The shift at 10:14 from the Israel typology to the theology of participation introduces a new and more severe argument. In chapter 8, Paul treated idol-food as theologically neutral but practically dangerous because of its effect on the weak. In 10:14-22, Paul treats participation in cultic idol-meals as theologically prohibited because it constitutes <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> with demons. The two positions are not contradictory; they address different situations. Chapter 8 addresses the private eating of food that happens to have been offered at a temple — marketplace purchases, social dinners. Chapter 10:14-22 addresses participation in the ritual meal itself — eating at the idol's table, in the temple, as part of the worship. The first is a matter of love and conscience; the second is a matter of idolatry and is flatly incompatible with belonging to Christ.</p>
          <p>The three-fold <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> argument (Lord's table, Israel's altar, idol table) in 10:16-20 establishes that eating is participating. The meal is not neutral; it creates a bond between the eater and the deity or power that the meal serves. This is true of the Lord's table (<hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> of Christ's blood and body), true of Israel's altar (<hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> of the altar), and true of the idol's table (<hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> of demons). The Corinthians cannot simultaneously be in communion with Christ and in communion with demons. The two relationships are exclusive.</p>
          <p>The question of 10:22 — "Shall we provoke the Lord to jealousy?" — reaches back to Deuteronomy 32, the same text from which Paul drew the word <hi type="italic">daimoniois</hi> in 10:20. Moses' Song describes Israel's history of provocation: they sacrificed to demons (32:17), they made God jealous with what is no god (32:21), and God's response was devastating judgment. Paul places the Corinthians on the same trajectory: if they participate in the tables of demons, they provoke the same jealousy, and they can expect the same consequences. The question "Are we stronger than he?" drives the point home with grim irony: the Corinthians who think they can handle the dual allegiance — who think their knowledge protects them — are not stronger than the God whose jealousy destroyed Israel in the wilderness.</p>
          <p>The practical guidelines of 10:23-30 then demonstrate that Paul's argument is not rigid legalism. The comprehensive prohibition of 10:14-22 applies to cultic meals at temples. But in the everyday situations of marketplace purchases and private dinners, freedom prevails. The earth is the Lord's; food is food; eat and give thanks. The exception arises only when someone explicitly identifies the food as a sacred offering — then the believer abstains, not because the food has been contaminated but because eating it would damage the other person's understanding of the community's commitments.</p>
          <p>The summary principle of 10:31 — "do all to the glory of God" — and the command of 10:32 — "give no offense to Jews or to Greeks or to the church of God" — bring the three-chapter argument to its practical and theological conclusion. Every action is measured by two criteria: does it glorify God, and does it benefit others? The Corinthians' persistent question throughout chapters 8-10 has been "what am I free to do?" Paul's persistent answer has been "what does love and God's glory require?"</p>
          <p>Several threads from earlier in the letter reach their culmination in this chapter.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">apollymi</hi> ("to destroy, to perish") runs from 1:18 (those who are perishing versus those who are being saved), through 8:11 (the weak brother being destroyed), to 10:9-10 (the Israelites destroyed by serpents and by the Destroyer). The vocabulary of destruction that opened the letter and that charged the idol-food argument in chapter 8 now finds its full historical illustration in Israel's wilderness devastation. The destruction Paul warned about in 8:11 is not hypothetical; it is the same kind of destruction that scattered Israel's corpses across the desert.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">oikodomeō</hi> ("to build up") runs from 3:9-14 (building on the foundation), through 8:1 (love builds up), to 8:10 (the ironic "building up" of the weak conscience toward self-destruction), and now to 10:23 (not all things build up). The criterion of building up — genuine edification of the community — has been Paul's consistent standard for evaluating behavior.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> ("right, authority, freedom") ran through 8:9 (the right that becomes a stumbling block) and dominated chapter 9 (Paul's apostolic rights, voluntarily surrendered). In chapter 10, the word does not appear, but its reality is everywhere: the Corinthians' freedom to eat, drink, and participate in social life is affirmed (10:25-27) but limited by the higher obligation to glorify God and not damage others.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi>, which appears in chapter 10 for the first time in the letter, will become a foundational concept for the remainder of the argument. It describes the bond that the communal meal creates — a bond with Christ (10:16), with the altar (10:18), and potentially with demons (10:20). This vocabulary will return in the discussion of the Lord's Supper in 11:17-34, where the Corinthians' failure to discern the body (11:29) and their divisions at the table (11:18-22) violate the very <hi type="italic">koinōnia</hi> that the meal is supposed to create.</p>
          <p>The chapter's most far-reaching contribution to the letter's argument is the demonstration that divine privilege does not automatically preserve. The Corinthians' confidence rests on what they have received: knowledge, baptism, the Spirit, the Lord's table. Chapter 10 demonstrates from Israel's history that having everything is not the same as being safe. Israel had everything — and most were overthrown. The Corinthians have everything. Whether they will be overthrown depends on whether they heed the warning, flee idolatry, seek the good of others rather than their own, and do everything for the glory of God. The privileges are real, but so is the danger. God is faithful and will provide the way of escape (10:13), but the way of escape must be taken. The person who thinks they stand must watch themselves, because the fall is not prevented by the standing; it is prevented by the watching.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.11">
        <title>1 Corinthians 11 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 10):</hi> Paul completed the three-chapter argument about idol-food that began in chapter 8. He opened chapter 10 with Israel's wilderness generation as a warning: all of them were "baptized into Moses," all ate the same spiritual food, all drank from the spiritual Rock — yet "with most of them God was not pleased, for they were overthrown in the wilderness" (10:1-5). The word "all" (<hi type="italic">pantes</hi>) appeared five times in four verses, hammering the point that universal reception of divine gifts did not prevent destruction. These events happened as <hi type="italic">typoi</hi> ("examples, patterns, types") for the community (10:6, 11). Paul catalogued Israel's specific failures — idolatry, sexual immorality, testing God, grumbling — and matched each to a historical punishment: twenty-three thousand fell in a single day, some were destroyed by serpents, some by the Destroyer (10:7-10). The application was direct: "let anyone who thinks that he stands take heed lest he fall" (10:12). The person who is confident in their standing is precisely the person at risk. Paul then addressed participation in idol meals with a new argument — not the stumbling-block argument of chapter 8 but a <hi type="italic">koinonia</hi> ("participation, fellowship, communion") argument. The cup the community blesses is a participation in the blood of Christ; the bread they break is a participation in the body of Christ (10:16). This participation is exclusive: "you cannot drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of demons; you cannot partake of the table of the Lord and the table of demons" (10:21). The question "shall we provoke the Lord to jealousy?" (10:22) echoed Deuteronomy 32:21, where Israel provoked God to jealousy by turning to foreign gods. Paul concluded with practical guidelines: eat what is sold in the market without inquiry (10:25); eat what an unbeliever serves without inquiry (10:27); but if someone identifies the food as sacrificed, abstain for the sake of the informer's conscience (10:28). The governing principle: "whether you eat or drink, or whatever you do, do all to the glory of God. Give no offense to Jews or to Greeks or to the church of God" (10:31-32). Paul's final words before chapter 11 were: "just as I try to please everyone in everything I do, not seeking my own advantage, but that of many, that they may be saved" (10:33).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 12):</hi> Paul turns to another question from the Corinthians' letter, signaled by the formula <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> ("now concerning"): "Now concerning spiritual gifts, brothers, I do not want you to be uninformed" (12:1). He opens by contrasting their former life — "when you were pagans you were led astray to mute idols" (12:2) — with the criterion for genuine Spirit-speech: no one speaking by God's Spirit says "Jesus is accursed," and no one can say "Jesus is Lord" except by the Holy Spirit (12:3). Paul then develops the diversity-within-unity principle: there are varieties of gifts but the same Spirit, varieties of service but the same Lord, varieties of activities but the same God who empowers them all (12:4-6). Each person receives a manifestation of the Spirit "for the common good" (<hi type="italic">pros to sympheron</hi>, 12:7). Paul lists the gifts — wisdom, knowledge, faith, healing, miracles, prophecy, distinguishing spirits, tongues, interpretation — and attributes them all to "one and the same Spirit, who apportions to each one individually as he wills" (12:11). Then comes the body metaphor that will dominate the rest of the chapter: "For just as the body is one and has many members, and all the members of the body, though many, are one body, so it is with Christ" (12:12). The members cannot say to one another "I have no need of you" (12:21). God has composed the body so that "there may be no division (<hi type="italic">schisma</hi>) in the body" (12:25). The word <hi type="italic">schisma</hi> echoes 1:10 and 11:18 — the same disease diagnosed at the letter's opening and in the middle of chapter 11 now receives its most sustained treatment through the body metaphor. The chapter concludes with Paul asking whether all possess the same gift — the implied answer is no — and then pointing forward: "earnestly desire the higher gifts. And I will show you a still more excellent way" (12:31), which leads into the love chapter (chapter 13).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 11 connects these:</hi> Chapter 11 stands at a structural hinge in the letter. The idol-food argument of chapters 8-10 has concluded. The spiritual-gifts argument of chapters 12-14 has not yet begun. Chapter 11 addresses two distinct problems in the community's assembly practice — conduct during prayer and prophecy (11:2-16) and conduct at the communal meal (11:17-34) — both of which concern what happens when the community "comes together" (<hi type="italic">synerchomai</hi>, a verb that appears five times in vv. 17-34: vv. 17, 18, 20, 33, 34, and will reappear in 14:23, 26).</p>
          <p>The transition from chapter 10 to chapter 11 is seamless at the level of principle. Paul has just urged the Corinthians to "do all to the glory of God" and to "give no offense to the church of God" (10:31-32). Chapter 11 applies these principles to the community's gathered worship. The idol-food argument concerned behavior in external settings — temple dining rooms, marketplace purchases, private dinners with unbelievers. Chapter 11 turns inward, to what happens when the community itself gathers. If behavior at external meals must be governed by love and the glory of God, how much more must behavior at the community's own meals and worship?</p>
          <p>The connection forward to chapter 12 is equally tight. In 11:29, Paul will warn about eating and drinking "without discerning the body" (<hi type="italic">me diakrinon to soma</hi>). In chapter 12, Paul will develop at length what "the body" means: the community is the body of Christ, composed of many members, each essential, none dispensable (12:12-27). The failure to "discern the body" in 11:29 is precisely the failure to recognize what chapter 12 will spell out — that the community is Christ's body and its members must be treated accordingly. The divisions at the meal (11:18-21), where the wealthy eat and drink to excess while the poor go hungry, are the practical expression of the body-denial that the metaphor of chapter 12 is designed to cure.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">schismata</hi> ("divisions") in 11:18 also reaches all the way back to 1:10, where Paul first named the Corinthian disease: "that there be no divisions (<hi type="italic">schismata</hi>) among you, but that you be united in the same mind and the same judgment." The divisions at the communal meal are another manifestation of the same fracture that produced the teacher-allegiance factions of chapters 1-4. The disease is the same; the symptoms rotate. Whether the divisions follow the lines of "I belong to Paul" and "I belong to Apollos" (1:12) or the lines of wealthy and poor at the meal table (11:21-22), the fundamental problem is identical: the community is split where it should be one.</p>
          <p>Verse 11:1 properly belongs to the preceding argument. "Be imitators of me, as I am of Christ" caps the entire self-limitation demonstration of chapters 8-10. The verb <hi type="italic">mimeomai</hi> ("to imitate") echoes 4:16, where Paul first urged the Corinthians to "be imitators of me" (<hi type="italic">mimetai mou ginesthe</hi>). In chapter 4, the exhortation came after Paul described the apostles' suffering and self-abasement (4:9-13). In 11:1, it comes after Paul described his voluntary surrender of rights for the sake of others (9:1-27) and his practice of seeking "the advantage of the many, that they may be saved" (10:33). The pattern Paul imitates in Christ is the pattern of voluntary self-limitation for the sake of others — precisely the pattern he has urged throughout the idol-food argument. This verse is the capstone of that argument, not the foundation of what follows.</p>
          <p>The new section begins at 11:2 with a shift in tone. Paul moves from exhortation about imitation to commendation of the Corinthians for maintaining the traditions he delivered to them — followed immediately by correction where those traditions are not being properly observed.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.1">
          <title>Verse 1: The Capstone of the Idol-Food Argument</title>
          <p>"Be imitators of me, as I am of Christ" (11:1).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">mimetai</hi> ("imitators") is the nominative plural of <hi type="italic">mimetes</hi>, from <hi type="italic">mimeomai</hi> ("to imitate, to follow the example of, to pattern oneself after"). Paul used this word in 4:16: "I urge you, then, be imitators of me" (<hi type="italic">parakaleo oun hymas, mimetai mou ginesthe</hi>). There the context was Paul's description of apostolic hardship — the apostles are "last of all," "a spectacle to the world," "fools for Christ's sake," "weak," "in disrepute" (4:9-10). The Corinthians, by contrast, considered themselves "wise," "strong," "held in honor" (4:10). The call to imitate Paul was a call to adopt the apostolic posture of self-lowering rather than the Corinthian posture of self-elevation.</p>
          <p>Here in 11:1, the call to imitation caps a different but related demonstration. Paul has shown that he voluntarily surrendered his apostolic rights — to financial support, to a wife, to freedom from manual labor (9:4-15). He "made himself a slave to all" (9:19). He did this not from weakness but from strength — the strength to forgo entitlements for the sake of others. Now he identifies the source of this pattern: <hi type="italic">kathos kago Christou</hi> — "as I also [imitate] Christ." Paul's self-limitation is not original to him; it is an imitation of Christ's own pattern. The cross itself is the supreme act of voluntary self-limitation — Christ "died for" the brothers (8:11). Paul imitates that pattern by surrendering his rights. The Corinthians are to imitate Paul's imitation of Christ.</p>
          <p>This verse completes a chain: Christ surrendered his life for others; Paul surrenders his rights for others; the Corinthians should surrender their freedom for others. The idol-food argument, which began with a question about meat, has arrived at a christological principle of self-giving love as the governing pattern for all community life.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.2">
          <title>Verse 2: Commendation and the Handing Down of Traditions</title>
          <p>"Now I commend you because you remember me in everything and maintain the traditions even as I delivered them to you" (11:2).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">epaino</hi> ("to commend, to praise") introduces a note of approval that stands in deliberate contrast to what will follow. Paul commends the Corinthians for two things: they "remember" him (<hi type="italic">mou memnesthe</hi> — perfect middle/passive of <hi type="italic">mimnesko</hi>, "you have remembered and continue to remember me") in everything (<hi type="italic">panta</hi>), and they "hold fast" (<hi type="italic">katechete</hi>, present active indicative of <hi type="italic">katecho</hi>, "to hold, to retain, to keep") the traditions (<hi type="italic">paradoseis</hi>) just as Paul delivered (<hi type="italic">paredoka</hi>, aorist of <hi type="italic">paradidomi</hi>, "to hand over, to deliver, to transmit") them.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">paradosis</hi> ("tradition, that which is handed down") is the noun form of <hi type="italic">paradidomi</hi>. In first-century Judaism, the transmission of authoritative teaching followed a formal chain: a teacher "received" (<hi type="italic">paralambanein</hi>) teaching from those before him and "delivered" (<hi type="italic">paradidonai</hi>) it to those after him. This is the language of the rabbinic chain of transmission described in the <hi type="italic">Mishnah</hi> (Avot 1:1: "Moses received Torah from Sinai and delivered it to Joshua, and Joshua to the elders..."). Paul uses the same paired verbs in 11:23 for the supper tradition: "I received from the Lord what I also delivered to you" (<hi type="italic">parelabon... paredoka</hi>). And in 15:3: "I delivered to you as of first importance what I also received" (<hi type="italic">paredoka... parelabon</hi>). The vocabulary is technical and deliberate: Paul stands in a chain of authoritative transmission, and the Corinthians are the recipients of that chain.</p>
          <p>The commendation is genuine but limited. Paul praises them for holding the traditions in general, but he is about to identify areas where their practice falls short. The word <hi type="italic">epaino</hi> will reappear in verse 17 in negated form — <hi type="italic">ouk epaino</hi> ("I do not commend") — and again in verse 22 — <hi type="italic">ti eipo hymin? epainezo hymas? en touto ouk epaino</hi> ("what shall I say to you? Shall I commend you? In this I do not commend you"). The chapter moves from commendation to correction to rebuke. The Corinthians maintain the traditions in general; in specifics, they fail.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.3">
          <title>Verse 3: The Chain of <hi type="italic">kephale</hi></title>
          <p>"But I want you to understand that the head of every man is Christ, the head of a wife is her husband, and the head of Christ is God" (11:3).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">thelo</hi> ("I want, I wish, I desire") plus <hi type="italic">hymas eidenai</hi> ("you to know") signals that Paul is introducing something the Corinthians need to understand as the foundation for what follows. The instruction about head coverings (vv. 4-16) rests on the principle stated here.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> ("head") is used metaphorically. In its literal sense, <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> simply means the physical head of the body — the word Paul will use in 12:21 when the "head" cannot say to the "feet" that it has no need of them. But in the LXX, <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> sometimes renders the Hebrew <hi type="italic">rosh</hi> in its figurative sense of "leader, chief, ruler." In Judges 11:11 (LXX), the people made Jephthah "head and leader" (<hi type="italic">eis kephalen kai eis archegon</hi>) over them. In Isaiah 7:8-9 (LXX), "the head (<hi type="italic">kephale</hi>) of Syria is Damascus, and the head of Damascus is Rezin; and the head of Ephraim is Samaria." In these LXX passages, <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> carries the sense of the one who stands at the top of a social or political structure — the chief, the representative figure, the one from whom authority flows.</p>
          <p>However, <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> can also carry the sense of "source" or "origin," particularly when the context involves derivation. In the creation narrative that Paul will invoke in verses 8-9, woman was made "from" man (<hi type="italic">ek tou andros</hi>) — man is the source from which woman was derived. Paul's argument in what follows draws on both dimensions: the man stands in a relationship to the woman that involves both origin (she was made from him, vv. 8-9, 12) and representative connection (what he does with his literal head reflects on his metaphorical head, and vice versa, vv. 4-5).</p>
          <p>The sequence Paul establishes is notable for its arrangement. He does not present a descending hierarchy from God down to woman. Instead, the order is: (1) the <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> of every man (<hi type="italic">andros</hi>) is Christ; (2) the <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> of woman/wife (<hi type="italic">gynaikos</hi>) is the man/husband (<hi type="italic">aner</hi>); (3) the <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> of Christ is God. The sequence begins with Christ-man, moves to man-woman, and concludes with God-Christ. The christological bookends (Christ is <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> of man; God is <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> of Christ) frame the man-woman relationship within a larger theological structure. The relationship between man and woman is not the primary subject; it is embedded within the relationship between God, Christ, and humanity.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">aner</hi> can mean either "man" (as distinct from woman) or "husband" (as distinct from wife). The word <hi type="italic">gyne</hi> can mean either "woman" or "wife." The context of the passage — which will involve praying and prophesying in the assembly, not exclusively marital relationships — suggests that Paul has both senses in view. The instruction applies to men and women generally when they function in the assembly, though the marital dimension is not excluded.</p>
          <p>The final clause — <hi type="italic">kephale de tou Christou ho theos</hi> ("the head of Christ is God") — places Christ in a relationship to God that parallels the relationship of woman to man. Christ is not independent of God; God is the source and authority from whom Christ derives and to whom Christ is related. This prevents the Christ-man relationship from being read as absolute or unmediated. Christ himself has a <hi type="italic">kephale</hi>. The chain is relational throughout, not a fixed ranking of worth or dignity.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.4-1Cor.11.6">
          <title>Verses 4-6: The Practical Instructions on Covering and Uncovering</title>
          <p>"Every man who prays or prophesies with his head covered dishonors his head, but every wife who prays or prophesies with her head uncovered dishonors her head, since it is the same as if her head were shaven" (11:4-5).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">proseuchomenos e propheteuon</hi> ("praying or prophesying") describes two activities that take place in the gathered assembly. Praying (<hi type="italic">proseuchomai</hi>) is addressing God; prophesying (<hi type="italic">propheteuo</hi>) is speaking God's word to the community. Both men and women are described as engaging in both activities. Paul does not question whether women pray or prophesy in the assembly; he assumes they do. The issue is not their participation but the manner of their participation — specifically, the state of their heads.</p>
          <p>The man who prays or prophesies <hi type="italic">kata kephales echon</hi> ("having [something] down from the head" — that is, with his head covered or veiled) <hi type="italic">kataischynei ten kephalen autou</hi> ("dishonors his head"). The verb <hi type="italic">kataischyno</hi> ("to put to shame, to dishonor, to disgrace") is a strong word. In the LXX, it describes the shame of defeated enemies (Psalm 35:4, LXX 34:4: "let them be put to shame and dishonored who seek my life"), the shame of those who trust in idols (Isaiah 44:11: "all his companions shall be put to shame"), and the shame of those who depart from God (Jeremiah 17:13: "those who turn away from you shall be written in the earth, for they have forsaken the fountain of living water"). To "dishonor the head" operates on two levels simultaneously: the man shames his literal head by covering it, and he shames his metaphorical head — Christ (v. 3) — by acting in a way that obscures the relationship.</p>
          <p>The woman who prays or prophesies <hi type="italic">akatakalypto te kephale</hi> ("with uncovered head") likewise dishonors her head. The adjective <hi type="italic">akatakalyptos</hi> ("uncovered, unveiled") describes the absence of a covering. She dishonors her head — again, both literal and metaphorical (her husband/man, v. 3). Paul immediately intensifies the point: "it is one and the same thing as if she were shaved" (<hi type="italic">hen gar estin kai to auto te exyrymene</hi>). The verb <hi type="italic">xyrao</hi> ("to shave, to make bald") describes the complete removal of hair. In the Greco-Roman world, a shaved head on a woman was a mark of deep shame. It was associated with the punishment of adulteresses (in some Greek traditions, a woman caught in adultery had her head shaved as a public disgrace), with mourning practices, and with the appearance of slaves. In the LXX, shaving the head is associated with mourning and judgment: Isaiah 3:24, "instead of well-set hair, baldness" (among the judgments on the daughters of Zion); Deuteronomy 21:12, the captive woman who shaves her head and mourns her family before being taken as a wife. The shaved head signals the stripping away of dignity. Paul's argument is that an uncovered head in the assembly communicates the same shame as a shaved head.</p>
          <p>"For if a wife will not cover her head, then she should cut her hair short. But since it is disgraceful for a wife to cut off her hair or shave her head, let her cover her head" (11:6).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">aischron</hi> ("disgraceful, shameful, base") describes a violation of what is socially fitting. Paul reasons from the conclusion back to the premise: if the woman finds it shameful to cut or shave her hair (which Paul takes as a shared cultural assumption), then she should recognize that praying uncovered communicates the same kind of shame, and therefore she should cover her head. The argument moves from an accepted cultural value (a woman's shame at having her hair cut or shaved) to the behavior in question (praying uncovered) by asserting their equivalence. The covering is the means by which the woman avoids the disgrace that an exposed head communicates.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.7-1Cor.11.9">
          <title>Verses 7-9: The Creation Argument</title>
          <p>"For a man ought not to cover his head, since he is the image and glory of God, but woman is the glory of man. For man was not made from woman, but woman from man. Neither was man created for woman, but woman for man" (11:7-9).</p>
          <p>Paul grounds his practical instruction in the creation narratives of Genesis. The word <hi type="italic">eikon</hi> ("image, likeness, representation") reaches back to Genesis 1:26-27 (LXX): "Let us make man (<hi type="italic">anthropon</hi>) according to our image (<hi type="italic">kat' eikona hemeteran</hi>) and likeness (<hi type="italic">kath' homoiosin</hi>)... and God made the man (<hi type="italic">ton anthropon</hi>); according to the image (<hi type="italic">kat' eikona</hi>) of God he made him; male and female he made them." In Genesis 1, both male and female are made in God's image. Paul, however, applies "image" (<hi type="italic">eikon</hi>) here to the man specifically, and introduces a second term — <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> ("glory") — that he applies differently to man and woman.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> has a range of meanings: "glory, radiance, splendor, honor, visible manifestation, reputation." In the LXX, the <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> of God is the visible manifestation of God's presence — the <hi type="italic">kabod YHWH</hi>, the weight and brightness of God that fills the tabernacle (Exodus 40:34) and the temple (1 Kings 8:11). Applied to a person, <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> can mean the visible dignity, the outward splendor, the distinguishing excellence that marks them. Paul says the man is the <hi type="italic">eikon kai doxa</hi> of God — the visible representation and manifest splendor of God. This is why the man's head should not be covered: to cover the man's head would be to veil the image and glory of God, to hide what should be displayed.</p>
          <p>The woman, Paul says, is the <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> of the man — the visible splendor and manifest distinction of the man. Paul does not say the woman is the <hi type="italic">eikon</hi> ("image") of man. She is not made in man's image; she is his glory — his crowning distinction, his visible splendor. The distinction is important: the man images God; the woman manifests man's glory. She is not a copy of man but the radiance that proceeds from him.</p>
          <p>Verses 8-9 provide the scriptural basis, drawn from Genesis 2 (not Genesis 1). "Man was not made from woman, but woman from man" (11:8) draws on Genesis 2:21-23 (LXX), where God takes a rib (or side) from the man and builds (<hi type="italic">okodomesen</hi>, the same root as <hi type="italic">oikodomeo</hi>, "to build up") it into a woman. The derivation is directional: the woman comes from (<hi type="italic">ek</hi>) the man. "Neither was man created for woman, but woman for man" (11:9) draws on Genesis 2:18 (LXX): "It is not good for the man to be alone; let us make for him a helper corresponding to him" (<hi type="italic">boethon kat' auton</hi>). The purpose is directional: the woman was made for (<hi type="italic">dia</hi>) the man — to be his helper, his counterpart, the one who answers the problem of his aloneness.</p>
          <p>Paul is reading Genesis 2 as the explanation for the practice he has instructed. The creation order — man first, woman from man and for man — establishes the relational structure that the covering practice expresses. The man's uncovered head displays the image and glory of God. The woman's covered head honors the relationship of derivation and purpose established at creation.</p>
          <p>It is essential to note what Paul does not say. He does not say the woman is inferior. He does not say she is not in God's image (Genesis 1:27 explicitly includes her). He does not say she cannot pray or prophesy. He is making an argument about relational structure — about the visible expression of the creation-order relationship between man and woman — not about capacity, worth, or function in worship. The woman prays and prophesies; the question is the manner in which she does so.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.10">
          <title>Verse 10: The Woman's Authority and the Angels</title>
          <p>"That is why a wife ought to have a symbol of authority on her head, because of the angels" (11:10).</p>
          <p>The Greek reads: <hi type="italic">dia touto opheilei he gyne exousian echein epi tes kephales dia tous angelous</hi> — literally, "because of this the woman ought to have authority on the head because of the angels." This verse is one of the most compressed and difficult in Paul's letters, and every major word requires attention.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">dia touto</hi> ("because of this, for this reason") looks back to the creation argument of verses 7-9. The covering instruction flows from the creation order.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">opheilei</hi> ("ought, is obligated to, is under obligation") indicates moral or relational obligation, not mere recommendation.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">exousian</hi> ("authority") is the accusative of <hi type="italic">exousia</hi>, the same word that appeared in 8:9 ("this right of yours") and dominated chapter 9 (Paul's apostolic "rights"). <hi type="italic">Exousia</hi> means "authority, right, power, freedom, liberty." The Greek text does not say "a sign of authority" or "a symbol of being under authority." It says the woman ought to have <hi type="italic">exousian</hi> — authority — on her head. The most natural reading of the Greek is that this is the woman's own authority. The covering on her head represents or embodies her authority — her authorization to pray and prophesy in the assembly. The covering is not a restriction but an enablement: it is what allows the woman to exercise her prophetic and priestly functions in the gathered community while honoring the relational order established at creation. The ESV's addition of "a symbol of" is an interpretive gloss; the Greek has simply <hi type="italic">exousian</hi>.</p>
          <p>This reading coheres with the fact that Paul has already assumed women pray and prophesy (v. 5). He has not questioned their right to do so. The covering is the means by which their participation is properly ordered. <hi type="italic">Exousia</hi> on the head is the woman's authority to participate, expressed in a form that also honors the <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> relationship of verse 3.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">dia tous angelous</hi> ("because of the angels") introduces a dimension that extends the argument beyond the human community. In first-century Jewish tradition, angels were understood to be present in the community's gathered worship. The Dead Sea Scrolls reflect this belief explicitly. The <hi type="italic">War Scroll</hi> (1QM 7:4-6) excludes those with physical blemishes from the battle camp "because the holy angels are with their armies." The <hi type="italic">Rule of the Congregation</hi> (1QSa 2:8-9) likewise bars those with bodily defects from the assembly of the community "because the angels of holiness are among their congregation." The belief that God's angels attend the worship assembly is not a Qumran peculiarity; it is rooted in the broader Jewish understanding of worship as a meeting point between heaven and earth. Psalm 138:1 (LXX 137:1) says "before the angels I will sing your praise" (<hi type="italic">enantion angelon psalo soi</hi>). Isaiah 6:1-7 depicts the heavenly worship where seraphim surround God's throne. When the community gathers for prayer and prophecy, it joins an assembly that includes heavenly beings. Proper order in worship matters not only because of human propriety but because of the heavenly audience. The angels, as guardians of the created order, observe whether the community's worship practice reflects the order God established.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.11-1Cor.11.12">
          <title>Verses 11-12: Mutual Dependence in the Lord</title>
          <p>"Nevertheless, in the Lord woman is not independent of man nor man of woman; for as woman was made from man, so man is now born of woman. And all things are from God" (11:11-12).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">plen</hi> ("nevertheless, however, but, yet") introduces a qualification that substantially rebalances the creation-order argument of verses 7-9. Paul does not retract what he said, but he prevents it from being pushed to a conclusion of permanent female subordination. The phrase <hi type="italic">en kyrio</hi> ("in the Lord") marks the sphere in which this qualification operates — within the new-creation community that belongs to Christ. Whatever the creation order establishes, the community "in the Lord" operates with a different dynamic: mutual dependence.</p>
          <p>The double negative structure is precise: <hi type="italic">oute gyne choris andros oute aner choris gynaikos en kyrio</hi> — "neither woman apart from man nor man apart from woman in the Lord." The word <hi type="italic">choris</hi> ("apart from, without, separate from, independent of") describes independence, self-sufficiency, the ability to exist or function without the other. Neither possesses this independence in the Lord. The man cannot say he has no need of the woman; the woman cannot say she has no need of the man. This anticipates the body language of chapter 12, where the eye cannot say to the hand "I have no need of you" (12:21).</p>
          <p>Verse 12 provides the reason by means of a chiastic reversal. "As the woman [came] from the man (<hi type="italic">ek tou andros</hi>), so also the man [comes] through the woman (<hi type="italic">dia tes gynaikos</hi>)." Paul takes the creation argument of verse 8 — woman was made from man — and counters it with the ongoing reality of natural reproduction: every man since Adam has come into existence through a woman. The original direction of derivation (woman from man, Genesis 2) is reversed in every subsequent generation (man from woman, in birth). The creation order is real, but it does not establish a permanent unidirectional dependence, because the natural order continually reverses the direction. Woman came from man once; man comes from woman always.</p>
          <p>The final clause — <hi type="italic">ta de panta ek tou theou</hi> ("and all things are from God") — places the entire discussion under divine sovereignty. Both the creation of woman from man and the birth of man from woman derive ultimately from God. God is the source (<hi type="italic">ek</hi>) of all things (<hi type="italic">ta panta</hi>). This phrase echoes 8:6, where Paul confessed "one God, the Father, from whom (<hi type="italic">ex hou</hi>) are all things." The relational structures between men and women are real, but they are secondary to the primary reality that everything comes from God. This prevents any party from absolutizing a relational claim. The man cannot elevate his creation-priority into an autonomous authority, because his authority itself comes from God. The woman cannot dismiss the creation-order argument, because the creation itself comes from God. Both stand under the same divine source.</p>
          <p>The effect of verses 11-12 is to prevent the creation argument of verses 7-9 from becoming a charter for male dominance. Paul affirms the creation order; he also affirms the mutual dependence that characterizes life "in the Lord." The two affirmations coexist. The covering practice expresses the creation order; the mutual dependence expresses the new-creation reality. Neither cancels the other.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.13-1Cor.11.16">
          <title>Verses 13-16: The Appeal to Nature and Universal Practice</title>
          <p>"Judge for yourselves: is it proper for a wife to pray to God with her head uncovered?" (11:13).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">en hymin autois krinate</hi> ("judge among/within yourselves") appeals to the Corinthians' own sense of what is fitting. The verb <hi type="italic">krino</hi> ("to judge, to decide, to evaluate") places the responsibility on the audience. Paul has made his theological argument (creation order, mutual dependence, the presence of angels); now he asks them to apply their own judgment to the question. The word <hi type="italic">prepon</hi> ("proper, fitting, appropriate, becoming") describes what is seemly — what accords with recognized standards of propriety. The appeal is to shared cultural intuition: does it seem right?</p>
          <p>"Does not nature itself teach you that if a man wears long hair it is a disgrace for him, but if a woman has long hair, it is her glory? For her hair is given to her for a covering" (11:14-15).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">physis</hi> ("nature") introduces a different kind of argument. Paul has argued from scripture (Genesis 2); now he argues from <hi type="italic">physis</hi> — the natural order, the way things are. The appeal to <hi type="italic">physis</hi> was common in Greco-Roman moral philosophy. The Stoics frequently invoked "nature" as a guide to proper behavior: what is "according to nature" (<hi type="italic">kata physin</hi>) is right; what is "against nature" (<hi type="italic">para physin</hi>) is wrong. In first-century usage, <hi type="italic">physis</hi> as a moral guide often reflected cultural convention more than biological necessity. What "nature teaches" is what shared experience and common practice confirm as fitting.</p>
          <p>Paul's argument: nature itself (<hi type="italic">he physis aute</hi>) teaches (<hi type="italic">didaskei</hi>) that long hair (<hi type="italic">komao</hi>, "to wear long hair, to let the hair grow") on a man is a dishonor (<hi type="italic">atimia</hi>, "dishonor, shame, degradation"), while long hair on a woman is her glory (<hi type="italic">doxa</hi>). The word <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> reappears from verse 7, where man was the "glory of God" and woman the "glory of man." Now the woman's own hair is her <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> — her visible splendor, her distinguishing beauty. The word <hi type="italic">anti</hi> ("in place of, for, as") in the phrase <hi type="italic">anti peribolaiou</hi> ("for a covering") indicates that the hair functions as a natural covering — it is given (<hi type="italic">dedotai</hi>, perfect passive, indicating divine gift) to her in place of or as a <hi type="italic">peribolaion</hi> ("a covering, a wrap, a garment thrown around"). Nature has already provided the woman with a covering; the practice of wearing an additional covering in worship extends and formalizes what nature has established.</p>
          <p>"If anyone is inclined to be contentious, we have no such practice, nor do the churches of God" (11:16).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">philoneikos</hi> ("contentious, fond of strife, quarrelsome") describes someone who loves to argue. Paul anticipates resistance and cuts it short. The phrase <hi type="italic">hemeis toiauten synetheian ouk echomen</hi> ("we have no such custom/practice") — the word <hi type="italic">synetheia</hi> ("custom, habitual practice") was used in 8:7 to describe the weak person's long familiarity with idols. Here it describes the established practice of the apostolic communities. The phrase <hi type="italic">oude hai ekklesiai tou theou</hi> ("nor the churches of God") expands the appeal beyond Paul's personal authority to the universal practice of all the communities. Whatever the Corinthians might argue in theory, the fact remains that no church of God follows a different practice. The appeal to universal church custom is Paul's final word on the matter. It is not his strongest theological argument, but it is his most pragmatically decisive one: this is what all the churches do.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.17-1Cor.11.22">
          <title>Verses 17-22: The Rebuke — Divisions at the Communal Meal</title>
          <p>"But in the following instructions I do not commend you, because when you come together it is not for the better but for the worse" (11:17).</p>
          <p>The transition from verse 16 to verse 17 is one of the sharpest in the letter. In verse 2, Paul commended (<hi type="italic">epaino</hi>) the Corinthians. Now: <hi type="italic">touto de parangello ouk epaino</hi> — "in giving this instruction I do not commend you." The word <hi type="italic">parangello</hi> ("to instruct, to command, to charge") is stronger than the language of commendation and correction in the first section. Paul is now issuing a charge. And the charge is accompanied by its reason: <hi type="italic">hoti ouk eis to kreisson alla eis to hesson synerchesthe</hi> — "because you come together not for the better but for the worse." The comparative adjectives <hi type="italic">kreisson</hi> ("better") and <hi type="italic">hesson</hi> ("worse") evaluate the outcome of their assemblies. When they gather, the result is not improvement but deterioration. Their coming together makes things worse, not better. The communal assembly, which should be the occasion for building up (<hi type="italic">oikodomeo</hi> — the positive action Paul has advocated since 8:1), has become the occasion for damage.</p>
          <p>"For, in the first place, when you come together as a church, I hear that there are divisions among you. And I believe it in part" (11:18).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">proton men</hi> ("in the first place, first of all") suggests Paul has more than one complaint, though the second is not explicitly marked (some scholars identify vv. 17-34 as the first complaint and 12:1-14:40 as the implied second). The phrase <hi type="italic">synerchomenon hymon en ekklesia</hi> ("when you come together as a church/assembly") uses the verb <hi type="italic">synerchomai</hi> ("to come together, to assemble, to gather") that will recur throughout this section (vv. 20, 33, 34) and later in 14:23, 26. The word <hi type="italic">ekklesia</hi> ("assembly, church, congregation") is the word for the gathered community — the <hi type="italic">qahal</hi> of the LXX, the assembly of God's people. The Corinthians come together as <hi type="italic">ekklesia</hi>, as God's assembled people; but their behavior contradicts their identity.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">schismata</hi> ("divisions, splits, tears") is the same word Paul used in 1:10: "that there be no divisions (<hi type="italic">schismata</hi>) among you." The repetition is not accidental. The divisions Paul first diagnosed in the opening chapters — factionalism around teachers (1:10-12), wisdom-based arrogance (1:18-2:16), inflated self-assessment (4:6-8) — have now produced a visible, physical manifestation in the community's central practice. The <hi type="italic">schismata</hi> are no longer merely intellectual or factional; they are expressed in the distribution of food and drink at the Lord's table. The community that is divided in its loyalties is now divided at its meal.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">apo merous ti pisteuo</hi> ("I believe it in part") may mean Paul believes some of the reports are accurate, or that the divisions are partial (affecting some, not all). Either way, the concession is minor; Paul proceeds to treat the problem as real and severe.</p>
          <p>"For there must be factions among you in order that those who are genuine among you may be recognized" (11:19).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">haireseis</hi> ("factions, parties, sects, choices") is related to <hi type="italic">haireo</hi> ("to choose, to take for oneself") and describes groups that have formed around particular choices or positions. The word <hi type="italic">dei</hi> ("it is necessary, it must be") does not express divine desire but divine permission of a revelatory process. Factions are not good, but they serve a purpose: they expose who is <hi type="italic">dokimos</hi> ("genuine, tested, approved, proven"). The word <hi type="italic">dokimos</hi> comes from the same root as <hi type="italic">dokimazo</hi> ("to test, to examine, to prove") — the verb Paul will use in verse 28 ("let a person examine himself"). In 9:27, Paul feared becoming <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> ("disqualified, rejected, failing the test"). The <hi type="italic">dokimoi</hi> are those who pass the test; the factions reveal who they are by how people behave when the community divides. Those who act with love and integrity in the midst of division are shown to be genuine. Those who use the occasion of gathering to indulge themselves at others' expense are shown to be otherwise.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">phaneroi</hi> ("recognized, manifest, visible, apparent") describes the revelatory function of the factions: the genuineness that was hidden becomes visible. The factions are a trial, and trials reveal character. This is not a justification of division but an explanation of its providential function: God permits what he does not approve because it serves to distinguish the faithful from the unfaithful.</p>
          <p>"When you come together, it is not the Lord's supper that you eat" (11:20).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">kyriakon deipnon</hi> ("the Lord's supper") appears only here in the entire New Testament. The adjective <hi type="italic">kyriakos</hi> ("belonging to the Lord, pertaining to the Lord") derives from <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> ("Lord") and marks the meal as the Lord's property — it belongs to him, it is held under his authority, it is defined by his character and purpose. The word <hi type="italic">deipnon</hi> ("supper, dinner, the main meal") refers to the principal meal of the day in Greco-Roman culture, typically eaten in the evening. The <hi type="italic">kyriakon deipnon</hi> is the meal that belongs to the Lord — the communal meal that the early communities shared as an enactment of their covenant identity in Christ.</p>
          <p>Paul's statement is devastating: <hi type="italic">ouk estin kyriakon deipnon phagein</hi> — "it is not the Lord's supper to eat." The negation is total. What the Corinthians eat when they come together is not the Lord's supper. Not because the bread and cup are absent, but because their behavior has emptied the meal of its identity. A meal at which some gorge themselves and others go hungry cannot be the Lord's supper, because the Lord's supper is defined by the Lord's self-giving (vv. 23-26), and self-indulgence at others' expense is the opposite of self-giving.</p>
          <p>"For in eating, each one goes ahead with his own meal. One goes hungry, another gets drunk" (11:21).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">to idion deipnon</hi> ("his own supper/meal") contrasts sharply with <hi type="italic">kyriakon deipnon</hi> ("the Lord's supper") in the previous verse. Instead of the Lord's meal, each person eats his own meal. The word <hi type="italic">idion</hi> ("one's own, private, personal") marks the privatization of what should be communal. The Lord's supper has been converted into a collection of private meals consumed in the same space.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">hos men peina, hos de methyei</hi> ("one goes hungry, another gets drunk") describes the extremes. The verb <hi type="italic">peinao</hi> ("to be hungry, to go without food") describes real hunger — not mild appetite but the absence of food. The verb <hi type="italic">methyo</hi> ("to be drunk, to be intoxicated") describes real drunkenness. The picture is of a gathering in which the wealthy members, who have arrived early and brought abundant food and wine, have consumed their provisions without waiting for the poorer members — likely slaves, laborers, freedmen, and those of lower social standing who could not leave their work as early. By the time the latecomers arrive, the food is gone and the wine has been drunk. The "communal" meal has replicated the social stratification of Corinthian society: the haves feast while the have-nots go without.</p>
          <p>The social dynamics of Greco-Roman dining illuminate the situation. In a Roman house, dining rooms (<hi type="italic">triclinia</hi>) were typically small, holding perhaps nine to fifteen people on couches. A larger gathering would require some people to eat in the atrium or courtyard, where the food and seating would be inferior. The host's social peers might recline in the <hi type="italic">triclinium</hi> with better food and wine; the lower-status members might receive lesser provisions in a less distinguished space. If the Corinthian assembly met in the house of a wealthy patron, these social conventions would naturally reassert themselves unless deliberately resisted. Paul's complaint suggests that the community has not resisted these conventions. It has allowed the secular social order to shape the Lord's meal.</p>
          <p>"What! Do you not have houses to eat and drink in? Or do you despise the church of God and humiliate those who have nothing? What shall I say to you? Shall I commend you in this? No, I will not" (11:22).</p>
          <p>The exclamation <hi type="italic">ti</hi> ("what!") expresses outrage. Paul asks two rhetorical questions, each more severe than the last.</p>
          <p>First: <hi type="italic">me gar oikias ouk echete eis to esthiein kai pinein</hi>? — "Do you not have houses for eating and drinking?" If you want to eat and drink to satisfy your own appetite, do it at home. The assembly is not the place for private consumption.</p>
          <p>Second: <hi type="italic">e tes ekklesias tou theou kataphroneite kai kataischynete tous me echontas</hi>? — "Or do you despise the church of God and humiliate those who have nothing?" The verb <hi type="italic">kataphroneo</hi> ("to despise, to look down on, to think little of, to treat with contempt") describes active disdain. The phrase <hi type="italic">tes ekklesias tou theou</hi> ("the church of God, the assembly of God") carries the full weight of the community's identity — this is God's assembly, God's own gathered people. To behave as the wealthy Corinthians do at the meal is to despise God's assembly itself. The verb <hi type="italic">kataischyno</hi> ("to put to shame, to humiliate, to disgrace") — the same verb that appeared in verses 4 and 5 regarding the dishonoring of one's head — describes the public humiliation of <hi type="italic">tous me echontas</hi> ("those who do not have, those who have nothing"). The phrase is blunt: the have-nots are shamed by the behavior of the haves. The communal meal, which should express the equality of all members before the Lord, instead reproduces and publicizes the inequalities of the surrounding society.</p>
          <p>The final sentence withdraws the commendation of verse 2: <hi type="italic">en touto ouk epaino</hi> — "in this I do not commend." The contrast is complete. Paul commended them for holding the traditions (v. 2); in their practice of the meal, he cannot commend them at all.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.23-1Cor.11.26">
          <title>Verses 23-26: The Tradition of the Lord's Supper</title>
          <p>"For I received from the Lord what I also delivered to you, that the Lord Jesus on the night when he was betrayed took bread, and when he had given thanks, he broke it, and said, 'This is my body, which is for you. Do this in remembrance of me.' In the same way also he took the cup, after supper, saying, 'This cup is the new covenant in my blood. Do this, as often as you drink it, in remembrance of me.' For as often as you eat this bread and drink the cup, you proclaim the Lord's death until he comes" (11:23-26).</p>
          <p>Paul now sets the community's abusive practice against the tradition he delivered to them. The rebuke gains its force by juxtaposition: this is what the meal means (vv. 23-26); that is what you have made of it (vv. 20-22).</p>
          <p>The paired verbs <hi type="italic">parelabon</hi> ("I received") and <hi type="italic">paredoka</hi> ("I delivered") are the technical terms for authoritative transmission, the same vocabulary used in verse 2 (<hi type="italic">paradosis/paradidomi</hi>) and in 15:3 for the gospel tradition itself. The phrase <hi type="italic">apo tou kyriou</hi> ("from the Lord") identifies the ultimate source of the tradition. Paul received this not as human invention but as tradition originating with the Lord himself, transmitted through the chain of witnesses. The authority behind the meal tradition is the Lord's own authority.</p>
          <p>The temporal marker <hi type="italic">en te nykti he paredideto</hi> ("on the night when he was betrayed/handed over") anchors the tradition in a specific historical moment. The verb <hi type="italic">paradidomi</hi> appears again, but now in its other sense: "to hand over, to betray, to deliver up." The same verb that describes the transmission of teaching (I delivered to you) also describes the betrayal of Jesus (the night he was handed over). The word carries both meanings simultaneously. The teaching was delivered; the teacher was delivered up. The supper tradition comes from the night of ultimate vulnerability.</p>
          <p>The actions: <hi type="italic">elaben arton</hi> ("he took bread"), <hi type="italic">eucharistesas eklasen</hi> ("having given thanks he broke it"). The verb <hi type="italic">eucharisteo</hi> ("to give thanks") is the origin of the term "eucharist" in later usage; in the first century it simply means "to give thanks." The breaking of bread (<hi type="italic">klao</hi>) was the customary action of the host at a Jewish meal — the head of the household broke the bread and distributed it. Jesus performs the host's role.</p>
          <p>The words over the bread: <hi type="italic">touto mou estin to soma to hyper hymon</hi> — "this is my body, which is for you." The word <hi type="italic">soma</hi> ("body") is the same word that has appeared throughout the letter with reference to the physical body (6:13-20, the body as temple) and will appear with reference to the communal body (12:12-27, the body of Christ). Jesus identifies the bread with his body — the body that will be given over in death. The phrase <hi type="italic">to hyper hymon</hi> ("which is for you") specifies the beneficiaries: you, the community, the people gathered at this table. Christ's body is given for them. This echoes 8:11: "the brother for whom Christ died." The death that the meal commemorates was for specific people — the people now eating the bread.</p>
          <p>The command: <hi type="italic">touto poieite eis ten emen anamnesin</hi> — "do this in remembrance of me." The word <hi type="italic">anamnesis</hi> ("remembrance, memorial, act of remembering") carries more weight than the modern English word "remembering" suggests. In the LXX, the concept of <hi type="italic">zikkaron/anamnesis</hi> describes a remembrance that makes the past event present and effective. The Passover is the paradigmatic example. In Exodus 12:14 (LXX), the Passover day "shall be to you a memorial (<hi type="italic">mnemosynon</hi>), and you shall keep it as a feast to the Lord throughout your generations." Israel does not merely think about the exodus at Passover; it reenacts the event, eats the meal, participates in the deliverance. The participants become present to the event, and the event becomes present to the participants. The <hi type="italic">anamnesis</hi> of the Lord's supper functions similarly: the community does not merely recall that Jesus died; it reenacts the meal, eats the bread, drinks the cup, and thereby participates in the death and its significance. The remembrance is performative, not merely cognitive.</p>
          <p>The cup: <hi type="italic">to poterion meta to deipnesai</hi> ("the cup after supper") — the cup comes after the meal (<hi type="italic">deipnesai</hi>, aorist infinitive of <hi type="italic">deipneo</hi>, "to eat supper"), indicating that in the original practice the bread and cup framed a full meal, with the bread at the beginning and the cup at the conclusion. The words over the cup: <hi type="italic">touto to poterion he kaine diatheke estin en to emo haimati</hi> — "this cup is the new covenant in my blood."</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">kaine diatheke</hi> ("new covenant") reaches directly to Jeremiah 31:31-34 (LXX 38:31-34), one of the most significant prophetic texts in the Jewish scriptures:</p>
          <p>"Behold, the days are coming, declares the Lord, when I will make a new covenant (<hi type="italic">diatheken kainen</hi>) with the house of Israel and the house of Judah, not like the covenant that I made with their fathers on the day when I took them by the hand to bring them out of the land of Egypt, my covenant that they broke, though I was their husband, declares the Lord. For this is the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel after those days, declares the Lord: I will put my law within them, and I will write it on their hearts. And I will be their God, and they shall be my people."</p>
          <p>The old covenant was the Sinai covenant, sealed with blood. In Exodus 24:6-8, Moses took the blood of the sacrificed oxen, threw half of it against the altar, read the book of the covenant to the people, and then threw the other half of the blood on the people, saying: "Behold the blood of the covenant (<hi type="italic">to haima tes diathekes</hi>) that the Lord has made with you in accordance with all these words." The new covenant is likewise sealed with blood — but not the blood of animals. It is sealed <hi type="italic">en to emo haimati</hi> — "in my blood." Jesus' blood is the ratification of the new covenant that Jeremiah prophesied. The cup, by symbolizing this blood, is a participation in the covenant itself.</p>
          <p>This connects to Paul's <hi type="italic">koinonia</hi> argument in 10:16: "the cup of blessing that we bless, is it not a participation (<hi type="italic">koinonia</hi>) in the blood of Christ?" The meal is covenant participation. When the community drinks the cup, it participates in the covenant that Christ's blood ratified. This is why the community's behavior at the meal matters so intensely: they are not eating a casual dinner; they are enacting a covenant. And the character of the covenant — established by self-giving love, sealed by the blood of one who died for others — is directly contradicted by the selfish behavior Paul described in verses 20-22.</p>
          <p>Verse 26: <hi type="italic">hosaki gar ean esthiete ton arton touton kai to poterion pinete, ton thanaton tou kyriou katangellete achri hou elthe</hi> — "for as often as you eat this bread and drink the cup, you proclaim the Lord's death until he comes."</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">katangello</hi> ("to proclaim, to announce, to declare") identifies the meal as an act of proclamation. Every eating and drinking is a proclamation (<hi type="italic">katangellete</hi> — present active indicative, "you proclaim") of the Lord's death. The meal is not silent consumption; it is public testimony. Each communal enactment of the supper announces to the community and the world that the Lord died — that his death has meaning, that it established a covenant, that it was "for you."</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">achri hou elthe</hi> ("until he comes") places the meal within an eschatological frame. The meal looks backward to the death (<hi type="italic">ton thanaton tou kyriou</hi>) and forward to the coming (<hi type="italic">achri hou elthe</hi> — the aorist subjunctive of <hi type="italic">erchomai</hi>, pointing to a future event). Between these two christological events — the cross and the <hi type="italic">parousia</hi> — the community practices the meal as its proclamation. The meal is bounded by the death that establishes the covenant and the coming that consummates it. This eschatological awareness echoes 7:29-31, where Paul said "the appointed time has grown short" and "the present form of this world is passing away." The community lives between the ages, and its meal practice is a visible expression of that between-the-ages existence: remembering the death, anticipating the coming.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.27-1Cor.11.29">
          <title>Verses 27-29: Unworthy Eating and the Failure to Discern the Body</title>
          <p>"Whoever, therefore, eats the bread or drinks the cup of the Lord in an unworthy manner will be guilty concerning the body and blood of the Lord" (11:27).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">hoste</hi> ("therefore, so that, consequently") draws the application from the tradition just recited. Because the meal has this meaning (vv. 23-26), the manner of eating carries this consequence.</p>
          <p>The adverb <hi type="italic">anaxios</hi> ("unworthily, in an unworthy manner") modifies the verb — it describes how the person eats and drinks, not what the person is. The question is not whether the eater is a "worthy" person but whether the eating is done in a manner worthy of the meal's meaning. Given the context, the "unworthy manner" is specifically the behavior Paul described in verses 20-22: eating one's own meal, going ahead without waiting for others, gorging while others go hungry, getting drunk while others have nothing. This is the manner that is <hi type="italic">anaxios</hi> — unworthy of a meal that proclaims the self-giving death of the Lord.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">enochos estai tou somatos kai tou haimatos tou kyriou</hi> — "will be guilty concerning the body and blood of the Lord." The adjective <hi type="italic">enochos</hi> ("guilty, liable, answerable, held accountable") with the genitive indicates the charge or the matter with respect to which one is held guilty. To eat the Lord's supper unworthily is to incur guilt with respect to the Lord's body and blood — that is, to profane the death that the bread and cup represent. The person who turns the memorial of self-sacrificial love into an occasion for selfish indulgence does not merely violate social etiquette; they profane the Lord's death itself. They treat with contempt the body that was given "for you" and the blood that sealed the new covenant. The guilt is not against the other community members (though that too is real, per v. 22); it is against the Lord whose death the meal commemorates.</p>
          <p>"Let a person examine himself, then, and so eat of the bread and drink of the cup" (11:28).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">dokimazeto</hi> ("let him examine, let him test, let him prove") is the imperative of <hi type="italic">dokimazo</hi>, the verb from the same root as <hi type="italic">dokimos</hi> ("genuine, tested") in verse 19 and <hi type="italic">adokimos</hi> ("disqualified") in 9:27. The examination Paul calls for is not a general moral inventory; it is specifically a testing of whether one's manner of eating is worthy of the meal. The self-examination concerns the social behavior at the table: Am I eating in a way that treats the community as the body of Christ? Am I waiting for others? Am I sharing rather than hoarding? Am I treating those who have nothing with dignity rather than contempt?</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">kai houtos</hi> ("and so, and in this way, and thus") indicates that the eating should follow the examination: examine yourself, and then eat. The eating is not prohibited; it is conditioned on prior self-examination. Paul does not say "do not eat" but "examine yourself and so eat." The meal continues; the manner of eating must change.</p>
          <p>"For anyone who eats and drinks without discerning the body eats and drinks judgment on himself" (11:29).</p>
          <p>The participle <hi type="italic">me diakrinon to soma</hi> ("not discerning the body, not distinguishing the body") uses the verb <hi type="italic">diakrino</hi> ("to distinguish, to discern, to judge, to differentiate"). The word <hi type="italic">soma</hi> ("body") appears without the qualifier "of the Lord" that accompanied it in verse 27. This is significant. In verse 27, "the body and blood of the Lord" clearly referred to the bread and cup as representations of Christ's sacrificial death. In verse 29, the unmodified "body" opens a second dimension of meaning.</p>
          <p>Throughout this letter, Paul has been building toward an identification of the community with the body of Christ. In 6:15: "Do you not know that your bodies are members of Christ?" In 10:17: "Because there is one bread, we who are many are one body, for we all partake of the one bread." In 12:12-13: "For just as the body is one and has many members... so it is with Christ. For in one Spirit we were all baptized into one body." The "body" that must be discerned in 11:29 includes the communal body — the assembled community as the body of Christ. To eat "without discerning the body" is to eat without recognizing that the people sitting around the table — including the hungry ones, the have-nots, those of lower social status — are the body of Christ. The failure to discern the body is precisely the failure that produced the abuse: the wealthy ate without seeing the poor as fellow members of Christ's body. They saw social inferiors where they should have seen the body of the Lord.</p>
          <p>The consequence of eating without discerning the body: <hi type="italic">krima heauto esthiei kai pinei</hi> — "he eats and drinks judgment on himself." The word <hi type="italic">krima</hi> ("judgment, verdict, condemnation, sentence") describes a judicial decision that goes against the person. The judgment is not merely future or hypothetical; as the next verse will show, it is already in effect. The person who eats without discerning the body consumes judgment along with the bread. The meal that should be a participation in the covenant becomes a participation in judgment.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.30-1Cor.11.32">
          <title>Verses 30-32: Present Judgment, Discipline, and the Risk of Condemnation</title>
          <p>"That is why many of you are weak and ill, and some have died" (11:30).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">dia touto</hi> ("because of this, for this reason") connects the Corinthians' current physical suffering directly to their abuse of the communal meal. The connection is not metaphorical or distant. Paul identifies a causal relationship: because you eat and drink judgment on yourselves (v. 29), many of you are weak (<hi type="italic">astheneis</hi> — "weak, feeble, without strength," the same word used of the "weak" brother in chapter 8), and ill (<hi type="italic">arrostoi</hi> — "sick, ailing, infirm"), and some have fallen asleep (<hi type="italic">koimontai</hi> — "have died," using the common early Christian euphemism for death; the verb <hi type="italic">koimao</hi>, "to fall asleep," will appear prominently in 15:6, 18, 20, 51 in the discussion of resurrection). Paul attributes physical sickness and actual death within the Corinthian community to their abuse of the Lord's supper.</p>
          <p>This is not a general theological statement about suffering. Paul identifies specific suffering within this specific community and traces it to a specific cause: their failure to discern the body when they eat the Lord's meal. The judgment of verse 29 is not waiting for the eschaton; it is already operative. God has already responded to the community's abuse with concrete physical consequences. The Corinthians can look around their assembly and see the evidence.</p>
          <p>"But if we judged ourselves truly, we would not be judged" (11:31).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">diekrinomen</hi> ("if we were discerning/judging ourselves") uses the same root as <hi type="italic">diakrinon</hi> ("discerning") in verse 29. The verb <hi type="italic">diakrino</hi> can mean "to discern, to distinguish, to judge, to examine." There is a wordplay linking the two verses: if we <hi type="italic">diakrinomen</hi> (discerned/judged) ourselves, we would not be <hi type="italic">krinometha</hi> (judged). Self-discernment prevents divine judgment. The conditional is contrary-to-fact in the present: the Corinthians are not judging themselves, and therefore they are being judged. If they were to examine their own behavior at the meal, if they were to discern the body in their midst, the divine judgment (weakness, illness, death) would not be necessary.</p>
          <p>"But when we are judged by the Lord, we are disciplined so that we may not be condemned along with the world" (11:32).</p>
          <p>This verse is one of the most theologically significant in the chapter. Paul distinguishes three tiers of divine response:</p>
          <p>First, self-judgment (v. 31): the community examines itself, corrects its behavior, and no further action is needed. This is the ideal.</p>
          <p>Second, discipline by the Lord (v. 32a): <hi type="italic">krinomenoi de hypo [tou] kyriou paideuometha</hi> — "being judged by the Lord, we are disciplined." The verb <hi type="italic">paideuo</hi> ("to discipline, to train, to educate, to correct, to chastise") is the word for parental and pedagogical correction. In the LXX, God disciplines Israel as a father disciplines a son. Deuteronomy 8:5: "Know then in your heart that, as a man disciplines (<hi type="italic">paideusei</hi>) his son, so the Lord your God disciplines (<hi type="italic">paideusei</hi>) you." Proverbs 3:11-12 (LXX): "My son, do not despise the Lord's discipline (<hi type="italic">paideias kyriou</hi>), nor grow weary when corrected (<hi type="italic">elenchomenos</hi>) by him. For the Lord disciplines (<hi type="italic">paideuei</hi>) the one he loves, and chastises every son he receives." The discipline is painful — people are sick and dying (v. 30) — but its character is parental, not annihilatory. It is corrective, aimed at producing change, not destructive, aimed at elimination.</p>
          <p>Third, condemnation with the world (v. 32b): <hi type="italic">hina me syn to kosmo katakrithomen</hi> — "so that we may not be condemned with the world." The verb <hi type="italic">katakrino</hi> ("to condemn, to pass judgment against, to sentence") describes the definitive judicial verdict. The phrase <hi type="italic">syn to kosmo</hi> ("with the world") identifies the company the Corinthians would keep if this condemnation fell: they would share the fate of the <hi type="italic">kosmos</hi> — the world that is under judgment, the world that stands outside the covenant community. The <hi type="italic">hina me</hi> ("so that not, in order that not") clause states the purpose of the discipline: the Lord disciplines so that the community may not be condemned along with the world.</p>
          <p>The structure reveals both assurance and danger. The assurance: God's current judgments (weakness, illness, even death) are discipline, not condemnation. They are the actions of a father correcting his children, not a judge sentencing criminals. The purpose of the discipline is to prevent the community from reaching the point of final condemnation. God intervenes with painful correction precisely because he does not want his people condemned with the world.</p>
          <p>The danger: the possibility of condemnation with the world is real. The <hi type="italic">hina me</hi> clause presupposes that, without the discipline, condemnation is the trajectory. The Corinthians' abuse of the meal is not a minor social faux pas that God overlooks. It is behavior that, if uncorrected, leads to the same verdict the world receives. The community is not automatically exempt from the world's fate. Insiders can be condemned with outsiders if their behavior contradicts their identity. This is the same logic that governed the Israel analogy in 10:1-12: Israel received all the divine gifts (baptism, spiritual food, spiritual drink) and yet was overthrown in the wilderness. The gifts did not guarantee the outcome. The Corinthians have received the Lord's supper, the covenant meal, the proclamation of Christ's death — but receiving these gifts while treating the body of Christ with contempt places them on the trajectory of condemnation.</p>
          <p>This verse connects to the chain of warnings that runs through the letter. In 3:17: "If anyone destroys God's temple, God will destroy him." In 5:5: the incestuous man is delivered to Satan "for the destruction of the flesh, so that his spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord." In 8:11: the weak brother is "being destroyed" by the knowledgeable person's careless freedom. In 9:27: Paul himself could be "disqualified" (<hi type="italic">adokimos</hi>). In 10:5: "with most of them God was not pleased, for they were overthrown in the wilderness." In 10:12: "let anyone who thinks that he stands take heed lest he fall." Now in 11:32: discipline is administered so that the community may not be condemned with the world. Each passage acknowledges the genuine possibility that those who belong to the community can suffer real spiritual consequences — including, at the extreme, sharing the world's condemnation. The letter never treats belonging to the community as an irrevocable guarantee against all possible loss. The gifts are real; the risks are also real. The discipline is God's merciful intervention to keep the community from reaching the point of no return.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">paideuo</hi> ("discipline") in verse 32 also anticipates the language Paul will use in other contexts for the formative work of God in the community's life. The discipline is not punishment for its own sake but training for maturity. The father who disciplines his son does so because the son's behavior, if uncorrected, will lead to ruin. The correction is an act of love, not an act of abandonment. God has not abandoned the Corinthians to the consequences of their meal-abuse. He has intervened — painfully, physically — to redirect them. The sickness and death in the community are signs of God's continued engagement, not signs of his withdrawal.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11.33-1Cor.11.34">
          <title>Verses 33-34: The Practical Solution</title>
          <p>"So then, my brothers, when you come together to eat, wait for one another — if anyone is hungry, let him eat at home — so that when you come together it will not be for judgment" (11:33-34).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">hoste, adelphoi mou</hi> ("so then, my brothers") draws the practical conclusion. The address <hi type="italic">adelphoi mou</hi> ("my brothers") reestablishes the familial bond after the severity of the rebuke. Paul's practical instruction is strikingly simple: <hi type="italic">allelous ekdechesthe</hi> — "wait for one another." The verb <hi type="italic">ekdechomai</hi> ("to wait for, to expect, to receive, to welcome") describes the act of waiting until all members have arrived before beginning the meal. The solution to the abuse of the Lord's supper is not a new theology of the meal but a new social practice: wait for the people who arrive late. The wealthy members who can arrive early must not begin eating until the laborers, slaves, and working poor have had time to arrive. The meal must be a communal act, not a staggered sequence of private meals.</p>
          <p>The parenthetical instruction — <hi type="italic">ei tis peina, en oiko esthieto</hi> ("if anyone is hungry, let him eat at home") — addresses the practical difficulty. If the wealthy cannot wait because they are too hungry, they should satisfy their hunger at home before coming to the assembly. The communal meal is not the place for satisfying private appetites. It is the place for communal covenant enactment. Private hunger belongs at home; the Lord's supper belongs to the community.</p>
          <p>The purpose: <hi type="italic">hina me eis krima synerchesthe</hi> — "so that you may not come together for judgment." The word <hi type="italic">krima</hi> ("judgment") echoes verse 29 (judgment on himself) and verse 34 (this occurrence). The assemblies that should be "for the better" (v. 17) have been "for the worse" — indeed, "for judgment." Paul's practical instruction aims to reverse this: come together in a way that avoids judgment. Wait for one another. Eat at home if necessary. Treat the assembly as what it is — the gathering of the body of Christ for the Lord's supper — and behave accordingly.</p>
          <p>The final phrase — <hi type="italic">ta de loipa hos an eltho diataxomai</hi> — "about the other things I will give directions when I come" — indicates that Paul has additional instructions about the meal that he will deliver in person. The letter has addressed the most urgent problems; the remaining matters can wait for his personal visit. This suggests that the issues Paul has addressed in verses 17-34 are not minor adjustments but urgent corrections that could not wait for his arrival.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.11">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 11 addresses two problems in the community's gathered worship, both of which concern the same fundamental question: does the community's practice when it assembles reflect its identity as the body of Christ?</p>
          <p>The chapter divides into three units. The first (v. 1) concludes the idol-food argument of chapters 8-10, capping Paul's demonstration of voluntary self-limitation with the command to imitate him as he imitates Christ. The second (vv. 2-16) addresses conduct during prayer and prophecy, specifically the question of head coverings. The third (vv. 17-34) addresses conduct at the communal meal, specifically the divisions that have corrupted the Lord's supper.</p>
          <p>The two worship-practice sections are linked by the contrast between commendation and rebuke. Paul commends the Corinthians for maintaining the traditions he delivered (v. 2), then corrects a specific failure in their application (vv. 3-16). He then explicitly withdraws commendation for their meal practice (v. 17), rebukes their divisions (vv. 18-22), recites the tradition they are violating (vv. 23-26), and warns of the consequences (vv. 27-34). The movement from commendation to correction to rebuke to warning traces a downward arc: the Corinthians are doing some things adequately and other things catastrophically.</p>
          <p>Both sections also share the theme of honor and shame. In the head-covering section, the verbs <hi type="italic">kataischyno</hi> ("to dishonor, to put to shame") and the nouns <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> ("glory") and <hi type="italic">atimia</hi> ("dishonor") dominate. The man who covers his head dishonors his <hi type="italic">kephale</hi>; the woman who prays uncovered dishonors her <hi type="italic">kephale</hi>. In the meal section, the verb <hi type="italic">kataischyno</hi> reappears (v. 22): the wealthy "humiliate those who have nothing." In both cases, the issue is whether the community's worship practice honors or shames the relationships that constitute it — the relationship between men and women, the relationship between Christ and the community, the relationship between wealthy and poor members.</p>
          <p>The head-covering section (vv. 2-16) draws its argument from multiple sources: the <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> relationship (v. 3), the creation narrative of Genesis 2 (vv. 7-9), the presence of angels in worship (v. 10), the mutual dependence of men and women in the Lord (vv. 11-12), the teaching of "nature" (vv. 14-15), and the universal practice of the churches (v. 16). No single argument carries the full weight. Paul marshals a variety of considerations — theological, scriptural, natural, ecclesial — to support a practice that he considers important for the community's ordered worship. The most theologically significant move is the qualification of verses 11-12, where Paul ensures that the creation-order argument does not become a charter for male autonomy: "in the Lord, woman is not independent of man nor man of woman." The mutual dependence within the new-creation community qualifies the creation-order hierarchy without abolishing it. The covering practice honors the creation order; the mutual dependence prevents the creation order from becoming oppressive.</p>
          <p>The meal section (vv. 17-34) is the more urgent of the two and carries the heavier theological freight. The core problem is that the community's social divisions have corrupted the Lord's supper, transforming it from a communal covenant meal into a collection of private meals that replicate Corinthian social stratification. The wealthy eat and drink to excess while the poor go hungry. Paul's response has three layers.</p>
          <p>First, he identifies the behavior as a contradiction of the meal's meaning. The supper recited in verses 23-26 — bread broken "for you," cup sealing the "new covenant in my blood," proclamation of "the Lord's death until he comes" — is a meal defined by self-giving love and covenant solidarity. The behavior described in verses 20-22 — each eating his own meal, one going hungry while another gets drunk — is the opposite of self-giving love and covenant solidarity. The two cannot coexist. The behavior has emptied the meal of its identity: "it is not the Lord's supper that you eat" (v. 20).</p>
          <p>Second, Paul identifies the consequence: judgment. Eating without discerning the body brings <hi type="italic">krima</hi> (v. 29). This judgment is already operative: weakness, illness, death within the community (v. 30). The judgment is not arbitrary divine punishment but the natural consequence of profaning the covenant meal. The community that eats the Lord's supper while treating members of the Lord's body with contempt has turned the meal into its own condemnation.</p>
          <p>Third, Paul identifies the purpose of the judgment: discipline. The weakness, illness, and death are not final condemnation but corrective discipline (<hi type="italic">paideia</hi>) — the Lord's parental correction aimed at preventing the community from being condemned with the world (v. 32). God disciplines because he will not abandon. The discipline is severe — people are dying — but its aim is restorative. It is the same logic as 5:5, where the incestuous man was delivered to Satan "for the destruction of the flesh, so that his spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord." Drastic action now prevents ultimate loss later. The destruction is real; the purpose is salvation.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">soma</hi> ("body") serves as the chapter's central term, and its multiple meanings create the chapter's deepest connections. The body of Christ is the bread (v. 24, "this is my body"). The body of Christ is the community (v. 29, "not discerning the body," in the context of social abuse; 10:17, "we who are many are one body"; and 12:12-27, the full development of the metaphor). To eat the bread without recognizing the community is to miss what the bread means. The bread is Christ's body given "for you" — for the people at the table. If the people at the table are being treated with contempt, the bread's meaning has been denied. The physical body of Christ (given in death) and the communal body of Christ (the assembled <hi type="italic">ekklesia</hi>) are inseparable. To dishonor one is to dishonor the other. The wealthy Corinthians who eat without waiting for the poor are guilty concerning the body and blood of the Lord (v. 27) precisely because they have failed to discern the body (v. 29) — the body that is the community itself.</p>
          <p>The chapter also advances the letter's sustained argument about the consequences of belonging to the community. Membership in the body of Christ is not a status that grants immunity from judgment. It is a relationship that creates obligations, and the failure to meet those obligations produces real consequences — up to and including death (v. 30) and, potentially, condemnation with the world (v. 32). The discipline that falls on the community is evidence of God's continued engagement with his people, not evidence of his indifference. But the possibility of being condemned with the world is not removed by membership; it is the danger that discipline is designed to prevent. The Corinthians' abuse of the Lord's supper places them in a position where, without divine corrective intervention, they could share the world's fate. The gifts of the covenant — the bread, the cup, the new covenant in Christ's blood — do not function as automatic protections. They must be received worthily, which means they must be received in a manner consistent with their meaning: self-giving love, covenant solidarity, the recognition of every member as part of Christ's body.</p>
          <p>This is the same principle that governed the Israel analogy of 10:1-12. Israel received the gifts — the cloud, the sea, the spiritual food, the spiritual drink from the Rock. And yet they were overthrown. Their privileges did not prevent their destruction. The Corinthians receive the gifts — the bread, the cup, the new covenant. And they too can be overthrown if their behavior contradicts what the gifts mean. The lesson from Israel's history is not abstract; it is being enacted in real time in the Corinthian community. People are already weak, already sick, already dying. The judgment has begun. The question is whether the community will respond to the discipline by correcting its behavior — or whether it will persist in its divisions and move toward the condemnation that the discipline is designed to prevent.</p>
          <p>Paul's practical solution is disarmingly simple: wait for one another (v. 33). The theological weight of the chapter — <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> relationships, the creation order, the supper tradition, covenant blood, the discernment of the body, divine discipline — resolves into an instruction of elementary social decency. Wait for the people who arrive late. Share the meal. Do not eat while others go hungry. The profundity of the theology and the simplicity of the instruction are not in tension; they are connected. The deepest theological truths about Christ's body given for others, about the new covenant sealed in blood, about the community as the body of Christ — all of these truths find their concrete expression in the act of waiting for a fellow member to arrive before you begin eating. The theology is not decoration on top of the ethics; the ethics is the theology lived out. To wait for one another is to discern the body. To discern the body is to avoid judgment. To avoid judgment is to remain on the path of salvation rather than the path of condemnation with the world.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.12">
        <title>1 Corinthians 12 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 11):</hi> Chapter 11 addressed two problems in the community's gathered worship. The first section (11:2-16) concerned conduct during prayer and prophecy — specifically, whether men and women should cover or uncover their heads when they pray or prophesy in the assembly. Paul grounded his instruction in a chain of <hi type="italic">kephale</hi> ("head") relationships (Christ-man, man-woman, God-Christ), the creation narrative of Genesis 2, the presence of angels in worship, and the mutual dependence of men and women "in the Lord" (11:11-12). The second section (11:17-34) addressed the communal meal — the <hi type="italic">kyriakon deipnon</hi> ("Lord's supper") — which had been corrupted by the community's social divisions. The wealthy ate and drank to excess before the poor arrived, turning the Lord's meal into a collection of private meals. Paul recited the supper tradition he had received and delivered (11:23-26): the bread that is Christ's body "for you," the cup that is the new covenant in Christ's blood, the proclamation of the Lord's death "until he comes." He then warned that eating and drinking "without discerning the body" (<hi type="italic">me diakrinon to soma</hi>, 11:29) brings judgment — and that this judgment was already operative in the community: "many of you are weak and ill, and some have died" (11:30). The judgment was discipline (<hi type="italic">paideia</hi>), not final condemnation — "we are disciplined so that we may not be condemned with the world" (11:32). The chapter closed with the simple practical instruction: "when you come together to eat, wait for one another" (11:33). The word <hi type="italic">schismata</hi> ("divisions") in 11:18 reached back to 1:10, where Paul first named the Corinthian disease; and the unqualified word <hi type="italic">soma</hi> ("body") in 11:29 pointed forward to what chapter 12 will develop at length — the community as the body of Christ.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 13):</hi> Paul introduces what he calls "a still more excellent way" (<hi type="italic">kath' hyperbolen hodon</hi>, 12:31b), which turns out to be love (<hi type="italic">agape</hi>). Chapter 13 is not a freestanding poem but the centerpiece of the argument that runs from chapters 12 through 14 about the proper exercise of spiritual gifts in the assembly. Paul opens by identifying what the gifts become without love: tongues without love are "a noisy gong or a clanging cymbal" (13:1); prophecy, knowledge of all mysteries, and faith that moves mountains are "nothing" without love (13:2); even self-sacrifice is "of no gain" without love (13:3). He then describes love's character — patient, kind, not envious, not boastful, not arrogant, not rude, not insistent on its own way, not irritable, not resentful (13:4-6). The behavioral portrait reads as the direct opposite of the Corinthian conduct Paul has been rebuking: the arrogance of the knowledge-party (8:1-2), the insistence on one's own rights (chapters 8-10), the rudeness at the meal table (11:20-22), and the self-promotion through spectacular gifts that chapter 12 addresses. Paul then declares that the gifts will cease — prophecy, tongues, knowledge will all pass away (13:8) — but love will never end (13:8). The community now sees "in a mirror dimly" but will one day see "face to face" (13:12). Three things abide: faith, hope, and love, "but the greatest of these is love" (13:13). Chapter 13 thus provides the governing disposition for the exercise of the gifts described in chapter 12 and the specific instructions of chapter 14. Without love, the gifts are worse than useless; with love, the gifts serve the community's building up.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 12 connects these:</hi> The transition from chapter 11 to chapter 12 is both a shift and a continuation. The shift is marked by the formula <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> ("now concerning"), which Paul has used throughout the letter to introduce topics raised by the Corinthians' own letter to him (7:1, "now concerning the matters about which you wrote"; 7:25, "now concerning the betrothed"; 8:1, "now concerning food offered to idols"). Each <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> introduces the Corinthians' question, which Paul then answers on his own terms. The <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> of 12:1 signals that the Corinthians have asked about <hi type="italic">ton pneumatikon</hi> — "the spiritual things" or "the spiritual persons" (the word is ambiguous) — and Paul is now taking up their question.</p>
          <p>The continuation runs deeper than the shift. Chapter 11 ended with the problem of the body not being discerned. The wealthy treated the poor as dispensable at the meal; they ate without recognizing that the people at the table were the body of Christ. Chapter 12 now develops the full theology of what "the body" is and why every member matters. The word <hi type="italic">soma</hi> ("body") appeared in 11:24 (the bread as Christ's body), 11:27 (guilt concerning the body and blood of the Lord), and 11:29 (not discerning the body). In chapter 12, <hi type="italic">soma</hi> will appear seventeen times, becoming the chapter's dominant image and the vehicle for Paul's most sustained treatment of the community's identity. The failure to "discern the body" in 11:29 is precisely the failure that chapter 12 is designed to cure: the failure to recognize that the community is one body composed of many members, each essential, none dispensable.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">schisma</hi> also bridges the chapters. In 1:10, Paul urged that there be "no divisions (<hi type="italic">schismata</hi>) among you." In 11:18, he said "I hear that there are divisions (<hi type="italic">schismata</hi>) among you" when the community assembles. In 12:25, God has composed the body "so that there may be no division (<hi type="italic">schisma</hi>) in the body." The same word that diagnosed the disease in the opening chapter and identified its symptom at the meal now appears as the thing God's own design of the body is intended to prevent. The body metaphor of chapter 12 is Paul's most comprehensive response to the <hi type="italic">schismata</hi> that have plagued the community from the beginning of the letter.</p>
          <p>The connection forward to chapter 13 is explicit: 12:31b announces "I will show you a still more excellent way," and chapter 13 delivers on that announcement. But the connection is also structural. Chapter 12 establishes that the gifts are diverse and that every member of the body has a function. Chapter 13 establishes the disposition (love) that must govern the exercise of every gift. Chapter 14 then applies these principles to the specific situation in the Corinthian assembly, where tongues and prophecy are the contested gifts. The three chapters form a single argument: diversity of gifts (12), governed by love (13), applied to the assembly's practice (14).</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.1">
          <title>Verse 1: The Corinthians' Question Introduced</title>
          <p>"Now concerning spiritual gifts, brothers, I do not want you to be uninformed" (12:1).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">peri de ton pneumatikon</hi> ("now concerning the spiritual [things/persons]") uses the genitive plural of the adjective <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi>, which can be either neuter (<hi type="italic">pneumatika</hi>, "spiritual things/gifts/manifestations") or masculine (<hi type="italic">pneumatikoi</hi>, "spiritual persons"). The ambiguity may be deliberate. If the Corinthians asked about "the spiritual ones" — that is, which people count as truly spiritual, or what marks a genuinely spiritual person — Paul's answer reframes the question entirely. The word <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi> has appeared earlier in the letter with significant freight. In 2:13-15, Paul distinguished between the <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi> ("spiritual person") who "judges all things" and the <hi type="italic">psychikos</hi> ("natural/unspiritual person") who "does not accept the things of the Spirit of God." In 3:1, Paul told the Corinthians: "I could not address you as spiritual people (<hi type="italic">pneumatikois</hi>) but as people of the flesh (<hi type="italic">sarkinois</hi>), as infants in Christ." The Corinthians apparently valued the designation <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi> and may have used it to rank themselves: those with spectacular gifts (especially tongues) were "spiritual," while those without them were not. If this is the background, Paul's answer in chapter 12 demolishes the ranking: every genuine member has the Spirit (12:3), every member receives a manifestation (12:7), and the Spirit distributes gifts "to each one individually as he wills" (12:11). There are no Spirit-less members in the body.</p>
          <p>Paul will redirect the Corinthians' vocabulary in verse 4, replacing <hi type="italic">pneumatikon</hi> with <hi type="italic">charismaton</hi> ("gifts of grace"). The shift is subtle but important. <hi type="italic">Pneumatikon</hi> emphasizes the Spirit as the source; <hi type="italic">charisma</hi> emphasizes <hi type="italic">charis</hi> ("grace") as the character of the gift. What the Corinthians call "spiritual things" Paul reframes as "grace-gifts" — things given freely, not earned or merited, not grounds for boasting or ranking.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ou thelo hymas agnoein</hi> ("I do not want you to be uninformed/ignorant") is the same disclosure formula Paul used in 10:1 ("I do not want you to be unaware, brothers, that our fathers were all under the cloud"). In both cases, the formula introduces material of critical importance that the audience needs to understand in order to avoid disaster. In 10:1, the material was Israel's wilderness history and the lesson that privilege does not guarantee perseverance. Here in 12:1, the material is the nature, source, and purpose of spiritual gifts — knowledge the Corinthians need in order to stop the divisions that their misuse of gifts has produced.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.2-1Cor.12.3">
          <title>Verses 2-3: The Criterion — From Mute Idols to the Confession of the Lord</title>
          <p>"You know that when you were pagans you were led astray to mute idols, however you were led. Therefore I want you to understand that no one speaking in the Spirit of God ever says 'Jesus is accursed!' and no one can say 'Jesus is Lord' except in the Holy Spirit" (12:2-3).</p>
          <p>Paul begins his answer not with a catalog of gifts but with a contrast between the Corinthians' former life and their present life. The phrase <hi type="italic">hote ethne ete</hi> ("when you were Gentiles/pagans") marks a boundary: there was a time when the Corinthians were <hi type="italic">ethne</hi> — nations, pagans, people outside the covenant community. The description of that former life is striking: <hi type="italic">pros ta eidola ta aphona hos an egesthe apagomenoi</hi> — literally, "to the mute idols, however you were led, being led away." Two words for "led" appear: <hi type="italic">egesthe</hi> (from <hi type="italic">ago</hi>, "to lead") and <hi type="italic">apagomenoi</hi> (from <hi type="italic">apago</hi>, "to lead away, to carry off"). The double use of "leading" language emphasizes the passive, involuntary character of their former religious experience. They were led — by what or by whom is left deliberately vague, but the implication is that they were led by the powers behind the idols, the <hi type="italic">daimonia</hi> ("demons") Paul identified in 10:20. Their worship of pagan gods was not the free exercise of their will but a form of spiritual captivity. They were "carried off" to idols.</p>
          <p>The idols are <hi type="italic">ta aphona</hi> — "mute, voiceless, without speech." The adjective <hi type="italic">aphonos</hi> ("voiceless, dumb, unable to speak") draws on a rich LXX tradition. Psalm 115:5-7 (LXX 113:13-15) describes idols: "They have mouths, but do not speak (<hi type="italic">ou lalēsousin</hi>); eyes, but do not see; ears, but do not hear; noses, but do not smell." Habakkuk 2:18-19: "What profit is an idol when its maker has shaped it, a metal image, a teacher of lies? For its maker trusts in his own creation when he makes mute idols (<hi type="italic">eidola kōpha</hi>)... Woe to him who says to a wooden thing, Awake!" The mute idol cannot speak, cannot prophesy, cannot communicate. The contrast with the Spirit, who empowers speech (v. 3), is pointed: the Corinthians' former worship was directed toward objects that could not speak back. Their present life is characterized by speech empowered by the Spirit — prophecy, tongues, words of wisdom and knowledge. The difference between pagan worship and Spirit-empowered worship is the difference between silence and speech, between captivity and freedom, between being led to voiceless objects and being enabled to confess the living Lord.</p>
          <p>Verse 3 establishes the criterion for distinguishing genuine Spirit-speech from its counterfeit. The word <hi type="italic">dio</hi> ("therefore, for this reason") connects the criterion to the contrast with pagan worship. Because the Corinthians came from a background of ecstatic pagan experience — where being "led" into frenzied states before mute idols was normal — they need a test by which to evaluate spiritual utterances.</p>
          <p>The test has two parts, stated as parallel negation and affirmation:</p>
          <p>First: <hi type="italic">oudeis en pneumati theou lalon legei anathema Iesous</hi> — "no one speaking by the Spirit of God says 'Jesus is accursed.'" The word <hi type="italic">anathema</hi> in the LXX corresponds to the Hebrew <hi type="italic">herem</hi> — that which is "devoted to destruction," placed under the ban of God. In Leviticus 27:28-29 (LXX), anything devoted (<hi type="italic">anathema</hi>) to the Lord cannot be redeemed; it must be destroyed. In Joshua 6:17-18, Jericho and its contents are <hi type="italic">anathema</hi>. In Deuteronomy 7:26, anyone who brings an abomination into his house becomes <hi type="italic">anathema</hi> like it. The cry <hi type="italic">anathema Iesous</hi> — "Jesus is accursed" or "let Jesus be devoted to destruction" — is a declaration that Jesus stands under God's ban. Who would say such a thing? In the synagogue context, Jews who rejected Jesus as Messiah might pronounce him accursed (cf. Deuteronomy 21:23, "cursed is everyone who is hanged on a tree," cited by Paul in Galatians 3:13). In the pagan context, those in ecstatic states might utter curses against Jesus. In the Corinthian assembly itself, if ecstatic speech were occurring without discernment, the possibility existed that what sounded like Spirit-speech was actually its opposite. Paul's criterion is clear: no genuine work of the Spirit produces a curse on Jesus. The Spirit and the cursing of Jesus are absolutely incompatible.</p>
          <p>Second: <hi type="italic">oudeis dynatai eipein kyrios Iesous ei me en pneumati hagio</hi> — "no one is able to say 'Jesus is Lord' except by the Holy Spirit." The confession <hi type="italic">kyrios Iesous</hi> ("Jesus is Lord") is the foundational Christian declaration. In the LXX, <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> translates the divine name YHWH. To confess <hi type="italic">Iesous</hi> as <hi type="italic">kyrios</hi> is to apply to Jesus the title that belongs to the God of Israel. This confession is not merely verbal — the verb <hi type="italic">dynatai</hi> ("is able") indicates capacity, not just willingness. The ability to confess Jesus as Lord — genuinely, from the heart, as a commitment of one's whole life — comes only from the Holy Spirit. This is not a claim that saying the words "Jesus is Lord" requires a special miracle; it is a claim that the genuine confession, with all its implications for life and allegiance, is itself a work of the Spirit.</p>
          <p>The theological effect of this criterion is enormous. If the mark of the Spirit is the confession "Jesus is Lord," then every genuine member of the community has the Spirit. There is no division between Spirit-haves and Spirit-have-nots. The person who speaks in tongues has the Spirit; so does the person who teaches, who serves, who administers, who helps. The Corinthian hierarchy — in which tongues-speakers rank above non-tongues-speakers as "more spiritual" — collapses at the foundation. The Spirit is not the possession of an elite; the Spirit is present in every person who confesses Jesus as Lord.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.4-1Cor.12.6">
          <title>Verses 4-6: One Source, Three Dimensions of Diversity</title>
          <p>"Now there are varieties of gifts, but the same Spirit; and there are varieties of service, but the same Lord; and there are varieties of activities, but it is the same God who empowers them all in everyone" (12:4-6).</p>
          <p>Paul now introduces his own vocabulary for what the Corinthians called <hi type="italic">pneumatika</hi> ("spiritual things"). The word <hi type="italic">charismaton</hi> (genitive plural of <hi type="italic">charisma</hi>, "gift of grace, free gift") comes from <hi type="italic">charis</hi> ("grace"). A <hi type="italic">charisma</hi> is not an achievement, an attainment, or a reward; it is a gift that flows from grace. The shift from the Corinthians' word (<hi type="italic">pneumatikon</hi>) to Paul's word (<hi type="italic">charismaton</hi>) reframes the entire discussion. The Corinthians think in terms of spiritual status; Paul thinks in terms of unmerited gifts. What you have received is a gift of grace; you did not earn it, and it gives you no basis for ranking yourself above others.</p>
          <p>The three-fold structure of verses 4-6 is parallel:</p>
          <list type="x-bullet">
            <item><hi type="italic">diaireseis de charismaton eisin, to de auto pneuma</hi> — "there are varieties of gifts, but the same Spirit"</item>
            <item><hi type="italic">kai diaireseis diakoniōn eisin, kai ho autos kyrios</hi> — "there are varieties of service, and the same Lord"</item>
            <item><hi type="italic">kai diaireseis energematōn eisin, ho de autos theos ho energōn ta panta en pasin</hi> — "there are varieties of activities, but the same God who works all things in all persons"</item>
          </list>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">diaireseis</hi> ("varieties, distributions, diversities, apportionments") comes from <hi type="italic">diaireō</hi> ("to divide, to distribute, to apportion") — the same root that will reappear in verse 11, where the Spirit "apportions" (<hi type="italic">diairoun</hi>) gifts to each person individually. The word does not describe chaotic fragmentation but ordered distribution. The diversity is not accident; it is apportionment — the deliberate distribution of different gifts to different persons.</p>
          <p>Each of the three parallel clauses pairs a form of diversity with the unity of its source:</p>
          <p>First: <hi type="italic">charismata</hi> ("gifts of grace") — the same Spirit. The gifts vary; the Spirit who gives them is one.</p>
          <p>Second: <hi type="italic">diakoniai</hi> ("forms of service, ministries") — the same Lord. The word <hi type="italic">diakonia</hi> means "service, ministry, attendance." It describes not the gift itself but the service that the gift enables. The same gift (say, teaching) may be exercised in various forms of service (teaching in the assembly, teaching in private, teaching children, teaching new converts). The Lord to whom all service is rendered is one: the <hi type="italic">kyrios Iesous</hi> of verse 3.</p>
          <p>Third: <hi type="italic">energemata</hi> ("workings, activities, effects, operations") — the same God. The word <hi type="italic">energema</hi> comes from <hi type="italic">energeō</hi> ("to work, to be active, to produce, to effect"). It describes the actual operation, the power at work in and through the gift and the service. God is the one <hi type="italic">ho energōn ta panta en pasin</hi> — "who works all things in all persons." The phrase <hi type="italic">ta panta en pasin</hi> ("all things in all") is comprehensive: every spiritual operation in every person comes from the one God.</p>
          <p>The three-fold pattern corresponds to Spirit, Lord (Christ), and God (Father). This is not a developed doctrine of divine relations but a functional description of how the community experiences the divine action: the Spirit gives the gifts, the Lord directs the service, and God powers the operation. The diversity of the community's life flows from the unity of its divine source. Because the source is one, the diversity cannot produce division. Different gifts, different services, different operations — but one Spirit, one Lord, one God. The <hi type="italic">schismata</hi> of 1:10 and 11:18 are a contradiction of the community's own origin: a community that springs from one God cannot be divided against itself.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.7">
          <title>Verse 7: The Purpose of Manifestation — The Common Good</title>
          <p>"To each is given the manifestation of the Spirit for the common good" (12:7).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">hekastō de didotai</hi> ("to each one is given") establishes two things simultaneously. First, the distribution is universal: <hi type="italic">hekastō</hi>, "to each one," with no exceptions. Every member of the community has received something. There are no empty-handed members. Second, the verb is passive (<hi type="italic">didotai</hi>, "is given"), reinforcing the grace-character of the gifts: the member does not generate or earn the manifestation; it is given to them.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">phanerōsis</hi> ("manifestation, disclosure, making visible") comes from <hi type="italic">phaneroō</hi> ("to make visible, to reveal, to manifest"). A <hi type="italic">phanerōsis tou pneumatos</hi> ("manifestation of the Spirit") is a making-visible of the Spirit's presence. The Spirit, who is not visible, becomes visible through what the member does. The tongue-speaker makes the Spirit visible in one way; the healer in another; the teacher in another. Each gift is a window through which the Spirit's presence can be seen. This word choice is significant: the gifts are not private possessions for the member's own benefit; they are manifestations — public displays of the Spirit's presence for the community to see.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">pros to sympheron</hi> ("for the common good, for what is profitable, for what is beneficial") states the purpose. The word <hi type="italic">sympheron</hi> (from <hi type="italic">sympherō</hi>, "to bring together, to be advantageous, to benefit") has appeared twice before in the letter. In 6:12: "All things are lawful for me, but not all things are helpful" (<hi type="italic">ou panta sympherei</hi>). In 10:23: "All things are lawful, but not all things are helpful" (<hi type="italic">all' ou panta sympherei</hi>). In both earlier uses, <hi type="italic">sympheron</hi> was the criterion by which lawful actions were to be evaluated: not "am I permitted?" but "does this benefit?" Now Paul applies the same criterion to spiritual gifts: the purpose of every manifestation of the Spirit is the common benefit. A gift exercised for private display, for self-promotion, for status enhancement, or for personal spiritual experience without regard for the community fails its purpose. The manifestation exists for the <hi type="italic">sympheron</hi> — the bringing-together, the mutual profit, the common advantage. The word itself contains the idea of convergence (<hi type="italic">syn</hi>, "together" + <hi type="italic">pherō</hi>, "to carry"): the gifts are meant to carry the community together, to build collective benefit, not to fracture the community into spiritual ranks.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.8-1Cor.12.10">
          <title>Verses 8-10: The Catalog of Manifestations</title>
          <p>"For to one is given through the Spirit the utterance of wisdom, and to another the utterance of knowledge according to the same Spirit, to another faith by the same Spirit, to another gifts of healing by the one Spirit, to another the working of miracles, to another prophecy, to another the ability to distinguish between spirits, to another various kinds of tongues, to another the interpretation of tongues" (12:8-10).</p>
          <p>Paul now illustrates the diversity of verse 4 with a catalog of nine manifestations. The list is not exhaustive — Paul will give a different and partially overlapping list in verse 28, and another in Romans 12:6-8. The variation between lists indicates that Paul is not defining a fixed set of offices but illustrating the principle of diversity. The specific gifts named are examples; the principle is that the Spirit gives different gifts to different persons.</p>
          <p>The catalog moves through three clusters:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Speech-related gifts (vv. 8-9a):</hi></p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Logos sophias</hi> ("utterance of wisdom, word of wisdom"): The word <hi type="italic">sophia</hi> ("wisdom") has been central to the letter since chapter 1, where Paul contrasted the "wisdom of the world" with the "foolishness" of the cross (1:18-25). The Corinthians prized <hi type="italic">sophia</hi> and used it as a marker of spiritual rank: those aligned with Apollos, the eloquent speaker, valued rhetorical <hi type="italic">sophia</hi> (1:12, 3:4). Paul has reframed wisdom throughout the letter: true wisdom is Christ crucified (1:30), and the wisdom Paul speaks is "not a wisdom of this age" but "God's wisdom, secret and hidden" (2:6-7). A <hi type="italic">logos sophias</hi> ("word of wisdom") given by the Spirit is an utterance that communicates this divine wisdom — not the rhetorical sophistication the Corinthians admire, but insight into God's purposes mediated through the Spirit.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Logos gnōseōs</hi> ("utterance of knowledge, word of knowledge"): The word <hi type="italic">gnōsis</hi> ("knowledge") has likewise been a contested term. In 8:1, Paul said "we all have knowledge" but then qualified: "knowledge puffs up, but love builds up." The Corinthians' <hi type="italic">gnōsis</hi> led them to eat idol-food without regard for the weak. A <hi type="italic">logos gnōseōs</hi> given by the Spirit is knowledge of a different kind — not the knowledge that inflates but knowledge communicated as a gift for the community's benefit.</p>
          <p>By placing <hi type="italic">sophia</hi> and <hi type="italic">gnōsis</hi> at the head of his list, Paul takes the Corinthians' most prized categories and subordinates them to the Spirit's distribution. Wisdom and knowledge are real gifts, but they are gifts — given by the Spirit to specific persons for the common good, not intellectual achievements that elevate their possessors above the community.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Pistis</hi> ("faith"): This is not saving faith (which all members possess) but a particular gift of faith — the trust in God's power that enables extraordinary action. This may be the faith to which Paul will refer in 13:2: "if I have all faith, so as to remove mountains." It is a specific, heightened form of trust given to some and not to all.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Power gifts (vv. 9b-10a):</hi></p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Charismata iamatōn</hi> ("gifts of healing"): Both words are plural — <hi type="italic">charismata</hi> (gifts) and <hi type="italic">iamatōn</hi> (of healings). The double plural suggests variety: different kinds of healing, given on different occasions, not a permanent possession of a "healer" but specific gifts for specific needs. The verb <hi type="italic">iaomai</hi> ("to heal") appears throughout the LXX and the Gospel narratives for physical healing of disease and disability.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Energēmata dynameōn</hi> ("workings of miracles, operations of powers"): The word <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> ("power, mighty work, miracle") has appeared in 1:18 (the cross is "the power of God") and 2:4 (Paul's preaching was "in demonstration of the Spirit and of power"). The plural <hi type="italic">energēmata</hi> ("workings, operations") echoes verse 6, where God is the one <hi type="italic">ho energōn</hi> ("who works") all things. The miracles are not human achievements but divine operations working through human agents.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Discernment and speech gifts (vv. 10b-10d):</hi></p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Prophēteia</hi> ("prophecy"): The word <hi type="italic">prophēteia</hi> describes speaking God's message to the community. In chapter 14, Paul will develop at length the role of prophecy in the assembly: the prophet "speaks to people for their upbuilding and encouragement and consolation" (14:3). Prophecy edifies the community; it communicates intelligible, applicable truth. Paul will rank prophecy above tongues precisely because prophecy builds up the assembly (14:1-5).</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Diakriseis pneumatōn</hi> ("distinguishing of spirits, discernments of spirits"): The word <hi type="italic">diakrisis</hi> (from <hi type="italic">diakrinō</hi>, "to distinguish, to discern, to judge") is the ability to evaluate whether a spiritual utterance genuinely comes from the Holy Spirit or from another source. The criterion of verse 3 — whether the utterance confesses or curses Jesus — is the foundational test; the gift of discernment is the ability to apply that test and to recognize the source of spiritual phenomena in the assembly. In a community where ecstatic utterance was common and the background was pagan religious experience (v. 2), this gift was essential.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Gene glōssōn</hi> ("various kinds of tongues"): The word <hi type="italic">gene</hi> ("kinds, types, families") indicates diversity within the gift itself: not one "tongue" but various kinds. The word <hi type="italic">glōssa</hi> ("tongue, language") can mean either the physical organ of speech or a language. In chapter 14, Paul will describe tongues as speech directed toward God rather than toward the community (14:2, "one who speaks in a tongue speaks not to men but to God"), unintelligible to the hearers without interpretation (14:9), and edifying to the speaker but not to the assembly unless interpreted (14:4). The placement of tongues at the end of the list is almost certainly deliberate. If the Corinthians ranked tongues first — as the supreme evidence of Spirit-possession — Paul places it last. The ordering is not accidental; it is corrective.</p>
          <p><hi type="italic">Hermēneia glōssōn</hi> ("interpretation of tongues"): The word <hi type="italic">hermēneia</hi> ("interpretation, translation, explanation") comes from <hi type="italic">hermēneuō</hi> ("to interpret, to translate, to explain"). The interpreter makes intelligible what the tongue-speaker has uttered. Without interpretation, the tongue remains private and unintelligible (14:13-17). The gift of interpretation pairs with and completes the gift of tongues, converting private utterance into communal edification.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.11">
          <title>Verse 11: The Spirit's Sovereign Distribution</title>
          <p>"All these are empowered by one and the same Spirit, who apportions to each one individually as he wills" (12:11).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">panta de tauta energei to hen kai to auto pneuma</hi> ("all these things are worked by one and the same Spirit") returns to the unity-theme of verses 4-6. The diversity of the catalog (nine gifts, distributed among different persons) could give the impression of fragmentation. Paul counters: all of them — every gift in the list, and by extension every spiritual manifestation in the community — are worked (<hi type="italic">energei</hi>, from <hi type="italic">energeō</hi>, "to work, to produce, to effect") by one and the same Spirit. The words <hi type="italic">hen</hi> ("one") and <hi type="italic">auto</hi> ("same") are emphatic: one Spirit, the same Spirit, not multiple spirits producing competing gifts.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">diairoun idia hekastō kathōs bouletai</hi> ("distributing to each one individually as he wills") contains three critical elements:</p>
          <p>First, <hi type="italic">diairoun</hi> ("distributing, apportioning, dividing out") echoes the <hi type="italic">diaireseis</hi> ("varieties, distributions") of verses 4-6. The distribution is the Spirit's sovereign act, not the community's election or the individual's achievement.</p>
          <p>Second, <hi type="italic">idia</hi> ("individually, privately, in one's own way") emphasizes that the distribution is particularized. Each person receives their own specific gift. The gifts are not generic; they are tailored to each individual. No two members necessarily have the same configuration of gifts.</p>
          <p>Third, <hi type="italic">kathōs bouletai</hi> ("as he wills") locates the decision in the Spirit's own purpose. The verb <hi type="italic">boulomai</hi> ("to will, to intend, to purpose, to desire") describes deliberate choice. The Spirit does not distribute randomly; he distributes according to his own purpose. This means two things simultaneously. It means the member who has a particular gift received it by the Spirit's decision, not by merit, effort, or spiritual superiority — therefore the gift is no basis for boasting. And it means the member who lacks a particular gift was not overlooked or rejected; the Spirit chose to give them a different gift — therefore the absence of a particular gift (say, tongues) is no evidence of spiritual inferiority. The Spirit wills what he wills. The community must receive the Spirit's distribution without complaint, without envy, and without pride.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.12-1Cor.12.13">
          <title>Verses 12-13: The Body Metaphor Introduced — Baptism into One Body</title>
          <p>"For just as the body is one and has many members, and all the members of the body, though many, are one body, so it is with Christ. For in one Spirit we were all baptized into one body — Jews or Greeks, slaves or free — and all were made to drink of one Spirit" (12:12-13).</p>
          <p>The particle <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") signals that the body metaphor is the ground and explanation of everything Paul has just said about diverse gifts from one Spirit. The metaphor of the body is not decorative; it is explanatory. The reason diverse gifts do not fragment the community is that the community is a body — and a body is, by definition, one thing composed of many parts.</p>
          <p>The analogy in verse 12 is stated with careful precision: <hi type="italic">kathaper gar to sōma hen estin kai melē polla echei, panta de ta melē tou sōmatos polla onta hen estin sōma</hi> — "for just as the body is one and has many members, and all the members of the body, though many, are one body." The word <hi type="italic">hen</hi> ("one") appears twice; the word <hi type="italic">polla</hi> ("many") appears twice. The sentence moves from unity to plurality and back to unity: the body is one — it has many members — the many members are one body. Multiplicity does not destroy unity; it constitutes it. A body that had only one member would not be a body at all. The many-ness is essential to the one-ness.</p>
          <p>The application is compressed into three words: <hi type="italic">houtōs kai ho Christos</hi> — "so it is with Christ." The comparison is not "so it is with the community" or "so it is with the church." Paul says "so it is with Christ." The community is not merely compared to a body; it is identified with Christ. Christ is a body with many members. The community's corporate existence is so deeply united with Christ that Paul can use the name "Christ" for the whole — head and members together. This identification has been building throughout the letter: "your bodies are members of Christ" (6:15); "we who are many are one body, for we all partake of the one bread" (10:17); the bread is "my body, which is for you" (11:24). The body metaphor is not a social analogy borrowed from Greco-Roman rhetoric; it is a christological claim. The community is Christ's body.</p>
          <p>The body metaphor was well known in the Greco-Roman world. The most famous precedent is the speech of Menenius Agrippa to the Roman plebeians (recorded by Livy, <hi type="italic">Ab Urbe Condita</hi> 2.32), in which a senator persuaded the seceding lower classes to return by telling the fable of the body's members rebelling against the stomach. In the standard Greco-Roman usage, the body metaphor typically served the interests of the powerful: it argued that the lower classes should accept their subordinate role because the body needs all its parts functioning in their assigned positions. Paul's use of the metaphor in what follows will subvert this conventional application. He does not argue that the "lower" members should accept their inferiority; he argues that God has given the "inferior" parts greater honor (12:23-24). The Pauline body metaphor does not reinforce the existing social order; it overturns it.</p>
          <p>Verse 13 grounds the community's bodily unity in a shared experience: <hi type="italic">kai gar en heni pneumati hēmeis pantes eis hen sōma ebaptisthēmen</hi> — "for indeed in one Spirit we all were baptized into one body." The word <hi type="italic">pantes</hi> ("all") echoes the five-fold <hi type="italic">pantes</hi> of 10:1-4, where "all" the Israelites were under the cloud, "all" passed through the sea, "all" were baptized into Moses, "all" ate the spiritual food, "all" drank the spiritual drink. The parallel is deliberate. As Israel was constituted as a people through baptism into Moses in the cloud and the sea (10:2), so the Corinthian community was constituted as a body through baptism in one Spirit into one body. The baptism creates the body. The community's unity is not a goal to be achieved but a fact established at baptism.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">eite Ioudaioi eite Hellēnes, eite douloi eite eleutheroi</hi> ("whether Jews or Greeks, whether slaves or free") names the primary social divisions of the Greco-Roman world: the ethnic-religious division between Jew and Gentile, and the legal-social division between slave and free. Both divisions are transcended — not erased but overcome — in the baptismal act that constitutes the one body. The diversity of the body includes persons from both sides of both divides. The body is not ethnically homogeneous (all Jewish or all Greek) or socially homogeneous (all slave or all free). It is constituted precisely by the inclusion of persons from every category. The diversity that the surrounding society treats as division, the body incorporates as richness.</p>
          <p>The final clause — <hi type="italic">kai pantes hen pneuma epotisthēmen</hi> ("and all were made to drink of one Spirit") — adds a second shared experience to baptism: drinking. The verb <hi type="italic">potizō</hi> ("to give drink, to water, to irrigate") creates a link to the wilderness narrative of 10:4, where Israel "drank from the spiritual Rock that followed them, and the Rock was Christ." Israel drank spiritual drink from the Rock; the community has been given to drink of the Spirit. The typological correspondence is precise: what Israel received in the wilderness, the community receives in the Spirit. But the difference is also clear: Israel drank and most were overthrown (10:5). The community has been given the Spirit to drink — but the warning of 10:1-12 remains in force. Receiving the Spirit, like receiving the manna and the water, does not automatically guarantee perseverance. The body must be discerned (11:29). The gifts must be exercised for the common good (12:7). The way of love must be followed (12:31b-13:13).</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.14-1Cor.12.20">
          <title>Verses 14-20: The Body Cannot Reject Its Own Members</title>
          <p>"For the body does not consist of one member but of many. If the foot should say, 'Because I am not a hand, I do not belong to the body,' that would not make it any less a part of the body. And if the ear should say, 'Because I am not an eye, I do not belong to the body,' that would not make it any less a part of the body. If the whole body were an eye, where would be the sense of hearing? If the whole body were an ear, where would be the sense of smell? But as it is, God arranged the members in the body, each one of them, as he chose. If all were a single member, where would the body be? As it is, there are many parts, yet one body" (12:14-20).</p>
          <p>The opening statement — <hi type="italic">to gar sōma ouk estin hen melos alla polla</hi> ("for the body is not one member but many") — is the premise from which the entire section flows. A body composed of one member is not a body. Multiplicity is constitutive of bodily existence. This applies directly to the spiritual-gifts situation: a community in which everyone had the same gift would not be a body. The diversity of gifts is not a deficiency to be corrected but the essential structure of the body's existence.</p>
          <p>Paul then imagines two self-exclusion scenarios. In both, a member with a "lesser" gift compares itself unfavorably to a member with a more prominent gift and concludes that it does not belong.</p>
          <p>The foot says: <hi type="italic">hoti ouk eimi cheir, ouk eimi ek tou sōmatos</hi> — "because I am not a hand, I am not of the body." The ear says: <hi type="italic">hoti ouk eimi ophthalmos, ouk eimi ek tou sōmatos</hi> — "because I am not an eye, I am not of the body." In both cases, the member defines belonging by possession of a different gift: because I do not have what you have, I do not belong. Paul's answer is blunt: <hi type="italic">ou para touto ouk estin ek tou sōmatos</hi> — "that does not make it any less a part of the body." The member's self-assessment is simply wrong. The foot's conviction that it does not belong does not change the fact that it belongs. Belonging to the body is not determined by which gift one has; it is determined by the baptism that incorporated every member into the body (v. 13).</p>
          <p>This section addresses the Corinthians who feel inferior because they lack the spectacular gifts — especially tongues, if tongues were the gift most prized in Corinth. The person who cannot speak in tongues and who therefore feels like a second-class member of the community is the foot saying "I am not a hand." Paul's response: you are part of the body. Your gift matters. Your sense that you do not belong is based on a false criterion.</p>
          <p>Verses 17-18 press the point with a reduction to absurdity: <hi type="italic">ei holon to sōma ophthalmos, pou hē akoē? ei holon akoē, pou hē osphrēsis?</hi> — "if the whole body were an eye, where would be the hearing? If the whole body were an ear, where would be the sense of smell?" A body that was entirely one organ would be monstrous, not glorious. An all-eye body could see but not hear, touch, move, or smell. The community that prizes one gift (tongues, or wisdom, or knowledge) to the exclusion of all others is attempting to become a monstrosity — a body that is nothing but a single organ. The absurdity of the image is the argument.</p>
          <p>Verse 18 provides the theological ground: <hi type="italic">nyni de ho theos etheto ta melē, hen hekaston autōn, en tō sōmati kathōs ēthelēsen</hi> — "but as it is, God placed the members, each one of them, in the body as he chose." The verb <hi type="italic">etheto</hi> ("placed, arranged, set") comes from <hi type="italic">tithēmi</hi> ("to set, to place, to appoint"). God is the subject. The arrangement of the body's members is God's act, not an accident or a human decision. The phrase <hi type="italic">kathōs ēthelēsen</hi> ("as he chose, as he willed") echoes verse 11, where the Spirit distributes "as he wills" (<hi type="italic">kathōs bouletai</hi>). The divine will governs both the Spirit's distribution of gifts and God's arrangement of the body. To complain about one's gift is to quarrel with God's arrangement. To wish the body were different — all one gift, all one member — is to second-guess the divine architect.</p>
          <p>Verses 19-20 state the conclusion: <hi type="italic">ei de ēn ta panta hen melos, pou to sōma? nyn de polla men melē, hen de sōma</hi> — "if all were one member, where would the body be? But as it is, there are many members, yet one body." The body exists because of its many members, not despite them. Unity and diversity are not in tension; they are mutually constitutive. The one body exists through its many members, and the many members find their identity through the one body.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.21-1Cor.12.26">
          <title>Verses 21-26: The Body Cannot Despise Its Own Members</title>
          <p>"The eye cannot say to the hand, 'I have no need of you,' nor again the head to the feet, 'I have no need of you.' On the contrary, the parts of the body that seem to be weaker are indispensable, and on those parts of the body that we think less honorable we bestow the greater honor, and our unpresentable parts are treated with greater modesty, which our more presentable parts do not require. But God has so composed the body, giving greater honor to the part that lacked it, that there may be no division in the body, but that the members may have the same care for one another. If one member suffers, all suffer together; if one member is honored, all rejoice together" (12:21-26).</p>
          <p>The first section (vv. 14-20) addressed members who excluded themselves because they felt inferior. This section addresses members who exclude others because they feel superior. The direction of the error is reversed: the self-excluder says "I do not belong"; the other-excluder says "I have no need of you."</p>
          <p>The eye says to the hand: <hi type="italic">ou chreian sou echo</hi> — "I have no need of you." The head says to the feet: <hi type="italic">ou chreian hymōn echō</hi> — "I have no need of you." The word <hi type="italic">chreia</hi> ("need, necessity, use") denies any dependence. The claim is self-sufficiency: I can function without you; you are expendable; your contribution is unnecessary. In the Corinthian context, this is the attitude of the spiritually elite toward those with lesser gifts — the tongues-speaker who regards the administrator as unspiritual, the prophet who dismisses the helper, the knowledge-possessor who looks down on the one who merely serves.</p>
          <p>This attitude also connects to the meal-abuse of 11:17-34. The wealthy who ate without waiting for the poor were effectively saying "I have no need of you" — your presence at the meal is irrelevant to my participation. The body metaphor addresses not only the gifts-controversy but the entire social dynamic that has produced division throughout the letter. The teacher-allegiance factions of chapters 1-4, the social stratification at the meal in chapter 11, and the gift-hierarchy of chapters 12-14 are all expressions of the same attitude: "I have no need of you."</p>
          <p>Paul's response in verses 22-24 does not merely assert that the "lesser" members are needed. He goes further: they receive greater honor. The argument has three steps:</p>
          <p>First (v. 22): <hi type="italic">alla pollō mallon ta dokounta melē tou sōmatos asthenestera hyparchein anagkaia estin</hi> — "on the contrary, the parts of the body that seem to be weaker are indispensable." The word <hi type="italic">dokounta</hi> ("seeming, appearing") echoes <hi type="italic">dokei</hi> in 10:12 ("anyone who thinks he stands") and 8:2 ("if anyone thinks he knows"). What "seems" may not reflect reality. The parts that seem weaker (<hi type="italic">asthenestera</hi>, comparative of <hi type="italic">asthenēs</hi>, "weak" — the same word used for the "weak" brother in chapters 8-9) are in fact <hi type="italic">anagkaia</hi> ("necessary, indispensable, essential"). The apparently weak members are the ones the body cannot do without. Internal organs, which are hidden and seem fragile, are more essential to life than the visible extremities. The spiritual application is direct: the members who seem least impressive may be the ones most essential to the community's survival.</p>
          <p>Second (v. 23): <hi type="italic">kai ha dokoumen atimotera einai tou sōmatos, toutois timēn perissoteran peritithemen</hi> — "those parts of the body that we regard as less honorable, we clothe with greater honor." The word <hi type="italic">atimotera</hi> ("less honorable," comparative of <hi type="italic">atimos</hi>) describes parts that lack public distinction. The word <hi type="italic">timēn</hi> ("honor") and its comparative <hi type="italic">perissoteran</hi> ("greater, more abundant") describe the compensating response: the body gives more honor to the parts that have less. The practice of clothing the body illustrates the point: the parts we cover (genitalia, for instance) are the parts we treat with the most careful attention — we clothe them, protect them, attend to their dignity. The "less honorable" parts receive more care, not less.</p>
          <p>Third (v. 24): <hi type="italic">ho de theos synekerasen to sōma, tō hysterounti perissoteran dous timēn</hi> — "God has composed the body, giving greater honor to the part that lacked it." The verb <hi type="italic">synkerannnymi</hi> (<hi type="italic">synekerasen</hi>, "composed, blended, mixed together, tempered") describes the deliberate act of combining different elements into a unified whole. God is the composer. The composition is not egalitarian in the sense that every part receives the same honor; it is compensatory — the part that lacks honor receives more. God's design for the body is deliberately counter-hierarchical: the parts that the world considers least are the parts God honors most. This echoes 1:26-29, where God chose "the foolish... the weak... the low and despised... things that are not, to bring to nothing things that are, so that no human being might boast in the presence of God." The God who chose the foolish and weak of the world to shame the wise and strong has composed the body on the same principle: the "lesser" parts receive greater honor.</p>
          <p>The purpose of this divine composition is stated in verse 25: <hi type="italic">hina mē ē schisma en tō sōmati, alla to auto hyper allēlōn merimnōsin ta melē</hi> — "so that there may be no division in the body, but that the members may have the same care for one another."</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">schisma</hi> appears here for the final time in the letter. In 1:10, Paul urged that there be "no <hi type="italic">schismata</hi> among you." In 11:18, he heard that there were <hi type="italic">schismata</hi> when the community assembled. Now he reveals that God has composed the body specifically to prevent <hi type="italic">schisma</hi>. The divisions that plague the Corinthian community are a violation of God's own design. They are not merely social failures; they are contradictions of the body's divinely intended structure. God composed the body so that <hi type="italic">schisma</hi> would not occur. The Corinthians have produced <hi type="italic">schisma</hi> nonetheless. The gap between God's design and the community's behavior is the measure of the community's failure.</p>
          <p>The alternative to <hi type="italic">schisma</hi> is <hi type="italic">to auto hyper allēlōn merimnōsin ta melē</hi> — "the members have the same care for one another." The verb <hi type="italic">merimnaō</hi> ("to be anxious about, to be concerned for, to care for") describes active concern. The phrase <hi type="italic">to auto</hi> ("the same") indicates equal concern: each member cares for every other member with the same intensity. There is no hierarchy of caring: the eye does not care for the hand less than the hand cares for the eye. The concern is mutual and equal.</p>
          <p>Verse 26 draws the practical consequence: <hi type="italic">kai eite paschei hen melos, sympaschei panta ta melē; eite doxazetai hen melos, synchairei panta ta melē</hi> — "if one member suffers, all suffer together; if one member is honored, all rejoice together." The verbs <hi type="italic">sympaschō</hi> ("to suffer together, to suffer with") and <hi type="italic">synchairō</hi> ("to rejoice together, to rejoice with") describe the shared experience of the body. Suffering is not private; it is communal. Honor is not private; it is communal. What happens to one member happens to all members. The body cannot compartmentalize its experience.</p>
          <p>This verse stands as the direct rebuke to the meal-abuse of chapter 11. When some members went hungry (<hi type="italic">paschei</hi>, "suffers") while others got drunk, the body was treating the suffering of its members as irrelevant. When some members were honored with food and drink while others were humiliated (11:22), the body was failing to share its experience. In a properly functioning body, the hunger of the poor would be felt by the wealthy; the abundance of the wealthy would be shared with the poor. The body metaphor is not abstract theology; it is the practical principle that should have prevented the abuse at the meal.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.27">
          <title>Verse 27: The Declaration of Identity</title>
          <p>"Now you are the body of Christ and individually members of it" (12:27).</p>
          <p>The shift from metaphor to declaration is complete. Paul has been developing an analogy — the community is like a body. Now he states the identity directly: <hi type="italic">hymeis de este sōma Christou</hi> — "you are the body of Christ." The pronoun <hi type="italic">hymeis</hi> ("you") is emphatic and plural — the whole community, addressed together, is the body of Christ. Not "you are like a body" or "you resemble a body" but "you are a body." The genitive <hi type="italic">Christou</hi> ("of Christ") identifies whose body this is. The community is not its own body; it is Christ's body. It belongs to him. It is the visible, tangible presence of Christ in Corinth.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">kai melē ek merous</hi> ("and members individually, and members in part") adds the complementary truth: each person is a <hi type="italic">melos</hi> ("member, limb, organ") <hi type="italic">ek merous</hi> ("in part, from a portion, individually"). No one person is the whole body. Each is a part. The whole exists through the parts; the parts exist within the whole. The tension between corporate identity (you are the body) and individual identity (each is a member) is held together, not resolved in favor of one or the other. The community's corporate identity does not erase the individual; the individual's distinct gift does not fracture the community.</p>
          <p>This verse also crystallizes what 11:29 meant by "not discerning the body." To eat the Lord's supper without discerning the body is to eat without recognizing that the people at the table — all of them, including the poor, the slaves, the ones without spectacular gifts — are the body of Christ. The bread is Christ's body (11:24). The community is Christ's body (12:27). To treat the bread with reverence while treating the community with contempt is a contradiction that brings judgment (11:29-30).</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.28">
          <title>Verse 28: God's Appointments in the Assembly</title>
          <p>"And God has appointed in the church first apostles, second prophets, third teachers, then miracles, then gifts of healing, helping, administrating, various kinds of tongues" (12:28).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">etheto</hi> ("appointed, placed, set") is the same word used in verse 18 for God's arrangement of the body's members. God is the subject of both sentences: God placed the members in the body (v. 18), and God appointed the roles in the assembly (v. 28). The continuity of agency reinforces the point: the community's structure is God's work, not a human arrangement. To dispute the structure is to dispute God.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">en tē ekklēsia</hi> ("in the church, in the assembly") locates the appointments within the gathered community. The word <hi type="italic">ekklēsia</hi> has appeared throughout the letter: "the church of God that is in Corinth" (1:2), "the churches of God" (11:16), "the church of God" that must not be despised (11:22). The appointments are not for the world at large but for the gathered community's internal life.</p>
          <p>Paul introduces an explicit ordering with ordinal numbers: <hi type="italic">prōton</hi> ("first"), <hi type="italic">deuteron</hi> ("second"), <hi type="italic">triton</hi> ("third"). After the first three, he drops the ordinals and uses <hi type="italic">epeita</hi> ("then, next") and a simple list. The ordering is significant:</p>
          <p>First: <hi type="italic">apostolous</hi> ("apostles"). The apostles occupy the primary position. In Paul's usage, <hi type="italic">apostolos</hi> ("one sent, emissary, apostle") describes those commissioned directly by the risen Christ to proclaim the gospel and establish communities. Paul defended his own apostleship in 9:1-2 ("Am I not an apostle? Have I not seen Jesus our Lord?") and traced the community's existence to his apostolic founding work (3:6, 10). The apostles are first because they are the foundation-layers (3:10-11); the community was built on their testimony.</p>
          <p>Second: <hi type="italic">prophētas</hi> ("prophets"). Prophets speak God's word to the community. They are ranked after apostles but before teachers. In 14:1-5, Paul will urge the Corinthians to pursue prophecy above tongues because "the one who prophesies speaks to people for their upbuilding and encouragement and consolation" (14:3). Prophecy serves the community's edification directly.</p>
          <p>Third: <hi type="italic">didaskalous</hi> ("teachers"). Teachers transmit the received tradition (<hi type="italic">paradosis</hi>, 11:2, 23) and instruct the community in its meaning. Teaching is the ongoing work of formation that follows the apostolic founding and the prophetic word.</p>
          <p>After these three foundational roles, Paul lists: <hi type="italic">epeita dynameis</hi> ("then miracles"), <hi type="italic">epeita charismata iamatōn</hi> ("then gifts of healing"), <hi type="italic">antilēmpseis</hi> ("helping, acts of assistance"), <hi type="italic">kybernēseis</hi> ("administrating, acts of governance, steering"), <hi type="italic">genē glōssōn</hi> ("various kinds of tongues").</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">antilēmpsis</hi> ("help, assistance, taking hold of") comes from <hi type="italic">antilambanomai</hi> ("to take hold of, to help, to assist"). In the LXX, the noun and verb describe God's help: Psalm 22:19 (LXX 21:20), "O Lord, do not be far from my help (<hi type="italic">tēs boētheias mou</hi>)"; Psalm 89:19 (LXX 88:20), "I have given help (<hi type="italic">antilēmpsin</hi>) to the mighty one." Applied to the community, it describes practical acts of service — assisting those in need, supporting the weak, doing the unglamorous work that keeps the community functioning.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">kybernēsis</hi> ("governance, steering, piloting, administration") comes from <hi type="italic">kybernētēs</hi> ("helmsman, pilot"), the person who steers the ship. The metaphor is nautical: someone must steer the community, give direction, make organizational decisions, guide the assembly's practical life. This is not the spectacular gift of tongues or prophecy; it is the ordinary, essential work of governance.</p>
          <p>The placement of tongues last (<hi type="italic">genē glōssōn</hi>, "various kinds of tongues") mirrors the first list (vv. 8-10), where tongues also appeared last. If the Corinthians valued tongues above all other gifts, Paul's consistent placement of tongues at the bottom of every list is a deliberate corrective. Tongues is a genuine gift of the Spirit (Paul will say in 14:18, "I thank God that I speak in tongues more than all of you"), but it is not the supreme gift, not the mark of spiritual maturity, and not the criterion by which the community should rank its members. Apostles, prophets, and teachers come first — the gifts that found, instruct, and build up the community through intelligible speech.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.29-1Cor.12.30">
          <title>Verses 29-30: The Rhetorical Questions — Diversity is Essential</title>
          <p>"Are all apostles? Are all prophets? Are all teachers? Do all work miracles? Do all possess gifts of healing? Do all speak with tongues? Do all interpret?" (12:29-30).</p>
          <p>Seven rhetorical questions, each introduced by the particle <hi type="italic">mē</hi>, which in Greek signals that the expected answer is "no." The questions follow the order of the list in verse 28 (with the addition of interpretation from v. 10). The expected answer to every question is emphatically negative: No, not all are apostles. No, not all are prophets. No, not all speak in tongues. No, not all interpret.</p>
          <p>The function of these questions is to make the diversity principle inescapable. The Corinthians cannot answer "yes" to any of them. No community consists entirely of apostles, or entirely of prophets, or entirely of tongues-speakers. The body requires different members performing different functions. The desire for everyone to have the same gift — or the ranking of members based on whether they have one particular gift — contradicts the body's essential structure.</p>
          <p>The final two questions are particularly pointed in the Corinthian context: <hi type="italic">mē pantes glōssais lalousin? mē pantes diermēneuousin?</hi> — "Do all speak with tongues? Do all interpret?" If tongues were the gift most prized and most sought in Corinth, Paul's question forces the community to acknowledge that not everyone has this gift — and that this is by God's design (v. 18), not a deficiency to be corrected.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12.31">
          <title>Verse 31: The Transition — Earnest Desire and a More Excellent Way</title>
          <p>"But earnestly desire the higher gifts. And I will show you a still more excellent way" (12:31).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">zēloute</hi> ("earnestly desire, be zealous for") can be read as either indicative ("you are earnestly desiring") or imperative ("earnestly desire!"). If indicative, Paul is acknowledging the Corinthians' existing pursuit of gifts — they are zealous, but their zeal is misdirected — and then redirecting them. If imperative, Paul is encouraging them to pursue the gifts that most benefit the community. In either reading, the phrase <hi type="italic">ta charismata ta meizona</hi> ("the greater gifts") points toward chapter 14, where Paul will identify prophecy as the gift to be preferred over tongues because prophecy builds up the assembly while uninterpreted tongues edify only the speaker (14:1-5).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">kai eti kath' hyperbolēn hodon hymin deiknymi</hi> ("and I will show you a still more excellent way") is the hinge between chapter 12 and chapter 13. The word <hi type="italic">hyperbolē</hi> ("excess, surpassing measure, extraordinary quality") describes something that goes beyond all comparison. The <hi type="italic">hodon</hi> ("way, path, road") is not a "higher gift" but a way — a manner, a disposition, a mode of life in which all gifts are exercised. The gifts are real and important; but there is something that surpasses all of them — not by replacing them but by governing them. That something is love (<hi type="italic">agapē</hi>), which chapter 13 will describe.</p>
          <p>This transition verse prevents chapter 12 from being read as a self-contained argument. The diversity of gifts and the unity of the body are real and essential truths; but they are incomplete without the disposition that governs the gifts' exercise. A body that knows it has many members but exercises its gifts without love is still dysfunctional. The body metaphor addresses the structural problem (we need every member); love addresses the dispositional problem (every member must act with care for every other member). Chapter 12 provides the anatomy of the community; chapter 13 provides the animating spirit.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.12">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 12 provides the most sustained development of the body of Christ metaphor in Paul's letters. The chapter addresses the Corinthians' question about "spiritual things" or "spiritual persons" (12:1), but Paul answers the question on entirely different terms than the Corinthians asked it. They apparently asked about the ranking and classification of spiritual gifts — which gifts are superior, which persons are more spiritual, how to evaluate spiritual status. Paul's answer demolishes the premise of the question: there is no ranking, because the gifts all come from one Spirit, are all given by grace, and all exist for the common good. The community is a body, and a body needs every member.</p>
          <p>The chapter's argument moves through five stages.</p>
          <p>First (vv. 1-3), Paul establishes the criterion for genuine Spirit-activity: not the spectacular character of the manifestation but the content of the confession. The test is simple — does the utterance confess Jesus as Lord or curse him? This criterion immediately democratizes the community's spiritual life. Every genuine member confesses Jesus as Lord; therefore every genuine member has the Spirit; therefore there are no Spirit-less members.</p>
          <p>Second (vv. 4-11), Paul describes the diversity of gifts within the unity of their source. The three-fold pattern of verses 4-6 (Spirit, Lord, God) establishes that all diversity flows from divine unity. The catalog of verses 8-10 illustrates the diversity. Verse 7 declares the purpose: the common good. Verse 11 declares the principle: the Spirit distributes as he wills, not as the community deserves or demands.</p>
          <p>Third (vv. 12-13), Paul introduces the body metaphor and grounds it in the shared experience of baptism. The community was constituted as one body through baptism in one Spirit. The diversity of the body (Jews and Greeks, slaves and free) is constitutive of its unity, not a threat to it.</p>
          <p>Fourth (vv. 14-26), Paul develops the body metaphor in two directions. In verses 14-20, he addresses the member who self-excludes: the foot that says "I am not a hand, so I do not belong." Paul's answer: you belong because God placed you in the body; your sense of inferiority is based on a false criterion. In verses 21-26, he addresses the member who excludes others: the eye that says to the hand "I have no need of you." Paul's answer: you do need them, and God has given the "lesser" members greater honor. The divine composition of the body is designed to prevent <hi type="italic">schisma</hi> (division) and produce mutual care (12:25). When one member suffers, all suffer; when one is honored, all rejoice (12:26).</p>
          <p>Fifth (vv. 27-31), Paul applies the metaphor directly: "you are the body of Christ" (v. 27). He lists God's appointments in the community (v. 28), poses rhetorical questions that force the community to acknowledge the necessity of diversity (vv. 29-30), and then points beyond gifts altogether to "a still more excellent way" (v. 31) — the way of love that chapter 13 will describe.</p>
          <p>Several threads from earlier in the letter reach important junctures in this chapter.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">schisma</hi>, which opened the letter's argument in 1:10 ("that there be no divisions among you") and recurred at the meal table in 11:18 ("I hear that there are divisions among you"), now receives its most comprehensive treatment. God has composed the body <hi type="italic">hina mē ē schisma en tō sōmati</hi> — "so that there may be no division in the body" (12:25). The divisions Paul has been diagnosing since chapter 1 — factionalism around teachers, social stratification at the meal, gift-based ranking in the assembly — are all violations of God's design for the body. The disease is one; the symptoms have varied; the cure is the recognition that the community is one body with many members, each essential, each placed by God.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">soma</hi> ("body") reaches the culmination of its long arc through the letter. In 6:15, Paul said "your bodies are members of Christ." In 10:16-17, the bread is participation in the body of Christ, and the one bread makes the many into one body. In 11:24, the bread is Christ's body "for you." In 11:27, eating unworthily brings guilt concerning the body and blood of the Lord. In 11:29, the one who eats without "discerning the body" eats judgment. Now in chapter 12, all these strands converge: the community IS the body of Christ (12:27), composed of many members (12:12), baptized into unity by one Spirit (12:13), arranged by God to prevent division and produce mutual care (12:24-25). The meal that the community eats (the bread that is Christ's body) creates and sustains the community that the body metaphor describes. To eat the bread without discerning the body (11:29) is to eat the sign of the community's unity while perpetuating the community's division. Chapter 12 makes this connection inescapable.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">sympheron</hi> ("beneficial, advantageous, for the common good") in 12:7 echoes its two earlier appearances in 6:12 and 10:23. In both earlier uses, Paul qualified the Corinthian slogan "all things are lawful" with the criterion "but not all things are helpful" (<hi type="italic">sympherei</hi>). The gifts are governed by the same criterion: they exist not for private enjoyment or public display but <hi type="italic">pros to sympheron</hi> — for the common benefit. A gift exercised without regard for the community's benefit fails the same test that freedom exercised without regard for the community's benefit failed.</p>
          <p>The five-fold "all" (<hi type="italic">pantes</hi>) of 10:1-4 finds its echo in the baptismal statement of 12:13: "we were all baptized into one body... and all were made to drink of one Spirit." In chapter 10, the "all" was followed by the devastating "but with most of them God was not pleased" (10:5). The parallel should not be lost on the Corinthians. Universal participation in divine gifts — whether Israel's baptism, food, and drink, or the community's baptism and Spirit-drinking — does not automatically prevent judgment. The body must be discerned. The gifts must be used for the common good. The members must care for one another. The "still more excellent way" of love must govern everything. Without these, the gifts become what Israel's gifts became — privileges enjoyed by people who were overthrown.</p>
          <p>The chapter's most subversive move is its reversal of the Corinthian value system. The Corinthians have been operating on a system of ranking: the wise above the foolish, the strong above the weak, the tongues-speakers above the silent, the wealthy above the poor. Paul has been dismantling this system since 1:26-29, where God chose "the foolish... the weak... the low and despised." Now the body metaphor carries the dismantlement to its fullest expression. The parts that "seem weaker" are indispensable (12:22). The "less honorable" parts receive greater honor (12:23). God has composed the body to give greater honor to the part that lacked it (12:24). The divine value system is the inverse of the Corinthian value system. The community must learn to see its members as God sees them — not ranking by the spectacularity of the gift but honoring each member as essential to the body's life.</p>
          <p>The chapter does not, however, erase all distinction. Paul explicitly orders the appointments: first apostles, second prophets, third teachers (12:28). There is a functional ordering in the community's life: some roles are foundational in a way that others are not. But the ordering is functional, not hierarchical in the sense of human worth. The apostle is not more valuable as a person than the helper; but the apostolic role is foundational in a way the helping role is not, because the community was built on apostolic testimony. The distinction is between the functional ordering of roles (which Paul maintains) and the hierarchical ranking of persons (which Paul abolishes). The foot is not less a member of the body than the eye; but the eye has a different function, and some functions are prior to others in the body's life. Both truths — functional ordering and personal equality — coexist in the body.</p>
          <p>The chapter also advances the letter's sustained concern with the consequences of community membership. In 10:1-12, Paul warned that Israel's privileges did not prevent judgment. In 11:27-32, he warned that unworthy participation in the Lord's supper brings judgment, discipline, and potentially condemnation with the world. Now in chapter 12, the body metaphor provides the positive counterpart to these warnings. The body is designed for mutual care (12:25), shared suffering, and shared rejoicing (12:26). When the community functions as the body God designed, it avoids the <hi type="italic">schisma</hi> that has plagued it, discerns the body in its midst, and exercises its gifts for the common good. The body metaphor is not merely descriptive; it is prescriptive. It tells the community what it is (the body of Christ) and what it must become (a community of mutual care where no member is dispensable). The gap between the two — between what the community is by divine design and what it has become through human sin — is the gap that the rest of the letter aims to close.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.13">
        <title>1 Corinthians 13 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.13">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 12):</hi> Paul opened a new section of the letter with the formula <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> ("now concerning"), signaling a response to another question from the Corinthians' letter: "Now concerning spiritual gifts, brothers, I do not want you to be uninformed" (12:1). The word <hi type="italic">pneumatikon</hi> can mean either "spiritual gifts" or "spiritual persons" — the ambiguity may be deliberate, since the Corinthians' problem involved both the gifts and the inflated self-regard of those who possessed them. Paul began by contrasting their former pagan life — "when you were pagans you were led astray to mute idols, however you were led" (12:2) — with the criterion for genuine Spirit-speech: no one speaking by God's Spirit says "Jesus is accursed" (<hi type="italic">anathema Iesous</hi>), and no one can say "Jesus is Lord" (<hi type="italic">kyrios Iesous</hi>) except by the Holy Spirit (12:3). The fundamental test of the Spirit's presence is christological confession, not spectacular manifestation.</p>
          <p>Paul then developed the diversity-within-unity principle through a three-fold statement: there are varieties of gifts (<hi type="italic">charismata</hi>) but the same Spirit, varieties of service (<hi type="italic">diakoniai</hi>) but the same Lord, varieties of activities (<hi type="italic">energemata</hi>) but the same God who empowers them all in everyone (12:4-6). Each person receives a manifestation of the Spirit <hi type="italic">pros to sympheron</hi> — "for the common good" (12:7). This phrase is critical: the purpose of every gift is the benefit of the community, not the enhancement of the individual. Paul then listed specific gifts — word of wisdom, word of knowledge, faith, gifts of healing, working of miracles, prophecy, distinguishing of spirits, various kinds of tongues, interpretation of tongues (12:8-10) — and attributed all of them to "one and the same Spirit, who apportions to each one individually as he wills" (12:11). The Spirit distributes; the individual does not choose.</p>
          <p>The chapter then introduced the body metaphor that dominates its second half: "For just as the body is one and has many members, and all the members of the body, though many, are one body, so it is with Christ. For in one Spirit we were all baptized into one body — Jews or Greeks, slaves or free — and all were made to drink of one Spirit" (12:12-13). The body metaphor addressed the Corinthian problem from two directions. First, no member can say to another "I have no need of you" (12:21) — the eye cannot dismiss the hand, the head cannot dismiss the feet. Second, no member can say of itself "because I am not a hand, I do not belong to the body" (12:15) — the foot is no less a member for not being a hand. Both the superiority complex and the inferiority complex are addressed. God has composed the body so that "there may be no division (<hi type="italic">schisma</hi>) in the body, but that the members may have the same care for one another" (12:25). The word <hi type="italic">schisma</hi> reaches all the way back to 1:10, where Paul first named the Corinthian disease: divisions. The body metaphor is the prescription for the disease that has afflicted the community from the start.</p>
          <p>The chapter concluded with a hierarchy among the gifts: "And God has appointed in the church first apostles, second prophets, third teachers, then miracles, then gifts of healing, helping, administrating, and various kinds of tongues" (12:28). The order is significant: tongues, which the Corinthians appear to have prized above all else, comes last. Paul then asked a series of rhetorical questions — "Are all apostles? Are all prophets? Are all teachers? Do all work miracles? Do all possess gifts of healing? Do all speak with tongues? Do all interpret?" (12:29-30) — each expecting the answer "no." No one gift belongs to all, and no one person possesses all gifts. Then came the transitional statement that leads directly into chapter 13: <hi type="italic">zeloute de ta charismata ta meizona. kai eti kath' hyperbolen hodon hymin deiknymi</hi> — "But earnestly desire the higher gifts. And I will show you a still more excellent way" (12:31). The word <hi type="italic">hodon</hi> ("way, path, road") introduces chapter 13 not as a digression about love in the abstract but as the "way" that surpasses even the most impressive spiritual gifts — the path that makes all other paths intelligible and all other gifts functional.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 14):</hi> Paul returns to the practical question of how spiritual gifts should function in the assembly. He opens with a direct instruction: "Pursue love, and earnestly desire the spiritual gifts, especially that you may prophesy" (14:1). The verb <hi type="italic">diokete</hi> ("pursue") applies to love — the subject of chapter 13. The verb <hi type="italic">zeloute</hi> ("earnestly desire") applies to spiritual gifts, echoing 12:31. Love is pursued; gifts are desired. The two commands are not in competition but in sequence: pursue love first, then desire the gifts, particularly prophecy. Chapter 14 then develops an extended comparison between tongues-speaking and prophecy. The one who speaks in tongues "speaks not to men but to God; for no one understands him" (14:2). The one who prophesies "speaks to people for their building up (<hi type="italic">oikodomen</hi>) and encouragement (<hi type="italic">paraklesin</hi>) and consolation (<hi type="italic">paramythian</hi>)" (14:3). The verb <hi type="italic">oikodomeo</hi> ("to build up") reappears as the governing criterion: "The one who speaks in a tongue builds up himself, but the one who prophesies builds up the church" (14:4). Tongues edifies the individual; prophecy edifies the community. Given that chapter 13 has just argued that love "does not seek its own" (13:5) and that everything must serve the common good, the superiority of prophecy over tongues follows directly.</p>
          <p>Paul does not forbid tongues — "I thank God that I speak in tongues more than all of you" (14:18) — but he insists that in the assembly, intelligibility governs: "in church I would rather speak five words with my mind in order to instruct others, than ten thousand words in a tongue" (14:19). The word <hi type="italic">nous</hi> ("mind") is central: worship must engage the mind, not bypass it. The argument reaches its practical conclusion in the regulation of worship: "when you come together, each one has a hymn, a lesson, a revelation, a tongue, or an interpretation. Let all things be done for building up (<hi type="italic">pros oikodomen</hi>)" (14:26). Everything in the assembly is measured by the criterion of community edification. Tongues-speakers must take turns, and only if an interpreter is present; prophets must take turns and be subject to evaluation by others (14:27-29). "For God is not a God of confusion (<hi type="italic">akatastasias</hi>) but of peace" (14:33). The chapter concludes: "all things should be done decently and in order" (14:40).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 13 connects these:</hi> Chapter 13 is not an interruption in the spiritual gifts discussion. It is the foundation on which the practical instruction of chapter 14 rests and the answer to the problem chapter 12 diagnosed. Chapter 12 established that the body has many members with diverse gifts, all distributed by one Spirit for the common good. But the Corinthians have been treating the gifts as occasions for competitive display — particularly the gift of tongues, which they appear to have elevated above all others as the mark of spiritual superiority. The body metaphor corrected the theology (all members are necessary, no gift is dispensable), but it did not yet name the governing principle that should direct the exercise of gifts within the body. Chapter 13 names that principle: <hi type="italic">agape</hi>, love. Chapter 14 then applies it: because love "does not seek its own" (13:5), gifts must be exercised for the community's benefit, not the individual's; because love "builds up" (from 8:1, the thesis that chapter 13 develops), prophecy — which builds up the church — is superior to tongues, which builds up only the speaker.</p>
          <p>The transition from 12:31 to 13:1 is seamless. Paul has just said "I will show you a still more excellent way" (12:31b). The word <hi type="italic">hodon</hi> ("way") is not a detour from the gifts discussion but the lens through which all gifts must be evaluated. The "way" is not an alternative to spiritual gifts but the manner in which they must be exercised. Without this way, even the most spectacular gifts are worthless (13:1-3). With this way, the gifts serve their intended purpose: the building up of the body.</p>
          <p>The transition from 13:13 to 14:1 is equally seamless. Chapter 13 concludes with "the greatest of these is love" (13:13). Chapter 14 opens with "pursue love" (<hi type="italic">diokete ten agapen</hi>) — the practical command that flows directly from the conclusion. The Corinthians are told to make love their pursuit, and then, within that pursuit, to desire the spiritual gifts, especially prophecy. The architecture is clear: love is the way (chapter 13); prophecy over tongues is the application of that way (chapter 14); the regulation of worship is the practical outworking of both (14:26-40).</p>
          <p>Chapter 13 also reaches far back into the letter's earlier arguments. In 8:1, Paul declared: "Knowledge puffs up, but love builds up" (<hi type="italic">he gnosis physioi, he de agape oikodomei</hi>). That verse was a compressed thesis statement; chapter 13 is its full development. The word <hi type="italic">physioo</hi> ("to puff up"), which has served as the diagnostic term for the Corinthian disease throughout the letter (4:6, 4:18, 4:19, 5:2, 8:1), reappears in 13:4: love "is not puffed up" (<hi type="italic">ou physiotai</hi>). The word <hi type="italic">gnosis</hi> ("knowledge"), which was the Corinthians' boast in 8:1 and the basis of their claim to freedom, reappears in 13:2 (if I have all knowledge), 13:8 (knowledge will pass away), 13:9 and 13:12 (we know in part). The word <hi type="italic">teleios</hi> ("mature, complete"), which appeared in 2:6 for those to whom Paul speaks wisdom, reappears in 13:10 ("when the complete comes"). The word <hi type="italic">nepios</hi> ("infant"), which Paul used in 3:1 to describe the Corinthians' spiritual immaturity, reappears in 13:11. The vocabulary of chapter 13 is drawn from the letter's own reservoir of key terms, and each reappearance carries the accumulated weight of its prior usage. Chapter 13 is not a self-contained poem inserted into the letter; it is the letter's argument in concentrated form.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.13.1-1Cor.13.3">
          <title>Verses 1-3: Without Love, the Most Impressive Gifts and Sacrifices Are Nothing</title>
          <p>"If I speak in the tongues of men and of angels, but have not love, I am a noisy gong or a clanging cymbal" (13:1).</p>
          <p>Paul begins with the gift the Corinthians evidently prized most: tongues. The phrase <hi type="italic">glossais ton anthropon kai ton angelon</hi> ("in the tongues of men and of angels") escalates from human languages to angelic speech. The Corinthians may have regarded their tongues-speaking as the language of heaven — a sign that the speaker had ascended to the realm of the angels and was participating in heavenly worship. The phrase "tongues of angels" suggests a tradition in which ecstatic speech was understood as the language spoken in God's throne room. The <hi type="italic">Testament of Job</hi> (48-50), a Jewish text likely circulating in various forms in the first century, describes Job's daughters receiving sashes that enabled them to speak in the language of angels and of the heavenly beings. Whether Paul endorses this understanding or merely grants it for the sake of argument, he accepts the most exalted possible claim for tongues-speaking: suppose you speak not only every human language but the very language of the angels. Even so, if love is absent, the speaker is <hi type="italic">chalkon echon e kymbalon alalazon</hi> — "a noisy gong or a clanging cymbal."</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">chalkos</hi> ("bronze, copper, brass") describes the metal itself, and by extension any object made from it. The word <hi type="italic">kymbalon</hi> ("cymbal") is a percussion instrument used in worship and festival. In the LXX, cymbals appear in Israel's worship: Psalm 150:5 (LXX 149:5) commands praise "with sounding cymbals" (<hi type="italic">en kymbalois euchois</hi>) and "with loud clashing cymbals" (<hi type="italic">en kymbalois alalagmou</hi>). In 1 Chronicles 15:16, the Levites are appointed with "instruments of music, harps and lyres and cymbals, sounding aloud" (<hi type="italic">en kymbalois</hi>) for the transport of the ark. Cymbals were legitimate instruments in Israel's worship, but used alone, without melody or harmonic context, they produce noise rather than music. The verb <hi type="italic">alalazo</hi> ("to clang, to clash, to ring, to wail") describes a sound that is loud but meaningless — a crash without content.</p>
          <p>In the Greco-Roman context, bronze gongs and cymbals were associated with the ecstatic worship of pagan cults, particularly the rites of Cybele and Dionysus, where loud percussive instruments accompanied frenzied states of possession. For the Corinthians, this comparison would carry a sharp edge: the tongues-speaking they prize, when divorced from love, produces the same meaningless racket as pagan ecstatic worship. It is noise without communication, sound without substance.</p>
          <p>The conditional structure <hi type="italic">ean</hi> ("if") with the subjunctive <hi type="italic">lalo</hi> ("I speak") marks this as a hypothetical case. Paul does not deny the gift of tongues. He grants it at its highest possible level — angelic speech — and then evacuates it of all value by removing love. The verb <hi type="italic">echo</hi> ("I have," in the negative <hi type="italic">me echo</hi>, "I do not have") treats love as something one possesses or does not possess, a governing disposition that shapes everything the person does. Without it (<hi type="italic">agapen de me echo</hi>), the person with the most exalted gift is nothing more than noise.</p>
          <p>"And if I have prophetic powers, and understand all mysteries and all knowledge, and if I have all faith, so as to remove mountains, but have not love, I am nothing" (13:2).</p>
          <p>Paul now moves from tongues to prophecy, which he will argue in chapter 14 is the superior gift. Even prophecy, the gift Paul himself ranks highest, is worthless without love. The word <hi type="italic">propheteia</hi> ("prophecy") describes speaking God's word to the community — the gift that "builds up and encourages and consoles" (14:3). Paul escalates further: not merely prophecy but <hi type="italic">eido ta mysteria panta kai pasan ten gnosin</hi> — "I understand all the mysteries and all the knowledge." The word <hi type="italic">mysterion</hi> ("mystery, secret, hidden plan") appeared in 2:7 for "God's wisdom, secret and hidden, which God decreed before the ages for our glory." The word <hi type="italic">gnosis</hi> ("knowledge") has been a contested term since 8:1, where the Corinthians boasted that "all of us possess knowledge." Paul hypothetically grants the knower total access: all mysteries, all knowledge. The word <hi type="italic">pasan/panta</hi> ("all") is repeated three times in the verse, building toward an absolute: all prophecy, all mysteries, all knowledge.</p>
          <p>Then comes all faith: <hi type="italic">pasan ten pistin hoste ore methistanai</hi> — "all faith, so as to remove mountains." The phrase recalls a saying of Jesus preserved in the tradition: "If you have faith like a grain of mustard seed, you will say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move" (Matthew 17:20; cf. Mark 11:23). Paul invokes the most extreme expression of faith imaginable — mountain-displacing faith. Even this, without love, renders the person <hi type="italic">ouden eimi</hi> — "I am nothing." The verdict is not that the person's work is diminished or their reward reduced. The verdict is ontological: the person is nothing. Without love, the most impressive constellation of spiritual endowments produces a zero.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">ouden</hi> ("nothing") recalls 3:7, where Paul said of himself and Apollos: "neither he who plants is anything, nor he who waters, but only God who gives the growth." There the "nothing" described the relative insignificance of human agents compared to God's action. Here the "nothing" is more severe: the gifted person without love is not merely insignificant in comparison to God but is, in themselves, nothing at all. Their gifts have not produced what gifts are meant to produce — the building up of the body in love — and therefore the person has produced nothing of value.</p>
          <p>"And if I give away all my possessions, and if I hand over my body so that I may boast, but have not love, I gain nothing" (13:3).</p>
          <p>Paul moves beyond spiritual gifts to acts of extraordinary sacrifice. The verb <hi type="italic">psomizo</hi> ("to feed, to give out in morsels, to distribute bit by bit") describes the breaking up and distribution of one's property to feed others. The phrase <hi type="italic">panta ta hyparchonta mou</hi> ("all my possessions") makes the sacrifice total: every possession, distributed to feed the hungry. In the LXX, feeding the poor is a mark of righteousness. Psalm 112:9 (LXX 111:9): "He has distributed freely; he has given to the poor; his righteousness endures forever." Isaiah 58:7: "Is it not to share your bread with the hungry and bring the homeless poor into your house?" The act Paul describes is the epitome of charitable generosity.</p>
          <p>The second clause is more difficult textually. Some manuscripts read <hi type="italic">ean paradō to soma mou hina kauthēsomai</hi> ("if I hand over my body to be burned"), while others read <hi type="italic">hina kauchēsomai</hi> ("so that I may boast"). The difference between <hi type="italic">kauthēsomai</hi> ("I will be burned") and <hi type="italic">kauchēsomai</hi> ("I will boast") is a single letter in Greek. If the reading is "to be burned," Paul envisions the ultimate physical sacrifice — martyrdom by fire. If the reading is "so that I may boast," Paul envisions handing over one's body to some extreme hardship or sacrifice in order to gain honor or renown. Either reading makes Paul's point: even the most dramatic act of self-sacrifice, carried to the point of either death or boasting-worthy suffering, profits nothing (<hi type="italic">ouden opheloumai</hi>) without love. The verb <hi type="italic">opheleo</hi> ("to profit, to benefit, to gain advantage") in the passive means "I am benefited, I gain." The negation is total: <hi type="italic">ouden</hi> — nothing. The person gains zero.</p>
          <p>The progression in verses 1-3 is deliberate. Paul moves from the spiritual gift the Corinthians most value (tongues) through the gift he himself most values (prophecy) to the most complete forms of spiritual knowledge (all mysteries, all knowledge) to the most extreme faith (mountain-moving) to the most radical forms of physical generosity (total distribution of possessions) and sacrifice (surrender of the body). At each stage, the result without love is the same: noise, nothing, no gain. The escalation makes the point inescapable. If tongues-speaking without love is worthless, the Corinthian who prizes tongues above all should take notice. But Paul does not stop there. Even prophecy, even comprehensive knowledge, even extraordinary faith, even martyrdom — all worthless without love. No gift, no sacrifice, no achievement of any kind compensates for the absence of love. Love is not a nice addition to the spiritual life; it is the condition without which the spiritual life is empty.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.13.4-1Cor.13.7">
          <title>Verses 4-7: The Character of Love</title>
          <p>"Love is patient and kind; love does not envy or boast; it is not arrogant or rude. It does not insist on its own way; it is not irritable or resentful; it does not rejoice at wrongdoing, but rejoices with the truth. Love bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, endures all things" (13:4-7).</p>
          <p>Paul now describes what love is and what it is not. The description unfolds in fifteen predications — two positive, eight negative, one contrastive pair, and four comprehensive positives. Each predication uses the verb in the third person singular with <hi type="italic">he agape</hi> ("love") as the subject: love itself is the actor. The description is not abstract but behavioral — love is defined by what it does and does not do, not by what it feels.</p>
          <p>The first-century hearer would recognize that nearly every characteristic Paul attributes to love is the opposite of a behavior the letter has diagnosed in the Corinthian community. The description functions simultaneously as a portrait of love and as a mirror held up to the community's failures.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love is patient" (<hi type="italic">he agape makrothymei</hi>)</hi> — the verb <hi type="italic">makrothymeo</hi> ("to be long-tempered, to bear patiently, to wait with endurance") describes restraint under provocation. The word is composed of <hi type="italic">makros</hi> ("long") and <hi type="italic">thymos</hi> ("passion, anger, spirit"). The long-tempered person does not react quickly to offense. In the LXX, <hi type="italic">makrothymia</hi> is a divine attribute. Exodus 34:6 (LXX): <hi type="italic">kyrios ho theos oiktirmon kai eleemon, makrothymos</hi> — "The Lord God, merciful and gracious, long-suffering." Numbers 14:18: <hi type="italic">makrothymos kyrios</hi> — "The Lord is long-suffering." Psalm 86:15 (LXX 85:15): <hi type="italic">sy, kyrie, ho theos oiktirmon kai eleemon, makrothymos</hi> — "You, O Lord, are a God merciful and gracious, long-suffering." The patience Paul attributes to love is the patience God exercises with his people — the restraint that delays judgment, that bears with failure, that does not destroy at the first provocation. The Corinthians have shown the opposite: they are quick to quarrel (1:11), quick to take offense, quick to assert their rights at the expense of others.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love is kind" (<hi type="italic">chresteuetai</hi>)</hi> — the verb <hi type="italic">chresteuomai</hi> ("to be kind, to show oneself useful, to act benevolently") appears only here in the New Testament. It derives from <hi type="italic">chrestos</hi> ("useful, kind, good, gracious"). In the LXX, the adjective <hi type="italic">chrestos</hi> describes God's goodness: Psalm 34:8 (LXX 33:9): <hi type="italic">geusasthe kai idete hoti chrestos ho kyrios</hi> — "Taste and see that the Lord is good." Psalm 100:5 (LXX 99:5): <hi type="italic">hoti chrestos ho kyrios</hi> — "For the Lord is good." Psalm 145:9 (LXX 144:9): <hi type="italic">chrestos kyrios tois sympasi</hi> — "The Lord is good to all." Kindness, like patience, is first a divine quality that love then embodies in human behavior.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love does not envy" (<hi type="italic">ou zeloi</hi>)</hi> — the verb <hi type="italic">zeloo</hi> ("to be jealous, to envy, to desire ardently") can be positive (to be zealous for something good) or negative (to envy what another has). Here the negative sense governs: love does not burn with resentment at another's gift, position, or recognition. The noun <hi type="italic">zelos</hi> ("jealousy") appeared in 3:3 as the primary evidence that the Corinthians were "still of the flesh": "for while there is jealousy (<hi type="italic">zelos</hi>) and strife (<hi type="italic">eris</hi>) among you, are you not of the flesh and behaving only in a human way?" The jealousy that diagnosed fleshliness in chapter 3 is the exact opposite of love in chapter 13. A community governed by love does not envy; a community consumed by jealousy over who has which gift, who follows which teacher, who holds which status, is a community without love.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love does not boast" (<hi type="italic">ou perpereuetai</hi>)</hi> — the verb <hi type="italic">perpereuomai</hi> ("to boast, to brag, to vaunt oneself") appears only here in the New Testament. It describes the self-promoting display that calls attention to one's own achievements or superiority. The Corinthians' boasting has been a persistent problem: they boast in their teachers (3:21), in their wisdom (4:7), in their tolerance of immorality (5:2, 5:6). Love does not do this.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love is not arrogant" (<hi type="italic">ou physiotai</hi>)</hi> — the verb <hi type="italic">physioō</hi> ("to puff up, to inflate, to make arrogant") is the single most diagnostic word in the entire letter for the Corinthian condition. It appeared in 4:6: "that none of you may be puffed up in favor of one against another." In 4:18: "some are puffed up, as though I were not coming to you." In 4:19: "the arrogance (<hi type="italic">physiosin</hi>) of those who are puffed up." In 5:2: "and you are puffed up" — the community's response to the incestuous man was not grief but inflation. In 8:1: "knowledge puffs up, but love builds up" — the thesis statement that chapter 13 develops. Now in 13:4, love <hi type="italic">ou physiotai</hi> — is not puffed up. The Corinthians' defining disease is the precise opposite of love's character. If love is not puffed up, and the Corinthians are puffed up, then the Corinthians do not have love, regardless of what gifts they possess. The word <hi type="italic">physioō</hi> appears seven times in 1 Corinthians — more than in all of Paul's other letters combined. It is the letter's signature diagnosis, and its appearance here, in the description of what love is not, draws the letter's entire argument to a point.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love is not rude" (<hi type="italic">ouk aschemonei</hi>)</hi> — the verb <hi type="italic">aschemoneo</hi> ("to behave indecently, to act improperly, to do what is unseemly") describes conduct that violates the standards of fitting behavior within the community. The cognate noun <hi type="italic">aschemosynē</hi> ("shameful behavior") and the adjective <hi type="italic">aschēmōn</hi> ("unseemly") appear in the letter's discussion of the body's less presentable parts (12:23). But the behavioral sense is primary here: love does not act in ways that shame or dishonor others. The Corinthians' conduct at the Lord's supper — gorging while others go hungry, humiliating those who have nothing (11:21-22) — is precisely the behavior of a community that is <hi type="italic">aschemonei</hi>.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love does not insist on its own way" (<hi type="italic">ou zetei ta heautes</hi>)</hi> — literally, "does not seek the things of itself." This is the exact principle Paul stated in 10:24: "Let no one seek his own good, but the good of his neighbor" (<hi type="italic">medeis to heautou zeteto alla to tou heterou</hi>). And in 10:33: Paul does not seek "my own advantage, but that of many, that they may be saved." The phrase <hi type="italic">ta heautes</hi> ("its own things") describes self-interest — the orientation that places personal benefit, personal rights, personal preferences above the well-being of others. The Corinthians have been relentlessly seeking their own: their own preferred teachers (chapters 1-4), their own sexual freedom (chapters 5-6), their own right to eat idol-food (chapters 8-10), their own meal before others arrive (11:21), their own preferred spiritual gift (chapters 12-14). Love does not do this. Love seeks the other's good.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love is not irritable" (<hi type="italic">ou paroxunetai</hi>)</hi> — the verb <hi type="italic">paroxuno</hi> ("to provoke, to irritate, to sharpen") in the passive means "to be provoked, to become irritated, to be exasperated." In the LXX, the word frequently describes God's anger when provoked by Israel's idolatry. Deuteronomy 9:18: "you had done what was evil in the sight of the Lord your God, provoking him to anger" (<hi type="italic">paroxunai auton</hi>). But when used of human relationships, <hi type="italic">paroxunetai</hi> describes the quick-tempered disposition that flares at every offense, the person who is constantly irritated by others. Love does not react this way.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love does not keep a record of wrongs" (<hi type="italic">ou logizetai to kakon</hi>)</hi> — the verb <hi type="italic">logizomai</hi> ("to reckon, to count, to calculate, to credit, to take into account") is an accounting term. In commercial and legal usage, it means to enter something into the ledger, to record a debt, to charge an amount to someone's account. In the LXX, the word appears in a crucial text: Psalm 32:2 (LXX 31:2): <hi type="italic">makarios aner hou ou me logisetai kyrios hamartian</hi> — "Blessed is the man against whom the Lord does not reckon sin." God's grace is defined by what he does not count. Love operates the same way: it does not keep a running ledger of wrongs received. It does not accumulate grievances. The person who catalogues every offense committed against them and produces the list at every conflict is operating in the opposite of love. The Corinthians, with their lawsuits against one another (6:1-8) and their competitive grievances, are keeping precisely the kind of record love refuses to maintain.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love does not rejoice at wrongdoing, but rejoices with the truth" (<hi type="italic">ou chairei epi te adikia, sunchairei de te aletheia</hi>)</hi> (13:6) — the verb <hi type="italic">chairo</hi> ("to rejoice, to be glad") appears in both its negative and positive forms. Love does not celebrate when injustice occurs (<hi type="italic">adikia</hi> — "unrighteousness, injustice, wrongdoing"). The word <hi type="italic">adikia</hi> echoes 6:1 and 6:9, where Paul addressed the Corinthians' lawsuits: "why not rather be wronged?" (<hi type="italic">adikeisthe</hi>, 6:7). The Corinthians who defraud their brothers (6:8: "you yourselves wrong and defraud — and that even your own brothers") are rejoicing in <hi type="italic">adikia</hi>. Love instead rejoices with (<hi type="italic">sunchairei</hi>, "rejoices together with") the truth (<hi type="italic">aletheia</hi>). The preposition <hi type="italic">syn</hi> ("with, together with") gives the verb a communal quality: love does not merely approve of truth from a distance but participates in truth's triumphs, celebrates when truth prevails.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love bears all things" (<hi type="italic">panta stegei</hi>)</hi> (13:7) — the verb <hi type="italic">stego</hi> ("to cover, to bear, to endure, to protect") can mean either "to cover over" (as in covering a roof — the related noun <hi type="italic">stege</hi> means "roof") or "to bear, to endure." Both senses may be active: love covers the faults of others (not exposing, not publicizing, not weaponizing another's failures) and love endures hardship without breaking. Paul used the same verb in 9:12: "we endure (<hi type="italic">stegomen</hi>) anything rather than put an obstacle in the way of the gospel of Christ." Paul's own practice of endurance is the embodiment of what love does.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love believes all things" (<hi type="italic">panta pisteuei</hi>)</hi> — the verb <hi type="italic">pisteuo</hi> ("to believe, to trust, to have faith in") does not describe gullibility or naivety. In the context of community life, it describes the disposition that extends trust to others rather than suspicion, that gives the benefit of the doubt, that does not assume the worst about a brother or sister. The jealousy and factionalism of the Corinthian community are built on suspicion and competitive evaluation. Love operates from trust.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love hopes all things" (<hi type="italic">panta elpizei</hi>)</hi> — the verb <hi type="italic">elpizo</hi> ("to hope, to expect, to look forward to") extends the posture of trust into the future. Love does not give up on people. It continues to hope for their growth, their restoration, their maturation. The word <hi type="italic">elpis</hi> ("hope") will reappear in verse 13 as one of the three things that abide.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Love endures all things" (<hi type="italic">panta hypomenei</hi>)</hi> — the verb <hi type="italic">hypomeno</hi> ("to endure, to remain under, to bear up under, to persevere") describes the capacity to sustain pressure without collapsing. The word is composed of <hi type="italic">hypo</hi> ("under") and <hi type="italic">meno</hi> ("to remain") — to remain under the weight, to stay in place when everything pushes you to abandon your post. In the LXX, the word describes the faithful who wait for God under duress. Psalm 27:14 (LXX 26:14): "Wait (<hi type="italic">hypomeinon</hi>) for the Lord; be strong, and let your heart take courage; wait (<hi type="italic">hypomeinon</hi>) for the Lord!" Isaiah 40:31: "they who wait (<hi type="italic">hoi hypomenontes</hi>) for the Lord shall renew their strength." The four-fold <hi type="italic">panta</hi> ("all things") — bears all, believes all, hopes all, endures all — gives the description a comprehensive, all-encompassing character. Love is not selective in its patience; it bears everything, trusts in every circumstance, hopes in every situation, endures every trial.</p>
          <p>The cumulative effect of the fifteen predications is devastating when read against the Corinthians' behavior. Point by point, the description of love is the description of what the Corinthian community is not. They are impatient with one another (factions, lawsuits). They are not kind (the wealthy humiliate the poor at the Lord's supper). They are jealous (<hi type="italic">zelos</hi>, 3:3). They boast (in their teachers, 3:21; in their tolerance, 5:6). They are puffed up (<hi type="italic">physioō</hi>, throughout the letter). They behave indecently (at the meal, at worship). They seek their own (their own rights, their own preferred gifts). They are irritable (quarreling, <hi type="italic">erides</hi>, 1:11). They keep records of wrongs (lawsuits, 6:1-8). They rejoice at wrongdoing (the case of incest, 5:1-2). They fail to bear, to trust, to hope, to endure. The portrait of love in 13:4-7 is simultaneously a portrait of everything the Corinthians are failing to be. It is not a sentimental poem but a surgical diagnosis.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.13.8-1Cor.13.12">
          <title>Verses 8-12: The Permanence of Love and the Temporality of Gifts</title>
          <p>"Love never ends. As for prophecies, they will pass away; as for tongues, they will cease; as for knowledge, it will pass away" (13:8).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">piptei</hi> in the phrase <hi type="italic">he agape oudepote piptei</hi> ("love never falls") means "to fall, to collapse, to fail." The negation <hi type="italic">oudepote</hi> ("never, at no time") is emphatic. Love does not fail — not ever, not under any circumstance. The verb <hi type="italic">piptō</hi> in the LXX describes the falling of walls (Joshua 6:5, 20 — the walls of Jericho fell), the falling of the mighty (2 Samuel 1:19 — "How the mighty have fallen"), and the collapse of what seemed permanent. Love is the thing that does not collapse. Everything else may fall; love does not.</p>
          <p>Paul then names three gifts that will pass away, and the three he names are not random. Prophecies (<hi type="italic">propheteia</hi>), tongues (<hi type="italic">glossai</hi>), and knowledge (<hi type="italic">gnosis</hi>) are the very gifts that dominate the Corinthians' spiritual landscape and the gifts Paul has been discussing throughout chapters 12-14.</p>
          <p>"Prophecies will be made inoperative" (<hi type="italic">prophetiai katargethesontai</hi>) — the verb <hi type="italic">katargeo</hi> ("to render inactive, to make of no effect, to abolish, to bring to an end") appeared in 1:28 for God's nullification of the things that "are" by choosing the things that "are not." It is a strong word: the gifts will not merely fade; they will be rendered inoperative, abolished, made obsolete. Prophecies will one day serve no function.</p>
          <p>"Tongues will cease" (<hi type="italic">glossai pausontai</hi>) — the verb <hi type="italic">pauo</hi> in the middle voice (<hi type="italic">pausontai</hi>) means "to cease, to stop of their own accord." Tongues will simply stop. The middle voice may suggest that tongues will cease on their own, without an external force shutting them down — they will reach their natural endpoint and stop.</p>
          <p>"Knowledge will be made inoperative" (<hi type="italic">gnosis katargethesetai</hi>) — the same verb as prophecy. Knowledge too will be abolished. The <hi type="italic">gnosis</hi> the Corinthians boast about — their theological understanding, their insight into the nature of idols and God — is a temporary gift. It will be made obsolete.</p>
          <p>The point is not that these gifts are bad but that they are temporary. They serve a function in the present age — the time between Christ's death and his return (cf. 11:26: "you proclaim the Lord's death until he comes"). But they belong to the interval. Love belongs to the destination.</p>
          <p>"For we know in part and we prophesy in part, but when the perfect comes, the partial will pass away" (13:9-10).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ek merous</hi> ("in part, partially, incompletely") describes the fragmentary nature of present knowledge and prophecy. The preposition <hi type="italic">ek</hi> with <hi type="italic">meros</hi> ("part, portion, share") indicates that what the community possesses now is a portion of something larger, a fragment of a whole. Present knowledge is not false; it is incomplete. Present prophecy is not fraudulent; it is partial. The community sees truly but not fully.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">to teleion</hi> ("the perfect, the complete, the mature, the whole") stands in direct contrast to <hi type="italic">ek merous</hi> ("in part"). The word <hi type="italic">teleios</hi> has already appeared in this letter. In 2:6, Paul spoke of wisdom "among the mature" (<hi type="italic">en tois teleiois</hi>) — those who have progressed beyond infancy. In 14:20, Paul will urge the Corinthians: "Brothers, do not be children (<hi type="italic">paidia</hi>) in your thinking. Be infants (<hi type="italic">nepiazete</hi>) in evil, but in your thinking be mature (<hi type="italic">teleioi</hi>)." The word describes completeness, wholeness, maturity — the state in which the partial gives way to the full.</p>
          <p>What is "the perfect" that is coming? Paul does not specify it by name. The context provides two complementary pointers. First, the eschatological framework of the letter places the community between two events: the death of Christ (11:26: "you proclaim the Lord's death") and the return of Christ (11:26: "until he comes"; 1:7: "as you wait for the revealing of our Lord Jesus Christ"; 15:23-28: the sequence of Christ's coming, his reign, and the subjection of all things to God). The "partial" characterizes the present age; the "complete" characterizes the age to come. When the Lord returns and all things are brought to their consummation, partial knowledge will be superseded by full knowledge, and partial prophecy will give way to unmediated reality. Second, verse 12 will specify the content of <hi type="italic">to teleion</hi> in relational terms: "then I shall know fully, even as I have been fully known." The perfect is the state of direct, unmediated knowledge of God — face-to-face encounter rather than mirror-reflected glimpses.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">katargethesetai</hi> ("will be made inoperative") reappears: when the complete arrives, the partial will be abolished. The partial is not destroyed because it was wrong but because it is no longer needed. A partial map becomes unnecessary when you arrive at the destination. A scaffolding is removed when the building is complete. The gifts of prophecy, tongues, and knowledge are the scaffolding of the present age; love is the building itself.</p>
          <p>"When I was a child, I spoke like a child, I thought like a child, I reasoned like a child. When I became a man, I gave up childish ways" (13:11).</p>
          <p>Paul introduces an analogy from human development. The word <hi type="italic">nepios</hi> ("child, infant") is the same word he used in 3:1 to describe the Corinthians: "I could not address you as spiritual people, but as people of the flesh, as infants (<hi type="italic">nepiois</hi>) in Christ." The connection is pointed: the community's attachment to spiritual gifts as markers of status is itself a form of spiritual infancy. Three verbs describe the child's behavior: <hi type="italic">elaloun</hi> ("I was speaking"), <hi type="italic">ephronoun</hi> ("I was thinking"), <hi type="italic">elogizomen</hi> ("I was reasoning") — all imperfect tense, describing continuous past activity. The child's speech, thought, and reasoning were characteristic of a child's level of development. They were not wrong for a child; they were appropriate to the stage. But <hi type="italic">hote gegona aner</hi> ("when I became a man") — the perfect tense of <hi type="italic">ginomai</hi>, marking a completed transition with lasting results — <hi type="italic">katergeka ta tou nepiou</hi> ("I put away the things of the child"). The same verb <hi type="italic">katargeo</hi> appears for the third time: the things of childhood are abolished, rendered inoperative, set aside as no longer appropriate.</p>
          <p>The analogy maps directly onto the gifts discussion. The spiritual gifts the Corinthians prize belong to the community's present stage of development — the "childhood" of the present age, the period of partial knowledge and incomplete understanding. These gifts are not wrong; they are appropriate to the stage. But they are not permanent. When maturity arrives — when <hi type="italic">to teleion</hi> comes — the gifts of the partial era will be set aside as naturally as an adult sets aside childhood toys. The Corinthians' fixation on tongues and other spectacular gifts is, by this analogy, a fixation on childhood things. Love is the mature reality that endures.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">nepios</hi> also connects to 14:20, where Paul will say: "Brothers, do not be children (<hi type="italic">paidia</hi>) in your thinking. Be infants (<hi type="italic">nepiazete</hi>) in evil, but in your thinking be mature (<hi type="italic">teleioi</hi>)." The letter consistently calls the Corinthians to grow up — to move from infancy to maturity, from partial understanding to complete understanding, from fascination with gifts to the governance of love.</p>
          <p>"For now we see in a mirror dimly, but then face to face. Now I know in part; then I shall know fully, even as I have been fully known" (13:12).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">esoptron</hi> ("mirror") describes the ancient bronze or copper mirror — a polished metal surface that produced a reflection, but one that was imperfect, somewhat distorted, indirect. In the LXX, the word appears in Wisdom of Solomon 7:26, where wisdom is described as "a reflection (<hi type="italic">esoptron</hi>) of eternal light, a spotless mirror of the working of God." The first-century mirror was not the clear glass mirror of the modern world. It provided a real image but a mediated, partial one. Looking at a mirror was looking at a representation, not at the thing itself.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">di' esoptrou en ainigmati</hi> ("through a mirror in an enigma/riddle") doubles the indirectness. The word <hi type="italic">ainigma</hi> ("riddle, dark saying, puzzle") appears in Numbers 12:8 (LXX), where God distinguishes Moses from other prophets: "With him I speak mouth to mouth (<hi type="italic">stoma kata stoma</hi>), clearly (<hi type="italic">eidos</hi>), and not in riddles (<hi type="italic">di' ainigmaton</hi>), and he beholds the form of the Lord." All prophets receive revelation <hi type="italic">en ainigmati</hi> — in riddles, through enigmatic visions and dark sayings. Moses alone received revelation "mouth to mouth," clearly, face to face. Paul's language in 13:12 draws directly on this distinction. The present mode of knowing God is the mode available to ordinary prophets: through a mirror, in riddles, indirectly. The future mode — <hi type="italic">tote de prosopon pros prosopon</hi> ("but then face to face") — is the mode God granted uniquely to Moses.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">prosopon pros prosopon</hi> ("face to face") echoes the LXX of several foundational passages. Genesis 32:30 (LXX 32:31): Jacob named the place Peniel, saying "I have seen God face to face (<hi type="italic">eidos theou prosopon pros prosopon</hi>), and yet my life has been delivered." Exodus 33:11: "The Lord used to speak to Moses face to face (<hi type="italic">enopios enopio</hi>), as a man speaks to his friend." Deuteronomy 34:10: "And there has not arisen a prophet since in Israel like Moses, whom the Lord knew face to face (<hi type="italic">prosopon kata prosopon</hi>)." In the LXX tradition, face-to-face encounter with God is the highest form of knowledge — unmediated, direct, personal. Paul says this is what awaits: when the partial gives way to the complete, the community will know God not through mirrors and riddles but face to face.</p>
          <p>The final clause — <hi type="italic">arti ginosko ek merous, tote de epignosomai kathos kai epegnosthen</hi> — "now I know in part, then I shall know fully, even as I have been fully known." The verb <hi type="italic">epignosomai</hi> (future of <hi type="italic">epiginosko</hi>, "to know fully, to recognize completely, to understand thoroughly") is strengthened by the prefix <hi type="italic">epi-</hi>, intensifying the basic verb <hi type="italic">ginosko</hi>. Present knowledge is <hi type="italic">ek merous</hi> — partial, fragmentary. Future knowledge will be <hi type="italic">epignosis</hi> — full recognition, complete understanding. And the standard for that future knowledge is established by the passive: <hi type="italic">kathos kai epegnosthen</hi> — "even as I have been fully known." God's knowledge of the believer is already complete. God already knows fully. The future consummation brings the believer's knowledge into correspondence with God's knowledge — the believer will know God as God already knows the believer.</p>
          <p>This language reaches back to 8:2-3, where Paul said: "If anyone thinks he knows something, he does not yet know as he ought to know. But if anyone loves God, he is known by him." The priority established in 8:2-3 was that being known by God matters more than knowing about God. Now in 13:12, that priority is maintained but a future convergence is promised: the one who is already known by God will come to know God fully. The present asymmetry — God knows me fully; I know partially — will be resolved at the consummation. The resolution does not mean the creature becomes equal to the Creator in knowledge; it means the creature's knowledge becomes direct, unmediated, face-to-face, no longer filtered through mirrors and riddles.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.13.13">
          <title>Verse 13: The Three That Remain</title>
          <p>"So now faith, hope, and love abide, these three; but the greatest of these is love" (13:13).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">nyni</hi> ("now") is temporal, referring to the present period — the time between the ages, the era in which the community lives as it awaits the completion. In this present time, three things <hi type="italic">menei</hi> ("abide, remain, endure"): <hi type="italic">pistis</hi> ("faith"), <hi type="italic">elpis</hi> ("hope"), and <hi type="italic">agape</hi> ("love"). The verb <hi type="italic">meno</hi> ("to remain, to abide, to stay, to endure") is the same verb used in 3:14 for the builder's work that "survives" the fire: "if the work of anyone survives (<hi type="italic">menei</hi>), he will receive a reward." The things that remain are the things that survive the testing. Prophecies are abolished; tongues cease; knowledge is made obsolete. But faith, hope, and love remain.</p>
          <p>The triad of faith, hope, and love appears elsewhere in Paul. In 1 Thessalonians 1:3, Paul remembers "your work of faith and labor of love and steadfastness of hope in our Lord Jesus Christ." In 1 Thessalonians 5:8, Paul urges: "putting on the breastplate of faith and love, and for a helmet the hope of salvation." The three appear to function as a summary of the Christian life: faith is the posture of trust toward God in the present; hope is the orientation toward God's future action; love is the active commitment to the good of others in the community and beyond.</p>
          <p>But the final clause breaks the equality: <hi type="italic">meizon de touton he agape</hi> — "but the greatest of these is love." The comparative <hi type="italic">meizon</hi> ("greater") elevates love above faith and hope. Why is love the greatest?</p>
          <p>The argument of the chapter provides the answer. Faith and hope belong to the present age's structure of partial knowledge and anticipation. Faith is the posture of trust in what is not yet seen. When the object of faith is fully present — when the Lord is revealed face to face — faith as trust in the unseen will be transformed into sight. Hope is the orientation toward what has not yet arrived. When the hoped-for consummation occurs, hope will be fulfilled and thus transformed. Faith and hope are oriented toward a future that will one day become present; when it does, they will be changed. Love, by contrast, is not oriented toward something it lacks. Love gives; it does not wait. Love bears, believes, hopes, endures (13:7). Love is the mode of God's own existence — God is patient, God is kind, God does not insist on his own way. When the consummation arrives and faith becomes sight and hope becomes possession, love will not be transformed because love is already the character of the consummated reality. Love is the substance of which faith and hope are the anticipation.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">meizon</hi> also carries resonance from 12:31, where Paul urged the Corinthians to desire <hi type="italic">ta charismata ta meizona</hi> — "the greater gifts." The Corinthians want the <hi type="italic">meizon</hi> gifts. Paul has just shown them the <hi type="italic">meizon</hi> reality: love. The "still more excellent way" (12:31) is not a higher gift alongside the others but the governing principle that makes all gifts valuable. Without love, the <hi type="italic">meizon</hi> gifts are nothing (13:1-3). With love, even the lesser gifts serve the body's edification. The Corinthians have been seeking greatness in the wrong category. They seek the greatest gift; Paul tells them the greatest thing is not a gift at all but the disposition that gives gifts their purpose.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.13">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 13 is the theological center of the three-chapter unit on spiritual gifts (chapters 12-14) and, in many ways, the theological center of the entire letter. It is not a detachable hymn to love inserted into a discussion of spiritual gifts. It is the argument's essential foundation: the principle without which the gifts discussion collapses and the principle that resolves the tensions diagnosed from the letter's opening paragraph.</p>
          <p>The chapter performs three interconnected functions.</p>
          <p>First (vv. 1-3), it establishes love as the non-negotiable condition for the value of all spiritual gifts and acts of sacrifice. The structure is hypothetical and escalating: if I have tongues — the gift the Corinthians prize — but not love, I am noise. If I have prophecy, knowledge, faith — the gifts Paul himself values most — but not love, I am nothing. If I sacrifice everything I own and even my body — the most extreme acts of devotion imaginable — but without love, I gain nothing. The progression is designed to dismantle every possible basis for self-congratulation except love. No gift, no sacrifice, no knowledge compensates for its absence. The Corinthians who compete for prominence through the display of spectacular gifts are building on nothing if love is absent from their exercise.</p>
          <p>Second (vv. 4-7), it describes love's character in terms that function simultaneously as a theological portrait and as a diagnosis of the community's failures. Each predication of what love is or is not corresponds to a specific problem Paul has addressed in the letter. Love is patient — the Corinthians are contentious. Love does not envy — the Corinthians are jealous. Love is not puffed up — the Corinthians are puffed up (the letter's signature diagnosis). Love does not seek its own — the Corinthians insist on their own rights. Love does not keep a record of wrongs — the Corinthians take each other to court. The description is not decorative but diagnostic: hold up this portrait and compare it to your community's behavior, and the gap between what you are and what love requires becomes visible.</p>
          <p>Third (vv. 8-13), it establishes love as the only permanent reality among the things the community possesses. Prophecy, tongues, and knowledge belong to the present age — the age of partial understanding, mirror-reflections, and riddles. They serve their function now, but they will be abolished when the complete arrives. Love never falls. Love survives the transition from the partial to the complete because love is the character of the completed reality itself. God's own nature is described throughout the LXX in the terms Paul uses for love: patient, kind, not keeping a record of wrongs (Psalm 32:2, where God does not reckon sin). Love is not waiting for the consummation; love is the substance of the consummation. Faith and hope are oriented toward the future; love is the future already operative in the present.</p>
          <p>The chapter's argument reaches back to 8:1 and brings it to full development. "Knowledge puffs up, but love builds up" was a compressed thesis; chapter 13 is its exposition. Knowledge — even theological knowledge about the one God and the nothingness of idols — produces inflation without love. Love produces construction — the building up of the body (<hi type="italic">oikodomeo</hi>, the governing verb from 3:9-14 through 14:26). The Corinthians possess knowledge; they lack love. Therefore they are puffed up instead of built up, divided instead of united, competing instead of serving.</p>
          <p>The chapter also advances the letter's sustained argument about maturity and immaturity. In 3:1, Paul called the Corinthians <hi type="italic">nepioi</hi> ("infants") because their factionalism proved their spiritual immaturity. In 13:11, Paul uses <hi type="italic">nepios</hi> again: "when I was a child, I spoke like a child, I thought like a child, I reasoned like a child." The gifts the Corinthians fixate on — especially tongues — are the speech of childhood. Growing up means putting away childish things and embracing the mature reality: love. The Corinthians' resistance to this message is itself evidence of their infancy. A community that prizes tongues over prophecy, display over edification, and personal status over communal well-being is a community that has not yet grown up.</p>
          <p>The chapter's placement between chapters 12 and 14 is structurally essential. Chapter 12 asked: how do the diverse gifts relate to one another within the one body? The answer: all are necessary, none is dispensable, and God distributes them as he wills. Chapter 14 will ask: how should gifts be exercised in the assembly? The answer: for building up, intelligibly, in orderly fashion. Chapter 13 provides the principle that connects the two: love. Love is the reason all members must be valued (because love does not say "I have no need of you"). Love is the reason prophecy is superior to tongues in the assembly (because love seeks the other's good, and prophecy is intelligible while tongues without interpretation is not). Love is the reason worship must be orderly (because love is not rude and does not insist on its own). Without chapter 13, chapter 14's practical instructions would lack their theological foundation. Without chapter 13, chapter 12's body metaphor would lack its animating principle. The body has many members (chapter 12); the body is animated by love (chapter 13); therefore the body's members exercise their gifts for the common good in an orderly assembly (chapter 14).</p>
          <p>Several vocabulary threads from earlier in the letter reach their culmination in this chapter.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">physioō</hi> ("to puff up"), which diagnosed the Corinthian disease in 4:6, 4:18, 4:19, 5:2, and 8:1, appears in 13:4 as the direct opposite of love's character. The diagnosis that has run through the letter now receives its definitive treatment: puffing up is not merely a social problem but the negation of love itself.</p>
          <p>The noun <hi type="italic">gnōsis</hi> ("knowledge"), which the Corinthians boasted about in 8:1 and which Paul affirmed as genuine but insufficient without love, appears in 13:2 (all knowledge), 13:8 (knowledge will pass away), 13:9 and 13:12 (we know in part). Knowledge is real and valuable, but it is temporary and partial. Love is permanent and complete.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">oikodomeō</hi> ("to build up"), which appeared in 3:9-14 for building on the foundation and in 8:1 for what love does, will appear again in 14:4 (prophecy builds up the church) and 14:26 (let all things be done for building up). Chapter 13 does not use the verb itself, but the concept governs the argument: love builds up because love seeks the other's good; gifts without love do not build up because they serve only the individual. The building metaphor that runs from chapter 3 through chapter 14 has love as its operating principle.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">teleios</hi> ("perfect, complete, mature") appeared in 2:6 for those to whom Paul speaks wisdom. Now in 13:10 it describes the consummation that will abolish the partial. The movement from <hi type="italic">nepios</hi> (infant, 3:1) to <hi type="italic">teleios</hi> (mature, 2:6, 13:10, 14:20) is the movement the Corinthians need to make — from the infantile fixation on gifts to the mature governance of love.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">prosopon pros prosopon</hi> ("face to face") in 13:12 draws on the LXX's deepest tradition of theophany — the unmediated encounter with God that was granted uniquely to Moses (Numbers 12:8, Deuteronomy 34:10). Paul says this encounter awaits the entire community. The present age offers mirrors and riddles; the consummation offers face-to-face knowledge of God. This eschatological promise grounds the chapter's argument about the temporality of gifts: gifts belong to the mirror-and-riddle age; love belongs to the face-to-face age.</p>
          <p>The chapter's final word — <hi type="italic">meizon de touton he agape</hi>, "the greatest of these is love" — answers the question the Corinthians have been asking throughout the letter, though they have been asking it in the wrong form. They asked: which teacher is greatest? (chapters 1-4). They asked: which knowledge is greatest? (chapter 8). They asked: which gift is greatest? (chapters 12-14). Paul's answer, at every turn, has been the same: the question itself is wrongly framed. The greatest thing is not a teacher, not a body of knowledge, not a spiritual gift. The greatest thing is love — the disposition that makes teachers servants instead of celebrities, that makes knowledge constructive instead of inflating, that makes gifts communal instead of competitive. Love is not one more item on the list of things to desire. Love is the reality that gives all other things their meaning, their function, and their value. Without it, the most impressive spiritual resume amounts to a noisy gong and a clanging cymbal. With it, even the humblest member of the body serves the good of the whole, and the community is built up into the dwelling place of God's Spirit.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.14">
        <title>1 Corinthians 14 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.14">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 12-13):</hi> Paul opened chapter 12 with the formula <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> ("now concerning"), which signals that he is addressing another question from the Corinthians' letter: "Now concerning spiritual things, brothers, I do not want you to be uninformed" (12:1). The word <hi type="italic">pneumatika</hi> ("spiritual things" or "spiritual gifts") in 12:1 governs the entire three-chapter unit (12-14). Paul began by establishing the criterion for genuine Spirit-speech: "no one speaking by the Spirit of God says 'Jesus is accursed,' and no one can say 'Jesus is Lord' except by the Holy Spirit" (12:3). He then developed the diversity-within-unity principle: there are varieties of gifts (<hi type="italic">charismata</hi>), varieties of service (<hi type="italic">diakoniai</hi>), and varieties of activities (<hi type="italic">energemata</hi>), but the same Spirit, the same Lord, the same God (12:4-6). Each person receives a manifestation of the Spirit <hi type="italic">pros to sympheron</hi> — "for the common good" (12:7). This phrase is the first statement of the principle that will dominate chapter 14: spiritual gifts exist for the benefit of the community, not for the individual.</p>
          <p>Paul listed the gifts — wisdom, knowledge, faith, healing, miracles, prophecy, distinguishing spirits, tongues, interpretation of tongues — and attributed them all to "one and the same Spirit, who apportions to each one individually as he wills" (12:11). Two features of this list are significant for chapter 14. First, tongues and interpretation of tongues appear last. Second, the Spirit distributes as he wills, not as the individual desires.</p>
          <p>Paul then developed the body metaphor at length (12:12-27). The body is one and has many members; no member can say to another "I have no need of you" (12:21). God has composed the body so that "there may be no division (<hi type="italic">schisma</hi>) in the body, but that the members may have the same care for one another" (12:25). The word <hi type="italic">schisma</hi> reaches back to 1:10 and 11:18 — the same disease of division that Paul has been diagnosing throughout the letter. The body metaphor is the cure: the community is one body, each member essential, none dispensable. Paul concluded: "Now you are the body of Christ, and individually members of it" (12:27). He then listed roles God has appointed in the <hi type="italic">ekklesia</hi>: "first apostles, second prophets, third teachers, then miracles, then gifts of healing, helping, administrating, and various kinds of tongues" (12:28). Again, tongues appear last. The rhetorical questions of 12:29-30 — "Are all apostles? Are all prophets? ... Do all speak with tongues? Do all interpret?" — expect the answer "no." Not everyone has the same gift. Then the pivotal transition: "But earnestly desire the higher gifts. And I will show you a still more excellent way" (12:31).</p>
          <p>The "more excellent way" is love (chapter 13). Paul opened with a series of conditional statements that strip every spiritual gift of its value when exercised without love. "If I speak in the tongues of men and of angels, but have not love, I am a noisy gong or a clanging cymbal" (13:1). The tongues of angels — the most exalted form of glossolalia imaginable — without love produces only noise. "If I have prophetic powers, and understand all mysteries and all knowledge, and if I have all faith, so as to remove mountains, but have not love, I am nothing" (13:2). Even prophecy with total knowledge and mountain-moving faith, without love, produces a person who is nothing. "If I give away all I have, and if I deliver up my body to be burned, but have not love, I gain nothing" (13:3). Even the ultimate acts of generosity and sacrifice, without love, gain nothing.</p>
          <p>Paul then described love's character in terms that directly address the Corinthian disease: love "does not envy or boast; it is not arrogant or rude. It does not insist on its own way" (13:4-5). The verb <hi type="italic">perpereuomai</hi> ("to boast") and the adjective <hi type="italic">physioutai</hi> ("is puffed up, is arrogant") echo the <hi type="italic">phusioo</hi> ("to puff up") of 4:6, 4:18, 4:19, 5:2, 8:1 — the persistent Corinthian problem of inflation. Love <hi type="italic">ou zetei ta heautes</hi> ("does not seek its own") — the same principle stated in 10:24, "let no one seek his own good, but the good of his neighbor," and in 10:33, "not seeking my own advantage." The character of love is the character that chapters 8-10 demanded in the idol-food argument.</p>
          <p>Paul then set the gifts within an eschatological frame: "Love never ends. As for prophecies, they will pass away; as for tongues, they will cease; as for knowledge, it will pass away" (13:8). The gifts are partial and temporary; love is permanent. The gifts belong to the present partial age; love belongs to the age that is coming and already arriving. "For now we see in a mirror dimly, but then face to face" (13:12). The gifts are the dim mirror; love is the substance they reflect. "So now faith, hope, and love abide, these three; but the greatest of these is love" (13:13).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 15):</hi> Paul turns to another major topic with the formula <hi type="italic">gnorizo de hymin</hi> ("now I would remind you" or "I make known to you"): the gospel he preached to them, in which they stand, and by which they are being saved — "if you hold fast to the word I preached to you — unless you believed in vain" (15:1-2). The conditional <hi type="italic">ean katechete</hi> ("if you hold fast") and the phrase <hi type="italic">ektos ei me eike episteusate</hi> ("unless you believed in vain") echo the conditional quality of the community's standing that has run through the entire letter. Paul then recites the <hi type="italic">kerygma</hi>: "Christ died for our sins in accordance with the Scriptures, that he was buried, that he was raised on the third day in accordance with the Scriptures" (15:3-4). The shift from the practical assembly instructions of chapter 14 to the foundational confession of chapter 15 is a shift from ordering the community's gathered worship to grounding the community's faith in the resurrection. The connection is not incidental: the community that gathers in chapter 14 does so because of the gospel events of chapter 15. The worship Paul is ordering in chapter 14 is the worship of a community constituted by the death, burial, and resurrection of Christ.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 14 connects these:</hi> Chapter 14 is the practical application of everything Paul established in chapters 12-13. Chapter 12 established the principle: there are many gifts, distributed by one Spirit, for the common good, and the community is one body with many members. Chapter 13 established the governing criterion: love is the way in which gifts must be exercised, and love does not seek its own but seeks the good of the other. Chapter 14 takes these principles and applies them to the specific problem in the Corinthian assembly: the disordered, self-serving exercise of the gift of tongues.</p>
          <p>The opening words of chapter 14 — <hi type="italic">diokete ten agapen, zeloute de ta pneumatika, mallon de hina propheteuete</hi> ("pursue love, and earnestly desire the spiritual gifts, especially that you may prophesy") — fuse the two preceding chapters into a single command. "Pursue love" reaches back to chapter 13. "Earnestly desire the spiritual gifts" reaches back to 12:31 ("earnestly desire the higher gifts"). "Especially that you may prophesy" introduces the specific argument of chapter 14: prophecy is the gift that most directly serves the community because it is intelligible speech that builds up others.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">oikodome</hi> ("building up, edification") and the verb <hi type="italic">oikodomeo</hi> ("to build up") govern the entire chapter. The word appears in verses 3, 4 (twice), 5, 12, 17, and 26. This vocabulary reaches back through the letter: in 3:9-14, the community was God's building (<hi type="italic">oikodome</hi>) and Paul described building on the foundation; in 8:1, love builds up (<hi type="italic">oikodomei</hi>) while knowledge puffs up; in 8:10, the verb was used with devastating irony when the weak person's conscience was "built up" toward self-destruction; in 10:23, "not all things build up." The criterion of <hi type="italic">oikodome</hi> has been Paul's consistent measure for evaluating behavior throughout the letter. Chapter 14 makes it the explicit, named standard for everything that happens in the assembly.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">synerchomai</hi> ("to come together, to assemble") reappears in 14:23 and 14:26, connecting the chapter to 11:17-34, where the same verb appeared five times in describing the communal meal. The assemblies Paul addressed in chapter 11 — where the community comes together for the Lord's supper — are the same assemblies he addresses in chapter 14 — where the community comes together for prophesying, teaching, singing, and speaking in tongues. The gathering is one; the problems are multiple. In chapter 11, the problem was social division at the meal. In chapter 14, the problem is communicative disorder in the exercise of gifts. In both cases, the behavior contradicts the community's identity as one body.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.14.1-1Cor.14.5">
          <title>Verses 1-5: The Governing Criterion — Prophecy Builds Up the Assembly</title>
          <p>"Pursue love, and earnestly desire the spiritual gifts, especially that you may prophesy" (14:1).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">diokete</hi> ("pursue, chase, follow after, run toward") is the present imperative of <hi type="italic">dioko</hi>, a verb that describes vigorous, active pursuit. In 1 Thessalonians 5:15, Paul uses it for pursuing what is good. The word implies that love does not arrive passively; it must be chased. The Corinthians must actively run after love as a hunter runs after prey.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">zeloute</hi> ("earnestly desire, be zealous for") is the present imperative of <hi type="italic">zeloo</hi>. The Corinthians are to desire the <hi type="italic">pneumatika</hi> — the spiritual things, the manifestations of the Spirit listed in 12:8-10. Paul does not say "stop desiring spiritual gifts." He says desire them — but within the framework of pursuing love. The two imperatives work together: pursue love, and within that pursuit, desire the gifts. Love governs the desiring.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">mallon de hina propheteuete</hi> ("and especially that you may prophesy") identifies one gift as the one most worth desiring. The word <hi type="italic">mallon</hi> ("more, rather, especially") is comparative: desire all the gifts, but especially desire prophecy. The reason will unfold throughout the chapter.</p>
          <p>"For one who speaks in a tongue speaks not to men but to God; for no one understands him, but he utters mysteries in the Spirit" (14:2).</p>
          <p>The conjunction <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") provides the reason prophecy should be preferred. The phrase <hi type="italic">ho lalon glosse</hi> ("the one speaking in a tongue") describes the person exercising the gift of tongues. The word <hi type="italic">glossa</hi> can mean "tongue" (the physical organ), "language," or the specific phenomenon of Spirit-prompted speech that is unintelligible to the hearers. Paul says this person <hi type="italic">ouk anthropois lalei alla theo</hi> — "speaks not to people but to God." The speech is directed vertically (toward God) rather than horizontally (toward the community). The reason: <hi type="italic">oudeis gar akouei</hi> ("for no one hears/understands"). The verb <hi type="italic">akouo</hi> can mean both "to hear" (physically) and "to understand" (comprehend). The people in the assembly hear the sound but do not understand the content. The tongue-speaker <hi type="italic">pneumati de lalei mysteria</hi> — "speaks mysteries in/by the Spirit." The word <hi type="italic">mysteria</hi> ("mysteries, hidden things, secrets") describes content that is concealed from the hearers. The Spirit (<hi type="italic">pneumati</hi>) is the agent or sphere of the speaking. The speech is genuine — it is Spirit-prompted and directed to God — but it is inaccessible to everyone else in the room.</p>
          <p>"On the other hand, the one who prophesies speaks to people for their upbuilding and encouragement and consolation" (14:3).</p>
          <p>The contrast is introduced by <hi type="italic">ho de propheteuon</hi> ("but the one who prophesies"). This person <hi type="italic">anthropois lalei</hi> — "speaks to people." The direction is horizontal: the speech reaches the community. Paul specifies three effects of prophecy:</p>
          <p>First, <hi type="italic">oikodomen</hi> ("upbuilding, building up, edification"). This is the chapter's controlling word. Prophecy builds up the community — it adds structural strength to the body, it constructs and reinforces.</p>
          <p>Second, <hi type="italic">paraklesin</hi> ("encouragement, exhortation, comfort, consolation"). The word <hi type="italic">paraklesis</hi> comes from <hi type="italic">parakaleo</hi> ("to call alongside, to summon, to urge, to encourage, to comfort"). In the LXX, the verb appears in Isaiah 40:1: "Comfort, comfort (<hi type="italic">parakaleite, parakaleite</hi>) my people, says your God." <hi type="italic">Paraklesis</hi> is the speech that strengthens the hearer to continue, that renews resolve, that brings comfort in hardship.</p>
          <p>Third, <hi type="italic">paramythian</hi> ("consolation, comfort, encouragement"). The word <hi type="italic">paramythia</hi> comes from <hi type="italic">paramytheomai</hi> ("to speak words of comfort, to console, to soothe"). It overlaps with <hi type="italic">paraklesis</hi> but carries a more personal, intimate tone — it is the comfort spoken to someone in distress.</p>
          <p>All three effects describe what the speech does for the hearers. Prophecy is intelligible speech that strengthens, encourages, and consoles the community. It is speech directed toward others for their benefit.</p>
          <p>"The one who speaks in a tongue builds up himself, but the one who prophesies builds up the church" (14:4).</p>
          <p>The verse states the contrast in its starkest form. The tongue-speaker <hi type="italic">heauton oikodomei</hi> ("builds up himself"). The verb <hi type="italic">oikodomeo</hi> is used positively — the self-edification is real and genuine. Paul is not dismissing tongues as worthless. But the reflexive pronoun <hi type="italic">heauton</hi> ("himself") marks the limit: the benefit does not extend beyond the speaker. The one who prophesies <hi type="italic">ekklesion oikodomei</hi> ("builds up the assembly/church"). The word <hi type="italic">ekklesia</hi> — the gathered community, the body of Christ, the <hi type="italic">qahal</hi> of God's people — is the object that prophecy builds up. The contrast is not between worthless and valuable but between individual and communal benefit.</p>
          <p>Given the argument of chapters 12-13, the communal benefit is clearly the higher value. The entire body metaphor of chapter 12 insisted that each member exists for the sake of the body, not for itself alone. The love of chapter 13 "does not seek its own" (<hi type="italic">ou zetei ta heautes</hi>, 13:5). A gift that builds up only the individual, exercised in the assembly where the community gathers, fails the criterion that governs the letter: the common good (<hi type="italic">to sympheron</hi>, 12:7), the building up of others (10:23-24), the love that does not seek its own (13:5).</p>
          <p>"Now I want you all to speak in tongues, but even more to prophesy. The one who prophesies is greater than the one who speaks in tongues, unless someone interprets, so that the church may be built up" (14:5).</p>
          <p>Paul reaffirms the legitimacy of tongues: <hi type="italic">thelo de pantas hymas lalein glossais</hi> — "I want all of you to speak in tongues." The desire is genuine. Tongues are a legitimate gift of the Spirit, and Paul wishes every member of the community had it. But — <hi type="italic">mallon de hina propheteuete</hi> — "and even more that you prophesy." The word <hi type="italic">mallon</hi> ("more, rather") again marks the comparative preference.</p>
          <p>The reason for the preference: <hi type="italic">meizon de ho propheteuon e ho lalon glossais</hi> — "the one who prophesies is greater than the one who speaks in tongues." The word <hi type="italic">meizon</hi> ("greater") does not describe the person's worth or spiritual status but the function's value to the community. The prophesier is "greater" because their speech serves the assembly directly.</p>
          <p>Then comes the qualifying exception: <hi type="italic">ektos ei me diermeneue</hi> — "unless someone interprets." The verb <hi type="italic">diermeneuo</hi> ("to interpret, to translate, to explain") describes the act of rendering the tongue into intelligible language. If someone interprets, the tongue becomes functionally equivalent to prophecy: it now conveys intelligible content to the assembly. The purpose clause confirms this: <hi type="italic">hina he ekklesia oikodomen labe</hi> — "so that the assembly may receive building up." The word <hi type="italic">oikodomen</hi> ("building up") appears for the third time in three verses. The governing criterion is unmistakable: everything in the assembly must serve the building up of the community.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.14.6-1Cor.14.12">
          <title>Verses 6-12: Intelligibility and the Analogy of Instruments</title>
          <p>"Now, brothers, if I come to you speaking in tongues, how will I benefit you unless I bring you some revelation or knowledge or prophecy or teaching?" (14:6).</p>
          <p>Paul puts himself into the scenario — <hi type="italic">ean eltho pros hymas glossais lalon</hi> ("if I come to you speaking in tongues") — to prevent the instruction from feeling like an attack on others. Even Paul's own tongues-speaking would not benefit (<hi type="italic">opheleso</hi>, from <hi type="italic">opheleo</hi>, "to benefit, to profit, to help") the Corinthians unless it were accompanied by intelligible content. He lists four forms of intelligible speech: <hi type="italic">apokalypsis</hi> ("revelation"), <hi type="italic">gnosis</hi> ("knowledge"), <hi type="italic">propheteia</hi> ("prophecy"), <hi type="italic">didache</hi> ("teaching"). These are the modes of speech that benefit the assembly because they convey comprehensible content. Tongues without interpretation conveys none of them.</p>
          <p>"If even lifeless instruments, such as the flute or the harp, do not give distinct notes, how will anyone know what is played? And if the bugle gives an indistinct sound, who will get ready for battle?" (14:7-8).</p>
          <p>Paul turns to analogy. The word <hi type="italic">apsycha</hi> ("lifeless, without soul") describes musical instruments — things that produce sound (<hi type="italic">phonen didonta</hi>, "giving voice/sound") without possessing life. Even these inanimate objects must produce distinct notes (<hi type="italic">diastolon tois phthongois</hi>, "distinction in the tones") for their sound to communicate. If the <hi type="italic">aulos</hi> ("flute, pipe") or the <hi type="italic">kithara</hi> ("harp, lyre") produces undifferentiated noise, no one will know what is being played (<hi type="italic">pos gnosthesetai to auloumenon e to kitharizomenon</hi> — "how will what is being piped or harped be recognized?").</p>
          <p>The military analogy of verse 8 intensifies the point. The <hi type="italic">salpinx</hi> ("trumpet, bugle") was the instrument of military command. In Israel's own history, the trumpet signaled advance, retreat, assembly, and alarm (Numbers 10:1-10). If the trumpet gives <hi type="italic">adelon phonen</hi> ("an unclear, uncertain, indistinct sound"), no one will prepare for battle (<hi type="italic">tis paraskeuasetai eis polemon</hi>). The consequence of indistinct communication is not merely aesthetic failure; it is practical paralysis. The army that cannot understand the trumpet call cannot fight. The community that cannot understand the speech in its assembly cannot be built up.</p>
          <p>"So with yourselves, if with your tongue you utter speech that is not intelligible, how will anyone know what is said? For you will be speaking into the air" (14:9).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">houtos kai hymeis</hi> ("so also you") applies the analogy directly. The word <hi type="italic">eusemon</hi> ("clear, intelligible, easily understood") describes speech that can be grasped by the hearer. Speech that is not <hi type="italic">eusemon</hi> fails the basic purpose of communication: <hi type="italic">pos gnosthesetai to laloumenon</hi> — "how will what is spoken be known?" The consequence: <hi type="italic">esesthe gar eis aera lalountes</hi> — "for you will be speaking into the air." The phrase <hi type="italic">eis aera</hi> ("into the air") describes speech that reaches no one. The words leave the mouth and dissipate. They do not land. They do not build. They are lost.</p>
          <p>"There are doubtless many different languages in the world, and none is without meaning, but if I do not know the meaning of the language, I will be a foreigner to the speaker and the speaker a foreigner to me" (14:10-11).</p>
          <p>Paul now moves from the analogy of musical instruments to the analogy of human languages. The word <hi type="italic">gene phonon</hi> ("kinds of voices/languages") acknowledges the diversity of languages in the world. None of them is <hi type="italic">aphonon</hi> ("voiceless, meaningless") — every language has meaning to its speakers. But meaning requires shared understanding. If Paul does not know <hi type="italic">ten dynamin tes phones</hi> ("the power/meaning of the language"), he will be <hi type="italic">barbaros</hi> ("a foreigner, a barbarian") to the speaker, and the speaker will be <hi type="italic">barbaros</hi> to him. The word <hi type="italic">barbaros</hi> originally imitated the sound of incomprehensible speech — "bar bar bar" — and came to mean anyone who spoke a language one could not understand. The result of unintelligible speech is mutual foreignness. In the assembly, which is supposed to be one body, tongues without interpretation creates the experience of being strangers to one another. The gift that should express unity produces the experience of alienation.</p>
          <p>"So with yourselves, since you are eager for manifestations of the Spirit, strive to excel in building up the church" (14:12).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">houtos kai hymeis</hi> ("so also you") applies the language analogy. Paul acknowledges the Corinthians' eagerness: <hi type="italic">epei zelotai este pneumaton</hi> — "since you are zealots for spirits/spiritual things." The word <hi type="italic">zelotai</hi> ("zealots, eager ones") describes their intense desire for spiritual manifestations. Paul does not rebuke this desire. He redirects it: <hi type="italic">pros ten oikodomen tes ekklesias zeteite hina perisseuete</hi> — "seek to abound for the building up of the assembly." The verb <hi type="italic">perisseuo</hi> ("to abound, to overflow, to excel") expresses abundance. The Corinthians want spiritual abundance; Paul says: direct that abundance toward the building up of the community. The word <hi type="italic">oikodomen</hi> appears for the fifth time. The repetition is relentless and deliberate: the community's edification is the measure of everything.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.14.13-1Cor.14.19">
          <title>Verses 13-19: The Mind and the Spirit — Both Must Function</title>
          <p>"Therefore, one who speaks in a tongue should pray that he may interpret" (14:13).</p>
          <p>The conjunction <hi type="italic">dio</hi> ("therefore, for this reason") draws the practical conclusion from the preceding argument. If building up the community is the criterion, and tongues without interpretation do not build up, then the tongue-speaker should pray for the additional gift of interpretation. The verb <hi type="italic">proseuschestho</hi> ("let him pray") is the present imperative: this should be the tongue-speaker's ongoing practice.</p>
          <p>"For if I pray in a tongue, my spirit prays but my mind is unfruitful" (14:14).</p>
          <p>The conjunction <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") explains why interpretation is necessary. Paul introduces a distinction between <hi type="italic">pneuma</hi> ("spirit") and <hi type="italic">nous</hi> ("mind"). If he prays in a tongue (<hi type="italic">glosse</hi>), his <hi type="italic">pneuma</hi> prays — his spirit is active and engaged in genuine communication with God. But his <hi type="italic">nous</hi> is <hi type="italic">akarpos</hi> ("unfruitful, barren, unproductive"). The word <hi type="italic">akarpos</hi> comes from the alpha-privative <hi type="italic">a-</hi> and <hi type="italic">karpos</hi> ("fruit") — the mind produces no fruit. It is not that the mind is sinning or failing; it is simply not engaged. The spirit operates without the mind's participation. The speech bypasses the cognitive faculties.</p>
          <p>This is Paul's most precise description of what happens in tongues-speaking. The spirit is active; the mind is not. The speech is real (it is directed to God, v. 2; the spirit prays genuinely, v. 14); but it is not cognitive. It does not pass through the <hi type="italic">nous</hi>. And because it does not pass through the speaker's mind, it cannot be communicated to other minds in the room.</p>
          <p>"What am I to do? I will pray with my spirit, but I will pray with my mind also; I will sing praise with my spirit, but I will sing with my mind also" (14:15).</p>
          <p>The question <hi type="italic">ti oun estin</hi> ("what then is [the solution]?") introduces Paul's resolution. The answer is not to suppress the spirit but to engage both spirit and mind. Paul will do both: <hi type="italic">proseuxomai to pneumati, proseuxomai de kai to noi</hi> — "I will pray with the spirit, and I will pray with the mind also." The word <hi type="italic">kai</hi> ("also") is crucial. It is additive, not substitutive. Paul does not say "I will pray with the mind instead of the spirit." He says "I will pray with the spirit, and also with the mind." Both faculties must be active.</p>
          <p>The same applies to singing: <hi type="italic">psalo to pneumati, psalo de kai to noi</hi> — "I will sing with the spirit, and I will sing with the mind also." The verb <hi type="italic">psallo</hi> ("to sing, to sing psalms, to make music") describes the musical worship of the assembly. The verb appears in the LXX throughout the Psalter: Psalm 27:6 (LXX 26:6), "I will sing and make melody (<hi type="italic">psalo</hi>) to the Lord"; Psalm 57:7 (LXX 56:8), "I will sing and make melody (<hi type="italic">psalo</hi>)." The singing in the assembly must engage both the spiritual and cognitive dimensions of the worshiper.</p>
          <p>"Otherwise, if you give thanks with your spirit, how can anyone in the position of an outsider say 'Amen' to your thanksgiving when he does not know what you are saying?" (14:16).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">epei</hi> ("otherwise, since") introduces the consequence of spirit-only worship. Paul shifts to the second person (<hi type="italic">euloges</hi>, "you bless/give thanks") to address the Corinthian directly. The phrase <hi type="italic">ho anaplerion ton topon tou idiotou</hi> ("the one filling the place of the uninstructed/outsider") describes someone present in the assembly who does not understand the tongue. The word <hi type="italic">idiotes</hi> (from <hi type="italic">idios</hi>, "one's own, private") means "a private person, one who is not an expert, an uninitiated person, an outsider." In this context, it describes someone who is in the assembly but cannot follow the tongue-speech — perhaps a newer member, a visitor, or simply someone who does not possess the gift of interpretation.</p>
          <p>This person <hi type="italic">pos erei to Amen</hi> ("how will he say the Amen") upon your <hi type="italic">eucharistia</hi> ("thanksgiving, giving of thanks")? The word Amen is a Hebrew word meaning "so be it, truly, let it be confirmed." In Jewish synagogue practice, and carried over into the early assemblies, the congregation said "Amen" at the conclusion of a prayer or blessing to confirm and participate in what had been said. The Amen was the community's ratification of the prayer — their collective agreement, their shared participation in the thanksgiving. But the Amen requires understanding. One cannot ratify what one has not comprehended. If the thanksgiving is in an unintelligible tongue, the <hi type="italic">idiotes</hi> cannot say Amen, because <hi type="italic">epide ti legeis ouk oiden</hi> ("since he does not know what you are saying"). The tongue-speaker's prayer may be genuine and beautiful — but it has cut the rest of the assembly out of the worship. The thanksgiving has become a private act performed in a public space.</p>
          <p>"For you may be giving thanks well enough, but the other person is not being built up" (14:17).</p>
          <p>The concession is genuine: <hi type="italic">sy men gar kalos eucharisteis</hi> — "you indeed give thanks well." Paul does not question the quality or sincerity of the tongue-speaker's thanksgiving. It is <hi type="italic">kalos</hi> ("well, beautifully, finely"). The spirit is genuinely engaged with God. But — <hi type="italic">all' ho heteros ouk oikodomeitai</hi> — "but the other person is not being built up." The word <hi type="italic">heteros</hi> ("the other") is the same word Paul used in 10:24: "let no one seek his own good, but the good of the other (<hi type="italic">tou heterou</hi>)." The criterion is not the quality of the speaker's own worship experience but the effect on the other. The beautiful thanksgiving that builds up no one else in the room fails the test. The word <hi type="italic">oikodomeitai</hi> ("is being built up") appears for the sixth time.</p>
          <p>"I thank God that I speak in tongues more than all of you" (14:18).</p>
          <p>Paul establishes his credentials before delivering the next instruction. The phrase <hi type="italic">eucharisto to theo, panton hymon mallon glossais lalo</hi> — "I thank God, I speak in tongues more than all of you" — is a claim of extensive personal experience with the very gift he is subordinating to prophecy. Paul is not an outsider criticizing what he does not know. He speaks in tongues more than any of them. His instruction to prefer prophecy in the assembly comes from the position of one who has more experience with tongues than the people he is instructing.</p>
          <p>"Nevertheless, in church I would rather speak five words with my mind in order to instruct others, than ten thousand words in a tongue" (14:19).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">alla</hi> ("but, nevertheless") introduces the contrast between private experience and public practice. The phrase <hi type="italic">en ekklesia</hi> ("in the assembly, in the church") marks the crucial distinction. Paul's extensive tongues-speaking (v. 18) presumably occurs in private prayer. But <hi type="italic">en ekklesia</hi> — in the gathered community — he would rather speak <hi type="italic">pente logous to noi mou</hi> ("five words with my mind") than <hi type="italic">myrious logous en glosse</hi> ("ten thousand words in a tongue"). The ratio is 1:2,000. Five words that instruct versus ten thousand words that do not. The word <hi type="italic">myrios</hi> ("ten thousand, innumerable") is the largest numerical unit in Greek — it represents an uncountable quantity. Five intelligible words are worth more than an uncountable quantity of unintelligible ones.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">katecheso</hi> ("that I might instruct, that I might teach, that I might catechize") comes from <hi type="italic">katecheo</hi> ("to sound down into, to instruct orally, to teach by word of mouth"). The word implies the transfer of content from one mind to another through intelligible speech. This is what the assembly needs: instruction that reaches the hearers, that enters their understanding, that equips them. Five words of genuine instruction outweigh a limitless flood of unintelligible sound.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.14.20-1Cor.14.25">
          <title>Verses 20-25: The Isaiah Citation and the Function of Tongues as a Sign</title>
          <p>"Brothers, do not be children in your thinking. Be infants in evil, but in your thinking be mature" (14:20).</p>
          <p>The address <hi type="italic">adelphoi</hi> ("brothers") signals a new phase of the argument. Paul shifts from practical illustration to scriptural argument. But first, a rebuke: <hi type="italic">me paidia ginesthe tais phresin</hi> — "do not be children in your thinking." The word <hi type="italic">paidia</hi> ("children, little ones") describes immaturity. The word <hi type="italic">phresin</hi> (dative plural of <hi type="italic">phren</hi>, "mind, understanding, way of thinking") describes the mental faculty. The Corinthians' fascination with tongues — with the spectacular, the ecstatic, the unintelligible — is childish thinking. Paul draws a contrast: <hi type="italic">alla te kakia nepiazete, tais de phresin teleioi ginesthe</hi> — "but in evil be infants, and in your thinking be mature." The word <hi type="italic">nepiazete</hi> ("be infants") from <hi type="italic">nepiazo</hi> means to be like a <hi type="italic">nepios</hi> ("infant, baby") — utterly inexperienced. In evil (<hi type="italic">kakia</hi>), be as innocent as babies. But in thinking (<hi type="italic">phresin</hi>), be <hi type="italic">teleioi</hi> ("mature, complete, full-grown"). The word <hi type="italic">teleios</hi> appeared in 13:10 ("when the perfect/complete comes") and 2:6 ("we do impart wisdom among the mature"). Maturity of mind is what Paul demands. The Corinthians' preference for the showy gift of tongues over the edifying gift of prophecy reflects immature thinking — thinking that values spectacle over substance.</p>
          <p>"In the Law it is written, 'By people of strange tongues and by the lips of foreigners will I speak to this people, and even then they will not listen to me, says the Lord'" (14:21).</p>
          <p>The formula <hi type="italic">en to nomo gegraptai hoti</hi> ("in the Law it is written that") introduces a scriptural citation. Paul uses <hi type="italic">nomos</hi> ("Law") broadly to refer to the scriptures, not narrowly to the Pentateuch alone, since the quotation comes from Isaiah. The citation is from Isaiah 28:11-12, though Paul's version differs from both the standard LXX text and the Hebrew (MT), adapting the passage to his rhetorical purpose.</p>
          <p>The original context of Isaiah 28 is essential for understanding Paul's use. Isaiah 28 opens with a pronouncement of woe against the "drunkards of Ephraim" — the leaders of the northern kingdom who reel with wine and stagger in their judgments (28:1-8). These leaders mock Isaiah's message, complaining that his instruction is too simple, fit only for those just weaned from milk: "precept upon precept, precept upon precept, line upon line, line upon line, here a little, there a little" (28:10). They reject the clear, intelligible word of God delivered through the prophet as too elementary.</p>
          <p>God's response is devastating: because they have refused the clear word spoken in their own language, God will speak to them through people of foreign lips and strange tongues — that is, through the Assyrian conquerors. The incomprehensible speech of foreign invaders will be God's word to Israel. "By people of strange lips and with a foreign tongue the Lord will speak to this people, to whom he has said, 'This is rest; give rest to the weary; and this is repose'; yet they would not hear" (Isaiah 28:11-12). The clear prophetic word — "this is rest" — was rejected. Now God will speak through incomprehensible foreign speech. And "even then they will not listen" — the foreign tongues will not convert them but will confirm their hardness.</p>
          <p>The foreign tongues in Isaiah are therefore not a blessing but a judgment sign. They are what happens when a people refuses the intelligible prophetic word. The clear message is rejected; the incomprehensible message follows as both consequence and confirmation of that rejection. The Assyrian invasion was not God's saving word to Israel; it was God's judgment on Israel for refusing the saving word.</p>
          <p>Paul quotes this passage because it establishes a scriptural precedent for the function of unintelligible speech: in the prophetic tradition, foreign tongues are a sign of judgment directed at those who will not believe. They do not convert; they confirm unbelief.</p>
          <p>"Thus tongues are a sign not for believers but for unbelievers, while prophecy is a sign not for unbelievers but for believers" (14:22).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">hoste</hi> ("thus, therefore, so then") draws the conclusion from the Isaiah citation. Paul applies Isaiah's precedent to the Corinthian situation. Tongues are <hi type="italic">eis semeion</hi> — "for a sign." The word <hi type="italic">semeion</hi> ("sign, indicator, mark, signal") describes something that points beyond itself to a larger reality. In the LXX, <hi type="italic">semeia</hi> are frequently signs of God's action — signs of judgment (the plagues on Egypt, Exodus 7:3; Deuteronomy 6:22), signs of divine presence (Exodus 4:8-9), signs of prophetic fulfillment (1 Samuel 10:7-9). But signs can be positive or negative; they can signal salvation or judgment.</p>
          <p>Paul says tongues are a sign <hi type="italic">ou tois pisteuousin alla tois apistois</hi> — "not for those who believe but for those who do not believe." Given the Isaiah background, the "sign" that tongues constitute for unbelievers is a judgment sign, not a missionary tool. In Isaiah, the foreign tongues confirmed Israel's hardness rather than converting them. Similarly, tongues in the assembly serve as a sign that marks unbelief — they signal judgment, not invitation.</p>
          <p>Prophecy, conversely, is <hi type="italic">ou tois apistois alla tois pisteuousin</hi> — "not for unbelievers but for believers." Prophecy serves the believing community. It is the intelligible word of God spoken to God's people for their building up (v. 3). This is its primary audience and function.</p>
          <p>"If, therefore, the whole church comes together and all speak in tongues, and outsiders or unbelievers enter, will they not say that you are out of your minds?" (14:23).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">oun</hi> ("therefore") draws the practical application. Paul describes a scenario: the whole <hi type="italic">ekklesia</hi> assembles (<hi type="italic">synelthe</hi>, from <hi type="italic">synerchomai</hi>, the verb of 11:17-34), and everyone speaks in tongues simultaneously. If <hi type="italic">idiotai</hi> ("uninstructed persons, outsiders") or <hi type="italic">apistoi</hi> ("unbelievers") enter, they will say <hi type="italic">mainesthe</hi> — "you are raving, you are mad, you are out of your minds."</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">mainomai</hi> ("to be mad, to rave, to be in a frenzy") is the word used for ecstatic frenzy in Greco-Roman religion. At the Oracle of Delphi, the Pythia entered an ecstatic state that was described with this vocabulary. The devotees of Dionysus were <hi type="italic">mainades</hi> ("maenads, mad women") — the raving, frenzied worshipers of the god. The Corybantic rites involved ecstatic dancing and unintelligible cries. First-century Corinth, a center of Greco-Roman religion and culture, would have been thoroughly familiar with these ecstatic traditions. When an outsider walks into a gathering where everyone is speaking in unintelligible ecstatic speech, they will classify what they see according to the categories they already know: this is another version of the Bacchic frenzy, the Corybantic rites, the ecstatic cults. The community becomes indistinguishable from pagan worship.</p>
          <p>This connects to Paul's concern in 12:2: "you know that when you were pagans you were led astray to mute idols, however you were led." The Corinthians' former life involved the kind of ecstatic, uncontrolled worship that characterized pagan religion. If the assembly of Christ looks identical to a pagan ecstatic gathering, the distinctiveness of the community has been erased. The outsider sees no difference between the assembly of the living God and a temple of Dionysus.</p>
          <p>This also illuminates why tongues are a "sign for unbelievers" in the negative, judgment sense described in verse 22. The tongues do not draw the unbeliever in; they repel the unbeliever by confirming their assumption that this is just another ecstatic cult. The sign "works" negatively: it marks the hearer as an outsider and confirms their distance from God's message, just as the Assyrian tongues in Isaiah confirmed Israel's refusal to hear.</p>
          <p>"But if all prophesy, and an unbeliever or outsider enters, he is convicted by all, he is called to account by all, the secrets of his heart are disclosed, and so, falling on his face, he will worship God and declare that God is really among you" (14:24-25).</p>
          <p>The contrasting scenario is dramatic. Instead of tongues, the assembly is prophesying — speaking intelligible, Spirit-empowered words. When the <hi type="italic">apistos</hi> ("unbeliever") or <hi type="italic">idiotes</hi> ("outsider") enters, three things happen.</p>
          <p>First, <hi type="italic">elegchetai hypo panton</hi> — "he is convicted by all." The verb <hi type="italic">elegcho</hi> ("to convict, to expose, to reprove, to bring to light") describes the exposing of hidden truth. In the LXX, the word appears in Proverbs 9:8: "reprove (<hi type="italic">elegxon</hi>) a wise man, and he will love you"; in Job 5:17: "blessed is the one whom God reproves (<hi type="italic">elegchei</hi>)." The conviction is not an accusation from outside but an exposure from within — the prophetic word lays bare what was hidden.</p>
          <p>Second, <hi type="italic">anakrinetai hypo panton</hi> — "he is examined/called to account by all." The verb <hi type="italic">anakrino</hi> ("to examine, to investigate, to judge, to scrutinize") appeared in 2:14-15: "the natural person does not accept the things of the Spirit of God... the spiritual person judges (<hi type="italic">anakrinei</hi>) all things." And in 4:3-4: Paul used the verb for being examined by a human court. The word has forensic overtones — it describes a thorough examination that penetrates beneath the surface.</p>
          <p>Third, <hi type="italic">ta krypta tes kardias autou phanera ginetai</hi> — "the hidden things of his heart become manifest/visible." The word <hi type="italic">krypta</hi> ("hidden things, secrets") and <hi type="italic">phanera</hi> ("manifest, visible, disclosed") create a contrast: what was concealed in the heart is brought into the open. The prophecy does not merely convey information; it penetrates the listener's inner life and exposes what is there.</p>
          <p>The result: <hi type="italic">pesion epi prosopon proskynesei to theo, apangellon hoti ontos ho theos en hymin estin</hi> — "falling on his face he will worship God, declaring that truly God is among you." The phrase <hi type="italic">pesion epi prosopon</hi> ("falling on the face") describes the posture of overwhelming reverence. In the LXX, it is the posture of those who encounter God's presence: Abraham fell on his face (Genesis 17:3), the people fell on their faces at the dedication of the temple (2 Chronicles 7:3), Ezekiel fell on his face before the glory of God (Ezekiel 1:28, 3:23, 43:3).</p>
          <p>The confession — <hi type="italic">ontos ho theos en hymin estin</hi> ("truly God is among you") — echoes the prophetic vision of the nations recognizing God's presence among his people. In Isaiah 45:14 (LXX), the defeated nations confess: "God is in you (<hi type="italic">en soi ho theos estin</hi>), and there is no other, no God besides." In Zechariah 8:23: "In those days ten men from the nations of every tongue shall take hold of the robe of a Jew, saying, 'Let us go with you, for we have heard that God is with you.'" The outsider's recognition that God is truly present in the assembly is the quintessential prophetic sign — the nations seeing God's presence among his people and being compelled to worship. This is what the assembly is supposed to produce: not the confirming of outsiders in their unbelief (which unintelligible tongues accomplish, v. 23), but the converting of outsiders to worship (which intelligible prophecy accomplishes, vv. 24-25).</p>
          <p>The contrast between the two scenarios could not be sharper. Tongues make the unbeliever say "you are mad." Prophecy makes the unbeliever fall on his face and worship God. The first repels; the second converts. The first confirms unbelief; the second breaks it open. The first isolates the community from the world; the second draws the world into the community's worship.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.14.26-1Cor.14.33">
          <title>Verses 26-33: Practical Rules for the Assembly</title>
          <p>"What then, brothers? When you come together, each one has a hymn, a lesson, a revelation, a tongue, or an interpretation. Let all things be done for building up" (14:26).</p>
          <p>The question <hi type="italic">ti oun estin, adelphoi</hi> ("what then is [the conclusion], brothers?") signals the transition from argument to practical instruction. Paul describes what happens when the community assembles (<hi type="italic">hotan synerchesthe</hi>, "when you come together" — the same verb as 11:17-34). The description reveals a picture of the early assembly's worship: each person (<hi type="italic">hekastos</hi>, "each one") brings a contribution. Paul lists five types: <hi type="italic">psalmon</hi> ("a psalm, a hymn, a song of praise"), <hi type="italic">didachen</hi> ("a lesson, a teaching"), <hi type="italic">apokalypsin</hi> ("a revelation"), <hi type="italic">glossan</hi> ("a tongue"), <hi type="italic">hermeneian</hi> ("an interpretation"). The worship is participatory — multiple members contribute — not a performance by a single speaker before a passive audience. Each person comes prepared with something to offer.</p>
          <p>The governing principle for this participatory worship: <hi type="italic">panta pros oikodomen ginestho</hi> — "let all things be done for building up." The word <hi type="italic">oikodomen</hi> appears for the seventh and final time. It is the chapter's last word on the subject: everything, without exception, must serve the edification of the community. The standard is comprehensive (<hi type="italic">panta</hi>, "all things") and absolute (<hi type="italic">ginestho</hi>, present imperative, "let it be, let it happen").</p>
          <p>"If any speak in a tongue, let there be only two or at most three, and each in turn, and let someone interpret. But if there is no one to interpret, let each of them keep silent in church and speak to himself and to God" (14:27-28).</p>
          <p>Paul now issues specific regulations. For tongues: <hi type="italic">kata duo e to pleiston treis</hi> — "by two or at the most three." The number is limited. Each in turn (<hi type="italic">ana meros</hi>, "by parts, in sequence") — not simultaneously. And <hi type="italic">heis diermeneuto</hi> — "let one interpret." One person provides the interpretation for each tongue-utterance.</p>
          <p>The critical condition: <hi type="italic">ean de me e diermeneutes</hi> — "but if there is no interpreter." If no one present can interpret, the tongue-speaker <hi type="italic">sigato en ekklesia</hi> — "let him be silent in the assembly." The verb <hi type="italic">sigao</hi> ("to be silent, to cease speaking, to hold one's peace") is a command to refrain from speaking in the public assembly. But Paul does not prohibit the tongue-speaker from praying altogether: <hi type="italic">heauto de laleito kai to theo</hi> — "let him speak to himself and to God." The tongues-speaking can continue as private prayer directed to God. The distinction between the public assembly and private devotion is sharp. What is appropriate for private prayer (tongues without interpretation) is not appropriate for the gathered community.</p>
          <p>"Let two or three prophets speak, and let the others weigh what is said" (14:29).</p>
          <p>The regulation for prophecy mirrors the regulation for tongues: <hi type="italic">duo e treis</hi> ("two or three"). Prophets do not have unlimited time. And <hi type="italic">hoi alloi diakrinetosan</hi> — "let the others discern/evaluate/weigh." The verb <hi type="italic">diakrino</hi> ("to distinguish, to discern, to evaluate, to judge") appeared in 11:29 ("not discerning the body") and 11:31 ("if we judged ourselves"). Here it describes the community's evaluation of prophetic speech. Prophecy is not exempt from scrutiny. The community tests what the prophets say. This reflects the gift of <hi type="italic">diakriseis pneumaton</hi> ("distinguishing of spirits") listed in 12:10. The prophetic word must be evaluated because not all speech that claims to be prophetic is genuine, and the community has a responsibility to discern what is truly from the Spirit.</p>
          <p>"If a revelation is made to another sitting there, let the first be silent. For you can all prophesy one by one, so that all may learn and all be encouraged" (14:30-31).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">sigato</hi> ("let him be silent") appears again, now applied to a prophet. If one prophet is speaking and another receives a revelation (<hi type="italic">apokalyphthe</hi>, aorist passive subjunctive of <hi type="italic">apokalypto</hi>, "to reveal, to uncover"), the first prophet must yield. The speaking is sequential, not simultaneous: <hi type="italic">kath' hena pantes</hi> ("one by one, all of you"). The purpose is stated with two <hi type="italic">hina</hi> clauses: <hi type="italic">hina pantes manthaniosin</hi> ("that all may learn") and <hi type="italic">hina pantes parakalontai</hi> ("that all may be encouraged/exhorted"). The word <hi type="italic">manthanio</hi> ("to learn") indicates that the assembly is an environment of instruction. The word <hi type="italic">parakaleo</hi> ("to encourage, to exhort, to comfort") echoes the <hi type="italic">paraklesis</hi> ("encouragement") of verse 3. The orderly, sequential practice of prophecy ensures that every member of the community receives instruction and encouragement.</p>
          <p>"And the spirits of prophets are subject to prophets" (14:32).</p>
          <p>This verse directly addresses a potential objection. A prophet might claim: "I cannot stop speaking — the Spirit compels me." Paul denies this. The phrase <hi type="italic">pneumata propheton prophetais hypotassetai</hi> — "the spirits of prophets are subject to prophets" — means that the prophetic spirit does not overwhelm the prophet's self-control. The verb <hi type="italic">hypotasso</hi> ("to subject, to subordinate, to place under, to submit") indicates voluntary ordering. The prophet remains in control of the prophetic utterance. This statement distinguishes Christian prophecy from the ecstatic possession models of Greco-Roman religion. In the cult of Apollo at Delphi, the Pythia was understood to be taken over by the god — her consciousness was displaced, her will overridden. In the Dionysiac cults, the devotees lost control of themselves in ecstatic frenzy. Paul repudiates this model. The Spirit works with the prophet, not against the prophet's will. The prophet can stop, can yield, can wait, can be silent — because the spirit is subject to the prophet, not the prophet to the spirit.</p>
          <p>"For God is not a God of confusion but of peace. As in all the churches of the saints" (14:33).</p>
          <p>The theological ground for orderly worship: <hi type="italic">ou gar estin akatastasias ho theos alla eirenes</hi> — "for God is not [a God] of disorder but of peace." The word <hi type="italic">akatastasia</hi> ("disorder, confusion, instability, unrest") describes a state of turmoil. In the LXX, the word appears in contexts of social and political upheaval. The word <hi type="italic">eirene</hi> ("peace") is the Greek equivalent of the Hebrew <hi type="italic">shalom</hi> — wholeness, proper order, well-being, the state of things when everything is in its right place. The character of God determines the character of worship. If God is a God of peace, then the assembly that worships him must reflect peace, not chaos. Disordered worship contradicts the nature of the God being worshiped.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">hos en pasais tais ekklesiais ton hagion</hi> ("as in all the churches of the saints") may conclude verse 33 or introduce verse 34. If it concludes verse 33, it appeals to the universal practice of all the communities: the orderly worship Paul describes is not a local regulation for Corinth but a universal standard. This is the same type of appeal Paul made in 11:16: "we have no such practice, nor do the churches of God." The Corinthians are not free to invent their own standards; the practice of all the assemblies is the norm. If the phrase introduces verse 34, it serves as a different kind of appeal (discussed below).</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.14.34-1Cor.14.35">
          <title>Verses 34-35: The Instruction About Women's Speech</title>
          <p>"The women should keep silent in the churches. For they are not permitted to speak, but should be in submission, as the Law also says. If there is anything they desire to learn, let them ask their husbands at home. For it is shameful for a woman to speak in church" (14:34-35).</p>
          <p>These verses present one of the most significant interpretive difficulties in the letter, because they appear to contradict what Paul said in 11:5, where he assumed without objection that women pray and prophesy in the assembly. In 11:5, the issue was not whether women could speak but the manner in which they spoke (the head-covering question). Paul's discussion of prophecy in 14:1-33 has likewise made no gender distinction: "you can all prophesy one by one" (14:31) used <hi type="italic">pantes</hi> ("all"), which includes women. The sudden instruction for women to be silent creates a tension that the first-century reader, who had already heard 11:5 read aloud earlier in the same letter, would have noticed.</p>
          <p>Several textual and contextual features require attention.</p>
          <p>First, the verb <hi type="italic">sigao</hi> ("to be silent"). This verb has appeared twice already in this chapter: in verse 28, the tongue-speaker is told to <hi type="italic">sigato</hi> ("be silent") if there is no interpreter; in verse 30, the prophet is told to <hi type="italic">sigato</hi> ("be silent") when another receives a revelation. In both cases, <hi type="italic">sigao</hi> denotes contextual silence — silence in a specific situation, not absolute and permanent muteness. The tongue-speaker who is told to be silent in the assembly is still permitted to "speak to himself and to God" (v. 28). The prophet who is told to be silent is still permitted to prophesy later (v. 31). The word <hi type="italic">sigao</hi> in this chapter means "refrain from speaking in this particular context," not "never speak under any circumstances."</p>
          <p>Second, the verb <hi type="italic">laleo</hi> ("to speak"). Throughout chapter 14, <hi type="italic">laleo</hi> has been used for tongues-speaking (vv. 2, 4, 5, 6, 9, 13, 18, 19, 23, 27, 28), for prophecy (vv. 3, 29), and for general speech in the assembly (vv. 11, 19, 21). If <hi type="italic">laleo</hi> in verse 34 means all forms of speech, then women are forbidden from prophesying — directly contradicting 11:5. If <hi type="italic">laleo</hi> here refers to a specific type of speech, the contradiction is resolved.</p>
          <p>Third, the specific type of speech indicated by the context. Verse 35 provides a clue: <hi type="italic">ei de ti mathein thelousin, en oiko tous idious andras eperotatesosan</hi> — "if they want to learn something, let them ask their own husbands at home." The verb <hi type="italic">eperotao</hi> ("to ask, to inquire, to question") describes the act of asking questions. The verb <hi type="italic">mathein</hi> ("to learn") describes the desire to understand. The instruction envisions women asking questions in the assembly — perhaps during the evaluation of prophecy (<hi type="italic">diakrinetosan</hi>, v. 29), where the community was weighing and testing prophetic utterances. If the "speaking" Paul prohibits is the asking of disruptive questions during the evaluation process, the instruction fits coherently within the chapter's concern for order without contradicting the permission to pray and prophesy in 11:5.</p>
          <p>Fourth, the appeal to "the Law" (<hi type="italic">ho nomos</hi>). Paul says "as the Law also says" (<hi type="italic">kathos kai ho nomos legei</hi>), but no specific Torah passage commands women to be silent in assemblies. The most commonly suggested reference is Genesis 3:16 (the statement that the woman's desire will be for her husband, and he will rule over her), but this passage addresses the consequences of the fall, not speech regulations in worship. Paul typically provides specific scriptural citations when arguing from Torah (as he did in v. 21 with the Isaiah quotation, in 9:9 with Deuteronomy 25:4, in 10:7 with Exodus 32:6). The vague appeal to "the Law" without a specific text is unusual.</p>
          <p>Fifth, the word <hi type="italic">aischron</hi> ("shameful, disgraceful") in verse 35: "for it is shameful for a woman to speak in the assembly." This word appeared in 11:6, where Paul himself used it for the shame of a woman cutting her hair. Here it describes the shame of a woman speaking. The question is whether <hi type="italic">aischron</hi> refers to all speech (contradicting 11:5) or to the specific disruptive speech Paul has been addressing.</p>
          <p>Sixth, some manuscripts (including the Western textual tradition) place verses 34-35 after verse 40 rather than after verse 33. This textual displacement suggests that early copyists were uncertain about where these verses belonged in the argument, which in turn suggests that the verses' relationship to the surrounding text was perceived as difficult even in antiquity.</p>
          <p>Seventh, the possibility that Paul is quoting a Corinthian position and then rebutting it. Throughout the letter, Paul has quoted Corinthian slogans and then corrected them: "all things are lawful" (6:12, 10:23), "food for the stomach and the stomach for food" (6:13), "it is good for a man not to touch a woman" (7:1), "we all have knowledge" (8:1). In each case, Paul cites the Corinthian position and then qualifies or contradicts it. If verses 34-35 represent a Corinthian claim — a faction within the community that wanted to silence women — then verse 36 is Paul's sharp rebuke: <hi type="italic">e aph' hymon ho logos tou theou exelthen, e eis hymas monous katenthesen</hi> — "Or was it from you that the word of God came? Or are you the only ones it has reached?" The particle <hi type="italic">e</hi> ("or") at the beginning of verse 36 can function as a disjunctive that introduces an indignant response. The masculine plural <hi type="italic">hymas monous</hi> ("you only" — masculine, not feminine) would then be addressed to the men who proposed silencing the women: "Do you think the word of God originated with you? Do you think you are the only ones it has reached?" This reading maintains consistency with 11:5 and with the participatory worship described in 14:26-31 (where "all" prophesy).</p>
          <p>The textual and contextual evidence does not permit a single definitive resolution. What can be said with confidence is that the chapter's broader argument — in which the criterion is the building up of the community through intelligible, orderly speech — governs whatever specific instruction verses 34-35 intend. The chapter has already required silence from tongue-speakers without interpreters (v. 28) and from prophets when another receives a revelation (v. 30). In both cases, the silence was specific, contextual, and ordered toward the community's edification. Whatever verses 34-35 require, they operate within this same framework of ordered speech in the assembly.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.14.36-1Cor.14.40">
          <title>Verses 36-40: The Closing Authority Claim and Summary</title>
          <p>"Or was it from you that the word of God came? Or are you the only ones it has reached?" (14:36).</p>
          <p>The particle <hi type="italic">e</hi> ("or") introduces a sharp challenge. The two questions are rhetorically parallel and both expect the answer "no." The first: <hi type="italic">aph' hymon ho logos tou theou exelthen</hi> — "was it from you that the word of God went out?" The Corinthians are not the source of the word of God. They did not originate the gospel; they received it. The second: <hi type="italic">eis hymas monous katentesen</hi> — "did it reach you only?" The Corinthians are not the exclusive recipients of the word. Other communities received it too. Both questions deflate the Corinthian presumption that they can set their own standards for worship practice. They are part of a larger network of communities, and the word of God that constitutes them came from outside them and extends beyond them. They cannot legislate for themselves in isolation from the practice of the other assemblies.</p>
          <p>Whether these questions rebuke the Corinthians for silencing women (if vv. 34-35 are a Corinthian quotation) or rebuke the Corinthians for resisting Paul's own instructions (if vv. 34-35 are Paul's words), the rhetorical force is the same: the Corinthians do not have the authority to override what comes from beyond their own community. The word of God did not originate in Corinth, and it does not terminate there.</p>
          <p>"If anyone thinks that he is a prophet, or spiritual, he should acknowledge that the things I am writing to you are a command of the Lord" (14:37).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">dokei</hi> ("thinks, supposes") introduces the same note of potentially mistaken self-assessment that appeared in 3:18 ("if anyone among you thinks he is wise"), 8:2 ("if anyone thinks he knows"), and 10:12 ("let anyone who thinks he stands take heed"). A person's self-assessment as a prophet or as <hi type="italic">pneumatikos</hi> ("spiritual") — the word that opened the entire discussion in 12:1 (<hi type="italic">peri de ton pneumatikon</hi>, "concerning the spiritual things/persons") — must be tested by whether they recognize the authority of what Paul is writing.</p>
          <p>The test: <hi type="italic">epiginosketō ha graphō hymin hoti kyriou estin entole</hi> — "let him recognize that what I write to you is a command of the Lord." The verb <hi type="italic">epiginosko</hi> ("to recognize, to acknowledge, to know fully") demands active acknowledgment, not passive awareness. The phrase <hi type="italic">kyriou estin entole</hi> ("it is a command of the Lord") stakes the highest possible claim for Paul's instructions. These are not Paul's personal preferences or pastoral suggestions. They are <hi type="italic">entole kyriou</hi> — a commandment of the Lord. The Lord whose death the community proclaims at the supper (11:26), the Lord whose table they share (10:21), the Lord into whom they were baptized (12:13) — this Lord commands what Paul has written. The person who claims to be a prophet or spiritual but does not recognize the Lord's authority behind Paul's instructions has failed the very test of spiritual authenticity.</p>
          <p>"If anyone does not recognize this, he is not recognized" (14:38).</p>
          <p>The verse is terse and final: <hi type="italic">ei de tis agnoei, agnoeitai</hi> — "if someone is ignorant, he is ignored" (or "he is not recognized"). The verb <hi type="italic">agnoeo</hi> ("to be ignorant, to not know, to fail to recognize") in the active voice describes the person's failure to recognize Paul's authority. The same verb in the passive voice (<hi type="italic">agnoeitai</hi>) describes the consequence: the person is not recognized — not recognized by God, not recognized by the community, not acknowledged as having the standing they claim. The one who refuses to recognize the Lord's command is, in turn, not recognized. The word creates a devastating symmetry: if you do not recognize this, you are not recognized. The self-proclaimed prophet who rejects Paul's instructions has forfeited the very title they claim.</p>
          <p>"So, my brothers, earnestly desire to prophesy, and do not forbid speaking in tongues" (14:39).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">hoste</hi> ("so, therefore") introduces the chapter's summary instruction. Paul addresses the community as <hi type="italic">adelphoi mou</hi> ("my brothers") — a warm address after the severity of verses 37-38. The instruction has two parts, one positive and one negative.</p>
          <p>Positive: <hi type="italic">zeloute to propheteuein</hi> — "earnestly desire to prophesy." The verb <hi type="italic">zeloute</hi> ("be zealous for, eagerly desire") echoes 14:1 (<hi type="italic">zeloute de ta pneumatika</hi>, "earnestly desire the spiritual gifts") and 12:31 (<hi type="italic">zeloute de ta charismata ta meizona</hi>, "earnestly desire the greater gifts"). The chapter began and ends with the same verb. The Corinthians should pursue prophecy as the gift that best serves the community.</p>
          <p>Negative: <hi type="italic">kai to lalein me koluete glossais</hi> — "and do not forbid speaking in tongues." The verb <hi type="italic">koluo</hi> ("to hinder, to prevent, to forbid, to stop") is negated: do not forbid tongues. After an entire chapter that has subordinated tongues to prophecy, Paul ensures that the pendulum does not swing too far. Tongues are a genuine gift of the Spirit (12:10, 28, 30). Paul himself speaks in tongues more than all of them (14:18). The gift is not to be suppressed. It is to be regulated, ordered, interpreted, and subordinated to the community's edification — but not forbidden.</p>
          <p>"But all things should be done decently and in order" (14:40).</p>
          <p>The final verse states the comprehensive principle: <hi type="italic">panta de euschemonos kai kata taxin ginestho</hi> — "let all things be done becomingly and according to order." The word <hi type="italic">euschemonos</hi> ("decently, becomingly, with propriety, in a seemly manner") comes from <hi type="italic">euschemon</hi> ("well-formed, elegant, proper, respectable"). In 7:35, Paul used the related noun <hi type="italic">euschemon</hi> for behavior that was "seemly" or "proper." In 12:24, the word appeared for the "presentable" parts of the body. The word describes what is fitting, what accords with the community's dignity as the body of Christ.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">kata taxin</hi> ("according to order, in proper sequence, in an orderly way") uses the word <hi type="italic">taxis</hi> ("order, arrangement, sequence, rank"). The word appears in the LXX for the ordered arrangement of the tabernacle and its furnishings (Exodus 40:4, 23), for the arrangement of the shewbread (Leviticus 24:8), and for military formations (1 Chronicles 12:33, 38). Order is not the enemy of the Spirit; it is the form the Spirit's work takes when it operates through a community of many members. The instruments must produce distinct notes (v. 7); the speakers must take turns (vv. 27, 30-31); the prophets must be evaluated (v. 29); the spirits must be subject to the prophets (v. 32). All of this is <hi type="italic">taxis</hi> — order. The Spirit who distributes gifts "as he wills" (12:11) is the Spirit of a God who is not a God of disorder but of peace (14:33).</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.14">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 14 is the practical culmination of the three-chapter unit (12-14) on spiritual gifts in the assembly. Where chapter 12 established the theology (one Spirit, many gifts, one body, many members) and chapter 13 established the criterion (love is the way gifts must be exercised), chapter 14 addresses the specific problem: the Corinthian assembly's disordered, self-serving exercise of tongues at the expense of intelligible, community-building speech.</p>
          <p>The chapter makes one sustained argument from beginning to end: in the gathered assembly, speech that builds up the community is more valuable than speech that does not, and prophecy — because it is intelligible, because it instructs, encourages, and consoles, because it convicts outsiders and manifests God's presence — is more valuable than uninterpreted tongues. Every sub-argument in the chapter serves this thesis.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">oikodome</hi>/<hi type="italic">oikodomeo</hi> ("building up / to build up") appears seven times (vv. 3, 4 twice, 5, 12, 17, 26) and functions as the chapter's structural keyword. It measures everything. Tongues build up the individual (v. 4); prophecy builds up the assembly (v. 4). The Corinthians should seek to abound in building up the assembly (v. 12). The thanksgiving in a tongue does not build up the other person (v. 17). All things must be done for building up (v. 26). The word ties this chapter to the entire letter: from the community as God's building in 3:9-14, through "love builds up" in 8:1, through "not all things build up" in 10:23, to the comprehensive criterion of 14:26. Building up the community has been Paul's consistent measure for evaluating all behavior, and chapter 14 makes it the explicit, named, repeated standard for the assembly's worship.</p>
          <p>The comparison between tongues and prophecy operates through a sequence of arguments, each approaching the same point from a different angle:</p>
          <p>First, the direct comparison (vv. 1-5): tongues speak to God, prophecy speaks to people; tongues build up the self, prophecy builds up the community; the prophesier is "greater" than the tongue-speaker unless someone interprets.</p>
          <p>Second, the analogy of instruments and languages (vv. 6-12): unintelligible sound — whether from a flute, a trumpet, or a foreign language — fails to communicate. It produces confusion rather than understanding. Speech must be intelligible to serve its purpose.</p>
          <p>Third, the spirit-mind distinction (vv. 13-19): tongues engage the spirit but leave the mind unfruitful. Both spirit and mind must be active in worship. Five intelligible words outweigh ten thousand unintelligible ones.</p>
          <p>Fourth, the Isaiah citation and the sign argument (vv. 20-25): tongues, in the scriptural precedent of Isaiah 28, function as a judgment sign for unbelievers — they confirm unbelief rather than converting it. Prophecy, by contrast, convicts the unbeliever, exposes the secrets of the heart, and produces worship. The assembly that prophesies makes God's presence visible to outsiders. The assembly that speaks in tongues makes outsiders think the community is mad.</p>
          <p>Each argument converges on the same conclusion: in the assembly, prophecy serves the community better than uninterpreted tongues. Paul then translates this conclusion into practical regulations (vv. 26-33): tongues limited to two or three, with interpretation; prophecy limited to two or three, with evaluation; speaking in sequence, not simultaneously; prophets subject to prophets; everything ordered because God is a God of peace, not disorder.</p>
          <p>Several threads from earlier in the letter reach important expressions in this chapter.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">schisma</hi> ("division") from 1:10, 11:18, and 12:25 is not named in chapter 14, but the disease it describes is present throughout. The disordered assembly — where individuals exercise gifts without regard for others, where the uninstructed person cannot say "Amen," where the outsider sees chaos — is another manifestation of the division that has plagued the community from the beginning. The factions of chapters 1-4 (allegiance to different teachers), the divisions at the meal in 11:17-22 (the wealthy eating while the poor go hungry), and the disordered exercise of gifts in chapter 14 (tongues without interpretation, simultaneous speaking, confusion) are all symptoms of the same disease: the failure to treat the community as one body.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">phusioo</hi> ("to puff up, to inflate"), which appeared in 4:6, 4:18, 4:19, 5:2, 8:1, and 13:4, is not named in chapter 14, but the behavior it describes is the background against which the chapter is written. The Corinthians' fascination with tongues — the most spectacular, the most showy, the most individual of the gifts — reflects the same inflation that produced the wisdom-boasting of chapters 1-4 and the knowledge-boasting of chapter 8. The preference for the impressive over the edifying is a form of puffing up. Paul's instruction to be "mature in thinking" (v. 20) and to seek building up rather than spectacle addresses this inflation directly.</p>
          <p>The connection between chapter 14 and the body metaphor of chapter 12 is fundamental. The body has many members, each essential. The eye cannot say to the hand "I have no need of you" (12:21). When one member suffers, all suffer (12:26). This means that the assembly's worship must serve every member, not only the gifted. The tongue-speaker who monopolizes the assembly with unintelligible speech is functionally saying to the uninstructed person: "I have no need of your comprehension. I have no need of your Amen. I do not need you to participate." This is the body-denial that the metaphor was designed to prevent. The regulations of chapter 14 are the practical application of the body theology of chapter 12: treat every member as essential, serve the whole body, do not use your gift in a way that excludes others from participation.</p>
          <p>The chapter's treatment of prophecy also connects to the broader scriptural tradition of prophetic speech in Israel. The vision of Numbers 11:29 — Moses' wish, "Would that all the Lord's people were prophets, that the Lord would put his Spirit on them!" — finds an echo in Paul's instruction: "you can all prophesy one by one, so that all may learn and all be encouraged" (14:31). Moses wished for a community of prophets; Paul instructs a community in which prophetic speech is available to all, exercised in order, for the benefit of all. The prophetic community is not one in which a single figure speaks and all others listen; it is one in which many speak, one by one, and the community learns and is encouraged through the collective exercise of the gift.</p>
          <p>The Isaiah 28 quotation in verse 21 is the chapter's most difficult passage, and its interpretation governs the understanding of verse 22. In Isaiah's original context, God's people had refused the clear prophetic word — "this is rest; give rest to the weary" (Isaiah 28:12). Because they refused, God would speak to them through foreign tongues — the speech of Assyrian conquerors — and "even then they will not listen." The foreign tongues were not a tool of conversion but a mark of judgment. Paul applies this precedent to tongues in the assembly: tongues function as a sign (<hi type="italic">semeion</hi>) for unbelievers, not in the sense of a positive missionary sign that draws them to faith, but in the sense of Isaiah's judgment sign that confirms their incomprehension. Verses 23-25 then illustrate both outcomes: tongues produce the judgment-sign effect (outsiders conclude the assembly is mad), while prophecy produces the opposite (outsiders are convicted and worship God). The contrast reinforces the chapter's thesis: prophecy serves the community and reaches outsiders; uninterpreted tongues serve neither.</p>
          <p>The statement that "the spirits of prophets are subject to prophets" (v. 32) is theologically significant for distinguishing the nature of Christian worship from the ecstatic traditions of Greco-Roman religion. The first-century Corinthian hearer would have been familiar with the Pythia at Delphi, the maenads of Dionysus, the Corybantic rites — all traditions in which the worshiper was understood to be possessed, overtaken, stripped of self-control by the divine power. Paul flatly rejects this model. The Spirit does not override the prophet's will. The prophet remains in control. This means that no one in the assembly can claim "the Spirit compelled me" as justification for disordered speech. The orderly exercise of gifts is not a suppression of the Spirit but an expression of the Spirit, because the Spirit works through self-controlled persons who voluntarily submit their gifts to the order of the community.</p>
          <p>The final verse — "all things should be done decently and in order" (v. 40) — captures the chapter's governing vision. The word <hi type="italic">euschemonos</hi> ("decently, becomingly") and the phrase <hi type="italic">kata taxin</hi> ("according to order") describe a community whose worship reflects the character of its God. The God who is not a God of confusion but of peace (v. 33) is worshiped by a community whose assembly reflects peace, not confusion. The gifts are real. The Spirit is active. Prophecy convicts and converts. Tongues speak mysteries to God. But the exercise of these gifts takes place within a structured, ordered, mutual practice in which every member can participate, every person can say "Amen," the outsider can encounter God's presence, and the body of Christ is built up. The order does not quench the Spirit; it is the form the Spirit's work takes in a community of many members who are one body.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.15">
        <title>1 Corinthians 15 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.15">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 14):</hi> Chapter 14 was the practical conclusion of the three-chapter unit (12-14) on spiritual gifts in the assembled community. Paul's governing criterion was <hi type="italic">oikodome</hi> ("building up"): every activity in the assembly must serve the edification of the community, not the enhancement of the individual. Prophecy was preferred over tongues because prophecy is intelligible speech that builds up others, while uninterpreted tongues build up only the speaker (14:4). Paul regulated the exercise of both gifts: tongues limited to two or three speakers, with interpretation required (14:27-28); prophecy limited to two or three, with communal evaluation (14:29). The reason for order was theological: "God is not a God of confusion but of peace" (14:33). The prophets' spirits are subject to the prophets (14:32) — the Spirit does not override self-control. Paul concluded with the comprehensive principle: "all things should be done decently and in order" (14:40). The final claim was that his instructions carried the authority of a command of the Lord (14:37), and anyone who fails to recognize this is not recognized (14:38).</p>
          <p>Throughout chapters 12-14, the community's identity as the body of Christ was foundational. The body has many members; each member has a function; no member can say to another "I have no need of you" (12:21). This body gathers for worship, and its worship must reflect the character of the God it serves. But all of this — the body, its gifts, its worship, its gathered life — depends on something that has not yet been argued in the letter: the foundational events that brought this community into existence. The community that gathers in chapter 14 exists because something happened. Chapter 15 names what happened.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (1 Corinthians 16):</hi> Paul turns to practical and logistical matters. He opens with the formula <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> ("now concerning"), signaling another topic from the Corinthians' letter: the collection for the saints in Jerusalem (16:1-4). He instructs each member to set aside money on the first day of every week, proportional to their income, so that no collecting needs to happen when Paul arrives. He then describes his travel plans (16:5-9): he intends to pass through Macedonia and may spend the winter with them. He commends Timothy to the community (16:10-11), notes that Apollos was urged to visit them (16:12), and delivers final exhortations: "Be watchful, stand firm in the faith, act like men, be strong. Let all that you do be done in love" (16:13-14). The verb <hi type="italic">stekete</hi> ("stand firm") in 16:13 echoes the <hi type="italic">hestekate</hi> ("you stand") of 15:1 — the community's standing in the gospel requires firmness and vigilance. The final exhortation to do all things "in love" (<hi type="italic">en agape</hi>) reaches back to the governing criterion of chapter 13. The letter ends with greetings, a warning against those who have no love for the Lord (16:22: "if anyone has no love for the Lord, let him be accursed"), and the prayer <hi type="italic">Marana tha</hi> ("Our Lord, come!" — 16:22), which expresses the eschatological expectation that chapter 15 has just developed at length.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 15 connects these:</hi> The transition from the ordered worship of chapter 14 to the resurrection argument of chapter 15 is not a shift of subject but a shift of depth. The community whose worship Paul has been ordering in chapters 11-14 exists because of the gospel events chapter 15 proclaims. Paul has been regulating what the community does when it gathers; now he addresses what constitutes the community in the first place. The opening words of chapter 15 — <hi type="italic">gnorizo de hymin, adelphoi, to euangelion ho euengelisamen hymin</hi> ("I make known to you, brothers, the gospel which I proclaimed to you") — reach back behind everything in the letter to the community's origin. Before there were factions (chapters 1-4), before there was sexual immorality (chapters 5-6), before there were lawsuits (6:1-8), before there were questions about marriage (chapter 7), idol-food (chapters 8-10), head coverings (11:2-16), the Lord's supper (11:17-34), and spiritual gifts (chapters 12-14) — before all of this, there was the gospel. Paul preached it; they received it; they stand in it; they are being saved through it. Everything in the letter presupposes the gospel, but the gospel itself has not yet been stated.</p>
          <p>The connection to chapter 14 is also specific. Paul has just argued that everything in the assembly must be intelligible and orderly because the assembled community is the body of Christ. But what makes this community the body of Christ? The answer is the gospel: Christ died, was buried, was raised. The body of Christ exists because Christ's body was raised. If Christ has not been raised, the community has no reason to gather, no Lord to confess, no body to constitute. Chapter 15 provides the foundation on which every preceding chapter rests.</p>
          <p>The transition to chapter 16 is equally natural. Chapter 15 concludes with the exhortation: "Therefore, my beloved brothers, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, knowing that in the Lord your labor is not in vain" (15:58). The phrase <hi type="italic">ouk estin kenos</hi> ("is not empty/vain") answers the <hi type="italic">eike</hi> ("in vain") of 15:2 and the <hi type="italic">kenos/kenon</hi> ("empty") of 15:10, 14. The community's labor has meaning because the resurrection is real. Chapter 16 then describes that labor in concrete terms: the collection for Jerusalem (the practical work of caring for fellow believers), the travel plans (the ongoing work of apostolic ministry), the exhortation to stand firm (the ongoing work of faithfulness). The resurrection grounds the labor; the labor follows from the resurrection.</p>
          <p>The chapter also addresses a specific problem within the community. In 15:12, Paul reveals the occasion for the argument: <hi type="italic">tines en hymin legousin hoti anastasis nekron ouk estin</hi> — "some among you say that there is no resurrection of the dead." This denial is the disease chapter 15 diagnoses. Who these "some" are and what exactly they deny is clarified by the argument itself: they appear to accept that Christ was raised (since they are members of the community) but deny that there will be a future bodily resurrection of the dead generally. Paul's argument is that these two beliefs are inseparable: if there is no resurrection of the dead, then Christ has not been raised; and if Christ has not been raised, the community's faith is empty.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.15.1-1Cor.15.2">
          <title>Verses 1-2: The Gospel in Which They Stand — Conditionally</title>
          <p>"Now I would remind you, brothers, of the gospel I preached to you, which you received, in which you stand, and by which you are being saved, if you hold fast to the word I preached to you — unless you believed in vain" (15:1-2).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">gnorizo</hi> ("I make known, I declare, I remind") opens a new section without the <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> formula, indicating that Paul is not responding to a question from the Corinthians' letter but raising an issue he has identified on his own. The word can mean either "I make known" (introducing new information) or "I remind" (calling attention to what is already known). Since the Corinthians already received this gospel (<hi type="italic">parelabete</hi>, "you received"), the sense is closer to "I remind" — but the need for reminding implies that the gospel's implications have not been fully grasped.</p>
          <p>Paul describes the gospel with four verbs in sequence, each marking a stage in the community's relationship to it:</p>
          <p>First, <hi type="italic">ho euengelisamen hymin</hi> — "which I proclaimed to you." The verb <hi type="italic">euangelizo</hi> ("to announce good news, to proclaim the gospel") is the aorist: Paul proclaimed this gospel at a definite point in the past, when the community was founded. The gospel is not Paul's invention; it is something he received (v. 3) and transmitted. But the act of proclamation was Paul's apostolic work.</p>
          <p>Second, <hi type="italic">ho kai parelabete</hi> — "which also you received." The verb <hi type="italic">paralambano</hi> ("to receive, to take over, to accept") is the technical term for receiving transmitted tradition. In Jewish usage, the paired verbs <hi type="italic">paralambano/paradidomi</hi> ("to receive/to hand over") describe the chain of tradition from teacher to student. The Corinthians received the gospel as tradition transmitted to them from a source prior to Paul.</p>
          <p>Third, <hi type="italic">en ho kai hestekate</hi> — "in which also you stand." The verb <hi type="italic">histemi</hi> in the perfect (<hi type="italic">hestekate</hi>) describes a settled, established position — "you have taken your stand and continue standing." The community's present posture is one of standing in the gospel. The perfect tense indicates an ongoing state resulting from a past action: they received the gospel, they took their stand in it, and they continue to stand. This is the same verb that appeared in 10:12: "let anyone who thinks he stands take heed lest he fall." The standing is real but not automatic.</p>
          <p>Fourth, <hi type="italic">di' hou kai sozesthe</hi> — "through which also you are being saved." The verb <hi type="italic">sozo</hi> ("to save, to rescue, to deliver, to preserve") is in the present tense: <hi type="italic">sozesthe</hi> — "you are being saved." The present tense is significant. Paul does not say "you were saved" (aorist, completed action) or "you will be saved" (future, yet to happen). He says "you are being saved" — salvation is a process currently underway. The same present tense appeared in 1:18, the letter's first statement about salvation: <hi type="italic">ho logos gar ho tou staurou tois men apollymenois moria estin, tois de sozomenois hemin dynamis theou</hi> — "the word of the cross is foolishness to those who are perishing, but to us who are being saved it is the power of God." In both 1:18 and 15:2, the present participle/indicative describes salvation as ongoing. The community is not yet fully saved; it is in the process of being saved. The process has a beginning (they received the gospel), a present (they stand in it and are being saved through it), and a future (the resurrection, which chapter 15 will develop).</p>
          <p>The conditional clause <hi type="italic">tini logo euengelisamen hymin ei katechete</hi> — "by what word I proclaimed to you, if you hold fast" — introduces a condition into the process of salvation. The verb <hi type="italic">katecho</hi> ("to hold fast, to hold down, to retain, to keep possession of") describes a firm grip on something. The present tense indicates ongoing action: keep holding fast, continue retaining. The conditional particle <hi type="italic">ei</hi> ("if") makes the holding fast a genuine condition: the salvation described by <hi type="italic">sozesthe</hi> depends on whether the community continues to hold fast to the word that was proclaimed. This is not a formality. Paul is about to argue that some in the community are in danger of letting go — of failing to hold fast — by denying the resurrection.</p>
          <p>The final phrase — <hi type="italic">ektos ei me eike episteusate</hi> — "unless you believed in vain" — introduces a second, more disturbing possibility. The word <hi type="italic">eike</hi> means "in vain, without purpose, without effect, to no avail, for nothing." The verb <hi type="italic">episteusate</hi> ("you believed") is the aorist: at a past moment, they believed. Paul raises the possibility that this believing was <hi type="italic">eike</hi> — in vain, without substance, without lasting effect. Believing "in vain" would mean that their initial act of faith was empty, that it grasped nothing real, that it produced no lasting change. If Christ has not been raised (as some among them are apparently saying), then their faith grasped a falsehood, and their believing was indeed in vain.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">eike</hi> will echo throughout the chapter. In verse 10, Paul says God's grace toward him was <hi type="italic">ou kene</hi> ("not empty, not vain") — the cognate adjective <hi type="italic">kenos</hi>. In verse 14, if Christ has not been raised, Paul's preaching is <hi type="italic">kenos</hi> ("empty") and their faith is <hi type="italic">kene</hi> ("empty"). In verse 17, if Christ has not been raised, their faith is <hi type="italic">mataia</hi> ("futile, pointless"). In verse 58, the chapter's final word of exhortation promises that their labor in the Lord <hi type="italic">ouk estin kenos</hi> ("is not vain/empty"). The vocabulary of vanity and emptiness forms an <hi type="italic">inclusio</hi>: the chapter opens with the possibility that their faith is "in vain" (<hi type="italic">eike</hi>, v. 2) and closes with the assurance that their labor is "not in vain" (<hi type="italic">ouk kenos</hi>, v. 58). Between these poles, the resurrection determines which outcome prevails. If the resurrection is real, nothing is in vain. If the resurrection is not real, everything is in vain.</p>
          <p>The two verses together present salvation as a process with conditions. The gospel was proclaimed, received, and stands as the ground of the community's life. Salvation is ongoing ("you are being saved"). But it is conditioned on holding fast ("if you hold fast"). And the content of what is held determines whether the faith is real or empty ("unless you believed in vain"). Paul does not present the community's standing as guaranteed by a past event alone. The past event (believing) initiated a process (being saved) that continues in the present (standing in the gospel) only if the community maintains its grip on the content of the gospel (holding fast). The possibility of believing in vain is real — Paul would not raise it otherwise — and the rest of the chapter will demonstrate what makes faith vain or substantial: the resurrection of Christ.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.15.3-1Cor.15.5">
          <title>Verses 3-5: The Received Tradition — The <hi type="italic">Kerygma</hi></title>
          <p>"For I delivered to you as of first importance what I also received: that Christ died for our sins in accordance with the Scriptures, that he was buried, that he was raised on the third day in accordance with the Scriptures, and that he appeared to Cephas, then to the twelve" (15:3-5).</p>
          <p>The conjunction <hi type="italic">gar</hi> ("for") connects this to the preceding: the gospel Paul proclaimed and the Corinthians received is now stated in its content. The phrase <hi type="italic">paredoka gar hymin en protois ho kai parelabon</hi> ("for I delivered to you as of first importance what I also received") uses the technical tradition-transmission vocabulary: <hi type="italic">paredoka</hi> ("I handed over, I delivered") and <hi type="italic">parelabon</hi> ("I received"). Paul is not the author of this message; he is its transmitter. He received it from those who preceded him in the faith and handed it on to the Corinthians. The phrase <hi type="italic">en protois</hi> means either "as of first importance" (this is the most important thing in the tradition) or "among the first things" (this was the earliest content transmitted to them). Both senses may be active: the <hi type="italic">kerygma</hi> is both the earliest and the most important element of the tradition.</p>
          <p>The <hi type="italic">kerygma</hi> itself consists of four statements, each introduced by <hi type="italic">hoti</hi> ("that"), forming a creedal pattern:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">First: <hi type="italic">hoti Christos apethanen hyper ton hamartion hemon kata tas graphas</hi> — "that Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures."</hi></p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">apethanen</hi> ("died") is the aorist of <hi type="italic">apothnesko</hi> — a completed, historical event. The phrase <hi type="italic">hyper ton hamartion hemon</hi> ("for our sins, on behalf of our sins") uses the preposition <hi type="italic">hyper</hi> ("for, on behalf of, for the sake of"), which indicates that Christ's death had a purpose related to the sins of those for whom he died. The pronoun <hi type="italic">hemon</hi> ("our") is first person plural — Paul includes himself and the community. The phrase <hi type="italic">kata tas graphas</hi> ("according to the Scriptures") indicates that Christ's death was not an accident but the fulfillment of scriptural patterns. Paul does not specify which scriptures he means, but the LXX provides multiple texts that a first-century Jewish reader would recognize: Isaiah 53:5-6 (LXX), where the servant is "wounded for our transgressions" (<hi type="italic">dia tas hamartias hemon</hi>) and "the Lord laid on him the iniquity of us all" (<hi type="italic">kyrios paredoken auton tais hamartiais hemon</hi>); Isaiah 53:8, where the servant is "cut off out of the land of the living" (<hi type="italic">airetai apo tes ges he zoe autou</hi>); Isaiah 53:12, where he "poured out his soul to death" (<hi type="italic">paredothe eis thanaton he psyche autou</hi>) and "bore the sin of many" (<hi type="italic">autos hamartias pollon anenken</hi>). The phrase <hi type="italic">kata tas graphas</hi> situates Christ's death within Israel's scriptural story, not as a novel event but as the fulfillment of what the prophets described.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Second: <hi type="italic">kai hoti etaphe</hi> — "and that he was buried."</hi></p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">etaphe</hi> ("he was buried") is the aorist passive of <hi type="italic">thapto</hi>. The burial confirms the reality of the death. Christ did not merely seem to die or faint on the cross; he was dead and was buried. In Jewish practice, burial follows death and marks its completion. The burial also serves as the bridge between the death and the resurrection: the one who was buried is the one who was raised. The same body that was laid in the tomb is the body that came out of the tomb. The burial anchors both events in physical, historical reality.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Third: <hi type="italic">kai hoti egegertai te hemera te trite kata tas graphas</hi> — "and that he has been raised on the third day according to the Scriptures."</hi></p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">egegertai</hi> is the perfect passive of <hi type="italic">egeiro</hi> ("to raise, to wake, to rouse"). The perfect tense is theologically significant: it describes a completed action with ongoing results. Christ was raised at a point in the past, and the effects of that raising continue into the present. He is not merely "he was raised" (aorist, a past event) but "he has been raised and remains in the raised state" (perfect, a past event with present consequences). The passive voice indicates that God is the agent: Christ did not raise himself; God raised him. This is consistent with Paul's language throughout the chapter and the letter: God acts, and Christ is the recipient and beneficiary of God's action.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">te hemera te trite</hi> ("on the third day") specifies the timing. The phrase <hi type="italic">kata tas graphas</hi> ("according to the Scriptures") again grounds the event in Israel's scriptural heritage. The specific texts behind "the third day" are debated, but first-century hearers would have known several possibilities. Hosea 6:2 (LXX): <hi type="italic">hygiasai hemas meta duo hemeras, en te hemera te trite anastesometha kai zesometha enopion autou</hi> — "he will heal us after two days; on the third day we shall rise and live before him." The verb <hi type="italic">anastesometha</hi> ("we shall rise") and the language of restoration after suffering on the third day maps onto the resurrection tradition. Jonah 2:1 (LXX 2:1): <hi type="italic">kai en Ionas en te koilia tou ketous treis hemeras kai treis nyktas</hi> — "and Jonah was in the belly of the sea creature three days and three nights." The pattern of descent into the realm of death and emergence after three days provides a scriptural type. Genesis 22:4, where Abraham saw the place of sacrifice "on the third day" (<hi type="italic">te hemera te trite</hi>), may also contribute: Isaac was, in narrative terms, given back from death on the third day (cf. Genesis 22:12, "you have not withheld your son").</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Fourth: <hi type="italic">kai hoti ophthe Kepha, eita tois dodeka</hi> — "and that he appeared to Cephas, then to the twelve."</hi></p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">ophthe</hi> (aorist passive of <hi type="italic">horao</hi>, "to see") means "he was seen" or "he appeared." The passive form indicates that the risen Christ presented himself to be seen; the appearances were initiated by Christ, not sought by the witnesses. In the LXX, the verb <hi type="italic">ophthe</hi> is used for divine appearances: Genesis 12:7, <hi type="italic">ophthe kyrios to Abram</hi> — "the Lord appeared to Abram"; Genesis 17:1, <hi type="italic">ophthe kyrios to Abram</hi> — "the Lord appeared to Abram"; Genesis 26:2, <hi type="italic">ophthe de auto kyrios</hi> — "the Lord appeared to him." The verb carries theophanic overtones: the risen Christ appeared to his followers in the same manner that the Lord appeared to the patriarchs.</p>
          <p>Cephas (the Aramaic name for Simon Peter) is named first. He is the primary witness to the resurrection. The phrase <hi type="italic">eita tois dodeka</hi> ("then to the twelve") refers to the inner circle of disciples. The number "twelve" functions as a title for the group even when its membership was not exactly twelve at the time (Judas having died and his replacement not yet appointed in the earliest tradition). The twelve represent the reconstituted Israel — their number corresponds to the twelve tribes — and their witness to the resurrection is a corporate, communal witness, not merely a collection of individual experiences.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.15.6-1Cor.15.11">
          <title>Verses 6-11: The Extended Witness List and Paul's Apostolic Standing</title>
          <p>"Then he appeared to more than five hundred brothers at one time, most of whom are still alive, though some have fallen asleep. Then he appeared to James, then to all the apostles. Last of all, as to one untimely born, he appeared also to me" (15:6-8).</p>
          <p>Paul extends the witness list beyond the received tradition of verses 3-5. The phrase <hi type="italic">epeita ophthe epano pentakosiois adelphois ephapax</hi> ("then he appeared to more than five hundred brothers at one time") describes a mass appearance. The word <hi type="italic">ephapax</hi> ("at one time, all at once") specifies that this was a single event, not scattered individual sightings. Paul adds that <hi type="italic">hoi pleiones menousin heos arti, tines de ekoimethsan</hi> — "most of them remain until now, but some have fallen asleep." This parenthetical serves a forensic function: the witnesses are available. If anyone doubts the report, the majority of these five hundred are still alive and can be questioned. The word <hi type="italic">koimao</hi> ("to fall asleep") is Paul's euphemism for death throughout this chapter (vv. 6, 18, 20, 51). The metaphor of sleep for death is significant: sleep implies waking. Those who have "fallen asleep" are not permanently dead; they await resurrection.</p>
          <p>James is then named as a separate witness. This is James the brother of Jesus, who became the leader of the Jerusalem community (cf. Galatians 1:19, 2:9). His inclusion as a resurrection witness is noteworthy because the pre-resurrection traditions suggest that Jesus' brothers did not believe during his ministry. James' emergence as a leader of the community is best explained by the tradition Paul reports: the risen Christ appeared to him.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">eita tois apostolois pasin</hi> ("then to all the apostles") designates a group broader than the twelve. The word <hi type="italic">apostoloi</hi> ("apostles, sent ones") here refers to all those who held the apostolic office, not merely the twelve disciples. The post-resurrection appearances commissioned them as apostles — "sent ones" — who would carry the gospel forward.</p>
          <p>Then comes Paul's own testimony: <hi type="italic">eschaton de panton hosperanei to ektromati ophthe kamoi</hi> — "last of all, as to one untimely born, he appeared also to me." The word <hi type="italic">eschaton</hi> ("last") marks Paul's experience as the final resurrection appearance. The word <hi type="italic">ektroma</hi> ("miscarriage, abortion, untimely birth") is startling and self-deprecating. An <hi type="italic">ektroma</hi> is a child born before its time — premature, malformed, not fully developed. Paul regards his own apostolic birth as abnormal, out of sequence, violating the natural order. He was not one of the twelve, did not follow Jesus during his earthly ministry, and persecuted the community before being conscripted into it. His apostleship is genuine but irregular — he was born as an apostle in a manner that breaks the pattern.</p>
          <p>"For I am the least of the apostles, unworthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God" (15:9).</p>
          <p>The reason for the self-deprecation: <hi type="italic">ego gar eimi ho elachistos ton apostolon, hos ouk eimi hikanos kaleisthai apostolos, dioti edioxa ten ekklesian tou theou</hi> — "for I am the least of the apostles, who am not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the assembly of God." The verb <hi type="italic">dioko</hi> ("to persecute, to pursue, to chase") describes Paul's pre-conversion activity against the community. The phrase <hi type="italic">ten ekklesian tou theou</hi> ("the assembly of God") — the same phrase used in 1:2 to address the Corinthians and in 10:32 to describe one of the groups that must not be offended — identifies the community Paul attacked as God's own assembly. His persecution was not merely an attack on people but an assault on God's <hi type="italic">ekklesia</hi>.</p>
          <p>"But by the grace of God I am what I am, and his grace toward me was not in vain. On the contrary, I worked harder than any of them, though it was not I, but the grace of God that is with me" (15:10).</p>
          <p>The contrast is introduced by the strong adversative <hi type="italic">de</hi> ("but"): <hi type="italic">chariti de theou eimi ho eimi</hi> — "by the grace of God I am what I am." The word <hi type="italic">charis</hi> ("grace, favor, gift") describes the unmerited action of God that transformed the persecutor into an apostle. Paul's identity is not self-made; it is grace-made. And this grace was <hi type="italic">ouk kene</hi> ("not empty, not vain"). The adjective <hi type="italic">kenos</hi> echoes the <hi type="italic">eike</hi> ("in vain") of verse 2 and anticipates the <hi type="italic">kenos/kenon</hi> of verses 14 and 58. God's grace toward Paul was not empty — it produced fruit: <hi type="italic">perissoteron auton panton ekopiasa</hi> — "I labored more abundantly than all of them." The verb <hi type="italic">kopiao</hi> ("to labor, to toil, to work to the point of weariness") describes the hard work of apostolic ministry. Yet Paul immediately qualifies: <hi type="italic">ouk ego de alla he charis tou theou he syn emoi</hi> — "not I, but the grace of God that is with me." The labor was real, but the power behind the labor was grace. Paul refuses to claim credit for what grace accomplished through him.</p>
          <p>"Whether then it was I or they, so we preach and so you believed" (15:11).</p>
          <p>The verse concludes the witness section by rendering the identity of the preacher irrelevant to the content of the proclamation. Whether Paul or the other apostles — <hi type="italic">eite oun ego eite ekeinoi</hi> — the content is the same: <hi type="italic">houtos keryssomen kai houtos episteusate</hi> — "thus we preach and thus you believed." The <hi type="italic">kerygma</hi> of verses 3-5 is not Paul's private doctrine; it is the shared proclamation of all the apostles. And the Corinthians' faith rests on this shared proclamation, not on the authority of any single apostle. This point connects to the letter's opening argument against factions (1:10-17): whether Paul, Apollos, or Cephas, the gospel is one.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.15.12-1Cor.15.19">
          <title>Verses 12-19: If No Resurrection, Then Everything Collapses</title>
          <p>"Now if Christ is proclaimed as raised from the dead, how can some of you say that there is no resurrection of the dead?" (15:12).</p>
          <p>The conjunction <hi type="italic">de</hi> ("now, but") marks a transition from the <hi type="italic">kerygma</hi> to its implications. Paul states the problem: <hi type="italic">ei de Christos kerussetai hoti ek nekron egegertai, pos legousin en hymin tines hoti anastasis nekron ouk estin</hi> — "if Christ is proclaimed as raised from the dead, how do some among you say there is no resurrection of the dead?" The word <hi type="italic">tines</hi> ("some") identifies a faction within the community — not all the Corinthians, but some of them. These "some" deny the <hi type="italic">anastasis nekron</hi> ("resurrection of the dead") — the general future resurrection of dead persons.</p>
          <p>The precise nature of their denial matters. They apparently accept that Christ was raised (they are members of the community that confesses this), but they deny that the dead generally will be raised. This could reflect a Greco-Roman philosophical aversion to bodily resurrection. In the Greek philosophical tradition, the body was often regarded as inferior to the soul, a prison from which the soul escaped at death. The Epicureans denied any afterlife at all. The Platonists affirmed the soul's immortality but would have found the idea of a resurrected body repugnant — why would the soul return to the inferior body it had finally escaped? For many educated Greeks, the idea of bodily resurrection was not merely unproven but philosophically absurd. When Paul preached the resurrection in Athens, "some mocked" (Acts 17:32). The Corinthian deniers may share this cultural assumption: they accept that something happened to Jesus after his death (perhaps spiritualized as an exaltation or a departure to the heavenly realm) but reject the idea that dead bodies will be raised.</p>
          <p>Paul's argument in verses 12-19 is a <hi type="italic">reductio ad absurdum</hi>: if the general resurrection is denied, then Christ's resurrection must also be denied, and if Christ's resurrection is denied, everything collapses.</p>
          <p>"But if there is no resurrection of the dead, then not even Christ has been raised" (15:13).</p>
          <p>The logic is tight: <hi type="italic">ei de anastasis nekron ouk estin, oude Christos egegertai</hi> — "if resurrection of the dead does not exist, not even Christ has been raised." The point is that Christ's resurrection is not a unique, isolated miracle disconnected from the general resurrection. Christ's resurrection is the first instance of the general resurrection. If the category does not exist, the first instance cannot exist either. Paul refuses to let the Corinthians have Christ's resurrection without the general resurrection; the two are a package.</p>
          <p>"And if Christ has not been raised, then our preaching is in vain and your faith is in vain" (15:14).</p>
          <p>The first consequence of Christ's non-resurrection: <hi type="italic">kenon ara kai to kerygma hemon, kene kai he pistis hymon</hi> — "empty then is our preaching, empty also your faith." The adjective <hi type="italic">kenos</hi> ("empty, hollow, without content, without substance") describes both the apostolic proclamation and the community's faith. If Christ has not been raised, the preaching proclaimed a falsehood, and the faith that rested on the preaching grasped an illusion. The word <hi type="italic">kenos</hi> echoes the <hi type="italic">eike</hi> ("in vain") of verse 2: believing in vain is believing in something that turns out to be empty. The preaching is not merely mistaken but <hi type="italic">kenon</hi> — it has no content, no substance, no reality behind it.</p>
          <p>"We are even found to be misrepresenting God, because we testified about God that he raised Christ, whom he did not raise if it is true that the dead are not raised" (15:15).</p>
          <p>The second consequence: <hi type="italic">heuriskometha de kai pseudomartyres tou theou</hi> — "we are found to be false witnesses of God." The word <hi type="italic">pseudomartyres</hi> ("false witnesses") is a legal term. In the LXX, false witness is condemned repeatedly: Exodus 20:16, "You shall not bear false witness (<hi type="italic">ou pseudomartyreseis</hi>) against your neighbor"; Deuteronomy 19:16-19, the law against false witnesses requires that the false witness receive the penalty they sought to impose on the accused. Paul and the other apostles testified (<hi type="italic">emartyresamen</hi>) that God raised Christ. If God did not raise Christ, then they gave false testimony about God — they attributed to God an action he did not perform. This is not a minor error; it is bearing false witness about God himself.</p>
          <p>"For if the dead are not raised, not even Christ has been raised. And if Christ has not been raised, your faith is futile and you are still in your sins" (15:16-17).</p>
          <p>Paul restates the logic of verse 13 and draws a further consequence. The word <hi type="italic">mataia</hi> ("futile, useless, pointless, vain") describes faith directed at a falsehood. The word appears in the LXX for the worthlessness of idols and the vanity of idol worship: Jeremiah 2:5 (LXX), the people "went after worthlessness (<hi type="italic">mataia</hi>) and became worthless"; Jeremiah 8:19, "they have provoked me with their idols and their foreign vanities (<hi type="italic">mataiois</hi>)." Faith in a Christ who was not raised is faith as empty as idol worship — it grasps nothing real.</p>
          <p>The further consequence: <hi type="italic">eti este en tais hamartiais hymon</hi> — "you are still in your sins." The word <hi type="italic">eti</hi> ("still, yet") is devastating. The gospel promised that Christ died <hi type="italic">hyper ton hamartion hemon</hi> — "for our sins" (v. 3). If Christ has not been raised, then his death accomplished nothing regarding sins. The sins remain. The community is in the same condition it was in before they heard the gospel. The forgiveness they thought they received was an illusion. The "still" implies continuity with their pre-faith state: nothing has changed.</p>
          <p>"Then those also who have fallen asleep in Christ have perished" (15:18).</p>
          <p>The consequence extends to the dead: <hi type="italic">ara kai hoi koimethentes en Christo apolonto</hi> — "then also those who fell asleep in Christ perished." The verb <hi type="italic">apollymi</hi> ("to destroy, to perish, to be lost") is the same verb that described the perishing of those who reject the cross in 1:18 and the destruction of the weak brother in 8:11. The perfect tense <hi type="italic">koimethentes</hi> ("having fallen asleep") describes believers who died trusting in Christ. If Christ has not been raised, they perished — not merely died but were destroyed, lost permanently. The phrase <hi type="italic">en Christo</hi> ("in Christ") makes the tragedy acute: they died in connection with Christ, trusting in him, belonging to him. If his resurrection was false, their trust was misplaced, and their death was final.</p>
          <p>"If in Christ we have hope in this life only, we are of all people most to be pitied" (15:19).</p>
          <p>The climax of the <hi type="italic">reductio</hi>: <hi type="italic">ei en te zoe taute en Christo elpikotes esmen monon, eleeinoteroi panton anthropon esmen</hi> — "if in this life only we have hoped in Christ, we are the most pitiable of all people." The comparative <hi type="italic">eleenoteroi</hi> ("more pitiable") is intensified by <hi type="italic">panton anthropon</hi> ("of all people"). If the resurrection is false, Christians are not merely mistaken — they are the most pathetic of all human beings. They have surrendered pleasures, endured persecution, risked death, and reorganized their entire lives around a falsehood. Everyone else at least lived for what is real; Christians lived for an illusion. The claim is not that the Christian life has no present benefits; the claim is that the Christian life involves such severe costs (which Paul has already catalogued: the self-denial of chapter 9, the daily danger he will describe in vv. 30-32) that only the resurrection makes those costs worthwhile.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.15.20-1Cor.15.28">
          <title>Verses 20-28: Christ the Firstfruits and the Subjection of All Things</title>
          <p>"But in fact Christ has been raised from the dead, the firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep" (15:20).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">nyni de</hi> ("but now, but in fact") marks the decisive turn from the hypothetical to the actual. After seven verses of consequences that would follow if Christ had not been raised, Paul returns to reality: <hi type="italic">Christos egegertai ek nekron</hi> — "Christ has been raised from the dead." The perfect tense <hi type="italic">egegertai</hi> reappears: he has been raised and remains raised. This is not hypothetical; it is the foundation of everything.</p>
          <p>The appositive <hi type="italic">apharche ton kekoimemenon</hi> ("firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep") introduces a metaphor drawn from Israel's agricultural and liturgical life. The word <hi type="italic">aparche</hi> ("firstfruits, first portion of the harvest") is a technical term from the Torah. In Leviticus 23:9-14, the Lord commands Israel to bring a sheaf of the firstfruits of the harvest (<hi type="italic">to dragma aparchen tou therismou hymon</hi> in the LXX) to the priest, who waves it before the Lord "on the day after the Sabbath" (Leviticus 23:11). The firstfruits offering was not the entire harvest; it was the first portion, offered to God as a pledge and guarantee that the full harvest would follow. The firstfruits and the full harvest are the same crop — the firstfruits is not a different kind of grain from the rest; it is the first of the same kind. The firstfruits sanctifies the rest: "If the dough offered as firstfruits is holy, so is the whole lump" (Romans 11:16).</p>
          <p>By calling Christ the <hi type="italic">aparche</hi> of those who have fallen asleep, Paul makes two claims simultaneously. First, Christ's resurrection is not an isolated event; it is the beginning of a harvest. Others will follow. Christ is the first to be raised; those who belong to him will be raised after him. Second, Christ's resurrection and the believers' resurrection are the same kind of event. The firstfruits and the full harvest are the same crop. What happened to Christ's body — it was raised, transformed, glorified — will happen to the bodies of those who have fallen asleep in him.</p>
          <p>"For as by a man came death, by a man has come also the resurrection of the dead. For as in Adam all die, so also in Christ shall all be made alive" (15:21-22).</p>
          <p>Paul introduces the Adam/Christ typology, which draws entirely on Genesis. The phrase <hi type="italic">epeidh gar di' anthropou thanatos, kai di' anthropou anastasis nekron</hi> — "for since through a human being death, also through a human being resurrection of the dead" — sets up the parallel in its simplest form. Death entered the world through one human being; resurrection enters the world through another human being.</p>
          <p>The reference to death through a human being is Genesis 2-3. God commanded the man: "of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil you shall not eat, for in the day that you eat of it you shall surely die" (Genesis 2:17, LXX: <hi type="italic">thanato apothaneisthe</hi>). The man ate, and death entered. In Genesis 3:19: "you are dust, and to dust you shall return" (<hi type="italic">ge ei kai eis gen apeleuse</hi>). Death is the consequence of Adam's transgression — not merely the death of Adam individually but the reign of death over all who share in Adam's humanity.</p>
          <p>Paul expands the parallel: <hi type="italic">hosper gar en to Adam pantes apothneskousin, houtos kai en to Christo pantes zoopoiethesontai</hi> — "for as in Adam all die, so also in Christ all shall be made alive." The verb <hi type="italic">zoopoieo</hi> ("to make alive, to give life, to quicken") is the future passive: "shall be made alive." The parallel is structured:</p>
          <list type="x-bullet">
            <item>In Adam: all die (present tense — death is the ongoing condition of humanity in Adam)</item>
            <item>In Christ: all shall be made alive (future tense — the giving of life is yet to come)</item>
          </list>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">pantes</hi> ("all") appears in both clauses. The scope of the "all" in each clause is determined by the prepositional phrase that governs it. "In Adam" all die — all who are in Adam, which is all humanity, since all share in Adam's mortality. "In Christ" all shall be made alive — all who are in Christ. The phrase <hi type="italic">en to Christo</hi> ("in Christ") is Paul's characteristic designation for those who belong to Christ, who are incorporated into Christ's body, who share in Christ's life. The "all" in the second clause is not a universal statement about all human beings without exception but a statement about all who are <hi type="italic">en Christo</hi>. Verse 23 will confirm this by specifying who the "all" are: "those who belong to Christ."</p>
          <p>"But each in his own order: Christ the firstfruits, then at his coming those who belong to Christ" (15:23).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">tagma</hi> ("order, rank, arrangement, division") is a military term for a unit or division within an army. Each unit has its appointed place in the sequence. The resurrection proceeds in ordered stages, not all at once:</p>
          <p>First <hi type="italic">tagma</hi>: <hi type="italic">Christos aparche</hi> — "Christ the firstfruits." Christ has already been raised. His resurrection is past (<hi type="italic">egegertai</hi>, perfect tense, v. 20).</p>
          <p>Second <hi type="italic">tagma</hi>: <hi type="italic">epeita hoi tou Christou en te parousia autou</hi> — "then those who belong to Christ at his coming." The word <hi type="italic">epeita</hi> ("then, afterward, next in sequence") indicates temporal separation. The second stage does not happen simultaneously with the first; it happens later. The phrase <hi type="italic">hoi tou Christou</hi> ("those of Christ, those who belong to Christ") defines who participates in the second stage: those who belong to Christ — not all human beings, but those who are in Christ, those whose relationship to him is real and enduring. The word <hi type="italic">parousia</hi> ("coming, arrival, presence") is the technical term for the Lord's return. In the LXX, <hi type="italic">parousia</hi> can describe the arrival of a king or dignitary. Paul used the word in 1 Thessalonians 4:15 for the Lord's coming at the end. The resurrection of those who belong to Christ happens at his <hi type="italic">parousia</hi> — when he arrives.</p>
          <p>"Then comes the end, when he delivers the kingdom to God the Father after destroying every rule and every authority and power" (15:24).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">eita</hi> ("then, next") introduces the third stage: <hi type="italic">to telos</hi> — "the end." The word <hi type="italic">telos</hi> can mean "end" (termination), "goal" (purpose), or "completion" (fulfillment). In this context, it describes the consummation of God's plan — the point at which the sequence reaches its destination. At this point, Christ <hi type="italic">paradido ten basileian to theo kai patri</hi> — "hands over the kingdom to God the Father." The verb <hi type="italic">paradidomi</hi> ("to hand over, to deliver, to entrust") is the same verb used for the transmission of tradition in verse 3 (<hi type="italic">paredoka</hi>, "I delivered"). Christ has been reigning; at the <hi type="italic">telos</hi>, he delivers the kingdom to the Father.</p>
          <p>The condition for this handover: <hi type="italic">hotan katargese pasan archen kai pasan exousian kai dynamin</hi> — "when he has abolished every rule and every authority and power." The verb <hi type="italic">katargeo</hi> ("to abolish, to render inoperative, to bring to an end") is the same verb used in 13:8-10 for the abolishing of prophecy, tongues, and knowledge when the perfect comes. Here it is applied to hostile powers: <hi type="italic">arche</hi> ("rule, sovereignty, principality"), <hi type="italic">exousia</hi> ("authority, power"), and <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> ("power, force, might"). These are not merely human political powers; they are the powers that stand behind the structures of death, sin, and oppression. Paul will identify the last of these powers in verse 26: death itself.</p>
          <p>"For he must reign until he has put all his enemies under his feet" (15:25).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">dei</hi> ("it is necessary, he must") indicates divine necessity: Christ's reign is not optional but required by God's plan. The verb <hi type="italic">basileuein</hi> ("to reign") describes Christ's present activity — he is currently reigning. The phrase <hi type="italic">achri hou the pantes tous echthrous hypo tous podas autou</hi> — "until he has placed all the enemies under his feet" — draws directly from Psalm 110:1 (LXX 109:1): <hi type="italic">eipen ho kyrios to kyrio mou, Kathou ek dexion mou, heos an tho tous echthrous sou hypopodion ton podon sou</hi> — "The Lord said to my Lord, 'Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet.'" This psalm was widely understood in first-century Judaism as a messianic text — a psalm about the coming king who would sit at God's right hand and rule over his enemies. Paul reads it christologically: Christ is the one seated at God's right hand, and he is currently reigning until the subjection of all enemies is complete.</p>
          <p>The image of placing enemies "under the feet" comes from the ancient Near Eastern practice of a conquering king placing his foot on the neck of a defeated enemy (cf. Joshua 10:24, where Joshua tells his commanders, "Come near; put your feet on the necks of these kings"). The metaphor describes total victory and absolute sovereignty.</p>
          <p>"The last enemy to be destroyed is death" (15:26).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">eschatos</hi> ("last, final") identifies death as the final power to be subdued. Death is not merely a natural process but an <hi type="italic">echthros</hi> — an "enemy." The personification of death as a hostile power echoes the LXX tradition. In Isaiah 25:8 (LXX): <hi type="italic">katiepien ho thanatos ischysas</hi> — "death, having prevailed, swallowed up" (the Hebrew reads: "He will swallow up death forever"). In Hosea 13:14 (LXX): <hi type="italic">ek cheirs hadou rhysomai autous kai ek thanatou lytrōsomai autous. Pou he dike sou, thanate? Pou to kentron sou, hade?</hi> — "I will deliver them from the hand of Hades, and I will redeem them from death. Where is your penalty, O death? Where is your sting, O Hades?" Paul will quote both of these texts explicitly in verses 54-55. Death is the last enemy because death is the consequence and instrument of all the other hostile powers. Sin produces death (v. 56); the powers that oppose God use death as their weapon. When death is destroyed, the last weapon in the enemy's arsenal is eliminated.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">katargeitai</hi> ("is destroyed, is being destroyed, is being rendered inoperative") is the present passive of <hi type="italic">katargeo</hi>. The present tense may indicate that the destruction of death is already underway — Christ's resurrection has already begun the process — or it may be a futuristic present, describing the certain future event as though it were already happening.</p>
          <p>"For 'God has put all things in subjection under his feet.' But when it says, 'all things are put in subjection,' it is plain that he is excepted who put all things in subjection under him" (15:27).</p>
          <p>Paul introduces a second psalm quotation: <hi type="italic">panta gar hypetaxen hypo tous podas autou</hi> — "for he subjected all things under his feet." This is from Psalm 8:6 (LXX 8:7): <hi type="italic">kai katestesas auton epi ta erga ton cheiron sou, panta hypetaxas hypokatō ton podon autou</hi> — "you set him over the works of your hands; you subjected all things under his feet." In its original context, Psalm 8 is about humanity — the "son of man" (<hi type="italic">ben adam/huios anthropou</hi>) whom God made "a little lower than the angels" and crowned with glory and honor, giving him dominion over the works of God's hands. Paul reads this psalm christologically: the "man" of Psalm 8 is Christ, the true human being who exercises the dominion that Adam forfeited.</p>
          <p>Paul then adds a clarification: <hi type="italic">hotan de eipe hoti panta hypotetaktai, delon hoti ektos tou hypotaxantos auto ta panta</hi> — "when it says 'all things are subjected,' it is clear that the one who subjected all things to him is excepted." God is the one who subjects all things to Christ; God himself is not among the "all things" subjected. The clarification prevents a misreading in which Christ's authority extends even over God. The Father subjects all things to the Son, but the Father himself remains above the Son in the ordering of authority.</p>
          <p>"When all things are subjected to him, then the Son himself will also be subjected to the one who put all things in subjection under him, that God may be all in all" (15:28).</p>
          <p>The final step: <hi type="italic">hotan de hypotagē auto ta panta, tote kai autos ho huios hypotagēsetai to hypotaxanti auto ta panta, hina ē ho theos panta en pasin</hi> — "when all things are subjected to him, then the Son himself will also be subjected to the one who subjected all things to him, so that God may be all in all." The Son's reign is not permanent in its present form. It is instrumental — it serves the purpose of subjecting all hostile powers. When that purpose is accomplished, the Son himself submits to the Father. The <hi type="italic">telos</hi> ("end") of verse 24 is this moment: when Christ has destroyed every enemy, he delivers the kingdom to the Father, and God becomes <hi type="italic">panta en pasin</hi> — "all in all, everything in everything."</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">panta en pasin</hi> describes a state in which God's sovereignty is uncontested, his presence fills all reality, and no rival power remains. In the LXX, God's sovereignty over all things is a recurring theme: Psalm 103:19 (LXX 102:19): <hi type="italic">en to ourano hetoimasen ton thronon autou, kai he basileia autou panton despozei</hi> — "in heaven he has established his throne, and his kingdom rules over all." The <hi type="italic">telos</hi> of God's plan is not the Son's independent rule but the Father's universal sovereignty, with the Son's reign as the means by which the Father's sovereignty is restored over a creation that had been subjected to hostile powers.</p>
          <p>The sequence of verses 20-28 thus describes a cosmic timeline:</p>
          <list type="x-ordered">
            <item>Christ has been raised — the firstfruits (v. 20)</item>
            <item>In Adam all die; in Christ all shall be made alive (vv. 21-22)</item>
            <item>Christ the firstfruits, first; then those who belong to Christ at his <hi type="italic">parousia</hi> (v. 23)</item>
            <item>Then the <hi type="italic">telos</hi>: Christ delivers the kingdom to the Father, having destroyed every hostile power (v. 24)</item>
            <item>Christ reigns until all enemies are under his feet (v. 25)</item>
            <item>The last enemy, death, is destroyed (v. 26)</item>
            <item>All things are subjected to Christ; then Christ is subjected to the Father (vv. 27-28)</item>
            <item>God is all in all (v. 28)</item>
          </list>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.15.29-1Cor.15.34">
          <title>Verses 29-34: Practical Consequences of the Resurrection</title>
          <p>"Otherwise, what do people mean by being baptized on behalf of the dead? If the dead are not raised at all, why are people baptized on their behalf?" (15:29).</p>
          <p>Paul shifts from the cosmic timeline to practical arguments that make sense only if the resurrection is real. The phrase <hi type="italic">epei ti poisousin hoi baptizomenoi hyper ton nekron</hi> ("otherwise what will those do who are being baptized for the dead?") introduces a practice that is notoriously difficult to interpret. The phrase <hi type="italic">hyper ton nekron</hi> ("on behalf of the dead, for the dead") most naturally reads as a practice in which living members of the community are baptized on behalf of persons who have died. Paul does not endorse or condemn this practice; he uses it as evidence. His argument is <hi type="italic">ad hominem</hi>: some of you are doing this; if there is no resurrection, the practice makes no sense.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ei holos nekroi ouk egeirontai</hi> ("if the dead are not raised at all") uses <hi type="italic">holos</hi> ("at all, altogether") to strengthen the denial. If dead people are simply not raised — not at all, not in any sense — then why would anyone undergo baptism for them? The practice presupposes that the dead have a future; if they do not, the practice is nonsensical. Paul is pointing to behavior within the community that contradicts the denial some of them are making. Their practice assumes what their theology denies.</p>
          <p>"Why are we in danger every hour?" (15:30).</p>
          <p>The second practical argument comes from Paul's own life: <hi type="italic">ti kai hemeis kindyneuomen pasan horan</hi> — "why do we also face danger every hour?" The verb <hi type="italic">kindyneuo</hi> ("to face danger, to be at risk, to be in peril") describes the ongoing threat that accompanies Paul's apostolic ministry. The phrase <hi type="italic">pasan horan</hi> ("every hour") indicates constant, unrelenting danger. If there is no resurrection, why would Paul endure such risk? He has nothing to gain. The danger is not hypothetical; it is Paul's daily reality.</p>
          <p>"I protest, brothers, by my pride in you, which I have in Christ Jesus our Lord, I die every day!" (15:31).</p>
          <p>The oath formula <hi type="italic">nē tēn hymeteran kauchēsin, adelphoi, hēn echo en Christo Iēsou to kyrio hēmōn</hi> — "by your boasting/my pride in you, brothers, which I have in Christ Jesus our Lord" — is a solemn asseveration. Paul swears by the boast he has in them — the pride he takes in the community he founded. Then the claim: <hi type="italic">kath' hēmeran apothnēsko</hi> — "I die every day." The phrase describes not literal daily death but the daily experience of mortal danger, of self-denial so radical that it amounts to a daily dying. Paul described this same reality in 2 Corinthians 4:10-11: "always carrying in the body the death of Jesus, so that the life of Jesus may also be manifested in our bodies." The daily dying is endured for the sake of the gospel — but it makes sense only if the dead will be raised. Without resurrection, the daily dying is pointless self-destruction.</p>
          <p>"What do I gain if, humanly speaking, I fought with beasts at Ephesus? If the dead are not raised, 'Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we die'" (15:32).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ei kata anthrōpon ethēriomachēsa en Epheso</hi> ("if, according to human reckoning, I fought with beasts at Ephesus") may refer to a literal encounter with wild animals in the arena (though Paul's Roman citizenship would normally have exempted him from this) or, more likely, to severe opposition in Ephesus that was so dangerous it felt like being thrown to wild beasts. The question <hi type="italic">ti moi to ophelos</hi> — "what benefit is there for me?" — is rhetorical. If the dead are not raised, there is no benefit. Paul's suffering is wasted.</p>
          <p>Paul then quotes Isaiah 22:13 (LXX): <hi type="italic">phagōmen kai piōmen, aurion gar apothnēskomen</hi> — "let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we die." In Isaiah's original context, the quotation describes the people of Jerusalem during the Assyrian siege. God called for weeping and mourning, but instead the people feasted — "slaughtering oxen and killing sheep, eating flesh and drinking wine" — because they assumed they were about to die anyway (Isaiah 22:12-13). The prophet condemned this attitude as a failure of faith: instead of turning to God, the people turned to hedonism. Paul's use of the quotation makes the same point in reverse: if there is no resurrection, then the hedonists are right. If death is final, the only rational response is to pursue pleasure while you can. The fact that Paul does not pursue pleasure — but instead faces danger every hour and dies every day — is itself evidence of his conviction that death is not final.</p>
          <p>"Do not be deceived: 'Bad company ruins good morals'" (15:33).</p>
          <p>The command <hi type="italic">mē planasthe</hi> ("do not be led astray, do not be deceived") warns against the influence of the resurrection-deniers. The verb <hi type="italic">planao</hi> ("to lead astray, to deceive, to cause to wander") describes not innocent error but a wandering from truth that has moral consequences. The quotation <hi type="italic">phtheirousin ēthē chrēstha homiliai kakai</hi> — "bad company corrupts good character" — is from the Athenian comic playwright Menander (<hi type="italic">Thais</hi>, fragment 218). The word <hi type="italic">homiliai</hi> ("companionship, association, company") describes the social relationships that shape a person's character. The word <hi type="italic">ēthē</hi> ("morals, character, customs, habits") describes the moral dispositions formed by those relationships. Paul warns that associating with those who deny the resurrection will corrupt the community's moral character. The denial of resurrection is not a harmless intellectual opinion; it is a moral toxin that will erode behavior.</p>
          <p>"Come to your right mind, and sin no more. For some have no knowledge of God. I say this to your shame" (15:34).</p>
          <p>The command <hi type="italic">eknēpsate dikaiōs kai mē hamartanete</hi> ("sober up righteously and stop sinning") uses the verb <hi type="italic">eknēpho</hi> ("to become sober, to come to one's senses from a drunken stupor"). The image is of people who have been intoxicated by false teaching and need to wake up. The phrase <hi type="italic">mē hamartanete</hi> ("stop sinning") indicates that the denial of the resurrection is not merely an intellectual error but a sin — a departure from the truth that has moral dimensions.</p>
          <p>The final statement — <hi type="italic">agnosian gar theou tines echousin, pros entropēn hymin lalō</hi> — "for some have ignorance of God; I say this to shame you" — is a stinging rebuke. The word <hi type="italic">agnosia</hi> ("ignorance, lack of knowledge") is the opposite of the <hi type="italic">gnosis</hi> the Corinthians boast about (8:1). Some among them, despite their claims to knowledge, have <hi type="italic">agnosia theou</hi> — "ignorance of God." They do not know God as the God who raises the dead. The phrase <hi type="italic">pros entropēn hymin</hi> ("to your shame") is the same rebuke Paul used in 6:5: "I say this to your shame." The community that prides itself on knowledge harbors members who are ignorant of God's most fundamental action.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.15.35-1Cor.15.41">
          <title>Verses 35-41: The Seed Analogy and the Diversity of Bodies</title>
          <p>"But someone will ask, 'How are the dead raised? With what kind of body do they come?'" (15:35).</p>
          <p>Paul introduces an objection through the diatribe form: <hi type="italic">alla erei tis</hi> ("but someone will say"). The two questions represent the skeptic's challenge: how (<hi type="italic">pos</hi>) does the resurrection happen mechanically, and with what kind (<hi type="italic">poio</hi>) of body do the dead come back? The second question is the one Paul will address at length. The skeptic assumes that a raised body must be the same kind of body that died — the same flesh, the same composition. Since a decomposed body obviously cannot be reconstituted (so the thinking goes), resurrection is impossible. Paul will demolish this assumption.</p>
          <p>"You foolish person! What you sow does not come to life unless it dies" (15:36).</p>
          <p>The address <hi type="italic">aphron</hi> ("foolish one, senseless one") is sharp but not vicious. In the LXX, <hi type="italic">aphron</hi> describes someone who lacks understanding: Psalm 14:1 (LXX 13:1): <hi type="italic">eipen aphrōn en kardia autou, Ouk estin theos</hi> — "the fool said in his heart, 'There is no God.'" The word denotes not stupidity but a failure to perceive what should be obvious. The objector has missed something that even agricultural experience should teach.</p>
          <p>The answer begins with an analogy from sowing: <hi type="italic">ho speireis, ou zoopoieitai ean mē apothane</hi> — "what you sow is not made alive unless it dies." The verb <hi type="italic">zoopoieo</hi> ("to make alive, to give life") reappears from verse 22. The grain seed must die in the ground before the plant can live. Death is the prerequisite for new life, not the negation of it. The seed is buried in the earth and decomposes; from that decomposition, the plant emerges. The analogy directly addresses the skeptic's assumption: the objector thinks death prevents resurrection; Paul's analogy shows that death enables resurrection.</p>
          <p>"And what you sow is not the body that is to be, but a bare kernel, perhaps of wheat or of some other grain. But God gives it a body as he has chosen, and to each kind of seed its own body" (15:37-38).</p>
          <p>The critical point: <hi type="italic">kai ho speireis, ou to sōma to genēsomenon speireis alla gymnon kokkon</hi> — "what you sow, you do not sow the body that will come to be, but a bare grain." The word <hi type="italic">gymnos</hi> ("bare, naked, stripped") describes the seed in its raw, unadorned state — just a kernel, nothing more. The seed does not look like the plant it will become. A wheat kernel does not resemble a stalk of wheat with ears of grain. The body that is sown (the seed) and the body that comes to be (the plant) are radically different in appearance and structure, yet they are continuous — the plant comes from the seed.</p>
          <p>Then the theological turn: <hi type="italic">ho de theos didosin auto sōma kathōs ēthelēsen, kai hekastō tōn spermatōn idion sōma</hi> — "but God gives it a body as he willed, and to each of the seeds its own body." The giving of the body is God's act, not a natural process operating independently of God. God chose (<hi type="italic">ēthelēsen</hi>, aorist of <hi type="italic">thelo</hi>) what kind of body each seed would produce. Wheat seeds produce wheat bodies; barley seeds produce barley bodies. Each seed gets its own (<hi type="italic">idion</hi>) body — distinct, appropriate to its kind, different from the seed that was planted. The application to resurrection is direct: the body that is buried (the seed) will not be the same as the body that is raised (the plant). God will give the raised person a new body, appropriate to the new mode of existence, just as God gives each seed a body appropriate to its kind.</p>
          <p>"Not all flesh is the same flesh, but there is one kind for humans, another for animals, another for birds, and another for fish" (15:39).</p>
          <p>Paul broadens the argument beyond seeds to the diversity of bodies in creation. The word <hi type="italic">sarx</hi> ("flesh") describes the physical material of living creatures. The point is that "body" does not mean one thing only. Different creatures have different kinds of flesh. The variety in creation demonstrates that God is not limited to a single form of embodiment. If God can make human flesh, animal flesh, bird flesh, and fish flesh — all radically different from one another — then God can certainly make a resurrection body that differs from the present earthly body.</p>
          <p>"There are heavenly bodies and earthly bodies, but the glory of the heavenly is of one kind, and the glory of the earthly is of another. There is one glory of the sun, and another glory of the moon, and another glory of the stars; for star differs from star in glory" (15:40-41).</p>
          <p>Paul now extends the diversity principle to the cosmic realm. The word <hi type="italic">sōmata</hi> ("bodies") is applied to celestial objects: sun, moon, stars. The word <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> ("glory, splendor, radiance, brightness") describes the distinctive quality of each. The sun has its own <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> — its radiance, its brilliance. The moon has a different <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> — reflected light, softer, dimmer. The stars each have their own <hi type="italic">doxa</hi>, and <hi type="italic">aster gar asteros diapherei en doxe</hi> — "star differs from star in glory." Even within the same category (stars), there is variation in glory.</p>
          <p>The argument accumulates: seeds produce different plants; different creatures have different flesh; heavenly bodies differ from earthly bodies; heavenly bodies differ from each other. The created order is characterized by diversity of form and glory. God has made bodies of every conceivable kind. The skeptic who asks "with what kind of body?" as though the question were unanswerable has failed to observe God's creativity in the world that already exists.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.15.42-1Cor.15.49">
          <title>Verses 42-49: The Resurrection Body Contrasted with the Present Body</title>
          <p>"So is it with the resurrection of the dead. What is sown is perishable; what is raised is imperishable" (15:42).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">houtōs kai hē anastasis tōn nekrōn</hi> ("so also the resurrection of the dead") applies all the preceding analogies to the resurrection. Paul now describes the transformation using four contrasting pairs, each structured as "sown in X, raised in Y":</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">First pair: "It is sown in perishability, it is raised in imperishability" (<hi type="italic">speiretai en phthora, egeiretai en aphtharsia</hi>).</hi></p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">phthora</hi> ("perishability, corruption, decay, destruction") describes the present body's subjection to decomposition and death. The body that goes into the ground decays — it is a body characterized by <hi type="italic">phthora</hi>. The word <hi type="italic">aphtharsia</hi> ("imperishability, incorruptibility, immortality") describes the resurrection body's freedom from decay. The raised body does not decompose, does not age, does not deteriorate. The contrast is not between physical and non-physical but between mortal and immortal.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Second pair: "It is sown in dishonor, it is raised in glory" (<hi type="italic">speiretai en atimia, egeiretai en doxe</hi>) (15:43a).</hi></p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">atimia</hi> ("dishonor, shame, disgrace, lowliness") describes the condition of a corpse. In Israelite and Greco-Roman culture, the dead body is an object of impurity and shame. Contact with a corpse made one unclean (Numbers 19:11-16). The body in death is stripped of dignity, subject to decay, repulsive. The word <hi type="italic">doxa</hi> ("glory, splendor, honor, radiance") describes the resurrection body's condition. The raised body is glorious — not merely restored to its former state but elevated to a condition of splendor it never possessed before.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Third pair: "It is sown in weakness, it is raised in power" (<hi type="italic">speiretai en astheneia, egeiretai en dynamei</hi>) (15:43b).</hi></p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">astheneia</hi> ("weakness, infirmity, frailty") describes the present body's vulnerability — its susceptibility to illness, injury, exhaustion, and death. The word <hi type="italic">dynamis</hi> ("power, strength, capacity") describes the resurrection body's robustness. The raised body is not fragile; it is characterized by power. The contrast runs through the letter: in 1:25, the "weakness of God" (<hi type="italic">to asthenes tou theou</hi>) was stronger than human strength; in 2:3, Paul came to Corinth "in weakness" (<hi type="italic">en astheneia</hi>). The word <hi type="italic">astheneia</hi> has described both the present human condition and the mode of God's operation in the present age. The resurrection transforms weakness into power.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Fourth pair: "It is sown a natural body, it is raised a spiritual body" (<hi type="italic">speiretai sōma psychikon, egeiretai sōma pneumatikon</hi>) (15:44a).</hi></p>
          <p>This is the most important and most frequently misunderstood of the four contrasts. The adjective <hi type="italic">psychikon</hi> ("natural, soulish, animated by <hi type="italic">psyche</hi>") comes from <hi type="italic">psyche</hi> ("soul, life, natural life-force"). The adjective <hi type="italic">pneumatikon</hi> ("spiritual, animated by the Spirit") comes from <hi type="italic">pneuma</hi> ("spirit, Spirit"). The contrast is not between "physical" and "non-physical" but between two modes of animation. The present body is a <hi type="italic">sōma psychikon</hi> — a body animated by the natural life-force (<hi type="italic">psyche</hi>), subject to the conditions of earthly existence: hunger, fatigue, aging, death. The resurrection body is a <hi type="italic">sōma pneumatikon</hi> — a body animated by the Spirit (<hi type="italic">pneuma</hi>), subject to the conditions of heavenly existence: imperishability, glory, power. Both are bodies (<hi type="italic">sōma</hi>). The <hi type="italic">pneumatikon</hi> body is not less physical than the <hi type="italic">psychikon</hi> body; it is differently constituted and differently animated. It is a body fit for life in the age to come, just as the <hi type="italic">psychikon</hi> body is a body fit for life in the present age.</p>
          <p>"If there is a natural body, there is also a spiritual body" (15:44b).</p>
          <p>Paul states the logic: <hi type="italic">ei estin sōma psychikon, estin kai pneumatikon</hi> — "if there is a natural body, there is also a spiritual body." The existence of the one implies the existence of the other. The two are paired realities, corresponding to the two ages (the present age and the age to come) and to the two representative humans (Adam and Christ).</p>
          <p>"Thus it is written, 'The first man Adam became a living being'; the last Adam became a life-giving spirit" (15:45).</p>
          <p>Paul now grounds the contrast in Genesis. The quotation is from Genesis 2:7 (LXX): <hi type="italic">kai eplasem ho theos ton anthrōpon, choun apo tēs gēs, kai enephysēsen eis to prosōpon autou pnoēn zōēs, kai egeneto ho anthrōpos eis psychēn zōsan</hi> — "and God formed the human being, dust from the earth, and breathed into his face the breath of life, and the human being became a living soul." Paul quotes the final clause with the addition of the name: <hi type="italic">egeneto ho prōtos anthrōpos Adam eis psychēn zōsan</hi> — "the first man Adam became a living soul/being." The word <hi type="italic">psyche</hi> here defines Adam's mode of existence: he is a <hi type="italic">psyche zōsa</hi> — a living soul, a being animated by the natural life-force. This is the <hi type="italic">psychikon</hi> existence — natural, earthly, mortal.</p>
          <p>The contrasting statement is Paul's own formulation, not a quotation: <hi type="italic">ho eschatos Adam eis pneuma zōopoioun</hi> — "the last Adam [became] a life-giving spirit." Christ is the "last Adam" — the final representative human. Where the first Adam became a living <hi type="italic">psyche</hi> (receiving life), the last Adam became a life-giving <hi type="italic">pneuma</hi> (giving life). The word <hi type="italic">zōopoioun</hi> ("life-giving, making alive") connects to verses 22 ("in Christ shall all be made alive," <hi type="italic">zōopoiēthēsontai</hi>) and 36 ("what you sow is not made alive unless it dies," <hi type="italic">zōopoieitai</hi>). Christ does not merely receive life, as Adam did; Christ gives life. He is the source of the new mode of existence — the <hi type="italic">pneumatikon</hi> life that characterizes the age to come.</p>
          <p>"But it is not the spiritual that is first but the natural, and then the spiritual" (15:46).</p>
          <p>Paul establishes the order: <hi type="italic">all' ou prōton to pneumatikon alla to psychikon, epeita to pneumatikon</hi> — "but not first the spiritual, but the natural, then the spiritual." The natural (<hi type="italic">psychikon</hi>) comes first in the sequence; the spiritual (<hi type="italic">pneumatikon</hi>) comes second. Adam came before Christ; the present age comes before the age to come; the <hi type="italic">sōma psychikon</hi> comes before the <hi type="italic">sōma pneumatikon</hi>. This sequence may respond to a Corinthian belief that the spiritual has always been primary — that the pneumatic existence is the original state to which the soul returns. Paul insists on the opposite order: the earthly, natural existence comes first; the heavenly, spiritual existence comes second. The movement is forward, not backward — from the earthly to the heavenly, from Adam to Christ, from perishability to imperishability.</p>
          <p>"The first man was from the earth, a man of dust; the second man is from heaven" (15:47).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">choikos</hi> ("dusty, made of dust, earthly") comes from <hi type="italic">chous</hi> ("dust, earth, soil") and reaches back to Genesis 2:7: God formed the man from <hi type="italic">chous apo tēs gēs</hi> ("dust from the earth"). Adam's origin defines his nature: he is <hi type="italic">choikos</hi> — earthy, dusty, made of and belonging to the ground. Christ is <hi type="italic">ex ouranou</hi> ("from heaven") — his origin is heavenly, and his nature corresponds to his origin.</p>
          <p>"As was the man of dust, so also are those who are of the dust, and as is the man of heaven, so also are those who are of heaven. Just as we have borne the image of the man of dust, we shall also bear the image of the man of heaven" (15:48-49).</p>
          <p>The typological logic extends from the representatives to those they represent. The phrase <hi type="italic">hoios ho choikos, toioutoi kai hoi choikoi</hi> ("as is the dusty one, so also are the dusty ones") means that all who share in Adam's nature share in Adam's characteristics — perishability, weakness, mortality. And <hi type="italic">hoios ho epouranios, toioutoi kai hoi epouranioi</hi> ("as is the heavenly one, so also are the heavenly ones") means that all who share in Christ's nature will share in Christ's characteristics — imperishability, glory, power.</p>
          <p>The conclusion: <hi type="italic">kathōs ephoresamen tēn eikona tou choikou, phoresomen kai tēn eikona tou epouraniou</hi> — "just as we bore the image of the dusty one, we shall also bear the image of the heavenly one." The word <hi type="italic">eikōn</hi> ("image, likeness, form") reaches back to Genesis 1:26-27, where God made humanity <hi type="italic">kat' eikona theou</hi> ("in the image of God"). The word also appears in the LXX of Genesis 5:3, where Adam "fathered a son in his own likeness, after his image" (<hi type="italic">kata tēn eikona autou</hi>). Humanity bears Adam's image — his dusty, mortal form. The promise is that believers will bear Christ's image — his heavenly, imperishable form. The verb <hi type="italic">phoresomen</hi> ("we shall bear, we shall wear") is the future tense: the bearing of Christ's image is not yet complete; it belongs to the resurrection future.</p>
          <p>The manuscripts divide on this verb. Nearly every early witness reads <hi type="italic">phoresōmen</hi>, the aorist subjunctive — "let us wear," a summons — while a smaller group reads <hi type="italic">phoresomen</hi>, the future indicative, "we shall wear." A single letter separates them, omega from omicron, and by the first century those two vowels had fallen together in pronunciation, so a scribe writing from dictation could not have heard which one was spoken. The external evidence favors the subjunctive, and modern critical editions print it; the context favors the future, and most English translations print that, since everything surrounding verse 49 is declarative — what will happen to the dead and the living, not what the Corinthians are told to do. Under the subjunctive the typology itself does not change, but its last step becomes an exhortation: having borne the one image, bear the other. The chapter ends in exhortation in either case (verse 58).</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.15.50-1Cor.15.57">
          <title>Verses 50-57: The Mystery — Transformation, Victory, and the Defeat of Death</title>
          <p>"I tell you this, brothers: flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God, nor does the perishable inherit the imperishable" (15:50).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">touto de phēmi, adelphoi</hi> ("this I say, brothers") introduces a solemn declaration. The phrase <hi type="italic">sarx kai haima</hi> ("flesh and blood") is a Semitic idiom for human nature in its present, mortal condition. It does not mean "physical matter" in the abstract but "the human being as constituted for earthly existence" — the <hi type="italic">psychikon</hi> mode of life. This mortal human nature <hi type="italic">basileian theou klēronomēsai ou dynatai</hi> — "is not able to inherit the kingdom of God." The verb <hi type="italic">klēronomeō</hi> ("to inherit, to receive as an inheritance, to obtain possession of") draws on the language of inheritance that runs through the scriptures. In the LXX, <hi type="italic">klēronomeō</hi> describes Israel's inheritance of the promised land: Deuteronomy 1:39, "your children... they shall go in there, and to them I will give it, and they shall possess it" (<hi type="italic">autoi klēronomēsousin autēn</hi>). The kingdom of God is the inheritance that the present mortal body is not equipped to receive. Transformation is necessary.</p>
          <p>The parallel clause — <hi type="italic">oude hē phthora tēn aphtharsian klēronomei</hi> — "nor does the perishable inherit the imperishable" — restates the same point in abstract terms. Perishability and imperishability are incompatible modes of existence. The one cannot simply become the other without transformation. The body must be changed.</p>
          <p>"Behold! I tell you a mystery. We shall not all sleep, but we shall all be changed" (15:51).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">mystērion</hi> ("mystery, hidden truth, divine secret") describes something that was previously concealed but is now revealed. In the LXX, the word appears in Daniel 2:28-29 (Theodotion): <hi type="italic">mystērion ho ouk estin sophos... ho dynatos dēlōsai to basilei, all' estin theos en ouranō apokalyptōn mystēria</hi> — "there is no wise man... who is able to declare the mystery to the king, but there is a God in heaven who reveals mysteries." A <hi type="italic">mystērion</hi> is not merely unknown information but divinely concealed truth that only God can reveal. Paul presents himself as the revealer of a divine secret.</p>
          <p>The content: <hi type="italic">pantes ou koimēthēsometha, pantes de allagēsometha</hi> — "we shall not all fall asleep, but we shall all be changed." The first <hi type="italic">pantes</hi> ("all") with the negation means "not all of us will die" — some members of the community will be alive when the Lord returns. The second <hi type="italic">pantes</hi> with <hi type="italic">allagēsometha</hi> ("we shall be changed") means all will undergo transformation — both the dead who are raised and the living who survive until the <hi type="italic">parousia</hi>. The verb <hi type="italic">allasso</hi> ("to change, to transform, to alter") describes a fundamental transformation of the body's mode of existence — from the <hi type="italic">psychikon</hi> to the <hi type="italic">pneumatikon</hi>, from the perishable to the imperishable.</p>
          <p>"In a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trumpet. For the trumpet will sound, and the dead will be raised imperishable, and we shall be changed" (15:52).</p>
          <p>The speed of the transformation: <hi type="italic">en atomo, en rhipe ophthalmou</hi> — "in an indivisible instant, in a blink of the eye." The word <hi type="italic">atomos</hi> ("indivisible, that which cannot be cut") describes the smallest conceivable unit of time — a moment so brief it cannot be divided further. The transformation is not a gradual process but an instantaneous event.</p>
          <p>The signal: <hi type="italic">en te eschate salpingi</hi> — "at the last trumpet." The word <hi type="italic">salpinx</hi> ("trumpet") has deep roots in Israel's scriptural tradition. In the LXX, the trumpet signals divine intervention. At Sinai: <hi type="italic">egenonto phōnai kai astrapai kai nephelē gnophōdēs ep' orous Sina, phōnē tēs salpingos echei mega</hi> — "there were voices and lightning and a dark cloud on Mount Sinai; the sound of the trumpet was very loud" (Exodus 19:16). The trumpet summoned Israel to the meeting with God. In Joel 2:1: <hi type="italic">salpísate salpingi en Siōn</hi> — "blow a trumpet in Zion" — the trumpet announces the Day of the Lord. In Isaiah 27:13: <hi type="italic">salpiggi megalē</hi> — "with a great trumpet" — the trumpet signals the gathering of the scattered people. The "last trumpet" indicates the final intervention of God in history — the moment when the present age gives way to the age to come.</p>
          <p>The sequence: <hi type="italic">salpisei gar, kai hoi nekroi egerthēsontai aphthartoi, kai hēmeis allagēsometha</hi> — "for the trumpet will sound, and the dead will be raised imperishable, and we shall be changed." The dead are raised first (<hi type="italic">egerthēsontai</hi>, future passive — God raises them), and they are raised <hi type="italic">aphthartoi</hi> ("imperishable") — already transformed, already in their new mode of existence. Then the living are changed (<hi type="italic">allagēsometha</hi>, future passive — God changes them). Both groups undergo transformation; neither enters the kingdom in their present form.</p>
          <p>"For this perishable body must put on imperishability, and this mortal body must put on immortality" (15:53).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">endysasthai</hi> ("to put on, to clothe oneself with") uses the metaphor of putting on clothing. The perishable body "puts on" imperishability as one puts on a garment. The mortal body "puts on" immortality. The image is not of exchange (discarding one body and receiving another) but of transformation (the existing body being clothed with a new quality). The verb <hi type="italic">dei</hi> ("must") indicates divine necessity: this transformation is not optional; it is required by the nature of the kingdom the body must inhabit.</p>
          <p>"When the perishable puts on the imperishable, and the mortal puts on immortality, then shall come to pass the saying that is written: 'Death is swallowed up in victory'" (15:54).</p>
          <p>Paul quotes Isaiah 25:8. The Hebrew reads: <hi type="italic">billa' hammawet laneetsach</hi> — "he will swallow up death forever." The LXX renders it differently: <hi type="italic">katiepien ho thanatos ischysas</hi> — "death, having prevailed, swallowed [them]" — nearly reversing the sense. Paul's version follows the Hebrew more closely: <hi type="italic">katepothe ho thanatos eis nikos</hi> — "death was swallowed up in/into victory." The verb <hi type="italic">katapino</hi> ("to swallow up, to devour, to consume") in the passive means death itself is consumed. The hunter becomes the hunted; the devourer is devoured. The word <hi type="italic">nikos</hi> ("victory") replaces the Hebrew <hi type="italic">laneetsach</hi> ("forever") and the LXX's <hi type="italic">ischysas</hi> ("having prevailed"). Death, which has reigned since Adam (v. 21), is finally consumed by victory.</p>
          <p>The broader context of Isaiah 25 is crucial for understanding Paul's use. Isaiah 25:6-8 describes a banquet on Mount Zion: "On this mountain the Lord of hosts will make for all peoples a feast of rich food, a feast of well-aged wine... And he will swallow up on this mountain the covering that is cast over all peoples, the veil that is spread over all nations. He will swallow up death forever." The swallowing of death occurs in the context of God's eschatological feast — the banquet for all peoples at the consummation. Paul situates the defeat of death within this prophetic vision: when the mortal puts on immortality, the prophecy of Isaiah 25 reaches its fulfillment.</p>
          <p>"O death, where is your victory? O death, where is your sting?" (15:55).</p>
          <p>Paul quotes Hosea 13:14, but with significant adaptation. The LXX of Hosea 13:14 reads: <hi type="italic">pou hē dikē sou, thanate? pou to kentron sou, hade?</hi> — "where is your penalty, O death? Where is your sting, O Hades?" Paul adapts the quotation: <hi type="italic">pou sou, thanate, to nikos? pou sou, thanate, to kentron?</hi> — "where, O death, is your victory? Where, O death, is your sting?" Paul substitutes <hi type="italic">nikos</hi> ("victory") for <hi type="italic">dikē</hi> ("penalty/justice") and addresses both taunts to <hi type="italic">thanatos</hi> ("death") rather than dividing them between death and Hades.</p>
          <p>The original context of Hosea 13 is a passage of divine judgment against Ephraim (the northern kingdom). God threatens destruction: "I will be your death, O death; I will be your plague, O Sheol" (Hosea 13:14b, in some readings). The verse can be read either as a threat (God summoning death against Israel) or as a promise (God defeating death on Israel's behalf). Paul reads it as a promise and places it at the climax of the resurrection argument: death, which has been humanity's enemy since Adam, is now taunted as a defeated foe. The sting has been removed; the victory has been taken away.</p>
          <p>"The sting of death is sin, and the power of sin is the law" (15:56).</p>
          <p>Paul identifies the mechanisms: <hi type="italic">to de kentron tou thanatou hē hamartia, hē de dynamis tēs hamartias ho nomos</hi> — "the sting of death is sin, and the power of sin is the law." The word <hi type="italic">kentron</hi> ("sting, goad, point") is the instrument by which death inflicts its wound. Death's weapon is sin — it is through sin that death entered (v. 21, through Adam) and through sin that death holds its power. The second clause — "the power of sin is the law" — is compressed and points to the argument Paul develops more fully in Romans 7: the law, which is holy and good in itself, is exploited by sin as the instrument of condemnation. The law reveals sin; sin uses the law to condemn; condemnation leads to death. The chain is: law exposes sin, sin produces death, death reigns. The resurrection breaks this chain by defeating death, the final link.</p>
          <p>"But thanks be to God, who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ" (15:57).</p>
          <p>The doxology: <hi type="italic">to de theo charis to didonti hēmin to nikos dia tou kyriou hēmōn Iēsou Christou</hi> — "thanks be to God who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ." The verb <hi type="italic">didonti</hi> ("who gives") is the present participle: God is giving the victory — it is an ongoing gift, not a one-time past event. The victory comes <hi type="italic">dia tou kyriou hēmōn Iēsou Christou</hi> ("through our Lord Jesus Christ") — Christ is the agent through whom God accomplishes the defeat of death. The <hi type="italic">nikos</hi> ("victory") that death lost (v. 55) is the <hi type="italic">nikos</hi> that God gives to the community through Christ.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.15.58">
          <title>Verse 58: The Concluding Exhortation</title>
          <p>"Therefore, my beloved brothers, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, knowing that in the Lord your labor is not in vain" (15:58).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">hōste</hi> ("therefore, so then") draws the practical conclusion from the entire chapter. The address <hi type="italic">adelphoi mou agapētoi</hi> ("my beloved brothers") is the warmest form of address in the letter — Paul speaks to people he loves, calling them to faithfulness on the basis of the resurrection he has just argued for.</p>
          <p>Three imperatives follow:</p>
          <p>First, <hi type="italic">hedraioi ginesthe</hi> ("be steadfast, be firm, be settled"). The adjective <hi type="italic">hedraios</hi> ("firm, steadfast, settled, seated") describes a person who does not move from their position. In 7:37, Paul used the word for the person who is "firm in heart" (<hi type="italic">hedraios en tē kardia</hi>) regarding their decision about marriage. The community is to be firm in their confession of the resurrection.</p>
          <p>Second, <hi type="italic">ametakinētoi</hi> ("immovable"). The adjective <hi type="italic">ametakinētos</hi> ("not to be moved, unshakeable") intensifies the first: not only firm but unmovable. No argument, no cultural pressure, no philosophical objection should dislodge them from the resurrection faith.</p>
          <p>Third, <hi type="italic">perisseuontes en to ergo tou kyriou pantote</hi> ("always abounding in the work of the Lord"). The verb <hi type="italic">perisseuo</hi> ("to abound, to overflow, to exceed, to be in abundance") describes an excess, a going-beyond-what-is-required. The community is to pour itself abundantly into the work of the Lord — not merely doing the minimum but overflowing with labor for the Lord's purposes. The word <hi type="italic">pantote</hi> ("always, at all times") makes the abounding constant.</p>
          <p>The ground for these imperatives: <hi type="italic">eidotes hoti ho kopos hymōn ouk estin kenos en kyriō</hi> — "knowing that your labor is not empty/vain in the Lord." The word <hi type="italic">kopos</hi> ("labor, toil, hard work") echoes Paul's own <hi type="italic">kopos</hi> in verse 10: "I labored (<hi type="italic">ekopiasa</hi>) more than all of them." The word <hi type="italic">kenos</hi> ("empty, vain, without content, without result") reaches back through the entire chapter: the possibility of believing in vain (<hi type="italic">eikē</hi>, v. 2), the emptiness of preaching if Christ has not been raised (<hi type="italic">kenon</hi>, v. 14), the emptiness of faith (<hi type="italic">kenē</hi>, v. 14), the vanity (<hi type="italic">mataia</hi>, v. 17) of faith without resurrection. The chapter opened with the threat of emptiness; it closes with the assurance of fullness. Because Christ has been raised, because the dead will be raised, because death will be destroyed, because God will be all in all — because all of this is true, the community's labor is not <hi type="italic">kenos</hi>. It has substance. It has content. It has a future. It will bear fruit.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">en kyriō</hi> ("in the Lord") qualifies both the labor and its non-emptiness. It is labor done in connection with the Lord — in his service, in his power, in his sphere of authority. And it is "not empty" specifically because it is "in the Lord" — the Lord who was raised, who is the firstfruits, who is the life-giving Spirit, who will return, who will subject all things, who will destroy death. The Lord's resurrection guarantees that labor done in connection with him has eternal significance.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.15">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 15 is the theological foundation of the entire letter. Everything Paul has argued in chapters 1-14 presupposes the resurrection, but the resurrection itself has not been stated and defended until now. The chapter addresses a specific crisis — some in the community deny the general resurrection of the dead (v. 12) — but the argument Paul constructs in response reaches far beyond the immediate crisis to ground the community's existence, ethics, worship, and hope in the reality of Christ's resurrection.</p>
          <p>The chapter's architecture is clear and cumulative:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">First (vv. 1-11): The gospel foundation.</hi> Paul states the <hi type="italic">kerygma</hi> — Christ died, was buried, was raised, appeared — as received tradition shared by all the apostles. This section establishes the historical basis: the resurrection is not Paul's private claim but the shared testimony of hundreds of witnesses, most of whom are still alive. The section also introduces the conditionality that runs through the chapter: the community is being saved "if you hold fast" and "unless you believed in vain" (v. 2). Salvation is ongoing and conditional on maintaining the content of the faith.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Second (vv. 12-19): The consequences of denial.</hi> Paul demonstrates through a chain of logical consequences that denying the general resurrection necessarily entails denying Christ's resurrection, which in turn empties the preaching, empties the faith, makes the apostles false witnesses, leaves the community in its sins, consigns the dead to destruction, and renders Christians the most pitiable of all people. The argument is designed to show the deniers that their position is not a minor adjustment but a total collapse of the faith.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Third (vv. 20-28): The cosmic scope of the resurrection.</hi> Christ is the firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep — the first instance of a harvest that will include all who belong to him. The Adam/Christ typology grounds the resurrection in the scriptural narrative: death came through one man (Adam); life comes through one man (Christ). The section then traces the cosmic sequence: Christ's resurrection, the believers' resurrection at his <hi type="italic">parousia</hi>, then the <hi type="italic">telos</hi> when Christ delivers the kingdom to the Father after destroying every hostile power, including death itself. The endpoint is God being "all in all."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Fourth (vv. 29-34): Practical evidence.</hi> Paul appeals to the community's own practice (baptism for the dead) and his own experience (daily danger) as evidence that the resurrection is assumed by the very life the community and the apostle lead. If there is no resurrection, these practices and this suffering are senseless. The section includes a sharp warning against the corrupting influence of those who deny the resurrection.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Fifth (vv. 35-49): The nature of the resurrection body.</hi> Paul addresses the skeptic's question about what kind of body the dead will have. Through the seed analogy, the diversity of flesh, and the variety of celestial and terrestrial bodies, he demonstrates that God is not limited to a single form of embodiment. The body that is sown (perishable, dishonorable, weak, natural) is not the body that is raised (imperishable, glorious, powerful, spiritual). The Adam/Christ typology returns: as humanity bears the image of the dusty Adam, it will bear the image of the heavenly Christ.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Sixth (vv. 50-57): The mystery of transformation and the defeat of death.</hi> Flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom; transformation is necessary. The living will be changed; the dead will be raised imperishable. The prophecies of Isaiah 25 and Hosea 13 are fulfilled: death is swallowed in victory; death's sting is removed. God gives the victory through Christ.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Seventh (v. 58): The practical exhortation.</hi> Because the resurrection is real, the community's labor is not in vain. They are to be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the Lord's work.</p>
          <p>Several threads from the letter reach important expressions in this chapter.</p>
          <p>The vocabulary of emptiness and vanity — <hi type="italic">eike</hi> ("in vain," v. 2), <hi type="italic">kenos</hi> ("empty," vv. 10, 14, 58), <hi type="italic">mataia</hi> ("futile," v. 17) — ties the chapter to the letter's persistent concern with what has real substance versus what is hollow. The Corinthians boast in their knowledge (8:1), their gifts (chapters 12-14), their teachers (chapters 1-4). Paul has been arguing throughout the letter that these things are empty without love (13:1-3), without edification (14:6-12), without regard for the weak (8:9-11). Now he reveals the ultimate source of emptiness: if Christ has not been raised, everything is empty. And the ultimate source of substance: if Christ has been raised, nothing is empty. The resurrection is the dividing line between vanity and meaning.</p>
          <p>The present tense of <hi type="italic">sozesthe</hi> ("you are being saved," v. 2) connects to 1:18 (<hi type="italic">hoi sozomenoi</hi>, "those who are being saved"). Salvation in this letter is never presented as a completed past event that cannot be reversed. It is an ongoing process. The community is being saved — present tense, not past. The condition for this ongoing salvation is holding fast to the gospel (<hi type="italic">ei katechete</hi>, v. 2). The possibility of believing in vain (<hi type="italic">eike episteusate</hi>, v. 2) is a genuine possibility, not a rhetorical decoration. Paul raises it because the denial of the resurrection by "some among you" demonstrates that it can happen: people can receive the gospel, stand in it for a time, and then adopt a position that empties their faith of its content.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">katargeo</hi> ("to abolish, to render inoperative"), which appeared in 13:8-11 for the abolishing of partial gifts when the perfect comes, reappears in 15:24-26 for the abolishing of hostile powers. The same verb describes both events because both belong to the same consummation. When the <hi type="italic">telos</hi> arrives, both the partial gifts (prophecy, tongues, knowledge) and the hostile powers (every rule, authority, power, and death) are <hi type="italic">katargeitai</hi> — rendered inoperative, made obsolete, abolished. The partial gifts are abolished because they are no longer needed (the complete has come). The hostile powers are abolished because they are defeated (Christ has subjected them). Both abolitions serve the same end: God being all in all.</p>
          <p>The Adam/Christ typology of verses 21-22 and 45-49 draws entirely on Genesis, as the methodology requires. Paul reads Genesis as the foundation of the human condition: death came through Adam's transgression; mortality is the inheritance Adam bequeathed to all who share his nature. The resurrection of Christ introduces a new humanity — a second representative human whose characteristics will define those who belong to him, just as Adam's characteristics defined those who belonged to him. The movement is from the earthy to the heavenly, from the <hi type="italic">psychikon</hi> to the <hi type="italic">pneumatikon</hi>, from the image of Adam to the image of Christ.</p>
          <p>The Old Testament citations in verses 25-27, 54-55 demonstrate that Paul's resurrection argument is not a departure from Israel's scriptures but a fulfillment of them. Psalm 110:1 (the enemies under the feet), Psalm 8:6 (all things in subjection), Isaiah 25:8 (death swallowed up), and Hosea 13:14 (death's sting removed) all describe events that, in their original contexts, pointed toward God's ultimate victory over the powers that oppose his people. Paul reads them as prophecies of what Christ's resurrection has inaugurated and what his <hi type="italic">parousia</hi> will complete. The scriptures are <hi type="italic">kata tas graphas</hi> ("according to the Scriptures," vv. 3-4) — the resurrection is not a novelty but the fulfillment of what the prophets and psalmists anticipated.</p>
          <p>The chapter's treatment of death as the "last enemy" (v. 26) connects to the Israel typology of chapter 10, where the wilderness generation was "overthrown" (<hi type="italic">katestrothēsan</hi>, 10:5) and "destroyed" (<hi type="italic">apolonto</hi>, 10:10) despite their privileges. Death has been the final word for all who have preceded the resurrection — even for God's own people. The promise of chapter 15 is that death will no longer have the last word. Christ's resurrection has begun the process of death's destruction; the believers' resurrection at the <hi type="italic">parousia</hi> will continue it; the <hi type="italic">telos</hi> will complete it. The God who is "not a God of the dead but of the living" (as Jesus argued in the tradition recorded in Mark 12:27) is bringing all things to a state in which death no longer reigns.</p>
          <p>The connection between the resurrection and ethics, which runs implicitly through the chapter, becomes explicit in verse 34 ("sober up and stop sinning") and verse 58 ("be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord"). The resurrection is not merely a doctrine to be believed but a reality that shapes behavior. If the dead are raised, then the self-denial Paul modeled in chapter 9 is worthwhile, the building up of the community Paul urged in chapters 12-14 has eternal significance, and the care for the weak that Paul demanded in chapter 8 is not wasted effort. If the dead are not raised, then the hedonists are right: "let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we die" (v. 32). The resurrection is the warrant for the entire ethical program of the letter.</p>
          <p>The closing exhortation — "knowing that in the Lord your labor is not in vain" (v. 58) — answers the opening warning — "unless you believed in vain" (v. 2). The chapter begins with a conditional possibility (your faith may be in vain) and ends with a settled assurance (your labor is not in vain). Between these two statements, Paul has established the resurrection of Christ, demonstrated its inseparability from the general resurrection, described the cosmic scope of what the resurrection accomplishes, addressed the nature of the resurrection body, and proclaimed the defeat of death. The community that holds fast to this gospel — that does not let go, that does not reduce the resurrection to a metaphor or deny it altogether — can be assured that everything they do in the Lord has meaning. The resurrection is the difference between vanity and substance, between emptiness and fullness, between the pitiable existence of verse 19 and the victorious existence of verse 57. The community that confesses the risen Christ is the community whose labor endures.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="1Cor.16">
        <title>1 Corinthians 16 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.16">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (1 Corinthians 15):</hi> Paul turned from the practical regulations for the assembly's worship (chapter 14) to the foundational question of bodily resurrection. He opened with the formula <hi type="italic">gnorizo de hymin</hi> ("now I make known to you") and restated the gospel he had preached to the Corinthians: "Christ died for our sins in accordance with the Scriptures, that he was buried, that he was raised on the third day in accordance with the Scriptures" (15:3-4). He then listed the witnesses to the resurrection — Cephas, the twelve, more than five hundred brothers at once, James, all the apostles, and finally Paul himself, "as to one untimely born" (15:5-8). Having established the factual basis, Paul addressed a problem: "how can some of you say that there is no resurrection of the dead?" (15:12). He then constructed a devastating chain of consequences: if there is no resurrection, Christ has not been raised; if Christ has not been raised, Paul's preaching is empty and the Corinthians' faith is empty; they are still in their sins; those who have fallen asleep in Christ have perished; and believers are the most pitiable of all people (15:13-19). But — <hi type="italic">nun de Christos egegertai ek nekron</hi>, "but in fact Christ has been raised from the dead" (15:20) — and he has become the <hi type="italic">aparche</hi> ("firstfruits") of those who have fallen asleep. The word <hi type="italic">aparche</hi> carries the weight of Israel's harvest theology: the firstfruits offered to God (Leviticus 23:10-11) are the pledge and guarantee of the full harvest to follow. Christ's resurrection is not an isolated event but the first sheaf of the coming harvest of all who belong to him.</p>
          <p>Paul then developed an Adam-Christ typology: as in Adam all die, so in Christ all shall be made alive, but each in proper order (<hi type="italic">tagma</hi>) — Christ the firstfruits, then those who are Christ's at his coming, then the end, when he delivers the kingdom to God the Father (15:22-28). He drew on Psalm 110:1 ("he must reign until he has put all his enemies under his feet") and Psalm 8:6 ("he has put all things under his feet") to describe the subjection of all powers, including death itself, the last enemy. He addressed the nature of the resurrection body through the analogy of seeds (15:35-44): what is sown is not the body that will be, just as a bare grain of wheat is not the stalk that grows from it. The body sown perishable is raised imperishable; sown in dishonor, raised in glory; sown in weakness, raised in power; sown a natural body (<hi type="italic">soma psychikon</hi>), raised a spiritual body (<hi type="italic">soma pneumatikon</hi>). Drawing on Genesis 2:7, Paul contrasted the first Adam, who became a living soul (<hi type="italic">psychen zosan</hi>), with the last Adam, who became a life-giving spirit (<hi type="italic">pneuma zoopoioun</hi>) (15:45). The chapter climaxed in a vision of transformation: "we shall not all sleep, but we shall all be changed" (15:51) — in a moment, at the last trumpet, the dead raised imperishable and the living transformed, mortality swallowed up by life. Paul drew on Isaiah 25:8 ("death is swallowed up in victory") and Hosea 13:14 ("O death, where is your sting?") for his triumphant declaration of victory over death through the Lord Jesus Christ (15:54-57).</p>
          <p>The chapter's final verse served as the bridge to everything that follows: "Therefore, my beloved brothers, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, forasmuch as you know that your labor is not in vain in the Lord" (15:58). The word <hi type="italic">hoste</hi> ("therefore") draws the practical conclusion from the resurrection argument. If the resurrection is real, if death is defeated, if the believers' labor is not in vain — then the community must be <hi type="italic">hedraios</hi> ("steadfast, settled, firm"), <hi type="italic">ametakinetos</hi> ("immovable, not to be shifted"), and <hi type="italic">perisseuontes</hi> ("abounding, overflowing") in the work of the Lord (<hi type="italic">to ergon tou kyriou</hi>). The word <hi type="italic">ergon</hi> ("work") and the verb <hi type="italic">kopos</hi> ("labor, toil") describe concrete activity, not abstract belief. The resurrection hope produces practical labor.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">This is the final chapter of the letter.</hi> There is no subsequent chapter to identify as "what the author argues immediately after." Instead, the letter closes by returning to and completing the concerns that opened it: the unity of the community with the wider body of believers, the proper treatment of workers and leaders, the supremacy of love as the governing principle, and the eschatological expectation that frames everything.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 16 connects to what precedes it:</hi> Chapter 16 is not a postscript tacked onto the theological argument of chapter 15. It is the concrete expression of the "work of the Lord" in which 15:58 commanded the Corinthians to abound. The "labor" that is "not in vain in the Lord" takes immediate, tangible form: collecting money for impoverished saints in Jerusalem (vv. 1-4), receiving and supporting workers who carry the gospel between communities (vv. 5-12), standing firm and acting in love (vv. 13-14), submitting to those who have devoted themselves to service (vv. 15-18), and maintaining the bonds of affection that hold the scattered assemblies together (vv. 19-24). The resurrection hope of chapter 15 is not meant to produce passive waiting; it is meant to produce the kind of active, other-directed, community-building work that chapter 16 describes.</p>
          <p>The chapter also functions as the closing bracket of the letter's structure. In 1:1-9, Paul described the Corinthians as "the assembly of God that is in Corinth," sanctified, enriched with gifts, awaiting the revealing of the Lord Jesus Christ, and held by God's faithfulness. The letter then exposed problem after problem: factionalism, sexual immorality, lawsuits, disordered worship, abuse of gifts, denial of resurrection. Chapter 16 returns to the opening themes: the community's connection to other assemblies (vv. 1, 19-20), the proper relationship to leaders like Paul, Apollos, and Timothy (vv. 5-12), the call to love (v. 14, echoing chapter 13), and the expectation of the Lord's coming (v. 22, echoing 1:7-8). The letter ends where it began — with a community that belongs to God, is connected to all the assemblies, and is waiting for its Lord.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.16.1-1Cor.16.4">
          <title>Verses 1-4: The Collection for the Saints in Jerusalem</title>
          <p>"Now concerning the collection for the saints: as I directed the churches of Galatia, so you also are to do" (16:1).</p>
          <p>The formula <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> ("now concerning") signals that Paul is addressing another question from the Corinthians' letter. This same formula has introduced each of the topics the Corinthians raised: <hi type="italic">peri de hon egrapsate</hi> ("now concerning what you wrote," 7:1, marriage), <hi type="italic">peri de ton parthenon</hi> ("now concerning the virgins," 7:25), <hi type="italic">peri de ton eidolothyton</hi> ("now concerning food offered to idols," 8:1), <hi type="italic">peri de ton pneumatikon</hi> ("now concerning spiritual things," 12:1). The collection was evidently a topic the Corinthians asked about, perhaps because Paul had mentioned it in an earlier communication or because they had heard about it from other communities.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">logeia</hi> ("collection, gathering of money") appears only here and in verse 2 in the entire New Testament. It is attested in Egyptian papyri as a term for a collection of money, especially for religious purposes — contributions gathered for temples or public works. Paul uses this common administrative word for what is, in fact, a deeply significant act. The <hi type="italic">logeias tes eis tous hagious</hi> ("the collection for the saints") identifies the recipients as "the saints" (<hi type="italic">tous hagious</hi>) — the holy ones, the community in Jerusalem. In Romans 15:25-27, Paul explains the rationale for this collection more fully: "At present, however, I am going to Jerusalem bringing aid to the saints. For Macedonia and Achaia have been pleased to make some contribution for the poor among the saints at Jerusalem. For they were pleased to do it, and indeed they owe it to them. For if the Gentiles have come to share in their spiritual blessings, they ought also to be of service to them in material blessings." The collection is not merely charitable giving. It is an act of reciprocity and recognition: the Gentile communities acknowledge their spiritual debt to the Jewish believers in Jerusalem from whom the gospel came, and they express that acknowledgment through material support.</p>
          <p>For a community riven by internal factionalism — "I am of Paul," "I am of Apollos," "I am of Cephas" (1:12) — the collection represents an expansion of vision. The Corinthians have been directed inward, competing with one another, evaluating leaders against one another, failing to care even for one another at the common meal (11:20-22). Now Paul directs them outward, toward a community they have never met, in a city they may never visit, bound to them only by the shared gospel. The love that "does not seek its own" (13:5) must extend beyond the walls of their own assembly.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">hosper dietaxa tais ekklesiais tes Galatias</hi> ("as I directed the churches of Galatia") establishes that the collection is not a local Corinthian project. Paul issued the same instructions (<hi type="italic">dietaxa</hi>, aorist of <hi type="italic">diatasso</hi>, "to arrange, to order, to direct") to the Galatian churches. The Corinthians are part of a network. The appeal to what other assemblies are doing echoes Paul's earlier appeals to universal practice: "as in all the churches of the saints" (14:33), "we have no such practice, nor do the churches of God" (11:16). The Corinthians do not operate in isolation.</p>
          <p>"On the first day of every week, each of you is to put something aside and store it up, as he may prosper, so that there will be no collecting when I come" (16:2).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">kata mian sabbatou</hi> ("on the first [day] of every week") uses <hi type="italic">sabbatou</hi> (genitive of <hi type="italic">sabbaton</hi>) to mean "week" — the first day of the week. This is the day of resurrection: "on the first day of the week" (<hi type="italic">te de mia ton sabbaton</hi>) Mary Magdalene came to the tomb and found it empty (Matthew 28:1; Mark 16:2; Luke 24:1; John 20:1). The early communities gathered on this day, as the <hi type="italic">Didache</hi> (14:1) later attests: "On the Lord's own day, gather and break bread and give thanks." Paul's instruction assumes a regular gathering on the first day, and he ties the practice of giving to this regular rhythm.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">hekastos hymon</hi> ("each one of you") individualizes the responsibility. Not merely the wealthy, not merely the leaders — each person participates according to their means. The phrase <hi type="italic">hoti ean euodotai</hi> ("as he may prosper, whatever success he has had") uses the verb <hi type="italic">euodoo</hi> ("to prosper, to succeed, to have a good journey"), a word that can describe both material success and general well-being. The instruction is proportional: each person sets aside according to what they have received that week. The verb <hi type="italic">thesaurizon</hi> ("storing up, treasuring") indicates an ongoing accumulation — each week, something is set aside and stored, so that the total grows over time.</p>
          <p>The practical purpose clause is revealing: <hi type="italic">hina me hotan eltho tote logeiai ginontai</hi> ("so that when I come, collections need not be made at that time"). Paul does not want the collection to be a high-pressure event at his arrival. He does not want to arrive and have to organize a collection drive. The money should already be gathered, week by week, through the regular practice of the community. This reflects both practical wisdom and theological principle: giving is not an extraordinary event produced by an apostle's presence but an ordinary practice embedded in the community's weekly rhythm.</p>
          <p>"And when I arrive, I will send those whom you accredit by letter to carry your gift to Jerusalem" (16:3).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">dokimasete</hi> ("you accredit, approve, test") comes from <hi type="italic">dokimazo</hi> ("to test, to examine, to approve after testing"). The word appeared in 3:13 ("the fire will test [<hi type="italic">dokimasei</hi>] what sort of work each one has done"), in 11:28 ("let a person examine [<hi type="italic">dokimazeto</hi>] himself"), and in 16:3 it applies to the selection of delegates. The Corinthians themselves — not Paul — choose the delegates. Paul's phrase <hi type="italic">di' epistolon</hi> ("by letters, through letters") describes letters of accreditation that the delegates would carry to Jerusalem, introducing and authorizing them. This was standard first-century practice: travelers carried letters from known communities to authenticate their identity and mission.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">charin</hi> ("gift, grace, gracious gift") used for the collection is striking. It is a cognate of <hi type="italic">charis</hi> ("grace"), the word Paul used for God's unmerited gift to the Corinthians (1:4). The money being sent to Jerusalem is not called a tax or an obligation but a <hi type="italic">charis</hi> — a gracious gift that mirrors the grace they themselves have received. In 2 Corinthians 8:9, Paul will make this connection explicit: "For you know the grace (<hi type="italic">charin</hi>) of our Lord Jesus Christ, that though he was rich, yet for your sake he became poor, so that you by his poverty might become rich." The collection is an act of grace modeled on the grace of Christ.</p>
          <p>"If it seems advisable that I should go also, they will accompany me" (16:4).</p>
          <p>Paul leaves open the possibility that he himself might travel to Jerusalem with the delegates. The conditional <hi type="italic">ean de axion e</hi> ("if it is worthy, if it seems fitting") uses the adjective <hi type="italic">axios</hi> ("worthy, fitting, appropriate"). The decision depends on whether the gift is substantial enough to warrant Paul's personal involvement. The phrase <hi type="italic">syn emoi poreusontai</hi> ("they will travel with me") indicates that even if Paul goes, the Corinthian delegates still accompany the gift. The community's own representatives will be present.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.16.5-1Cor.16.9">
          <title>Verses 5-9: Paul's Travel Plans</title>
          <p>"I will visit you after passing through Macedonia, for I intend to pass through Macedonia" (16:5).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">eleusomai</hi> ("I will come") states Paul's intention. The phrase <hi type="italic">hotan Makedonian dielthon</hi> ("when I pass through Macedonia") reveals the planned route: from Ephesus (where Paul is writing, v. 8) north through Macedonia (the province containing Thessalonica and Philippi) and then south to Corinth in Achaia. The alternative would have been a direct sea crossing from Ephesus to Corinth across the Aegean. Paul's choice of the overland route through Macedonia may serve multiple purposes: visiting other communities, collecting from the Macedonian churches (2 Corinthians 8:1-5 later describes the generosity of the Macedonians), and allowing time for the Corinthians to prepare.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">Makedonian dielthon</hi> ("passing through Macedonia") is echoed by <hi type="italic">Makedonian gar dierchomai</hi> ("for I am passing through Macedonia"). The repetition underscores that the Macedonian visit is part of the plan, not an afterthought. Paul is not merely passing through Corinth on his way elsewhere; he intends Corinth as a destination.</p>
          <p>"And perhaps I will stay with you or even spend the winter, so that you may help me on my journey, wherever I go" (16:6).</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">tychos</hi> ("perhaps") introduces uncertainty. Paul may stay (<hi type="italic">parameno</hi>, "remain alongside, stay with") or even spend the winter (<hi type="italic">paracheimazo</hi>, "to winter, to spend the cold season"). Winter travel in the Mediterranean was dangerous — sea travel largely ceased from November through February due to storms. If Paul arrives in Corinth before winter, he would naturally remain until spring, when travel resumed.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">propempsete</hi> ("you may help me on my journey, you may send me on my way") uses <hi type="italic">propempo</hi>, a word with technical significance in the early communities. It describes the practice of equipping a departing traveler with provisions, companions, and commendation letters for the next stage of their journey. The word appears in Acts 15:3 (the church in Antioch "sending on their way" Paul and Barnabas), Romans 15:24 (Paul hopes the Romans will <hi type="italic">propempsete</hi> him to Spain), 3 John 6 ("you will do well to send them on their journey in a manner worthy of God"), and Titus 3:13 ("do your best to help Zenas and Apollos on their way"). The practice expressed the interdependence of the communities: no worker traveled alone or unequipped. The receiving community took responsibility for the departing worker's next stage.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">hou ean poreuomai</hi> ("wherever I go") leaves the ultimate destination open. Paul has a general plan but not a fixed itinerary. This flexibility is characteristic of Paul's travel patterns and reflects the conditional quality of all human planning that he will make explicit in the next verse.</p>
          <p>"For I do not want to see you now just in passing. I hope to spend some time with you, if the Lord permits" (16:7).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ou thelo gar hymas arti en parodo idein</hi> ("for I do not want to see you now in passing") explains why Paul is not coming directly: he does not want a brief, superficial visit. The word <hi type="italic">parodos</hi> ("passing by, passage through") describes a brief stop on the way to somewhere else. Given the severity of the problems the letter addresses, a casual visit would not suffice. Paul needs extended time — perhaps the winter — to address the issues in person.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ean ho kyrios epitrepse</hi> ("if the Lord permits") is not a pious formula but a theological conviction. Paul's plans are subordinated to the Lord's direction. The verb <hi type="italic">epitrepo</hi> ("to permit, to allow, to turn over to") places the final authority over Paul's movements with the Lord, not with Paul's own intentions. James 4:15 reflects the same conviction: "instead you ought to say, 'If the Lord wills, we will live and do this or that.'" As it turns out, Paul's plans did change significantly: according to 2 Corinthians, what actually followed was a "painful visit" (2 Corinthians 2:1) and a change in travel plans that some Corinthians accused Paul of unreliability for making (2 Corinthians 1:15-17). The conditional "if the Lord permits" proves to have been more than a formality.</p>
          <p>"But I will stay in Ephesus until Pentecost" (16:8).</p>
          <p>This verse establishes Paul's location at the time of writing: Ephesus, the major city of the province of Asia on the western coast of Asia Minor. The mention of Pentecost (<hi type="italic">tes pentekostes</hi>, "the fiftieth [day]") — the Jewish festival celebrated fifty days after Passover (Leviticus 23:15-16) — serves as a time marker. Paul plans to remain in Ephesus until this festival, then depart for Macedonia and eventually Corinth. If the letter was written in the spring, Paul would be arriving in Corinth in late summer or autumn, with the possibility of wintering there.</p>
          <p>The mention of Pentecost rather than a secular date suggests that the Jewish liturgical calendar still governed Paul's sense of time, even in a predominantly Gentile context. Pentecost — the festival of harvest, the festival that later Jewish tradition associated with the giving of the Torah at Sinai — was Paul's time reference. This is consistent with the man who wrote "Christ our Passover lamb has been sacrificed" (5:7) and who drew naturally on Israel's calendar and festivals as the framework for the community's life.</p>
          <p>"For a wide door for effective work has opened to me, and there are many adversaries" (16:9).</p>
          <p>The metaphor <hi type="italic">thyra gar moi aneogen megale kai energes</hi> ("for a great and effective door has opened for me") describes an opportunity for productive ministry. The word <hi type="italic">thyra</hi> ("door") as a metaphor for opportunity appears in Acts 14:27 ("God had opened a door of faith to the Gentiles") and Colossians 4:3 ("that God may open to us a door for the word"). The adjective <hi type="italic">megale</hi> ("great, large") and <hi type="italic">energes</hi> ("effective, productive, at work") describe an opportunity that is both vast in scope and fruitful in result. Paul's work in Ephesus is flourishing.</p>
          <p>But alongside the opportunity stands opposition: <hi type="italic">kai antikeimenoi polloi</hi> ("and adversaries are many"). The participle <hi type="italic">antikeimenoi</hi> (from <hi type="italic">antikeimai</hi>, "to lie opposite, to oppose, to resist") describes those who actively oppose Paul's work. The word appears in the LXX for enemies and opponents: in Zechariah 3:1, the Satan "stands at the right hand to oppose" (<hi type="italic">antikesthai</hi>) the high priest Joshua. The nature of the opposition is not specified. Acts 19 records the riot of Demetrius the silversmith, who organized the craftsmen of Ephesus against Paul because the gospel was undermining the trade in silver shrines of Artemis (Acts 19:23-41). Paul himself refers to fighting "with beasts at Ephesus" (15:32), whether literally or metaphorically. The juxtaposition of opportunity and opposition is characteristic of Paul's experience: the effectiveness of the work provokes the resistance.</p>
          <p>The two halves of the verse together explain why Paul remains in Ephesus: the opportunity is too significant to abandon, even though the opposition is fierce. The open door holds him; the adversaries do not drive him away. This is itself a demonstration of the steadfastness and immovability that 15:58 commanded.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.16.10-1Cor.16.11">
          <title>Verses 10-11: Instructions Concerning Timothy</title>
          <p>"When Timothy comes, see that you put him at ease among you, for he is doing the work of the Lord, as I am" (16:10).</p>
          <p>The conditional <hi type="italic">ean de elthe Timotheos</hi> ("if/when Timothy comes") uses the subjunctive, indicating some uncertainty about the timing. Timothy has apparently been sent ahead, perhaps on the route through Macedonia (Acts 19:22: "he sent into Macedonia two of those who ministered to him, Timothy and Erastus"), and may arrive in Corinth before Paul's letter or shortly after.</p>
          <p>The instruction <hi type="italic">blepete hina aphobos genetai pros hymas</hi> ("see that he becomes without fear among you") is remarkable. The verb <hi type="italic">blepete</hi> ("see to it, take care, watch out") is an imperative of vigilance. The adverb <hi type="italic">aphobos</hi> ("without fear, fearlessly") indicates that Paul expects Timothy might have reason to be afraid of the Corinthians. This is an extraordinary statement about a community of believers. Why would a fellow worker need to be afraid?</p>
          <p>The answer lies in the Corinthians' established pattern, diagnosed throughout the letter: they evaluate people by worldly standards. The faction problem of chapters 1-4 arose because they compared leaders according to rhetorical skill, wisdom, and impressiveness. They boasted in human teachers (3:21, 4:6). They judged Paul himself as unimpressive: his bodily presence is "weak" and his speech "of no account" (2 Corinthians 10:10, a later description but reflecting an established Corinthian attitude). Timothy, who may have been young (1 Timothy 4:12 says "let no one despise your youth") and perhaps less commanding in presence than Apollos, would be vulnerable to the same dismissive treatment.</p>
          <p>Paul's counter to this anticipated dismissal is a single statement: <hi type="italic">to gar ergon kyriou ergazetai hos kago</hi> ("for he is doing the work of the Lord, as I am"). The phrase <hi type="italic">ergon kyriou</hi> ("work of the Lord") directly echoes 15:58: "always abounding in the work of the Lord (<hi type="italic">to ergon tou kyriou</hi>), forasmuch as you know that your labor is not in vain in the Lord." The verbal repetition is deliberate. The "work of the Lord" that 15:58 commanded the Corinthians to abound in is the same work Timothy is doing. To despise Timothy is to despise the very work the Corinthians themselves have been told to pursue. The phrase <hi type="italic">hos kago</hi> ("as I also") places Timothy's work on the same level as Paul's: the work is the same work, serving the same Lord, regardless of the worker's perceived impressiveness.</p>
          <p>"So let no one despise him. Help him on his way in peace, that he may return to me, for I am expecting him with the brothers" (16:11).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">exoutheneseito</hi> (from <hi type="italic">exoutheneo</hi>, "to despise, to treat with contempt, to count as nothing, to make of no account") is sharp. The word appeared in 1:28, where Paul described God's method of operation: "God chose what is low and despised in the world (<hi type="italic">ta exouthenemena</hi>), even things that are not, to bring to nothing things that are." The Corinthians' God chose the despised; the Corinthians must not despise the workers God has sent. The word also appeared in 6:4 for those who are "of no account in the church." The community that has been warned against evaluating by worldly standards is now explicitly told not to apply those standards to Timothy.</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">propempsate</hi> ("help him on his way, send him on his way") is the same verb used in verse 6 for how the Corinthians should send Paul. Timothy deserves the same treatment Paul expects: provision, companionship, and commendation for his journey. The phrase <hi type="italic">en eirene</hi> ("in peace") specifies the manner: Timothy should leave Corinth at peace, not harassed, not demeaned, not embroiled in factional conflict. The word <hi type="italic">eirene</hi> ("peace") echoes 14:33: "God is not a God of disorder but of peace." The peace that characterizes God's nature should characterize the community's treatment of workers.</p>
          <p>Paul is expecting Timothy back: <hi type="italic">ekdechomai gar auton meta ton adelphon</hi> ("for I am expecting him with the brothers"). The phrase <hi type="italic">meta ton adelphon</hi> ("with the brothers") indicates that other believers are traveling with Timothy or are expected to return with him. The word <hi type="italic">ekdechomai</hi> ("to await, to expect, to look for") appeared in 11:33 ("when you come together to eat, wait for one another") and 16:11 uses it for Paul's expectation of Timothy's return. The community is a network of persons in motion — traveling, being sent, being expected, returning — bound together by the work of the Lord.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.16.12">
          <title>Verse 12: Concerning Apollos</title>
          <p>"Now concerning our brother Apollos, I strongly urged him to visit you with the other brothers, but it was not at all his will to come now. He will come when he has opportunity" (16:12).</p>
          <p>The formula <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> ("now concerning") signals the final question from the Corinthians' letter. They had apparently asked about Apollos — when he would visit, whether he could come to them. The factionalism of chapters 1-4 supplies the background: "I am of Apollos" was one of the slogans (1:12, 3:4). Acts 18:24-28 describes Apollos as "an eloquent man, competent in the Scriptures," who "powerfully refuted the Jews in public, showing by the Scriptures that the Christ was Jesus." He was precisely the kind of rhetorically impressive leader the Corinthians prized. Their request for Apollos may have been driven by genuine affection, or by the factional preference that Paul diagnosed in chapters 1-4, or both.</p>
          <p>Paul's description of Apollos is deliberate: <hi type="italic">Apollou tou adelphou</hi> ("Apollos the brother"). Not "Apollos the teacher," not "Apollos the eloquent," not even "Apollos my fellow apostle." Simply "the brother" (<hi type="italic">tou adelphou</hi>). The title reduces Apollos to the same level as every other member of the community. He is a brother. This is consistent with Paul's argument in chapters 3-4: "What then is Apollos? What is Paul? Servants (<hi type="italic">diakonoi</hi>) through whom you believed, as the Lord assigned to each" (3:5). Apollos is a servant, a brother — not a celebrity, not a faction leader.</p>
          <p>Paul says <hi type="italic">polla parekalesa auton</hi> ("I strongly urged him") — the verb <hi type="italic">parakaleo</hi> ("to urge, to exhort, to encourage, to beseech") indicates that Paul actively encouraged Apollos to visit Corinth. This detail is important because it refutes any suspicion that Paul was preventing Apollos from visiting out of rivalry. Paul wanted Apollos to go. The phrase <hi type="italic">hina elthe pros hymas meta ton adelphon</hi> ("that he should come to you with the brothers") specifies that Apollos would travel with a group, not alone.</p>
          <p>But — <hi type="italic">kai pantos ouk en thelema hina nun elthe</hi> ("and it was not at all [his] will to come now"). The word <hi type="italic">thelema</hi> ("will, desire, intention") is emphatic: <hi type="italic">pantos ouk</hi> ("not at all, by no means"). Apollos's refusal was firm. The subject of <hi type="italic">thelema</hi> is ambiguous in the Greek — it could mean "it was not at all God's will" or "it was not at all his [Apollos's] will." Most naturally, given the immediate context (Paul urged him, but it was not his will), the will is Apollos's own. He chose not to come at this time. Why Apollos declined is not stated. He may have been aware of the faction problem and judged that his presence would inflame rather than heal it. He may have had other commitments. Paul does not explain, nor does he criticize the decision. The closing phrase <hi type="italic">eleusetai de hotan eukairese</hi> ("he will come when he has opportunity") is matter-of-fact. Apollos will come eventually, on his own timetable. The word <hi type="italic">eukaireo</hi> ("to have opportunity, to find a convenient time") indicates that the timing is Apollos's to determine.</p>
          <p>The entire verse dissolves any attempt to play Paul and Apollos against each other. Paul urged Apollos to come. Apollos declined. Paul reports the refusal without complaint. Apollos will come when he can. There is no rivalry, no competition, no jealousy — exactly what Paul argued in 3:5-9: "I planted, Apollos watered, but God gave the growth. So neither he who plants nor he who waters is anything, but only God who gives the growth."</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.16.13-1Cor.16.14">
          <title>Verses 13-14: Five Imperatives</title>
          <p>"Be watchful, stand firm in the faith, act like men, be strong. Let all that you do be done in love" (16:13-14).</p>
          <p>Paul delivers five rapid imperatives that compress the concerns of the entire letter into a single series of commands. The first four are staccato, military in rhythm; the fifth reframes the first four under the governance of love.</p>
          <p>First: <hi type="italic">gregorite</hi> ("be watchful, stay awake, keep watch"). The verb <hi type="italic">gregoreo</hi> ("to be awake, to be vigilant, to watch") is the word used for eschatological watchfulness in the teaching of Jesus: "Stay awake (<hi type="italic">gregoreite</hi>), for you do not know on what day your Lord is coming" (Matthew 24:42); "Watch (<hi type="italic">gregoreite</hi>) therefore, for you know neither the day nor the hour" (Matthew 25:13). In 1 Thessalonians 5:6, Paul used the same word: "so then let us not sleep, as others do, but let us keep awake (<hi type="italic">gregoromen</hi>) and be sober." The command reaches back to the eschatological orientation that opened the letter — the community is "waiting for the revealing (<hi type="italic">apokalypsin</hi>) of our Lord Jesus Christ" (1:7) — and forward to the <hi type="italic">maranatha</hi> cry of verse 22. The watchfulness is not mere alertness to danger; it is the posture of a community expecting its Lord's return.</p>
          <p>Second: <hi type="italic">stekete en te pistei</hi> ("stand firm in the faith"). The verb <hi type="italic">steko</hi> ("to stand, to stand firm, to hold one's ground") describes the posture of someone who will not be moved. The phrase <hi type="italic">en te pistei</hi> ("in the faith") uses the article: "the faith" — not faith as a subjective feeling but "the faith" as the content of what was believed and transmitted. This is the faith of 15:1-4: "the gospel which I preached to you, which also you received, in which also you stand (<hi type="italic">hestekate</hi>)." The verb <hi type="italic">hestekate</hi> ("you stand") in 15:1 and the imperative <hi type="italic">stekete</hi> ("stand firm") in 16:13 use the same root. Standing in the faith means standing in the gospel of Christ's death, burial, and resurrection. The Corinthians who were questioning the resurrection (15:12) needed precisely this command: stand firm in the very gospel that constitutes you.</p>
          <p>Third: <hi type="italic">andrizesthe</hi> ("act like men, be courageous, show yourselves men"). The verb <hi type="italic">andrizomai</hi> ("to act courageously, to play the man") carries the sense of courage and strength. In the LXX, this verb appears as a divine command at moments of crisis. Deuteronomy 31:6: <hi type="italic">andrizesthe kai ischysate</hi> ("be strong and courageous"), spoken by Moses to all Israel before they enter the land. Joshua 1:6: <hi type="italic">ischye kai andrizou</hi> ("be strong and courageous"), spoken by God to Joshua at the threshold of the conquest. Joshua 1:9: <hi type="italic">ischye kai andrizou</hi> ("be strong and courageous, do not be frightened and do not be dismayed, for the LORD your God is with you wherever you go"). 1 Chronicles 22:13: <hi type="italic">andrizou kai ischye</hi> ("be strong and courageous"), David's charge to Solomon for the building of the temple. Psalm 27:14 (LXX 26:14): <hi type="italic">andrizou kai krataioustho he kardia sou, kai hypomeinon ton kyrion</hi> ("be courageous and let your heart be strengthened, and wait for the Lord"). The LXX consistently pairs <hi type="italic">andrizou</hi> with a form of <hi type="italic">ischyo</hi> or <hi type="italic">krataioo</hi> ("be strong"). Paul follows this LXX pattern precisely, pairing <hi type="italic">andrizesthe</hi> with <hi type="italic">krataiousthe</hi>. The Corinthians are being addressed with the same language God used for Israel at its defining moments: the entrance into the land, the conquest, the building of the temple. The community faces its own battles and its own building (the <hi type="italic">ekklesia</hi> is God's building, 3:9), and it needs the same courage.</p>
          <p>Fourth: <hi type="italic">krataiousthe</hi> ("be strong, be strengthened"). The verb <hi type="italic">krataioo</hi> ("to make strong, to strengthen") completes the LXX pair. In the LXX passages cited above, the pairing of <hi type="italic">andrizomai</hi> and <hi type="italic">krataioo</hi> is standard language for divine encouragement in the face of daunting tasks. The two verbs together describe a community that needs both courage (the inner quality) and strength (the capacity for endurance and action).</p>
          <p>Fifth: <hi type="italic">panta hymon en agape ginestho</hi> ("let all your things be done in love"). This final imperative reframes everything. The word <hi type="italic">panta</hi> ("all things, everything") is comprehensive — it covers every action, every interaction, every exercise of gifts, every act of worship, every dealing with other believers and with outsiders. The word <hi type="italic">agape</hi> ("love") reaches back to chapter 13, where Paul defined love's character at length: love is patient, kind, not envious, not boastful, not arrogant, not rude, does not insist on its own way (13:4-5). Love "does not seek its own" (<hi type="italic">ou zetei ta heautes</hi>, 13:5) — the principle that governed the arguments about idol food (chapters 8-10), the communal meal (chapter 11), and the exercise of spiritual gifts (chapter 14). The watchfulness, firmness, courage, and strength of the first four imperatives must all be exercised <hi type="italic">en agape</hi> — in the sphere of love. Courage without love becomes aggression. Firmness without love becomes rigidity. Watchfulness without love becomes suspicion. Strength without love becomes domination. Love is the medium in which all the other virtues must operate.</p>
          <p>The placement of this imperative is not accidental. It comes after the chapter on the resurrection (15) and before the concluding greetings (vv. 19-24). The community that has been told to "always abound in the work of the Lord" (15:58) is now told that the work must be done in love. The community that will cry "<hi type="italic">Maranatha</hi> — our Lord, come!" (v. 22) is told that while they wait, everything must be governed by love. The love of chapter 13, which "never ends" (13:8) while prophecy, tongues, and knowledge all pass away, is the permanent quality that survives into the age to come. The Corinthians are to live now by the principle that will govern eternity.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.16.15-1Cor.16.18">
          <title>Verses 15-18: The Household of Stephanas and the Recognition of Service</title>
          <p>"Now I urge you, brothers — you know that the household of Stephanas were the first converts in Achaia, and that they have devoted themselves to the service of the saints" (16:15).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">parakaleo</hi> ("I urge, I beseech, I exhort") opens a new section, but the address <hi type="italic">adelphoi</hi> ("brothers") maintains the warm tone of the preceding imperatives. Paul draws attention to a specific household: <hi type="italic">ten oikian Stephana</hi> ("the household of Stephanas"). This household appeared in 1:16, where Paul mentioned it in the context of his baptism practice: "I did baptize also the household of Stephanas. Beyond that, I do not know whether I baptized anyone else." The household of Stephanas was among those Paul personally baptized — a notable distinction, since Paul generally left baptizing to others (1:14-17).</p>
          <p>Paul identifies this household as <hi type="italic">he aparche tes Achaias</hi> ("the firstfruits of Achaia"). The word <hi type="italic">aparche</hi> ("firstfruits") appeared in 15:20 and 15:23, where Christ was called "the firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep." In the LXX, the firstfruits (<hi type="italic">aparche</hi>, translating the Hebrew <hi type="italic">reshit</hi> or <hi type="italic">bikkurim</hi>) were the first portion of the harvest, offered to God as a consecration of the whole crop (Exodus 23:19; Leviticus 23:10-11; Deuteronomy 26:1-11). The firstfruits belonged to God and were the pledge of the full harvest. By calling Stephanas's household "the firstfruits of Achaia," Paul says they were the first people in the province to come to faith — the initial harvest offering, the pledge and guarantee of the larger harvest that followed. Their conversion opened the way for every subsequent believer in the region.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">eis diakonian tois hagiois etaxan heautous</hi> ("they devoted themselves to the service of the saints") uses three significant words. First, <hi type="italic">diakonia</hi> ("service, ministry") — the word Paul used for the apostles' work in 3:5 ("servants [<hi type="italic">diakonoi</hi>] through whom you believed") and in 12:5 ("there are varieties of service [<hi type="italic">diakonion</hi>]"). Second, <hi type="italic">hagiois</hi> ("the saints, the holy ones") — the same designation used for the Jerusalem believers in verse 1. Third, <hi type="italic">etaxan heautous</hi> ("they ordered themselves, they appointed themselves, they arranged themselves"). The verb <hi type="italic">tasso</hi> ("to order, to arrange, to appoint, to assign") with the reflexive <hi type="italic">heautous</hi> ("themselves") describes voluntary self-ordering. No one assigned Stephanas's household to serve. They arranged themselves for service. They saw the need and gave themselves to meeting it. This self-ordering for the sake of others is the antithesis of the self-seeking that has been the Corinthians' persistent disease.</p>
          <p>"Be subject to such as these, and to every fellow worker and laborer" (16:16).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">hypotassesthe</hi> ("be subject, submit yourselves") uses <hi type="italic">hypotasso</hi> ("to place under, to subordinate, to subject"), the same verb that appeared in 15:27-28 (all things subjected under Christ), in 14:32 (spirits of prophets subject to prophets), and in 14:34 (the contested instruction about women being "in subjection"). Here the verb is used for the community's voluntary submission to those who have given themselves to service. The principle is striking: authority in the community flows from service, not from status. Those who have devoted themselves to <hi type="italic">diakonia</hi> — to the hard, unglamorous work of serving the saints — are the ones to whom the community should submit. This inverts the Corinthians' value system, which evaluated leaders by eloquence, wisdom, and impressiveness (chapters 1-4). Paul says: submit to those who serve.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">kai panti to synergounti kai kopionti</hi> ("and to every fellow worker and laborer") extends the principle beyond Stephanas's household to all who do similar work. The word <hi type="italic">synergounti</hi> (from <hi type="italic">synergeio</hi>, "to work together with") identifies these persons as co-workers — people laboring alongside Paul and alongside one another. The word <hi type="italic">kopionti</hi> (from <hi type="italic">kopiao</hi>, "to labor, to toil, to work hard to the point of exhaustion") describes the demanding character of the work. The same verb appeared in 15:10 ("I labored [<hi type="italic">ekopiasa</hi>] more abundantly than they all") and 15:58 ("your labor [<hi type="italic">kopos</hi>] is not in vain in the Lord"). The laborers of 16:16 are the very people doing the "work of the Lord" that 15:58 commanded.</p>
          <p>"I rejoice at the coming of Stephanas and Fortunatus and Achaicus, because they have made up for your absence" (16:17).</p>
          <p>Paul names three individuals: Stephanas (the head of the household mentioned in 1:16 and 16:15), Fortunatus, and Achaicus. These three are very likely the bearers of the Corinthians' letter to Paul (the letter referenced in 7:1: "now concerning the matters about which you wrote"). They traveled from Corinth to Ephesus, carrying the community's questions and presumably reporting on the community's situation. This would explain how Paul knows about both the written questions (answered with the <hi type="italic">peri de</hi> formula in chapters 7, 8, 12, and 16) and the unwritten problems (reported by Chloe's people in 1:11, and possibly supplemented by these three).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">to hymeteron hysterema houtoi aneplerosan</hi> ("they made up for your absence, they supplied what was lacking from you") uses two significant words. The noun <hi type="italic">hysterema</hi> ("deficiency, lack, what is missing") and the verb <hi type="italic">anapleroo</hi> ("to fill up, to supply, to complete") together describe a gap being filled. The "lack" that Paul felt was the Corinthians' physical absence — he could not be with them — and Stephanas, Fortunatus, and Achaicus filled that gap by their presence. They embodied the community in Paul's presence. Through them, Paul had contact with the community that the distance otherwise prevented.</p>
          <p>"For they refreshed my spirit as well as yours. Give recognition to such people" (16:18).</p>
          <p>The verb <hi type="italic">anepausan</hi> ("they refreshed, they gave rest to") comes from <hi type="italic">anapauo</hi> ("to give rest, to refresh, to cause to rest"). In the LXX, <hi type="italic">anapausis</hi> ("rest") is a loaded word: Exodus 33:14 ("my presence will go with you, and I will give you rest [<hi type="italic">anapausin</hi>]"); Psalm 95:11 (LXX 94:11) ("they shall not enter my rest [<hi type="italic">katapausin</hi>]"); Isaiah 28:12 ("this is the rest [<hi type="italic">anapausis</hi>]" — the very passage Paul cited in 14:21). The verb describes genuine renewal and restoration. These three men refreshed Paul's spirit (<hi type="italic">to emon pneuma</hi>) by their visit — by bringing news, by answering questions, by simply being present. And Paul says they refreshed the Corinthians' spirits too (<hi type="italic">kai to hymon</hi>) — perhaps because their visit to Paul would result in this letter being sent back, or because their faithful representation of the community honored and served the community itself.</p>
          <p>The imperative <hi type="italic">epiginoskete oun tous toioutous</hi> ("recognize then such persons, acknowledge such people") uses <hi type="italic">epiginosko</hi> ("to recognize, to acknowledge, to know fully"), the same verb Paul used in 14:37: "let him recognize (<hi type="italic">epiginosketio</hi>) that what I write to you is a command of the Lord." Recognition is not mere awareness but active acknowledgment — giving due honor to those who serve. The Corinthians, who have been evaluating leaders by the wrong standards, need to learn to recognize the right people: not the eloquent, not the impressive, but those who serve, who labor, who travel across the sea to carry letters and refresh spirits.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.16.19-1Cor.16.20">
          <title>Verses 19-20: Greetings from the Churches</title>
          <p>"The churches of Asia send you greetings. Aquila and Prisca, together with the church in their house, send you hearty greetings in the Lord" (16:19).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">hai ekklesiai tes Asias</hi> ("the churches of Asia") refers to the assemblies in the Roman province of Asia, whose capital was Ephesus, where Paul is writing. These communities — in Ephesus, and in the surrounding cities — extend their greeting (<hi type="italic">aspazontai</hi>, from <hi type="italic">aspazomai</hi>, "to greet, to welcome, to embrace") to the Corinthian community. The greeting binds the scattered assemblies together. The Corinthians are not an isolated community; they exist within a network of assemblies that care for one another across geographical distances. This echoes the opening of the letter: the community exists "together with all those who in every place call upon the name of our Lord Jesus Christ" (1:2).</p>
          <p>The naming of Aquila and Prisca (Priscilla) is significant. According to Acts 18:2-3, Aquila and Priscilla were Jewish tentmakers who had come to Corinth from Rome after Claudius's expulsion of Jews from the city. Paul lived and worked with them in Corinth — "he went to see them, and because he was of the same trade he stayed with them and worked, for they were tentmakers by trade" (Acts 18:2-3). They are the Corinthians' old friends, people who shared the community's early life. They apparently moved to Ephesus with Paul (Acts 18:18-19) and now host a house-church there: <hi type="italic">ten kat' oikon auton ekklesion</hi> ("the assembly in their house"). The word <hi type="italic">ekklesia</hi> is used for this household gathering — the assembly that meets in a private home, the basic unit of the early community's life. The Corinthians would recognize Aquila and Prisca and would know that the greeting carries the warmth of a shared history.</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">polla en kyrio</hi> ("much/heartily in the Lord") modifies the greeting: they greet the Corinthians abundantly, and they do so "in the Lord" — within the sphere of their shared relationship to Christ. The greeting is not merely social courtesy; it is an expression of the bond that unites communities across the Aegean.</p>
          <p>"All the brothers send you greetings. Greet one another with a holy kiss" (16:20).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">pantes hoi adelphoi</hi> ("all the brothers") extends the greeting from the named individuals (Aquila and Prisca) to the entire community of believers where Paul is. The word <hi type="italic">pantes</hi> ("all") is inclusive: no one is excluded from the greeting.</p>
          <p>The imperative <hi type="italic">aspasasthe allelous en philemati hagio</hi> ("greet one another with a holy kiss") introduces a liturgical practice of the early assemblies. The word <hi type="italic">philema</hi> ("kiss") with the adjective <hi type="italic">hagion</hi> ("holy") describes a physical gesture of greeting that was set apart — distinguished from ordinary social kissing — as an expression of the community's sacred bond. The same instruction appears in Romans 16:16 ("greet one another with a holy kiss"), 2 Corinthians 13:12, 1 Thessalonians 5:26, and 1 Peter 5:14 ("greet one another with the kiss of love"). The practice likely occurred when the community assembled for worship and was connected to the liturgy. The instruction to greet one another (<hi type="italic">allelous</hi>, "one another" — reciprocal) with this kiss applies within the Corinthian community itself. This community, which has been fragmenting into factions and treating one another with contempt, is told to greet one another with a physical gesture of affection and holiness.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.16.21-1Cor.16.24">
          <title>Verses 21-24: Paul's Own Hand and Final Words</title>
          <p>"I, Paul, write this greeting with my own hand" (16:21).</p>
          <p>The phrase <hi type="italic">ho aspasmos te eme cheiri Paulou</hi> ("the greeting in my own hand — Paul's") signals a shift: Paul takes the pen from his secretary (<hi type="italic">amanuensis</hi>) and writes the final lines himself. Ancient letters were commonly dictated; the sender's autograph at the end served as authentication. Romans 16:22 identifies the secretary of that letter: "I Tertius, who wrote this letter, greet you in the Lord." Galatians 6:11: "See with what large letters I am writing to you with my own hand." Colossians 4:18: "I, Paul, write this greeting with my own hand." 2 Thessalonians 3:17: "I, Paul, write this greeting with my own hand. This is the sign of genuineness in every letter of mine; this is how I write." The autograph guarantees that the letter genuinely comes from Paul. The Corinthians, holding this letter, would see the handwriting change at this point — the professional script of the secretary giving way to Paul's own hand. The final words carry the weight of personal presence: Paul himself, physically touching the papyrus, addressing the community he founded.</p>
          <p>"If anyone has no love for the Lord, let him be accursed. Our Lord, come!" (16:22).</p>
          <p>This verse contains two distinct elements: an anathema formula and an Aramaic liturgical cry.</p>
          <p>First: <hi type="italic">ei tis ou philei ton kyrion, eto anathema</hi> ("if anyone does not love the Lord, let him be accursed"). The verb <hi type="italic">phileo</hi> ("to love, to have affection for, to be devoted to") describes personal attachment and devotion. The word is not <hi type="italic">agapao</hi> (the verb of chapter 13) but <hi type="italic">phileo</hi>, which in common usage carries a warmer, more personal connotation — the love of friendship, of intimate affection, of deep personal attachment. The condition is stated in the negative: if anyone <hi type="italic">ou philei</hi> ("does not love") the Lord. The consequence: <hi type="italic">eto anathema</hi> ("let him be accursed, let him be devoted to destruction").</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">anathema</hi> has a long history in the LXX. It translates the Hebrew <hi type="italic">herem</hi> — the ban of total destruction. In Joshua 6:17-18, Jericho and everything in it was declared <hi type="italic">herem</hi> (<hi type="italic">anathema</hi>): "the city and all that is within it shall be devoted to the LORD for destruction." In Joshua 7:1, 11-12, Achan took some of the <hi type="italic">herem</hi> and brought disaster on all Israel: "Israel has sinned; they have transgressed my covenant... they have taken some of the devoted things (<hi type="italic">anathema</hi>)." In 1 Samuel 15, Saul was commanded to devote the Amalekites to <hi type="italic">herem</hi> (<hi type="italic">anathema</hi>); his failure to do so completely cost him the kingdom. The word describes something set apart for destruction, removed from the community and from God's favor, placed under an irreversible curse.</p>
          <p>Within this letter, the word <hi type="italic">anathema</hi> appeared in 12:3: "no one speaking by the Spirit of God ever says 'Jesus is anathema (<hi type="italic">anathema</hi>).'" There, anathema was the declaration that the Spirit prevents anyone from making about Jesus. Here in 16:22, the logic is inverted: the person who does not love the Lord is themselves anathema. The symmetry is precise. To call Jesus accursed is the mark of not having the Spirit (12:3). To fail to love the Lord results in being accursed oneself (16:22). Love for the Lord is the irreducible minimum of belonging to the community. All the problems Paul has addressed — the factionalism, the immorality, the lawsuits, the disordered worship, the denial of resurrection — are, at their root, failures of love for the Lord. The anathema formula places a boundary around the community: those who do not love the Lord are outside.</p>
          <p>This connects to the series of exclusion warnings that run through the letter. In 3:17: "if anyone destroys God's temple, God will destroy him." In 5:5: the incestuous man is to be "delivered to Satan for the destruction of the flesh." In 5:11-13: "purge the evil person from among you." In 6:9-10: "do you not know that the unrighteous will not inherit the kingdom of God?" In 10:21-22: "you cannot drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of demons." The anathema of 16:22 is the letter's final boundary marker, stated in the most elemental terms: love for the Lord, or exclusion from the community.</p>
          <p>Second: <hi type="italic">marana tha</hi> ("Our Lord, come!"). This is an Aramaic phrase preserved in its original language within a Greek letter. The phrase consists of either <hi type="italic">marana tha</hi> ("our Lord, come!" — an imperative, a prayer for the Lord's return) or <hi type="italic">maran atha</hi> ("our Lord has come" — a perfect tense declaration). The imperative reading fits the context better: placed alongside the anathema formula, the cry "Our Lord, come!" appeals to the returning Lord as the one who will execute the judgment implicit in the anathema.</p>
          <p>The preservation of this Aramaic phrase in a Greek letter written to a predominantly Gentile community is extraordinary. It testifies to the antiquity and sacredness of the formula. The earliest Aramaic-speaking community of believers — the community in Jerusalem and Palestine — used this cry in their worship, and it was transmitted to the Greek-speaking communities without translation, preserved in its original language because of its liturgical weight. The <hi type="italic">Didache</hi> (10:6), a first-century or early second-century text, preserves the same phrase in its eucharistic liturgy: "Let grace come and let this world pass away. Hosanna to the God of David. If anyone is holy, let him come; if anyone is not, let him repent. <hi type="italic">Maranatha</hi>. Amen." In the <hi type="italic">Didache</hi>, the <hi type="italic">maranatha</hi> cry appears immediately after a holiness formula (an exclusion of the unholy and a call to repentance) — exactly the same pairing that Paul presents in 16:22: first the exclusion of the one who does not love the Lord (anathema), then the cry for the Lord's coming (<hi type="italic">maranatha</hi>).</p>
          <p>The eschatological cry connects to the letter's opening and closing frames. In 1:7-8, Paul described the community as "waiting for the revealing (<hi type="italic">apokalypsin</hi>) of our Lord Jesus Christ, who will sustain you to the end, guiltless in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ." The community exists in expectation. In 4:5, Paul warned: "do not pronounce judgment before the time, before the Lord comes (<hi type="italic">heos an elthe ho kyrios</hi>)." In 11:26, the Lord's supper is celebrated "until he comes" (<hi type="italic">achri hou elthe</hi>). In 15:23, "those who are Christ's" will be made alive "at his coming" (<hi type="italic">en te parousia autou</hi>). The <hi type="italic">maranatha</hi> of 16:22 is the cry that expresses this expectation: "Our Lord, come!" The community that celebrates the supper "until he comes" cries out for him to come. The community that awaits the "revealing" at the end asks for that end to arrive. The eschatological tension of the entire letter — the "already" of Christ's resurrection and the "not yet" of the final transformation — is compressed into two Aramaic words.</p>
          <p>"The grace of the Lord Jesus be with you" (16:23).</p>
          <p>The benediction <hi type="italic">he charis tou kyriou Iesou meth' hymon</hi> ("the grace of the Lord Jesus [be] with you") is Paul's standard closing blessing. The word <hi type="italic">charis</hi> ("grace") appeared in the opening greeting (1:3, "grace to you and peace") and in the description of the Corinthians' enrichment (1:4, "the grace of God given to you in Christ Jesus"). The letter began with grace and ends with grace. The source of the grace is identified: <hi type="italic">tou kyriou Iesou</hi> ("of the Lord Jesus"). The community's life began in grace and continues in grace. Whatever corrections Paul has made, whatever sharp words he has delivered, whatever boundaries he has drawn, the final word is grace. The letter does not end with the anathema but with the blessing.</p>
          <p>"My love be with you all in Christ Jesus. Amen" (16:24).</p>
          <p>The final sentence is intensely personal: <hi type="italic">he agape mou meta panton hymon en Christo Iesou</hi> ("my love [be] with all of you in Christ Jesus"). Paul, who has spent sixteen chapters correcting, rebuking, instructing, warning, and regulating, closes with a declaration of personal love. The word <hi type="italic">agape</hi> ("love") — the word of chapter 13, the word that governs everything (v. 14, "let all be done in love") — now describes Paul's own relationship to the community. The phrase <hi type="italic">meta panton hymon</hi> ("with all of you") is inclusive: Paul's love is not for one faction or another, not for the mature or the immature, not for those who agree with him or those who resist him. It is for all of them. The phrase <hi type="italic">en Christo Iesou</hi> ("in Christ Jesus") locates the love in its proper sphere: Paul's love for the Corinthians exists within and because of their shared relationship to Christ. It is not mere personal affection; it is love that flows from the bond they share in Christ.</p>
          <p>The word Amen ("truly, so be it, let it be confirmed") closes the letter with the community's word of ratification — the same Amen that the uninstructed person could not say to unintelligible speech (14:16), the Amen that requires understanding and assent. Here it seals the entire letter.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="1Cor.16">
          <title>The Chapter's Argument as a Whole</title>
          <p>1 Corinthians 16 is not a miscellaneous appendix of practical details. It is the concrete expression of the theological and ethical vision that the entire letter has been building. Every section of the chapter connects to arguments Paul made earlier, and together they show what a community living by the gospel actually does.</p>
          <p>The collection for Jerusalem (vv. 1-4) is the material expression of the unity Paul has demanded throughout. The community that was fragmenting internally — "I am of Paul," "I am of Apollos," "I am of Cephas" (1:12) — is now directed outward, toward fellow believers they have never met, in a city they may never visit. The love that "does not seek its own" (13:5) takes the form of money set aside week by week for people across the sea. The grace (<hi type="italic">charis</hi>) that the Corinthians received from God (1:4) flows outward as a grace-gift (<hi type="italic">charin</hi>, 16:3) to Jerusalem. The proportional giving — "as he may prosper" (16:2) — reflects the principle that gifts are given by God and are to be used for others (4:7, 12:7). The collection is also a test of the Corinthians' willingness to recognize their place within the larger body. They are part of a network of assemblies, not an autonomous entity. What happens in Jerusalem matters to Corinth, and what Corinth has materially, Jerusalem needs.</p>
          <p>The travel plans (vv. 5-9) reveal a world in which communities are connected by traveling workers who carry the gospel, carry letters, carry money, and carry news from place to place. Paul, Timothy, Apollos, Stephanas, Fortunatus, Achaicus, Aquila, and Prisca — these are the sinews that hold the scattered assemblies together. The community is not a self-contained unit but a node in a living network. Paul's plans are subordinated to the Lord's permission (v. 7) and shaped by the opportunities and opposition he encounters (v. 9). The worker who "abounds in the work of the Lord" (15:58) does so within the constraints and openings that the Lord provides.</p>
          <p>The instructions about Timothy (vv. 10-11) directly address the disease diagnosed in chapters 1-4. The Corinthians evaluated leaders by worldly standards — rhetorical skill, wisdom, impressiveness. Timothy may lack these qualities, and Paul anticipates that the Corinthians will despise him. The command "let no one despise him" (16:11) is the practical application of the cross-centered logic of 1:18-31: God chose the foolish, the weak, the low, the despised. The Corinthians' God works through apparent weakness. To despise Timothy is to revert to the worldly evaluation that the cross has overthrown.</p>
          <p>The note about Apollos (v. 12) closes the faction narrative that opened the letter. In 1:12, "I am of Apollos" was a slogan of division. In 3:5-9, Paul redefined the relationship: Paul planted, Apollos watered, God gave the growth; neither the planter nor the waterer is anything, but only God. In 4:6, Paul said he had "applied all these things to myself and Apollos for your benefit." Now in 16:12, the resolution is complete: Apollos is "the brother." Paul urged him to visit. He declined. There is no competition, no rivalry, no jealousy. The faction leaders whom the Corinthians pitted against each other are, in Paul's telling, simply brothers who serve the same Lord and make their own decisions about when and where to travel.</p>
          <p>The five imperatives (vv. 13-14) compress the letter's theology into a series of commands. "Be watchful" addresses the eschatological expectation of 1:7-8 and chapter 15. "Stand firm in the faith" addresses the gospel foundation of 15:1-4 and the wavering on resurrection. "Be courageous" and "be strong" draw on the LXX language of divine encouragement spoken to Israel at its moments of crisis, placing the community in continuity with Israel's story. "Let all be done in love" reaches back to chapter 13 and governs everything else. The series moves from eschatological alertness through doctrinal firmness through courage and strength to the comprehensive principle of love. The order matters: love is last not because it is least but because it is the environment in which all the other virtues must exist.</p>
          <p>The commendation of Stephanas's household (vv. 15-18) provides the letter's model for legitimate leadership. The Corinthians have been evaluating leaders by eloquence and status. Paul offers a different criterion: service. Stephanas's household "devoted themselves to the service of the saints" (16:15). They saw the need and gave themselves to meeting it. No one appointed them; they appointed themselves. And to these self-appointed servants — not to the eloquent, not to the impressive, not to the faction leaders — the community is told to submit (<hi type="italic">hypotassesthe</hi>, 16:16). Authority in the community flows from voluntary service, not from social standing or rhetorical skill. This inverts the Corinthian value system and embodies the theology of the cross: God chose the weak, the low, the despised (1:27-28), and the community's leadership structure should reflect God's values, not the world's.</p>
          <p>The greetings (vv. 19-20) bind the assemblies together across the Aegean. The churches of Asia, Aquila and Prisca with the assembly in their house, all the brothers — the web of relationships is dense and warm. The holy kiss (v. 20) is the physical gesture of this bond: a community that greets one another with holiness, not competition; with affection, not contempt.</p>
          <p>The final words in Paul's own hand (vv. 21-24) bring the letter to a close with four elements that correspond to the letter's major themes. The anathema (v. 22a) draws the boundary: love for the Lord is the irreducible minimum, and the one who does not love the Lord stands outside. The <hi type="italic">maranatha</hi> (v. 22b) expresses the eschatological hope: the community lives in expectation of its Lord's return, the hope that grounds the resurrection argument of chapter 15 and the watchfulness of verse 13. The grace benediction (v. 23) returns to the opening greeting: the letter began with grace (1:3) and ends with grace. And the personal declaration of love (v. 24) — "my love be with you all in Christ Jesus" — closes the letter with the very quality that chapter 13 declared greatest and verse 14 commanded as the governing principle of everything.</p>
          <p>Several threads from throughout the letter reach their final expression in this chapter.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">ergon</hi> ("work") and its cognates provide a structural thread. In 3:13-15, each person's work (<hi type="italic">ergon</hi>) will be tested by fire. In 9:1, the Corinthians are Paul's <hi type="italic">ergon</hi> ("work") in the Lord. In 15:58, the community is to abound in the <hi type="italic">ergon</hi> of the Lord, knowing their <hi type="italic">kopos</hi> ("labor") is not in vain. In 16:10, Timothy is doing the <hi type="italic">ergon</hi> of the Lord. In 16:16, the community is to submit to every <hi type="italic">synergon</hi> ("fellow worker") and <hi type="italic">kopion</hi> ("laborer"). The letter's vision of work runs from the foundation Paul laid (3:10) through the testing of fire (3:13) to the resurrection hope that guarantees the work's value (15:58) to the concrete workers — Timothy, Stephanas, Fortunatus, Achaicus — who embody the work in chapter 16. The theological argument about resurrection (chapter 15) and the practical instructions about workers (chapter 16) are linked by this vocabulary: the work is not in vain because the resurrection is real, and the workers who carry out the work deserve recognition precisely because the work matters.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">charis</hi> ("grace") provides another thread. In 1:3-4, grace was the opening gift from God. In 3:10, Paul laid the foundation "according to the grace of God given to me." In 15:10, Paul attributed his labor to God's grace: "by the grace of God I am what I am, and his grace toward me was not in vain." In 16:3, the collection is called a <hi type="italic">charin</hi> — a grace-gift. In 16:23, the closing benediction invokes "the grace of the Lord Jesus." The letter begins and ends with grace, and the word links God's initiative (1:4), Paul's labor (15:10), the community's generosity (16:3), and the Lord's final blessing (16:23) into a single arc. Everything is grace — received from God, enacted through labor, expressed through giving, sealed by the Lord's own grace.</p>
          <p>The word <hi type="italic">agape</hi> ("love") runs from chapter 13 through the closing. In 13:1-13, love was defined and exalted as the greatest of the permanent qualities. In 14:1, the assembly's worship was to begin from "pursue love." In 16:14, everything is to be done "in love." In 16:22, the one who does not love (<hi type="italic">philei</hi>) the Lord is accursed. In 16:24, Paul's own love (<hi type="italic">agape</hi>) is with all of them in Christ Jesus. The letter's trajectory on love moves from theological definition (chapter 13) through practical application in worship (chapter 14) to comprehensive command (16:14) to boundary (16:22) to Paul's own personal expression (16:24). Love is not merely a topic Paul addresses; it is the atmosphere in which the letter ends.</p>
          <p>The eschatological frame that opened the letter (1:7-8: "waiting for the revealing of our Lord Jesus Christ, who will sustain you to the end, guiltless in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ") closes with the <hi type="italic">maranatha</hi> cry (16:22). The community that was described in the opening as waiting is now, at the close, actively crying out for the Lord's coming. The passive "waiting" of 1:7 becomes the active "Our Lord, come!" of 16:22. Between these two poles — the patient waiting and the urgent crying out — the community lives its daily life: collecting money, receiving workers, greeting one another, standing firm, acting in love. The eschatological hope does not lift the community out of the world; it grounds the community more deeply in the practical work of love, service, and mutual care that constitutes the "work of the Lord" until he comes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
